window.__COURSE__=JSON.parse("{\"meta\":{\"version\":\"course-2026.09.1\",\"built\":\"2026-09-29\",\"checked\":\"2026-09-29\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"guide\",\"tag\":\"IB OFFICIAL\",\"title\":\"Economics guide\",\"version\":\"First assessment 2022\",\"note\":\"Syllabus content, command terms, assessment outline. Checked against the text of the supplied PDF on 29 September 2026. The IB may have issued updates since; confirm current requirements with your school.\"},{\"id\":\"tsm\",\"tag\":\"IB OFFICIAL\",\"title\":\"Economics teacher support material\",\"version\":\"First assessment 2022\",\"note\":\"Pedagogical guidance: used for structure and inquiry design, not quoted.\"},{\"id\":\"roadmap\",\"tag\":\"TEACHER-CREATED\",\"title\":\"Curriculum Architecture & Strategic Teaching Roadmap (Arjun Agrawal)\",\"version\":\"Supplied 2026\",\"note\":\"Sequencing, hours and difficulty are a teacher’s recommendations, not IB requirements.\"},{\"id\":\"miscdb\",\"tag\":\"TEACHER-CREATED\",\"title\":\"IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\",\"version\":\"51 entries, supplied 2026\",\"note\":\"Misconceptions and observations are a teacher’s, not IB examiner findings.\"},{\"id\":\"examiner\",\"tag\":\"TEACHER-CREATED\",\"title\":\"How to Think Like an IB Economics Examiner (Arjun Agrawal)\",\"version\":\"Supplied 2026\",\"note\":\"A teacher’s interpretation of assessment; official wording is the guide’s.\"},{\"id\":\"platform\",\"tag\":\"ARJUN AGRAWAL ANALYSIS\",\"title\":\"Original explanations written for this platform\",\"version\":\"course-2026.09.1\",\"note\":\"Lessons, model cards, checks and examples are original. Illustrative numbers are invented and labelled.\"}],\"unavailable\":[\"Economics, Ellie Tragakes, 3rd edition (Cambridge, 2020)\",\"Diploma Programme Assessment Procedures 2026\",\"IB Academic Integrity Policy\",\"IB Programme Standards and Practices\"],\"counts\":{\"spines\":31,\"spineWords\":127966,\"misc\":51,\"gloss\":250,\"glossNew\":86,\"compare\":24,\"ct\":33,\"checklists\":12,\"concepts\":9,\"lens\":17,\"intel\":6},\"hash\":\"5dc5ffeadf184822\"},\"spines\":{\"1.1\":{\"code\":\"1.1\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Every society wants more than its land, labour, capital and enterprise can produce. Economics studies how people, firms and governments decide what to do with those limited resources, and what they give up each time. The production possibilities curve and the circular flow are the first models that make those decisions visible.\",\"why\":\"Every budget, whether a household’s, a firm’s or a government’s, is a set of trade-offs. Seeing the alternative given up is what separates an economic argument from a wish list.\",\"q\":\"If a government spends more on hospitals, what does the country give up, and who decides?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Reading a line graph: axes, points and slope\",\"sub\":null},{\"t\":\"Stocks (amounts) versus flows (amounts per period)\",\"sub\":null},{\"t\":\"Simple ratios, for working out what is given up per unit gained\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the four factors of production and the income each earns\",\"Define scarcity, opportunity cost and free good\",\"Identify the three basic economic questions and the three economic systems\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why scarcity forces choice even in a rich economy\",\"Explain why a zero-price good can still have an opportunity cost\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a news event as a move towards, a move along, or a shift of the PPC\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse why a PPC bows outward when resources are specialised\",\"Analyse what happens to national income when leakages exceed injections\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate market-led and government-led answers to the basic economic questions\",\"Evaluate what a PPC cannot tell a society about which point to choose\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate an opportunity cost from two points on a PPC (illustrative data)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a PPC showing opportunity cost, unemployment of resources, actual growth and growth in production possibilities\",\"Draw PPCs with increasing and with constant opportunity cost\",\"Draw the circular flow with leakages and injections\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a point inside, on or beyond a PPC\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"A social science of choices\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Economics is a social science: it studies how people behave and interact when they use scarce resources. It can rarely run controlled experiments on whole economies, and the people it studies can react to its predictions. Microeconomics studies individual decision-makers and markets, such as the market for rice. Macroeconomics studies the whole economy: total output, the price level, unemployment and growth. Nine key concepts run through both: scarcity, choice, efficiency, equity, economic well-being, sustainability, change, interdependence and intervention (guide PDF pp. 18–20, 28).\"},{\"h\":\"The four factors of production\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Factors of production are the resources used to make goods and services. Each is limited, each can improve in quality, and each earns its own kind of income. Capital is not money. Money is only a claim on resources; capital means goods made in order to produce other goods, such as machines, roads and software. Many economists also count workers’ skills and health as human capital.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Factor\",\"Meaning\",\"Income\"],\"rows\":[[\"Land\",\"Natural resources, such as soil, minerals and fish stocks\",\"Rent\"],[\"Labour\",\"Human effort, physical and mental\",\"Wages\"],[\"Capital\",\"Man-made aids to production; human capital\",\"Interest\"],[\"Entrepreneurship\",\"Organising the other factors and bearing risk\",\"Profit\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Scarcity forces choice, and choice has a cost\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Scarcity means resources are limited relative to the needs and wants they could satisfy. It is not a shortage, which is a market condition at one price and can disappear when the price changes. Scarcity persists however rich a society becomes. So every use of a resource has an opportunity cost: the value of the next best alternative forgone. That is one alternative, not the sum of all of them, and not the money spent. Scarcity also has a future dimension: fish stocks or groundwater used up today are unavailable to later generations, which links scarcity to sustainability.\",\"because\":[\"Wants are, in practice, unlimited\",\"Resources are limited\",\"So not every want can be met\",\"So using a resource for one purpose means not using it for another\",\"So every choice has an opportunity cost\"]},{\"h\":\"Free goods and zero-price goods are different\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"A free good is not scarce, so using it has no opportunity cost: sunlight, or air in most places. A zero price is a different thing. A state school may charge no fees, but its teachers and buildings could have been used elsewhere, so it has an opportunity cost that taxpayers bear. The test is scarcity, not the price tag. Pollution can become excessive partly because clean air is treated as free when, near busy roads and factories, it has in fact become scarce.\"},{\"h\":\"Three questions, three systems\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Every economy must decide what and how much to produce, how to produce it, and for whom. A free market economy leaves these decisions mainly to prices and private owners. A planned economy gives them to the state, which owns most means of production and sets output targets. A mixed economy combines the two. Every real economy is mixed; what differs is the balance. So the useful question is how far, and where, government should intervene.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Feature\",\"Free market\",\"Planned\",\"Mixed\"],\"rows\":[[\"Decisions made by\",\"Consumers and firms, through prices\",\"State planners, through targets\",\"Markets for most goods; government for some, such as defence and health\"],[\"Typical strength\",\"Prices signal wants; profit rewards efficiency\",\"Can direct resources to priority goals and narrow inequality\",\"Can pair market incentives with public provision\"],[\"Typical weakness\",\"Under-provides public goods; can create wide inequality\",\"Planners lack information; weak incentives; shortages\",\"The right balance is contested; governments can fail too\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The PPC: scarcity, choice and opportunity cost in one diagram\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A production possibilities curve (PPC) shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce in a period, with fixed resources and technology fully and efficiently used. Points beyond the curve are unattainable: scarcity. Society must pick a point: choice. Moving along the curve gives up some of one good for more of the other: opportunity cost. A point inside shows unemployed or misused resources; it is attainable, just wasteful. If resources suit both goods equally, opportunity cost is constant and the PPC is a straight line. Usually they do not, so the curve bows outward.\",\"because\":[\"Resources are not equally good at producing both goods\",\"The first resources moved into good Y are those best suited to it\",\"Later ones are less suited to Y and better at X\",\"So each extra unit of Y costs more X\",\"So opportunity cost rises and the curve is concave to the origin\"],\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Event\",\"On the PPC\",\"What it shows\"],\"rows\":[[\"Idle workers find jobs\",\"A point inside moves towards the curve\",\"Actual growth\"],[\"More X, less Y, all resources employed\",\"Movement along the curve\",\"The opportunity cost of X\"],[\"Workers’ skills improve\",\"The curve shifts outward\",\"Growth in production possibilities\"],[\"An earthquake destroys capital\",\"The curve shifts inward\",\"Lost production possibilities\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The circular flow of income\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The circular flow models the economy as flows between decision-makers. Households sell factor services to firms and receive wages, rent, interest and profit. They spend that income on firms’ output, which becomes firms’ revenue. Some income leaks out: saving through the financial sector, taxes to government, and spending on imports from the foreign sector. Other spending is injected: investment, government spending and export revenue. When injections exceed leakages, the flow of income grows; when leakages exceed injections, it shrinks. Because each sector’s income depends on others’ spending, the model shows interdependence.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Sector\",\"Leakage\",\"Injection\"],\"rows\":[[\"Financial sector\",\"Saving (S)\",\"Investment (I)\"],[\"Government\",\"Taxes (T)\",\"Government spending (G)\"],[\"Foreign sector\",\"Imports (M)\",\"Exports (X)\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A government with a fixed budget builds hospitals instead of a road\",\"cause\":\"A government funds two new hospitals without raising its total budget.\",\"mechanism\":\"Builders, land and materials are drawn away from their next best use, a planned road.\",\"immediate\":\"Health capacity rises and the road is cancelled; the road, not the money, is the opportunity cost.\",\"secondary\":\"Road users bear that cost through slower travel, while patients and health workers gain.\",\"longrun\":\"If healthier workers are more productive, the PPC may shift outward; if the road would have raised productivity more, growth in production possibilities is slower.\",\"depends\":\"What the next best alternative really was, each project’s productivity, who gains and loses, and whether resources were idle; if they were, the opportunity cost is lower.\"},{\"t\":\"Households decide to save a larger share of their income\",\"cause\":\"Worried about their jobs, households save more of each unit of income.\",\"mechanism\":\"Saving is a leakage: income is not spent on firms’ output.\",\"immediate\":\"Firms’ sales fall and unsold stock builds up.\",\"secondary\":\"Firms cut output and jobs, so household incomes fall, which can reduce spending further.\",\"longrun\":\"If the financial sector lends the extra saving for investment, that injection can offset the leakage, and new capital can shift the PPC outward.\",\"depends\":\"Whether saving becomes investment, business confidence, interest rates, and whether government spending or exports rise at the same time.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Production possibilities curve (PPC)\",\"dg\":\"ppc\",\"purpose\":\"Shows scarcity, choice and opportunity cost for a whole economy, and separates using existing capacity from expanding it.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Two goods, or two groups such as capital and consumer goods\",\"Fixed quantity and quality of factors of production\",\"Fixed technology\",\"Points on the curve use all resources fully and efficiently\"],\"mechanism\":\"The curve joins maximum output combinations. Moving along it shifts resources between goods; its slope is the opportunity cost of the horizontal-axis good in units of the other.\",\"prediction\":\"At full employment, more of one good means less of another. Output rises without that sacrifice only by using idle resources or by shifting the curve.\",\"application\":\"Budget trade-offs, the cost of unemployment, actual versus potential growth and, at HL, comparative advantage (4.1).\",\"limitations\":[\"Real economies produce millions of goods, not two\",\"It shows efficient points, not which point society prefers\",\"It is silent on who receives the output\"],\"real\":\"Economies already near full employment that switched heavily to military production in wartime typically cut civilian consumption, a movement along the curve; an economy starting with many idle resources could raise both. In a recession, rising unemployment moves an economy inside its curve.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling a point inside the curve impossible, or showing re-employment of idle resources as an outward shift.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) and Paper 2: name both goods on the axes, label PPC₁ and PPC₂, mark points A, B and C, and explain in words the opportunity cost or type of growth.\",\"deeper\":\"The outward bow is a claim about how specialised resources are, not a law of nature. The model also has a dynamic reading. Choosing more capital goods now means fewer consumer goods today but a larger outward shift later, so the PPC links present sacrifice with future growth. If growth runs down natural capital such as forests or fish stocks, the same logic can shift the curve inward, which ties the model to sustainability.\"},{\"model\":\"The circular flow of income\",\"dg\":\"circular\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how one group’s spending becomes another’s income, and why national income rises or falls.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Spending on output becomes income for the factors that produced it\",\"Flows are measured per period\",\"Money leaves only through leakages and enters only through injections\"],\"mechanism\":\"If injections exceed leakages, spending and income rise; if leakages exceed injections, they fall. The flow settles where planned leakages equal planned injections.\",\"prediction\":\"Other things equal, a fall in an injection or a rise in a leakage tends to reduce national income and employment.\",\"application\":\"Measuring national income (3.1), aggregate demand (3.2), the multiplier (HL, 3.6) and trade (Unit 4).\",\"limitations\":[\"It shows flows, not stocks such as wealth, debt or natural capital\",\"It leaves the environment outside the loop\",\"Leakages are not simply harmful: saving can fund investment\"],\"real\":\"During the COVID-19 pandemic, household saving rose sharply in many high-income economies while governments raised spending; the model helps explain why governments saw a case for injections to offset the extra leakage.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating leakages as waste. Saving can return as investment, and imports are how households enjoy goods made abroad.\",\"exam\":\"Draw households and firms with money and real flows, attach the three leakages and injections to the right sectors, and use the diagram to explain interdependence or a change in income.\",\"deeper\":\"The loop mirrors an accounting identity: output, income and expenditure are three measures of one flow, which is why 3.1 offers three ways to measure GDP. Ecological economists argue the diagram is incomplete because the economy sits inside the environment, drawing materials and energy from it and returning waste. That critique links this model to the circular economy in 1.2 and to sustainability in 4.7.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The PPC predicts that at full employment more of one good costs some of another, while an economy with idle resources can raise output with little sacrifice.\",\"evidence\":\"Output tends to fall below its long-run trend in recessions and recover afterwards, which fits moving inside the frontier and back. But the frontier cannot be observed; estimates of potential output are uncertain and often revised.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists see recessions as avoidable movements inside the curve that policy can reduce. Others argue that part of the apparent spare capacity is already lost, for example as skills decay in long unemployment, so the curve has shifted inward. The answer affects how hard a government should push spending.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Are wants really unlimited?\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"The course treats wants as unlimited, which is why scarcity persists. Not every economist accepts this in full. J. K. Galbraith argued that many wants in rich societies are created by the process that satisfies them, through advertising and comparison with others. Ecological economists add that the tightest limit may be the planet’s capacity to absorb waste, not labour or capital. The first view questions whether more output raises well-being; the second questions whether the PPC can keep shifting outward on finite natural resources.\"},{\"h\":\"Planning and the information problem\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"Why have planned economies struggled to answer the basic questions? One influential answer, from Friedrich Hayek, is information. Knowledge about costs, local conditions and wants is scattered across millions of people, and prices condense it into signals anyone can act on. A planner must collect it, and much of it cannot be written down. Critics reply that large firms plan internally without prices, and that markets fail too, under-providing public goods and over-producing pollution (2.8, 2.9). So the practical debate is about the mix.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Economics starts from a mismatch: people want more than can be produced. Land, workers, machines and business skill are limited, so a society must choose what to make, how, and for whom. Every choice has a cost that is easy to miss: the best thing you gave up. A new hospital costs the road the builders would otherwise have built. The PPC draws that trade-off as a curve, and the circular flow shows how one person’s spending becomes another’s income.\",\"exam\":\"Define first: opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone. Then draw the model the question needs. For a trade-off, name both goods on the PPC axes, mark two points on the curve and show what is given up. For growth, separate a move from inside towards the curve (actual growth) from an outward shift (growth in production possibilities), and name the cause. For interdependence, use the circular flow and name the leakage or injection that changes. Evaluate by asking whether resources were really fully employed, who bears the opportunity cost, and how the choice affects the economy’s future capacity.\",\"deep\":\"These two models do quite different jobs. The PPC is about capacity: what an economy could produce, silent on whether anyone will buy it. The circular flow is about spending: income and expenditure, silent on capacity. Together they frame Unit 3’s central question: is output inside the frontier because spending is too low, or is the frontier itself the limit? Opportunity cost runs under both. Leaving resources idle also has a cost, the output they could have produced, which is why unemployment counts as waste, not a neutral outcome. Neither model says who gains: a point on the curve can be efficient and still deeply unequal. Both also take the environment as given, which is the gap that sustainability questions later in the course try to fill.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"here\":\"Scarcity is why economics exists: limited resources set against growing wants.\",\"tension\":\"Using resources harder today can deepen scarcity for future generations.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on water restrictions or overfishing can use scarcity to explain why a resource is being rationed.\"},{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"Every point on a PPC is a choice, and the slope shows its opportunity cost.\",\"tension\":\"A choice that is efficient overall can load its cost onto one group.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 3 article on a government budget can use choice to ask which spending was given up.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"The circular flow shows that one sector’s spending is another’s income.\",\"tension\":\"Saving more is sensible for one household, but if many save at once, incomes elsewhere can fall.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 4 article on falling export orders can use interdependence to trace effects on domestic jobs.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do rich countries still face scarcity?\",\"expect\":\"Many students expect high incomes to end scarcity once basic needs are met.\",\"model\":\"Scarcity is relative: while wants exceed what resources can produce, choices and opportunity costs remain at any income.\",\"evidence\":\"Rich countries still argue over health, pensions, defence and climate spending, and consumption per person has tended to keep rising with income rather than levelling off.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes. Scarcity changes form, towards time, clean air or housing in popular cities, but does not disappear.\",\"assume\":\"That wants keep growing, and that resources and technology are fixed at any moment.\",\"change\":\"If wants were satiable, or technology made some goods almost costless to copy, as with digital files, scarcity in those goods would weaken.\",\"policy\":\"Wealth does not remove trade-offs; spending decisions still need an explicit comparison with the alternatives given up.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a PPC, the difference between actual growth and growth in production possibilities.\",\"check\":[\"Both axes name a good\",\"Actual growth shown as a move from a point inside towards the curve\",\"Actual growth linked to using idle or misused resources\",\"Growth in production possibilities shown as a shift from PPC₁ to PPC₂\",\"The shift linked to more or better resources or technology\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a school that charges no fees still has an opportunity cost.\",\"check\":[\"Defines opportunity cost as the value of the next best alternative forgone\",\"Identifies the scarce resources used: teachers, buildings, materials\",\"Names a plausible alternative use of them\",\"Distinguishes a zero price from a free good\",\"States that a free good is one that is not scarce\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define opportunity cost.\",\"a\":\"The value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the four factors of production and the income each earns.\",\"a\":\"Land (rent), labour (wages), capital (interest), entrepreneurship (profit).\"},{\"q\":\"What does a point inside a PPC show?\",\"a\":\"Unemployed or inefficiently used resources: attainable, but not productively efficient.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: on its PPC an economy can make 100 tonnes of food and 40 machines, or 80 tonnes and 50 machines. What is the opportunity cost of the extra 10 machines?\",\"a\":\"20 tonnes of food, or 2 tonnes per machine.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the three leakages and three injections in the circular flow.\",\"a\":\"Leakages: saving, taxes, imports. Injections: investment, government spending, exports.\"},{\"q\":\"Why can a PPC not tell a society which point to choose?\",\"a\":\"It shows what is possible, not what people prefer; choosing needs information on preferences and a value judgement about distribution.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Unemployed workers find jobs; resources and technology are unchanged. What happens on the PPC?\",\"opts\":[\"The PPC shifts outward\",\"The economy moves from inside the PPC towards the curve\",\"The economy moves along the PPC\",\"The PPC shifts inward\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Using idle resources raises actual output without changing what the economy could produce, so the point moves towards the existing curve. An outward shift is tempting because output grew, but the curve moves only when resources or technology change.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-17\"},{\"q\":\"A city park charges no entry fee. Is it a free good?\",\"opts\":[\"Yes, because visitors pay nothing\",\"Yes, because the government provides it\",\"No, because its land and upkeep have other possible uses\",\"No, because only natural resources can be free goods\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A free good is not scarce, so it has no opportunity cost. The park’s land and staff could be used elsewhere, so it has an opportunity cost at a zero price. The first option confuses price with cost.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"A student revises for an evening. Her next best option was a paid shift; her third was a film. What is the opportunity cost of revising?\",\"opts\":[\"The shift and the film together\",\"The paid shift only\",\"The film only\",\"Nothing, because revising costs no money\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Opportunity cost is the single next best alternative, the paid shift. Adding every option overstates it, since she could only do one that evening. The last option treats opportunity cost as money spent.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using a PPC, scarcity, choice and opportunity cost, or a type of growth (questions can come from all four units, including Unit 1: guide PDF p. 66)\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using the circular flow, how a change in a leakage or injection affects national income\",\"Paper 2: identify from a PPC whether a change shows actual growth or growth in production possibilities\",\"Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g) in later units: opportunity cost as an evaluation tool, such as the cost of a subsidy\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"draw\",\"distinguish\",\"identify\",\"outline\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies a PPC showing choice and opportunity cost, unemployment of resources, actual growth and growth in production possibilities; a PPC contrasting increasing and constant opportunity cost; and the circular flow with leakages and injections (guide PDF p. 29). Name the goods on the axes and label every flow.\",\"calc\":\"None is set for 1.1. Reading an opportunity cost off a PPC prepares for HL-only work in 4.1: calculating opportunity costs from data to identify comparative advantage, and linear PPC diagrams (guide PDF p. 51).\",\"apply\":\"Name the real goods or sectors in the context, such as health versus defence spending, not good X and good Y.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh whether resources were fully employed, who bears the opportunity cost, and short-run sacrifice against long-run capacity.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating opportunity cost as money spent or as all alternatives\",\"Calling a point inside the PPC unattainable\",\"Shifting the PPC outward when idle resources are re-employed\",\"Omitting leakages and injections from the circular flow\"],\"strong\":[\"Define before using a term\",\"Label every axis, curve and point, and refer to the diagram\",\"Separate actual growth from growth in production possibilities\",\"Carry opportunity cost into later evaluation\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a production possibilities curve, the difference between unemployment of resources and growth in production possibilities.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a circular flow of income diagram, how a fall in export revenue could affect national income.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain how a mixed economy combines the price mechanism with government intervention to answer the basic economic questions.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"why\":\"The PPC and circular flow are the first models whose assumptions 1.2 teaches you to question.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"The price mechanism is the market’s way of answering what, how and for whom.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"why\":\"The circular flow underpins the output, income and expenditure measures of national income.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"why\":\"HL only: linear PPCs with different opportunity costs show comparative advantage.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"why\":\"Because the PPC and circular flow are models, the next step is how economists build and test models, and where values enter.\"},\"ia\":\"No IA article can be based on Unit 1: each must come from Unit 2, 3 or 4 (guide PDF p. 75). Unit 1 tools still work inside a commentary: an article about a government funding one project instead of another (Unit 3) could use a PPC and opportunity cost, framed through choice.\",\"ee\":\"How has the opportunity cost of land in a named town changed as housing, farming and green space compete for it? Planning records and land prices could make this researchable.\",\"tok\":\"The PPC assumes resources and technology are fixed, yet real economies change both constantly. In what sense can a model built on fixed conditions give knowledge about a changing world?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students use a PPC to show scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, unemployment and both kinds of growth, and draw the circular flow with leakages and injections.\",\"starter\":\"Ask: name something scarce in your life that is not money, and what you gave up to use it today.\",\"activity\":\"Groups make paper cups and paper planes for five minutes, moving workers between them and recording output combinations; plot them as a PPC, then add a template (better technology) and replot.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a point inside the curve ever a sensible choice? Consider a recession or a decision to protect a forest.\",\"check\":\"Students sketch mini-PPCs for four headlines, labelling each as a move towards, a move along, or a shift.\",\"extension\":\"Why does the curve bow outward, and when is it straight? HL students link the straight case to 4.1.\",\"homework\":\"Draw the circular flow from memory with all leakages and injections, and explain in three sentences what happens if saving rises.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define scarcity, opportunity cost, free good and the factors of production.\",\"I can explain why scarcity forces choice even in a rich economy.\",\"I can draw a PPC showing opportunity cost, unemployment, actual growth and growth in production possibilities.\",\"I can explain increasing versus constant opportunity cost.\",\"I can calculate an opportunity cost from a PPC.\",\"I can compare free market, planned and mixed economies.\",\"I can draw the circular flow and explain interdependence.\",\"I can explain what a PPC cannot tell us, such as which point a society should choose.\",\"I can explain these ideas without my notes.\"]},\"1.2\":{\"code\":\"1.2\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Economists explain behaviour by building simple models, stating their assumptions and checking predictions against evidence. That is positive economics: claims about what is, or what would happen. Deciding what should happen also needs value judgements, which is normative economics. Economic ideas have themselves changed as events tested them, from Adam Smith’s case for markets to behavioural and ecological economics.\",\"why\":\"A policy argument that mixes evidence with opinion is hard to test and easy to misuse. Separating the two shows which part data could settle and which part is a choice about values.\",\"q\":\"When economists disagree about raising the minimum wage, are they disagreeing about facts, about values, or both?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"The PPC and circular flow as examples of models\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Reading a scatter graph and telling correlation from causation\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define positive economics, normative economics and ceteris paribus\",\"Distinguish equity from equality\",\"Outline the shifts in economic thought from the 18th to the 21st century named in the guide\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why models simplify reality on purpose\",\"Explain how refutation makes a claim testable\",\"Explain why value judgements cannot be settled by evidence alone\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a statement as positive or normative and justify the classification\",\"Identify the ceteris paribus condition in a given model\"],\"analyse\":[\"Separate the positive and normative parts of a policy argument\",\"Analyse why economists with the same evidence may recommend different policies\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the usefulness of a model whose assumptions are unrealistic\",\"Evaluate how far an event such as stagflation changed which ideas were dominant\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a data series as evidence for or against a hypothesis, noting what else may have changed\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Models are useful because they leave things out\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"An economic model is a simplified representation of part of the economy, in words, diagrams or equations. It ignores detail on purpose so that one relationship can be seen clearly. A map showing every tree would be useless for finding a route; a model including every influence on behaviour would predict nothing. Models are built from logic: if people respond to incentives and a price rises, less should be bought. A model is judged by whether its predictions are useful and survive testing, not by whether every assumption is literally true.\"},{\"h\":\"Ceteris paribus: holding the rest still\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Ceteris paribus means “other things being equal”. It lets a model isolate the effect of one variable by assuming every other relevant influence is unchanged. The law of demand is a ceteris paribus claim about price and quantity demanded. In the real world many things change at once, so data rarely show one relationship cleanly. That is not a flaw in the assumption; it is a warning about reading evidence. If price and quantity sold both rise, something else, such as income, probably changed too.\",\"because\":[\"Many variables influence behaviour at once\",\"A model varies one and holds the others constant\",\"So it predicts a single cause-and-effect link\",\"But real data mix several changes\",\"So testing needs methods that separate one effect from the rest\"]},{\"h\":\"The positive method: hypothesis, evidence, refutation\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Positive economics makes statements about what is, was or would be, which can in principle be checked against evidence. The method is a loop. Form a hypothesis from logic, build a model, derive a prediction, and compare it with empirical evidence. If the evidence contradicts the prediction, the hypothesis is refuted and must be dropped or revised. If the evidence fits, the hypothesis survives for now; a later test could still contradict it. Because whole economies rarely allow controlled experiments, economists rely on statistics, natural experiments, and field and laboratory experiments.\"},{\"h\":\"Positive and normative statements\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A normative statement contains a value judgement about what ought to be. Evidence alone cannot settle it, because two people who agree on every fact can still weigh outcomes differently. Normative does not mean wrong or unscientific: policy needs value judgements, because choosing between outcomes means deciding what matters. A useful test: could data, in principle, show the statement to be false? If so, it is positive, even if it turns out false. Words such as should, ought, fair, too high and better usually signal a normative claim.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Statement\",\"Type\",\"Why\"],\"rows\":[[\"Unemployment fell last year\",\"Positive\",\"Official data can confirm or contradict it\"],[\"Unemployment matters more than inflation\",\"Normative\",\"It ranks outcomes by importance\"],[\"A tax on sugary drinks reduces their consumption\",\"Positive\",\"Testable against purchase data, whatever the result\"],[\"The government should tax sugary drinks\",\"Normative\",\"It depends on how health, freedom and fairness are weighed\"],[\"Income is less equally distributed than twenty years ago\",\"Positive\",\"Measurable, for example with a Gini coefficient\"],[\"Income is distributed unfairly\",\"Normative\",\"Fairness is a value judgement\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Equity is not equality\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Equality describes outcomes that are the same or similar across people or groups, such as equal incomes. Equity means fairness, and fairness is a normative idea: a fair distribution need not be an equal one. Some argue it is fair for people who work longer hours to earn more; others that fairness means everyone can meet basic needs, or starts life with equal opportunities. Measuring inequality is positive economics; deciding how much is acceptable is normative. The guide defines the two terms separately (guide PDF pp. 19, 29), while noting that in practice inequity is often discussed in terms of inequality.\"},{\"h\":\"How economic thought has changed\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Economic ideas developed in response to the problems of their time. The guide lists the main shifts to study and sets them at AO2 depth (guide PDF pp. 29–30). One thread runs through them: how far markets coordinate themselves, and when government should act. Recent decades added two questions: how people really decide, and how the economy depends on the natural world. Treat the labels below as simplifications; economists within each tradition disagreed with one another.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Period\",\"Idea named in the guide\",\"Core claim, simplified\"],\"rows\":[[\"18th century\",\"Adam Smith and laissez faire\",\"Self-interested exchange in competitive markets can serve the common good; the state has limited but real roles, such as defence and justice\"],[\"19th century\",\"Classical microeconomics (utility); the concept of the margin\",\"Choices are made at the margin, weighing the extra benefit and extra cost of one more unit\"],[\"19th century\",\"Classical macroeconomics (Say’s law)\",\"Production creates the income to buy what is produced, so general gluts should not persist\"],[\"19th century\",\"Marxist critique\",\"Capitalism rests on the exploitation of labour and is prone to crises\"],[\"20th century\",\"Keynesian revolution\",\"Total spending can stay too low for long periods; government can raise it\"],[\"20th century\",\"Monetarist / new classical counter-revolution\",\"Inflation is mainly a monetary matter; active demand management can do more harm than good\"],[\"21st century\",\"Behavioural economics; circular economy\",\"People decide with limited information and self-control; the economy must work within environmental limits\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Testing a hypothesis: does a tax on sugary drinks reduce consumption?\",\"cause\":\"A government taxes sugary drinks; economists hypothesise that, ceteris paribus, higher prices reduce the quantity bought.\",\"mechanism\":\"Logic gives a prediction; researchers compare purchases before and after the tax, ideally against a similar area without it.\",\"immediate\":\"If purchases fall relative to the comparison, the evidence is consistent with the hypothesis; if not, it is refuted for that setting.\",\"secondary\":\"Other changes, such as a health campaign or firms cutting sugar in recipes, can mislead, so careful studies try to control for them.\",\"longrun\":\"Repeated tests across places build a body of evidence; the hypothesis gains credibility but stays open to refutation.\",\"depends\":\"Data quality, whether a credible comparison exists, how many other variables changed, and whether a result from one country transfers to another.\"},{\"t\":\"How stagflation in the 1970s shifted economic thinking\",\"cause\":\"Many high-income economies experienced stagflation: high inflation and high unemployment at the same time.\",\"mechanism\":\"The Keynesian approach of the time was often read as offering a trade-off between inflation and unemployment; both rising together was hard to explain within that reading.\",\"immediate\":\"Monetarist and new classical economists, including Milton Friedman and later Robert Lucas, gained influence by stressing money growth and expectations.\",\"secondary\":\"Policy in several countries shifted towards controlling inflation and towards supply-side reform.\",\"longrun\":\"Later mainstream macroeconomics combined elements of both traditions; the 2008 global financial crisis reopened debate about demand, finance and government.\",\"depends\":\"How the evidence is read (oil price shocks also mattered) and which assumptions about expectations and price flexibility one accepts.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The positive method: hypothesis, test, refutation\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains how economists turn a logical claim into a testable one and decide whether to keep it.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Economic relationships are regular enough to predict\",\"Relevant variables can be measured\",\"Other influences can be held constant or controlled statistically\"],\"mechanism\":\"Logic generates a hypothesis; a model derives a prediction under ceteris paribus; evidence is gathered; the hypothesis is refuted, revised or provisionally kept.\",\"prediction\":\"Theories that repeatedly survive tests become more trusted; those that fail are revised or dropped.\",\"application\":\"Judging claims in data-response questions and spotting correlation presented as causation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Controlled experiments on whole economies are rarely possible\",\"Behaviour and institutions change, so past results may not hold\",\"What researchers choose to test can reflect their values\"],\"real\":\"Minimum wage rises that differ between neighbouring regions have been studied as natural experiments; findings vary across studies and settings, which is one reason the debate continues.\",\"misconception\":\"Thinking evidence can establish a theory for all time. Evidence can refute a claim; agreement only means it has survived so far.\",\"exam\":\"In Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g), questioning whether data show causation, and naming what ceteris paribus hides, is evaluation.\",\"deeper\":\"Refutation is messier in practice. A failed prediction can be blamed on an auxiliary assumption, such as measurement error, rather than on the core theory, so theories are rarely dropped after one test. Milton Friedman’s well-known methodological essay argued that assumptions need not be realistic if predictions are accurate; critics reply that unrealistic assumptions can make a model fail exactly when conditions change.\"},{\"model\":\"Two views of the macroeconomy: self-correcting markets and demand deficiency\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Frames the long-running debate about whether economies return to full employment on their own.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Classical view: flexible prices and wages clear markets\",\"Keynesian view: wages and prices can be slow to fall, and spending depends on confidence\",\"Both: the short run and the long run can behave differently\"],\"mechanism\":\"Classical: falling spending lowers wages and prices until output recovers. Keynesian: falling incomes cut spending further, so an economy can stay below full employment unless government or the central bank adds demand.\",\"prediction\":\"Classical: little need for active policy. Keynesian: stimulus can raise output and jobs in a slump.\",\"application\":\"Evaluating AD/AS (3.2) and fiscal, monetary and supply-side policy (3.5–3.7).\",\"limitations\":[\"Both labels simplify traditions with internal disputes\",\"Which fits better may depend on the time horizon and circumstances\",\"Neither, in its simple form, explained financial crises well\"],\"real\":\"The Great Depression gave Keynes’s 1936 General Theory its audience; 1970s stagflation helped monetarist ideas spread.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating the schools as teams where one is simply right, instead of asking which assumptions hold in the case at hand.\",\"exam\":\"In Unit 3 answers, naming the school behind an argument and the assumption it rests on turns assertion into evaluation.\",\"deeper\":\"Much modern macroeconomics works within a synthesis: many models accept that prices adjust slowly in the short run, a Keynesian feature, and that expectations and long-run supply matter, features stressed by monetarists and new classical economists. Disagreement is often about magnitudes, such as the size of the fiscal multiplier. Heterodox traditions, including post-Keynesian, Austrian, Marxist, feminist and ecological economics, challenge the mainstream from different directions.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The positive method says hypotheses should be tested against evidence and dropped when refuted, and that positive and normative claims can be kept apart.\",\"evidence\":\"In practice, economic evidence is often contested: the same data can support different readings, and results from one country or decade may not transfer. Choices such as which outcome to measure can reflect values before any data are collected.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists see this as a reason for better methods, such as natural experiments. Others argue that a clean positive–normative split is an ideal, and that stating values openly is better than presenting them as technical findings.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why “19th-century microeconomics” is a loose label\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"The guide groups utility and the margin under 19th-century microeconomics. Historians usually call this work neoclassical rather than classical. In the 1870s, Jevons, Menger and Walras independently developed marginal utility, shifting attention from the cost of production to choice at the margin. Alfred Marshall’s Principles of Economics (1890) combined that with costs of production in the demand and supply analysis that textbooks still use. So the diagrams of Unit 2 come from this tradition, while classical economists such as Smith and Ricardo focused more on production, distribution and growth.\"},{\"h\":\"From a linear to a circular economy\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"Standard models treat the environment as outside the economy: a source of free inputs and a sink for waste. A linear economy takes materials, makes products and throws them away. Circular-economy thinking aims to keep materials in use through durable design, repair, reuse and recycling. Ecological economists go further, arguing that the economy is a subsystem of the environment, and frameworks such as Kate Raworth’s “doughnut” set social needs against planetary limits. Critics question whether such goals can be reached without slowing growth.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Economists try to work out how the world works before arguing about how it should work. They build simple models, like a map that leaves out the trees, and hold everything else still so one cause can be seen. They then check predictions against real data and drop or change ideas that fail. Questions about what should happen, such as whether a tax is fair, need value judgements as well as evidence. Economic ideas have changed over time as new problems exposed the limits of old ones.\",\"exam\":\"Methodology can be examined directly, and it also powers evaluation in every unit. Define precisely: positive statements can be tested against evidence; normative statements contain value judgements. When a model predicts something, name the ceteris paribus condition it relies on, then ask what happens if that condition breaks in the context, such as incomes falling while a tax rises. When data are given, ask whether they show causation or only correlation. When a policy is recommended, identify the value judgement: whose well-being counts, and whether efficiency or equity comes first. Naming the school of thought behind an assumption, such as flexible wages, explains why economists disagree.\",\"deep\":\"The positive–normative split is useful but not watertight. Choosing which outcome to measure, which model to use or which risk to test for already involves judgements about what matters. Even efficiency carries a value judgement: it ranks outcomes by total surplus, not by who receives it. Economists also face a problem physicists do not: people react to predictions, so a forecast of inflation can change the inflation it forecast. For these reasons many economists argue for transparency rather than claims of neutrality: state the assumptions, show the evidence, and separate the value judgement so readers can reject it without rejecting the analysis. History supports that caution: ideas that looked settled, such as a stable trade-off between inflation and unemployment, were revised when conditions changed.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Economic ideas change as the world changes: the Depression, stagflation and climate change each tested dominant theories.\",\"tension\":\"A theory that worked in one era can mislead in the next, but dropping it too fast loses what it got right.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 3 article on a central bank changing strategy can use change to ask which assumptions about the economy have shifted.\"},{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"Economists choose what to include in a model and what to hold constant, and those choices shape conclusions.\",\"tension\":\"A simpler model is clearer but can omit the variable that matters most in a particular case.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on a nudge policy can use choice to contrast rational and behavioural views of decisions.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Equity is where normative economics enters most directly: fairness is a value judgement, while equality can be measured.\",\"tension\":\"Policies that make outcomes more equal may weaken incentives, and people weigh the two differently.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 3 article on a tax change can use equity to separate the measurable effect on inequality from the judgement about fairness.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do well-informed economists give opposite advice on the same policy?\",\"expect\":\"Students often expect experts with the same data to agree, and read disagreement as bias or error.\",\"model\":\"The positive–normative distinction predicts two sources of disagreement: different readings of uncertain evidence, and different weights on outcomes such as efficiency and equity.\",\"evidence\":\"Surveys of professional economists suggest wide agreement on some questions, such as rent controls tending to reduce the quantity and quality of rental housing, and much less where evidence is mixed or values differ.\",\"match\":\"Largely yes: disagreement concentrates where evidence is weakest or where a policy creates clear winners and losers.\",\"assume\":\"That positive and normative claims can be separated, and that evidence is good enough to settle the positive part.\",\"change\":\"Better data or a credible natural experiment can narrow positive disagreement; neither can settle a disagreement about values.\",\"policy\":\"Policymakers should ask advisers to separate what the evidence shows from what they recommend, so the value judgement is open to democratic debate.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why economic models use the ceteris paribus assumption, and what it hides.\",\"check\":[\"Defines ceteris paribus as holding other relevant variables constant\",\"Explains that it isolates the effect of one variable\",\"Gives an example, such as the law of demand holding income constant\",\"Notes that in real data many variables change at once\",\"Concludes that it is a tool for clarity, not a claim about reality\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain the difference between positive and normative statements, using one example of each about the same policy.\",\"check\":[\"Positive: about what is or would be, testable in principle\",\"Normative: contains a value judgement about what ought to be\",\"Gives a matched pair on one policy\",\"Notes that a positive statement can be false and still positive\",\"Notes that normative does not mean wrong or unscientific\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"What does ceteris paribus mean?\",\"a\":\"Other things being equal: every relevant variable except the one studied is held constant.\"},{\"q\":\"Positive or normative: “The minimum wage should rise to reduce poverty”?\",\"a\":\"Normative: “should” signals a value judgement.\"},{\"q\":\"What is refutation?\",\"a\":\"Showing that evidence contradicts a hypothesis’s prediction, so the hypothesis must be dropped or revised.\"},{\"q\":\"Distinguish equity from equality.\",\"a\":\"Equality means similar outcomes; equity means fairness, a normative judgement that need not mean equal outcomes.\"},{\"q\":\"What does Say’s law claim, and who challenged it most directly in the 20th century?\",\"a\":\"That production creates the income to buy it, so general gluts should not persist; Keynes argued total spending could stay too low.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a good’s price rose 3% and sales rose 5% in the same year. Does this refute the law of demand?\",\"a\":\"No. The law holds ceteris paribus; income or tastes probably shifted demand, so the data do not isolate the price effect.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Which statement is positive?\",\"opts\":[\"Governments should spend more on public transport\",\"Cutting bus fares will increase the number of bus trips, ceteris paribus\",\"Income inequality in this country is too high\",\"It is unfair that rural areas have fewer buses\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A positive statement can be tested against evidence whatever the result, and the bus-fare claim can be. “Inequality is too high” is tempting because inequality can be measured, but “too high” is a value judgement.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Over a year, both the price and the quantity sold of a smartphone rose. A commentator says this refutes the law of demand. What is the best response?\",\"opts\":[\"The law is refuted, because the data contradict it\",\"The data are unreliable and should be ignored\",\"Other factors, such as incomes or tastes, probably shifted demand, so the data do not isolate the effect of price\",\"The smartphone must be an inferior good\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The law of demand holds other things equal. If demand shifted right, price and quantity can rise together without contradicting it. Ignoring data is not the answer; the point is what the data can and cannot isolate.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-01\"},{\"q\":\"A tax change makes after-tax incomes more equal. A commentator says “this makes the tax system more equitable.” Which is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"The claim is positive, because equality can be measured\",\"The claim is normative, because equity is about fairness\",\"Equity and equality mean the same, so the claim is simply true\",\"The claim is neither positive nor normative\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Whether incomes became more equal is positive and measurable; whether that is more equitable depends on a view of fairness. The first option is tempting because it sounds measurable, but it confuses equality with equity.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain the role of assumptions such as ceteris paribus in economic models\",\"Paper 1 (a): distinguish positive from normative economics, with examples\",\"Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g): methodology as evaluation, questioning causation in data, naming the assumption and school behind an argument, and separating values from analysis\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"outline\",\"suggest\",\"comment\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies no diagram for 1.2 (guide PDF pp. 29–30). A demand curve can illustrate what ceteris paribus holds constant.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Name the variable held constant in the question’s model, or quote the value judgement in the stimulus text.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh how realistic the assumptions are here, whether data show causation, whose values drive the recommendation, and whether another school’s assumptions would change the conclusion.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating normative as meaning wrong or unscientific\",\"Calling a statement positive when it contains a value word such as “too high” or “fair”\",\"Treating ceteris paribus as a description of reality\",\"Listing names from economic history without their ideas and assumptions\"],\"strong\":[\"Separate the positive and normative parts of an argument explicitly\",\"Name the assumption a conclusion depends on and test it against the context\",\"Distinguish correlation from causation\",\"Use schools of thought to explain disagreement, not to decorate\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain why economists use the ceteris paribus assumption when building models, and one difficulty it creates when testing them.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Distinguish between positive and normative economics, using examples from one policy of your choice.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain why a model built on unrealistic assumptions can still be useful to economists, and one risk of relying on it.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"why\":\"The PPC and circular flow are the first models whose assumptions 1.2 teaches you to question.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"why\":\"The law of demand is a ceteris paribus claim; every shift of the curve relaxes that assumption.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"why\":\"Behavioural economics, the 21st-century strand here, is developed as a critique of rational choice.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"The Keynesian versus monetarist/new classical debate reappears as competing views of aggregate supply.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"The equity–equality distinction frames every question about inequality and redistribution.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"why\":\"Because demand theory is the first full model built under ceteris paribus, it puts the method of 1.2 to work.\"},\"ia\":\"No IA article can be based on Unit 1 (guide PDF p. 75), but 1.2 shapes every commentary. An article from Unit 2, 3 or 4 that quotes a politician’s claim about a policy lets a student separate the positive claim from the value judgement, framed through equity or intervention.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did a named central bank’s change of strategy reflect a change in economic ideas rather than in economic conditions? Its published statements and inflation data could make this feasible.\",\"tok\":\"If two economists agree on all the evidence about a minimum wage but disagree on whether to raise it, what kind of knowledge, if any, could settle their disagreement?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students classify statements as positive or normative, explain ceteris paribus, and outline how the main schools differ in their assumptions.\",\"starter\":\"Show six headlines; students sort them into “could be checked with data” and “depends on values”.\",\"activity\":\"Pairs write two positive and two normative statements about one policy, such as a sugar tax, then swap and challenge each other’s classifications.\",\"discussion\":\"Is efficiency a value-free goal? Who benefits when a policy maximises total surplus?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: classify three statements, naming the word that signals any value judgement.\",\"extension\":\"Make a card for each school the guide names: the problem of its time, its core assumption, one policy it favoured.\",\"homework\":\"Annotate a news quotation about a policy: which parts are positive, which normative, and what is held constant?\"},\"after\":[\"I can define positive economics, normative economics and ceteris paribus.\",\"I can classify a statement as positive or normative and justify it.\",\"I can explain why models simplify and what ceteris paribus hides.\",\"I can explain how refutation works and why evidence can refute a theory but not settle it for good.\",\"I can distinguish equity from equality.\",\"I can outline how economic thought changed from Adam Smith to behavioural and circular-economy ideas.\",\"I can analyse why economists with the same evidence may disagree.\",\"I can use methodology to evaluate arguments in any unit.\",\"I can explain these ideas without my notes.\"]},\"2.1\":{\"code\":\"2.1\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Demand is the whole relationship between a good’s price and the quantity consumers are willing and able to buy, with everything else held constant. A change in the good’s own price moves buyers along that relationship. A change in anything else, such as income or the price of a substitute, moves the whole curve. Most errors in this topic come from confusing the two.\",\"why\":\"Firms use demand to set prices and plan output; governments use it to predict the effects of taxes and subsidies. Getting the cause and direction of a change right is the base of every market diagram that follows.\",\"q\":\"Why might people buy fewer large cars when petrol becomes more expensive, even if car prices do not change?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Scarcity and opportunity cost: why buyers must choose\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Ceteris paribus: holding other variables constant\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"Plotting a schedule of values as a graph\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define demand, quantity demanded and the law of demand\",\"List the five non-price determinants of demand in the guide\",\"Define substitutes and complements\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a change in a good’s own price moves along the demand curve rather than shifting it\",\"Explain how market demand is built from individual demand\",\"HL only: explain the income and substitution effects and diminishing marginal utility as reasons for the downward slope\"],\"apply\":[\"Attribute a news event to the correct determinant and direction of shift\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a price change for a substitute or complement affects demand for a related good\",\"Analyse why price and quantity can rise together without contradicting the law of demand\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate how realistic ceteris paribus is when several determinants change at once\"],\"calculate\":[\"Add individual quantities to find market demand at each price (illustrative data)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a downward-sloping demand curve with axes naming the good\",\"Draw a movement along, and a shift of, the demand curve with arrows and labels\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read a schedule or diagram to say whether demand or quantity demanded changed\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Demand is a relationship, not a number\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Demand is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are willing and able to buy at each possible price over a period of time, ceteris paribus. Three parts of that definition matter. Willing and able: wanting a car without the money to buy one is not demand. At each possible price: demand is the whole schedule or curve. Over a period: 500 coffees a day is a different demand from 500 a year. Quantity demanded is a single point on the curve: the amount bought at one particular price.\"},{\"h\":\"The law of demand\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The law of demand states that, ceteris paribus, as the price of a good rises, the quantity demanded falls, and as the price falls, the quantity demanded rises. Because price and quantity demanded are inversely related, the demand curve slopes down from left to right. The axes must name the good: the price of the good on the vertical axis, the quantity of it per period on the horizontal. At a higher price, some buyers switch to alternatives, some buy fewer units, and some leave the market.\"},{\"h\":\"From individual to market demand\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Each consumer has an individual demand curve. Market demand is the sum of all consumers’ quantities demanded at each price. This is called horizontal summation, because at each price you add quantities along the horizontal axis. The table uses illustrative data for three buyers of a snack. More consumers means a greater quantity demanded at every price, which is why the number of consumers is a determinant of market demand.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Price ($)\",\"Ana\",\"Ben\",\"Chen\",\"Market demand\"],\"rows\":[[\"4\",\"1\",\"0\",\"2\",\"3\"],[\"3\",\"2\",\"1\",\"3\",\"6\"],[\"2\",\"3\",\"2\",\"4\",\"9\"],[\"1\",\"4\",\"4\",\"5\",\"13\"]]}},{\"h\":\"What shifts demand\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A change in any determinant other than the good’s own price changes the quantity demanded at every price, so the whole curve shifts. An increase in demand is a shift to the right; a decrease is a shift to the left. The guide names five non-price determinants (guide PDF p. 31). For income, the direction depends on whether the good is normal or inferior, a distinction developed with income elasticity in 2.5.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Determinant\",\"Example of a change\",\"Effect on demand\"],\"rows\":[[\"Income\",\"Incomes rise\",\"Normal good: increases. Inferior good, such as some budget own-brand foods: decreases\"],[\"Tastes and preferences\",\"A product becomes fashionable, or a health scare hits it\",\"Increases / decreases\"],[\"Future price expectations\",\"Buyers expect the price to rise soon\",\"Increases now, as purchases are brought forward\"],[\"Price of a substitute\",\"The price of tea rises\",\"Demand for coffee increases\"],[\"Price of a complement\",\"The price of games consoles falls\",\"Demand for games increases\"],[\"Number of consumers\",\"Population grows, or new buyers enter the market\",\"Increases\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Movement or shift: the distinction that carries the marks\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Keep two vocabularies apart. A change in the good’s own price causes a change in quantity demanded: a movement along the existing curve, called an extension when price falls and a contraction when price rises. A change in any non-price determinant causes a change in demand: the whole curve shifts. So “the price rose and demand fell” is wrong; the correct sentence is “the price rose and quantity demanded fell”. The price of a related good counts as a non-price determinant: a rise in the price of tea shifts the demand curve for coffee.\",\"because\":[\"The demand curve is drawn holding every non-price determinant constant\",\"So it already shows how quantity responds to the good’s own price\",\"So an own-price change is a move to another point on the same curve\",\"Any other change breaks the ceteris paribus condition\",\"So a new curve is needed: demand has shifted\"],\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Aspect\",\"Change in quantity demanded\",\"Change in demand\"],\"rows\":[[\"Caused by\",\"The good’s own price only\",\"A non-price determinant\"],[\"On the diagram\",\"Movement along D, e.g. from A to B\",\"Shift from D₁ to D₂\"],[\"Vocabulary\",\"Extension or contraction\",\"Increase (right) or decrease (left)\"],[\"Example\",\"Cinema tickets get cheaper, so more are bought\",\"Incomes rise, so more tickets are bought at every price\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why the curve slopes down (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 31). A price fall has two effects. The substitution effect: the good is now cheaper relative to alternatives, so consumers switch towards it. The income effect: the same money income buys more, so real income rises and, for a normal good, consumers buy more. Underneath sits the law of diminishing marginal utility: beyond some point, each extra unit consumed in a period adds less satisfaction than the one before, so consumers buy more units only at lower prices. For an inferior good the income effect works the other way, but the substitution effect usually dominates.\",\"because\":[\"The price of a normal good falls\",\"It becomes cheaper relative to substitutes: the substitution effect\",\"Consumers’ real income rises: the income effect\",\"Both effects lead consumers to buy more\",\"So quantity demanded rises and the curve slopes downward\"]}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A price cut for games consoles and the market for games\",\"cause\":\"A manufacturer cuts the price of its console.\",\"mechanism\":\"For consoles this is an own-price change, so quantity demanded extends along the console demand curve. For games, a complement, it is a non-price determinant.\",\"immediate\":\"Console sales rise (a movement); the demand curve for games shifts right.\",\"secondary\":\"Game publishers can sell more at the same price; demand for rival consoles, which are substitutes, may shift left.\",\"longrun\":\"Firms may price consoles low on purpose and earn more from games, a strategy that relies on the complementary link.\",\"depends\":\"How closely the goods are used together, how close the rival consoles are as substitutes, and whether buyers expect further price cuts.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The demand curve and the law of demand\",\"dg\":\"dshift\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how the quantity consumers are willing and able to buy responds to price, and separates price effects from other influences.\",\"assumptions\":[\"All non-price determinants are held constant\",\"Quantities are rates per period, such as per week\",\"HL only: diminishing marginal utility, with the income and substitution effects, makes quantity demanded rise as price falls\"],\"mechanism\":\"An own-price change moves buyers to another point on the same curve; a change in a non-price determinant changes quantity demanded at every price and shifts the curve.\",\"prediction\":\"Ceteris paribus, a higher price reduces quantity demanded; an increase in demand raises the quantity bought at each price.\",\"application\":\"Explaining sales after a price change, predicting the effect of income, taste or related-price changes, and building equilibrium in 2.3.\",\"limitations\":[\"Real data rarely hold other things constant, so observed points mix movements and shifts\",\"Some buyers read a high price as a sign of quality or status\",\"It says nothing about the size of the response; that needs elasticity (2.5)\"],\"real\":\"When fuel prices rise, the quantity of fuel bought tends to fall only a little in the short run, a movement along the fuel demand curve, while demand for large, fuel-hungry cars can shift left because fuel and cars are complements.\",\"misconception\":\"Saying “demand fell” after a price rise and shifting the curve left. A price change moves along the curve; only a non-price determinant shifts it.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) and Paper 2: axes “Price of X” and “Quantity of X”, curves D₁ and D₂, points P₁, Q₁ and P₂, Q₂, an arrow showing the shift, and the determinant named in the text.\",\"deeper\":\"A demand curve is a thought experiment, not a line traced by data. Observed prices and quantities are points where demand meets supply, so a scatter of them usually mixes shifts of both curves. Separating them is called the identification problem; economists tackle it with natural experiments, such as a harvest failure that moves price while demand stays put. If price itself changes how a good is perceived, the neat line between movements and shifts weakens.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The law of demand predicts that, other things equal, a higher price reduces quantity demanded, and the model separates this price effect from shifts caused by other determinants.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of many markets, from fuel and tobacco to food, typically find that higher prices go with lower quantities once other factors are accounted for. Raw data, though, can show price and quantity rising together while demand is shifting.\",\"interp\":\"Most economists read this as strong support for the law, with the size of the response varying widely. Behavioural economists add that how a price is framed, anchored or defaulted can change behaviour even when the price itself does not, which the simple model leaves out.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Demand is a flow, and time matters\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Demand is measured per period, and the period changes the answer. When a price rises, buyers may respond little at first, because habits and equipment are fixed, and more later, as they find substitutes. Durable goods add a twist: if people expect prices to fall, they may delay buying, so today’s demand drops even though nothing about the good has changed. The single demand curve compresses all of this into one line, which is why elasticity in 2.5 separates the short run from the long run.\"},{\"h\":\"Exceptions at the edge of the law\",\"tag\":\"Behaviour\",\"p\":\"Giffen goods and status goods are not in the guide, but they mark the law’s boundary. A Giffen good is a staple that takes most of a very poor household’s budget: when its price rises, the household becomes so much poorer that it drops dearer foods and buys more of the staple. Evidence is rare, though one well-known field study of very poor households in parts of China found behaviour consistent with it. Some status goods sell partly because they are expensive. Both exceptions are narrow, and many apparent exceptions turn out to be shifts of demand.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Demand is not how much people want something. It is how much they are willing and able to buy at each possible price. Picture a price list with a quantity next to every price: the whole list is demand. If the good’s own price changes, buyers move to a different row of the same list. If something else changes, such as their income or a rival product’s price, they write a new list. Moving along the curve versus shifting it is the heart of the topic.\",\"exam\":\"Define demand with all three parts: willing and able, at each price, over a period. Draw the curve with axes naming the good and label it D. If the question changes the good’s own price, show a movement along D and call it a change in quantity demanded. If it changes income, tastes, expectations, related prices or the number of consumers, shift the curve, label D₁ and D₂, add an arrow and name the determinant. Explain the chain in words: what changed, why buyers respond, and what happens to quantity at each price. Evaluate by asking whether other determinants changed at the same time and whether the good is normal or inferior for these buyers.\",\"deep\":\"The demand curve compresses a theory of choice into one line. Each consumer is spending a limited budget across many goods, so demand for one good depends on its price, all other prices, income and preferences. A two-dimensional curve freezes all but one of those variables. That is why the movement–shift distinction is not a vocabulary rule but a statement about which variable is doing the work. At HL, the income and substitution effects split the response to a price change into two channels, and diminishing marginal utility explains why extra units are worth less. The limits are empirical and behavioural: observed data mix shifts and movements, preferences can change, and some purchases are driven by status, habit or what others buy.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"A demand curve summarises consumers’ choices at each price, given limited budgets and many alternatives.\",\"tension\":\"Consumers may not choose as the model assumes; habits and framing can matter as much as price.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on a price rise for a popular service can use choice to explain switching to substitutes.\"},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Changes in income, tastes, expectations, related prices or population shift demand; that is how markets register new conditions.\",\"tension\":\"Several determinants often change together, making it hard to pin a change in sales on one cause.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article about a craze or a health scare can use change to analyse a shift in demand.\"},{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"here\":\"Demand counts only wants backed by the ability to pay, so scarce income limits it.\",\"tension\":\"The needs of people without income do not show up as demand, which raises questions of equity.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on rising food prices can use scarcity to explain why low-income households cut back most.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do sales of some goods rise even when their prices go up?\",\"expect\":\"Many students expect this to break the law of demand.\",\"model\":\"Ceteris paribus, a higher price lowers quantity demanded, so rising price and sales together suggest demand has shifted right.\",\"evidence\":\"Such episodes usually coincide with rising incomes, changing tastes, expected further price rises or more buyers; cases where the higher price itself raises quantity demanded appear rare.\",\"match\":\"Mostly yes, once shifts are separated from movements; apparent contradictions usually mean ceteris paribus was broken.\",\"assume\":\"Stable preferences, and that the price itself does not change how the good is perceived.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that a price rise alone raised perceived quality or status, with nothing else changing, would point to a genuine exception.\",\"policy\":\"A government predicting the effect of a tax must separate the price effect from simultaneous changes in tastes or income, or it will misjudge the policy.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a diagram, the difference between a change in quantity demanded and a change in demand.\",\"check\":[\"Axes labelled with the price and quantity of a named good\",\"An own-price change shown as a movement along one curve\",\"Vocabulary: extension or contraction of quantity demanded\",\"A non-price determinant shown as a shift from D₁ to D₂ with an arrow\",\"A specific determinant named with its direction\",\"States that after a shift, a different quantity is demanded at each price\"]},{\"prompt\":\"HL only: explain why the demand curve for a normal good slopes downward, using the income and substitution effects.\",\"check\":[\"A price fall makes the good relatively cheaper: the substitution effect\",\"A price fall raises real income: the income effect\",\"For a normal good both effects increase quantity demanded\",\"Diminishing marginal utility means more units are bought only at lower prices\",\"Concludes that price and quantity demanded are inversely related\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define demand.\",\"a\":\"The quantity of a good consumers are willing and able to buy at each possible price over a period, ceteris paribus.\"},{\"q\":\"The price of a good rises. Has demand changed?\",\"a\":\"No. Quantity demanded falls: a contraction along the existing curve.\"},{\"q\":\"Coffee and tea are substitutes. The price of tea falls. What happens to demand for coffee?\",\"a\":\"It decreases (shifts left): at each coffee price, some buyers switch to cheaper tea.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: at $2, three consumers demand 3, 2 and 4 units. What is market quantity demanded at $2?\",\"a\":\"9 units, the horizontal sum of individual quantities.\"},{\"q\":\"Give two causes of a rightward shift in demand for a normal good.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: higher income, a rise in a substitute’s price, a fall in a complement’s price, favourable tastes, an expected price rise, more consumers.\"},{\"q\":\"Can price and quantity sold both rise without breaking the law of demand?\",\"a\":\"Yes, if demand shifted right, for example because incomes rose; the observed points then lie on different demand curves.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"The price of cinema tickets rises. What has changed in the market for cinema tickets?\",\"opts\":[\"Demand has decreased\",\"Quantity demanded has decreased\",\"Demand and quantity demanded have both decreased\",\"Demand has increased because tickets are worth more\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"An own-price change moves buyers along the existing curve, so quantity demanded falls (a contraction). “Demand has decreased” is tempting because fewer tickets are sold, but demand is the whole curve, and it has not moved.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-01\"},{\"q\":\"Petrol and large cars are complements. The price of petrol rises sharply. What happens in the market for large cars?\",\"opts\":[\"A movement up along the demand curve for large cars\",\"A rightward shift of demand for large cars\",\"A leftward shift of demand for large cars\",\"No change, because car prices have not changed\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A complement’s price is a non-price determinant for large cars, so demand falls at every price: a leftward shift. A movement along the curve would need a change in the price of large cars themselves.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-01\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. The price of a normal good falls. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"Only the substitution effect raises quantity demanded\",\"The substitution and income effects both raise quantity demanded\",\"The income effect lowers quantity demanded because real income falls\",\"Diminishing marginal utility shifts the demand curve right\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A price fall makes the good relatively cheaper and raises real income; for a normal good both raise quantity demanded. Diminishing marginal utility explains the slope, not a shift, so the last option confuses the two.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2: define demand or the law of demand in the context of the text\",\"Paper 2: explain, using a demand diagram, how an event in the text affects demand for a good\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using a diagram, the difference between a change in quantity demanded and a change in demand\",\"HL only, Paper 1 (a): explain the law of demand through the income and substitution effects or diminishing marginal utility\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"identify\",\"analyse\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies a downward-sloping demand curve and a diagram of movements along and shifts of it (guide PDF pp. 31–32). Axes: “Price of [good]” and “Quantity of [good]”. Show a movement between marked points on D, or a shift D₁ → D₂ with an arrow.\",\"calc\":\"None is set for 2.1. Adding individual quantities into market demand is useful practice; demand calculations with elasticity come in 2.5.\",\"apply\":\"Name the good, the market and the specific determinant in the text, such as a named substitute or a reported change in income.\",\"eval\":\"Consider whether other determinants changed at the same time, whether the good is normal or inferior for these buyers, and how long buyers take to adjust.\",\"errors\":[\"Writing “price rose so demand fell” and shifting the curve\",\"Labelling axes “P” and “Q” without naming the good\",\"Shifting the wrong good’s curve when a related good’s price changes\",\"HL: using diminishing marginal utility to explain a shift rather than the slope\"],\"strong\":[\"Use “demand” and “quantity demanded” precisely throughout\",\"Name the determinant and explain why buyers respond before drawing the shift\",\"Refer to points and curves on the diagram in the text\",\"At HL, explain the slope through both effects rather than asserting it\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using diagrams, why a fall in the price of streaming subscriptions and a rise in consumers’ incomes affect the market for streaming subscriptions in different ways.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"HL only. Explain how the income and substitution effects account for the downward slope of the demand curve for a normal good.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why the price and the quantity sold of a good can both rise without contradicting the law of demand.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"why\":\"The law of demand is a ceteris paribus claim; every shift relaxes that assumption.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"why\":\"Supply uses the same movement–shift logic from the producer’s side.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"Demand meets supply in equilibrium, and demand shifts drive changes in price and quantity.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"Elasticity measures how far quantity demanded responds to price and income, and YED formalises normal and inferior goods.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"Aggregate demand borrows the language of movements and shifts, though its slope has macroeconomic reasons.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"why\":\"Because a market needs sellers as well as buyers, supply builds the matching curve before the two meet in 2.3.\"},\"ia\":\"An article about changing spending on a named product, such as switching to a substitute after a price rise, a craze, or falling sales as incomes fall, suits a Unit 2 commentary. Choice or change could frame it, and the analysis must say clearly whether demand or quantity demanded changed.\",\"ee\":\"How has demand for a named local good or service responded to a change in a non-price determinant, such as a new competitor or population change? Sellers’ records could make this researchable.\",\"tok\":\"The law of demand holds “other things equal”, yet other things are rarely equal. How can we know that a law seldom observed in its pure form is true?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students define demand, draw the curve correctly and distinguish every movement from every shift.\",\"starter\":\"Each student writes how many of a snack they would buy at five prices; combine the class’s schedules into market demand.\",\"activity\":\"Card sort of ten events (a rise in income, a fall in the good’s price, a rise in a substitute’s price and so on) into movement or shift; students then draw two of each.\",\"discussion\":\"A coffee chain raises its prices and its sales still rise. Has the law of demand failed?\",\"check\":\"Mini-whiteboards: the teacher reads an event; students show “movement” or “shift” and the direction.\",\"extension\":\"HL: split a price fall for a normal good into income and substitution effects, then ask what changes for an inferior good.\",\"homework\":\"Draw three demand diagrams from news stories, labelling the good, the determinant and the direction.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define demand, quantity demanded and the law of demand.\",\"I can draw a demand curve with correctly labelled axes.\",\"I can distinguish a movement along the demand curve from a shift, using the right vocabulary.\",\"I can explain how each non-price determinant shifts demand.\",\"I can add individual quantities to find market demand.\",\"I can analyse the effect of a price change for a substitute or complement.\",\"HL only: I can explain the downward slope using the income and substitution effects and diminishing marginal utility.\",\"I can explain why ceteris paribus seldom holds in a real market and what that means for reading data.\",\"I can explain demand without my notes.\"]},\"2.2\":{\"code\":\"2.2\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Supply is the whole relationship between a good’s price and the quantity producers are willing and able to sell, with everything else held constant. A higher price usually makes producing more worthwhile, so the curve slopes upward. A change in the good’s own price moves along the curve; a change in costs, technology, taxes, related goods, expectations or the number of firms shifts it.\",\"why\":\"Supply explains why prices change when costs, technology or government policy change. It is the producer half of every market diagram, and where taxes and subsidies first enter the analysis.\",\"q\":\"Why does a drought push up the price of coffee even though coffee drinkers have not changed their habits?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Factors of production and their costs\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Opportunity cost of using land or machines for one product rather than another\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Ceteris paribus\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"The difference between demand and quantity demanded\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define supply, quantity supplied and the law of supply\",\"List the six non-price determinants of supply in the guide\",\"Distinguish joint supply from competitive supply\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a change in a good’s own price moves along the supply curve\",\"Explain how market supply is built from individual producers’ supply\",\"HL only: explain how diminishing marginal returns and increasing marginal costs underlie the upward slope\"],\"apply\":[\"Attribute a news event to the correct supply determinant and direction of shift\",\"Show an indirect tax or a subsidy as a shift of supply\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a price change for a jointly or competitively supplied good affects supply of a related good\",\"Analyse why a specific tax shifts supply vertically by the tax per unit\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate how quickly and fully producers can respond to a price change\"],\"calculate\":[\"Add producers’ quantities to find market supply at each price (illustrative data)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw an upward-sloping supply curve with axes naming the good\",\"Draw a movement along, and a shift of, the supply curve\",\"Draw a specific tax as a parallel upward shift and a subsidy as a parallel downward shift\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read a diagram to say whether supply or quantity supplied changed\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Supply and quantity supplied\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Supply is the quantity of a good or service that producers are willing and able to offer for sale at each possible price over a period of time, ceteris paribus. Like demand, it is a whole schedule or curve, not one number. Quantity supplied is a single point on it: the amount offered at one particular price. Both willing and able matter: a firm may want to sell more but lack the workers, machines or land to do so. The price of the good goes on the vertical axis and the quantity per period on the horizontal.\"},{\"h\":\"The law of supply\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The law of supply states that, ceteris paribus, as the price of a good rises, the quantity supplied rises, and as the price falls, the quantity supplied falls. The relationship is direct, so the supply curve slopes upward. At SL, two reasons are enough. A higher price raises the potential profit on each unit, so existing firms have an incentive to produce more. It also makes it worthwhile to move resources away from other products into this one, and can draw in producers whose costs were previously too high.\",\"because\":[\"The price of the good rises\",\"Revenue per unit rises while costs are unchanged\",\"Producing more becomes more profitable\",\"Firms expand output and switch resources towards this good\",\"So quantity supplied rises: a movement up along the curve\"]},{\"h\":\"From individual to market supply\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Market supply is the sum of all producers’ quantities supplied at each price, a horizontal summation. The table uses illustrative data for three farms selling strawberries each week. Notice that Farm B supplies nothing at the lowest price: its costs are too high to make selling worthwhile until the price rises. More firms in the market raise quantity supplied at every price, which is why the number of firms is a determinant of market supply.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Price per kg ($)\",\"Farm A\",\"Farm B\",\"Farm C\",\"Market supply (kg)\"],\"rows\":[[\"2\",\"10\",\"0\",\"5\",\"15\"],[\"3\",\"15\",\"5\",\"10\",\"30\"],[\"4\",\"20\",\"10\",\"15\",\"45\"],[\"5\",\"25\",\"20\",\"20\",\"65\"]]}},{\"h\":\"What shifts supply\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A change in anything other than the good’s own price alters the quantity offered at every price, so the whole curve shifts. An increase in supply is a shift to the right (or downward); a decrease is a shift to the left (or upward). The guide lists six non-price determinants (guide PDF p. 32). Taxes and subsidies appear here as determinants; their effects on prices, revenue and stakeholders are analysed in 2.7.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Determinant\",\"Example of a change\",\"Effect on supply\"],\"rows\":[[\"Costs of factors of production\",\"Wages or energy prices rise\",\"Decreases\"],[\"Related goods: competitive supply\",\"The price of wheat rises; farmers can grow wheat or barley on the same land\",\"Supply of barley decreases as land switches to wheat\"],[\"Related goods: joint supply\",\"The price of beef rises, so more cattle are brought to market\",\"Supply of leather, a by-product, increases\"],[\"Indirect taxes and subsidies\",\"A tax or a subsidy per unit\",\"Tax: decreases. Subsidy: increases\"],[\"Future price expectations\",\"Producers of a storable good expect its price to rise\",\"Current supply may decrease as stock is held back\"],[\"Technology\",\"Automation cuts the cost of each unit\",\"Increases\"],[\"Number of firms\",\"New firms enter the market\",\"Increases\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Movement or shift on the supply side\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The discipline from demand applies here too. Only a change in the good’s own price causes a change in quantity supplied: a movement along the existing curve, an extension when price rises and a contraction when it falls. Every other change causes a change in supply: a shift. A common slip is to treat higher input costs as a movement. Costs are a non-price determinant, so a rise in wages shifts the whole curve left. A rise in the price of a competitively supplied good is also a shift for this good.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Aspect\",\"Change in quantity supplied\",\"Change in supply\"],\"rows\":[[\"Caused by\",\"The good’s own price only\",\"A non-price determinant\"],[\"On the diagram\",\"Movement along S, e.g. from A to B\",\"Shift from S₁ to S₂\"],[\"Vocabulary\",\"Extension or contraction\",\"Increase (right) or decrease (left)\"],[\"Example\",\"Coffee prices rise, so growers sell more\",\"A drought destroys part of the coffee crop\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Taxes and subsidies are vertical shifts\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"A specific (per-unit) indirect tax raises the cost of supplying each unit by the amount of the tax. Producers now need a price higher by exactly that amount to offer any given quantity, so the supply curve shifts vertically upward by the tax per unit, parallel to the original. An ad valorem tax, a percentage of the price, adds more at higher prices, so the new curve diverges from the old one. A subsidy per unit works in reverse: supply shifts vertically downward by the subsidy. Reading the shift vertically lets you measure the tax or subsidy on the diagram in 2.7.\"},{\"h\":\"Why the curve slopes up (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 32). In the short run at least one factor, such as the size of a factory, is fixed. The law of diminishing marginal returns states that as more units of a variable factor, such as labour, are added to a fixed factor, the extra output from each additional unit eventually falls. If each extra worker adds less output, each extra unit of output needs more labour, so its marginal cost rises. A profit-seeking firm supplies an extra unit only if the price covers that marginal cost, so more output needs a higher price.\",\"because\":[\"At least one factor is fixed in the short run\",\"Adding more of a variable factor eventually yields smaller increases in output\",\"So each extra unit of output costs more to produce: increasing marginal cost\",\"Firms supply an extra unit only if price covers its marginal cost\",\"So quantity supplied rises only as price rises\"]}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Higher energy prices for bakeries\",\"cause\":\"The price of the gas and electricity that bakeries use rises.\",\"mechanism\":\"Energy is an input, so the cost of each loaf rises and bakeries offer fewer loaves at every price: supply shifts left (upward) from S₁ to S₂.\",\"immediate\":\"At the original price, quantity supplied falls short of quantity demanded, so a shortage appears.\",\"secondary\":\"Bread prices rise towards a new equilibrium with a higher price and lower quantity, as consumers move along their demand curve (2.3).\",\"longrun\":\"Some bakeries may close, cutting supply further; others may buy energy-saving ovens, a technology change that shifts supply back to the right.\",\"depends\":\"Energy’s share of total costs, how long the price rise lasts, whether bakeries can switch fuel, and how responsive demand is (2.5).\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The supply curve and the law of supply\",\"dg\":\"sshift\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how the quantity producers are willing and able to sell responds to price, separating that response from changes in production conditions.\",\"assumptions\":[\"All non-price determinants are held constant\",\"Firms are price takers aiming for profit\",\"HL only: in the short run, diminishing marginal returns make marginal cost rise as output grows\"],\"mechanism\":\"An own-price change moves producers to another point on the same curve; a change in costs, technology, taxes or subsidies, related goods’ prices, expectations or the number of firms shifts the curve.\",\"prediction\":\"Ceteris paribus, a higher price raises quantity supplied; lower costs or better technology increase supply at every price.\",\"application\":\"Predicting how cost shocks, new technology, taxes and subsidies affect a market, and forming equilibrium in 2.3.\",\"limitations\":[\"Supply can respond slowly: crops, mines and factories take time to expand (2.6)\",\"Some goods, such as land in a city centre, have almost fixed supply\",\"HL only: diminishing returns is a short-run idea; in the long run all factors vary and costs per unit can even fall\",\"Firms with market power set price and output together, so a supply curve is not well defined for them (HL, 2.11)\"],\"real\":\"Droughts or frosts in major growing regions tend to reduce the supply of crops such as coffee and push world prices up. Falling manufacturing costs for solar panels have increased supply and lowered panel prices over time.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating a rise in input costs as a movement along the supply curve. Only the good’s own price moves along it; costs shift the whole curve.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) and Paper 2: axes “Price of X” and “Quantity of X”, curves S₁ and S₂, an arrow for the shift, the determinant named, and for a specific tax the vertical distance labelled.\",\"deeper\":\"The upward-sloping curve belongs to competitive, price-taking firms. In the short run it reflects rising marginal cost. In the long run, when firms can enter and leave and all factors vary, industry supply can be much flatter, and some industries see costs per unit fall as output grows. Firms with market power have no supply curve in this sense, which is why HL market-structure analysis in 2.11 uses marginal cost and marginal revenue instead.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The law of supply predicts that a higher price raises quantity supplied, and that lower costs and better technology increase supply at every price.\",\"evidence\":\"Output in many markets tends to expand when prices rise persistently, often with a delay; farm output, for example, responds more after a planting season than within one. Falling production costs, as in electronics and solar panels, have been followed by large increases in supply and lower prices.\",\"interp\":\"Most economists see strong support for the direction of the law but stress that the size and speed of the response depend on time, spare capacity and storability (2.6). Some argue that in industries with large fixed costs and falling average costs, an upward-sloping curve describes firms’ behaviour poorly.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Reading a supply shift two ways\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"A leftward shift and an upward shift are the same event seen from two directions. Read horizontally, producers offer less at each price. Read vertically, they need a higher price to offer each quantity. The vertical reading is the useful one for taxes: a specific tax raises the minimum acceptable price by exactly the tax per unit. It also shows why, for competitive firms, the supply curve can be read as a marginal cost curve: its height at any quantity is the lowest price at which that unit would be supplied.\"},{\"h\":\"Expectations can cut both ways\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"The guide lists future price expectations as a determinant of supply. For storable goods, such as grain in silos or oil in tanks, an expected price rise can reduce supply today as producers hold stock back to sell later. Over a longer horizon, the same expectation can encourage investment in new capacity, raising future supply. Which effect dominates depends on storage costs, interest rates and how confident producers are. Holding stock back in this way can make prices more volatile in the short run.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Supply is how much producers are willing and able to sell at each possible price. Think of a farmer deciding how many strawberries to pick and bring to market. A better price makes the extra effort worthwhile, so she brings more: a movement along her supply curve. If her fuel costs rise, or a new machine makes picking cheaper, her whole plan changes at every price: the curve shifts. Taxes push supply back and subsidies push it out, because they change what each unit costs the producer.\",\"exam\":\"Define supply with willing and able, each price and a period. Draw an upward-sloping curve with axes naming the good. For a change in the good’s own price, show a movement along S and call it a change in quantity supplied. For a change in costs, technology, taxes or subsidies, related goods’ prices, expectations or the number of firms, shift the curve and name the determinant. Show a specific tax as a parallel upward shift by the tax per unit and a subsidy as a parallel downward shift. Explain why producers respond, using cost and profit. Evaluate by asking how quickly producers can adjust, whether the change is temporary, and how the final price effect depends on demand.\",\"deep\":\"The supply curve summarises how the cost of extra units changes as output grows. For a price-taking firm, the price needed to supply one more unit equals the marginal cost of that unit; adding across firms gives market supply. At HL, diminishing marginal returns explain why marginal cost rises in the short run. This framing clarifies what shifts the curve: anything that changes the cost of each unit, or the number of firms, moves it, while a change in the price received only moves producers along it. The model has boundaries. Over longer periods firms build capacity and enter or leave, so supply becomes more responsive. Firms with market power choose price and output together, so the simple supply curve does not describe them.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Shifts in supply record changes in costs, technology and the number of firms.\",\"tension\":\"A cost fall that helps consumers can squeeze firms that cannot adapt, so change creates winners and losers.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article about rising input costs in a named industry can use change to analyse a leftward shift in supply.\"},{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"here\":\"Inputs are scarce, so supplying more of one good draws resources from others, as competitive supply shows.\",\"tension\":\"Land used for one crop is not available for another, forcing trade-offs between producers and consumers of each.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on farmers switching crops can use scarcity to explain competitive supply.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Indirect taxes and subsidies act on supply by changing what each unit costs producers.\",\"tension\":\"A subsidy that increases supply also costs taxpayers and can distort choices between products.\",\"ia\":\"A Unit 2 article on a subsidy for electric vehicles can use intervention, showing the supply shift before analysing its effects.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do the prices of some goods fall over time even as demand for them grows?\",\"expect\":\"Many students expect rising demand to push prices up, so falling prices look puzzling.\",\"model\":\"If supply increases faster than demand, through better technology, lower costs or more firms, price can fall even as quantity rises.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence from electronics and solar panels suggests large, sustained falls in production cost and price alongside fast-growing sales.\",\"match\":\"Yes, once both curves are allowed to shift: the supply side explains the pattern.\",\"assume\":\"That firms compete, so lower costs are passed on in lower prices rather than kept as profit.\",\"change\":\"If a few firms dominated and could restrict output, costs could fall without prices following.\",\"policy\":\"Policies that encourage research, competition and entry can raise supply and lower prices; subsidies may speed this up but carry an opportunity cost for taxpayers.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a diagram, how a rise in wages affects the supply of a good.\",\"check\":[\"Axes labelled with the price and quantity of a named good\",\"Wages identified as a factor cost, a non-price determinant\",\"Supply shifts left (upward) from S₁ to S₂ with an arrow\",\"At each price producers now supply less because cost per unit has risen\",\"Uses “decrease in supply”, not “fall in quantity supplied”\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Distinguish joint supply from competitive supply, with an example of each.\",\"check\":[\"Joint supply: goods produced together, so more of one means more of the other\",\"An example, such as beef and leather\",\"Competitive supply: goods that compete for the same resources\",\"An example, such as wheat and barley on the same land\",\"A price rise for one good increases supply of its joint product but decreases supply of its competing product\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define supply.\",\"a\":\"The quantity of a good producers are willing and able to offer for sale at each possible price over a period, ceteris paribus.\"},{\"q\":\"The price of oranges rises. What happens on the orange supply diagram?\",\"a\":\"A movement up along the supply curve: quantity supplied extends; supply itself does not change.\"},{\"q\":\"How is a specific tax of $2 per unit shown?\",\"a\":\"Supply shifts vertically upward by $2 at every quantity, parallel to the original curve.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: at $4 per kg, three farms supply 20, 10 and 15 kg. What is market quantity supplied?\",\"a\":\"45 kg, the horizontal sum of individual quantities.\"},{\"q\":\"A farmer can grow maize or soybeans on the same land. Soybean prices rise. What happens to maize supply?\",\"a\":\"It decreases (shifts left), because land moves to soybeans: they are in competitive supply.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: why does marginal cost rise in the short run?\",\"a\":\"Diminishing marginal returns: with a fixed factor, each extra unit of a variable factor adds less output, so each extra unit of output needs more inputs.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Wages in the clothing industry rise. What happens in the market for clothing?\",\"opts\":[\"A movement down along the supply curve\",\"A fall in quantity supplied with no shift\",\"A leftward shift of the supply curve\",\"A rightward shift, because firms need more revenue\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Wages are a factor cost, a non-price determinant, so firms supply less at every price: supply shifts left. A movement is tempting because quantity falls, but a movement along supply needs a change in the price of clothing itself.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-02\"},{\"q\":\"A specific tax of $1 per unit is placed on bottled drinks. How should the supply curve be drawn?\",\"opts\":[\"Shifted horizontally left by one unit\",\"Shifted vertically upward by $1 at every quantity\",\"Pivoted so that the gap grows at higher prices\",\"Unchanged, because consumers pay the tax\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The tax adds $1 to the cost of supplying each unit, so the price producers need for any quantity rises by exactly $1: a parallel vertical shift. A pivot describes a percentage (ad valorem) tax, and a horizontal shift of a fixed amount does not show the tax per unit.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Beef prices rise and farmers send more cattle to market. What happens to the supply of leather?\",\"opts\":[\"It decreases, because leather and beef are in competitive supply\",\"It increases, because leather is jointly supplied with beef\",\"Quantity supplied of leather extends along its curve\",\"Nothing, because the price of leather has not changed\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Leather is a by-product of beef, so more cattle processed means more hides at every leather price: supply shifts right. A movement along the curve is tempting, but that needs a change in leather’s own price; this change comes from another good.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-02\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2: define supply or the law of supply in the context of the text\",\"Paper 2: explain, using a supply diagram, the effect of a change in costs, technology, or a tax or subsidy named in the text\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using diagrams, the difference between a change in quantity supplied and a change in supply\",\"HL only, Paper 1 (a): explain the law of supply through diminishing marginal returns and increasing marginal cost\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"identify\",\"analyse\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies an upward-sloping supply curve and a diagram of movements along and shifts of it (guide PDF p. 32). Axes: “Price of [good]” and “Quantity of [good]”. Show S₁ → S₂ with an arrow; for a specific tax or subsidy, draw a parallel vertical shift and label the distance.\",\"calc\":\"None is set for 2.2. Adding producers’ quantities into market supply is useful practice; PES comes in 2.6 and tax and subsidy calculations (HL only) in 2.7.\",\"apply\":\"Name the good and the specific determinant in the text, such as a drought, a named input cost or a reported subsidy.\",\"eval\":\"Consider how long producers need to adjust, whether the change is temporary, whether firms can switch products, and how the price effect depends on demand.\",\"errors\":[\"Showing a cost change as a movement along the supply curve\",\"Shifting supply for a change in the good’s own price\",\"Drawing a specific tax as a horizontal or non-parallel shift\",\"Confusing joint supply with competitive supply\"],\"strong\":[\"Use “supply” and “quantity supplied” precisely\",\"Explain the producer’s reasoning, cost per unit and profit, behind each shift\",\"Label the vertical distance of a tax or subsidy shift\",\"At HL, link the slope of supply to rising marginal cost\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using diagrams, why an improvement in production technology and a rise in the price of smartphones affect the market for smartphones in different ways.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"HL only. Explain why the law of diminishing marginal returns leads to an upward-sloping supply curve in the short run.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using diagrams, how a rise in fertiliser prices and a subsidy for farmers would each affect the supply of wheat.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"why\":\"The costs of land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship are the first determinant of supply.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"Supply meets demand in equilibrium; supply shifts explain many price changes.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"why\":\"PES measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to price, and why that grows with time.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Taxes and subsidies, introduced here as shifts, are analysed for their effects on prices, revenue and stakeholders.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"Short-run aggregate supply applies the idea that rising costs shift supply to the whole economy.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"Because demand and supply are now both defined, 2.3 brings them together to explain how price and quantity are determined.\"},\"ia\":\"An article about changing production conditions for a named product, such as a harvest failure, rising energy costs or a new subsidy, suits a Unit 2 commentary. Change or intervention could frame it, and the diagram should show a supply shift with the determinant named.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent have changes in input costs explained changes in the price of a named agricultural product in a named country? Price and weather data could make this researchable.\",\"tok\":\"Economists say a higher price causes firms to supply more. When price and output rise together in the data, how can we know which caused which?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students define supply, draw the curve, and distinguish movements from shifts, including taxes and subsidies as vertical shifts.\",\"starter\":\"Ask: if the price of hand-made bracelets doubled, would you make more? What would stop you making many more?\",\"activity\":\"Groups make paper items at a fixed table with fixed materials, adding one worker at a time and recording extra output (diminishing returns for HL); then offer different prices per item to trace a supply curve.\",\"discussion\":\"Why might a farmer supply less today if she expects prices to rise next month?\",\"check\":\"Students draw a specific tax as a supply shift on mini-whiteboards, labelling the vertical distance.\",\"extension\":\"Compare joint and competitive supply using a farm product from the local area.\",\"homework\":\"Classify eight news events as a movement along or a shift of supply, then draw two of them fully labelled.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define supply, quantity supplied and the law of supply.\",\"I can draw an upward-sloping supply curve with correctly labelled axes.\",\"I can distinguish a movement along the supply curve from a shift.\",\"I can explain how each non-price determinant shifts supply, including joint and competitive supply.\",\"I can draw a specific tax and a subsidy as vertical shifts of supply.\",\"I can add producers’ quantities to find market supply.\",\"HL only: I can explain the upward slope using diminishing marginal returns and increasing marginal costs.\",\"I can explain what limits how quickly and fully producers respond to price.\",\"I can explain supply without my notes.\"]},\"2.3\":{\"code\":\"2.3\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Nobody sets a competitive market price. Shortages bid it up and surpluses push it down, towards the point where the quantity buyers want equals the quantity sellers offer. That equilibrium is where the market is heading, and the price along the way carries information and incentives to everyone in it.\",\"why\":\"Prices coordinate millions of decisions without anyone planning them, and the competitive equilibrium is the efficiency benchmark against which market failure and intervention are judged.\",\"q\":\"Why do tickets for a sold-out tour resell for far more than face value, while unsold winter coats end up on the sale rail?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"A change in demand (a shift) versus a change in quantity demanded (a movement)\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"The non-price determinants that shift the supply curve\",\"sub\":\"2.2\"},{\"t\":\"The law of supply: why a higher price calls forth more output\",\"sub\":\"2.2\"},{\"t\":\"Scarcity and opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Finding the area of a triangle from values on two axes\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define market equilibrium, excess demand and excess supply\",\"State the three functions of the price mechanism\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain how price adjustment removes a shortage or a surplus\",\"Explain why social surplus is largest where MB = MC\"],\"apply\":[\"Use the price mechanism to explain a real price change\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse two simultaneous shifts and identify which outcome is indeterminate\",\"Separate the resource-allocation role of price from its rationing role\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate whether an observed price is evidence that a market has cleared\",\"Evaluate the claim that the competitive outcome is the best one, separating efficiency from equity\"],\"calculate\":[\"HL only: calculate consumer, producer and social surplus from a diagram\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw an equilibrium diagram showing a shift from P₁, Q₁ to P₂, Q₂\",\"Shade and label consumer and producer surplus\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read the size of a shortage or surplus at a stated price from a diagram or schedule\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Equilibrium: where two sets of plans match\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Market equilibrium is the price at which the quantity buyers plan to buy equals the quantity sellers plan to sell. The market clears: nothing is left unsold, and every buyer willing to pay that price can buy. Nothing inside the market pushes the price away, so it persists until a determinant of demand or supply changes. Treat it as a target the market moves towards rather than a place it sits.\"},{\"h\":\"How a shortage removes itself\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Below the equilibrium price, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied: excess demand, or a shortage. Stock runs out, queues form and sellers see they could charge more. As price rises, quantity supplied expands and quantity demanded contracts, both as movements along the existing curves, until they meet. A surplus works in reverse, as unsold stock leads sellers to cut prices. Neither curve shifts during the adjustment.\",\"because\":[\"Price is below equilibrium\",\"so Qd > Qs: a shortage\",\"so buyers compete and sellers raise prices\",\"so Qs expands and Qd contracts along the curves\",\"until Qd = Qs\"]},{\"h\":\"Three jobs a price does\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide groups the functions of the price mechanism into resource allocation, made up of signalling and incentive, and rationing (guide PDF p. 32). Signalling is information about scarcity. The incentive is the reward that draws resources towards the good. Rationing allocates what already exists among competing buyers. Answers that describe only rationing miss how prices redirect resources over time.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Function\",\"After a rise in demand for bicycles\"],\"rows\":[[\"Signalling\",\"The higher price shows producers that buyers value bicycles more\"],[\"Incentive\",\"Higher expected profit leads firms to add shifts or enter the market\"],[\"Rationing\",\"Only buyers willing and able to pay the higher price obtain one\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Surplus: the gains from trade\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Consumer surplus is the difference between the most a buyer would pay for a unit and the price paid: the area below the demand curve and above the price. Producer surplus is the difference between the price received and the lowest price at which a firm would have supplied the unit: the area above the supply curve and below the price. Social (community) surplus is their sum, the total gain from trade measured in money.\"},{\"h\":\"Efficient, but not necessarily fair\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"If there are no externalities, demand shows each unit’s marginal benefit (MB) and supply its marginal cost (MC). Up to the equilibrium quantity each unit is worth more than it costs, so social surplus is largest where MB = MC: allocative efficiency (guide PDF pp. 32–33). This is a claim about the size of the gains, not their distribution. Demand reflects ability to pay, so an efficient outcome can still be one many would judge unfair.\",\"because\":[\"Below Qe, MB > MC for each extra unit\",\"so producing it adds to social surplus\",\"beyond Qe, MC > MB, so producing it subtracts\",\"so surplus is largest where MB = MC, at equilibrium\"]},{\"h\":\"HL only: measuring surplus from a diagram\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Calculating consumer and producer surplus from a diagram is HL only (guide PDF p. 33). With straight-line curves each surplus is a triangle: ½ × base × height. Illustrative data: demand meets the price axis at $20, supply at $6, and equilibrium is $10 and 500 units. Consumer surplus = ½ × 500 × (20 − 10) = $2,500. Producer surplus = ½ × 500 × (10 − 6) = $1,000. Social surplus = $3,500. Measure heights from the equilibrium price, not from zero.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A poor harvest in the world coffee market\",\"cause\":\"Frost or drought cuts the harvest in a major producing country, shifting supply left.\",\"mechanism\":\"At the old price quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so buyers bid the price up.\",\"immediate\":\"Price rises and quantity falls; the higher price rations the smaller crop among roasters.\",\"secondary\":\"The price signals scarcity and rewards releasing stored beans and harvesting more intensively.\",\"longrun\":\"New planting responds slowly, so the price may drift back down over several seasons.\",\"depends\":\"PED and PES, stocks held, and whether traders expect the shortage to last.\"},{\"t\":\"Surge pricing for ride-hailing at peak times\",\"cause\":\"Demand for rides jumps in heavy rain while the number of drivers working is briefly fixed.\",\"mechanism\":\"The platform raises fares until rides demanded roughly match the drivers available.\",\"immediate\":\"The higher fare rations rides towards passengers willing to pay most.\",\"secondary\":\"The fare also signals demand and rewards drivers who log on or move to busy areas.\",\"longrun\":\"Where peaks are predictable, drivers plan hours around them, making supply more responsive.\",\"depends\":\"Drivers’ responsiveness, public views of fairness and regulation; some governments, including in India, have capped surge pricing.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Competitive market equilibrium\",\"dg\":\"ds\",\"purpose\":\"Explains how competitive markets set price and quantity and how both change after a shock.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Many buyers and sellers, none able to influence price\",\"Prices free to move\",\"Ceteris paribus: one determinant changes at a time\"],\"mechanism\":\"Excess demand bids price up and excess supply pushes it down until quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\",\"prediction\":\"After a shift, price and quantity move to the new intersection.\",\"application\":\"Predicting the direction of change after shocks such as harvest failures.\",\"limitations\":[\"Contracts, menu costs and fairness concerns can make prices sticky\",\"Firms with market power set prices (2.11)\",\"With two simultaneous shifts, one outcome is indeterminate\"],\"real\":\"Fresh produce prices often rise after poor harvests and fall back as supply recovers, broadly as predicted, though timing varies by crop.\",\"misconception\":\"Believing price is set by the seller’s costs alone; costs work through supply, but equilibrium needs both curves (MIC-03, teacher-created Misconception Database).\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) and Paper 2 (c)–(f): draw the diagram, mark the shortage or surplus at the old price and explain the adjustment step by step.\",\"deeper\":\"Laboratory markets, pioneered by Vernon Smith, often show prices converging on the predicted equilibrium quickly, even with a few traders who know only their own values and costs. That supports the model’s logic. Real markets add frictions the laboratory removes, such as search costs, long contracts and sellers’ fear of looking unfair, and markets with long production lags can cycle around equilibrium rather than settle.\"},{\"model\":\"Consumer and producer surplus and allocative efficiency\",\"dg\":\"ds\",\"purpose\":\"Measures the gains from trade and sets the efficiency benchmark.\",\"assumptions\":[\"No externalities, so demand shows marginal social benefit and supply marginal social cost\",\"Willingness to pay measures benefit\",\"A unit of surplus counts the same whoever receives it\"],\"mechanism\":\"Each unit traded adds the gap between its value to the buyer and its cost to the seller.\",\"prediction\":\"Social surplus is largest at the competitive equilibrium; any other output creates a welfare loss.\",\"application\":\"The benchmark for welfare losses from taxes and price controls (2.7), externalities (2.8), market power (2.11) and tariffs (4.2).\",\"limitations\":[\"Externalities break the link between private and social values\",\"Willingness to pay reflects income, so equity is ignored\",\"Biases may separate what people pay from the benefit they receive (2.4)\"],\"real\":\"Debates on price caps for essential goods use surplus analysis to show who gains and loses, alongside fairness.\",\"misconception\":\"Shading producer surplus above the price line instead of between the price and the supply curve.\",\"exam\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 33): calculate the areas; HL papers can include HL-only content (guide PDF p. 68). All students use the areas to explain welfare loss.\",\"deeper\":\"Surplus under an ordinary demand curve is an approximation: when a good takes a large share of income, a price change also alters real income, so the area measures welfare only roughly. For most goods the error is small. A deeper issue is that adding surplus across people treats a dollar gained by a wealthy household as equal to a dollar gained by a poor one, so distribution needs separate judgement.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Competitive markets clear through price changes, and the resulting equilibrium maximises social surplus.\",\"evidence\":\"Experimental markets tend to converge on the predicted price. Yet some real prices stay below market-clearing levels: popular events sell out at face value and resell for much more. Survey work by Kahneman, Knetsch and Thaler in the 1980s found many people regard raising prices during a demand surge as unfair.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists see sticky prices as rational protection of reputation and future sales; others see social norms the model omits. They agree on the direction of pressure, not on how fully prices respond.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why the model needs many small sellers\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"The efficiency result depends on price-taking. A firm that can influence price may gain by restricting output below the point where MB = MC, because the higher price on the units it still sells outweighs the profit lost on the units it gives up. Social surplus then falls short of its maximum, the core of the HL analysis of market power in 2.11. The competitive model is a benchmark rather than a description of most markets: it shows what efficiency would look like so you can judge how far a real market falls short.\"},{\"h\":\"Rationing by price, queue or lottery\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"Price is one way to ration a scarce good; queues, lotteries and need-based rules are others. Price sends the good to those willing and able to pay most, maximising measured surplus but favouring higher incomes. Queues ration by time and waste the hours spent waiting. Lotteries are cheap to run, though winners may resell. When event organisers, schools or health systems choose a non-price method, they are usually trading some efficiency for a view of fairness.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Picture a stall selling strawberries. If the price is too low, they sell out by mid-morning and late customers leave empty-handed, so the seller realises she could charge more. If the price is too high, boxes are left at closing time and she cuts it. Either way the price moves towards the level where what she brings matches what people want at that price: equilibrium. Along the way the price tells everyone how scarce strawberries are, rewards sellers for bringing more and decides who gets what is available.\",\"exam\":\"Define equilibrium precisely: the price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. Draw a labelled diagram with axes for the price and quantity of the named good, both curves, and P₁, Q₁. For a change, shift the correct curve, mark the shortage or surplus at the old price, and explain the adjustment as movements along both curves to P₂, Q₂. Name signalling and incentive as well as rationing. For efficiency, shade both surpluses and explain that social surplus is maximised because MB = MC. Evaluate by asking whether prices can adjust, how long adjustment takes, and whether an efficient outcome is also equitable.\",\"deep\":\"Behind the diagram is a claim about coordination: no one needs to know why a price has changed, because the rise alone leads buyers to economise and sellers to expand. The welfare claim is narrower than it looks. Demand measures marginal benefit only if buyers judge their own benefit well and no one else is affected; supply measures marginal cost only if producers bear all costs. Surplus is counted in money, so preferences are weighted by purchasing power. Equilibrium itself is a theoretical resting point. Because new shocks keep arriving, the model is best read as predicting the direction of pressure on prices rather than the price on any given day. Its value is as a reference: it shows what a market would do without frictions, so departures can be measured.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Competitive equilibrium is the reference point for allocative efficiency: social surplus is largest where MB = MC.\",\"tension\":\"The efficient outcome can still be one many would judge unfair.\",\"ia\":\"An article about a price cap or rationing scheme, judged by what happens to consumer and producer surplus.\"},{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"here\":\"The equilibrium price measures how scarce a good is relative to what people want of it.\",\"tension\":\"Rationing by price resolves scarcity efficiently but excludes buyers with low incomes.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a shortage or price spike, read through who is priced out.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do organisers of popular concerts often sell tickets for less than fans would pay?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect sellers to charge the most the market will bear.\",\"model\":\"A price below equilibrium creates a shortage, so the price should be bid up until the market clears.\",\"evidence\":\"Face-value tickets often sell out quickly and reappear on resale sites at much higher prices.\",\"match\":\"Partly. The shortage appears, but the adjustment happens in the resale market, not in the organiser’s price.\",\"assume\":\"The model assumes a one-off sale to anonymous buyers; artists deal with fans repeatedly and value goodwill.\",\"change\":\"If higher face values did not harm reputation or future sales, below-equilibrium pricing would be harder to explain.\",\"policy\":\"Resale caps and named tickets are non-price rationing: they trade fans’ access against surplus lost when tickets cannot move to those who value them most.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how the price mechanism removes a shortage in a competitive market.\",\"check\":[\"Price is below equilibrium, so Qd > Qs\",\"Buyers compete and sellers raise the price\",\"Qs expands and Qd contracts as movements along the curves\",\"No curve shifts during adjustment\",\"Adjustment ends where Qd = Qs\",\"Names signalling, incentive and rationing\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why social surplus is maximised at the competitive equilibrium.\",\"check\":[\"Defines consumer and producer surplus as areas\",\"Links demand to MB and supply to MC\",\"Units below Qe have MB > MC and add surplus\",\"Units beyond Qe have MC > MB\",\"States the no-externality assumption\",\"Notes that efficiency says nothing about fairness\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define excess supply.\",\"a\":\"At the current price, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded; price is above equilibrium.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the three functions of the price mechanism.\",\"a\":\"Signalling and incentive (resource allocation) and rationing.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is producer surplus on a market diagram?\",\"a\":\"Above the supply curve and below the price, up to the quantity traded.\"},{\"q\":\"Demand and supply both increase. What happens to equilibrium price and quantity?\",\"a\":\"Quantity rises; the price change depends on which shift is larger.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: demand meets the price axis at $30; equilibrium is $18 and 200 units. Calculate consumer surplus.\",\"a\":\"½ × 200 × (30 − 18) = $1,200.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is allocative efficiency not a judgement that an outcome is fair?\",\"a\":\"It concerns the total size of surplus, not its distribution, and demand reflects ability to pay.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"The cost of making phone cases falls while demand for them rises. What can be predicted about the new equilibrium?\",\"opts\":[\"Price and quantity both rise\",\"Quantity rises; the price change depends on the relative size of the shifts\",\"Price falls and quantity rises\",\"Price rises; the quantity change cannot be predicted\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Both shifts raise quantity, but they push price in opposite directions, so the price change depends on which is larger. The first option reasons from the demand shift alone.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-03\"},{\"q\":\"Which area shows producer surplus at equilibrium?\",\"opts\":[\"Below the demand curve and above the price\",\"Above the supply curve and below the price\",\"The whole area below the supply curve\",\"The rectangle of price × quantity\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Producer surplus is the gap between the price received and the lowest acceptable price for each unit. The first option is consumer surplus, the most common swap; the rectangle is total revenue.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-08\"},{\"q\":\"A student writes: “Social surplus is maximised at equilibrium, so the outcome is fair.” What is the best response?\",\"opts\":[\"Correct: maximum surplus means everyone is as well off as possible\",\"Incorrect: efficiency concerns the size of total surplus, not how it is shared or who can afford to buy\",\"Incorrect: surplus is maximised only at the lowest possible price\",\"Correct, provided there is no shortage\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Allocative efficiency is silent on distribution, and demand counts only buyers able to pay. The first option confuses efficiency with equity; a price below equilibrium creates a shortage, so units worth producing go unproduced.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with a diagram, how the price mechanism responds to a change in demand or supply\",\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): identify a shortage or surplus at a stated price and explain the adjustment\",\"HL only: calculate consumer and producer surplus from a diagram (guide PDF p. 33)\",\"Paper 1 (b), in later subtopics such as 2.7 and 2.8: the competitive equilibrium as the benchmark when evaluating intervention, weighing efficiency against equity (2.3 itself is set at AO2/AO4, guide PDF pp. 32–33)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"draw\",\"identify\",\"calculate\",\"analyse\"],\"dg\":\"Equilibrium with axes labelled for the named good, both curves and P₁, Q₁; for a change, the shifted curve, P₂, Q₂ and the shortage or surplus at the old price; for efficiency, both surpluses shaded and labelled.\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 33): consumer and producer surplus from a diagram, using ½ × base × height. No calculation is specified for SL here.\",\"apply\":\"Name the market and the shock from the context and use its prices or quantities on the diagram.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh how fast prices adjust, who bears the adjustment, and efficiency against equity.\",\"errors\":[\"Shifting a curve when only the good’s own price has changed\",\"Explaining a price change through one curve only\",\"Naming rationing but not signalling and incentive\",\"Swapping the surplus areas or measuring heights from zero\"],\"strong\":[\"Show the shortage or surplus at the old price before explaining adjustment\",\"Describe adjustment as movements along both curves\",\"Link surplus to MB = MC and state the no-externality assumption\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a demand and supply diagram, explain how the price mechanism would respond to a large rise in demand for electric bicycles.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why social surplus is maximised at the competitive market equilibrium.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Illustrative data: in a market for bottled water, demand meets the price axis at $4.00, supply meets it at $0.50, and equilibrium is $1.50 and 6,000 bottles a week. Calculate consumer, producer and social surplus, and explain what happens to social surplus if output is limited to 4,000 bottles.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"why\":\"Equilibrium analysis relies on the demand curve and the shift–movement distinction.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Price controls, taxes and subsidies are measured as departures from equilibrium and changes in surplus.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Externalities break the link between demand and marginal social benefit, so equilibrium is no longer efficient.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"Tariff and quota analysis uses the same consumer and producer surplus areas.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"why\":\"Because the efficiency result rests on consumers and firms maximising rationally, which 2.4 (HL only) tests; SL students continue to 2.5.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a sudden shortage, price spike or unsold stock in a named market, where you can show the move between equilibria. Scarcity could frame it, with efficiency and equity used to judge the outcome.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent do resale prices for sold-out events in one city suggest that face-value prices are set below market-clearing levels?\",\"tok\":\"If a market price emerges without anyone choosing it, in what sense can an economist claim to know the ‘right’ price?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students explain how price adjustment removes a shortage or surplus and why equilibrium maximises social surplus.\",\"starter\":\"Show an empty shelf and a clearance rail; ask what each suggests about the price.\",\"activity\":\"Trading-pit simulation: buyers hold value cards, sellers cost cards; compare traded prices by round with the curves built from the cards (a method suggested in Arjun Agrawal’s teacher-created roadmap).\",\"discussion\":\"Is the equilibrium price fair to a buyer whose value card was below it? What would a lottery do instead?\",\"check\":\"Draw a stated shift, mark the shortage at the old price and order the adjustment steps.\",\"extension\":\"HL: calculate surplus from the simulation’s curves and compare it with the surplus realised in trading.\",\"homework\":\"Answer the retrieval questions without notes, then correct them in another colour.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define equilibrium, excess demand and excess supply.\",\"I can explain how price adjustment removes a shortage or surplus.\",\"I can draw a shift and label P₁, Q₁, P₂ and Q₂.\",\"I can explain signalling, incentive and rationing with an example.\",\"I can shade both surpluses and explain why their sum is largest where MB = MC.\",\"HL only: I can calculate consumer and producer surplus from a diagram.\",\"I can explain why an efficient outcome need not be a fair one.\",\"I can explain equilibrium without notes and connect it to 2.7 and 2.8.\"]},\"2.4\":{\"code\":\"2.4\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Demand and supply models assume consumers maximise satisfaction and firms maximise profit. Behavioural economics finds that people depart from this in systematic, predictable ways, and firms often pursue other goals. The whole subtopic is HL only (guide PDF p. 33).\",\"why\":\"If behaviour departs from the model predictably, the design of a choice can change outcomes as much as prices can.\",\"q\":\"Why does enrolling workers in a pension by default, with the right to leave, lead far more of them to save than asking them to sign up?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The law of demand and what a demand curve represents\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"HL: utility and diminishing marginal utility behind the law of demand\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"How economists use models built on simplifying assumptions\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"Consumer surplus and allocative efficiency\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the three assumptions of rational consumer choice\",\"Define choice architecture, default, restricted and mandated choices, and a nudge\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain how rules of thumb, anchoring, framing and availability produce systematic departures from rational choice\",\"Explain bounded rationality, bounded self-control and bounded selfishness with examples\"],\"apply\":[\"Identify which assumption a described behaviour violates\",\"Distinguish a nudge from a regulation and from a price-based intervention\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse why a default changes behaviour although no option is removed\",\"Analyse why a firm might pursue market share, growth, CSR or satisficing instead of maximum profit\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the evidence on nudges, including why effects are often smaller at scale\",\"Evaluate whether an alternative business objective is consistent with long-run profit\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a trial comparing opt-in and opt-out schemes, separating participation from the outcome that matters\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend whether a nudge, a tax or a regulation better suits a stated policy goal\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"The consumer the demand curve assumes\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The standard model of consumer choice rests on three assumptions, each HL only in the guide (guide PDF p. 33). Rationality: consumers have consistent preferences and choose the option they rank highest. Utility maximisation: they spend a limited income so as to gain the most satisfaction possible. Perfect information: they know the prices, qualities and consequences of every option. None is literally true. The question is whether departures are large and systematic enough to change predictions or policy.\"},{\"h\":\"Biases: shortcuts that tilt decisions\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Many departures come from mental shortcuts that save effort and usually work, but err in predictable directions. Because many people err the same way, the errors do not cancel out across a market. The guide names four biases: rules of thumb, anchoring, framing and availability. Firms exploit them in pricing and presentation, and governments can draw on the same patterns when designing policy.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Bias\",\"What happens\",\"Example\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rule of thumb\",\"A simple habit replaces calculation\",\"Buying the brand one’s parents bought\"],[\"Anchoring\",\"An initial number pulls later judgements towards it\",\"A high “was” price makes a sale price seem a bargain\"],[\"Framing\",\"The same information, presented differently, changes the choice\",\"“90% fat-free” tends to appeal more than “10% fat”\"],[\"Availability\",\"Easily recalled events are judged more likely\",\"Interest in flood insurance can rise after a well-publicised flood\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Bounded rationality, self-control and selfishness\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide also lists broader limits (guide PDF p. 33). Bounded rationality, a term associated with Herbert Simon, means people are rational within limits of information, time and processing power, so they often settle for a good-enough choice. Bounded self-control means people fail to act on their own plans. Bounded selfishness means people care about others and about fairness. Imperfect information means consumers often cannot judge quality or long-term effects.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Limit\",\"Assumption challenged\",\"Example\"],\"rows\":[[\"Bounded rationality\",\"Utility maximisation\",\"Comparing three phone contracts, not three hundred\"],[\"Bounded self-control\",\"Consistent preferences\",\"Paying for a gym membership that is rarely used\"],[\"Bounded selfishness\",\"Pure self-interest\",\"Donating to charity\"],[\"Imperfect information\",\"Perfect information\",\"Being unable to judge a used car’s quality\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Choice architecture and why defaults work\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Choice architecture is how options are presented: their order, wording, number and what happens if a person does nothing. The guide names three forms (guide PDF p. 33). A default applies unless the person chooses otherwise, such as pension enrolment. A restricted choice limits options but leaves some choice, such as a short canteen menu. A mandated choice requires a decision, with no default. Defaults are powerful because inertia and the sense that the default is advice keep many people where they are placed.\",\"because\":[\"The default applies if no action is taken\",\"switching needs effort and a decision\",\"people procrastinate and read the default as advice\",\"so many stay with the default\",\"so behaviour shifts without any option being removed\"]},{\"h\":\"Nudges and what the evidence shows\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"A nudge, in the sense popularised by Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein, changes choice architecture to steer behaviour without banning options or significantly changing prices. Read the evidence carefully. Pension defaults have strong support. For many other nudges, such as reminder letters or social-norm messages, trials run at scale by government units have on average found effects that are real but small, and noticeably smaller than in early published studies. Publication bias, the tendency for striking results to reach print, is one likely reason.\"},{\"h\":\"Firms that do not maximise profit\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The standard theory assumes firms maximise profit, producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost (HL, 2.11). The guide lists four alternative objectives, all HL only (guide PDF p. 33). They often overlap with profit over a longer horizon, so it is hard to tell from outside which objective a firm is pursuing. Profit maximisation also needs information about costs and demand that managers may simply not have.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Objective\",\"Aim\",\"Possible reason\"],\"rows\":[[\"Corporate social responsibility\",\"Ethical conduct towards workers, communities and the environment beyond legal minimums\",\"Owners’ values, reputation, attracting staff and investors\"],[\"Market share\",\"The largest share of market sales\",\"Economies of scale, network effects, deterring entry\"],[\"Growth\",\"Larger sales, assets or workforce\",\"Managers’ pay and status, spreading risk\"],[\"Satisficing\",\"Satisfactory results across several goals\",\"Limited information, competing stakeholder demands\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Switching a workplace pension from opt-in to opt-out\",\"cause\":\"An employer, or a government rule, makes pension enrolment the default, with the right to opt out.\",\"mechanism\":\"Inertia, procrastination and reading the default as advice keep most workers enrolled.\",\"immediate\":\"Participation rises sharply although the options themselves are unchanged.\",\"secondary\":\"Many stay at the default contribution rate, including some who would have chosen more.\",\"longrun\":\"More households build retirement savings, though adequacy depends on the default rate, which can anchor behaviour.\",\"depends\":\"The default rate, ease of opting out, and household finances: opting out can be sensible for someone in debt.\"},{\"t\":\"A delivery platform pursues market share, not short-run profit\",\"cause\":\"The firm expects the largest platform to benefit from network effects.\",\"mechanism\":\"It prices below the profit-maximising level and spends heavily on promotions.\",\"immediate\":\"Losses rise while market share grows; rivals may match the price cuts.\",\"secondary\":\"Weaker rivals may exit or merge, reducing competition.\",\"longrun\":\"If it gains market power it may raise prices and turn a profit, so share was a route to profit; if not, investors may withdraw.\",\"depends\":\"Network effects, investors’ patience, barriers to entry and competition policy (2.11).\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Choice architecture and nudge theory\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains how the design of a decision changes behaviour without prices or bans.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Departures from rationality are systematic\",\"Opting out stays easy and cheap\",\"The designer knows which outcome is better for the chooser\"],\"mechanism\":\"The designer sets the default, orders or frames options, or mandates a choice, so inertia and biases work towards the target.\",\"prediction\":\"Behaviour shifts towards the nudged option at low public cost.\",\"application\":\"Pension saving, organ-donor registration, energy use, healthy eating and tax payment.\",\"limitations\":[\"Effects at scale are often small, and some fade\",\"Participation can rise without the underlying outcome improving\",\"Designers may misjudge what is good for people\",\"Firms can use the same techniques against consumers\"],\"real\":\"England moved to opt-out organ donation in 2020, following Wales in 2015. Evaluations suggest opt-out can raise consent rates, but actual donations also depend on families’ agreement and hospital capacity.\",\"misconception\":\"Presenting nudges as costless: design, testing and monitoring cost money, and a poor default can lock people into a bad outcome.\",\"exam\":\"HL: Paper 3 part (b) asks for a recommendation (guide PDF p. 71), where a nudge can be weighed against a tax or regulation on effect size, cost, freedom and evidence.\",\"deeper\":\"Early nudge studies reported large effects. A 2022 comparison of trials run by two government nudge units with published academic studies found the units’ average effect much smaller, which the authors linked partly to publication bias. Meta-analyses disagree about the average effect once that bias is adjusted for. The defensible conclusion is that some nudges, especially defaults, work reliably, while others have small or uncertain effects.\"},{\"model\":\"Profit maximisation and alternative business objectives\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains what firms aim for, which shapes price and output.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Owners control the firm and want maximum profit\",\"Managers know marginal revenue and marginal cost\",\"Profit is the only objective\"],\"mechanism\":\"A profit maximiser produces where MR = MC (HL, 2.11); a firm with another objective chooses differently, such as a lower price to gain share.\",\"prediction\":\"Firms seeking share or growth tend to charge less and produce more than a profit maximiser.\",\"application\":\"Explaining loss-making expansion, ethical sourcing, and firms that hold prices down despite inelastic demand.\",\"limitations\":[\"Alternatives often serve long-run profit, so the line blurs\",\"Managers’ goals may differ from owners’ (a principal–agent problem)\",\"CSR claims may be public relations rather than practice\"],\"real\":\"Several ride-hailing and delivery platforms operated at a loss for years while expanding, consistent with pursuing market share first.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating satisficing as bad management, or naming allocative efficiency as a business objective when it describes a market outcome.\",\"exam\":\"HL Paper 1 or Paper 3: explain an objective, then judge it against long-run profit.\",\"deeper\":\"Milton Friedman argued in 1970 that a business’s social responsibility is to increase its profits within the rules, leaving social goals to owners and governments. CSR supporters reply that reputation, staff loyalty and investor pressure make responsible conduct profitable, which, if true, narrows the disagreement. The harder test is a firm that gives up profit for social ends in ways shareholders cannot easily reverse.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Rational choice predicts that presentation and defaults should barely affect decisions when switching is cheap.\",\"evidence\":\"Pension defaults are a strong counterexample: participation rose sharply where enrolment became the default, including in single firms that changed nothing else and in the United Kingdom after 2012, where employers also had to contribute. For many other nudges, large-scale trials suggest smaller effects than early studies.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists add frictions such as limited attention to an otherwise sound model; others see a deeper challenge to treating choices as a measure of welfare. They also disagree on whether governments, themselves subject to bias and lobbying, should design defaults.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Are rules of thumb a failure or a skill?\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"Research associated with Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky emphasises how mental shortcuts produce errors. Gerd Gigerenzer and others stress that simple rules often perform well in uncertain, fast-changing settings. Both can be right. A rule of thumb for choosing a pension fund may cost a saver a great deal over decades; the same kind of rule for choosing lunch costs almost nothing. Choice architecture matters most for decisions that are important, infrequent and complex, which is where biases do most damage.\"},{\"h\":\"Who designs the choice architecture?\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"Every choice has an architecture, designed or not, and governments are not the only architects. Firms set defaults for subscriptions, privacy settings and add-ons, often to their own advantage. Critics call harmful design that makes cancelling or comparing difficult “sludge”, and some consumer protection rules now target it. This matters for evaluation: a nudge is not neutral simply because choices remain open, and the realistic alternative to a government nudge is often a firm’s nudge rather than none.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Economics often assumes you shop like a careful calculator: you know every price, weigh every option and pick the best. Real people take shortcuts. You might buy the brand you bought last time, trust a “was 50, now 25” label, or put off starting a savings plan for another month. These are not random slips. They follow patterns, so businesses and governments can predict them and design choices around them, for instance by making saving the default. Firms are not perfect calculators either, and some openly aim for goals other than the largest profit.\",\"exam\":\"This subtopic is HL only (guide PDF p. 33). State the assumption being tested: rationality, utility maximisation or perfect information. Name the behavioural concept that challenges it, such as anchoring or bounded self-control, and explain its mechanism with an example rather than just the label. When discussing policy, define a nudge and distinguish it from a tax or regulation. Evaluate using the strength of evidence (strong for defaults, smaller for many other nudges), design and monitoring costs, freedom of choice and who decides what is better. For business objectives, explain the alternative clearly, then judge whether it ultimately serves long-run profit.\",\"deep\":\"The rational model is a benchmark, not a portrait. It predicts the direction of responses to prices and incomes well for whole markets, where individual quirks can average out. Behavioural economics targets cases where they do not: decisions that are infrequent, complex, delayed in their consequences or presented in a particular way. There, observed choices may not reveal what people would want on reflection, which weakens the use of demand curves as measures of benefit. That has a policy edge. If people predictably under-save, a default can raise saving cheaply. But the same logic can justify paternalism, and governments designing nudges face their own information limits and biases. The honest position is case by case: which decisions, which people, and how strong the evidence is.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"The subtopic asks how people actually choose, and whether the design of a choice changes the outcome.\",\"tension\":\"Steering people towards a better choice can weaken the sense that the choice is their own.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a default or opt-out scheme, analysed through how the design of the choice changes behaviour.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Nudges add a low-cost tool alongside taxes and regulation (consumer nudges, HL only, in 2.7).\",\"tension\":\"A nudge is cheaper and less coercive than a ban but may achieve much less.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a government behavioural campaign, weighing its likely effect against a tax or regulation.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"If choices do not reflect what people would want on reflection, competitive markets may not maximise well-being.\",\"tension\":\"Judging someone’s well-being for them raises questions of paternalism.\",\"ia\":\"An article on under-saving, judged by whether behaviour serves people’s long-term interests.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why does making pension saving the default raise participation so much when leaving takes little effort?\",\"expect\":\"If people are rational, a default should make little difference.\",\"model\":\"Rational choice predicts similar participation under opt-in and opt-out; behavioural models predict more under opt-out because of inertia and procrastination.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of firms that changed only the default, and of national schemes such as the UK’s (which also required employer contributions), have found large rises in participation, with many savers staying at the default rate.\",\"match\":\"No. The rational model underpredicts the power of the default; the behavioural model explains both the rise and the anchoring.\",\"assume\":\"Consistent preferences, and the assumption that small switching costs do not matter.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that those kept in by the default later regretted it, or cut other saving, would weaken the welfare case.\",\"policy\":\"Defaults can raise saving cheaply, but the default rate needs careful choice and review.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a default option can change behaviour even though it removes no choices.\",\"check\":[\"Defines a default as the option applying if no action is taken\",\"Notes that rational choice predicts little effect when switching is cheap\",\"Explains inertia or procrastination as bounded self-control\",\"Explains the default being read as advice, or anchoring\",\"Gives an example such as pension auto-enrolment\",\"Notes a limitation, such as anchoring at a low contribution rate\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a firm might pursue market share rather than maximum short-run profit.\",\"check\":[\"Defines profit maximisation (MR = MC) and market share\",\"Explains lower prices or promotions to win customers\",\"Links share to scale economies, network effects or deterring entry\",\"Considers whether the aim is long-run profit\",\"Identifies what the outcome depends on\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the three assumptions of rational consumer choice.\",\"a\":\"Consumer rationality, utility maximisation and perfect information.\"},{\"q\":\"What is anchoring?\",\"a\":\"Relying heavily on an initial reference value, such as a “was” price, when making a later judgement.\"},{\"q\":\"Distinguish a restricted choice from a mandated choice.\",\"a\":\"A restricted choice limits the options but leaves some choice; a mandated choice requires a decision, with no default.\"},{\"q\":\"Give an example of bounded selfishness.\",\"a\":\"Donating to charity, or rejecting an offer seen as unfair even at a cost to oneself.\"},{\"q\":\"Why might a nudge work less well when rolled out than in the original study?\",\"a\":\"Striking early results are more likely to be published, settings differ, and effects can fade.\"},{\"q\":\"What is satisficing?\",\"a\":\"Aiming for satisfactory results across several goals rather than the maximum in one.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Behavioural economists argue that departures from rational choice are best described as:\",\"opts\":[\"Random, so they cancel out across a market\",\"Systematic and often predictable, so they can shift market outcomes\",\"Rare, and limited to people with low financial literacy\",\"Caused only by a lack of information\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Biases such as anchoring and inertia push many people the same way, so they do not cancel out, which is why defaults change aggregate outcomes. If departures were random, as the first option suggests, the rational model would still predict markets well.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-20\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a nudge rather than a regulation or a price-based policy?\",\"opts\":[\"A tax on sugary drinks\",\"A ban on energy-drink sales to under-16s\",\"Making a lower-sugar drink the default in a meal deal, with other drinks still available at the same price\",\"A subsidy on fresh fruit\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A nudge changes how a choice is presented without removing options or significantly changing prices. The tax and subsidy work through price and the ban removes an option, even though all four target the same behaviour.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"A family-owned firm aims for a “reasonable” profit and stable jobs rather than maximum profit. This is best described as:\",\"opts\":[\"Satisficing\",\"Evidence of poor management\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Growth maximisation\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Satisficing means aiming for satisfactory outcomes across several goals, often a sensible response to limited information and competing stakeholders, not a sign of poor management. Allocative efficiency describes a market outcome, not a firm’s goal.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"HL Paper 1 (a): explain two behavioural limitations of rational consumer choice\",\"HL Paper 1 (b): discuss how far the rationality assumption limits demand theory\",\"HL Paper 3: weigh a nudge against a tax or regulation and recommend a policy\",\"HL Paper 1 or Paper 3: explain alternative business objectives and judge them against long-run profit\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"to what extent\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies no diagram for 2.4 (guide PDF p. 33). A demand diagram can support an argument, such as a demand shift after a change in default, but the analysis rests on the behavioural mechanism.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Name the precise bias or bound at work in the context and identify the choice architecture described.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh evidence strength, costs, freedom of choice and who sets the default; for firms, whether an objective ultimately serves profit.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating “irrational” as random or foolish\",\"Naming a bias without explaining its mechanism\",\"Calling taxes or bans nudges\",\"Presenting nudges as costless\"],\"strong\":[\"Link each behavioural concept to the assumption it challenges\",\"Develop a real example, such as pension auto-enrolment, including its limits\",\"Separate participation from the outcome that matters\",\"Recognise that firms also design choice architecture\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Explain two reasons why consumers may not make the choices predicted by the model of rational consumer choice.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Using real-world examples, evaluate the use of choice architecture by governments to increase household saving.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Illustrative data: in a three-month trial, cafés that made a reusable cup the default saw single-use cup sales fall by 8%, while a town that charged a small fee per cup saw them fall by 25%. Recommend a policy for a city that wants to cut single-use cup waste, weighing nudges against charges.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"why\":\"The HL assumptions behind the law of demand are the rational-choice foundations that 2.4 critiques.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Consumer nudges (HL only) are compared there with taxes, subsidies and regulation (guide PDF p. 36).\"},{\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"why\":\"Imperfect information develops into asymmetric information, where one side knows more than the other.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"why\":\"Profit maximisation at MR = MC is the benchmark for judging alternative business objectives.\"},{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"why\":\"The guide traces the growing role of behavioural economics in how economic thought has changed (guide PDF p. 30).\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"Because elasticity measures how strongly quantities respond to prices and incomes, the response both rational and behavioural models try to predict.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a default, an opt-out scheme, a framing change, or a firm’s stated non-profit objective. Choice or intervention could frame it; name the bias or architecture involved rather than just describing the policy.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has an opt-out or default scheme in one country changed the outcome it targets, such as actual saving or donations, rather than only participation?\",\"tok\":\"If choices can be changed by how options are framed, can observed choices tell economists what people really want?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"HL students identify which rational-choice assumption a behaviour violates and evaluate a nudge against a tax or regulation.\",\"starter\":\"Half the class estimates a quantity after seeing a high number, half after a low one; compare averages (small classes give noisy results).\",\"activity\":\"Field investigation: students record choice architecture in a supermarket or canteen (defaults, placement, framing) and classify each example, an inquiry the guide itself suggests (guide PDF p. 35).\",\"discussion\":\"Is a default pension contribution rate a helpful nudge or a decision made for people?\",\"check\":\"Six short scenarios: name the bias or bound in each and the assumption it violates.\",\"extension\":\"Explain why a large-scale nudge evaluation found smaller effects than an early study.\",\"homework\":\"A paragraph comparing a nudge and a tax for reducing sugary-drink consumption, with one evaluative point for each.\"},\"after\":[\"I can state the three assumptions of rational consumer choice.\",\"I can explain rules of thumb, anchoring, framing and availability with examples.\",\"I can explain bounded rationality, self-control and selfishness and the assumption each challenges.\",\"I can define default, restricted and mandated choices and distinguish a nudge from a tax or regulation.\",\"I can evaluate nudges using evidence, including why effects are often smaller at scale.\",\"I can explain CSR, market share, growth and satisficing and judge whether each serves long-run profit.\",\"I can explain without notes why departures from rationality are systematic.\"]},\"2.5\":{\"code\":\"2.5\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Demand theory says which way quantity moves when price or income changes; elasticity says how far. Price elasticity of demand (PED) compares the percentage change in quantity demanded with the percentage change in price, and income elasticity (YED) does the same for income. The magnitudes decide what happens to revenue, tax receipts and whole industries.\",\"why\":\"Firms need PED to predict revenue; governments need it to predict what a tax will raise and how far it changes behaviour.\",\"q\":\"Why can a government raise large sums by taxing fuel and tobacco, when a similar tax on one brand of trainers would mostly drive buyers elsewhere?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"A change in demand versus a change in quantity demanded\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"Substitutes, complements, normal and inferior goods\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"Calculating a percentage change from original and new values\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"Total revenue as price × quantity, shown as a rectangle under the demand curve\",\"sub\":null},{\"t\":\"How a supply shift moves price and quantity along the demand curve\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define PED and YED and state the formula for each\",\"List the determinants of PED\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain the PED–total revenue relationship using the price effect and the quantity effect\",\"Explain what the sign and size of YED show\"],\"apply\":[\"Use PED to predict the revenue and quantity effects of a price change or tax\",\"Classify goods from elasticity data\"],\"analyse\":[\"HL only: explain why PED falls moving down a straight-line demand curve\",\"HL only: explain why PED for primary commodities is generally lower than for manufactured products\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the importance of PED for firms and governments, including how reliable published estimates are\",\"HL only: evaluate how far YED explains changes in an economy’s sectoral structure\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate PED and YED from data, keeping the sign\",\"Calculate a missing change in price, quantity, income or total revenue from a given elasticity\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw elastic, inelastic and constant-PED demand, and the revenue gained and lost from a price change\",\"Draw an Engel curve with income elastic, income inelastic and inferior ranges\",\"HL only: draw PED ranges on a straight-line demand curve\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret an elasticity in context, stating the price range and time period it applies to\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Elasticity turns direction into size\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Elasticity measures how strongly one variable responds to a change in another, in percentage terms. PED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price. Because price and quantity demanded move in opposite directions, PED is negative for almost all goods, and it is often quoted as an absolute value. Demand is price elastic if |PED| > 1, price inelastic if |PED| < 1 and unit elastic if |PED| = 1. Classify by magnitude, not sign: a PED of −1.8 shows a stronger response than −0.6, though −1.8 is the lower number.\",\"because\":[\"Price rises 10%\",\"quantity demanded falls 4%\",\"PED = −4 ÷ 10 = −0.4\",\"|PED| < 1: price inelastic\"]},{\"h\":\"Five shapes to know\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for relatively elastic and inelastic demand and three constant-PED cases (guide PDF p. 33). A vertical curve is perfectly inelastic: quantity does not respond. A horizontal curve is perfectly elastic: above that price, quantity demanded falls to zero. Unit elastic demand is a curve on which every price × quantity rectangle has the same area. Be careful comparing curves by steepness: slope depends on the axis scales, while elasticity does not.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"|PED|\",\"Description\",\"Conventional drawing\"],\"rows\":[[\"0\",\"Perfectly inelastic\",\"Vertical\"],[\"Between 0 and 1\",\"Relatively inelastic\",\"Steep over the relevant range\"],[\"1\",\"Unit elastic\",\"Rectangular hyperbola\"],[\"Greater than 1\",\"Relatively elastic\",\"Shallow over the relevant range\"],[\"∞\",\"Perfectly elastic\",\"Horizontal\"]]}},{\"h\":\"What makes demand more or less elastic\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide lists four determinants (guide PDF p. 34): the number and closeness of substitutes, the degree of necessity, the proportion of income spent on the good, and time. Substitutes are often the most influential: one brand of petrol has far more elastic demand than petrol as a whole. HL only: these determinants explain why PED for primary commodities such as wheat or copper is generally lower than for manufactured products. Commodities tend to be necessities or essential inputs with few substitutes, while manufactured products compete with many close alternatives.\"},{\"h\":\"PED and total revenue: two effects in competition\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Total revenue (TR) is price × quantity. A price rise has two opposing effects: each unit earns more (the price effect), but fewer units are sold (the quantity effect). PED says which wins. With inelastic demand, quantity falls proportionally less than price rises, so TR rises. With elastic demand, the quantity effect dominates and TR falls. At unit elasticity they cancel. On a diagram, compare the revenue rectangle gained from the higher price with the rectangle lost from lower sales.\",\"because\":[\"Price rises 10% and PED = −1.5\",\"quantity falls about 15%\",\"the quantity effect outweighs the price effect\",\"so total revenue falls\"]},{\"h\":\"Why PED matters for firms and governments\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Firms use PED to predict the revenue effect of a price change and how much of a cost increase they can pass on. PED alone speaks to revenue, not profit: a price rise that lowers revenue can still raise profit if producing fewer units cuts costs by more. Governments use PED when choosing what to tax. Where demand is inelastic, a tax raises substantial revenue but reduces consumption only a little, so raising revenue and changing behaviour pull in different directions. The less elastic demand is relative to supply, the more of a tax’s burden consumers bear (2.7).\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: PED changes along a straight-line demand curve\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A straight line has a constant slope but not a constant elasticity (HL only; guide PDF pp. 33–34). Near the top, price is high and quantity low, so a given price change is a small percentage of price while the quantity change is a large percentage of quantity: demand is elastic. Near the bottom the reverse holds. |PED| = 1 at the midpoint, where TR is at its maximum. Illustrative data: on the line Q = 100 − 10P, a cut from $8 to $7 gives PED = −4; a cut from $3 to $2 gives PED ≈ −0.43.\",\"because\":[\"ΔQ ÷ ΔP is constant on a straight line\",\"PED = (ΔQ ÷ ΔP) × (P ÷ Q)\",\"P ÷ Q is large near the top and small near the bottom\",\"so |PED| falls from above 1, through 1 at the midpoint, to below 1\"]},{\"h\":\"Income elasticity: which goods grow as incomes rise\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"YED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in income. The sign separates normal goods (positive) from inferior goods (negative); “inferior” describes the response, not the quality. The size separates income inelastic demand, between 0 and 1 and typical of necessities, from income elastic demand, above 1 and typical of luxuries and services (guide PDF p. 34). An Engel curve shows the relationship between income and quantity demanded.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"YED\",\"Type of good\",\"Example\"],\"rows\":[[\"Negative\",\"Inferior\",\"Long-distance coach travel for households that switch to rail or cars\"],[\"Between 0 and 1\",\"Normal, income inelastic (necessity)\",\"Basic foods, electricity\"],[\"Greater than 1\",\"Normal, income elastic (luxury or service)\",\"Foreign holidays, restaurant meals\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A higher excise tax on cigarettes\",\"cause\":\"The government raises the tax per pack, and the retail price rises.\",\"mechanism\":\"Short-run demand is price inelastic because of addiction and few close substitutes, so quantity falls proportionally less than price rises.\",\"immediate\":\"Tax revenue rises substantially while consumption falls a little; smokers spend more in total.\",\"secondary\":\"Some buyers turn to cross-border or illicit supply, or to vaping.\",\"longrun\":\"Demand becomes more elastic as fewer young people start, so consumption falls further and the initial gain in tax revenue tends to shrink.\",\"depends\":\"Short- against long-run PED, substitutes (legal and illegal), enforcement, and how regressive the burden is.\"},{\"t\":\"HL only: rising incomes and the structure of the economy\",\"cause\":\"Real incomes grow steadily over several decades.\",\"mechanism\":\"Demand for goods with YED below 1, such as basic foods, grows more slowly than income, while demand for goods and services with YED above 1 grows faster (guide PDF p. 34).\",\"immediate\":\"Firms selling income-elastic products see sales grow fastest and expand.\",\"secondary\":\"Labour and capital move towards faster-growing sectors, often manufacturing first and then services.\",\"longrun\":\"The primary sector’s share of output and employment falls and services’ share rises, a pattern seen in many economies as incomes rise.\",\"depends\":\"The YED values themselves, productivity growth in each sector, trade and relative price changes.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Price elasticity of demand and total revenue\",\"dg\":\"ped\",\"purpose\":\"Measures how strongly quantity demanded responds to price and predicts the effect on total revenue.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Other determinants of demand are held constant\",\"The value applies to a stated price range and time period\",\"Changes are measured from the original values\"],\"mechanism\":\"The balance between the price effect and the quantity effect, summarised by PED, determines the change in total revenue.\",\"prediction\":\"With inelastic demand a price rise raises TR; with elastic demand it lowers TR; at unit elasticity TR is unchanged.\",\"application\":\"Pricing decisions, forecasting tax revenue and consumption effects, and tax incidence in 2.7.\",\"limitations\":[\"Estimates vary with method, place and period\",\"A firm’s PED depends on how rivals respond\",\"Short-run values can mislead long-run policy\",\"Revenue is not profit\"],\"real\":\"Studies of fuel demand generally find it price inelastic in the short run and more elastic over several years as people change vehicles and travel habits.\",\"misconception\":\"Reasoning from the sign rather than the magnitude, or treating a whole straight-line curve as inelastic because it looks steep (MIC-04, teacher-created Misconception Database).\",\"exam\":\"Paper 2 (b): calculate PED and comment on the value; Paper 1: explain and evaluate PED’s importance with the revenue diagram.\",\"deeper\":\"The point formula PED = (ΔQ ÷ ΔP) × (P ÷ Q) explains the HL result: the first term is fixed on a straight line and the second falls moving down it. It also shows why a profit-maximising firm with market power sets price in the elastic range: in the inelastic range it could raise price, sell less, earn more revenue and incur lower costs. That link is developed in 2.11.\"},{\"model\":\"Income elasticity of demand and the Engel curve\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Shows how demand responds to income and which industries grow as economies develop.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Prices and other determinants are held constant\",\"YED is stable over the income range considered\"],\"mechanism\":\"The sign of YED identifies normal or inferior goods; its size separates necessities from luxuries and services.\",\"prediction\":\"As incomes rise, spending shifts towards goods with YED above 1; in a recession, sales of income-elastic products fall fastest.\",\"application\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 34): firms forecast sales over the business cycle; YED helps explain the shift from primary production to manufacturing and services.\",\"limitations\":[\"YED varies with income level: one household’s luxury is a richer household’s necessity\",\"New products and changing tastes shift Engel curves\",\"Trade lets production differ from domestic consumption\"],\"real\":\"The share of household budgets spent on food tends to fall as incomes rise, a regularity known as Engel’s law, observed across countries and over time.\",\"misconception\":\"Believing an inferior good is poor quality, or muddling the sign of YED (MIC-06, teacher-created Misconception Database).\",\"exam\":\"Paper 2 (b): calculate YED and classify the good; HL: use YED to explain structural change or commodity dependence (4.9).\",\"deeper\":\"With income on the vertical axis, a straight Engel curve from the origin shows YED = 1, a necessity’s curve steepens as income rises, a luxury’s flattens and an inferior good’s bends backwards; some books swap the axes, so label clearly. The development link is powerful but contested in detail: slowly growing world demand for primary products can hold back commodity exporters, although demand from fast-growing economies has at times reversed that pattern.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"PED depends on substitutes, necessity, share of income and time, and inelastic demand makes price rises raise revenue.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies generally find demand for fuel, tobacco and basic foods price inelastic in the short run and more elastic over longer periods. Estimates for the same good vary widely across countries, periods and methods.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists treat elasticities as stable enough to guide tax policy; others stress that they shift with market definition, time and rivals’ responses. They agree on direction, not on how much weight a precise number can bear.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Where do elasticity numbers come from?\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Observed prices and quantities are intersections of demand and supply, and both curves shift, so a scatter of points does not trace out a demand curve. To estimate PED, economists need something that moves supply without moving demand, such as weather affecting a harvest, or they use tax changes and randomised price tests. Different methods and periods can give very different values for the same good. Before relying on a published estimate, ask what market, period and price range it covers and how demand was identified.\"},{\"h\":\"Short run, long run and habit\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Many responses take time. After a fuel price rise most drivers can at first only cut some trips; over years they can buy efficient vehicles, move or change how they travel. Habit and addiction slow the response, and expectations matter: a rise believed temporary provokes less adjustment than one believed permanent. Using a short-run elasticity to judge a long-term tax tends to understate how far consumption will eventually fall and to overstate long-run revenue.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Suppose your local café raises the price of a coffee by 10 per cent. If three other cafés share the street, many customers walk next door and sales fall sharply: demand is price elastic. If it is the only café in a hospital, most people pay up: demand is price inelastic. Elasticity measures that difference in percentages, and it explains why a price rise brings in more money for one seller and less for another. Income elasticity asks a similar question about income: when people get richer, do they buy much more of something, a little more, or less?\",\"exam\":\"Define PED or YED precisely, then show each calculation step: both percentage changes, the formula and the answer with its sign. Classify the value and comment on what it means in context, including the period it covers. For revenue, draw the demand curve with the two revenue rectangles and explain the price effect against the quantity effect. When evaluating PED’s importance, go beyond description: estimates are uncertain and change over time, rivals react, and revenue is not profit. HL answers should show changing PED along a straight-line curve and use primary-commodity and YED arguments where the question allows (guide PDF pp. 33–34).\",\"deep\":\"Elasticity is a local property. It belongs to a market definition, a price range and a time period, not to a good in the abstract. That is why one straight-line demand curve contains every degree of elasticity, why demand for petrol is inelastic while demand for one brand is elastic, and why short-run and long-run estimates differ. The elasticity of a whole commodity market also differs from that facing one producer: a farmer selling a standard crop at the going price faces nearly perfectly elastic demand, even if market demand is inelastic. Income elasticity adds a dynamic view. As incomes grow, the composition of demand changes and pulls resources between sectors, which is part of the long-run story of development, alongside productivity change and trade.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"PED summarises how readily buyers can choose alternatives when price rises, which is why substitutes are central.\",\"tension\":\"Taxing goods with few alternatives raises revenue but burdens people who cannot easily switch.\",\"ia\":\"An article about a firm’s price rise, analysed through the choices open to its buyers.\"},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Elasticities change with time, and YED explains how the mix of goods and industries changes as incomes grow.\",\"tension\":\"A policy judged on short-run elasticity may look very different once buyers adjust.\",\"ia\":\"An article about spending shifting as incomes rise or fall, framed through YED.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"PED decides whether an indirect tax mainly raises revenue or mainly changes behaviour, and who bears it.\",\"tension\":\"The more inelastic demand is, the more a tax raises and the less it changes consumption.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a tax on tobacco, sugar or fuel, weighing revenue against behaviour change.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do governments tax fuel, tobacco and alcohol so heavily rather than, say, restaurant meals?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect governments to spread taxes evenly, or to tax luxuries such as restaurant meals hardest.\",\"model\":\"Where demand is inelastic, a tax raises price with only a small fall in quantity, so revenue is large and fairly predictable.\",\"evidence\":\"Demand for fuel and tobacco is generally estimated to be inelastic in the short run, less so over time; both also carry external costs.\",\"match\":\"Largely in the short run. Over time demand becomes more elastic, and cross-border or illicit trade can make demand for taxed supply more elastic than for the good itself.\",\"assume\":\"Ceteris paribus and the time period of the estimate matter most.\",\"change\":\"Elastic long-run demand would mean more behaviour change and less revenue than planned.\",\"policy\":\"Inelastic demand suits revenue while elastic demand suits behaviour change, and the regressive burden on low-income households needs weighing.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a price cut can raise a firm’s total revenue for one product but reduce it for another.\",\"check\":[\"Defines PED and TR = P × Q\",\"Identifies the price effect and the quantity effect\",\"With elastic demand, quantity rises proportionally more than price falls, so TR rises\",\"With inelastic demand, TR falls\",\"Links the difference to a determinant such as substitutes\",\"Refers to the revenue rectangles on a diagram\"]},{\"prompt\":\"HL only: explain why PED changes along a straight-line demand curve.\",\"check\":[\"The slope is constant but elasticity is not\",\"Uses PED = (ΔQ ÷ ΔP) × (P ÷ Q) or percentage reasoning\",\"P ÷ Q is high near the top and low near the bottom\",\"Identifies elastic, unit elastic and inelastic ranges\",\"Links the midpoint to maximum TR\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the formula for PED.\",\"a\":\"PED = percentage change in quantity demanded ÷ percentage change in price.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: price rises from $2.00 to $2.50 and quantity demanded falls from 400 to 340. Calculate PED.\",\"a\":\"%ΔP = +25%, %ΔQd = −15%, so PED = −0.6: price inelastic.\"},{\"q\":\"Demand is price elastic and price rises. What happens to total revenue?\",\"a\":\"It falls: quantity falls proportionally more than price rises.\"},{\"q\":\"Income rises 5% and quantity demanded rises 12%. Calculate YED and classify the good.\",\"a\":\"YED = 2.4: normal and income elastic, such as a luxury or service.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a negative YED show?\",\"a\":\"An inferior good: quantity demanded falls as income rises. It says nothing about quality.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: why is PED for primary commodities generally lower than for manufactured products?\",\"a\":\"Many are necessities or essential inputs with few substitutes; manufactured products face many close substitutes.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Two demand curves are drawn on separate diagrams. Curve A looks steeper than curve B. What can you conclude about their price elasticities?\",\"opts\":[\"A is less elastic than B at every price\",\"Nothing reliable without the percentage changes at the relevant prices\",\"A is perfectly inelastic\",\"B is unit elastic\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Slope depends on the units and scales chosen, while PED compares percentage changes, and PED varies even along one straight line. The first option is the tempting error of reading elasticity from steepness.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-04\"},{\"q\":\"A cinema faces PED = −1.8 and raises ticket prices by 10%. What happens to its total revenue?\",\"opts\":[\"It rises, because each ticket earns more\",\"It falls, because quantity falls by about 18%\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It rises by 18%\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"With elastic demand the quantity effect outweighs the price effect: 1.10 × 0.82 ≈ 0.90, so revenue falls by roughly a tenth. The first option ignores the quantity effect entirely.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-05\"},{\"q\":\"When average incomes rise by 6%, demand for long-distance coach travel falls by 3%. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"It is a normal good with income inelastic demand\",\"It is an inferior good with YED = −0.5\",\"It is a luxury with YED = 2\",\"Its demand is price elastic\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"YED = −3 ÷ 6 = −0.5; the negative sign marks an inferior good, which describes the response, not the quality of service. YED says nothing about PED, so the last option does not follow.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-06\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (b): calculate PED or YED and comment on the value\",\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): explain, with a diagram, a price change’s effect on revenue\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate PED’s importance for firms or governments\",\"HL only: PED along a straight line, primary commodities, YED and sectoral change (guide PDF pp. 33–34)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"calculate\",\"comment\",\"explain\",\"draw\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"Elastic and inelastic demand; the constant-PED cases; revenue gained and lost; an Engel curve with labelled axes. HL only: PED ranges on a straight-line demand curve.\",\"calc\":\"PED and YED from data, and a change in price, quantity demanded, income or total revenue (guide PDF p. 34). Keep the sign, show both percentage changes and measure from the original values unless told otherwise. Paper 1 allows no calculator (guide PDF p. 66).\",\"apply\":\"Use the text’s figures and tie the value to a determinant in context, such as the substitutes these buyers have.\",\"eval\":\"Consider the estimate’s reliability, its time period, rivals’ or smugglers’ responses, revenue against profit, and who bears a tax.\",\"errors\":[\"Reasoning from the sign rather than the magnitude\",\"Inferring elasticity from steepness\",\"Recommending a price rise for revenue without checking PED\",\"Calling an inferior good low quality\"],\"strong\":[\"Show every calculation step and interpret the number in context\",\"Explain revenue through price and quantity effects with rectangles shaded\",\"Attach a time period and price range to any elasticity claim\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: when a gym raised its monthly fee from $40 to $46, membership fell from 2,000 to 1,760. Calculate the PED for membership and comment on what the value means for the gym’s revenue.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that taxing goods with price inelastic demand is the most effective way for a government to raise revenue.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Illustrative data: a government estimates PED for sugary drinks at −0.6 in the short run and −1.1 in the long run. Using both estimates, recommend whether a sugary-drinks tax is better justified as a way to raise revenue or to cut consumption.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"why\":\"PES applies the same percentage method to supply; together PED and PES decide how a shock splits between price and quantity.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"The revenue from an indirect tax and its incidence depend on PED.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"why\":\"HL: a firm with market power sets price in the elastic range of its demand curve.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"why\":\"Low PED and YED for primary commodities help explain unstable, slow-growing export earnings in commodity-dependent economies.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"why\":\"Because PES applies the same method to supply, and the effect of any shock depends on both elasticities together.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a firm changing a price, a new indirect tax, or spending shifting as incomes change. Choice, change or intervention could frame it; use the article’s figures to discuss PED or YED rather than asserting them.\",\"ee\":\"How price elastic is demand for a specific local service, such as bus travel after a fare change, and how does the estimate compare with published ranges once other changes are accounted for?\",\"tok\":\"Can an elasticity estimated from past data count as knowledge about how people will respond in future, when the conditions behind it keep changing?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students calculate and interpret PED and YED and explain the PED–revenue relationship.\",\"starter\":\"“If your favourite café raised prices by 10%, would you still go? What if it were the only café for a mile?”\",\"activity\":\"Groups calculate TR and PED at several prices on a straight-line demand schedule and find where TR peaks; HL groups link the peak to the midpoint.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a government tax goods with inelastic demand, knowing the burden falls on consumers who cannot switch?\",\"check\":\"Three quick calculations: PED from data, a missing quantity from a given PED, and YED with classification.\",\"extension\":\"Explain why two published PED estimates for one good differ.\",\"homework\":\"Comment on a PED calculated from illustrative data, naming its time period and one limitation.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define PED and YED and state their formulas.\",\"I can calculate PED and YED and find a missing value from a given elasticity.\",\"I can explain the determinants of PED and the PED–revenue relationship with a diagram.\",\"I can draw the constant-PED cases and an Engel curve.\",\"HL only: I can explain changing PED along a straight line and low PED for primary commodities.\",\"HL only: I can use YED to explain firms’ decisions and sectoral change.\",\"I can evaluate PED’s usefulness for firms and governments without notes.\"]},\"2.6\":{\"code\":\"2.6\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Price elasticity of supply (PES) measures how readily producers change the quantity they supply when price changes. It depends mostly on time and flexibility: spare capacity, the ability to store output, how easily resources move in, and how fast costs rise as output grows. When supply is inelastic, a rise in demand shows up mainly as a higher price rather than more output.\",\"why\":\"PES tells a government whether a subsidy will raise output or mainly producers’ incomes, and tells a crop or metal exporter why its prices swing so much.\",\"q\":\"Why can the price of onions or coffee rise steeply within weeks, while the price of phone chargers barely moves when demand jumps?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The law of supply and what shifts the supply curve\",\"sub\":\"2.2\"},{\"t\":\"The PED formula and measuring responsiveness in percentages\",\"sub\":\"2.5\"},{\"t\":\"Calculating a percentage change\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"How a demand shift moves price and quantity along the supply curve\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define PES and state its formula\",\"List the five determinants of PES\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why PES tends to rise as the time period lengthens\",\"Explain why any straight-line supply curve through the origin has PES = 1\"],\"apply\":[\"Use PES to predict whether a rise in demand will mainly raise price or output in a stated market\"],\"analyse\":[\"HL only: explain why PES for primary commodities is generally lower than for manufactured products\",\"Analyse how low PED and low PES together make commodity prices volatile\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the claim that a subsidy will raise output, given the market’s PES\",\"Evaluate how far governments can make supply more elastic\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate PES from data, and a missing change in price or quantity supplied from a given PES\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw relatively elastic and inelastic supply, and perfectly elastic, perfectly inelastic and unit elastic supply\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a PES value in context, stating the time period it applies to\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"What PES measures\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"PES = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price. Because price and quantity supplied move in the same direction, PES is positive. Supply is price elastic if PES > 1, price inelastic if PES < 1 and unit elastic if PES = 1. The theoretical range runs from 0, perfectly inelastic, where quantity supplied cannot change, to infinity, perfectly elastic, where firms supply any quantity at the going price but none below it.\",\"because\":[\"Price rises 20%\",\"quantity supplied rises 8%\",\"PES = 8 ÷ 20 = 0.4\",\"so supply is price inelastic: output responds less than proportionally\"]},{\"h\":\"Reading PES from a straight line\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The constant-PES cases are benchmarks to draw (guide PDF p. 34). The unit elastic case surprises many students: any straight-line supply curve through the origin has PES = 1 at every point, whatever its slope, because quantity changes in the same proportion as price. A straight line meeting the price axis above the origin is elastic throughout; one meeting the quantity axis is inelastic throughout. Steepness alone is not a reliable guide.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Straight-line supply curve\",\"PES\"],\"rows\":[[\"Vertical\",\"0\"],[\"Meeting the quantity axis\",\"Less than 1\"],[\"Through the origin, any slope\",\"1 at every point\"],[\"Meeting the price axis above the origin\",\"Greater than 1\"],[\"Horizontal\",\"∞\"]]}},{\"h\":\"What makes supply more or less elastic\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide names five determinants (guide PDF p. 34). Supply is more elastic when firms have longer to adjust; when factors of production can move easily into the industry; when firms have unused capacity, such as idle machines or a spare shift; when output can be stored and released as price rises; and when costs rise only slowly as output expands. If each extra unit is much dearer to produce, a higher price calls forth only a little more output.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Determinant\",\"Example of elastic supply\"],\"rows\":[[\"Time\",\"New plants can be built over years\"],[\"Factor mobility\",\"Programmers switching between projects\"],[\"Unused capacity\",\"A bakery running one shift of three\"],[\"Ability to store\",\"Grain held in silos\"],[\"Slowly rising costs\",\"Mass-produced goods using standard inputs\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Time: the determinant behind the others\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Economists often separate three periods. In the momentary period output cannot change, so supply is perfectly inelastic apart from any stocks that can be released. In the short run, firms can vary some inputs, such as hours and materials, but not the size of their plant. In the long run all inputs can vary and new firms can enter, so supply is most elastic. Capacity, factor mobility and storage all look different after a year than after a week.\",\"because\":[\"Price rises today\",\"momentarily, only existing stock can be sold\",\"in the short run firms add shifts and buy more materials\",\"in the long run they build capacity and new firms enter\"]},{\"h\":\"HL only: why primary commodities have low PES\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"HL students explain why PES for primary commodities is generally lower than for manufactured products (guide PDF p. 34). Crops follow growing seasons and cannot be harvested early; tree crops such as coffee or cocoa take several years to bear fruit. Land is fixed in supply, and new mines need long exploration and construction. Many foods are perishable, limiting storage. Manufacturers can often add shifts, use spare capacity and hold stocks of finished goods.\",\"because\":[\"A commodity’s price rises\",\"farmers must wait for the next season\",\"new trees or mines take years\",\"perishable output cannot be held back\",\"so quantity supplied barely responds in the short run\"]},{\"h\":\"Low PES plus low PED: volatile prices\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"When both demand and supply are price inelastic, a small shift in either curve causes a large price change, because quantities adjust very little. Primary commodity markets often combine both. In 2021 frost in Brazil damaged coffee trees and world coffee prices rose steeply; replacing trees takes years, so supply could not respond quickly. For economies relying on a few commodity exports, such swings make export earnings and farm incomes unstable, a theme developed in 4.9.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Frost damages coffee trees in a major producing country\",\"cause\":\"Frost destroys part of the coming harvest and damages trees, shifting world supply left.\",\"mechanism\":\"With inelastic demand and little short-run capacity to grow more elsewhere, the shortage is cleared mainly by a large price rise.\",\"immediate\":\"World prices rise steeply; drawing down warehouse stocks softens the rise.\",\"secondary\":\"Farmers elsewhere tend trees more intensively and plant more; roasters change blends.\",\"longrun\":\"New trees add supply only after several years, and if many farmers plant at once, prices may later fall sharply.\",\"depends\":\"Stocks, PED for coffee, planting lags and farmers’ expectations of future prices.\"},{\"t\":\"A surge in demand for a manufactured good\",\"cause\":\"Demand for bicycles rises sharply, for example when many people avoid public transport.\",\"mechanism\":\"Producers with unused capacity and parts in stock raise output quickly, so supply is fairly elastic and price rises only a little.\",\"immediate\":\"Output rises and retailers sell from stock.\",\"secondary\":\"Once capacity is full or component suppliers cannot keep up, supply becomes much less elastic: waiting lists lengthen and prices rise more.\",\"longrun\":\"New capacity and suppliers make supply more elastic again; if demand falls back, firms may be left with excess stock.\",\"depends\":\"Spare capacity, the component supply chain, factor mobility and whether firms expect the surge to last.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Price elasticity of supply and its determinants\",\"dg\":\"pes\",\"purpose\":\"Measures how readily output responds to price and predicts how a demand shock splits between price and output.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Other determinants of supply are held constant\",\"The value applies to a stated time period and price range\",\"Firms respond to price as profit-seekers\"],\"mechanism\":\"Time, spare capacity, storage, factor mobility and the rate at which costs rise determine how far output can respond to a price change.\",\"prediction\":\"With inelastic supply a rise in demand mainly raises price; with elastic supply it mainly raises output. PES rises with time.\",\"application\":\"Explaining price spikes, the output effects of subsidies, and tax incidence alongside PED (2.7).\",\"limitations\":[\"PES can change along one curve, falling sharply near full capacity\",\"Firms may not expand if they expect a price rise to be temporary\",\"Estimates are hard to separate from simultaneous demand shifts\"],\"real\":\"Where land is scarce and planning rules tight, housing supply tends to respond slowly, so rising demand shows up mainly as higher prices.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating PES as fixed rather than time-dependent (MIC-07, teacher-created Misconception Database), or inverting the formula.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 2 (b): calculate PES and explain a determinant from the text; Paper 1 (a): explain, with a diagram, why supply of a named product is inelastic.\",\"deeper\":\"An industry’s supply can be elastic at low output, where spare capacity is plentiful, and nearly vertical near full capacity. The same shape reappears in macroeconomics, where some views of aggregate supply are flat with plenty of spare capacity and steep near full employment (3.2). It explains why a subsidy or a demand stimulus can have very different effects depending on where a market starts.\"},{\"model\":\"HL only: PES of primary commodities and manufactured products\",\"dg\":\"pes\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why supply of crops, metals and energy responds slowly to price, and why their prices are volatile.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Biology and geology set production lags\",\"Storage is limited or costly for many primary products\",\"Manufacturers can vary shifts, capacity and stocks more easily\"],\"mechanism\":\"Growing seasons, fixed land, long mine lead times and perishability keep short-run commodity supply inelastic.\",\"prediction\":\"Shocks cause larger price changes for primary commodities than for most manufactured products, especially in the short run.\",\"application\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 34): commodity price volatility, unstable export earnings (4.9) and the case for storage or buffer stocks.\",\"limitations\":[\"Storable commodities such as grain or metals have higher short-run PES\",\"Some manufactured goods face bottlenecks, such as specialised components\",\"Over long periods commodity supply can respond strongly, even overshoot\"],\"real\":\"India’s onion prices rose sharply in 2019 after heavy rain damaged the crop, and the government restricted exports; onions are perishable and a new crop takes months to grow.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating all primary commodities alike or ignoring storage: grain in silos can be released far faster than fresh vegetables.\",\"exam\":\"HL only: explain low commodity PES with a diagram showing a supply shift against inelastic demand, then link to commodity dependence (4.9).\",\"deeper\":\"Production lags can generate price cycles. In the cobweb model, farmers plant according to this season’s price: a high price leads to a large harvest next season, which drives the price down, which leads to less planting. Whether the cycle dies out depends on the relative elasticities of demand and supply. Real markets are less mechanical because farmers form expectations and traders hold stocks, but boom-and-bust patterns in some crops fit the idea.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Supply becomes more elastic over time and is less elastic for primary commodities, so their prices move more after shocks.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of agricultural supply generally find small responses within a season and larger ones over years. Commodity prices are, on most measures, more volatile than manufactured-goods prices, and housing supply appears less elastic where land is scarce or planning is tight.\",\"interp\":\"Economists broadly agree on the time pattern but disagree about causes in some markets: natural limits such as land and climate, or rules such as planning and licensing that could change. The answer shapes whether policy should target supply itself or its consequences.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Is inelastic supply natural or made?\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"Some limits on supply come from nature: land area, growing seasons, the geology of ore deposits. Others come from rules. Planning restrictions can make housing supply inelastic where building is physically easy, and licensing can slow the supply response of taxis or some professions. Where the limit is institutional, a government can in principle raise PES, but the rules often exist for reasons such as safety or environmental protection. Evaluating a policy that relies on a supply response means asking what holds supply back and whether that constraint should be relaxed.\"},{\"h\":\"Expectations and the supply response\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Firms respond to the prices they expect, not only today’s. If producers believe a price rise is temporary, they may not invest in capacity, so measured PES stays low even over a longer period. If they expect it to last, they may expand before prices peak. Producers who have seen past booms end in gluts may be cautious. This is one reason estimated supply elasticities vary so widely: the same price change can carry different signals depending on what producers believe.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine the price of strawberries doubles overnight. A farmer cannot grow more by tomorrow; the strawberries in the field are all there are, so supply barely responds. Now imagine demand for phone cases doubles. A factory with spare machines can run an extra shift next week. That difference is price elasticity of supply. It depends on how much time producers have, whether they can store goods, whether they have spare capacity and how easily they can bring in workers and machines. When supply cannot respond, a rise in demand shows up mainly as a higher price.\",\"exam\":\"Define PES and write the formula the right way up: percentage change in quantity supplied over percentage change in price. Show both percentage changes and note that the result is positive. Classify the value and link it to a determinant in the text, such as spare capacity or storage. Draw relatively elastic and inelastic supply, or the constant cases, with labelled axes; a straight line through the origin is unit elastic. In evaluation, stress time: a short-run value may understate the long-run response. HL answers should explain why primary commodities have low PES and connect this, with low PED, to price volatility (guide PDF p. 34).\",\"deep\":\"PES is a statement about the costs and constraints of expanding output. A firm expands when a higher price covers the extra cost of producing more, so PES reflects how fast marginal cost rises with output. Spare capacity, mobile inputs and stocks keep that cost from rising quickly; fixed land, specialised equipment and long production lags make it rise steeply. Time loosens every constraint, which is why long-run supply is more elastic. The idea reaches well beyond single markets. It explains why commodity prices swing more than manufactured prices, why subsidies to industries near capacity raise the price producers receive more than output, and why producers bear more of a tax when supply is less elastic than demand. It carries into macroeconomics too, where the elasticity of aggregate supply shapes whether higher spending raises output or prices.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"PES describes how producers respond to change, and the response grows with time.\",\"tension\":\"Short-run rigidity can mean painful price spikes before the long-run supply response arrives.\",\"ia\":\"An article about a price spike after a demand surge or supply shock, analysed through the time producers need to respond.\"},{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"here\":\"Low PES means scarcity cannot quickly be relieved by more output, so price does most of the rationing.\",\"tension\":\"Price rationing of an inelastically supplied staple, such as a basic food, hurts low-income households most.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a shortage of a staple food or housing, examining what limits the supply response.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do prices of some crops swing far more than prices of manufactured goods?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect prices to follow costs, and farming costs do not usually change that fast.\",\"model\":\"Low short-run PES, with low PED, means a shift in either curve is absorbed mainly by price rather than quantity.\",\"evidence\":\"Commodity prices are generally found to be more volatile than manufactured-goods prices, and farm supply responses are small within a season.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes, though storage, trade and expectations complicate it: storable commodities tend to be less volatile than perishable ones.\",\"assume\":\"Production lags and limited storage matter most.\",\"change\":\"If cheap storage or fast trade made short-run supply more elastic, volatility should fall; evidence that swings were mainly speculative would change the explanation.\",\"policy\":\"Options include storage investment, crop insurance, export diversification and export restrictions during shortages, each with costs for some stakeholders.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why PES tends to be higher in the long run than in the short run.\",\"check\":[\"Defines PES and states the formula\",\"Describes the momentary period with fixed output\",\"Short run: some inputs vary but capacity is fixed\",\"Long run: all inputs vary and firms can enter\",\"Links time to capacity and factor mobility\",\"Uses an example\"]},{\"prompt\":\"HL only: explain why PES for primary commodities is generally lower than for manufactured products.\",\"check\":[\"Defines primary commodities and PES\",\"Explains production lags from seasons or mine development\",\"Refers to fixed land or specialised resources\",\"Explains storage limits for perishable goods\",\"Contrasts manufacturing’s spare capacity, extra shifts and stocks\",\"Links low PES to price volatility\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the formula for PES.\",\"a\":\"PES = percentage change in quantity supplied ÷ percentage change in price.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is PES positive?\",\"a\":\"Price and quantity supplied move in the same direction along a supply curve.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the PES of a straight-line supply curve through the origin?\",\"a\":\"1 at every point, whatever its slope.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: price rises 10% and quantity supplied rises 25%. Calculate and classify PES.\",\"a\":\"PES = 2.5: price elastic supply.\"},{\"q\":\"Name three determinants of PES.\",\"a\":\"Any three of: time, factor mobility, unused capacity, ability to store, the rate at which costs rise.\"},{\"q\":\"Why might a subsidy do little to raise output where supply is very inelastic?\",\"a\":\"Output cannot expand much, so most of the subsidy raises producers’ incomes instead.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Which statement about PES is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"A product’s PES is fixed once estimated\",\"PES tends to rise as producers have more time to adjust\",\"PES is highest in the momentary period\",\"PES depends only on the slope of the supply curve\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Over time firms can hire, invest and enter, so supply becomes more elastic. The first option treats PES as a fixed property of a good; the momentary period has the lowest PES, not the highest.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-07\"},{\"q\":\"Two straight-line supply curves both start at the origin; one is much steeper. What are their PES values?\",\"opts\":[\"The steeper curve has the lower PES\",\"Both have PES = 1 at every point\",\"The steeper curve has PES = 0\",\"The flatter curve has PES greater than 1\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"On any straight line through the origin, quantity is proportional to price, so each percentage change in price brings the same percentage change in quantity. Judging elasticity by steepness is the tempting mistake.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the wheat price rises from $200 to $250 a tonne and quantity supplied rises from 50 to 55 million tonnes. What is PES?\",\"opts\":[\"2.5\",\"0.4\",\"0.1\",\"4\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"%ΔQs = 5 ÷ 50 = 10% and %ΔP = 50 ÷ 200 = 25%, so PES = 10 ÷ 25 = 0.4. The answer 2.5 inverts the formula, putting the price change on top.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (b): calculate PES from data and explain a determinant using the text\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with a diagram, why supply of a product is price inelastic\",\"HL only: explain why primary commodities have lower PES than manufactured products (guide PDF p. 34)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"calculate\",\"explain\",\"draw\",\"comment\",\"identify\"],\"dg\":\"Relatively elastic and inelastic supply, and the three constant cases (a straight line through the origin is unit elastic). For volatility, a shift against inelastic curves showing a large price change and a small quantity change.\",\"calc\":\"PES, and a change in price or quantity supplied, from data (guide PDF p. 34). Quantity change goes on top; show both percentage changes; the answer is positive.\",\"apply\":\"Identify from the text what limits or allows the supply response: harvest timing, storage, spare capacity or skilled labour.\",\"eval\":\"Stress the time horizon, producers’ expectations, whether constraints are natural or institutional, and who gains or loses when prices swing.\",\"errors\":[\"Inverting the PES formula\",\"Treating PES as fixed and ignoring time\",\"Judging elasticity by steepness\",\"Ignoring storage and spare capacity\"],\"strong\":[\"Name the time period a PES value refers to\",\"Link each determinant to the product in the text\",\"Combine PED and PES to explain volatility\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: when the price of fresh tomatoes rose from $1.60 to $2.00 per kilo, quantity supplied to a city market rose from 12,000 to 12,600 kilos a week. Calculate the PES and explain one reason why the value is low.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a diagram, explain why the price of a perishable crop may rise sharply after a poor harvest.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Illustrative data: a country earns most of its export revenue from cocoa, whose world price has varied by more than 40% between years. Recommend one policy to reduce the effect of this volatility on farmers’ incomes, referring to PED and PES.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"PES uses the same percentage method as PED; together they decide how a shock splits between price and quantity.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Tax and subsidy incidence and the effects of price controls depend on PES as well as PED.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"Supply that is elastic with spare capacity and inelastic near full capacity reappears in the shape of aggregate supply.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"why\":\"Low PES and PED for primary commodities help explain volatile export earnings in commodity-dependent economies.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Because the effects of taxes, subsidies and price controls on price, output and each stakeholder depend on PED and PES together.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a sudden price rise for a crop, metal, housing or a manufactured good in short supply. Change or scarcity could frame it; identify the determinant limiting the supply response in that market.\",\"ee\":\"How has investment in storage or irrigation changed the price elasticity of supply of one crop in one region, judged from several seasons of price, planting and harvest records?\",\"tok\":\"Economists say supply is inelastic “in the short run”. Is a claim that depends so heavily on the chosen time period a genuine piece of knowledge about the world?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students calculate PES, explain its determinants and link low PES to price volatility.\",\"starter\":\"If demand for strawberries and for phone cases both doubled tomorrow, which price would rise more, and why?\",\"activity\":\"Students place ten products (fresh fish, gold, software, housing, canned soup and others) on a PES spectrum for the short run and again for the long run, justifying each with a named determinant.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a government restrict food exports when domestic prices spike, given the effect on importing countries?\",\"check\":\"Two calculations (PES from data, a missing quantity) and one sentence naming the determinant in a scenario.\",\"extension\":\"HL: draw the same supply shock against inelastic and against elastic curves and compare the price changes.\",\"homework\":\"Explain, with a diagram, why a perishable crop’s price can move sharply after a poor harvest.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define PES and state its formula.\",\"I can calculate PES and find a missing change in price or quantity supplied.\",\"I can explain the five determinants of PES with examples.\",\"I can draw the constant-PES cases and explain why a line through the origin is unit elastic.\",\"I can explain why PES rises as the time period lengthens.\",\"HL only: I can explain why primary commodities have lower PES than manufactured products.\",\"I can explain how low PED and PES together make prices volatile and link this to 2.7 and 4.9.\",\"I can explain PES without notes.\"]},\"2.7\":{\"code\":\"2.7\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Governments step into markets to raise revenue, support firms or low-income households, change how much is produced or consumed, correct market failure and promote equity. Their tools range from price controls, indirect taxes and subsidies to direct provision, regulation and, at HL, consumer nudges. Each tool changes the price or quantity that buyers and sellers face, and who gains or loses depends on elasticity, on whether a price control binds and on how people adjust over time.\",\"why\":\"Every intervention creates winners and losers. A government that misjudges who bears a tax, who is left out by a shortage or what a subsidy costs taxpayers may end up harming the people it meant to help.\",\"q\":\"When a government taxes petrol, who really pays: drivers, fuel companies, or both?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Movements along and shifts of the supply curve, including indirect taxes and subsidies as non-price determinants of supply\",\"sub\":\"2.2\"},{\"t\":\"Equilibrium, excess demand (shortage) and excess supply (surplus)\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Consumer surplus, producer surplus and allocative efficiency at equilibrium\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Price elasticity of demand and its determinants\",\"sub\":\"2.5\"},{\"t\":\"Price elasticity of supply and why it rises over time\",\"sub\":\"2.6\"},{\"t\":\"Areas of rectangles and triangles, for the HL calculations\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the seven reasons the guide gives for government intervention in markets (guide PDF p. 35).\",\"Define a price ceiling, a price floor, a specific tax, an ad valorem tax and a per-unit subsidy.\",\"Describe direct provision, command-and-control regulation and legislation, and (HL only) consumer nudges as forms of intervention.\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a price ceiling has an effect only when it is set below the equilibrium price, and a price floor only when it is set above it.\",\"Explain why the burden of an indirect tax falls mostly on whichever side of the market has the less elastic curve.\"],\"apply\":[\"Apply the ceiling, floor, tax and subsidy diagrams to a named market such as rented housing, cereals, fuel or tobacco.\",\"Identify the stakeholders in a news article about an intervention and state how each is affected.\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a binding price ceiling leads to non-price rationing, parallel markets and a possible fall in quality.\",\"Analyse why a minimum price for a crop can leave the government holding stocks that must be stored, destroyed or sold abroad.\",\"Analyse how the effects of a price control change between the short run and the long run as supply becomes more elastic.\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate an intervention against its stated aim, weighing effectiveness, cost to government, unintended consequences and equity.\",\"Evaluate whether a tax or subsidy would meet an aim better than regulation, direct provision or (HL only) a nudge.\"],\"calculate\":[\"HL only (guide PDF p. 36): calculate the shortage or surplus, consumer spending, producer revenue and government spending under a price ceiling or price floor.\",\"HL only (guide PDF p. 36): calculate tax revenue, subsidy cost, the consumer and producer shares of a tax or subsidy, and the welfare loss.\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a binding price ceiling and a binding price floor, labelling Pe, Pmax or Pmin, Qd, Qs and the shortage or surplus.\",\"Draw a specific tax (parallel shift), an ad valorem tax (pivoting shift) and a per-unit subsidy, labelling Pc, Pe, Pp, Qe, Q₁, revenue or spending, and the welfare loss.\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read the consumer burden, producer burden, government revenue and welfare loss from a labelled tax diagram.\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL Paper 3: recommend an intervention for a stated market problem and justify it with evidence from the data provided.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Seven reasons to intervene\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide lists seven aims for intervention in markets (guide PDF p. 35): earning government revenue, supporting firms, supporting households on low incomes, influencing the level of production, influencing the level of consumption, correcting market failure and promoting equity. One tool can serve several aims, and aims can clash. A fuel subsidy supports low-income households but also encourages the use of a good that pollutes. A tobacco tax raises most revenue when demand is price inelastic, which is exactly when it does least to cut smoking. Good evaluation starts by asking which aim a policy is meant to serve.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Aim\",\"Typical tools\",\"Illustration\"],\"rows\":[[\"Earn revenue\",\"Indirect taxes on goods with inelastic demand\",\"Excise duties on fuel\"],[\"Support firms\",\"Subsidies, price floors\",\"Minimum support prices for crops\"],[\"Support low-income households\",\"Price ceilings, subsidies, direct provision\",\"Subsidised staple foods\"],[\"Influence production\",\"Subsidies to expand it; taxes or regulation to limit it\",\"Grants for renewable energy\"],[\"Influence consumption\",\"Taxes, regulation, nudges (HL only)\",\"Age limits on tobacco sales\"],[\"Correct market failure\",\"Taxes, subsidies, regulation, provision\",\"Studied in 2.8 and 2.9\"],[\"Promote equity\",\"Direct provision, price controls, subsidies\",\"Free primary education\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Price controls: only a binding control changes anything\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A price ceiling (maximum price) is a legal limit on how high a price may go; to bind, it must sit below the equilibrium price. A price floor (minimum price) is a legal limit on how low a price may go; to bind, it must sit above equilibrium. Either way the market cannot clear, and trade takes place on the short side of the market: at Qs under a ceiling and at Qd under a floor. A ceiling leaves a shortage that must be rationed by queues, waiting lists, favouritism or parallel markets. A floor leaves a surplus that goes unsold unless the government buys it.\",\"because\":[\"The ceiling Pmax is set below Pe\",\"Quantity demanded rises to Qd and quantity supplied falls to Qs\",\"The shortage is Qd − Qs, and only Qs is traded\",\"Price can no longer ration, so queues, luck or connections decide who buys\",\"Some buyers end up paying more, in time or on a parallel market, than the legal price suggests\"],\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Price ceiling\",\"Price floor\"],\"rows\":[[\"Set\",\"Below equilibrium\",\"Above equilibrium\"],[\"Creates\",\"Shortage (Qd > Qs)\",\"Surplus (Qs > Qd)\"],[\"Quantity traded\",\"Qs, below Qe\",\"Qd, below Qe, unless the government buys the surplus\"],[\"Typical aim\",\"Help consumers, often low-income ones\",\"Help producers or workers\"],[\"Side effects\",\"Non-price rationing, parallel markets, lower quality\",\"Unsold stocks, storage costs, protection of inefficient producers\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Specific and ad valorem taxes\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services, collected from firms. A specific tax is a fixed amount per unit, such as a set sum per litre of fuel. An ad valorem tax is a percentage of the price, such as a sales tax or value added tax (VAT). Both raise firms’ costs, so the supply curve shifts vertically upward. A specific tax gives a parallel shift because the tax per unit is the same at every price. An ad valorem tax gives a pivoting shift, with the gap widening at higher prices, because a fixed percentage of a larger price is a larger sum.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Specific tax\",\"Ad valorem tax\"],\"rows\":[[\"Tax per unit\",\"A fixed amount\",\"A percentage of the price\"],[\"Shift of supply\",\"Parallel, upward by the tax\",\"Pivots upward; the gap widens as price rises\"],[\"Example\",\"Duty per litre of fuel or per packet of cigarettes\",\"Sales tax or VAT\"],[\"Revenue calculation (HL only)\",\"Tax per unit × Q₁\",\"(Pc − Pp) × Q₁ at the new equilibrium\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Who pays? Incidence follows elasticity\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The legal duty to hand a tax to the government says little about who bears it. The burden is shared through price changes. Consumers bear the rise in the price they pay, Pc − Pe, on every unit they still buy; producers bear the fall in the price they keep, Pe − Pp. Whichever side of the market has the less elastic curve bears the larger share, because it is less able to switch away. If demand is less elastic than supply, consumers carry most of the tax; if supply is less elastic, producers do. At HL, each share is the price change multiplied by Q₁ (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"because\":[\"The tax shifts supply up by the full tax per unit\",\"Demand is price inelastic, so buyers cut purchases only a little as the price rises\",\"The price consumers pay, Pc, rises by most of the tax\",\"The price producers keep, Pp, falls only slightly below Pe\",\"So consumers bear most of the burden, whoever legally pays the tax\"]},{\"h\":\"Subsidies: cheaper for buyers, costly for taxpayers\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A subsidy is a payment by government to firms, often per unit of output, that lowers their costs. Supply shifts vertically downward by the subsidy, so the price consumers pay falls to Pc and quantity rises to Q₁, while producers receive Pc plus the subsidy, Pp. The consumer price falls by less than the full subsidy unless supply is perfectly elastic or demand perfectly inelastic: the gain is shared according to elasticity, just as a tax burden is. The government pays the subsidy on every unit sold, subsidy × Q₁, and that spending has an opportunity cost. Where no positive externality exists, the extra output beyond Qe is a misallocation of resources and a welfare loss.\"},{\"h\":\"A stakeholder scorecard\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for the consequences of intervention for markets and stakeholders (guide PDF p. 36). The table sets out the usual first-round effects in a competitive market. Treat each cell as a starting hypothesis to test against the context: elasticities, the time horizon, enforcement and how the government raises or spends the money all change the picture. Workers and third parties count too. Output that falls under a tax or a ceiling can cost jobs, and output that rises under a subsidy can create them.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Stakeholder\",\"Price ceiling\",\"Price floor\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Subsidy\"],\"rows\":[[\"Consumers\",\"Lower price for those who buy; others go without\",\"Pay more, buy less\",\"Pay more, buy less\",\"Pay less, buy more\"],[\"Producers\",\"Lower price and sales; revenue falls\",\"Higher price; sell more only if the surplus is bought\",\"Keep less per unit, sell less\",\"Receive more per unit, sell more\"],[\"Government\",\"Enforcement costs; may need to add supply\",\"Cost of buying and storing any surplus\",\"Gains revenue\",\"Pays subsidy × Q₁\"],[\"Society\",\"Welfare loss and a shortage\",\"Welfare loss and misallocation\",\"Welfare loss, unless correcting an externality\",\"Welfare loss, unless correcting an externality\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Beyond prices: provision, regulation and nudges\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Not every intervention works through a price. With direct provision, the state supplies a service itself, often free at the point of use and paid for from taxation, as with many schools, clinics and roads. Command-and-control regulation and legislation set rules backed by penalties: emission limits, safety standards, age limits or outright bans. Consumer nudges (HL only, guide PDF p. 36) change the way choices are presented, for example by making a healthy or saving option the default, without banning alternatives or changing prices much. Each avoids some weaknesses of taxes and subsidies but brings costs of its own.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Tool\",\"Strength\",\"Limitation\"],\"rows\":[[\"Direct provision\",\"Reaches people regardless of income\",\"Opportunity cost; at a zero price, queues or waiting lists\"],[\"Regulation and legislation\",\"Clear, quick and certain if enforced\",\"Enforcement costs; the same rule for firms with very different compliance costs\"],[\"Consumer nudges (HL only)\",\"Cheap; keeps freedom of choice\",\"Effects vary and may fade; concerns about manipulation\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A specific tax on cigarettes, where demand is price inelastic\",\"cause\":\"The government adds a fixed amount per packet, aiming both to raise revenue and to reduce smoking.\",\"mechanism\":\"Firms’ costs rise by the tax, so supply shifts vertically upward by that amount; because smokers are dependent and have few substitutes, quantity demanded falls only a little as price rises.\",\"immediate\":\"The consumer price rises by most of the tax, quantity falls modestly, and revenue is large because the post-tax quantity stays high.\",\"secondary\":\"Low-income smokers pay a larger share of their income in tax, so the tax is regressive; higher prices can encourage smuggling and cross-border buying.\",\"longrun\":\"Demand tends to become more elastic over time as smokers quit and fewer young people start, so quantity falls further and revenue may fall.\",\"depends\":\"PED and PES, enforcement against illicit trade, whether revenue funds help to quit, and whether the main aim is revenue or lower consumption, since the two pull in opposite directions.\"},{\"t\":\"A binding rent ceiling in a city with a growing population\",\"cause\":\"The city caps rents below the market level to make housing affordable for tenants.\",\"mechanism\":\"At the capped rent more households want to rent, while landlords’ returns fall, so quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.\",\"immediate\":\"Sitting tenants pay less, but newcomers face a shortage and long searches; access depends on luck, connections or side payments rather than price.\",\"secondary\":\"Landlords may cut maintenance, sell flats to owner-occupiers or switch to short-term lets, so both the quality and the number of controlled flats fall.\",\"longrun\":\"Rental supply becomes more elastic over time, so the shortage tends to widen as fewer new rental homes are built, and tenants move less because they fear losing a controlled flat.\",\"depends\":\"How far below equilibrium the cap sits, whether new buildings are exempt, enforcement, the elasticity of supply, and whether the government adds housing through direct provision.\"},{\"t\":\"A minimum support price for wheat, with the government buying the surplus\",\"cause\":\"To support and stabilise farm incomes, the government promises to buy any wheat offered at a minimum price above equilibrium.\",\"mechanism\":\"The higher price raises quantity supplied along the supply curve and cuts quantity demanded by private buyers, leaving a surplus that the government buys.\",\"immediate\":\"Farm incomes rise and become more predictable; consumers pay more; the government spends the minimum price times the surplus, plus storage costs.\",\"secondary\":\"Stocks build up and must be stored, given away, destroyed or sold abroad, which can depress world prices for farmers elsewhere; land and water shift towards the supported crop.\",\"longrun\":\"Supply becomes more elastic as farmers switch land and invest in yields, so the surplus and its cost tend to grow, and less efficient producers stay in farming.\",\"depends\":\"The PES of wheat, the level of the floor compared with world prices, storage capacity, the opportunity cost of government spending, and the political influence of farm groups.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Price ceiling (maximum price)\",\"dg\":\"ceiling\",\"purpose\":\"Shows what happens when the law holds a price below the market equilibrium.\",\"assumptions\":[\"A competitive market with a downward-sloping demand curve and an upward-sloping supply curve\",\"The ceiling is set below Pe and enforced\",\"Product quality stays fixed\"],\"mechanism\":\"At Pmax, quantity demanded rises to Qd and quantity supplied falls to Qs. Only Qs is traded, so the excess demand, Qd − Qs, has to be rationed by something other than price.\",\"prediction\":\"A persistent shortage, a smaller quantity traded, lower producer revenue and a welfare loss. Buyers who obtain the good gain; buyers left without it lose.\",\"application\":\"Rent controls and caps on the prices of staple foods, fuel or medicines.\",\"limitations\":[\"Quality is not fixed in practice: sellers may cut quality or maintenance, which raises the effective price\",\"Parallel markets can undermine enforcement\",\"With a monopoly seller, a well-chosen ceiling can raise output, a case beyond the competitive model\"],\"real\":\"Berlin capped most rents in 2020, and Germany’s constitutional court struck the law down in 2021 because rent law is a federal matter. Several analyses reported fewer flats advertised for rent while the cap applied, which fits the model, although isolating the policy’s effect is difficult.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing the ceiling above Pe and still describing a shortage. A ceiling above equilibrium does not bind, so price and quantity are unchanged.\",\"exam\":\"Use it in Paper 1 or Paper 2 answers on a named market: label Pe, Pmax below it, Qs, Qd and the shortage. HL only: calculate the shortage and the change in spending and revenue from data (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"The welfare-loss triangle on a ceiling diagram is a minimum estimate. If the scarce units go to buyers chosen by queue or lottery rather than to those who value them most, a further misallocation loss arises. Time spent queuing is a real cost that eats into the consumer gain. And if sellers respond by cutting quality, the effective price falls less than the legal price suggests. Research on rent control generally finds gains for sitting tenants and losses for newcomers, so the central evaluative question is often who the policy actually helps.\"},{\"model\":\"Price floor (minimum price)\",\"dg\":\"floor\",\"purpose\":\"Shows what happens when the law holds a price above the market equilibrium.\",\"assumptions\":[\"A competitive market\",\"The floor is set above Pe and enforced\",\"Where the government buys the surplus, it can store or dispose of it\"],\"mechanism\":\"At Pmin, quantity supplied rises to Qs and quantity demanded falls to Qd. Private buyers take only Qd. If the government buys the surplus at Pmin, producers sell Qs and the government spends Pmin × (Qs − Qd).\",\"prediction\":\"A surplus, higher prices for consumers, higher incomes for producers who sell, a cost to government if it buys the surplus, and a welfare loss: fewer units are bought than at Qe and, if the government buys the surplus, resources are also used to produce units that consumers do not want at that price.\",\"application\":\"Support prices for crops, minimum unit pricing for alcohol, and the minimum wage in the labour market.\",\"limitations\":[\"Storing, disposing of or exporting the surplus brings costs that the basic diagram leaves out\",\"In labour markets the employment effect of a minimum wage depends on market structure, and evidence is mixed\",\"Supply becomes more elastic over time, so the surplus tends to grow\"],\"real\":\"India sets minimum support prices for many crops and buys large quantities of wheat and rice, part of which is distributed through its public distribution system. The policy supports farm incomes but has been criticised for encouraging water-intensive crops in states where groundwater is falling.\",\"misconception\":\"Expecting a shortage from a price floor. A binding floor sits above equilibrium and creates excess supply.\",\"exam\":\"Label Pe, Pmin above it, Qd, Qs and the surplus; if the government buys the surplus, shade its spending. HL only: calculate the surplus, consumer spending, producer revenue and government spending (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"Scotland’s minimum unit pricing for alcohol, in force since 2018, is a floor with an unusual feature: the extra money paid per unit goes to sellers, not to the government as it would with a tax. Supporters value the way it targets cheap, strong drinks favoured by heavy drinkers. Critics point to the transfer to the alcohol industry and the burden on low-income drinkers. Official evaluations have suggested a fall in alcohol-related harm, but separating the policy’s effect from other trends is a hard problem of causal inference.\"},{\"model\":\"Indirect tax: specific and ad valorem\",\"dg\":\"tax\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how a tax changes price, quantity, government revenue and welfare, and who bears the burden.\",\"assumptions\":[\"A competitive market\",\"The tax is collected from firms, per unit (specific) or as a percentage of price (ad valorem)\",\"Straight-line curves for the HL calculations\",\"No externality in the market (2.8 relaxes this)\"],\"mechanism\":\"The tax raises firms’ costs, so supply shifts vertically upward: in parallel for a specific tax, pivoting for an ad valorem tax. The price consumers pay rises to Pc, the price producers keep falls to Pp, and quantity falls from Qe to Q₁.\",\"prediction\":\"Revenue = (Pc − Pp) × Q₁. Consumer burden = (Pc − Pe) × Q₁ and producer burden = (Pe − Pp) × Q₁, with the less elastic side bearing more. The triangle between Q₁ and Qe is the welfare loss.\",\"application\":\"Excise duties on fuel, alcohol and tobacco (specific); sales taxes and VAT (ad valorem).\",\"limitations\":[\"Real pass-through can differ from the competitive model; with market power, prices can rise by more than the tax\",\"Taxes on goods that take a large share of poorer households’ budgets are regressive\",\"High taxes invite smuggling and cross-border shopping\"],\"real\":\"India’s Goods and Services Tax, introduced in 2017, replaced many central and state indirect taxes with a tax on value added charged at several rates. Many governments also rely on fuel duties, whose revenue holds up because short-run demand for fuel is price inelastic.\",\"misconception\":\"Shifting demand instead of supply, splitting the burden equally, or calculating revenue with the pre-tax quantity Qe instead of Q₁.\",\"exam\":\"Draw S and S + tax, label Pc, Pe, Pp, Qe and Q₁, and shade revenue and the welfare loss; use PED and PES to explain the split. HL only: calculate revenue, each side’s burden and the welfare loss (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"Economists treat pass-through as an empirical question rather than something to assume. Studies of taxes on sugary drinks in several US cities find that pass-through to shelf prices varied between cities and shops, often substantial but incomplete, and that some shoppers bought outside the taxed area. Other research suggests people react more strongly to taxes included in displayed prices than to taxes added at the till, so the way a tax is shown may matter as well as its size. Where sellers have market power, prices can rise by more than the tax.\"},{\"model\":\"Per-unit subsidy\",\"dg\":\"subsidy\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how a payment to producers changes price, output, government spending and welfare.\",\"assumptions\":[\"A competitive market\",\"A fixed subsidy per unit paid to producers\",\"No positive externality (2.8 relaxes this)\"],\"mechanism\":\"The subsidy lowers firms’ costs, so supply shifts vertically downward by the subsidy. The consumer price falls to Pc, producers receive Pp = Pc + subsidy, and quantity rises from Qe to Q₁.\",\"prediction\":\"Government spending = subsidy × Q₁. Consumers gain Pe − Pc per unit and producers gain Pp − Pe per unit, with the less elastic side gaining more. Without an externality, output beyond Qe creates a welfare loss.\",\"application\":\"Farm support, fuel and fertiliser subsidies, and support for renewable energy and public transport.\",\"limitations\":[\"It leaves out how the subsidy is financed: higher taxes, borrowing or cuts elsewhere\",\"Subsidies can protect inefficient firms and are politically hard to remove\",\"Subsidies to exporters can breach trade rules and provoke retaliation (4.2)\"],\"real\":\"Indonesia sharply reduced fuel subsidies around the end of 2014 and redirected spending to other priorities, and Nigeria removed its petrol subsidy in 2023, after which pump prices rose steeply. Both removals were politically contentious, which illustrates how subsidies create groups who resist their removal.\",\"misconception\":\"Assuming the consumer price falls by the full subsidy, or measuring government spending as the subsidy per unit rather than subsidy × Q₁.\",\"exam\":\"Draw S and S + subsidy, label Pp, Pe, Pc, Qe and Q₁, and shade the government’s spending. HL only: calculate spending, the consumer and producer gains and the welfare loss (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"In the competitive model it makes no difference to the outcome whether a subsidy is paid to sellers or to buyers: final prices and quantity are the same, and the split of the gain depends on elasticities alone. The real differences are practical, such as administrative cost, the risk of fraud and how visible the policy is to voters. Political economy matters too: a subsidy concentrates benefits on an organised group while spreading the cost thinly across taxpayers, which helps explain why subsidies are easier to start than to end.\"},{\"model\":\"Non-price tools: direct provision, regulation and consumer nudges\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains interventions that act on quantities, rules or the way choices are presented rather than on prices.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Government has enough information to set the right rule or level of provision\",\"Rules can be monitored and enforced\",\"Nudges (HL only): choices are shaped by defaults, framing and salience, not only by prices\"],\"mechanism\":\"Direct provision puts the good in users’ hands, usually financed by taxation. Regulation limits or requires behaviour, with penalties for breaches. Nudges alter the choice environment, for instance by making the desired option the default.\",\"prediction\":\"Provision raises use of the service; regulation achieves a known limit if it is enforced; nudges shift behaviour cheaply, though their effects vary widely between settings.\",\"application\":\"State schools and clinics; emission limits, safety standards and age limits; default enrolment in pension schemes or organ-donor registers.\",\"limitations\":[\"Provision has an opportunity cost and may be run inefficiently without a profit motive\",\"Uniform rules ignore that some firms can comply more cheaply than others\",\"Nudges (HL only) may have small or short-lived effects and raise concerns about manipulation\"],\"real\":\"When the UK introduced automatic enrolment into workplace pensions from 2012, making saving the default, participation rose substantially. It is one of the best-documented nudges and a useful HL example.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating regulation as costless, or treating nudges as a replacement for taxes and rules rather than a complement to them.\",\"exam\":\"Use these tools as alternatives in evaluation: after analysing a tax or subsidy, weigh whether regulation, provision or (HL only) a nudge would meet the aim more cheaply or fairly. The 2.7 diagram list covers only ceilings, floors, taxes and subsidies (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"Economists distinguish tools that fix a price from tools that fix a quantity. A tax sets the price of an activity and lets people decide how much to do; a regulation sets the quantity and leaves the cost of meeting it uncertain. When getting the quantity wrong would be very harmful, as with a toxic chemical, a rule may be better. When firms’ compliance costs differ widely, a price-based tool usually reaches the same outcome more cheaply. Most economists see nudges as additions to prices and rules, not substitutes for them.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"In a competitive labour market, a minimum wage set above the equilibrium wage is a price floor. Firms hire fewer workers while more people want jobs, so the model predicts unemployment, concentrated among the least skilled.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence is mixed. A well-known study by Card and Krueger of fast-food restaurants in New Jersey and neighbouring Pennsylvania in the early 1990s found no clear fall in employment after New Jersey raised its minimum wage, and research on Seattle’s large increases reached different conclusions depending on the data and methods used. Many studies find small employment effects for moderate increases, with larger effects more likely when the minimum is high relative to local wages.\",\"interp\":\"Some economists read this as a sign that employers have power to set wages (monopsony), in which case a moderate minimum wage can raise both pay and employment. Others argue that firms adjust through hours, prices, automation or slower hiring over longer periods, which short-run studies can miss. The disagreement is partly about market structure and partly about the time horizon.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why price controls tend to look worse with time\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Supply is usually more elastic in the long run than in the short run, because firms can build, convert or leave the industry. Under a rent ceiling the short-run shortage may be modest, since the stock of flats is fixed. Over the years fewer rental homes are built or maintained, landlords convert flats to other uses, and the shortage widens. A price floor for a crop works in reverse: farmers switch land to the supported crop and invest to raise yields, so the surplus and the cost of buying it grow. Judging a price control at a single moment can therefore understate its long-run cost.\"},{\"h\":\"Efficiency is not the only test\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"The welfare-loss triangle measures lost surplus, but it counts a dollar to a poor household and a dollar to a rich one as equal. Many interventions aim at equity instead: a rent cap or food subsidy may be accepted despite a welfare loss if it protects vulnerable households. The fair question is whether a policy achieves its aim at a reasonable cost compared with the alternatives. A cash transfer to low-income households often helps them at a lower efficiency cost than a price control, which is why many economists prefer it, although transfers bring their own problems of targeting and administration.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine the government wants bread to be cheaper, so it sets a legal maximum price below the market price. More people now want bread, but bakers earn less per loaf and bake fewer. There is not enough to go round, so queues form and some people go without. A tax works the other way: it pushes the price up and the quantity down, and whoever finds it harder to switch away, buyers or sellers, pays most of it. A subsidy lowers the price but costs taxpayers money. Every tool helps someone and costs someone else something.\",\"exam\":\"Start by defining the intervention precisely, including where it sits relative to equilibrium: a binding ceiling below Pe, a binding floor above Pe, a specific tax as a fixed sum per unit. Draw the diagram with axes labelled for the named good, and mark the old and new prices and quantities. Then build the chain: the policy changes a price or a cost, which changes quantity demanded and supplied, which produces the shortage, surplus, revenue or spending shown on the diagram. Use PED and PES to explain who bears a tax or gains from a subsidy. Evaluate against the stated aim, naming each stakeholder, the time horizon, enforcement and the opportunity cost of government spending. HL only: support the analysis with calculations from data (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deep\":\"The competitive model shows interventions either as wedges between what buyers pay and what sellers receive, or as limits on price that stop the market clearing. Its predictions about incidence and welfare loss follow from the slopes of the curves, which is why elasticity sits at the centre of the analysis. But the assumptions are strong. With market power, a tax can raise prices by more than the tax, and a well-set price ceiling can raise output. With weak enforcement, parallel markets set their own prices. With behavioural biases, the way a tax is displayed can matter as much as its size. And the model is static: supply adjusts over time, so the short-run and long-run effects of the same policy can differ sharply. A strong analysis uses the diagram as a starting point, then asks which assumptions fit the market in question.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Every tool in this subtopic is a form of intervention, and each diagram shows how the tool changes the outcome the market would reach on its own.\",\"tension\":\"An intervention that meets one aim, such as affordable rents, can undermine another, such as an adequate supply of housing.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new rent cap or fuel subsidy: judge whether the intervention achieves its aim, and for whom.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Many interventions, especially price ceilings, subsidies and direct provision, are justified by fairness rather than by efficiency.\",\"tension\":\"A policy that makes a good cheaper for everyone may help better-off households more than poor ones.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a tax on a basic good: analyse whether the burden falls more heavily on low-income households.\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"In a market without externalities, taxes, subsidies and price controls move output away from the level that maximises social surplus, creating a welfare loss.\",\"tension\":\"Accepting some welfare loss may be the price of a fairer outcome.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a minimum price for a crop: weigh the welfare loss and the surplus against the gains to farmers.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do rent caps meant to help tenants often make flats harder to find?\",\"expect\":\"Before studying the model, many people expect a rent cap to make housing cheaper for everyone who needs it.\",\"model\":\"A binding ceiling cuts the quantity of rental housing supplied and raises the quantity demanded, creating a shortage that tends to grow as supply becomes more elastic over time.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of rent control, including research on San Francisco, generally find that tenants in controlled flats gain lower rents and more stability, while some landlords withdraw properties from the rental market. Reports from Berlin’s short-lived cap pointed in a similar direction, though the evidence there is limited.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes: the gains to sitting tenants and the losses to newcomers both fit the model. The model is weaker at predicting changes in quality and the size of the effects.\",\"assume\":\"The elasticity of rental supply, how strictly the cap is enforced and whether new buildings are exempt matter most.\",\"change\":\"If new construction were exempt and the government added supply directly, the shortage could be smaller; if demand were rising quickly, it could be larger.\",\"policy\":\"A rent cap may give short-term relief, but lasting affordability usually needs more supply, for example through planning reform or social housing, with targeted support for low-income tenants.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a binding price ceiling creates a shortage, and what happens to the goods that are available.\",\"check\":[\"The ceiling is set below the equilibrium price\",\"At the lower price quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls\",\"The shortage is Qd − Qs, and only Qs is traded\",\"Non-price rationing, such as queues, waiting lists or favouritism, decides who buys\",\"Parallel markets or lower quality may appear\",\"The shortage tends to grow as supply becomes more elastic over time\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why consumers bear most of a specific tax on a good with price-inelastic demand.\",\"check\":[\"The tax shifts supply vertically upward by the tax per unit\",\"With inelastic demand, quantity demanded falls only a little as price rises\",\"The consumer price rises by most of the tax (Pc − Pe is large)\",\"Producers keep a price close to the original (Pe − Pp is small)\",\"Who legally pays the tax does not decide who bears it\",\"Revenue is tax × the post-tax quantity, which stays high\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Where must a price floor be set to have any effect, and what does it create?\",\"a\":\"Above the equilibrium price. It creates excess supply, a surplus equal to Qs − Qd.\"},{\"q\":\"How does the supply curve shift under a specific tax compared with an ad valorem tax?\",\"a\":\"A specific tax shifts supply upward in parallel by the tax per unit; an ad valorem tax pivots it upward, with the gap widening as price rises.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: a $2 specific tax cuts quantity from 100 to 80 units. Calculate the tax revenue and the welfare loss.\",\"a\":\"Revenue = $2 × 80 = $160. Welfare loss = ½ × $2 × (100 − 80) = $20.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: a $3 per-unit subsidy raises quantity from 500 to 600 units. What does it cost the government?\",\"a\":\"$3 × 600 = $1,800, using the new quantity rather than the original one.\"},{\"q\":\"Demand for a good is more elastic than supply. Who bears most of an indirect tax on it?\",\"a\":\"Producers, because consumers can switch away more easily, so the price consumers pay rises by less than the price producers keep falls.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one reason why an indirect tax on a necessity may be judged unfair.\",\"a\":\"It is regressive: low-income households spend a larger share of their income on the good, so the tax takes a larger share of their income.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the equilibrium price of bread is $5. The government sets a maximum price of $6. What happens?\",\"opts\":[\"A shortage, because a maximum price restricts sellers\",\"A surplus, because bakers want to supply more at $6\",\"Nothing: the ceiling does not bind because it is above equilibrium\",\"The price rises to $6, the legal maximum\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A price ceiling binds only when it is set below the equilibrium price. At $6 the market still clears at $5, so price and quantity are unchanged. A shortage would follow only from a ceiling below $5.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-11\"},{\"q\":\"A specific tax is collected from cigarette producers. Demand for cigarettes is much less elastic than supply. Who bears most of the tax?\",\"opts\":[\"Producers, because they hand the tax to the government\",\"Consumers, because they are less able to switch away\",\"Both equally, because the tax is shared\",\"The government, because sales fall\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Incidence depends on relative elasticities, not on who legally pays. Inelastic demand lets producers pass most of the tax on through a higher price. Choosing “producers” confuses the legal payer with the economic burden.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-09\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a $3 per-unit subsidy is paid to producers. The price consumers pay falls from $10 to $8. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"Consumers gain $3 per unit\",\"Consumers gain $2 per unit and producers gain $1 per unit\",\"Producers gain $3 per unit and consumers gain nothing\",\"The government’s total cost is $3\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Consumers pay $2 less. Producers now receive $8 + $3 = $11, which is $1 more than before. The consumer price falls by less than the subsidy because the gain is shared, and the government’s cost is $3 multiplied by the new quantity, not $3.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-10\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with a diagram, the consequences of a price ceiling or price floor in a named market\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate the use of an indirect tax, a subsidy or a price control to achieve a stated aim\",\"Paper 2 (b)–(f): draw and explain a tax, subsidy or price-control diagram from the text; HL only: calculate revenue, spending, incidence or welfare loss from the data\",\"HL Paper 3: calculate the effects of an intervention on stakeholders and recommend a policy\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"draw\",\"explain\",\"calculate\",\"analyse\",\"distinguish\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"Label the axes Price and Quantity for the named good. Ceiling: Pmax below Pe, with Qs, Qd and the shortage. Floor: Pmin above Pe, with Qd, Qs and the surplus. Tax: S and S + tax (parallel for specific, pivoting for ad valorem), with Pc, Pe, Pp, Qe, Q₁, the revenue rectangle and the welfare-loss triangle. Subsidy: S and S + subsidy, with Pp, Pe, Pc, Qe, Q₁ and the spending rectangle.\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 36): shortage (Qd − Qs) or surplus (Qs − Qd); consumer spending, producer revenue and government spending under price controls; tax revenue = tax × Q₁; subsidy cost = subsidy × Q₁; consumer and producer shares of a tax or subsidy; welfare loss = ½ × tax (or subsidy) × change in quantity, for straight-line curves.\",\"apply\":\"Name the market, the instrument and its size, and use the elasticities the context suggests (for example, inelastic demand for fuel or tobacco) to explain the specific outcome.\",\"eval\":\"Judge the policy against its stated aim; weigh consumers, producers, workers, government and taxpayers; contrast the short run with the long run; consider enforcement, unintended consequences and at least one alternative tool.\",\"errors\":[\"Drawing a ceiling above equilibrium or a floor below it, then describing a shortage or surplus\",\"Shifting demand rather than supply for a tax collected from producers\",\"Splitting the tax burden equally, or ignoring elasticity\",\"Calculating revenue or subsidy cost with the pre-intervention quantity\"],\"strong\":[\"Tie elasticity values directly to the size of each stakeholder’s burden or gain\",\"Name every stakeholder, including workers, taxpayers and third parties\",\"Judge the policy against its own aim, over both the short run and the long run\",\"Weigh a realistic alternative, such as a cash transfer, regulation or direct provision\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why a maximum price on rented housing may lead to a shortage and to non-price rationing.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the use of subsidies to support farmers’ incomes.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: in a market for sugary soft drinks, equilibrium is 400,000 litres a week at $2.00 per litre. After a specific tax of $0.50 per litre, consumers pay $2.40 and 340,000 litres are sold. (i) Calculate the government revenue, the share of the tax borne by consumers and by producers, and the welfare loss the model shows if there is no externality (HL only). (ii) Recommend whether the government should raise the tax further to reduce sugar consumption.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"PED decides how far a tax raises the consumer price and how much revenue it yields.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"why\":\"PES, and the way it rises over time, explains why shortages under ceilings and surpluses under floors tend to grow.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Taxes, subsidies and regulation return as tools to correct externalities, where they can raise welfare rather than reduce it.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Indirect taxes can be regressive and minimum wages are listed as a policy to reduce poverty (guide PDF p. 46), so these diagrams feed the equity debate.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"Production subsidies and tariffs reappear as trade protection, analysed with the same logic of price wedges and welfare loss.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Because 2.8 applies the tax, subsidy and regulation tools from this subtopic to externalities, where intervention can move the market towards the social optimum.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a new or changed indirect tax, subsidy, price cap, minimum price or regulation in a specific market, ideally with figures on price or quantity. Intervention or Equity can frame the commentary: who gains, who loses, and whether the policy meets its aim.\",\"ee\":\"An investigation of how much of a local tax on sugary drinks, or a change in fuel duty, was passed through to shelf prices, using price data collected from shops before and after the change.\",\"tok\":\"If the burden of a tax depends on elasticities that economists can only estimate, how can policymakers claim to know who will pay for their decisions?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can draw each of the four intervention diagrams and use elasticity to explain who gains and who loses.\",\"starter\":\"Show two headlines, one on a rent cap and one on a fuel tax, and ask students to predict who pays and who benefits before any theory is taught.\",\"activity\":\"Run a classroom market with buyer and seller cards, then impose a price ceiling below the clearing price and record the shortage and who gets served; repeat with a per-unit tax.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a policy that creates a welfare loss ever justified? Use a rent cap and a food subsidy to test equity against efficiency.\",\"check\":\"Mini-whiteboards: draw a tax on a good with inelastic demand and shade the consumer burden, producer burden, revenue and welfare loss.\",\"extension\":\"HL: calculate revenue, each side’s burden and the welfare loss from a diagram with numbered axes, then repeat with more elastic demand and compare. (Deriving these from demand and supply equations is enrichment beyond the 2022 guide.)\",\"homework\":\"Find a current example of each intervention, identify its stakeholders, and write one evaluative sentence per example on whether it meets its aim.\"},\"after\":[\"I can state the seven reasons the guide gives for intervening in markets.\",\"I can explain why a price ceiling binds only below equilibrium and a price floor only above it.\",\"I can draw a ceiling, a floor, a specific tax, an ad valorem tax and a subsidy, with every price and quantity labelled.\",\"I can use PED and PES to explain who bears a tax and who gains from a subsidy.\",\"HL only: I can calculate revenue, subsidy cost, each side’s share, shortages, surpluses and welfare loss.\",\"I can set out the consequences of each intervention for consumers, producers, government, workers and society.\",\"I can evaluate an intervention against its stated aim over the short run and the long run.\",\"I can connect indirect taxes and subsidies to externalities in 2.8 and to inequality in 3.4.\",\"I can explain tax incidence without notes, using an example of my own.\"]},\"2.8\":{\"code\":\"2.8\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Markets weigh only the costs and benefits that buyers and sellers themselves feel. When production or consumption spills costs or benefits onto third parties, the market quantity differs from the socially optimal quantity, where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost. External costs lead to too much output, external benefits to too little, and resources that anyone can use but no one owns tend to be used up faster than they can recover.\",\"why\":\"Pollution, congestion, disease and depleted fisheries all arise when private choices ignore effects on others. A government, community or firm needs to know which curve has diverged, and by how much, before it can choose a sensible response.\",\"q\":\"Why does a market for fossil-fuel electricity, left alone, produce more carbon emissions than society would choose?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency where marginal benefit equals marginal cost, and social (community) surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Demand as marginal private benefit and supply as marginal private cost\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"How indirect taxes and subsidies shift supply, and who bears them\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Regulation, direct provision and (HL only) nudges as policy tools\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Price elasticity of demand, which decides how far a tax cuts consumption\",\"sub\":\"2.5\"},{\"t\":\"The area of a triangle, for the HL welfare-loss calculation\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define an externality, marginal social cost, marginal social benefit, a merit good, a demerit good and a common pool resource.\",\"State that the socially optimal output is where MSB = MSC, with allocative efficiency and social surplus maximised.\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a production externality separates MSC from MPC, while a consumption externality separates MSB from MPB.\",\"Explain why a common pool resource is overused: it is rivalrous but non-excludable.\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a real case as one of the four externality types and name the third party affected.\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a Pigouvian or carbon tax, a subsidy, regulation or education moves the market towards the social optimum.\",\"Analyse why a tradable permit scheme fixes the quantity of pollution while a carbon tax fixes its price.\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate responses to externalities and common pool resources against measurement difficulty, effectiveness and consequences for stakeholders.\",\"Evaluate why international cooperation on global sustainability problems is hard to achieve, monitor and enforce.\"],\"calculate\":[\"HL only (guide PDF p. 36): calculate the welfare loss from an externality diagram as the area of the triangle between Qopt and Qmarket.\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw an allocative efficiency diagram and all four externality diagrams, labelling MPC, MSC, MPB and MSB, marking Qopt and Qmarket, and placing the welfare-loss triangle with its apex at the social optimum.\",\"Draw diagrams showing a Pigouvian or carbon tax on a polluting industry, a subsidy, regulation and education moving the market towards Qopt.\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read the external cost or benefit per unit, the market and optimal quantities, and the welfare loss from a diagram.\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL Paper 3: recommend a policy, or mix of policies, for a named externality or common pool resource, using the data provided.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"The social optimum: MSB = MSC\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Demand reflects marginal private benefit (MPB), the benefit to the buyer of one more unit; supply reflects marginal private cost (MPC). Marginal social benefit (MSB) adds any benefit to third parties, and marginal social cost (MSC) adds any cost to third parties. The socially optimal output is where MSB = MSC: resources are allocated efficiently and social (community) surplus is at its maximum (guide PDF p. 36). An externality is a cost or benefit of production or consumption that falls on a third party and is not reflected in the price. When one exists, the market settles where MPB = MPC, away from the optimum, and the market fails.\"},{\"h\":\"Four externalities: which curve separates?\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Ask two questions. Does the spillover come from producing the good or from consuming it? Does it harm or help the third party? A production externality separates MSC from MPC; a consumption externality separates MSB from MPB. Negative externalities lead to over-production or over-consumption, positive ones to under-production or under-consumption. In all four cases the welfare-loss triangle has its apex at the social optimum, where MSB = MSC, and its vertical side at Qmarket, spanning the gap between MSB and MSC there.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Type\",\"Example\",\"Curves\",\"Market outcome\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"rows\":[[\"Negative production\",\"Smoke from a factory harms residents’ health\",\"MSC above MPC\",\"Qmarket > Qopt: over-production\",\"Between MSC and MSB, from Qopt to Qmarket\"],[\"Negative consumption\",\"Drivers in a city centre delay one another and pollute\",\"MSB below MPB\",\"Qmarket > Qopt: over-consumption\",\"Between MSC and MSB, from Qopt to Qmarket\"],[\"Positive production\",\"A firm’s training raises the skills of workers who later join other firms\",\"MSC below MPC\",\"Qmarket < Qopt: under-production\",\"Between MSB and MSC, from Qmarket to Qopt\"],[\"Positive consumption\",\"Vaccination lowers the risk of infection for others\",\"MSB above MPB\",\"Qmarket < Qopt: under-consumption\",\"Between MSB and MSC, from Qmarket to Qopt\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why the market overshoots with an external cost\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The market is not careless; it responds to the prices it sees. A polluting firm pays for labour, energy and materials, but not for the damage its emissions do to people downstream or downwind. Its supply curve therefore reflects MPC, not MSC. Competition pushes output to where MPB = MPC, so the last units produced cost society more than they are worth, and every unit between Qopt and Qmarket adds to the welfare loss. Note that some external cost remains at Qopt: the optimum is the output at which the extra benefit just justifies the extra harm, not an output with no pollution at all.\",\"because\":[\"Firms choose output where MPB = MPC\",\"The cost of pollution falls on third parties, not on the firm\",\"So MSC lies above MPC by the external cost per unit\",\"At Qmarket, MSC exceeds MSB for the last units produced\",\"So resources are over-allocated to the good, and a welfare loss arises between Qopt and Qmarket\"]},{\"h\":\"Merit and demerit goods\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A merit good, such as education or vaccination, is one that society judges people should consume more of than the market provides. It is under-consumed for two possible reasons: positive consumption externalities, and consumers underestimating their own private benefit through poor information or short-sightedness. A demerit good, such as tobacco or alcohol, is over-consumed for the mirror-image reasons. The two reasons often overlap but are distinct: an externality affects third parties, while a misjudged private benefit affects the consumer. The labels also carry a value judgement about what is good for people, which is why policy towards these goods is often contested.\"},{\"h\":\"Common pool resources and the tragedy of the commons\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A common pool (or common access) resource is rivalrous but non-excludable (guide PDF p. 36): fish in the open ocean, groundwater, common grazing land, forests, or the atmosphere’s capacity to absorb emissions. What one user takes is unavailable to others, yet no one can easily be stopped from taking. Each user enjoys the full benefit of extracting one more unit but bears only a small part of the cost of a smaller stock. The result, which Garrett Hardin called the tragedy of the commons in 1968, is overuse. Extraction beyond the rate at which the resource renews is unsustainable production: it imposes negative externalities on other users and on future generations.\"},{\"h\":\"The policy toolkit\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide lists the responses to know (guide PDF pp. 36–37). Market-based tools change incentives: Pigouvian and carbon taxes raise MPC towards MSC, subsidies lower MPC or raise MPB, and tradable permits cap total pollution while letting firms trade the right to emit. Rules and information act more directly: legislation and regulation set limits or standards, and education campaigns aim to move MPB towards MSB. Government provision supplies merit goods itself. For common pool resources, collective self-governance and international agreements can set and enforce limits on use. No tool is best everywhere; the choice depends on measurement, elasticity, enforcement and who bears the cost.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Response\",\"Works on\",\"Main strength\",\"Main limitation\"],\"rows\":[[\"Pigouvian or carbon tax\",\"Raises MPC towards MSC\",\"Uses the price signal; raises revenue\",\"External cost is hard to measure; cuts quantity only a little if demand is inelastic\"],[\"Tradable permits\",\"Caps total emissions; trading sets a price\",\"Certainty about quantity; cuts made where cheapest\",\"Permit price can be volatile; cap may be set too loosely\"],[\"Legislation and regulation\",\"Sets limits, bans or standards\",\"Direct and easy to understand\",\"Enforcement cost; one rule for firms with different costs\"],[\"Education and awareness\",\"Moves MPB towards MSB\",\"Tackles misjudged private benefits\",\"Slow; effects hard to measure\"],[\"Subsidies\",\"Lowers MPC or raises MPB\",\"Raises output or use of merit goods\",\"Opportunity cost; hard to withdraw\"],[\"Government provision\",\"Supplies the good directly\",\"Reaches people who cannot pay\",\"Cost to taxpayers; may be run inefficiently\"],[\"Collective self-governance\",\"Users agree and enforce their own rules\",\"Uses local knowledge; cheap monitoring\",\"Works best in small, stable communities\"],[\"International agreements\",\"Coordinate limits across countries\",\"Tackle problems no country can solve alone\",\"Free riding; weak monitoring and enforcement\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why global commons need cooperation, and why it is hard\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Many sustainability problems cross borders: greenhouse gases mix in the atmosphere, fish migrate between national waters, and rivers flow through several countries. The guide stresses the global nature of these issues and the challenges of monitoring and enforcement (guide PDF p. 37). The Montreal Protocol of 1987, which phased out most ozone-depleting chemicals, is widely seen as a success, helped by the availability of substitutes. The 2015 Paris Agreement relies on national pledges without binding penalties for missing them, and progress so far is mixed.\",\"because\":[\"Emissions from any country affect the whole atmosphere\",\"Each country bears the full cost of its own cuts but shares the benefit with all\",\"So each has an incentive to free ride on the efforts of others\",\"No world government can enforce limits\",\"So cooperation depends on monitoring, trust, reciprocity and diplomatic or trade pressure\"]}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A carbon tax on fossil-fuel electricity generation\",\"cause\":\"The government taxes each tonne of carbon dioxide emitted, aiming to make generators pay for the external cost of climate damage.\",\"mechanism\":\"The tax raises generators’ marginal private cost, so MPC shifts up towards MSC; if the tax equals the external cost at the optimum, output falls to Qopt.\",\"immediate\":\"Fossil-fuel electricity becomes more expensive to produce, its price rises and less is sold; the government collects revenue on the remaining output.\",\"secondary\":\"Low-carbon generators become relatively cheaper, so production switches towards them; household bills rise, which weighs most on low-income homes; energy-intensive firms may move production abroad.\",\"longrun\":\"The price signal rewards investment in cleaner technology and energy saving, so emissions can keep falling, while revenue may shrink as the taxed activity declines.\",\"depends\":\"How accurately the external cost is estimated, the PED for electricity, the availability of low-carbon substitutes, how the revenue is used, and whether trading partners price carbon too.\"},{\"t\":\"A cap-and-trade permit scheme for industrial emissions\",\"cause\":\"The government caps total emissions from covered industries and issues permits adding up to the cap, by auction or free allocation.\",\"mechanism\":\"Each firm must hold a permit for every tonne it emits; firms that can cut emissions cheaply do so and sell spare permits, while firms facing costly cuts buy them, so a market price for emissions emerges.\",\"immediate\":\"Emissions from covered firms are limited to the cap, and the cuts are made where they cost least.\",\"secondary\":\"The permit price moves with the economy and with fuel prices; if too many permits are issued, the price collapses and the incentive to cut weakens; auctioned permits raise revenue, free ones do not.\",\"longrun\":\"If the cap is tightened year by year, a rising permit price rewards investment in cleaner processes.\",\"depends\":\"The level and credibility of the cap, how permits are allocated, the quality of monitoring and reporting, which sectors are covered, and political pressure from industry.\"},{\"t\":\"Overfishing in an open-access fishery\",\"cause\":\"Demand for fish rises and boats acquire better equipment, while no one controls access to the fishing grounds.\",\"mechanism\":\"Each crew catches as much as is privately profitable, because fish it leaves in the sea may simply be caught by others; the cost of a smaller breeding stock is spread across all users.\",\"immediate\":\"The total catch rises for a time and the fish stock falls.\",\"secondary\":\"Catch per boat falls and costs rise as fish become scarcer; the stock may fall below the level at which it can regenerate, threatening incomes in fishing communities.\",\"longrun\":\"Without limits the fishery can collapse, as the cod fishery off Newfoundland did in the early 1990s; recovery can take decades, if it happens at all.\",\"depends\":\"Whether users can agree and enforce rules, the use of catch quotas or tradable quotas, the quality of monitoring, and whether the stock lies in international waters where no single government has authority.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Negative externality of production, corrected by a Pigouvian or carbon tax\",\"dg\":\"negprod\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why a polluting industry produces more than the social optimum, and how a tax can bring output back towards it.\",\"assumptions\":[\"No consumption externality, so MPB = MSB\",\"The external cost per unit can be estimated\",\"A competitive industry with straight-line curves\"],\"mechanism\":\"The market settles where MPB = MPC at Qmarket. MSC lies above MPC by the external cost, so the optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a lower Qopt. A per-unit tax equal to the external cost at Qopt shifts MPC up so that it passes through the optimum, and output falls to Qopt.\",\"prediction\":\"Without intervention: over-production, a price that is too low and a welfare loss between MSC and MSB from Qopt to Qmarket. With a well-set tax: output at Qopt and revenue for the government.\",\"application\":\"Emissions from power stations, factories, cement and steel works; carbon taxes on fossil fuels. The guide asks for a carbon tax diagram showing the effects on a particular polluting industry (guide PDF pp. 36–37).\",\"limitations\":[\"The external cost is hard to measure and value, so the right tax is an estimate\",\"If demand is price inelastic, output falls only a little; that can still be efficient if the tax equals the external cost, but a government hoping for a large cut in quantity may be disappointed, and the tax then acts mainly as a source of revenue\",\"Production may move to countries without the tax (carbon leakage), and energy taxes can be regressive\"],\"real\":\"Sweden introduced a carbon tax in 1991 and raised it over time to one of the highest rates in the world. Its emissions have fallen while its economy has grown, although economists debate how much of the fall the tax itself caused.\",\"misconception\":\"Saying a negative production externality leads to under-production, or drawing MSC below MPC.\",\"exam\":\"Draw MPC, MSC and MPB = MSB, project Qmarket and Qopt, and shade the triangle with its apex at the optimum. For policy, show MPC + tax meeting MSB at Qopt. HL only: calculate the welfare loss from the diagram (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deeper\":\"Pigou set out the case for a corrective tax in 1920. Coase later argued that, where property rights are clear and bargaining is cheap, polluters and victims could negotiate an efficient outcome without a tax, whoever holds the rights. When millions of people share the harm, bargaining is impractical, which is one reason climate policy relies on government action, such as carbon pricing and regulation, rather than on private bargaining. The practical difficulty is the number itself: estimates of the social cost of carbon vary widely, mainly because of the discount rate applied to damage decades ahead.\"},{\"model\":\"Negative externality of consumption (demerit goods)\",\"dg\":\"negcons\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why consumers buy more of a harmful good than is socially optimal.\",\"assumptions\":[\"No production externality, so MPC = MSC\",\"Demand measures consumers’ private benefit\",\"The external cost per unit can be estimated\"],\"mechanism\":\"Consumers buy where MPB = MPC at Qmarket. Their consumption imposes costs on others, such as smoke, noise, congestion or strain on public health services, so MSB lies below MPB. The optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a lower Qopt.\",\"prediction\":\"Over-consumption and a welfare loss between MSC and MSB from Qopt to Qmarket. A tax shifts MPC up; regulation limits sales or use; education aims to shift MPB down towards MSB.\",\"application\":\"Tobacco, alcohol, sugary drinks, car use in congested cities, single-use plastics.\",\"limitations\":[\"Demand for many demerit goods is price inelastic, so taxes cut consumption only a little and fall heavily on poorer consumers\",\"Bans or very high taxes can create illegal markets\",\"Much of the harm may fall on the consumer, which is an information or self-control problem more than an externality\"],\"real\":\"The UK’s Soft Drinks Industry Levy, introduced in 2018, taxes manufacturers according to the sugar content of their drinks. Many producers cut the sugar in their recipes before it took effect, showing that a tax can change what firms make, not only what consumers pay.\",\"misconception\":\"Shifting a cost curve for a consumption externality. Here the divergence is between MPB and MSB, and MSC = MPC.\",\"exam\":\"Draw MPB, MSB below it and MPC = MSC; label Qopt and Qmarket and shade the welfare loss. To show education, shift MPB left towards MSB; to show a tax, shift MPC up. Evaluate using PED, regressivity and enforcement.\",\"deeper\":\"Behavioural economics (HL only, 2.4; guide PDF p. 33) adds the idea of internalities: harms consumers impose on their future selves through present bias or poor information. These are not externalities in the strict sense, but they form a large part of the case for taxing tobacco or sugar. The argument is contested. Supporters see corrective taxes as helping people act on their own long-run preferences; critics see paternalism and point out that such taxes take a larger share of income from poorer households.\"},{\"model\":\"Positive externality of consumption (merit goods)\",\"dg\":\"poscons\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why a good whose use benefits others is under-consumed by the market.\",\"assumptions\":[\"No production externality, so MPC = MSC\",\"The external benefit per unit can be estimated\",\"Price is the main barrier to consumption\"],\"mechanism\":\"Consumers buy where MPB = MPC at Qmarket. Their consumption benefits others, so MSB lies above MPB, and the optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a higher Qopt.\",\"prediction\":\"Under-consumption and a welfare loss between MSB and MSC from Qmarket to Qopt. A subsidy, direct provision, rules such as compulsory schooling, or education can raise consumption towards Qopt.\",\"application\":\"Vaccination, education, preventive health care, public transport use.\",\"limitations\":[\"Subsidies and provision have an opportunity cost\",\"The external benefit is hard to measure, so support may be too large or too small\",\"Where the barrier is distance, time or distrust rather than price, a subsidy may achieve little\"],\"real\":\"India’s mass COVID-19 vaccination campaign from 2021 made vaccines free at government centres, reflecting the benefit to others of lower transmission. Take-up also depended on access and trust, which a lower price alone could not supply.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating “merit good” and “positive externality” as the same thing, or shifting a cost curve instead of MSB.\",\"exam\":\"Draw MPB, MSB above it and MPC = MSC; label Qmarket and Qopt and shade the welfare loss, which lies to the left of Qopt. Show a subsidy as MPC shifting down, or education as MPB shifting right.\",\"deeper\":\"In the model a subsidy can be paid to consumers, shifting MPB up, or to producers, shifting MPC down; either can bring output to Qopt, so governments choose on practical grounds such as administrative cost and targeting. Vaccination also shows threshold effects: once enough people are immune, the external benefit of each extra vaccination can fall, so MSB need not be a straight line. Real policy usually combines subsidy, provision, information and rules, because each tackles a different barrier.\"},{\"model\":\"Positive externality of production\",\"dg\":\"posprod\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why activities whose production benefits others, such as research and training, are under-produced.\",\"assumptions\":[\"No consumption externality, so MPB = MSB\",\"Firms cannot charge the third parties who benefit\",\"The external benefit per unit can be estimated\"],\"mechanism\":\"Firms produce where MPB = MPC at Qmarket. Their production benefits others, so MSC lies below MPC, and the optimum, where MSB = MSC, is at a higher Qopt.\",\"prediction\":\"Under-production and a welfare loss between MSB and MSC from Qmarket to Qopt. A per-unit subsidy equal to the external benefit, or direct government funding, can raise output towards Qopt.\",\"application\":\"Research and development, worker training, beekeeping near orchards, farmers restoring wetlands.\",\"limitations\":[\"External benefits are hard to measure\",\"Firms may lobby for support without generating real spillovers\",\"Subsidies have an opportunity cost and can be hard to withdraw\"],\"real\":\"Many governments fund basic research directly and give tax relief for business research and development, on the argument that new knowledge spreads to other firms. The size of these spillovers is hard to measure, and estimates differ widely.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing MSC above MPC for a positive production externality, or placing the welfare loss to the right of Qopt.\",\"exam\":\"Draw MPC, MSC below it and MPB = MSB; label Qmarket and Qopt. Show a subsidy shifting MPC down to meet MSB at Qopt, then evaluate its cost and the measurement problem.\",\"deeper\":\"Knowledge has features of a public good (2.9): an idea can be used by many people at once, and it is hard to stop others copying it. Patents tackle the second problem by granting temporary exclusive rights, which strengthens the incentive to innovate but creates market power and higher prices while the patent lasts. Governments therefore mix tools: patents, grants, tax credits and public research institutions. Economists disagree about the right balance, partly because spillovers are so hard to measure.\"},{\"model\":\"Common pool resources and the tragedy of the commons\",\"dg\":\"cpr\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why rivalrous but non-excludable resources tend to be overused and depleted.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Users cannot be excluded\",\"One user’s extraction reduces what is left for others\",\"Users act independently, with no enforceable rules or property rights\"],\"mechanism\":\"Each user compares the private benefit of extracting one more unit with its private cost, ignoring the cost that a smaller stock imposes on other and future users. The marginal social cost of extraction therefore exceeds the marginal private cost, and extraction settles above the sustainable rate.\",\"prediction\":\"Overuse, depletion and possible collapse of the resource; negative externalities for current and future users; a welfare loss and a threat to sustainability.\",\"application\":\"Ocean fisheries, groundwater, common grazing land, forests, and the atmosphere as a sink for greenhouse gases.\",\"limitations\":[\"It assumes users cannot organise, yet many communities manage shared resources successfully\",\"Quotas and property rights need monitoring, and can shut out poorer traditional users\",\"Stocks can collapse suddenly, which smooth curves do not capture\"],\"real\":\"After the Newfoundland cod collapse, Canada imposed a moratorium on fishing in 1992. Countries such as New Zealand and Iceland use individual transferable quotas, which give each holder a tradable share of a total allowable catch; many studies link such schemes to more stable stocks, although the evidence varies by fishery.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling a common pool resource a public good. It is rivalrous, which is why it is overused rather than under-provided.\",\"exam\":\"The guide lists common pool resources and unsustainable production creating negative externalities (guide PDF p. 36) but names no separate diagram for them; a negative production externality diagram, or the extraction diagram here, can show overuse. Evaluate quotas, permits, taxes, self-governance and international agreements.\",\"deeper\":\"Elinor Ostrom, awarded the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 2009, documented communities that had managed forests, pastures, fisheries and irrigation systems for generations without either private ownership or state control. She identified shared features: clear boundaries, rules suited to local conditions, users involved in making the rules, monitoring by people accountable to users, graduated penalties and cheap ways to settle disputes. Her work suggests that the tragedy of the commons is a risk rather than a certainty, and that collective self-governance is a real policy option.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"A carbon tax or a permit scheme puts a price on each tonne emitted. Firms with cheap ways to cut emissions cut first, so the model predicts that a given reduction is achieved at the lowest total cost.\",\"evidence\":\"Experience is mixed but informative. In the early phases of the EU Emissions Trading System, launched in 2005, too many permits were issued and the permit price fell to low levels, weakening the incentive; after reforms that removed surplus permits, prices rose. Studies generally find that carbon pricing has reduced emissions compared with what would otherwise have happened, but estimates of the size of the effect vary widely.\",\"interp\":\"Supporters see the early problems as design errors, with caps set too loosely under industry pressure, rather than a failure of the idea. Critics argue that carbon prices have been too low and too narrow to drive deep cuts on their own, and that regulation, support for clean technology and public investment are also needed. Others focus on the effects on low-income households and on firms’ competitiveness.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"The optimum is an estimate, not an observation\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Every externality diagram assumes we know the gap between private and social curves. In reality it has to be estimated. Health damage from air pollution can be valued through medical costs and lost working days, and people’s willingness to pay to avoid harm can be surveyed, but each method has weaknesses. For climate change, much of the damage falls decades ahead, so the answer depends heavily on the discount rate used to compare future harm with present cost, and careful studies can reach very different values. This is why the guide highlights the challenge of measuring externalities (guide PDF p. 37), and why good evaluation treats Qopt as a range.\"},{\"h\":\"Who pays for correcting an externality?\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"A carbon or fuel tax raises the cost of energy, which takes a larger share of income from low-income households. Critics see this as unfair; defenders reply that the revenue can be returned. Some governments pay carbon-tax revenue back to households as equal lump sums, which can leave many lower-income households better off overall while keeping the price signal. Regulation hides its costs in higher prices, which makes compensation harder to target, and permits given away free transfer value to existing firms. Choosing a tool is therefore also a choice about who bears the cost, and political support often depends on it.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"When a factory makes steel, it pays for workers, energy and iron ore, but not for the smoke that makes its neighbours ill. Because the smoke costs the factory nothing, steel is cheaper than it should be, and more is made and bought than society would choose. The opposite happens with vaccination: you are protected, but so are the people around you, and you do not count their benefit, so too few people get vaccinated. Shared resources such as fish in the sea are overused because each boat takes what it can before others do.\",\"exam\":\"Define the externality, name the third party and state which curve separates: MSC from MPC for production, MSB from MPB for consumption. Draw the correct diagram with all curves labelled, Qmarket and Qopt projected to the axis, and the welfare-loss triangle with its apex at MSB = MSC. Explain the chain from the missing price to the misallocation of resources. Then analyse a policy on the diagram, such as a tax raising MPC to MSC or a subsidy lowering MPC. Evaluate with measurement difficulty, PED, enforcement, stakeholder effects and the time horizon, and compare at least one alternative, such as tradable permits or regulation. HL only: calculate the welfare loss when data are given (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"deep\":\"The externality diagrams rest on one idea: prices coordinate decisions well only when they carry all the relevant costs and benefits. Pigou’s answer was to add the missing cost through a tax; Coase’s was to define property rights so that the affected parties can bargain; Ostrom’s was to recognise that communities can build and enforce their own rules. Each works under different conditions. Taxes need a good estimate of damage; bargaining needs few parties and low transaction costs; self-governance needs a stable community that can monitor its members. Global problems such as climate change meet none of these conditions easily, which is why they combine national policy with international agreements that are hard to enforce. The diagrams also assume smooth marginal damage, whereas many environmental harms have thresholds beyond which damage rises sharply, which strengthens the case for firm quantity limits.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Sustainability\",\"here\":\"Overuse of common pool resources and unpriced pollution run down natural resources that future generations will need.\",\"tension\":\"Limits on extraction protect future users but can cut incomes in fishing, farming or mining communities now.\",\"ia\":\"An article on fishing quotas or groundwater limits: ask whether current use can continue without reducing future options.\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Externalities make output differ from the level where MSB = MSC, and the welfare-loss triangle measures the surplus lost.\",\"tension\":\"The most efficient tool on paper may be rejected if its costs fall on groups with low incomes or political power.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a congestion charge: judge whether it moves traffic towards the socially optimal level.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Every response in the guide, from carbon taxes to education, is an attempt to change private incentives or behaviour.\",\"tension\":\"Governments may lack the information to set a tax or cap correctly, so intervention can itself misallocate resources.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new plastic-bag charge or ban: compare the chosen intervention with an alternative the government could have used.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do fish stocks collapse even though every fishing crew wants the fishery to survive?\",\"expect\":\"Before studying the model, many students expect that people who depend on a resource will look after it.\",\"model\":\"In an open-access fishery, each crew gains the full value of its extra catch but bears only a small share of the cost of a smaller stock, so extraction exceeds the sustainable rate.\",\"evidence\":\"Collapses such as that of the Newfoundland cod fishery in the early 1990s fit the prediction. Yet Ostrom and others documented many fishing and farming communities that have managed shared resources sustainably over long periods.\",\"match\":\"Partly. The model predicts well where access is open and users are many and anonymous; it predicts poorly where users form stable communities with their own rules.\",\"assume\":\"That users cannot communicate, make agreements or punish rule-breakers, and that no one can be excluded.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that a community could monitor catches and sanction overfishing would weaken the case for outside intervention.\",\"policy\":\"Options include total allowable catches with tradable quotas, protected areas, support for community management and international agreements for shared stocks, each needing effective monitoring.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, with reference to the curves, why a negative externality of production leads to a welfare loss.\",\"check\":[\"Firms produce where MPC = MPB, at Qmarket\",\"The external cost means MSC lies above MPC\",\"The social optimum is where MSC = MSB, at a lower Qopt\",\"Between Qopt and Qmarket each unit costs society more than it is worth\",\"The welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB from Qopt to Qmarket, with its apex at the optimum\",\"Resources are over-allocated to the good\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a common pool resource tends to be overused, and why this threatens sustainability.\",\"check\":[\"It is rivalrous: one user’s extraction reduces what is left for others\",\"It is non-excludable: no one can easily be stopped from using it\",\"Each user gains the full benefit of extraction but bears only part of the cost of depletion\",\"So extraction exceeds the sustainable rate, because MSC exceeds MPC\",\"The stock can fall below the level at which it regenerates, harming future users\",\"Quotas, property rights, self-governance or agreements can limit use\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the condition for the socially optimal level of output.\",\"a\":\"Marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost (MSB = MSC), where social surplus is maximised.\"},{\"q\":\"For a positive externality of production, which curve lies below which?\",\"a\":\"MSC lies below MPC, because production creates benefits for third parties that lower the cost to society.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: Qmarket = 120 units, Qopt = 90 units, and the gap between MSC and MSB at Qmarket is $8. Calculate the welfare loss.\",\"a\":\"½ × (120 − 90) × $8 = $120.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a common pool resource overused while a public good is under-provided?\",\"a\":\"A common pool resource is rivalrous, so each user’s extraction depletes it for others; a public good is non-rivalrous, so the problem is that too few pay for it, not that it runs out.\"},{\"q\":\"Which fixes the quantity of emissions: a carbon tax or a tradable permit scheme?\",\"a\":\"A permit scheme fixes the quantity and lets the price vary; a carbon tax fixes the price and lets the quantity vary.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one reason why international agreements on emissions are hard to enforce.\",\"a\":\"Each country bears the full cost of its own cuts but shares the benefit, so it has an incentive to free ride, and no world authority can impose penalties.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Loud music at a late-night party disturbs the neighbours. How should this externality be drawn?\",\"opts\":[\"MSC above MPC\",\"MSB below MPB\",\"MSB above MPB\",\"MSC below MPC\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The noise comes from consuming the party, so the benefit curves separate: society’s benefit is lower than the partygoers’ private benefit, and MSB lies below MPB. Choosing “MSC above MPC” treats it as a production externality, but the cost curves are unchanged here.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-13\"},{\"q\":\"An ocean fishery has no catch limits. Which classification fits best?\",\"opts\":[\"A public good, because no one can be excluded\",\"A common pool resource, because it is rivalrous but non-excludable\",\"A merit good, because fish are nutritious\",\"A private good, because fish are sold in markets\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Fish caught by one boat cannot be caught by another, so the fishery is rivalrous, and access cannot easily be restricted, so it is non-excludable. The public-good answer is tempting because of non-excludability, but a public good must also be non-rivalrous; that difference is why fisheries are overused.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-16\"},{\"q\":\"On a diagram of a positive consumption externality, where is the welfare loss?\",\"opts\":[\"The triangle between MSB and MSC from Qmarket to Qopt, with its apex at Qopt\",\"The triangle between MSC and MSB to the right of Qopt\",\"The whole area between MSB and MPB\",\"The triangle between MPB and MPC to the left of Qmarket\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Units between Qmarket and Qopt are worth more to society (MSB) than they cost (MSC) but are not consumed; that lost surplus is the triangle, with its apex at the social optimum. Placing the triangle beyond Qopt copies the negative-externality diagram, where the loss comes from units that should not be produced.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-13\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using a diagram, why a named externality leads to market failure\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate government responses, such as a carbon tax, tradable permits or regulation, to a negative externality or to overuse of a common pool resource\",\"Paper 2 (b)–(f): identify the externality in the text, draw the diagram and explain the welfare loss; HL only: calculate the welfare loss from a diagram\",\"HL Paper 3: calculate the welfare loss or the effect of a tax, and recommend a policy for a named externality\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"explain\",\"analyse\",\"calculate\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"Label the axes Costs and benefits (or Price) and Quantity of the named good. Show the curves that apply (MPC, MSC, MPB, MSB), project Qmarket and Qopt to the axis, and shade the welfare-loss triangle with its apex at MSB = MSC. For policy, show a tax shifting MPC up, a subsidy shifting MPC down, regulation limiting output at Qopt, or education moving MPB towards MSB.\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 36): welfare loss = ½ × (difference between Qmarket and Qopt) × the vertical gap between MSB and MSC at Qmarket, which equals the external cost or benefit per unit at that output; the triangle formula assumes straight-line curves.\",\"apply\":\"Name the third party, the external effect and the curve that separates in the specific case, and use a real policy (for example a named carbon tax, congestion charge or fishing quota) as evidence.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh measurement difficulty, PED, enforcement and monitoring, effects on consumers, producers, workers and government, short-run versus long-run effects, and the case for combining tools or cooperating internationally.\",\"errors\":[\"Shifting a cost curve for a consumption externality, or a benefit curve for a production externality\",\"Placing the welfare-loss triangle on the wrong side of Qopt or between the wrong pair of curves\",\"Calling a common pool resource a public good\",\"Asserting that a tax corrects the externality without discussing measurement, elasticity or enforcement\"],\"strong\":[\"Name the third party and justify which curve separates before drawing\",\"Keep the diagram and the prose consistent, with Qmarket and Qopt clearly marked\",\"Compare two policies on certainty, cost, fairness and feasibility\",\"Treat the social optimum as an estimate and discuss what would change the conclusion\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using an appropriate diagram, why the market for private car travel in a congested city may lead to market failure.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the use of carbon taxes and tradable permits to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a chemical industry produces 500 tonnes a month, where MPC = MPB. Each tonne causes $30 of external damage, and the social optimum is 400 tonnes. (i) Calculate the welfare loss (HL only). (ii) Recommend a policy the government could use to move output towards the optimum, referring to the data and to one stakeholder affected.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"The tax, subsidy and regulation diagrams from 2.7 become corrective tools here, acting on MPC and MPB.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"why\":\"Public goods share non-excludability with common pool resources but differ on rivalry, which is why one is under-provided and the other overused.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"why\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 33): biases, choice architecture and nudges help explain why demerit goods are over-consumed and offer tools beyond taxes.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"The conflict between high economic growth and environmental sustainability (guide PDF p. 45) is an externality problem at the scale of the whole economy.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"why\":\"Sustainable development builds on the idea that unpriced resource use shifts costs onto future generations.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"why\":\"Because public goods complete the market-failure picture: with rivalry and excludability in hand, you can see why common pool resources are overused while public goods are under-provided.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a carbon tax, congestion charge, plastic-bag charge, sugar tax, vaccination campaign or fishing quota, ideally with evidence on the external effect. Sustainability or Efficiency can frame the commentary: which curve has diverged, and does the policy move the market towards the optimum?\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has a named community’s collective management of a local common pool resource, such as a fishery, forest or groundwater source, been sustainable, judged against Ostrom’s design principles?\",\"tok\":\"The socially optimal quantity of pollution depends on money values for future harm. Is choosing a discount rate a matter of knowledge or of values?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can draw all four externality diagrams correctly and evaluate one policy response for each.\",\"starter\":\"Give pairs eight short scenarios (a party, a factory, a vaccine, a training course and so on) and ask them to sort each by production or consumption and by positive or negative before any curves are drawn.\",\"activity\":\"Run a fishing game: groups take tokens from a shared pool that regrows by a set proportion each round; play first with open access, then with a quota the class agrees, and compare the stocks left.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a carbon tax or a permit scheme the better way to cut emissions in your country? Consider certainty, cost, fairness and politics.\",\"check\":\"Card sort: match each externality to its diagram, the curve that separates and the position of the welfare-loss triangle.\",\"extension\":\"HL: calculate the welfare loss from a diagram with numbered axes, then find the per-unit tax that would move output to the optimum.\",\"homework\":\"Find a news article on one externality; identify its type, draw the diagram and write a short paragraph evaluating the policy described.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define an externality and state the MSB = MSC condition for the social optimum.\",\"I can say which curve separates for each of the four externality types, and why.\",\"I can draw all four diagrams with the welfare-loss triangle’s apex at the social optimum.\",\"HL only: I can calculate the welfare loss from a diagram.\",\"I can explain why common pool resources are overused and why this threatens sustainability.\",\"I can analyse how taxes, permits, regulation, education, subsidies and provision act on the curves.\",\"I can evaluate a policy using measurement difficulty, elasticity, enforcement and stakeholder effects.\",\"I can explain why international cooperation is needed for global commons and why it is hard to achieve.\",\"I can connect this subtopic to 2.7, 2.9, 3.3 and 4.7 without notes.\"]},\"2.9\":{\"code\":\"2.9\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Some goods are non-rivalrous, so one person’s use leaves no less for anyone else, and non-excludable, so people cannot be stopped from benefiting. For these public goods, each person can benefit whether or not they pay, so few pay voluntarily and firms cannot cover their costs. The market therefore supplies far too little, often none, which is why governments usually pay for public goods from taxation.\",\"why\":\"National defence, flood barriers, street lighting and basic research support daily life and production, yet no firm can easily charge for them. Deciding how much to provide, and who should produce it, is a central government choice.\",\"q\":\"Why does no private firm build a flood barrier to protect a town, even when the residents want protection?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency and the welfare loss when output is below the social optimum\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Common pool resources: rivalrous but non-excludable\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Merit goods and why they are under-consumed\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Direct provision as a form of government intervention\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Opportunity cost and the choices that scarcity forces on governments\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define rivalry, excludability, a public good and the free-rider problem.\",\"State the two government responses the guide lists: direct provision and contracting out to the private sector (guide PDF p. 37).\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why non-excludability leads to free riding, and why free riding leaves the market supplying too little or none.\",\"Explain why a good must be both non-rivalrous and non-excludable to count as a public good.\"],\"apply\":[\"Test a named good against both characteristics and classify it as a private good, public good, common pool resource or quasi-public good.\"],\"analyse\":[\"Distinguish a public good from a merit good and from a common pool resource.\",\"Analyse why a public good provided free at the point of use still has an opportunity cost.\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate direct provision and contracting out on cost, quality, accountability and the information government needs.\",\"Evaluate how technology or pricing can make a good excludable and change how it can be supplied.\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Construct a rivalry–excludability grid and place named goods in it; the guide specifies no diagram for 2.9 (guide PDF p. 37), so this is a classification aid.\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a text to decide whether a market failure arises from public-good characteristics, from an externality, or from both.\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL Paper 3: recommend whether a government should supply a public good directly or contract it out, using evidence from the case.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Two tests: rivalry and excludability\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A good is rivalrous if one person’s use reduces the amount available to others: a sandwich I eat cannot be eaten by you. It is non-rivalrous if one more user takes nothing away, like a street light that lights the path for everyone who walks by. A good is excludable if people who do not pay can be prevented from using it, and non-excludable if they cannot. A public good is both non-rivalrous and non-excludable (guide PDF p. 37). Classify a good by these characteristics, not by who provides it.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Excludable\",\"Non-excludable\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rivalrous\",\"Private goods: food, clothing, a haircut\",\"Common pool resources: ocean fish, groundwater (2.8)\"],[\"Non-rivalrous\",\"A streaming service or an uncongested toll road (often called club goods, a term beyond the guide)\",\"Public goods: national defence, flood barriers, street lighting\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The free-rider problem\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A free rider is someone who benefits from a good without paying for it. Non-excludability makes free riding possible: once a flood barrier protects a town, every household in it is protected. Each household therefore has a reason to understate how much it values the barrier and hope that others pay. When many people reason this way, voluntary payments fall far short of the cost, and a firm cannot earn enough revenue to build it. The market supplies far less than the socially optimal quantity, often none. Units that would be worth more to society than they cost are not produced, which is a welfare loss.\",\"because\":[\"Once built, the barrier protects every household in the town\",\"No household can be excluded, so each benefits whether or not it pays\",\"So each has an incentive to hide its true valuation and let others pay\",\"So revenue falls far short of the cost, and no firm supplies the barrier\",\"So the market under-provides, often supplying none at all\"]},{\"h\":\"Why non-rivalry matters as well\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Non-excludability alone is not enough. An ocean fishery is non-excludable but rivalrous, so its problem is overuse, not under-provision (2.8). Non-rivalry adds two further points. First, an extra user of a public good costs society nothing, so even if exclusion were possible, turning people away would waste benefit. Second, the social benefit of each unit is the sum of what every user gains, because all of them consume the same unit. That is why the value of a flood barrier or a defence system to society can be far larger than any one person’s willingness to pay.\"},{\"h\":\"Public, merit or common pool? Classify by characteristics\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Students often call anything the state supplies a public good. State schooling and public hospitals are usually merit goods: a classroom place or a hospital bed is rivalrous, and people can be excluded from it. Governments provide them for equity or because of positive externalities, not because they are public goods. Conversely, some public goods are financed privately, for example through advertising or donations. The question to ask is what the good is like, not who pays for it or who produces it.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Public good\",\"Merit good\",\"Common pool resource\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rivalrous?\",\"No\",\"Usually yes\",\"Yes\"],[\"Excludable?\",\"No\",\"Yes\",\"No\"],[\"Market outcome\",\"Under-provided, often not at all\",\"Under-consumed\",\"Overused\"],[\"Main cause\",\"Free riding\",\"Positive externalities or underestimated private benefit\",\"No user bears the full cost of depletion\"],[\"Typical response\",\"Direct provision or contracting out\",\"Subsidy, provision, regulation, education\",\"Quotas, permits, taxes, self-governance\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Quasi-public goods and changing technology\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"Few goods are pure public goods. A road is non-rivalrous when it is empty but rivalrous when it is congested, and tolls or electronic charging can make it excludable. Goods like this are often called quasi-public goods. Technology can move a good between categories. Broadcast television was once non-excludable; encryption and subscriptions made it excludable, so private firms could charge for it. Satellite navigation signals, by contrast, remain open to anyone with a receiver. Where technology makes exclusion cheap, private provision becomes possible, although charging for a non-rivalrous good still keeps out some users who would benefit at no extra cost to anyone.\"},{\"h\":\"Government responses: direct provision and contracting out\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide names two responses (guide PDF p. 37). With direct provision, a public body produces the good, such as an army or a state flood-defence agency, and taxation pays for it. With contracting out, the government still pays and decides how much to provide, but hires a private firm, often chosen by competitive tender, to produce it. Either way, the government has to decide the quantity without market signals, because people do not reveal their true valuations. It may use cost–benefit analysis, surveys of willingness to pay, expert judgement and the political process, each of which is imperfect.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Direct provision\",\"Contracting out\"],\"rows\":[[\"Who pays\",\"Government, from taxation\",\"Government, from taxation\"],[\"Who produces\",\"A public-sector body\",\"A private firm under contract\"],[\"Possible strengths\",\"Direct control of quality; clear accountability\",\"Competition for the contract can cut costs; specialist skills\"],[\"Possible weaknesses\",\"Weak incentive to control costs\",\"Firms may cut quality the contract does not specify; monitoring costs; few bidders\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Free at the point of use is not free\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"A public good financed by taxation has no price for its users, but it still uses scarce resources. The labour, materials and land used for a flood barrier could have built schools or clinics, and the taxes that fund it reduce households’ spending power. This opportunity cost means a government must weigh each public good against other uses of its budget. Government decisions can also go wrong: lobbying, the timing of elections or poor information may lead to too much of some public goods and too little of others. Identifying a market failure is a reason to consider intervention; it does not by itself show that intervention will do better.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A coastal town at risk of flooding, with no government action\",\"cause\":\"Rising sea levels increase the flood risk to homes and businesses in a low-lying town.\",\"mechanism\":\"A barrier would protect everyone behind it, so each household and firm has an incentive to let others pay; voluntary contributions fall short of the cost, and no firm can profit from building it.\",\"immediate\":\"The barrier is not built, or a much smaller defence is built, even though the town’s combined valuation of protection may exceed the cost.\",\"secondary\":\"Flood damage and insurance costs rise, property values fall, and firms become reluctant to invest in the town.\",\"longrun\":\"Households and firms that can move leave, the local tax base shrinks, and those who remain, often the poorer residents, carry more of the risk.\",\"depends\":\"The size of the community (small groups coordinate more easily), trust between residents, whether a local authority can levy a compulsory charge, and whether some benefits can be made excludable.\"},{\"t\":\"A government contracts out street lighting to a private firm\",\"cause\":\"The local authority wants to cut the cost of street lighting while keeping the service free to users.\",\"mechanism\":\"Firms bid for a multi-year contract, and the winner is paid to meet set standards, so competition for the contract replaces competition in a market.\",\"immediate\":\"If bidding is competitive, costs may fall and newer technology, such as low-energy lamps, may be introduced faster.\",\"secondary\":\"The firm has an incentive to cut aspects of quality that the contract does not specify or that are hard to observe, so the authority must spend on monitoring.\",\"longrun\":\"At renewal the existing contractor may hold an advantage, and weak competition or renegotiation can erode the early savings.\",\"depends\":\"How precisely quality can be measured, the number of credible bidders, the contract’s length and penalties, and the authority’s skill at monitoring.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Public goods and the free-rider problem\",\"dg\":\"pubgood\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why markets under-provide goods that are non-rivalrous and non-excludable.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The good is non-rivalrous and non-excludable\",\"People act in their own interest and can hide how much they value the good\",\"Beneficiaries are numerous, so no single payment is decisive\",\"There is no compulsory payment such as a tax\"],\"mechanism\":\"Because non-payers cannot be excluded, each person’s best private choice is to let others pay. Revealed demand therefore understates the true marginal social benefit, which is the sum of every beneficiary’s valuation because all consume the same units. Firms cannot collect enough revenue to cover their costs.\",\"prediction\":\"The market supplies far less than the socially optimal quantity, often none at all; welfare is lost because units worth more to society than they cost are not produced.\",\"application\":\"National defence, flood barriers, street lighting, disease surveillance, basic research and free satellite navigation signals.\",\"limitations\":[\"Few goods are pure public goods; many are quasi-public\",\"Small groups can sometimes organise voluntary provision\",\"Private provision is possible through advertising, sponsorship, bundling or donations\",\"The model does not tell a government how much to provide, because true valuations stay hidden\"],\"real\":\"The civilian signal from the US GPS satellite system can be used free of charge by anyone in the world with a receiver. It is non-rivalrous and, in practice, non-excludable, and it is financed by the US government rather than by its users.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling anything the government provides a public good, or treating non-excludability alone as enough to make a good public.\",\"exam\":\"In Paper 1 or Paper 2, define both characteristics, apply them to the named good, and explain free riding and the resulting under-provision. The guide specifies no diagram for 2.9 (guide PDF p. 37); a labelled sketch can support the argument but is not required.\",\"deeper\":\"Samuelson showed in 1954 that, for a public good, the efficient quantity is where the sum of everyone’s marginal valuations equals marginal cost, whereas for a private good each person buys until their own valuation equals the price. Coase later studied British lighthouses and found that many had been built privately and paid for by fees collected at ports, which made the service partly excludable; later historians have argued that the state’s role was larger than he suggested. The lesson is that institutions can sometimes create excludability where textbooks assume none.\"},{\"model\":\"Direct provision versus contracting out\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Compares the two responses the guide lists for supplying public goods (guide PDF p. 37).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The government finances the good through taxation in both cases\",\"The government decides the quantity and the required quality\",\"Quality can be at least partly specified and monitored\"],\"mechanism\":\"Under direct provision, public-sector bodies produce the good. Under contracting out, the government pays a private firm, usually chosen by competitive tender, to produce it to a specified standard.\",\"prediction\":\"Contracting out can lower costs where competition for the contract is strong and quality is easy to measure; direct provision may be preferred where quality is hard to specify or public accountability matters most.\",\"application\":\"Road building and maintenance, street lighting, refuse collection, defence equipment and some prison and IT services.\",\"limitations\":[\"Contracts are incomplete, so firms may cut quality that is not written into them\",\"Few bidders or corruption in tendering can raise costs\",\"Direct provision may lack incentives to control costs or innovate\"],\"real\":\"For about a quarter of a century the UK used the Private Finance Initiative to have private firms finance, build and maintain public assets such as hospitals and schools. Hospitals and schools are merit goods rather than public goods, but the contracting problems are similar. It was criticised for high long-run costs, and the government stopped signing new deals under it in 2018.\",\"misconception\":\"Assuming contracting out turns the good into a private good. The government still pays for it and decides how much is provided.\",\"exam\":\"Use it for evaluation in Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): compare the two responses on cost, quality, accountability and information, with a named example, and link back to why the market would not supply the good at all.\",\"deeper\":\"Oliver Hart, who later shared the 2016 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for work on contracts, argued with co-authors that private contractors have a strong incentive to cut costs, including in aspects of quality that cannot be written into a contract. Their analysis of prisons suggested that in-house provision is more attractive where such hidden quality matters, as with the use of force, and contracting out more attractive where quality is easy to specify, as with refuse collection. The choice therefore depends on the service, not on a general rule.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The free-rider model predicts that self-interested people will contribute nothing, or very little, to a non-excludable good, so voluntary provision fails.\",\"evidence\":\"In laboratory public-goods games, participants typically contribute a substantial share of their money at first, although contributions tend to fall when the game is repeated unless free riders can be punished. Outside the laboratory, the lifeboat rescue charity serving the UK and Ireland and the online encyclopaedia Wikipedia run largely on donations and volunteers.\",\"interp\":\"Many economists read this as evidence of bounded selfishness (HL only, 2.4; guide PDF p. 33): people care about fairness, reputation and the satisfaction of giving. Others point out that voluntary provision rarely reaches the scale of national defence or flood protection, so the model’s central prediction of under-provision still holds for large, anonymous groups.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"How much should a government provide?\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"For a private good, prices reveal how much people value it. For a public good there is no such signal, and asking people directly invites strategic answers: those who expect to pay may understate their valuation, and those who do not may overstate it. Governments therefore use cost–benefit analysis, which estimates benefits such as avoided flood damage or lives saved and compares them with costs. Valuing lives, time and environmental quality involves contested assumptions, so two analyses of the same project can disagree. The political process fills the gap, which brings its own risks of lobbying and short-term thinking.\"},{\"h\":\"Private routes to public goods\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"The textbook conclusion that markets cannot supply public goods describes a strong tendency, not a law. Radio and free-to-air television have long been financed by selling audiences to advertisers. Open-source software and online encyclopaedias rely on volunteers and donations. Residents’ associations and business improvement districts can raise compulsory or near-compulsory contributions for local services such as security or street cleaning. Each route works by bundling the public good with something excludable or by using social pressure. None scales easily to national defence or large flood defences, which is why taxation remains the main source of finance.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of a fireworks display over a city. Once it starts, everyone can watch, and one more person watching does not spoil the view for anyone else. If a firm tried to sell tickets, most people would simply watch from their windows for free. The firm would not earn enough to pay for the show, so it would not happen, even though thousands of people would enjoy it. That is a public good: no one can be kept out, and sharing it does not use it up. Governments often pay for goods like this from taxes.\",\"exam\":\"Define a public good using both characteristics and apply them to the good in the question: explain why one more user does not reduce what is available and why non-payers cannot be excluded. Then set out the free-rider problem as a chain: non-excludability, the incentive to hide one’s valuation, too little revenue, under-provision or no provision, and welfare loss. Distinguish the good from a merit good or common pool resource where the question or text invites it. For evaluation, compare direct provision with contracting out on cost, quality and accountability, and note the opportunity cost of tax finance and the government’s difficulty in knowing how much to provide. The guide requires no diagram for 2.9 (guide PDF p. 37).\",\"deep\":\"Samuelson’s analysis showed that for a public good the efficient quantity is where the sum of all individuals’ marginal benefits equals marginal cost, because everyone consumes the same unit. For a private good, by contrast, each person buys until their own marginal benefit equals the price. The difficulty is that no simple mechanism makes people reveal their marginal benefits truthfully when their payment depends on what they report. Economists have designed voting and tax schemes that encourage honest reporting in theory, but they are rarely used in practice, so real provision depends on political institutions and on estimates. The public-goods problem also reaches beyond physical goods: basic knowledge, disease surveillance and a stable climate share non-rivalry and non-excludability, which links this subtopic to externalities and to international cooperation.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Because the market under-provides public goods, government finance, through direct provision or contracting out, is the main way they are supplied.\",\"tension\":\"Intervention overcomes free riding but relies on the government judging a quantity that people do not reveal.\",\"ia\":\"An article on cuts to street lighting or flood-defence budgets: examine who loses when provision falls and why the market will not fill the gap.\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Under-provision means that units worth more to society than they cost are not produced, which is a welfare loss.\",\"tension\":\"Contracting out may lower costs but can reduce quality that the contract fails to specify.\",\"ia\":\"An article on outsourcing a public service: weigh lower costs against quality and accountability.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"Public goods such as defence, flood protection and safe public spaces underpin security and living standards for everyone.\",\"tension\":\"Money spent on one public good cannot also be spent on health or education, so gains in well-being have to be weighed against each other.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new flood-defence scheme: assess how it affects households who could not have paid for protection themselves.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"If theory predicts free riding, why do so many people donate to lifeboat rescue services or edit online encyclopaedias for free?\",\"expect\":\"Before studying the model, many students expect that people will pay for things they value.\",\"model\":\"The free-rider model predicts that self-interested people will contribute little or nothing to a non-excludable good, so voluntary provision fails.\",\"evidence\":\"Laboratory games and real donations show that many people do contribute, especially at first and where giving is visible; contributions tend to fall over time unless free riders face disapproval or penalties.\",\"match\":\"Only partly. The model underestimates contributions in small or close-knit groups but correctly predicts that voluntary giving falls short for large, costly public goods.\",\"assume\":\"That people care only about their own material payoff, and that no one can observe or sanction free riders.\",\"change\":\"If giving were visible and socially rewarded, or if the group were small, voluntary provision could go much further than the model predicts.\",\"policy\":\"Governments can finance core public goods through taxation while encouraging voluntary provision, for example through matched funding or tax relief on donations.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why the market is likely to under-provide a flood barrier.\",\"check\":[\"A flood barrier is non-excludable: everyone behind it is protected\",\"It is non-rivalrous: protecting one more house does not reduce protection for others\",\"Non-excludability allows free riding, so people hide their true valuation\",\"Voluntary payments fall short of the cost, so no firm can profit from supplying it\",\"The market supplies too little or none, below the socially optimal quantity\",\"The resulting welfare loss is a case for government finance\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Distinguish a public good from a merit good and from a common pool resource, using one example of each.\",\"check\":[\"A public good is non-rivalrous and non-excludable, for example national defence\",\"A merit good is usually rivalrous and excludable, for example a school place\",\"A common pool resource is rivalrous but non-excludable, for example an ocean fishery\",\"Public goods are under-provided because of free riding\",\"Merit goods are under-consumed because of positive externalities or underestimated private benefits\",\"Common pool resources are overused rather than under-provided\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define a public good.\",\"a\":\"A good that is non-rivalrous (one person’s use does not reduce what is available to others) and non-excludable (non-payers cannot be prevented from benefiting).\"},{\"q\":\"What is the free-rider problem?\",\"a\":\"People who cannot be excluded can benefit without paying, so each has an incentive to let others pay, and voluntary payments fall short of the cost.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is a place at a state school usually not a public good?\",\"a\":\"It is rivalrous and excludable: places are limited and admission can be restricted. It is better described as a merit good.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the two government responses to public goods listed in the guide.\",\"a\":\"Direct provision, and contracting out to the private sector (guide PDF p. 37).\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a public good provided free of charge still have an opportunity cost?\",\"a\":\"The resources and tax revenue it uses could have funded other goods and services, or been left with households to spend.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one limitation of contracting out a public service.\",\"a\":\"The firm may cut aspects of quality that the contract does not specify, so the government must spend on monitoring.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Which of the following is a public good in the economic sense?\",\"opts\":[\"A place at a state secondary school\",\"A town’s flood-warning siren\",\"A bed in a public hospital\",\"A seat on a city bus\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The siren warns everyone within earshot, and one more listener does not weaken the warning for others, so it is non-excludable and non-rivalrous. The school place is provided by the state but is rivalrous and excludable, so it is not a public good; it is usually classed as a merit good.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-15\"},{\"q\":\"A good is non-excludable but rivalrous. What is the most likely market problem?\",\"opts\":[\"It will be under-provided because of free riding\",\"It will be overused and depleted\",\"It will be provided efficiently\",\"It can be provided only by the government\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Non-excludable and rivalrous describes a common pool resource, such as an ocean fishery, where each user’s extraction depletes the stock for others. Under-provision is the public-good problem, which also requires non-rivalry; non-excludability alone is not enough.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-16\"},{\"q\":\"A city provides street lighting free of charge. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"It has no opportunity cost because users pay nothing\",\"It has an opportunity cost: the resources and taxes used could have gone elsewhere\",\"It costs nothing once installed because it is non-rivalrous\",\"It stops being a public good because the city owns it\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Being free at the point of use does not make a good free to society: labour, electricity and equipment are scarce, and the taxes that pay for them have other uses. Non-rivalry means an extra user adds no cost, but running the lights still does.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, using examples, why a free market may fail to provide public goods\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate direct provision and contracting out as government responses to public goods\",\"Paper 2 (a)–(c): define a public good or the free-rider problem, or distinguish a public good from a merit good, using the text\",\"Paper 2 (g): evaluate government spending on a good described in the text, using its characteristics\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"distinguish\",\"explain\",\"examine\",\"evaluate\",\"compare and contrast\",\"discuss\",\"justify\"],\"dg\":\"The guide sets no required diagram for 2.9 (guide PDF p. 37). If a diagram helps, a rivalry–excludability grid, or a sketch with MSB above revealed demand and Qmarket below Qopt, can support the argument, provided it is labelled and explained.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Apply both tests to the specific good in the question or text, say who free rides and who pays, and use a real case of public provision or contracting out.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh direct provision against contracting out on cost, quality and accountability; consider the opportunity cost of tax finance, the government’s information problem and whether technology could make the good excludable.\",\"errors\":[\"Calling any government-provided good a public good\",\"Relying on non-excludability alone and ignoring rivalry\",\"Confusing public goods with common pool resources or merit goods\",\"Assuming a public good has no cost because it is free at the point of use\"],\"strong\":[\"Test the named good against both characteristics before classifying it\",\"Build the free-rider chain step by step to under-provision and welfare loss\",\"Recognise quasi-public goods and how technology changes excludability\",\"Evaluate the forms of provision with a real example and the opportunity cost of funding\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using examples, why a free market may fail to provide public goods such as flood defences.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that governments should contract out the production of public goods to private firms.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a flood barrier for a town of 20,000 households would cost $30 million. A survey suggests that households value protection at $2,000 each on average, yet a voluntary appeal raised only $4 million. (i) Calculate the total estimated benefit and explain why it differs so much from the sum raised. (ii) Recommend whether the local government should build the barrier itself or contract it out.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Common pool resources share non-excludability with public goods but are rivalrous, so they are overused rather than under-provided.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"Direct provision, listed among the government tools in 2.7, is the main response to public goods.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"why\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 33): bounded selfishness helps explain why people contribute to public goods more than the free-rider model predicts.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Public goods are paid for from government revenue, so their provision competes with other government expenditure.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Interventionist supply-side policies such as the provision of infrastructure and support for research include goods with public-good features (guide PDF p. 50).\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"why\":\"Because asymmetric information (HL only, guide PDF p. 37) is the next source of market failure, where the problem lies in what buyers and sellers know rather than in the good itself; SL students continue with 3.1.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about spending on flood defences, street lighting, national defence, public broadcasting or a research programme, or about contracting such services out. Intervention or Economic well-being can frame the commentary: why the market would not supply the good, and whether the chosen form of provision serves the public well.\",\"ee\":\"How has a named city financed its flood defences, and to what extent does the free-rider problem explain the balance between public funding and private contributions?\",\"tok\":\"If people have reasons to hide how much they value a public good, on what basis can a government claim to know how much of it society wants?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can classify goods using rivalry and excludability, and explain why free riding leads to under-provision.\",\"starter\":\"Show ten images, such as a pizza, a lighthouse, a fishery, a toll road, a school and street lights, and ask pairs to place each on a two-by-two rivalry–excludability grid.\",\"activity\":\"Run a public-goods game: each student secretly puts some of ten tokens into a shared pot that is doubled and split equally; play several rounds, then allow discussion and compare contributions.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a city pay a private firm to run its street lighting? Weigh cost, quality and accountability.\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: classify three goods and justify each with both characteristics in one sentence.\",\"extension\":\"Research one example of a public good financed privately and explain how its supplier overcomes the free-rider problem.\",\"homework\":\"Write a short answer distinguishing a public good, a merit good and a common pool resource, with one real example of each.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define a public good using both non-rivalry and non-excludability.\",\"I can explain the free-rider problem as a chain from non-excludability to under-provision.\",\"I can place a named good on the rivalry–excludability grid and justify the placement.\",\"I can distinguish public goods from merit goods and common pool resources.\",\"I can explain why a free public good still has an opportunity cost.\",\"I can compare direct provision with contracting out on cost, quality and accountability.\",\"I can evaluate how technology or pricing can change whether a good is excludable.\",\"I can connect public goods to 2.8, 3.6 and 3.7.\",\"I can explain without notes why a private firm would not build a town’s flood barrier.\"]},\"2.10\":{\"code\":\"2.10\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"HL only. Markets work well when both sides know what is being traded. When one side knows more, such as the seller of a used car or the buyer of insurance, price cannot reflect true quality or risk. Trades that would benefit both sides are lost, or happen on the wrong terms: a market failure caused by unevenly held information.\",\"why\":\"Warranties, credit checks, insurance contracts and consumer-protection law largely exist to deal with it.\",\"q\":\"Why might an honest seller of a reliable used car be unable to get a fair price for it?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency: MSB = MSC maximises community surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"What market failure and welfare loss mean\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Bounded rationality and imperfect information\",\"sub\":\"2.4\"},{\"t\":\"Legislation, regulation and information campaigns as forms of intervention\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define asymmetric information, adverse selection and moral hazard\",\"State the guide’s government responses (legislation and regulation, provision of information) and private responses (signalling, screening)\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why adverse selection arises before a transaction and moral hazard after it\",\"Explain why asymmetric information is a market failure\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a real case as adverse selection or moral hazard and match a suitable response\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how pricing for average quality drives high-quality sellers out\",\"Analyse who acts in signalling and who acts in screening\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate a response in a named market on credibility, cost, enforcement and whether the information is understood\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a news report to identify which side holds the missing information\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a response to an information problem in a stated market, justified against an alternative\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"HL only: when one side knows more\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"This whole subtopic is HL only (guide PDF p. 37). Asymmetric information exists when one party to a transaction knows more than the other about what is being traded. If buyers cannot see quality, or sellers cannot see risk, price cannot reflect it. Some trades that would benefit both sides then do not happen, and others happen on the wrong terms. Resources do not go where marginal benefit equals marginal cost, so this is a market failure, even though each person acts sensibly given what they know.\"},{\"h\":\"Adverse selection: hidden information before the deal\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Adverse selection arises before a transaction, when one side has hidden information about quality or risk. Used-car sellers know whether their car is reliable; buyers do not, so they offer a price reflecting average quality. Owners of good cars find that price too low and hold back, so the cars on offer are worse than average, and buyers lower their offers again. Insurance runs the other way round: people who know they are high-risk are keenest to buy cover priced for average risk.\",\"because\":[\"Buyers cannot tell good cars from poor ones\",\"so they pay only for expected average quality\",\"sellers of above-average cars withdraw\",\"so average quality on offer falls, and offers fall with it\",\"and the market shrinks towards low quality\"]},{\"h\":\"Moral hazard: hidden action after the deal\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Moral hazard arises after a transaction, when one party changes its behaviour because someone else now bears part of the cost, and the change is hard to observe. An insured driver may take less care; a bank that expects a rescue may take bigger risks; an unsupervised employee may put in less effort. To tell the two apart, check timing, then what is hidden: a characteristic or an action. The remedies differ.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Adverse selection\",\"Moral hazard\"],\"rows\":[[\"When\",\"Before the transaction\",\"After the transaction\"],[\"What is hidden\",\"Characteristics: quality, risk\",\"Actions: care, effort, risk-taking\"],[\"Example\",\"High-risk drivers keenest to insure\",\"Insured drivers take less care\"],[\"Typical remedy\",\"Signalling, screening, disclosure, compulsory insurance\",\"An excess, co-payments, monitoring, rules on risk-taking\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Signalling and screening: who acts?\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"These are the private responses in the guide (guide PDF p. 37). Signalling is done by the informed party to reveal quality: a seller offers a warranty, an applicant shows a qualification, a firm builds a brand it would lose by selling poor products. Screening is done by the uninformed party to extract information: an insurer asks health questions, a lender checks credit history, an employer sets a test. A signal is credible only if a low-quality type would find it too costly to copy, as a long warranty on an unreliable car would be.\"},{\"h\":\"Government responses: rules and information\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide names legislation and regulation, and provision of information (guide PDF p. 37). Laws can require disclosure, such as ingredient lists or the full cost of a loan, ban misleading claims, and license professions so that patients need not judge a doctor’s competence. Compulsory insurance keeps low-risk people in the pool. Governments also publish information, such as inspection ratings. Each response has costs: regulators may know little more than consumers, rules must be enforced, and disclosure helps only if people understand it and act on it.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A used-car market in which buyers cannot check quality\",\"cause\":\"Sellers know whether their car is reliable; buyers cannot tell before buying.\",\"mechanism\":\"Buyers offer a price based on expected average quality.\",\"immediate\":\"Owners of above-average cars are offered less than their cars are worth, so many keep them.\",\"secondary\":\"Average quality on offer falls, buyers cut their offers again, and more good cars leave.\",\"longrun\":\"The market thins towards low quality unless warranties, inspections or dealer reputation restore trust.\",\"depends\":\"How cheaply quality can be verified, whether dealers value repeat business, and consumer-protection law.\"},{\"t\":\"Comprehensive insurance and the care people take\",\"cause\":\"An insurer covers the full cost of losses but cannot observe how carefully policyholders behave.\",\"mechanism\":\"The policyholder bears little of the cost of a loss, so the private return to taking care falls.\",\"immediate\":\"Some policyholders take less care, and claims rise.\",\"secondary\":\"Premiums rise for everyone, which may lead careful customers to buy less cover.\",\"longrun\":\"Contracts adapt: an excess, no-claims discounts and monitoring make policyholders share the cost of their behaviour.\",\"depends\":\"How far behaviour responds to cover, the cost of monitoring, the size of the excess, and whether the loss, such as injury, is one people avoid anyway.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Adverse selection: the market for ‘lemons’\",\"dg\":\"asym\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why a market can shrink when sellers know quality and buyers do not.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Sellers know quality; buyers know only the average\",\"Quality cannot be checked cheaply before purchase\",\"Buyers pay no more than expected value\"],\"mechanism\":\"Buyers pay for average quality, so sellers of above-average goods withdraw. Average quality falls, the price buyers will pay falls, and more sellers leave: the market unravels towards low quality.\",\"prediction\":\"Fewer trades than under full information, lower average quality, and incentives for warranties and inspections.\",\"application\":\"Used goods, insurance, lending, hiring and online marketplaces.\",\"limitations\":[\"Reputation and repeat business reward honesty\",\"Cheap verification, such as reviews or inspections, can remove the asymmetry\",\"Full collapse is extreme; many markets settle at a thinner, discounted equilibrium\"],\"real\":\"George Akerlof’s 1970 paper on used cars gave the model its name. Dealer warranties and vehicle-history reports suggest the problem is costly enough to pay to reduce.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling it moral hazard. Adverse selection concerns what is hidden at the moment of sale.\",\"exam\":\"In HL Paper 1 or 2, set out the unravelling chain, say why it is a market failure, then evaluate responses.\",\"deeper\":\"In insurance the buyer is the informed party. Michael Rothschild and Joseph Stiglitz showed that insurers can screen with a menu of contracts, such as cheaper cover with a large excess that mainly low-risk customers choose, so the choice reveals the type. Akerlof, Michael Spence and Stiglitz shared the 2001 Nobel memorial prize in economics for this work. Evidence is more mixed than theory: in some insurance markets, people who buy more cover are lower risk.\"},{\"model\":\"Moral hazard and the principal–agent problem\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains why shielding people from consequences changes their actions.\",\"assumptions\":[\"One party’s actions cannot be observed or verified\",\"The acting party bears only part of the cost of a bad outcome\",\"People respond to incentives\"],\"mechanism\":\"The principal (insurer, employer or taxpayer) bears some of the cost of the agent’s hidden choices, so the agent takes less care or more risk than if it bore the full cost.\",\"prediction\":\"More risk-taking or less effort after the contract, and contracts that share costs or add monitoring.\",\"application\":\"Insurance, employment, health care and government rescues of banks.\",\"limitations\":[\"People avoid uninsurable losses, such as injury, so behaviour may change little\",\"Monitoring technology can reveal actions\",\"Hard to measure, as risky people may also choose more cover\"],\"real\":\"After the 2008 global financial crisis, many economists argued that expected state support for very large banks had encouraged risk-taking, and regulators raised capital requirements. The size of the effect is debated.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating moral hazard as lying at the point of sale. It is a change in behaviour after the deal.\",\"exam\":\"In HL Paper 1 (b) or Paper 3 (b), note that a policy shielding people from losses, such as a bailout, may create moral hazard.\",\"deeper\":\"Principal–agent theory makes the trade-off precise. Full insurance spreads risk but removes the incentive to take care; no insurance keeps the incentive but leaves people exposed. The best contract usually lies between, with partial cover, so some risk stays with people who would prefer to shed it. No contract reaches the full-information optimum, because the hidden action cannot be written into it: a second-best outcome.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Hidden information should shrink markets and lower average quality; insurance should reduce the care people take.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests both effects exist but vary widely. Some insurance studies find that people with more cover claim more; others find the opposite. Many used-goods markets work reasonably well because of warranties and reviews.\",\"interp\":\"One reading is that private institutions absorb much of the damage. Economists disagree about when the remaining gap justifies mandates rather than lighter rules.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Selection or behaviour? A hard identification problem\",\"tag\":\"Causality\",\"p\":\"If people with generous insurance make more claims, is that adverse selection, because risky people chose generous cover, or moral hazard, because cover made them careless? The correlation alone cannot tell you. Economists look for cases where cover changes for reasons unrelated to risk. The RAND Health Insurance Experiment in the United States randomly assigned families to different levels of cost-sharing, which ruled out selection: families who paid less of the cost used more health care.\"},{\"h\":\"When better information hurts some people\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"Telematics, large datasets and genetic testing let insurers price risk more precisely. That reduces adverse selection and can raise efficiency. It also means people identified as high-risk may face much higher premiums or be refused cover, often for reasons outside their control, and some countries restrict insurers’ use of genetic test results. Efficiency and equity pull in different directions here, a tension that returns in 2.12. How much information insurers may use is a normative choice.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine buying a second-hand phone online. The seller knows whether the battery is failing; you do not, so you offer a cautious price. Sellers with excellent phones think your offer too low and keep them, so the phones left are worse, and you grow more cautious still. That is adverse selection: a problem hidden before the deal. Now you insure the phone and stop using a case. That is moral hazard: a change in behaviour after the deal because someone else pays. Either way, trades that would help people are lost.\",\"exam\":\"Define asymmetric information and name the problem by timing: adverse selection before the transaction, moral hazard after it. Explain the chain in context, for example: buyers pay for average quality, so high-quality sellers withdraw and average quality falls. State why this is a market failure: trades whose benefits exceed their costs do not happen, so community surplus is lost. The guide lists no diagram for 2.10, so a clear written chain carries the analysis. Evaluate responses by asking who acts, whether the information is credible and understood, what enforcement costs, and who loses, such as high-risk people excluded by screening.\",\"deep\":\"The competitive model assumes price summarises everything relevant about a good. Asymmetric information breaks that link. When quality or risk is hidden, price must both ration the good and sort the types, and it usually cannot do both. The market either pools types at one price, driving out the better ones, or relies on costly devices to separate them. Both fall short of the full-information optimum, so economists call the results second-best. The policy question is therefore comparative: signals, screening, regulation and mandates each narrow the gap at a cost, and the best choice depends on who holds the information, how cheaply it can be verified and how the response affects equity. A disclosure rule, for instance, is cheap to introduce but works only if people read it.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Hidden information or action stops trades whose benefits exceed their costs, so community surplus is lost.\",\"tension\":\"Pricing risk precisely can raise efficiency while pricing high-risk people out.\",\"ia\":\"An article on rising insurance premiums, analysed as lost trades and surplus.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Governments regulate, license, mandate insurance or publish information when private responses fall short.\",\"tension\":\"Regulators may know little more than consumers, and rules carry compliance costs.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new labelling or disclosure rule, judged on whether it is understood and enforced.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do most insurance policies make the customer pay the first part of any claim?\",\"expect\":\"Insurers might be expected to cover everything, since protection is what customers buy.\",\"model\":\"Moral hazard predicts that full cover weakens care, so an excess keeps some cost with the policyholder; a customer choosing a large excess also reveals low expected claims.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests people use less when they pay part of the cost, though the effect varies by market.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes, but selection and changed behaviour produce similar data, so which dominates is unclear.\",\"assume\":\"Policyholders respond to the cost they bear, and insurers cannot observe care.\",\"change\":\"Cheap, accurate monitoring would let insurers price behaviour directly.\",\"policy\":\"Rules on minimum cover trade protection against the incentive to take care.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how adverse selection can cause a market for used cars to shrink.\",\"check\":[\"Sellers know quality; buyers do not\",\"Buyers offer a price based on expected average quality\",\"Owners of above-average cars withdraw\",\"Average quality and buyers’ offers fall further\",\"Mutually beneficial trades are lost: a market failure\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Distinguish signalling from screening, with an example of each, and explain when a signal is credible.\",\"check\":[\"Signalling is done by the informed party\",\"A signalling example, such as a warranty\",\"Screening is done by the uninformed party\",\"A screening example, such as a credit check\",\"A signal is credible if too costly for a low-quality type to copy\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define asymmetric information.\",\"a\":\"One party to a transaction has more or better information than the other about what is traded.\"},{\"q\":\"Which problem arises before a transaction, and which after?\",\"a\":\"Adverse selection before (hidden characteristics); moral hazard after (hidden actions).\"},{\"q\":\"An insurer asks applicants about existing medical conditions. Signalling or screening?\",\"a\":\"Screening: the uninformed party acts to extract information.\"},{\"q\":\"On an adverse-selection sketch, who leaves first, and what happens to price?\",\"a\":\"Sellers of above-average goods; average quality and price then fall.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is asymmetric information a market failure?\",\"a\":\"Trades whose benefits exceed their costs do not happen, so community surplus is lost.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one limitation of mandatory disclosure.\",\"a\":\"It helps only if people read, understand and act on it, and it must be enforced.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Once her phone is insured against damage, Mei stops using a protective case. Which problem is this?\",\"opts\":[\"Adverse selection\",\"Moral hazard\",\"Signalling\",\"Screening\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Her behaviour changed after the deal because the insurer bears the cost: moral hazard. Adverse selection also involves insurance, but concerns hidden characteristics before the deal.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-17\"},{\"q\":\"A used-car dealer offers a two-year warranty on every car it sells. This is best described as:\",\"opts\":[\"Screening by the buyer\",\"Signalling by the seller\",\"Moral hazard by the dealer\",\"Government provision of information\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The informed party makes a costly promise to reveal quality: signalling. Screening is done by the uninformed party, for example a buyer paying for an inspection.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-17\"},{\"q\":\"Standard nutrition labels become compulsory. What matters most for them to reduce the information problem?\",\"opts\":[\"Consumers understand the labels and use them when choosing\",\"The labels raise the price of less healthy food\",\"Firms advertise more in response\",\"The market was already allocatively efficient\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Disclosure narrows the gap only if the information is understood and used. A label does not change price directly, so the second option confuses information with a tax.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"HL Paper 1 (b): evaluate responses to asymmetric information in a named market (HL papers include HL extension material, guide PDF p. 68)\",\"HL Paper 1 (a): explain how asymmetric information causes market failure\",\"HL Paper 3 (b): recommend a policy where an information problem is part of the context\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"to what extent\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"The guide lists no diagram for 2.10 (guide PDF p. 37). A written causal chain carries the analysis; an optional sketch must be explained in words.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Say who holds the information, what is hidden and when, in the article’s named market, then match the response to the problem.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh credibility, comprehension, enforcement cost, whether private signals already work, and who screening excludes.\",\"errors\":[\"Swapping adverse selection and moral hazard, or ignoring timing\",\"Saying the uninformed buyer signals\",\"Assuming disclosure works without asking whether it is understood or enforced\"],\"strong\":[\"Name the problem, its timing and what is hidden, in context\",\"Build a step-by-step chain such as the unravelling of average quality\",\"Compare a private and a government response on credibility, cost and equity\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain how asymmetric information between buyers and sellers can lead to market failure in the market for used cars.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that private responses such as warranties and credit checks make government intervention unnecessary in markets with asymmetric information.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative scenario: in Country Z, health insurance is voluntary, and insurers report that young, healthy adults are dropping out while premiums rise for those who remain. Recommend a policy the government could introduce, explaining the information problem and one drawback of your recommendation.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Information failure also explains the overconsumption of demerit goods, and the responses overlap.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"why\":\"Brands that signal quality also act as non-price competition and barriers to entry.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Microfinance (guide PDF p. 59) is partly a response to lenders’ lack of information about poor borrowers.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"why\":\"Because market power is the next HL market failure, evaluated in the same way: a private advantage weighed against the cost of intervention.\"},\"ia\":\"An article on insurance premiums, an online-marketplace scam, a food-labelling rule or mis-sold financial products would suit this subtopic, framed through Efficiency (lost trades) or Intervention (a disclosure or licensing rule).\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did a mandatory disclosure rule, such as vehicle-history reports, change prices in a chosen market? Price data before and after the rule could be compared.\",\"tok\":\"When a deal fails because one side cannot verify the other’s claims, is the obstacle a gap in knowledge, a lack of trust, or both, and can economics tell them apart?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students distinguish adverse selection from moral hazard by timing and match each to a credible response.\",\"starter\":\"Sort six short scenarios into ‘before the deal’ and ‘after the deal’.\",\"activity\":\"A ‘lemons’ auction: sellers hold hidden car values, buyers bid knowing only the range; record falling quality and prices, then allow a warranty.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a degree a signal of ability, evidence of skills, or both?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: classify three cases and name a response for each.\",\"extension\":\"Explain how random assignment in the RAND experiment rules out selection.\",\"homework\":\"Answer the Paper 1 (a) practice question with a numbered causal chain.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define asymmetric information, adverse selection and moral hazard\",\"I can tell the two problems apart by timing and by what is hidden\",\"I can explain how pricing for average quality drives good products out\",\"I can distinguish signalling from screening and say who acts\",\"I can explain the government responses named in the guide\",\"I can evaluate a response on credibility, cost, enforcement and equity\",\"I can connect this topic to 2.4 and 2.8 and explain it without notes\"]},\"2.11\":{\"code\":\"2.11\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"HL only. Market power is a firm’s ability to set price above marginal cost because buyers lack close substitutes. Every profit-maximising firm produces where MC = MR, but market structure decides how far price rises above MC: not at all in perfect competition, and usually most in monopoly. The gap means lost output and surplus, though large firms may also bring lower costs and innovation.\",\"why\":\"Concentrated markets can mean higher prices, lower output and less choice, which is why competition authorities exist.\",\"q\":\"Why can a firm with few rivals charge well above the cost of its last unit, and when should the government step in?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Diminishing marginal returns and increasing marginal cost (HL)\",\"sub\":\"2.2\"},{\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency: MB = MC and maximum community surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Welfare loss as lost community surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Profit maximisation and other business objectives\",\"sub\":\"2.4\"},{\"t\":\"Totals, averages and marginals from a table\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the defining conditions of the four market structures\",\"Define TR, TC, AR, AC, MR, MC, abnormal and normal profit, and losses\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why firms maximise profit where MC = MR and read price from AR\",\"Explain why free entry removes long-run abnormal profit\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a real market using barriers to entry, product type and a concentration ratio\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse the welfare loss of monopoly against a competitive market with the same costs\",\"Analyse a payoff matrix to explain the incentives to collude and to cheat\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the claim that monopoly is worse for society than competition\",\"Evaluate legislation, regulation, government ownership and fines\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate profit, MC, MR, AC and AR from data and find the profit-maximising output (HL only)\",\"Calculate a concentration ratio from market shares\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw the perfectly competitive, monopoly and monopolistically competitive firm with abnormal profit, normal profit and losses\",\"Draw monopoly welfare loss, natural monopoly and collusive oligopoly\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a cost-and-revenue table and a payoff matrix\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a response to a dominant firm or cartel, justified against an alternative\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"HL only: price takers and price makers\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"This subtopic is HL only (guide PDF pp. 37–39). Market power is the ability of a firm, or firms acting together, to raise price above the level perfect competition would produce. A perfectly competitive firm is a price taker: one of many sellers of an identical product. A firm facing a downward-sloping demand curve is a price maker. Structures differ in the number of firms, the product and barriers to entry: economies of scale, patents and licences, control of a key resource, brands and network effects. At a price maker’s chosen output AR > MC, so some units worth more than they cost go unproduced: a market failure.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Perfect competition\",\"Monopolistic competition\",\"Oligopoly\",\"Monopoly\"],\"rows\":[[\"Firms\",\"Very many\",\"Many\",\"A few, interdependent\",\"One, or one dominant\"],[\"Product\",\"Homogeneous\",\"Differentiated\",\"Similar or differentiated\",\"No close substitutes\"],[\"Entry\",\"Free\",\"Free\",\"High barriers\",\"High barriers\"],[\"Firm’s demand\",\"Horizontal: P = D = AR = MR\",\"Downward-sloping, fairly elastic\",\"Depends on rivals\",\"Market demand\"],[\"Long-run profit\",\"Normal\",\"Normal\",\"Abnormal possible\",\"Abnormal possible\"],[\"Price and MC\",\"P = MC\",\"P > MC\",\"P > MC\",\"P > MC\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Costs, revenues and the MC = MR rule\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Total revenue (TR) is P × Q; average revenue (AR = TR ÷ Q) equals price, so the AR curve is the demand curve. Marginal revenue (MR = ΔTR ÷ ΔQ) equals P for a price taker but is below P for a price maker, which must cut price on all units to sell one more. Marginal cost (MC = ΔTC ÷ ΔQ) cuts average cost (AC = TC ÷ Q) at its minimum: below AC it pulls the average down, above AC it pulls it up. Profit = TR − TC = (AR − AC) × Q. Normal profit (AR = AC) just keeps the firm in the industry and is counted within costs; AR > AC is abnormal profit, AR < AC a loss.\",\"because\":[\"If MR > MC, the next unit adds more to revenue than to cost\",\"so producing it raises profit\",\"if MR < MC, the last unit lowers profit\",\"so profit peaks where MC = MR, with MC rising\",\"and price is read from AR at that output\"]},{\"h\":\"Free entry pushes profit back to normal\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A perfectly competitive firm faces a horizontal demand curve at the market price, so P = D = AR = MR and it produces where MC = P. In the short run it earns abnormal profit if price exceeds AC, normal profit if P = AC, and a loss if price is below AC. Abnormal profit attracts entry, supply rises and price falls until firms earn normal profit at minimum AC; losses cause exit. Because each firm sets P = MC, the market is allocatively efficient and community surplus is maximised. Monopolistic competition follows the same entry logic, but each firm’s demand slopes down, though more elastically than a monopolist’s. In the long run AR just touches AC: normal profit, yet P > MC, a cost accepted in exchange for variety.\"},{\"h\":\"Monopoly, welfare loss and natural monopoly\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A monopolist faces the market demand curve. It produces Qm where MC = MR and charges Pm read from AR. Barriers let abnormal profit persist, though weak demand or high costs could leave normal profit or a loss. A competitive industry with the same costs would produce where MC meets demand, at a lower price and higher output. Units between the two outputs are valued above their marginal cost but not produced; that lost surplus is the welfare loss. In a natural monopoly, economies of scale are so large that AC is still falling where it meets market demand, so one firm can supply the whole market more cheaply than two or more.\"},{\"h\":\"Oligopoly: collusion, rivalry and the prisoner’s dilemma\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Oligopolists are interdependent: each firm’s best price depends on rivals’ reactions. Colluding firms, in a formal cartel or tacitly, can act as a monopoly, setting joint output where MC = MR; price-fixing is illegal in many countries. Non-collusive firms often avoid price cuts that risk a price war, competing instead through branding and innovation. In the illustrative matrix, a low price is each firm’s dominant strategy, as it pays more whatever the rival does, so both earn 30 when each could earn 50: the prisoner’s dilemma, with an incentive to collude and an incentive to cheat. A concentration ratio measures dominance: CR4 is the combined share of the four largest firms.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Illustrative profit in €m (A’s, B’s)\",\"B: high price\",\"B: low price\"],\"rows\":[[\"A: high price\",\"50, 50\",\"20, 70\"],[\"A: low price\",\"70, 20\",\"30, 30\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Large firms: benefits, risks and government responses\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for balance (guide PDF p. 39). Large firms may gain economies of scale, as in natural monopoly, and abnormal profit may fund research and development. The risks are higher prices, lower output and less consumer choice. The guide’s responses to abuse of significant market power are legislation and regulation, government ownership and fines. Each has costs as well as benefits.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Response\",\"How it works\",\"Main limitation\"],\"rows\":[[\"Legislation\",\"Bans cartels and abuse of dominance; lets mergers be blocked\",\"Slow, costly, hard to establish\"],[\"Regulation\",\"Price caps and service rules, often for natural monopolies\",\"Regulator lacks cost data; capture risk\"],[\"Government ownership (nationalisation)\",\"The state runs the firm, pricing nearer to cost\",\"Weaker pressure to cut costs\"],[\"Fines\",\"Penalties deter cartels and abuse\",\"Weak if small relative to gains\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"New cafés open near profitable ones (monopolistic competition)\",\"cause\":\"Existing cafés earn abnormal profit, and entry is easy.\",\"mechanism\":\"New firms enter with similar but differentiated products.\",\"immediate\":\"Each café’s demand shifts left and becomes more elastic as close substitutes multiply.\",\"secondary\":\"Price and sales per café fall, so abnormal profit shrinks.\",\"longrun\":\"Entry stops when AR just touches AC: normal profit, with P > MC.\",\"depends\":\"Strength of brand loyalty, entry costs such as rent, and demand growth.\"},{\"t\":\"A regulator caps the price of a natural monopoly\",\"cause\":\"One firm runs a water network; AC falls across the whole market and MC lies below AC.\",\"mechanism\":\"The regulator sets a maximum price below the profit-maximising price.\",\"immediate\":\"Output rises and consumers gain, while abnormal profit falls.\",\"secondary\":\"A cap at P = MC would be allocatively efficient but below AC, so the firm would need a subsidy; a cap at P = AC gives normal profit.\",\"longrun\":\"A cap fixed for several years rewards cost-cutting; one set too low can deter investment.\",\"depends\":\"The regulator’s knowledge of costs, the cap’s level and the risk of regulatory capture.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Monopoly compared with perfect competition\",\"dg\":\"monopoly\",\"purpose\":\"Shows why a single seller restricts output and raises price, and measures the surplus lost.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The monopolist maximises profit and faces market demand, so MR lies below AR\",\"A competitive industry would have the same MC, acting as its supply curve\",\"Barriers stop entry\"],\"mechanism\":\"The monopolist produces Qm where MC = MR and charges Pm from AR. A competitive industry with the same costs would produce where MC meets demand. Units between Qm and that output are valued above MC but not produced.\",\"prediction\":\"Higher price, lower output, P > MC and a welfare-loss triangle.\",\"application\":\"Mergers, dominant platforms, patent-protected products and unregulated utilities.\",\"limitations\":[\"Scale economies may give a monopolist lower costs and a lower price\",\"Static: it ignores innovation and the threat of entry\",\"Demand and MC are estimates, so the loss is uncertain\"],\"real\":\"The European Commission fined Google in several antitrust cases in the late 2010s, and the EU’s Digital Markets Act sets advance rules for the largest platforms. Their effects are still debated.\",\"misconception\":\"Reading price from MR, or saying the monopolist charges ‘the highest possible price’. It picks the profit-maximising point on the demand curve.\",\"exam\":\"For HL Paper 1 (b): label Pm, Qm, the competitive outcome and the welfare loss, then evaluate with scale economies and R&D.\",\"deeper\":\"Arnold Harberger’s estimates in the 1950s suggested the allocative loss from monopoly in US manufacturing was small. Later economists argued the cost could be larger once two further effects are counted: resources spent winning and defending market power (rent-seeking), and slack cost control in sheltered firms (X-inefficiency). The welfare-loss triangle is therefore a starting point for evaluation, not its conclusion.\"},{\"model\":\"Oligopoly as a game: the prisoner’s dilemma\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Shows why interdependent firms can end up worse off together, and why collusion is unstable.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Two firms each choose a high or a low price\",\"Payoffs are known but agreements cannot be enforced\",\"Each maximises its own profit in a one-off game\"],\"mechanism\":\"Whatever the rival does, each firm earns more with a low price, so a low price is the dominant strategy. Both choose it, and each earns less than if both had kept prices high.\",\"prediction\":\"Without enforceable agreements, low prices or price wars; joint gains give an incentive to collude, undercutting an incentive to cheat.\",\"application\":\"Price wars, advertising budgets, and cartel quotas such as those agreed by OPEC+ oil producers.\",\"limitations\":[\"Repeated interaction lets the threat of retaliation sustain tacit collusion\",\"Real payoffs are rarely known\",\"Firms may compete on quality and innovation instead of price\"],\"real\":\"After Reliance Jio entered India’s mobile market in 2016 with very low prices, rivals cut prices and several operators exited or merged. The pattern fits a price war, though debt and regulation also mattered.\",\"misconception\":\"Believing ‘oligopolists always collude’, or confusing a dominant strategy with the outcome that is best for both firms.\",\"exam\":\"Identify each dominant strategy and the equilibrium, then explain the incentives to collude and cheat with a real case.\",\"deeper\":\"An outcome in which neither firm can gain by changing its own choice alone is a Nash equilibrium. When the game repeats with no known end, strategies that reward cooperation and punish cheating can sustain high prices. Competition authorities turn the dilemma on cartels: leniency programmes cut penalties for the first member to report the cartel, so each member fears being reported by the others.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"More concentrated markets should show prices further above marginal cost, and mergers that remove close rivals should raise prices.\",\"evidence\":\"Merger studies often find price rises where close competitors combined, though some mergers cut costs. Research in the 2010s suggested average markups have risen in some advanced economies; the measurement is disputed.\",\"interp\":\"One view blames weak competition enforcement. Another sees higher markups as a return on heavy fixed investment by highly productive firms, which would call for a different policy.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Perfect competition is a benchmark, not a description\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"Few markets, perhaps some agricultural commodities, come close to perfect competition. Its value is as a yardstick showing where P = MC. But it is static, taking technology and products as given. Joseph Schumpeter argued that innovation comes from firms expecting temporary market power, so an economy of price takers might be efficient today yet slow to improve. The guide’s point that abnormal profit may finance R&D (guide PDF p. 39) reflects this dynamic view.\"},{\"h\":\"What a concentration ratio can miss\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"A CR4 adds the shares of the four largest firms, so it cannot tell four equal firms from one leader with most of the market. It also depends on how the market is defined: national figures can hide local monopolies, such as a town’s only cinema, and ignore imports that discipline domestic firms. Competition authorities often use the Herfindahl–Hirschman Index, which sums the squared shares of all firms and so weights large firms more heavily. The HHI goes beyond the guide, but shows why a concentration ratio alone rarely settles a case.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Picture the only bakery in a remote town. It can raise prices without losing every customer, because there is nowhere else to go. It will not charge an absurd price, because it would sell almost nothing; it picks the most profitable price. That price is above what the last loaf costs to bake, so some people who would happily pay more than that cost go without. That lost benefit is the welfare loss. When a second bakery opens, the first loses some of its power; when many open, prices fall towards cost.\",\"exam\":\"Define market power and identify the structure from evidence: number of firms, barriers, product type and a concentration ratio. Draw the relevant diagram with MC through the minimum of AC, MR below AR, output at MC = MR and price read up to AR; shade profit as (AR − AC) × Q. For welfare, compare monopoly with a competitive market of the same costs and shade the welfare loss. Explain the chain: restricted output, P > MC, allocative inefficiency. Evaluate by weighing economies of scale, natural monopoly and R&D against higher prices and less choice, and consider barriers, time horizon and the risks of regulation or state ownership.\",\"deep\":\"This subtopic rests on one rule applied four times: each firm maximises profit given the demand curve it faces. The structures differ in that demand curve and in whether entry competes profit away. Two complications matter. In oligopoly, the demand a firm faces depends on rivals’ reactions, so there is no single answer without assumptions about strategy; that is why game theory is needed. And the welfare comparison assumes monopoly and competition share the same costs. With strong economies of scale, a single firm may have lower costs, so its profit-maximising price could be below that of many small firms. Whether market power harms society is therefore an empirical question about costs, demand, innovation and entry, not a matter of definition.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Where P > MC, output is below the allocatively efficient level and surplus is lost.\",\"tension\":\"Large firms may be allocatively inefficient yet gain scale economies and fund R&D.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a merger, analysed with the welfare-loss diagram and possible cost savings.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"Each oligopolist’s best price depends on its rivals’ choices.\",\"tension\":\"Cooperation raises firms’ profits but harms consumers.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a price war or cartel, analysed with a payoff matrix.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Legislation, regulation, government ownership and fines limit the abuse of market power.\",\"tension\":\"Intervention can lower prices but may weaken investment.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a price cap or competition fine, judged on price, output and innovation.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why might a competition authority block a merger of two supermarket chains that would lower their costs?\",\"expect\":\"Lower costs might be expected to mean lower prices for shoppers.\",\"model\":\"Removing a rival lets the merged firm raise price above MC; lower MC pushes the other way.\",\"evidence\":\"Merger studies often find price rises where close competitors combine; promised savings are sometimes smaller than claimed.\",\"match\":\"Partly: the outcome depends on local market shares, entry conditions and the size of the savings.\",\"assume\":\"Profit maximisation, difficult entry and genuine cost savings.\",\"change\":\"Easy entry by rivals, or evidence that savings would reach consumers.\",\"policy\":\"Approval with conditions, such as selling stores where the two chains overlap.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a monopolist charges more and produces less than a competitive industry with the same costs.\",\"check\":[\"MR lies below AR\",\"Output where MC = MR; price read from AR\",\"A competitive industry produces where MC meets demand\",\"So monopoly price is higher and output lower\",\"Units valued above MC go unproduced: the welfare loss\",\"The comparison assumes identical costs\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why abnormal profit disappears in the long run under monopolistic competition.\",\"check\":[\"Short-run abnormal profit where AR > AC at the MC = MR output\",\"No barriers, so firms enter with close substitutes\",\"Each firm’s demand shifts left and becomes more elastic\",\"Entry continues until AR just touches AC\",\"Profit is normal, but P > MC remains\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"What is normal profit?\",\"a\":\"Profit where AR = AC: the minimum return that keeps the firm in the industry, counted within costs.\"},{\"q\":\"On a monopoly diagram, where are output and price found?\",\"a\":\"Output where MC = MR; price read up from that output to AR.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: TC rises from $200 to $230 as output rises from 10 to 11. What is MC?\",\"a\":\"ΔTC ÷ ΔQ = $30 ÷ 1 = $30.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the five largest firms hold 30%, 22%, 18%, 10% and 5%. What is the CR4?\",\"a\":\"30 + 22 + 18 + 10 = 80%.\"},{\"q\":\"What is a dominant strategy?\",\"a\":\"A choice that gives a higher payoff whatever the rival does.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one possible benefit of monopoly.\",\"a\":\"Scale economies may lower costs; abnormal profit may fund R&D.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A monopolist produces where MC = MR. Where is its price read from?\",\"opts\":[\"The MR curve at that output\",\"The AR (demand) curve directly above that output\",\"The point where MC cuts AR\",\"The minimum point of AC\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Price is what buyers pay for the chosen quantity, shown by AR. MR is tempting, but for a price maker it lies below price.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-18\"},{\"q\":\"In long-run equilibrium, a monopolistically competitive firm:\",\"opts\":[\"keeps abnormal profit because its product is differentiated\",\"earns normal profit, with price above marginal cost\",\"earns normal profit, with price equal to marginal cost\",\"produces at the minimum of its AC curve\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Entry erodes abnormal profit until AR just touches AC, where AC is still falling, so P > MC. The last two options describe perfect competition.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Where should the MC curve cut the AC curve?\",\"opts\":[\"At the minimum point of AC\",\"Where AC is still falling\",\"At the minimum point of MC\",\"At the profit-maximising output\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"MC below AC pulls the average down and MC above it pulls it up, so they meet where AC stops falling. Crossing at MC’s own minimum is a common error.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-19\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"HL Paper 1 (a) or (b): explain a structure’s short- and long-run outcomes, or evaluate whether monopoly harms society (HL papers include HL extension material, guide PDF p. 68)\",\"Paper 2 at HL: calculate profit, AR, AC, MR or MC from data, or read a payoff matrix\",\"HL Paper 3: calculate from cost and revenue data, then recommend a response to market power\"],\"ct\":[\"calculate\",\"draw\",\"explain\",\"compare and contrast\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"The guide lists ten 2.11 diagrams, from the price-taking firm to monopolistic competition, plus a simple game-theory payoff matrix (guide PDF pp. 38–39). Draw MC through the minimum of AC and MR below AR for price makers; mark output at MC = MR and read price up to AR; shade profit or loss as (AR − AC) × Q and label the welfare loss.\",\"calc\":\"HL only: profit, MC, MR, AC and AR from data (guide PDF p. 38). Show formulas and units, and check the MC = MR output against the highest TR − TC.\",\"apply\":\"Classify the named market with evidence and draw the diagram for that firm.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh higher prices and less choice against scale economies and R&D; consider barriers, time horizon and government failure.\",\"errors\":[\"Reading price from MR instead of AR\",\"Drawing MC through AC away from its minimum, or MR as steep as AR\",\"Claiming long-run abnormal profit under monopolistic competition\",\"Condemning monopoly without weighing scale economies or innovation\"],\"strong\":[\"Identify the structure with evidence\",\"Label every intersection and area precisely\",\"State the identical-costs assumption\",\"Judge a named policy against an alternative\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why a firm in monopolistic competition earns only normal profit in the long run.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that governments should prevent mergers between large firms.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a monopolist sells 1 to 5 units at prices of $50, $45, $40, $35 and $30. Total cost is $20 at zero output, then $40, $55, $75, $100 and $135. (i) Calculate MR and MC. (ii) Find the profit-maximising output and profit. (iii) Recommend whether a regulator should cap the price.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"Competitive equilibrium, where MB = MC, is the benchmark for measuring monopoly welfare loss.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"why\":\"Goals other than profit, such as market share, can change the MC = MR prediction.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"why\":\"Regulators of natural monopolies face an information gap: the firm knows its costs better.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Anti-monopoly regulation, privatisation and deregulation return as market-based supply-side policies (guide PDF p. 49).\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"2.12\",\"why\":\"Because abnormal profit from market power flows to the owners of firms, and 2.12 asks why market outcomes can leave income and wealth unequally distributed.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on a merger ruling, a cartel fine, a price war or a price cap suit this subtopic, framed through Intervention or Interdependence.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did a new entrant, such as a budget airline on a route, change prices in a chosen market? Fare data before and after entry could be compared.\",\"tok\":\"Economists judge real markets against perfect competition, which almost no market matches. Can a model that describes almost nothing directly still give reliable knowledge about real markets?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students locate output at MC = MR and price on AR, and evaluate monopoly against a competitive benchmark.\",\"starter\":\"Pairs rank ten familiar markets by how easily a newcomer could enter.\",\"activity\":\"A two-firm pricing game from a payoff matrix, played once, then over ten rounds; compare cooperation.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a dominant platform that is free to users be broken up?\",\"check\":\"Mini-whiteboards: draw a monopoly with MC through minimum AC, then mark Qm, Pm, profit and the welfare loss.\",\"extension\":\"Use the prisoner’s dilemma to explain leniency programmes.\",\"homework\":\"Complete the Paper 3 practice calculations.\"},\"after\":[\"I can describe the four structures and the barriers that separate them\",\"I can define the cost and revenue concepts and types of profit\",\"I can explain MC = MR and read price from AR\",\"I can draw the guide’s diagrams for each market structure\",\"I can calculate profit, MC, MR, AC, AR and a concentration ratio\",\"I can explain monopoly welfare loss and natural monopoly\",\"I can use a payoff matrix to explain collusion and cheating\",\"I can evaluate monopoly and government responses without notes\"]},\"2.12\":{\"code\":\"2.12\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"HL only. A free market pays each household for the factors of production it sells: wages for labour, rent for land, interest for capital and profit for enterprise. Households own very different amounts of these factors, and the market values them differently, so incomes and wealth can be highly unequal even when markets are efficient. Whether that is fair is a normative question.\",\"why\":\"The market that allocates resources also decides for whom goods are produced, so governments must judge how far to accept its distribution.\",\"q\":\"If every market in an economy worked efficiently, would the distribution of income be fair?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Factors of production and their rewards: wages, rent, interest and profit\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"The circular flow of income model\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Equity versus equality, and positive versus normative statements\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency and community surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Promoting equity as a reason for intervention\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State that factor incomes are payments for factor services households sell to firms\",\"Distinguish equity from equality, and income (a flow) from wealth (a stock)\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a free market distributes income according to the quantity and market value of factors owned\",\"Explain why an allocatively efficient outcome can still be judged inequitable\"],\"apply\":[\"Use the circular flow model to show why households owning few marketable factors receive little income\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how wealth and income reinforce each other over time\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Discuss whether unequal market outcomes are a problem, separating positive from normative claims\",\"Examine whether inequity is a market failure in the same sense as externalities\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw the circular flow, labelling factor services and factor incomes\"],\"interpret\":[\"Classify statements about the distribution of income as positive or normative\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"HL only: efficient is not the same as fair\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"This subtopic is HL only, and its guide content is narrow: how the workings of a free market economy may produce an unequal distribution of income and wealth, shown with the circular flow model (guide PDF p. 39). The guide treats equity as fairness, a normative idea, distinct from equality, which describes how similar outcomes are (guide PDF p. 19). Markets answer the question ‘for whom?’ by purchasing power: goods go to those willing and able to pay. That can be efficient. Whether it is fair depends on how purchasing power was distributed.\"},{\"h\":\"Incomes come from what you own\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"In the circular flow, households sell the services of land, labour, capital and enterprise to firms, and firms pay them factor incomes: rent, wages, interest and profit. A household’s market income is therefore the quantity of factors it owns multiplied by the price the market pays for them. A surgeon, a landlord and a shareholder receive large factor incomes; a household with only unskilled labour receives the market wage for that labour; a household with nothing to sell receives nothing. The market does not ask how ownership came about, or what anyone needs.\",\"because\":[\"Households own different amounts of land, skills, capital and enterprise\",\"firms pay for factors at prices set in factor markets\",\"scarce factors in high demand earn high rewards\",\"so owners of more, or more valuable, factors receive more income\",\"and their spending decides for whom output is produced\"]},{\"h\":\"Income and wealth: a flow and a stock\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Income is a flow received over a period, such as wages per month. Wealth is a stock of assets owned at a point in time, such as property, shares and savings. The two reinforce each other. Wealth yields income through rent, interest and dividends; saved income adds to wealth; and wealth can be inherited, so advantages pass across generations. Available data suggest that in most countries wealth is distributed more unequally than income, consistent with this cumulative process.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Income\",\"Wealth\"],\"rows\":[[\"Type of variable\",\"Flow (per period)\",\"Stock (at a point in time)\"],[\"Examples\",\"Wages, rent, interest, profit\",\"Housing, land, shares, savings\"],[\"How markets widen gaps\",\"Scarce skills and assets earn more\",\"Returns are reinvested; inheritance\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why some people receive little market income\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"People outside the labour market, such as young children, many older people, carers and some people with long-term illness or disability, have little labour to sell, so a pure market gives them little or no income. Differences in education and training create differences in pay. Shifts in demand, as when technology raises demand for some skills and reduces it for others, change factor prices regardless of effort. Market power (2.11) lets owners of dominant firms earn abnormal profit. 3.4 examines these and other causes of inequality, and the policies that respond to them (guide PDF pp. 45–46).\"},{\"h\":\"Efficiency and equity are separate tests\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A market can be allocatively efficient and inequitable at the same time. Allocative efficiency asks whether resources go where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost, with benefit measured by willingness and ability to pay; it takes the distribution of income as given. A wealthy buyer’s demand counts for more than a poor buyer’s need. Many economists therefore treat inequity as separate from market failure in the allocative sense, although the guide groups both among outcomes that may be socially undesirable (guide PDF p. 35).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Efficiency\",\"Equity\"],\"rows\":[[\"Question\",\"Do resources go where MSB = MSC?\",\"Is the distribution fair?\"],[\"Judgement\",\"Largely positive, based on estimates\",\"Normative: depends on values\"],[\"Can a free market achieve it?\",\"It can, without market failures\",\"Not necessarily: outcomes reflect who owns what\"],[\"Where the course responds\",\"Market failure (2.8–2.11)\",\"Inequality and poverty (3.4)\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Unequal ownership of factors in a free market\",\"cause\":\"Household A owns rental property, shares and a professional qualification; household B owns only its members’ unskilled labour.\",\"mechanism\":\"Factor markets pay each household the market price for what it sells.\",\"immediate\":\"A receives rent, dividends and a high salary; B receives the market wage for unskilled work, or nothing if unemployed.\",\"secondary\":\"A can save much of its income and buy more assets; B saves little.\",\"longrun\":\"The wealth gap widens as A’s returns are reinvested, and inheritance can pass the advantage on.\",\"depends\":\"Returns on assets relative to wage growth, access to education and credit for B’s children, and taxes and transfers.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The circular flow: factor markets and distribution\",\"dg\":\"circular\",\"purpose\":\"Shows where market incomes come from, and so why the distribution of income follows factor ownership.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Households own the factors and sell their services to firms\",\"Firms pay factor incomes and sell output to households\",\"In the free-market version, no government taxes or makes transfers\"],\"mechanism\":\"Factor services flow to firms and factor incomes flow back. Each household’s share equals the factors it supplies multiplied by their market prices.\",\"prediction\":\"Households with more, or more highly valued, factors receive more income; those with none receive no market income.\",\"application\":\"Explaining the market origin of inequality before taxes and transfers.\",\"limitations\":[\"It shows flows between sectors, not between rich and poor, so the distribution must be explained in words\",\"It does not explain why ownership differs\",\"It is static and ignores how wealth accumulates\"],\"real\":\"In many high-income countries, available data suggest market income is considerably more unequal than income after taxes and transfers.\",\"misconception\":\"Using injections and withdrawals to explain inequality. The relevant flows are factor services and factor incomes.\",\"exam\":\"For HL Paper 1 (a), draw and label the factor-market flows, then explain why unequal ownership produces unequal incomes.\",\"deeper\":\"Economists distinguish the functional distribution of income, the shares going to labour and to capital, from the personal distribution among households. The circular flow speaks to the first. The second depends on who owns capital: if asset ownership is concentrated, a rising capital share widens household inequality. Thomas Piketty argued that when the return on capital exceeds the growth rate, wealth tends to concentrate; critics dispute the evidence and the mechanism.\"},{\"model\":\"Efficiency given the distribution of income\",\"dg\":\"ds\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why a market that maximises community surplus can still produce an outcome many judge unfair.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Demand reflects willingness and ability to pay\",\"Community surplus adds money gains regardless of who receives them\",\"The income distribution is taken as given\"],\"mechanism\":\"At the competitive equilibrium every unit worth more to a buyer than it costs is produced. But value is measured in money, and more income means stronger demand, so goods flow to purchasing power rather than need.\",\"prediction\":\"A different starting distribution would give a different efficient outcome.\",\"application\":\"Judging markets for essentials such as housing, health care and food.\",\"limitations\":[\"Fairness is normative, so economics can clarify trade-offs but not settle them\",\"Redistribution may weaken incentives, by an amount that is disputed\",\"Some inequality reflects choices about effort, education or risk\"],\"real\":\"In many large cities, housing markets ration homes to those able to pay, which is why governments debate rent controls (2.7).\",\"misconception\":\"Calling inequality an allocative market failure. A market can reach MSB = MSC and still be judged inequitable.\",\"exam\":\"In HL Paper 1 (b) or Paper 3 (b), say whether a problem is inefficiency, inequity or both.\",\"deeper\":\"Welfare economics makes this precise. The first fundamental theorem says that, under strict conditions, competitive markets reach a Pareto-efficient outcome, where no one can gain without someone losing, but it says nothing about which one. The second says any efficient outcome could be reached by redistributing initial endowments and letting markets work, using lump-sum transfers that do not depend on behaviour. Real taxes do depend on behaviour, so redistribution usually carries some efficiency cost.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Free-market incomes depend on the quantity and price of factors owned, so unequal ownership produces unequal incomes, and saving and inheritance can widen wealth gaps further.\",\"evidence\":\"Available data suggest wealth is more concentrated than income, and that inequality varies widely between countries with similar market systems.\",\"interp\":\"That variation suggests institutions and policy shape final outcomes. Economists disagree about how much market inequality reflects productivity and effort rather than inheritance, luck and power.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Which inequality: income, wealth or opportunity?\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"The guide notes that inequality can refer to income, wealth or human opportunity (guide PDF p. 19), and these tell different stories. Income can be measured before or after taxes and transfers, over a year or a lifetime. Wealth is harder to measure because assets are revalued and the richest households are under-represented in surveys. Opportunity asks whether outcomes depend on circumstances people did not choose, such as parents’ income. Arguments about fairness often turn on which measure is used.\"},{\"h\":\"Is a market outcome fair if the process was fair?\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"Robert Nozick held that a distribution is just if it arose from voluntary exchange and legitimately acquired property. John Rawls asked what rules people would choose behind a ‘veil of ignorance’, not knowing their own position, and argued that inequalities are acceptable only if they benefit the least advantaged. A utilitarian argument notes that an extra dollar probably adds more well-being to a poor household than a rich one, which favours some redistribution if incentive costs are small. Economics can clarify each position’s costs; it cannot choose between them.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of the economy as a marketplace where everyone sells what they own. Some own land, buildings or shares; some own rare skills; many own only their time and effort; a few own nothing they can sell. The market pays each person the going price for what they sell, so those who own more, or own what the market values highly, earn far more. Nobody needs to cheat for this to happen. The market can be efficient while the incomes it hands out are very unequal. Whether that is fair is a question about values.\",\"exam\":\"Define equity as fairness, a normative idea distinct from equality. Draw the circular flow with households and firms, labelling factor services flowing to firms and factor incomes (wages, rent, interest, profit) flowing to households. Explain the chain: households own different quantities of factors and factor prices differ, so incomes differ; saved income and inheritance build wealth, which yields further income. Distinguish efficiency from equity: a market can be allocatively efficient yet inequitable, because demand reflects ability to pay. Evaluate by noting that some inequality reflects effort or choice, that redistribution may affect incentives, and that 3.4 supplies the measures and policies.\",\"deep\":\"Market prices do two jobs. They allocate resources, signalling where factors are most valued, and they distribute income, deciding what each factor owner receives. The first job is why economists value markets; the second is why markets are contested. The two cannot be fully separated: taxing high factor incomes weakens the signal that drew factors to those uses, the source of the classic efficiency–equity trade-off. How steep that trade-off is remains an empirical question. Some redistribution, such as spending on children’s education and health, may raise future productivity and improve both equity and efficiency; very high marginal tax rates may reduce work, saving or investment. A market can pass the efficiency test and still fail a society’s test of fairness.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Market incomes follow the ownership and price of factors, not need; whether the result is unfair is a normative judgement.\",\"tension\":\"Reducing inequality can weaken incentives, while leaving it can limit opportunity.\",\"ia\":\"An article on rising rents or a wealth gap, tracing the gap back to factor ownership.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"The distribution of income shapes access to housing, health care and education, not only average living standards.\",\"tension\":\"Growth that raises average income may leave some households no better off.\",\"ia\":\"An article on the cost of living for low-income households, linking market income to well-being.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do market incomes differ so widely between households in the same economy?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect differences in effort to explain most of the gap.\",\"model\":\"Incomes depend on the quantity and market value of the factors owned, including inherited assets and skills.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests effort and skills matter, but inherited wealth, parental background and returns on assets also explain a substantial part, in proportions that vary by country.\",\"match\":\"Largely, though the model does not explain why ownership is unequal.\",\"assume\":\"Competitive factor markets and ownership taken as given.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that market power or discrimination sets factor prices would shift the explanation towards institutions.\",\"policy\":\"Widening access to education, capital and credit may matter as much as redistributing income afterwards.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using the circular flow model, why a free market economy may produce an unequal distribution of income.\",\"check\":[\"Households sell factor services to firms\",\"Firms pay rent, wages, interest and profit\",\"Ownership of factors and factor prices differ, so incomes differ\",\"Those with nothing to sell receive no market income\",\"Saved income and inheritance build wealth, which earns further income\",\"A labelled diagram showing the factor-market flows\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a market can be allocatively efficient and inequitable at the same time.\",\"check\":[\"Allocative efficiency: MSB = MSC, maximising community surplus\",\"Demand reflects ability to pay, so depends on income\",\"The efficient outcome takes the income distribution as given\",\"Equity is a normative judgement, distinct from equality\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Distinguish income from wealth.\",\"a\":\"Income is a flow over a period, such as wages; wealth is a stock of assets at a point in time.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the four factor incomes.\",\"a\":\"Wages, rent, interest and profit.\"},{\"q\":\"Which circular-flow flow explains differences in market income?\",\"a\":\"Factor incomes paid by firms to households for factor services.\"},{\"q\":\"Distinguish equity from equality.\",\"a\":\"Equality describes how similar outcomes are; equity is fairness, a normative judgement.\"},{\"q\":\"Why may people outside the labour market receive no market income?\",\"a\":\"Market income comes from selling factor services; with nothing to sell, they receive nothing.\"},{\"q\":\"Is inequality an allocative market failure?\",\"a\":\"Not necessarily: a market can reach MSB = MSC with a distribution many judge unfair.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A rental housing market is where MSB = MSC, but many low-income families cannot afford a home. Which statement is most accurate?\",\"opts\":[\"The market is allocatively inefficient, so it has failed\",\"The outcome may be allocatively efficient but judged inequitable\",\"The outcome is equitable because it is efficient\",\"The outcome shows a negative consumption externality\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"At MSB = MSC the allocation is efficient given existing incomes; fairness is a separate, normative test. The first option confuses inequity with allocative inefficiency.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Which of these is a stock rather than a flow?\",\"opts\":[\"Annual wages\",\"Monthly rent received\",\"The value of a household’s shares and property\",\"Interest received each year on savings\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Wealth is measured at a point in time; the other options are incomes received over a period. Confusing them hides how wealth generates income.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Which part of the circular flow best explains why market incomes differ between households?\",\"opts\":[\"Household spending on goods and services\",\"Factor incomes paid for factor services\",\"Injections of government spending\",\"Withdrawals of saving\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Market income is payment for factors sold, so ownership and factor prices drive income differences. Injections and withdrawals mainly change the level of national income; they do not explain why market incomes differ, although taxes and government transfers can change the final distribution (3.4).\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"HL Paper 1 (a): explain, using the circular flow model, why a free market may result in unequal income and wealth (HL papers include HL extension material, guide PDF p. 68)\",\"HL Paper 1 (b): discuss whether an efficient market economy can be inequitable, drawing on 3.4\",\"HL Paper 3 (b): use equity as one criterion for judging a recommended policy\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"discuss\",\"examine\",\"to what extent\"],\"dg\":\"Circular flow model illustrating why the free market results in inequalities (guide PDF p. 39): factor services to firms and factor incomes to households labelled, with the link to unequal ownership explained in words.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Identify which factors the group in the context owns or lacks, and trace its income gap to factor ownership and prices.\",\"eval\":\"Separate positive from normative claims; weigh effort and choice against inheritance and opportunity; note incentive effects of intervention.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating inequality as allocative market failure\",\"Confusing income with wealth, or equity with equality\",\"Explaining inequality with injections and withdrawals instead of factor flows\"],\"strong\":[\"Use the circular flow with factor flows labelled\",\"Explain how wealth and income reinforce each other\",\"State that equity is normative and distinct from efficiency\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a circular flow diagram, why a free market economy may result in an unequal distribution of income.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, discuss the view that a market economy which allocates resources efficiently has no need for government intervention to promote equity.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: in Country Q, the richest fifth of households receive about half of market income but about a third of income after taxes and transfers. Explain why a free market may produce the larger gap, and recommend one policy to reduce inequality of opportunity, noting a drawback.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"why\":\"The circular flow and the question ‘for whom to produce?’ are the tools 2.12 uses.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"why\":\"Abnormal profit from market power flows to firms’ owners, one route to concentrated wealth.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"3.4 measures inequality with the Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient and evaluates policies to reduce it.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"why\":\"Inequality indicators and the inequality-adjusted HDI (guide PDF p. 57) show how distribution affects measured development.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Because 2.12 explains why market outcomes can be unequal, and 3.4 measures that inequality and evaluates the policies that address it.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on rising rents, executive pay or a widening wealth gap suit this subtopic, framed through Equity (is it fair?) or Economic well-being (effects on living standards).\",\"ee\":\"To what extent do differences in home ownership explain differences in household income between regions of a chosen country? Published regional data could be compared.\",\"tok\":\"Economists can describe how a market distributes income, but judging whether that distribution is fair requires value judgements. Where does economic knowledge end and ethical judgement begin?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students use the circular flow to explain unequal market incomes and distinguish efficiency from equity.\",\"starter\":\"Give each student a card listing what they ‘own’ and ask them to predict their income ranking.\",\"activity\":\"A factor-market simulation: firms hire card-holders at posted prices; saving buys assets; three rounds show gaps compounding.\",\"discussion\":\"Was the final distribution fair? Separate positive from normative statements.\",\"check\":\"Students draw the circular flow, label the factor flows and link them to inequality in two sentences.\",\"extension\":\"Apply Nozick’s and Rawls’s views of justice to the simulation’s result.\",\"homework\":\"Answer the Paper 1 (a) practice question with a labelled diagram.\"},\"after\":[\"I can distinguish income from wealth, and equity from equality\",\"I can name the four factor incomes and the factors they reward\",\"I can draw the circular flow and label the factor-market flows\",\"I can explain why unequal ownership of factors produces unequal incomes\",\"I can explain how wealth and income reinforce each other\",\"I can explain why a market can be efficient and inequitable at once\",\"I can link this subtopic to 3.4 and explain it without notes\"]},\"3.1\":{\"code\":\"3.1\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"A country’s economic activity can be counted three ways: what is produced, what is earned, or what is spent. In principle the three totals match, because every sale is someone’s income. That total, GDP, says something about living standards only after removing price changes and dividing by population, and even then it measures output, not well-being.\",\"why\":\"Governments, central banks and firms act on whether real output is growing or shrinking, so users need to know what the figures can show.\",\"q\":\"If a country’s GDP rose by 6% last year, are its people 6% better off?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Factors of production and their incomes: wages, rent, interest and profit\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"The circular flow of income with leakages and injections\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Points inside the PPC and outward shifts of the PPC\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Percentage change and index numbers\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define GDP and GNI and state the difference\",\"Name three alternative well-being measures: the OECD Better Life Index, the Happiness Index and the Happy Planet Index\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain, with the circular flow, why the three approaches give the same total\",\"Explain why real GDP per capita at PPP suits living-standard comparisons better than nominal GDP\"],\"apply\":[\"Label a data series as nominal or real, total or per capita, before drawing a conclusion\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a change in an injection or leakage alters national income\",\"Distinguish a slower growth rate from falling output on a business cycle diagram\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate GDP or GNI per capita as a measure of well-being over time and between countries\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate nominal GDP (expenditure approach) and nominal GNI\",\"Convert nominal GDP to real GDP and real GDP per capita\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw the circular flow with leakages (S, T, M) and injections (I, G, X)\",\"Draw the business cycle with a trend line and labelled phases\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret nominal GDP, deflator and population data to judge whether real output per person rose\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"The circular flow: one person’s spending is another’s income\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Households supply factors of production to firms and receive wages, rent, interest and profit. They spend much of that income on firms’ output, which pays the next round of incomes. Three leakages take money out of the flow: saving, taxes and imports. Three injections add spending that does not come from domestic households’ consumption: investment, government spending and exports. National income is stable when total injections equal total leakages. If injections exceed leakages, national income rises; if leakages exceed injections, it falls.\"},{\"h\":\"Three approaches, one total\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The output approach adds the value added at each stage of production, so intermediate goods are not counted twice. The income approach adds the factor incomes production generates. The expenditure approach adds spending on final output: C + I + G + (X − M). Imports are subtracted because they sit inside C, I and G but were produced abroad. In principle the three totals are equal; published figures differ slightly because each uses different data.\",\"because\":[\"A household spends $50 on a meal\",\"The $50 is the value added by the restaurant and its suppliers\",\"The same $50 is paid out as wages, rent, interest and profit\",\"So spending = output = income = $50\"]},{\"h\":\"GDP and GNI: produced where, earned by whom\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Gross domestic product (GDP) is the total market value of final goods and services produced within a country’s borders in a period. Gross national income (GNI) is the income earned by a country’s residents wherever they produce: GDP plus profits, interest, dividends and wages received from abroad, minus the same kinds of income paid to non-residents. Where foreign-owned firms produce much of the output and send profits home, GNI is below GDP.\"},{\"h\":\"Nominal, real, per capita and PPP\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Nominal GDP values output at current prices, so it rises with inflation even if nothing more is made. Real GDP values output at base-year prices: real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ price deflator × 100. Dividing by population gives real GDP per capita. Between countries, market exchange rates mislead because a dollar buys more in some places than others; a purchasing power parity (PPP) rate makes a common basket cost the same everywhere.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Adjustment\",\"Corrects for\",\"Still misses\"],\"rows\":[[\"Real\",\"Price changes over time\",\"Quality changes, new products\"],[\"Per capita\",\"Population size\",\"Distribution\"],[\"PPP\",\"Price levels between countries\",\"Non-market output, environment\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The business cycle: fluctuations around a rising trend\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Real GDP does not grow smoothly. The business cycle shows actual output moving above and below a long-term trend that represents potential output: what the economy can produce with resources fully and normally employed. Expansions lead to a peak; contractions lead to a trough. A recession is a period of falling real output; two consecutive quarters of negative growth is a common rule of thumb, but definitions differ between countries and institutions. The distance between actual and potential output is the output gap.\"},{\"h\":\"GDP’s blind spots, and three alternatives\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"GDP counts market output. It omits unpaid work and much of the informal economy, ignores leisure, adds spending on cleaning up pollution without subtracting the damage, and says nothing about distribution. Between countries, data quality also differs. The guide names three alternative measures of well-being (guide PDF p. 42). The OECD Better Life Index combines 11 topics, from housing to life satisfaction, with user-chosen weights. Happiness indices, such as the World Happiness Report ranking, use people’s ratings of their own lives. The Happy Planet Index relates well-being and life expectancy to ecological footprint.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Measure\",\"Adds\",\"Weakness\"],\"rows\":[[\"Better Life Index\",\"Many dimensions, open weights\",\"Covers mainly OECD economies\"],[\"Happiness Index\",\"People’s own judgement\",\"Shaped by culture and wording\"],[\"Happy Planet Index\",\"Sustainability\",\"Can rank poorer countries above rich ones\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Export demand rises and the circular flow expands\",\"cause\":\"Incomes rise in the main trading partners, so foreign buyers spend more on the country’s goods.\",\"mechanism\":\"Export revenue is an injection, paid out by exporters as factor incomes.\",\"immediate\":\"Injections exceed leakages, so national income rises.\",\"secondary\":\"Households spend part of the extra income, creating further rounds; part leaks out as saving, taxes and imports.\",\"longrun\":\"Income stops rising when leakages have grown to match the higher injections.\",\"depends\":\"How much extra income leaks out, spare capacity (otherwise prices rise instead), and whether foreign demand lasts.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Circular flow of income\",\"dg\":\"circular\",\"purpose\":\"Links spending, output and income, and shows why a change in one flow changes national income.\",\"assumptions\":[\"All spending on output becomes factor income\",\"Leakages and injections depend on different decisions\",\"Prices are constant\"],\"mechanism\":\"If injections exceed leakages, income rises until leakages grow to match; if leakages exceed injections, income falls.\",\"prediction\":\"A rise in an injection or a fall in a leakage raises national income.\",\"application\":\"Explaining how falling exports cut incomes across the economy.\",\"limitations\":[\"It hides how banks channel saving into investment\",\"It shows direction, not size or timing\"],\"real\":\"A recession in a major trading partner tends to cut export orders, then incomes and local spending in exporting regions.\",\"misconception\":\"Believing saving must equal investment for equilibrium; total leakages must equal total injections.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a): explain the three approaches or a change in an injection, labelling each sector, leakage and injection.\",\"deeper\":\"The link between planned leakages and injections underlies the Keynesian multiplier (3.6). If every household tries to save more at once, total saving may not rise, because lower spending lowers incomes: the paradox of thrift. Whether it happens depends on whether extra saving is channelled into investment, which depends on business confidence and interest rates. The loop also hides stocks, such as wealth and debt, that shape the flows.\"},{\"model\":\"Business cycle\",\"dg\":\"cycle\",\"purpose\":\"Separates short-term fluctuations in real output from long-term growth in capacity.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Potential output grows along a smooth trend\",\"Deviations from trend are temporary\"],\"mechanism\":\"Swings in spending and supply shocks push actual output above or below a trend that rises as capacity grows.\",\"prediction\":\"Output below trend brings rising unemployment and weak inflation; output above trend brings inflationary pressure.\",\"application\":\"Locating an economy before recommending policy.\",\"limitations\":[\"Real cycles are irregular, so the diagram cannot forecast\",\"Potential output is estimated and revised, not observed\",\"A deep recession may lower the trend itself\"],\"real\":\"Many economies contracted sharply in 2020 under pandemic restrictions and then recovered unusually fast: a cycle driven by a sudden shock.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling a slowdown a recession, or putting price rather than real GDP on the vertical axis.\",\"exam\":\"Label real GDP and time, the trend line and the phases, and link the output gap to unemployment or inflation.\",\"deeper\":\"Economists disagree about what drives the cycle. Keynesian accounts stress swings in demand, especially investment and confidence. Real business cycle theory, a new classical approach, argues that fluctuations mainly reflect productivity shocks. Because potential output is estimated statistically, the measured output gap depends on the method chosen, and early estimates are sometimes revised enough to change sign, which complicates policy made in real time.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"If GDP per capita measures goods and services available per person, higher values should mean higher living standards.\",\"evidence\":\"Across countries, higher real GDP per capita is generally associated with longer lives, more schooling and higher average life satisfaction, though the gain per extra dollar is smaller at high incomes. Within some rich countries, reported happiness changed little over long periods of growth (the Easterlin paradox), a finding that is disputed.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that income matters most when it lifts people out of deprivation, after which health and security weigh more. Many economists treat GDP as a necessary but insufficient guide to well-being.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"What GDP includes by convention\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"National accountants make judgement calls students rarely see. Public services such as schooling are valued at cost, because they are not sold. Owner-occupiers are treated as renting their homes to themselves, and an estimated rent is added to GDP so that it does not change when tenants buy their homes. The informal economy is estimated, with methods that differ between countries. Each convention is reasonable, but together they make GDP a constructed statistic, and international comparisons inherit the differences.\"},{\"h\":\"When GDP and GNI tell different stories\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"In Ireland, profits that multinational firms book there inflate GDP well beyond residents’ income, so its statistics office publishes an adjusted measure of national income. Migrants’ remittances are a different case: under international accounting standards, money sent home by people living and working abroad is a transfer, not income from production, so it falls outside GNI and enters a broader measure, national disposable income. Check which measure a source uses before comparing countries.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Picture every sale of a finished good or service in a country over a year. Each one is spending for the buyer, income for the seller’s workers and owners, and a piece of output. Add up any of the three and you get GDP. A bigger number does not mean a better year, though. If prices rose, the number grows without more being made, so economists remove price changes to get real GDP. If the population grew, output is shared among more people, so they divide by population. Even then, GDP counts what is sold, not whether people are healthier or happier.\",\"exam\":\"Define GDP precisely: the total market value of final goods and services produced within a country in a given period. For living standards, use real GDP per capita, at PPP when comparing countries, and say what each adjustment corrects. Use the circular flow to explain why the three approaches agree, and the business cycle to locate an economy against its trend. To evaluate, weigh GDP’s strengths (regular, comparable, linked to jobs and tax revenue) against named limitations: distribution, non-market activity, environmental costs and the composition of output. Apply them to a specific country and suggest a complementary measure rather than dismissing GDP.\",\"deep\":\"GDP measures market production using accounting rules; it is not a theory of welfare. Its logic is that market prices reflect what buyers will pay at the margin, so output valued at those prices is a coherent measure of activity. The logic weakens where there are no prices (unpaid work, public services), where prices omit costs (pollution), and where totals hide distribution. Economists respond in two ways. Some adjust GDP: green accounting subtracts resource depletion, and distributional accounts split growth between income groups. Others prefer a dashboard of indicators, arguing that no single number can summarise well-being. The disagreement is partly technical and partly normative, because any composite index embeds judgements about what matters and how much it should count.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"GDP per capita is the usual proxy for material living standards; how far can it be trusted?\",\"tension\":\"Output that rises because people work longer may lower well-being GDP does not record.\",\"ia\":\"A growth-figures article, read through whether growth reached typical households.\"},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"The cycle and real growth rates describe how activity changes over time.\",\"tension\":\"Short-run swings can be mistaken for long-run changes in capacity.\",\"ia\":\"A recession or recovery article, analysed by locating the economy against its trend.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"One sector’s spending is another’s income, and GNI tracks income crossing borders.\",\"tension\":\"Foreign investment can raise GDP while much of the income leaves the country.\",\"ia\":\"A falling-export-demand article traced through the circular flow to domestic incomes.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do some countries with high GDP per capita rank below poorer ones on well-being measures?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect the richest countries to top every ranking.\",\"model\":\"Well-being indices add health, environment and life satisfaction, so a country strong on those can outrank a richer one.\",\"evidence\":\"Rankings on the Better Life Index, happiness surveys and the Happy Planet Index do differ from GDP rankings.\",\"match\":\"Partly: income and well-being are correlated, but less strongly at high incomes, and weighting choices drive part of the gap.\",\"assume\":\"Whether self-reported satisfaction is comparable across cultures, and how each index weights its parts.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that a different weighting reverses the rankings.\",\"policy\":\"Track a dashboard of indicators alongside GDP.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why the output, income and expenditure approaches give the same total in principle.\",\"check\":[\"Output is measured as value added to avoid double counting\",\"Spending on final output becomes firms’ revenue\",\"Revenue is paid out as wages, rent, interest and profit\",\"The circular flow links the three in one loop\",\"Imports are subtracted because they were produced abroad\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why real GDP per capita at PPP is better than nominal GDP for comparing living standards in two countries, and what it still misses.\",\"check\":[\"Real figures remove price changes\",\"Per capita divides by population\",\"PPP adjusts for different price levels, unlike exchange rates\",\"It is an average and ignores distribution\",\"It omits non-market output, leisure and environmental costs\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the expenditure approach formula for GDP.\",\"a\":\"GDP = C + I + G + (X − M).\"},{\"q\":\"Nominal GDP is $600bn and the deflator is 125 (base year = 100). Calculate real GDP.\",\"a\":\"600 ÷ 125 × 100 = $480bn in base-year prices.\"},{\"q\":\"GDP is $300bn; residents earn $12bn abroad and non-residents earn $20bn in the country. Calculate GNI.\",\"a\":\"300 + 12 − 20 = $292bn.\"},{\"q\":\"What does the trend line on a business cycle diagram represent?\",\"a\":\"Potential output, rising as productive capacity grows.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a PPP conversion correct for, and what not?\",\"a\":\"Different price levels between countries; not distribution, non-market output or environmental costs.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one reason GDP per capita could rise while well-being falls.\",\"a\":\"Output rises because people work much longer hours, or growth brings pollution GDP does not subtract.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Nominal GDP rose by 5% in a year when the GDP deflator rose by 6%. What happened to real GDP?\",\"opts\":[\"It rose by about 5%\",\"It rose by about 11%\",\"It fell by about 1%\",\"It was unchanged\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Real growth ≈ nominal growth minus the rise in the deflator: 5% − 6% ≈ −1%. Choosing a 5% rise reads a nominal figure as output, when higher prices explain all of the increase.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-02\"},{\"q\":\"Real GDP growth slows from 4% to 1%. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"Real output fell\",\"The economy entered a recession\",\"Real output rose, but more slowly\",\"The price level fell\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Positive growth, however small, means real output is still rising: the economy slowed, not contracted. A recession needs a negative growth rate.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Two countries have the same real GDP per capita at PPP. What can you conclude?\",\"opts\":[\"Their living standards are the same\",\"Their average output per person, adjusted for price levels, is similar\",\"Their incomes are equally distributed\",\"Their citizens work the same hours\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The figure is an average adjusted for price levels; it says nothing about distribution, leisure or health, so equal figures do not mean equal living standards.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-01\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (b)–(f): calculate GDP, GNI, real GDP or real GDP per capita from data and comment\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain the circular flow, the three approaches or the business cycle with a diagram\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate GDP per capita as a measure of well-being\"],\"ct\":[\"calculate\",\"define\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"Circular flow with sectors, leakages (S, T, M) and injections (I, G, X); business cycle with real GDP against time, trend line and phases (guide PDF pp. 41–42).\",\"calc\":\"SL and HL: nominal GDP (expenditure approach), nominal GNI, real GDP and real GNI using a deflator, and real GDP or GNI per capita (guide PDF pp. 41–42). None is marked HL only.\",\"apply\":\"Say whether the country’s figures are nominal or real, total or per capita, and at PPP, before any claim about living standards.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh comparability against omissions, and ask who gains from growth.\",\"errors\":[\"Comparing nominal figures across years\",\"Dividing by the deflator without multiplying by 100\",\"Treating a slower growth rate as falling output\",\"Comparing GDP per capita at market exchange rates\"],\"strong\":[\"Label every figure nominal or real, total or per capita\",\"Show each calculation step with units and base year\",\"Tie limitations to the context, such as a large informal sector\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: in 2025 a country’s nominal GDP was $540 billion, its GDP deflator was 120 (base year 2020 = 100) and its population was 15 million. Calculate real GDP per capita in 2020 prices.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a business cycle diagram, explain the difference between a slowdown in economic growth and a recession.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate whether a government should judge its economic success by a well-being index rather than by growth in real GDP per capita.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"why\":\"Output inside the PPC appears here as a negative output gap.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"The expenditure components become the components of aggregate demand.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Real GDP growth measures the growth objective; contractions bring cyclical unemployment.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"why\":\"GNI per capita at PPP is the HDI’s indicator for its standard-of-living dimension.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"Because C + I + G + (X − M) are the components of aggregate demand, and the AD–AS model explains the swings the business cycle records.\"},\"ia\":\"An article on new GDP figures, a recession, or a well-being measure suits this subtopic. Frame it through economic well-being (did growth reach households?) or change (where is the economy against its trend?), and check whether its figures are nominal or real.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent does real GDP per capita explain differences in life satisfaction across a set of countries, compared with health, inequality and environmental quality?\",\"tok\":\"If GDP and a well-being index give different verdicts on whether life is improving, on what grounds can we decide which is closer to the truth?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can calculate real GDP per capita and evaluate what it shows about living standards.\",\"starter\":\"Show two headlines about one economy, one on record nominal GDP and one on falling real incomes, and ask how both can be true.\",\"activity\":\"Token-passing circular flow: groups act as sectors and track the flow when an injection or leakage changes.\",\"discussion\":\"Higher GDP per capita or higher Better Life Index score: which country would you choose, and why?\",\"check\":\"From nominal GDP, a deflator and population, calculate real GDP per capita and say whether it rose.\",\"extension\":\"Compare GDP and GNI for Ireland and one other economy, and explain the gap.\",\"homework\":\"Draw the business cycle from memory and distinguish a slowdown from a recession in three sentences.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define GDP and GNI and explain the difference.\",\"I can explain why the three approaches give the same total, using the circular flow.\",\"I can draw the circular flow and the business cycle with correct labels.\",\"I can calculate nominal GDP, GNI, real GDP and real GDP per capita.\",\"I can apply real, per capita and PPP adjustments to a real country’s data.\",\"I can evaluate GDP against the three alternative well-being measures.\",\"I can connect the expenditure components to aggregate demand.\",\"I can explain the difference between a slowdown and a recession without notes.\"]},\"3.2\":{\"code\":\"3.2\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Aggregate demand (AD) is total planned spending on a country’s output at each price level; aggregate supply (AS) is the output firms will produce at each price level. Where they meet sets real GDP and the price level. The real argument is about supply: monetarist/new classical economists expect a return to full employment as wages and prices adjust; Keynesians argue an economy can stay below it for years.\",\"why\":\"Whether a recession corrects itself decides whether governments should act. Stimulus at full capacity mostly raises prices; waiting for slow adjustment leaves people unemployed.\",\"q\":\"When a recession strikes, should a government wait for the economy to recover on its own?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"A movement along a curve versus a shift of the curve\",\"sub\":\"2.1\"},{\"t\":\"Real GDP and the components C + I + G + (X − M)\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"The business cycle and potential output\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"The Keynesian revolution and the monetarist/new classical counter-revolution\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the components of AD and the determinants of each\",\"State the determinants of SRAS and of long-run supply\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why AD slopes downwards without market demand logic\",\"Explain why the new classical LRAS is vertical and the Keynesian AS has a horizontal section\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a real shock as a shift of AD, SRAS or LRAS, with direction\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse a fall in AD in both models\",\"Trace the adjustment from short-run to long-run equilibrium\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the claim that an economy returns to full employment without intervention\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw AD, SRAS and LRAS with deflationary and inflationary gaps on the output axis\",\"Draw the Keynesian AS with its three sections\"],\"interpret\":[\"Judge from output, price and unemployment data whether a demand or supply shock occurred\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Aggregate demand is not a big demand curve\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"AD = C + I + G + (X − M), plotted against the average price level with real output on the horizontal axis. It slopes downwards, but not because buyers switch to cheaper substitutes: when the whole price level rises, no domestic good has become cheaper relative to other domestic goods. The slope comes from three macro effects. A change in the price level is therefore a movement along AD, not a shift.\",\"because\":[\"The price level rises\",\"Real wealth falls, so households cut consumption (wealth effect)\",\"Interest rates rise, cutting C and I (interest-rate effect)\",\"Domestic goods become dearer than foreign goods, so X falls and M rises (trade effect)\",\"Real output demanded falls: a movement up along AD\"]},{\"h\":\"What shifts aggregate demand\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"AD shifts when something other than the price level changes spending on one of its components. The guide lists the determinants of each (guide PDF p. 42). Investment tends to be the most volatile component, so swings in business confidence feature in many business cycles. Transfer payments such as pensions are not part of G: they affect AD through households’ disposable income, and so through C.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Component\",\"Determinants (guide PDF p. 42)\"],\"rows\":[[\"C\",\"Consumer confidence, interest rates, wealth, income taxes, household debt, expected future prices\"],[\"I\",\"Interest rates, business confidence, technology, business taxes, corporate debt\"],[\"G\",\"Political and economic priorities\"],[\"X − M\",\"Trading partners’ incomes, exchange rates, trade policies\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Short-run aggregate supply\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) shows the real output firms produce at each price level while input prices, especially money wages, are fixed. It slopes upwards: if the price level rises while costs stay put, each unit is more profitable, so firms produce more. SRAS shifts when costs change at every level of output. The guide names costs of factors of production (wages, raw materials, energy) and indirect taxes (guide PDF p. 42). Higher costs or taxes shift SRAS left.\"},{\"h\":\"Two views of aggregate supply\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The schools disagree about what happens below full employment. Monetarist/new classical economists draw a vertical long-run aggregate supply curve (LRAS) at potential output: in the long run wages and prices are flexible, so output depends on real resources, not the price level. Keynesians draw one AS curve: horizontal with plenty of idle resources, rising as bottlenecks appear, vertical at full employment output.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Monetarist/new classical\",\"Keynesian\"],\"rows\":[[\"Wages and prices\",\"Flexible in the long run\",\"Sticky downwards\"],[\"Long-run equilibrium\",\"At potential output\",\"Possibly below full employment\"],[\"Higher AD, long run\",\"Only higher prices\",\"More output if spare capacity\"],[\"Policy implication\",\"Supply side, low inflation\",\"Manage demand to close gaps\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Deflationary and inflationary gaps\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A deflationary (recessionary) gap exists when equilibrium real output is below full employment (potential) output: there is spare capacity and unemployment above the natural rate. An inflationary gap exists when equilibrium output is above potential: firms stretch capacity, and wages and prices face upward pressure. In the new classical diagram, a gap is the horizontal distance between short-run equilibrium and LRAS; in the Keynesian diagram, a deflationary gap is an equilibrium on the horizontal or rising section.\"},{\"h\":\"From short run to long run: adjustment or persistence\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Short-run equilibrium is where AD meets SRAS. In the new classical model, long-run equilibrium lies on LRAS, where unemployment equals the natural rate. After a fall in AD, surplus labour pushes money wages down, or makes them grow more slowly; lower costs shift SRAS right until output returns to potential at a lower price level. The guide calls this automatic adjustment to full employment equilibrium (guide PDF p. 43). Keynesians doubt each step: money wages resist cuts, and falling incomes and heavier real debts can cut spending further, so a deflationary gap can persist until AD recovers.\"},{\"h\":\"What shifts long-run supply\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"LRAS and the Keynesian AS shift right when capacity grows (guide PDF p. 43): more or better factors of production (a larger, healthier or better-educated workforce, more capital), better technology, greater efficiency, and better institutions such as secure property rights and reliable courts. A rightward LRAS shift raises potential output. A rightward AD shift only changes how much existing capacity is used.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A collapse in business confidence\",\"cause\":\"Firms expect weak sales and postpone investment.\",\"mechanism\":\"I falls, so AD shifts left; lower incomes then cut consumption.\",\"immediate\":\"Real output falls, unemployment rises and inflation eases.\",\"secondary\":\"Falling incomes deepen cuts in spending and hiring.\",\"longrun\":\"New classical: wages adjust and output returns to potential. Keynesian: the gap may persist until confidence or policy revives AD.\",\"depends\":\"Wage flexibility, debt levels, expectations and any policy response.\"},{\"t\":\"A sharp rise in world energy prices\",\"cause\":\"Energy, an input to almost all production, becomes dearer.\",\"mechanism\":\"Unit costs rise, so SRAS shifts left.\",\"immediate\":\"The price level rises while real output falls (stagflation).\",\"secondary\":\"If workers bargain for higher wages to protect real incomes, SRAS shifts left again.\",\"longrun\":\"If the shock fades and wages do not chase prices, SRAS recovers; if energy stays dear, some capital may become uneconomic and potential output fall.\",\"depends\":\"Energy intensity of production, whether the shock is temporary, and inflation expectations.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Monetarist/new classical model: vertical LRAS\",\"dg\":\"defgap\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why, in this view, output returns to potential and AD affects only prices in the long run.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Wages and prices are flexible in the long run\",\"Surplus labour pushes money wages down\",\"Potential output depends on resources, technology, efficiency and institutions\"],\"mechanism\":\"A deflationary gap pushes wages down and SRAS right until output reaches potential; an inflationary gap pushes wages up and SRAS left.\",\"prediction\":\"In the long run output equals potential and unemployment equals the natural rate, whatever AD.\",\"application\":\"Arguing for supply-side policy and low inflation rather than managing demand.\",\"limitations\":[\"Money wages rarely fall much, so adjustment may take years\",\"Falling prices raise real debt burdens\",\"Long slumps may lower potential output itself\"],\"real\":\"After the 2008 financial crisis, unemployment stayed high for years in several economies; critics read this as slow adjustment, supporters note that unemployment in many of them eventually fell back.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing LRAS upward-sloping, or shifting LRAS for a change in AD.\",\"exam\":\"Show the short-run equilibrium, the gap on the output axis and the SRAS shift back to LRAS, then question its speed.\",\"deeper\":\"New classical economists add rational expectations: if people anticipate a policy’s effect on prices, wages adjust quickly and even the short-run output effect may be small. The debate turns on how expectations form and how quickly contracts are renegotiated. Evidence that wages are sticky does not settle it, because stickiness can be rational. What matters for policy is the cost of waiting against the risks of intervening, such as lags.\"},{\"model\":\"Keynesian AS model\",\"dg\":\"lras\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why a deflationary gap can persist and why the effect of higher AD depends on spare capacity.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Money wages and prices are sticky downwards\",\"With large spare capacity, output can rise without higher unit costs\"],\"mechanism\":\"On the horizontal section, higher AD raises output only; on the rising section, output and prices; on the vertical section, prices only.\",\"prediction\":\"Equilibrium can settle below full employment; raising AD with spare capacity adds output and jobs with little inflation.\",\"application\":\"Arguing for stimulus in a deep recession and caution near full employment.\",\"limitations\":[\"A perfectly flat section is a simplification\",\"Full employment output is treated as fixed, though it grows\"],\"real\":\"Keynes developed these ideas during the Great Depression of the 1930s, when mass unemployment persisted for years in many industrial economies.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing a smooth curve that loses the horizontal section, or assuming any rise in AD is inflationary.\",\"exam\":\"Name the model, state which section the economy is in, then predict the effect.\",\"deeper\":\"Keynes argued that even flexible money wages might not restore full employment. Wage cuts lower costs but also workers’ incomes and spending; if prices fall too, real wages may not fall at all, while real debts rise. Later Keynesians gave reasons for stickiness: menu costs, long contracts, and efficiency wages, where firms pay above market-clearing rates to protect morale and productivity.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The new classical model predicts that deflationary gaps close as wages and prices adjust; the Keynesian model predicts they can persist.\",\"evidence\":\"Wage data in many countries show that money-wage cuts are rare, even in deep recessions. After the 1930s and 2008–09, unemployment stayed high for years in several economies. Unemployment eventually fell, but after 2008–09 output in many advanced economies stayed below its pre-crisis trend, which some economists read as hysteresis.\",\"interp\":\"Many economists read this as adjustment that happens but slowly, so the dispute is about speed and the cost of waiting. Keynesians stress that long gaps may damage potential output; new classical economists stress policy lags and inflation risk.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why money wages resist cuts\",\"tag\":\"Behaviour\",\"p\":\"Surveys of employers suggest firms avoid pay cuts because they damage morale and productivity and prompt the best workers to leave. Workers judge fairness partly in money terms: a 2% pay cut with zero inflation feels worse than a pay freeze with 2% inflation, though the real effect is similar. Contracts and minimum wages add stickiness. So labour markets often adjust through hiring freezes and job losses rather than lower pay, which is why Keynesians expect a deflationary gap to show up as unemployment.\"},{\"h\":\"Hysteresis: when a demand shock damages supply\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"The neat separation of AD and LRAS weakens in long recessions. Workers unemployed for years may lose skills and contacts, and firms that postpone investment leave a smaller capital stock. Both reduce potential output, so a long deflationary gap can shift LRAS left and the economy recovers to a lower path. If this effect, hysteresis, is strong, the case for acting quickly against recessions is stronger, a point some economists in both traditions accept.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of the whole economy as one enormous shop. Aggregate demand is everything households, firms, the government and foreigners plan to buy from it. Aggregate supply is how much its owners will produce. Where the two meet decides how much gets made and the general level of prices. The big argument is what happens after a slump. One side says wages and prices will adjust and the shop will be busy again before long. The other says wages rarely fall, so the shop can stay half-empty for years unless someone boosts spending.\",\"exam\":\"Name the model before you draw it. Label the axes average price level and real output, and label every curve and equilibrium. For a demand change, say which component moved and why; for a cost change, shift SRAS; for a change in capacity, shift LRAS or the Keynesian AS. Mark any gap on the output axis. In a discussion question, contrast the new classical prediction of adjustment with the Keynesian prediction of persistence, and then evaluate: how flexible are wages in this economy, how deep is the gap, how long would adjustment take, and what are the risks of intervening?\",\"deep\":\"AD–AS compresses two ideas into one picture. The demand side is the circular flow: spending creates income. The supply side is a claim about how prices and wages behave, and everything contentious lives there. If wages and prices adjust quickly, demand shocks are short-lived and output is governed by real resources: the new classical view. If they adjust slowly, demand shocks cause long unemployment: the Keynesian view. Modern macroeconomics blends the two, assuming stickiness in the short run and flexibility in the long run, and argues about how long the short run lasts, how expectations form, and whether long slumps lower potential output. The IB course presents the two schools as the two ends of that spectrum, which is why naming the model matters.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"The model shows how shocks move an economy from potential and what may bring it back.\",\"tension\":\"Higher AD can raise output now but only prices later.\",\"ia\":\"An energy-shock article, separating short-run from long-run effects.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"AD depends on trading partners’ incomes and exchange rates.\",\"tension\":\"Openness spreads demand but also imports shocks.\",\"ia\":\"A falling-exports article traced through net exports to AD.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"The two views of AS imply opposite roles for demand management.\",\"tension\":\"Acting risks inflation and lags; waiting risks long unemployment.\",\"ia\":\"A stimulus article, judged by where the economy sits on AS.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why did unemployment stay high for years after some deep recessions, if wages and prices adjust?\",\"expect\":\"Lower wages should make hiring cheaper, so unemployment should fall quickly.\",\"model\":\"New classical: surplus labour cuts wages and shifts SRAS right. Keynesian: sticky wages and weak demand keep the gap open.\",\"evidence\":\"Money-wage cuts are uncommon, and after the 1930s and 2008–09 several economies had high unemployment for years.\",\"match\":\"Partly: adjustment seems to work, but more slowly than the simple model implies.\",\"assume\":\"Wage flexibility, expectations, and whether potential output stays fixed.\",\"change\":\"Evidence of quick wage cuts and fast unaided recoveries.\",\"policy\":\"If adjustment is slow and costly, demand management in deep recessions has a stronger case.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why the AD curve slopes downwards.\",\"check\":[\"The axis is the average price level, not one good’s price\",\"Substitution cannot explain it because all prices change together\",\"Wealth effect on C\",\"Interest-rate effect on C and I\",\"Trade effect on X and M\",\"A price-level change is a movement along AD\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain how, in the new classical model, a deflationary gap closes, and why Keynesians doubt it.\",\"check\":[\"Output is below potential, unemployment above the natural rate\",\"Surplus labour pushes money wages down\",\"Lower costs shift SRAS right\",\"Output returns to potential at a lower price level\",\"Keynesians: sticky wages and falling incomes keep the gap open\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the four components of AD.\",\"a\":\"C + I + G + (X − M).\"},{\"q\":\"Name two determinants of investment in the guide.\",\"a\":\"Any two of interest rates, business confidence, technology, business taxes, corporate debt.\"},{\"q\":\"What shifts SRAS to the left?\",\"a\":\"Higher factor costs, such as wages or energy, or higher indirect taxes.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the new classical LRAS vertical?\",\"a\":\"With flexible long-run wages and prices, output depends on real resources, not the price level.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if AD rises on the horizontal section of the Keynesian AS?\",\"a\":\"Output rises with no change in the price level.\"},{\"q\":\"Where is a deflationary gap measured?\",\"a\":\"On the real output axis, from equilibrium output to potential output.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"The average price level rises while nothing else changes. What happens on an AD–AS diagram?\",\"opts\":[\"AD shifts left\",\"A movement up along AD\",\"AD shifts right\",\"LRAS shifts left\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The price level is on the axis, so its change is a movement along AD; only a change in a determinant of a component shifts AD. Shifting AD left confuses the price level with a determinant.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-05\"},{\"q\":\"Which best explains why AD slopes downwards?\",\"opts\":[\"Consumers switch to cheaper goods\",\"A lower price level raises real wealth, lowers interest rates and makes domestic goods more competitive\",\"Firms produce more when prices are higher\",\"Governments spend more when prices fall\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The slope rests on wealth, interest-rate and trade effects. Substitution explains a market demand curve, but when the whole price level changes there is no cheaper domestic good to switch to.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-04\"},{\"q\":\"An economy is on the horizontal section of the Keynesian AS. Government spending rises. What is the most likely result?\",\"opts\":[\"Higher prices, no change in output\",\"Higher output, little change in prices\",\"Higher output and much higher prices\",\"Lower output\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"With idle workers and equipment, firms can expand without higher unit costs, so output rises rather than prices. The first option describes the vertical section, at full employment.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-06\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with AD–AS, the effect of a change in a determinant of AD or SRAS\",\"Paper 1 (b): discuss whether an economy returns to full employment without intervention\",\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): draw AD–AS for an event in the text\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"draw\",\"distinguish\",\"analyse\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"AD and its shifts; SRAS and its shifts; the new classical LRAS and the Keynesian AS and their shifts; gaps; short-run and long-run equilibrium (guide PDF pp. 42–43).\",\"calc\":\"No calculations are listed for this subtopic (guide PDF pp. 42–43).\",\"apply\":\"Identify the component or cost that changed, the curve that shifts, and where the economy sits relative to potential.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh wage flexibility, gap size, time horizon and expectations, and the risks of acting against waiting.\",\"errors\":[\"Shifting AD for a price-level change\",\"Explaining the AD slope by substitution\",\"Drawing LRAS upward-sloping\",\"Assuming every rise in AD is inflationary\"],\"strong\":[\"Name and justify the model before drawing it\",\"Trace cause → component → curve → output, prices, jobs\",\"Separate short-run from long-run effects\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a Keynesian AS diagram, explain why an increase in aggregate demand may affect real output and the price level differently depending on spare capacity.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, discuss the view that wage flexibility is enough to close a deflationary gap.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative context: a text reports falling consumer confidence, higher energy import prices and a weakening currency in an economy with rising unemployment. Using the text and your knowledge of economics, evaluate whether the main problem is weak aggregate demand or a supply shock.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"why\":\"The expenditure components of GDP become AD, and the business cycle’s output gap is the gap shown here.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Deflationary gaps show cyclical unemployment; AD and SRAS shifts show demand-pull and cost-push inflation.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Fiscal policy shifts AD; the Keynesian model is its main justification.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Supply-side policies aim to shift LRAS or the Keynesian AS right.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Exchange rates move net exports and imported costs, shifting AD and SRAS.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Because growth, unemployment and inflation are all analysed as shifts and gaps in the AD–AS model.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on a recession, a stimulus package, falling business confidence or an energy-price shock suit this subtopic. Frame the analysis through change (short run against long run) or intervention (should government act, given where the economy sits on AS?).\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did money wages adjust during a recent recession in a chosen economy, and what does this suggest about the speed of return to full employment?\",\"tok\":\"Keynesian and new classical economists read the same recession data and reach opposite policy conclusions: is the disagreement about facts, models or values?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can use both views of AS to predict and evaluate the effect of a fall in AD.\",\"starter\":\"If every price and wage doubled overnight, would anyone buy less? Use answers to show why AD needs macro reasons.\",\"activity\":\"Diagram relay: groups shift the right curve for a shock card, then pass it on for the next group to add long-run adjustment.\",\"discussion\":\"Should governments wait for wages to adjust after a recession? Each side names evidence that would change its mind.\",\"check\":\"Classify five shocks as a movement along AD or a shift of AD, SRAS or LRAS.\",\"extension\":\"Draw a long deflationary gap that shifts LRAS left through hysteresis.\",\"homework\":\"Draw a deflationary gap in both models and explain in 150 words why the predictions differ.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define AD and state its components and their determinants.\",\"I can explain the AD slope without market demand logic.\",\"I can explain the shapes of SRAS, the new classical LRAS and the Keynesian AS.\",\"I can draw shifts, gaps and short-run and long-run equilibrium correctly.\",\"I can apply the model to a real shock by naming what changed.\",\"I can evaluate whether an economy returns to full employment without intervention.\",\"I can explain the difference between the two views of AS without notes.\"]},\"3.3\":{\"code\":\"3.3\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Governments pursue several macroeconomic objectives at once: economic growth, low unemployment, low and stable inflation and, at HL, a sustainable level of government debt. Each has a standard measure, and each measure hides something. The objectives can also pull against each other, so choosing between them means judging whose costs matter most.\",\"why\":\"Every policy in Unit 3 is judged against these objectives, and unemployment and inflation hurt different people in different ways.\",\"q\":\"Is 10% unemployment worse for a country than 10% inflation?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The PPC: points inside the curve and outward shifts\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Real GDP, the business cycle and the output gap\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"AD–AS shifts, deflationary gaps and the natural rate\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"A minimum wage as a price floor in the labour market\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define growth, unemployment, inflation, disinflation and deflation\",\"State which types of unemployment form the natural rate\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain short-term and long-term growth with the PPC and AD–AS\",\"Explain why the CPI may misstate a household’s change in living costs\"],\"apply\":[\"Diagnose the type of unemployment or cause of inflation in a news extract\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse why demand-driven deflation tends to be more harmful than supply-driven deflation\",\"Analyse the conflict between low unemployment and low inflation\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the relative costs of unemployment and inflation for different stakeholders\",\"Evaluate whether high growth can be reconciled with sustainability and equity\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate growth, unemployment and inflation rates from data\",\"Construct a weighted price index (HL only)\",\"Express government debt as a percentage of GDP (HL only)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a deflationary gap, a minimum wage and a fall in labour demand to show unemployment\",\"Draw demand-pull and cost-push inflation and deflation\",\"Draw short-run and long-run Phillips curves (HL only)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Distinguish disinflation from deflation in a CPI series\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend whether to prioritise inflation or unemployment, given data\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Growth: using capacity versus adding to it\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Economic growth is an increase in real GDP, measured as its percentage change between periods. Short-term growth uses existing capacity: a move from inside the PPC towards it, or a rise in AD that lifts output where there is spare capacity. Long-term growth adds capacity: the PPC shifts out and LRAS shifts right. Growth can raise living standards and fund public services, but it can also deplete resources, add pollution, and widen or narrow income gaps depending on who gains.\"},{\"h\":\"Measuring unemployment, and what the rate hides\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A person is unemployed if they are of working age, without work, available and actively seeking work. The labour force is the employed plus the unemployed, and the unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force × 100. Surveys and benefit-claimant counts give different figures. The rate can understate slack: discouraged workers leave the labour force, part-timers wanting full-time work count as employed, informal work is hard to record, and averages hide gaps by region, age and gender.\"},{\"h\":\"Four causes of unemployment, and their costs\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide lists four causes (guide PDF p. 44). The natural rate of unemployment is structural plus seasonal plus frictional unemployment: what remains at full employment output. Cyclical unemployment is the extra that appears when AD falls short. Each type needs a different remedy. Unemployment has personal costs (lost income, stress, lost skills), social costs (strain on families and communities) and economic costs (lost output, lower tax revenue, higher benefit spending).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Type\",\"Cause\",\"Diagram\",\"Typical remedy\"],\"rows\":[[\"Cyclical\",\"Too little AD in a downturn\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Demand-side policy\"],[\"Structural\",\"Skills or location mismatch; labour-market rigidities\",\"Fall in labour demand in one market; minimum wage\",\"Retraining, mobility, reform\"],[\"Frictional\",\"Searching between jobs\",\"None required\",\"Better job matching\"],[\"Seasonal\",\"Demand varies by season\",\"None required\",\"Diversifying work\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Measuring inflation with the CPI\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level. The consumer price index (CPI) prices a basket of goods and services bought by a typical household, weighting each item by its share of spending, and sets the base year at 100. Inflation rate = (CPI this year − CPI last year) ÷ CPI last year × 100. The CPI has limits: the basket may not match a particular group’s spending, it adjusts slowly to new products and quality changes, and fixed weights miss consumers switching away from goods whose prices rise fastest.\",\"because\":[\"Illustrative data: the base-year basket costs $80, so the index is 100\",\"The same quantities cost $86 a year later\",\"Index = 86 ÷ 80 × 100 = 107.5\",\"Inflation = (107.5 − 100) ÷ 100 × 100 = 7.5%\"]},{\"h\":\"Inflation, disinflation and deflation\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Demand-pull inflation comes from AD outgrowing capacity; cost-push inflation from rising costs shifting SRAS left, so output falls. The guide’s costs of high inflation (guide PDF p. 44): uncertainty; redistribution, when inflation is unexpected, from lenders and fixed-income earners to borrowers; weaker saving; lost export competitiveness if inflation exceeds trading partners’; slower growth; and blurred price signals. Deflation comes from falling AD or rising SRAS. The guide’s costs of deflation include deferred spending, a heavier real debt burden, bankruptcies and cyclical unemployment, redistribution to lenders, and weaker monetary policy once interest rates near zero.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Inflation rate\",\"Price level\"],\"rows\":[[\"Inflation\",\"Positive\",\"Rising\"],[\"Disinflation\",\"Falling but positive\",\"Rising more slowly\"],[\"Deflation\",\"Negative\",\"Falling\"]]}},{\"h\":\"HL only: a sustainable level of government debt\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Government (national) debt is the stock of outstanding government borrowing, measured as a percentage of GDP (guide PDF p. 44). A budget deficit, spending above revenue in a year, is a flow that adds to the debt; debt can keep rising while the deficit shrinks. The guide lists the costs of a high debt: debt-servicing costs, since interest payments use money that could fund other spending; credit-rating downgrades that raise borrowing costs; and pressure for higher future taxes or lower future spending.\"},{\"h\":\"When objectives conflict\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"Low unemployment and low inflation can conflict: raising AD to cut unemployment pushes the economy up the rising section of AS, adding to inflation, and pushing unemployment below the natural rate tends to raise wage demands. HL only: this trade-off is shown on AD–AS and on the short-run Phillips curve; in the long run, once expectations adjust, the Phillips curve is vertical at the natural rate (guide PDF pp. 44–45). Growth can also conflict with low inflation, with environmental sustainability and with equity in income distribution. On relative costs, unemployment concentrates heavy losses on the jobless, while moderate inflation spreads smaller costs widely; very high inflation, though, damages the whole economy.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Demand-driven deflation becomes a spiral\",\"cause\":\"After a credit boom collapses, households and firms cut spending to repay debt, and AD falls sharply.\",\"mechanism\":\"Prices start to fall; people expecting further falls delay purchases, and the real value of debts rises.\",\"immediate\":\"Output falls and cyclical unemployment rises.\",\"secondary\":\"Bankruptcies rise, banks lend less, and AD falls again.\",\"longrun\":\"Growth can stay weak for years; with nominal interest rates near zero, real rates stay high and monetary policy loses power.\",\"depends\":\"Whether deflation is expected to continue, private debt levels, central bank credibility, and whether prices are falling because of weak demand or rising productivity.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Demand-pull and cost-push inflation\",\"dg\":\"costpush\",\"purpose\":\"Identifies the source of inflation, which decides the right policy response.\",\"assumptions\":[\"AD and SRAS determine the price level and real output\",\"Sustained inflation needs repeated shifts, not one\"],\"mechanism\":\"Demand-pull: AD shifts right near capacity, raising prices and output. Cost-push: SRAS shifts left as energy, wage or import costs rise, raising prices while output falls.\",\"prediction\":\"Demand-pull inflation comes with falling unemployment; cost-push inflation with rising unemployment (stagflation).\",\"application\":\"Choosing a response: cutting AD suits demand-pull but worsens cost-push output losses.\",\"limitations\":[\"Real episodes often mix both causes\",\"Expectations and wage bargaining can turn a one-off shock into ongoing inflation\"],\"real\":\"The 1970s oil shocks are the standard cost-push case; Europe’s 2022 energy price rises are a recent one.\",\"misconception\":\"Defining inflation as high prices rather than a sustained rise in the price level, or shifting AD for cost-push inflation.\",\"exam\":\"Draw the correct shift, label P₁ → P₂ and Y₁ → Y₂, and link the cause to the policy that fits.\",\"deeper\":\"Milton Friedman argued that sustained inflation is a monetary phenomenon: without money growth, a cost shock would raise some prices and lower others. Keynesians stress wage bargaining, market power and supply shocks. Modern central banks focus on expectations: if people expect inflation to stay near target, a cost shock may raise prices once without starting a wage-price spiral, which is why credibility matters.\"},{\"model\":\"Short-run and long-run Phillips curves (HL only)\",\"dg\":\"phillips\",\"purpose\":\"HL only: shows the possible trade-off between unemployment and inflation, and why it may vanish in the long run.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Inflation expectations are fixed in the short run\",\"Expectations adjust to actual inflation over time\",\"A natural rate of unemployment exists\"],\"mechanism\":\"Along the short-run curve (SRPC), higher AD cuts unemployment and raises inflation. Once workers expect the higher inflation, the SRPC shifts up and unemployment returns to the natural rate, where the long-run curve (LRPC) is vertical.\",\"prediction\":\"Unemployment can be held below the natural rate only temporarily, at the cost of higher inflation.\",\"application\":\"Evaluating demand management aimed at low unemployment, and explaining stagflation.\",\"limitations\":[\"The natural rate cannot be observed and may shift\",\"Anchored expectations can make the SRPC very flat\",\"Supply shocks shift the SRPC\"],\"real\":\"In the 1970s many industrial economies saw inflation and unemployment rise together, which a stable trade-off could not explain.\",\"misconception\":\"Believing in a permanent trade-off, or drawing the LRPC downward-sloping.\",\"exam\":\"Label inflation rate (vertical), unemployment rate (horizontal), SRPC, LRPC and the natural rate.\",\"deeper\":\"The curve comes from A. W. Phillips’s 1958 study of UK wage inflation and unemployment. Milton Friedman and Edmund Phelps argued in the late 1960s that any trade-off would disappear once expectations adjusted, which 1970s stagflation appeared to confirm. In the 2010s the short-run curve looked unusually flat in several economies, perhaps because anchored expectations muted wage pressure; the inflation of 2021–22 revived the debate.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"AD–AS, and at HL the short-run Phillips curve, suggest that cutting unemployment tends to raise inflation, while the long-run view says the trade-off disappears once expectations adjust.\",\"evidence\":\"The 1970s saw inflation and unemployment rise together after supply shocks, and in the 2010s several advanced economies combined low unemployment with low inflation. Available evidence suggests the relationship is real but unstable across periods and countries.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that credible central banks anchored expectations and flattened the relationship; another is that the natural rate fell. Keynesians see more room for demand policy to cut unemployment; monetarists stress the risk of unanchored inflation.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Whose unemployment? Definitions and data\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Unemployment figures rest on choices that look technical. Survey measures count as unemployed anyone without work who is available and searched recently; claimant counts depend on benefit rules that governments can change. Countries treat students and informal workers differently. When China’s statistics bureau paused its youth unemployment series in 2023 and later resumed it with a revised method, analysts debated how comparable the figures were. Before comparing unemployment across countries or years, check the definition and any change of method.\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: when is government debt sustainable?\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Debt is measured against GDP because national income ultimately services it, so the ratio can fall even while debt rises, if nominal GDP grows faster. A useful rule compares the interest rate on the debt (r) with nominal GDP growth (g). When r is below g, a government can run a modest primary deficit (a deficit before interest payments) and still stabilise the ratio; when r rises above g, the same deficits push it up. There is no single safe threshold: credibility, the currency of borrowing and who holds the debt all matter.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Governments want three things from the economy: more output over time, jobs for people who want them, and prices that rise slowly and predictably. Each has a yardstick: the growth rate of real GDP, the unemployment rate, and the inflation rate from the CPI. The trouble is that the goals can clash. Push spending up to create jobs, and prices may rise faster. Grow fast, and pollution or inequality may rise. Much of economic policy is about choosing between these clashes and deciding who carries the cost.\",\"exam\":\"Start by defining each objective precisely: inflation as a sustained rise in the general price level; unemployment as people able, willing and actively seeking work but without a job. Diagnose before prescribing: name the type of unemployment or cause of inflation and draw the matching diagram. Show calculations step by step with the base period stated. In evaluation, compare costs for stakeholders such as savers, borrowers, young job-seekers and exporters, separate short run from long run, and address conflicts between objectives. HL answers can deepen the argument with the short-run and long-run Phillips curves and with the costs of a high government debt.\",\"deep\":\"The objectives are proxies for well-being, not ends in themselves. Growth matters because it widens consumption possibilities; unemployment because it wastes resources and harms people; inflation because unexpected or volatile inflation distorts decisions and redistributes wealth arbitrarily. Economists disagree about relative costs. Some studies of survey data suggest a percentage point of unemployment lowers reported life satisfaction more than a percentage point of inflation, largely because the unemployed suffer heavily. Yet very high inflation can undermine the monetary system itself, as hyperinflations show. Weighting the objectives therefore needs value judgements about whose losses count, and over what time horizon. A central bank’s mandate, such as an inflation target alongside concern for employment, is one society’s answer to that question, and it can be revised when priorities change.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"Unemployment and inflation damage living standards in different ways for different groups.\",\"tension\":\"Cutting inflation quickly may require a period of higher unemployment.\",\"ia\":\"A cost-of-living article, analysed by who loses most from inflation.\"},{\"c\":\"Sustainability\",\"here\":\"Growth that depletes resources can conflict with future needs; at HL, so can debt.\",\"tension\":\"Fast growth now can reduce resources and fiscal room later.\",\"ia\":\"A growth-target article, framed by whether the growth can last.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Inflation redistributes between borrowers and lenders, and growth can widen or narrow income gaps.\",\"tension\":\"Disinflation policy may hit hardest the workers who lose jobs.\",\"ia\":\"An article on real wages lagging prices, analysed by who bears the burden.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why can unemployment and inflation rise at the same time, if they are supposed to trade off?\",\"expect\":\"If more spending lowers unemployment and raises prices, the two should move in opposite directions.\",\"model\":\"A cost shock shifts SRAS left, raising prices and cutting output, so both rise; at HL, the short-run Phillips curve shifts up.\",\"evidence\":\"The oil shocks of the 1970s were followed by rising inflation and, in many economies, rising unemployment.\",\"match\":\"Largely, once the shock is identified: a stable trade-off describes demand shocks with given expectations, not supply shocks or shifting expectations.\",\"assume\":\"Whether wages chase prices.\",\"change\":\"Anchored expectations could let a cost shock’s inflation fade.\",\"policy\":\"Cost-push inflation poses a dilemma: tightening fights inflation but deepens the output loss.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why deflation caused by a fall in AD can harm an economy.\",\"check\":[\"Deflation is a sustained fall in the price level, not disinflation\",\"People delay purchases expecting lower prices, cutting AD\",\"The real value of debt rises, so debtors cut spending\",\"Bankruptcies and cyclical unemployment rise\",\"Interest rates near zero limit monetary policy\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain how the CPI measures inflation and why it may misstate the change in the cost of living.\",\"check\":[\"A typical household’s basket is priced each period, weighted by spending\",\"The basket’s cost is indexed to a base year of 100\",\"Inflation is the percentage change in the index\",\"The basket may not match a given group’s spending\",\"Quality changes, new products and substitution bias it\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Real GDP rises from $400bn to $412bn. Calculate the growth rate.\",\"a\":\"(412 − 400) ÷ 400 × 100 = 3%.\"},{\"q\":\"47 million people are employed and 3 million unemployed. Calculate the unemployment rate.\",\"a\":\"3 ÷ 50 × 100 = 6%.\"},{\"q\":\"The CPI rises from 125 to 130. Calculate the inflation rate.\",\"a\":\"(130 − 125) ÷ 125 × 100 = 4%, not 5%.\"},{\"q\":\"Which types of unemployment make up the natural rate?\",\"a\":\"Structural, seasonal and frictional (guide PDF p. 44).\"},{\"q\":\"Distinguish disinflation from deflation.\",\"a\":\"Disinflation: inflation falls but prices still rise. Deflation: the price level falls.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: debt is $900bn and GDP $1,200bn. State the debt ratio and one cost of high debt.\",\"a\":\"75% of GDP; for example, higher interest payments, which have an opportunity cost in other public spending.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"The inflation rate falls from 6% to 2%. What has happened to the price level?\",\"opts\":[\"It has fallen\",\"It is still rising, but more slowly\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It has fallen by 4%\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Positive inflation means the price level is still rising; a fall from 6% to 2% is disinflation, while deflation needs a negative rate. Choosing a fall in prices confuses the rate with the level.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-09\"},{\"q\":\"A coal mine closes and its workers lack the skills for the region’s growing service jobs. What type of unemployment results?\",\"opts\":[\"Cyclical\",\"Structural\",\"Frictional\",\"Seasonal\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A skills mismatch is structural and would persist even with strong AD, so demand stimulus would do little. Cyclical unemployment comes from a general shortfall of AD.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-08\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. According to the long-run Phillips curve, what happens if a government keeps raising AD to hold unemployment below the natural rate?\",\"opts\":[\"Unemployment stays lower at a stable, higher inflation rate\",\"Inflation keeps rising as expectations adjust\",\"Inflation falls because output is higher\",\"Unemployment and inflation both fall\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Each time workers come to expect higher inflation, the SRPC shifts up, so holding unemployment below the natural rate needs ever-rising inflation. The first option describes a stable trade-off, which the long-run view rejects.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-11\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (b)–(f): calculate a growth, unemployment or inflation rate and comment\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain a type of unemployment or cause of inflation with its diagram\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate unemployment against inflation, or conflicts between objectives\",\"Paper 3 (HL): weighted price index, debt ratio or Phillips curves\"],\"ct\":[\"calculate\",\"define\",\"distinguish\",\"explain\",\"evaluate\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"PPC showing actual growth and growth in production possibilities; AD and LRAS increases raising output; deflationary gap; minimum wage; fall in labour demand in one market; demand-pull, cost-push and deflation (guide PDF pp. 43–44). HL only: AD–AS trade-off and short-run and long-run Phillips curves (guide PDF pp. 44–45).\",\"calc\":\"SL and HL: growth rate, unemployment rate, and inflation rate from data using quantities as CPI weights. HL only: a weighted price index (guide PDF pp. 43–44).\",\"apply\":\"Use the country’s data, check its definitions, and name the type of unemployment or cause of inflation first.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh costs across savers, borrowers, the unemployed and exporters, and short run against long run.\",\"errors\":[\"Confusing disinflation with deflation\",\"Subtracting index numbers instead of calculating a percentage change\",\"Prescribing demand stimulus for structural unemployment\",\"HL: drawing a downward-sloping LRPC\"],\"strong\":[\"Diagnose the cause before recommending anything\",\"Interpret each calculated figure\",\"Compare costs for named groups and say which objective matters most here\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: in the base year a household basket contains 10 units of food at $4 and 5 units of transport at $8. A year later food costs $4.40 and transport $8.40, with the same quantities. Calculate the inflation rate.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that deflation is a greater threat to an economy than inflation.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: government debt rises from $600bn to $660bn while nominal GDP rises from $1,000bn to $1,050bn. Calculate the debt-to-GDP ratio in both years, then recommend whether the government should prioritise cutting its budget deficit next year.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"AD–AS gaps and shifts show cyclical unemployment, demand-pull and cost-push inflation, and growth.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Unemployment and inflation are causes of poverty, and growth can widen inequality.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"why\":\"Central banks set interest rates to meet inflation targets, weighing unemployment.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"why\":\"The conflict between growth and environmental sustainability returns in sustainable development.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Because unemployment, inflation and the growth–equity conflict feed directly into the distribution of income and the causes of poverty.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on an inflation release, a cost-of-living squeeze, rising youth unemployment or a government’s debt fit this subtopic. Frame the commentary through economic well-being (who is hurt, and how much?) or equity (who gains and loses from inflation?), and diagnose the cause before choosing a diagram.\",\"ee\":\"How far did energy prices, compared with demand-side factors, explain changes in a chosen economy’s inflation rate between 2021 and 2023?\",\"tok\":\"Unemployment and inflation statistics depend on definitions chosen by statistical agencies; can a statistic be both objective and shaped by human choices?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can diagnose the type of unemployment or cause of inflation and draw the matching diagram.\",\"starter\":\"Show a CPI series that rises every year, more slowly in the last two, and ask whether prices are falling.\",\"activity\":\"Basket-building: groups price an illustrative basket in two years, compute the index and inflation rate, then change the weights.\",\"discussion\":\"A young worker facing 10% unemployment or a pensioner facing 10% inflation: who loses more?\",\"check\":\"Six scenario cards classified by unemployment type or inflation cause, each with its diagram named.\",\"extension\":\"HL: plot one country’s inflation and unemployment over time and discuss whether a stable Phillips curve appears.\",\"homework\":\"Three calculations (growth, unemployment, inflation) from an illustrative table, each with a one-sentence interpretation.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define growth, unemployment, inflation, disinflation and deflation precisely.\",\"I can calculate growth, unemployment and inflation rates, and (HL) a weighted price index.\",\"I can distinguish the four types of unemployment and draw the matching diagrams.\",\"I can draw demand-pull and cost-push inflation and deflation.\",\"I can explain the costs of inflation and deflation for different stakeholders.\",\"I can evaluate the relative costs of unemployment and inflation and the conflicts between objectives.\",\"HL: I can draw short-run and long-run Phillips curves and explain the costs of high government debt.\",\"I can explain disinflation versus deflation without notes.\"]},\"3.4\":{\"code\":\"3.4\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Inequality is about how income and wealth are shared; poverty is about whether people fall below a threshold of what they need. Economists measure the first with the Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient, and the second with absolute, relative and multidimensional measures. Whether a distribution is fair is a normative judgement; the economics lies in tracing causes and weighing policy trade-offs.\",\"why\":\"Inequality and poverty shape health, education and social mobility, and tax and benefit systems are among the largest things governments run.\",\"q\":\"Should a government tax high incomes more heavily to reduce inequality, and would it cost growth?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The meaning of equity and equality; positive and normative statements\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"},{\"t\":\"A minimum wage as a price floor in the labour market\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Real GDP per capita as an average, and PPP\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"Unemployment and inflation as sources of hardship\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Distinguish equality from equity, and income from wealth\",\"Define absolute and relative poverty and name the MPI’s three dimensions\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain how the Gini coefficient is derived from the Lorenz curve\",\"Explain why indirect taxes tend to be regressive relative to income\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a tax as progressive, regressive or proportional from data\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a named cause widens income gaps\",\"Analyse how redistribution moves the Lorenz curve\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate anti-poverty and redistributive policies, weighing equity against incentives and cost\",\"Evaluate the impact of inequality on growth, living standards and social stability\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate total tax and average tax rates from data (HL only)\",\"Calculate indirect tax paid from expenditure and a tax rate (HL only)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a labelled Lorenz curve and show a change in distribution\",\"Construct a Lorenz curve from quintile data (HL only)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Compare Gini coefficients before and after taxes and transfers\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend a policy mix to reduce poverty in a context with data\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Equality describes; equity judges\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Equality describes how evenly income, wealth or opportunity is shared. Equity is a judgement about fairness, and fairness need not mean equal shares. Equity of opportunity asks whether people start with comparable chances in education, health and access to jobs; equity of outcome asks whether the resulting distribution is acceptable. Many people accept gaps that reward effort or risk but not gaps rooted in birth or discrimination. Economists measure inequality; how much is too much involves values.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Equality\",\"Equity\"],\"rows\":[[\"Kind of statement\",\"Positive: measurable\",\"Normative: a judgement\"],[\"Question\",\"How evenly is income shared?\",\"Is this distribution fair?\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Income and wealth\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Income is a flow received over a period: wages, rent, interest, profit and transfers. Wealth is a stock at a point in time: housing, savings, shares and pensions, minus debts. Wealth generates income, and saved income adds to wealth. In most countries with data, wealth is far more unequal than income, because it accumulates over lifetimes, can be inherited and earns returns that can be reinvested.\"},{\"h\":\"The Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A Lorenz curve plots the cumulative percentage of income against the cumulative percentage of population, ranked from poorest to richest. With perfect equality it lies on the 45° line of equality; the more unequal the distribution, the further it bows below. Gini coefficient = area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve (A) ÷ total area under the line of equality (A + B). It runs from 0 (perfect equality) to 1. Constructing the curve from quintile data is HL only (guide PDF p. 45).\",\"because\":[\"Illustrative quintile shares: 5%, 10%, 15%, 25%, 45%\",\"Cumulative: 5%, 15%, 30%, 55%, 100%\",\"Plot against 20%, 40%, 60%, 80%, 100% of population\",\"The poorest 40% receive 15% of income, so the curve sags and the Gini is well above 0\"]},{\"h\":\"Measuring poverty\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Absolute poverty is income too low to meet basic needs such as food, water, shelter and clothing, judged against a fixed threshold. Relative poverty is income well below the typical level in a society, often below 50% or 60% of median income, so it moves with the distribution. The guide lists single indicators (international poverty lines, minimum income standards) and composite indicators such as the Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) (guide PDF p. 45). Measurement is hard: surveys are infrequent, informal income is hard to value, and households may not share resources equally.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Measure\",\"Captures\",\"Misses\"],\"rows\":[[\"International poverty line (dollars a day at PPP)\",\"Absolute deprivation\",\"Non-income needs\"],[\"Relative line\",\"Exclusion from normal life\",\"Absolute hardship\"],[\"Minimum income standard\",\"An acceptable minimum, as the public judges it\",\"Cross-country comparison\"],[\"MPI\",\"Deprivation in health, education, living standards\",\"Income; weights are judgements\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why inequality and poverty arise\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide lists the causes (guide PDF p. 45). Unequal opportunity and different levels of human capital lead to different earnings. Owners of land, capital and financial assets receive rent, profit and interest that can compound. Discrimination by gender, race or other characteristics lowers pay and access to jobs. Unequal status and power shape who captures income, and tax and benefit systems can narrow or widen gaps. Globalisation and technological change have tended to raise returns to high skills and capital relative to routine work. Market-based supply-side policies, such as cutting top tax rates, may raise efficiency but widen inequality.\"},{\"h\":\"Taxes: progressive, regressive, proportional; direct and indirect\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Taxes are classified by the average tax rate, total tax ÷ total income × 100, as income rises. A progressive tax takes a rising share of income, so its marginal rate (the rate on an extra unit of income) exceeds its average rate. A proportional tax takes a constant share; a regressive tax a falling share, even if the rich pay more money. Direct taxes fall on personal income, corporate income and wealth; indirect taxes fall on spending and tend to be regressive, because poorer households spend a larger share of income. Average and marginal tax rates, and calculating tax paid and average rates, are HL only (guide PDF p. 46).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Tax\",\"Average rate as income rises\",\"Example\"],\"rows\":[[\"Progressive\",\"Rises\",\"Banded income tax\"],[\"Proportional\",\"Constant\",\"Flat tax, no allowance\"],[\"Regressive\",\"Falls\",\"Sales tax, against income\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Policies beyond taxation\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide names further policies (guide PDF p. 46). Investment in human capital, such as early-years education and health care, tackles unequal opportunity but pays off slowly. Transfer payments such as pensions and child benefits raise low incomes directly; means-tested transfers target need but can create a poverty trap if withdrawn steeply as earnings rise. Targeted spending provides goods in kind, such as free school meals. A universal basic income pays everyone unconditionally, avoiding poverty traps at high gross cost. Anti-discrimination laws address unequal pay; minimum wages raise low pay but, if set high, may reduce hiring.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A more progressive income tax funds larger transfers\",\"cause\":\"The government raises the top marginal tax rate and increases cash transfers to low-income households.\",\"mechanism\":\"Disposable income falls at the top and rises at the bottom.\",\"immediate\":\"The post-tax Lorenz curve moves towards the line of equality, the Gini falls, and relative poverty may fall.\",\"secondary\":\"High earners may avoid tax, relocate or work less; low-income households spend more of their extra income, supporting AD.\",\"longrun\":\"If revenue funds human capital, opportunity may widen; if avoidance is large, revenue and redistribution shrink.\",\"depends\":\"How responsive taxable income is to tax rates, how steeply transfers are withdrawn, and enforcement.\"},{\"t\":\"Persistent inequality: growth, living standards and social stability\",\"cause\":\"Large gaps in income and wealth persist across generations.\",\"mechanism\":\"Poorer families often cannot borrow enough to invest in education and health, so talented children acquire less human capital.\",\"immediate\":\"Social mobility is lower, abilities are underused, and the poorest face worse health and housing.\",\"secondary\":\"Concentrated wealth may buy political influence; some studies link high inequality with lower trust and more social tension.\",\"longrun\":\"Potential growth may be lower; against this, some inequality rewards work, saving, risk-taking and innovation.\",\"depends\":\"Whether inequality reflects opportunity or effort, public services and institutions, and the time horizon.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient\",\"dg\":\"lorenz\",\"purpose\":\"Summarises how income or wealth is distributed, for comparisons over time, between countries, or before and after redistribution.\",\"assumptions\":[\"People or households can be ranked by income\",\"Income is measured consistently, for example disposable income adjusted for household size\"],\"mechanism\":\"Cumulative income shares are plotted against cumulative population shares; the further the curve lies below the line of equality, the higher the Gini.\",\"prediction\":\"Progressive taxes and transfers move the post-tax curve towards the line of equality and lower the Gini.\",\"application\":\"Measuring the effect of a tax-and-benefit system.\",\"limitations\":[\"Two crossing curves can share a Gini\",\"It shows shares, not income levels or poverty\",\"Surveys tend to under-record top incomes\"],\"real\":\"Official statistics in many high-income countries show a noticeably lower Gini for disposable income than for market income, reflecting redistribution through taxes and transfers.\",\"misconception\":\"Reading the Gini as the share of people who are poor, or thinking a curve further from the diagonal means a lower Gini.\",\"exam\":\"Label axes as cumulative % of population and of income, draw the line of equality, and show the shift the policy causes. HL only: construct the curve from quintile data.\",\"deeper\":\"Because the Gini is most sensitive to changes in the middle of the distribution, some economists prefer the Palma ratio: the income share of the richest 10% divided by that of the poorest 40%. It rests on an observed regularity that in many countries the households between the poorest 40% and the richest 10% (deciles 5 to 9) receive roughly half of income, so most variation comes from the tails. Any single number discards information, so say which part of the distribution changed.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"A long-standing view, captured in Arthur Okun’s image of a “leaky bucket”, holds that redistribution trades equity against efficiency: taxes and transfers weaken incentives and lose resources on the way.\",\"evidence\":\"Cross-country evidence is mixed. Some studies, including work by IMF economists, find that moderate redistribution is not associated with slower growth and that high inequality is associated with shorter growth spells; other work finds that high tax rates reduce reported taxable income.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that the trade-off exists but its size depends on design: spending on education and health may raise efficiency, while steep benefit withdrawal lowers it.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why the poverty line is a choice\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Every poverty figure rests on a line someone chose. An absolute line shows whether the poorest can buy a basic basket, but it depends on PPP conversions and on what counts as basic. A relative line moves with median income, which produces a surprise: in a recession that cuts middle incomes more than the lowest, measured relative poverty can fall although no one is better off. Composite indices such as the MPI avoid income altogether but need choices about indicators and weights. The questions to ask are which line, set by whom, and why.\"},{\"h\":\"Does inequality help or harm growth?\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"Economists have argued both ways. The incentive view holds that unequal rewards encourage effort, saving and innovation, so compressing incomes may slow growth. The opportunity view holds that high inequality wastes talent when poorer families cannot invest in education, and that concentrated wealth can capture political influence. Simon Kuznets suggested inequality might first rise and then fall as countries develop, but later data show no single pattern. Some studies suggest the source matters: gaps from unequal opportunity tend to do more damage than gaps from differences in effort.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine lining up everyone in a country from lowest income to highest. Inequality asks how much of the total income each part of the line receives. Poverty asks how many people near the front fall below what they need, either to survive or to take part in normal life in that country. A Lorenz curve draws the line-up as a picture, and the Gini coefficient turns it into one number. Governments can change the picture with taxes and benefits, but deciding what is fair is a question of values as well as economics.\",\"exam\":\"Start by separating equality, which can be measured, from equity, which is a judgement about fairness. Define the Lorenz curve and Gini precisely, draw the curve with cumulative percentages on both axes and the line of equality, and show how a policy shifts it. Distinguish absolute from relative poverty and say what each measure misses. To evaluate a policy, trace its mechanism, such as a progressive tax funding transfers, then weigh equity gains against incentive effects, cost, targeting and administration, for named stakeholders. HL answers may add average tax rate and indirect tax calculations and a Lorenz curve built from quintile data.\",\"deep\":\"Inequality results from how an economy rewards factors of production and how institutions redistribute those rewards. Market incomes reflect productivity, but also bargaining power, inheritance, discrimination and luck; the tax-and-benefit system then reshapes them. Economists studying top income shares from tax records have found large shifts over the twentieth century in several countries, which suggests that policy and institutions, not only technology, shape distribution. The policy debate turns on three questions: how large are the incentive costs of redistribution in practice, which instruments reduce inequality of opportunity most cost-effectively, and how much weight society puts on the worst-off. Only the first two are empirical questions; the third is a value judgement that economics can inform with evidence on costs and consequences but cannot settle on its own.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"The subtopic rests on the difference between measuring inequality and judging whether it is fair.\",\"tension\":\"More equal outcomes through high taxes may conflict with rewarding effort and risk.\",\"ia\":\"A tax-reform article, analysed by who gains and loses.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"Poverty is a direct measure of low well-being, and inequality shapes health and opportunity.\",\"tension\":\"Growth can raise average well-being while leaving the poorest behind.\",\"ia\":\"A child-poverty article, examined through what its measure captures.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Taxes, transfers, minimum wages and UBI all change the distribution markets produce.\",\"tension\":\"Targeted help can create poverty traps; universal help costs more.\",\"ia\":\"A minimum-wage article, weighing higher pay against possible effects on hiring.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why might a country’s relative poverty rate fall during a recession?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect poverty to rise as jobs are lost and incomes fall.\",\"model\":\"Relative poverty is measured against a share of median income; if middle incomes fall more than benefit-supported low incomes, the line falls and fewer people are below it.\",\"evidence\":\"After the 2008–09 recession, analysts in some high-income countries observed relative poverty rates flat or falling while median incomes fell.\",\"match\":\"Yes, but the result reflects the measure, not better living standards.\",\"assume\":\"That benefits protect the lowest incomes while the median falls.\",\"change\":\"An absolute line fixed in real terms, or a multidimensional measure, could reverse the conclusion.\",\"policy\":\"Track several poverty measures, not one.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how the Gini coefficient is derived from a Lorenz curve.\",\"check\":[\"Horizontal axis: cumulative % of population, poorest first\",\"Vertical axis: cumulative % of income\",\"The 45° line shows perfect equality\",\"Gini = A ÷ (A + B)\",\"It runs from 0 (equality) to 1, and higher means more unequal\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why an indirect tax can be regressive even though everyone pays the same rate.\",\"check\":[\"It is levied on spending, not income\",\"Regressivity is judged by the average rate relative to income\",\"Poorer households spend a larger share of income\",\"So tax paid is a larger share of their income\",\"The judgement can change if measured over a lifetime\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Distinguish income from wealth.\",\"a\":\"Income is a flow over a period; wealth is a stock of assets minus debts at a point in time.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a Gini coefficient of 0 mean?\",\"a\":\"Perfect equality: the Lorenz curve lies on the line of equality.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: income tax is 0% on the first $10,000 and 20% above it. Find total tax and the average rate on $30,000.\",\"a\":\"20% × $20,000 = $4,000; average rate = 4,000 ÷ 30,000 × 100 ≈ 13.3%.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: a shopper spends $220 on goods whose prices include a 10% sales tax. How much tax was paid?\",\"a\":\"220 × 10 ÷ 110 = $20 (if $220 were pre-tax spending, $22).\"},{\"q\":\"Name the MPI’s three dimensions.\",\"a\":\"Health, education and living standards.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one advantage and one disadvantage of a universal basic income.\",\"a\":\"No poverty trap, as it is not withdrawn; but a high gross cost.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A country’s Gini coefficient rises from 0.30 to 0.36. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"The share of people in poverty rose from 30% to 36%\",\"Income became more unequally distributed\",\"Average income fell\",\"The Lorenz curve moved closer to the line of equality\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A higher Gini means a larger area between the Lorenz curve and the line of equality. It is a ratio of areas, not a share of people, and says nothing about average income.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"A 10% sales tax applies to all spending. A earns $20,000 and spends $18,000 before tax; B earns $100,000 and spends $50,000. Relative to income, the tax is:\",\"opts\":[\"Progressive: B pays more money\",\"Proportional: both pay 10%\",\"Regressive: it takes 9% of A’s income and 5% of B’s\",\"Neutral: it is levied on spending\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A pays $1,800 (9% of income), B pays $5,000 (5%). The average rate falls as income rises, so the tax is regressive; the same rate on spending does not make it proportional to income.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Every income in a country, including the median, rises by 10%. Relative poverty is income below 60% of the median. What happens to the relative poverty rate?\",\"opts\":[\"It falls by 10%\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It rises\",\"It falls to zero\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The line rises by 10% with every income, so the same people stay below it. An absolute poverty measure would show a fall if the 10% rise is in real terms; a relative measure responds only to changes in distribution.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): use a Lorenz curve to explain a change in distribution, or classify a tax from data\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain causes of inequality, or absolute versus relative poverty\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate policies to reduce inequality or poverty\",\"Paper 3 (HL): construct a Lorenz curve, calculate tax rates, recommend a policy\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"distinguish\",\"explain\",\"construct\",\"calculate\",\"evaluate\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"A Lorenz curve with cumulative % of population and of income on the axes, the line of equality, and a change in distribution (guide PDF p. 45). HL only: construction from quintile data.\",\"calc\":\"HL only: indirect tax paid from a given expenditure and rate; total tax and average tax rates from data (guide PDF p. 46); Lorenz construction from quintile data (guide PDF p. 45).\",\"apply\":\"Use the country’s Gini, poverty measures and tax structure, and name the causes at work there.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh equity gains against incentives, cost and targeting, and short-run relief against long-run opportunity.\",\"errors\":[\"Reading the Gini as a percentage of people\",\"Calling a tax progressive because the rich pay more money\",\"Comparing relative poverty across countries as if the line were common\"],\"strong\":[\"Separate measurement of inequality from the judgement of equity\",\"Use a labelled Lorenz curve in the argument\",\"Match the policy to the cause\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a Lorenz curve, explain how a progressive income tax combined with transfer payments affects the distribution of income.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate a universal basic income as a way to reduce poverty compared with means-tested transfers.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: income quintile shares are 4%, 9%, 15%, 24% and 48%. Construct a Lorenz curve, calculate the average tax rate for a household earning $40,000 under a tax of 0% on the first $12,000 and 25% above it, and recommend one policy to reduce relative poverty.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Unemployment and inflation are causes of poverty, and growth can conflict with equity.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.12\",\"why\":\"HL: the market’s inability to achieve equity explains the micro roots of the distribution analysed here.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Fiscal policy uses progressive taxes and transfers, the main redistributive tools.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Market-based supply-side policies may widen inequality; education and training can narrow it.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"why\":\"The MPI and inequality-adjusted indices extend into measuring development.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"why\":\"Because with the objectives and their distributional effects in place, the next question is how policy pursues them, starting with the central bank’s interest rate.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on a tax reform, a minimum-wage rise, a UBI pilot, new poverty statistics or a gender pay gap fit this subtopic. Frame the commentary through equity (is it fairer, and for whom?) or intervention (what does it cost, and what incentives does it create?), and check which measure the article uses.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent have changes to a chosen country’s taxes and transfers altered the Gini coefficient of disposable income over the past two decades?\",\"tok\":\"The Gini coefficient is precise, but the judgement that a distribution is unfair is not; can economics say how much inequality is too much, or only describe it?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can draw and interpret a Lorenz curve (HL: construct it from quintile data) and evaluate one policy to reduce inequality.\",\"starter\":\"Share 20 sweets among five volunteers in the proportions of a real quintile distribution; ask whether the split is equal, fair, or both.\",\"activity\":\"Pairs cumulate illustrative quintile shares for two countries and plot both Lorenz curves (construction is HL only; SL pairs can interpret curves supplied ready-made), and show a transfer programme’s effect.\",\"discussion\":\"Is inequality a problem in itself, or only when it comes from unequal opportunity?\",\"check\":\"From a two-band tax schedule, classify the tax; HL students also calculate average rates at two incomes.\",\"extension\":\"Compare a country’s Gini before and after taxes and transfers using official statistics.\",\"homework\":\"A 200-word evaluation of UBI or a minimum-wage rise as an anti-poverty policy, naming two stakeholders.\"},\"after\":[\"I can distinguish equality from equity, and income from wealth.\",\"I can draw a Lorenz curve and explain how the Gini coefficient is derived.\",\"I can distinguish absolute, relative and multidimensional poverty and what each misses.\",\"I can explain the main causes of inequality and poverty with examples.\",\"I can classify taxes as progressive, regressive or proportional, and direct or indirect.\",\"HL: I can construct a Lorenz curve from quintile data and calculate tax paid and average rates.\",\"I can evaluate taxes, transfers, UBI, minimum wages and human capital policies.\",\"I can explain why an indirect tax may be regressive without notes.\"]},\"3.5\":{\"code\":\"3.5\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Monetary policy is the central bank’s attempt to steer total spending through the price and quantity of money. Its main lever is the policy interest rate, whose effect spreads to loan rates, asset prices, the exchange rate and expectations. A cut aims to raise aggregate demand; a rise aims to cool it. The hard questions are how far the signal travels and how long it takes.\",\"why\":\"A rate change alters mortgage repayments, the return a firm needs from a new project and the value of the currency, so it reaches almost every economic decision.\",\"q\":\"If a central bank cuts interest rates during a recession, why might firms still refuse to invest?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The components of AD, and interest rates as a determinant of C and I\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"Deflationary and inflationary gaps; the Keynesian AS curve versus SRAS and LRAS\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"The macroeconomic objectives, including low and stable inflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"},{\"t\":\"Real versus nominal values\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"Percentage-point changes versus percentage changes\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the goals of monetary policy, including inflation targeting\",\"Identify the four tools: open market operations, minimum reserve requirements, the central bank minimum lending rate and quantitative easing (HL only — guide PDF p. 47)\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why the policy rate influences, but does not set, the rates households and firms pay\",\"Distinguish the nominal interest rate from the real interest rate\"],\"apply\":[\"Apply the transmission mechanism to a news report of a rate change, naming the components of AD likely to respond first\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse, with an AD/AS diagram, how monetary policy can close deflationary and inflationary gaps\",\"Explain money creation by commercial banks and the determination of the equilibrium interest rate (HL only — guide PDF p. 47)\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate monetary policy when rates are near zero or confidence is weak, weighing its flexibility against the long lag before it affects inflation\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate a real interest rate from given data, keeping any negative sign (guide PDF p. 48)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw AD/AS diagrams for expansionary and contractionary monetary policy (guide PDF p. 48)\",\"Draw the money market diagram with a vertical money supply curve (HL only — guide PDF p. 47)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Identify from a table of policy rates and inflation the periods when real interest rates were negative\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a policy-rate decision using data on the output gap, inflation and confidence (HL Paper 3 — guide PDF p. 68)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"What monetary policy is, and what it aims at\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Monetary policy is the use of interest rates and the money supply by a central bank to influence aggregate demand. The syllabus lists five goals: low and stable inflation, often pursued through an explicit inflation target; low unemployment; smaller business-cycle fluctuations; a stable environment for long-term growth; and external balance (guide PDF p. 47). Many central banks have operational independence: the target is set by law or by the government, and the bank chooses how to reach it. For many, price stability is the primary legal objective; the US Federal Reserve instead has a dual mandate that gives employment similar weight.\"},{\"h\":\"The transmission mechanism\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A rate cut does not reach households directly. It lowers the cost at which commercial banks obtain funds, and they pass some or all of the change on to loan, mortgage and saving rates. The policy rate is a lever, not the rate a household pays: pass-through is weaker when many loans are on fixed rates or banks are short of capital. Several channels then work at once — borrowing costs and the reward for saving, asset prices and wealth, the exchange rate, and expectations of future income and inflation.\",\"because\":[\"The central bank cuts its policy rate\",\"banks’ funding costs fall, and loan and mortgage rates follow, at least in part\",\"borrowing becomes cheaper and saving less rewarding, so C and I tend to rise\",\"lower returns on domestic assets can weaken the currency, raising X − M\",\"AD shifts right, raising real GDP and, closer to capacity, the price level\"]},{\"h\":\"Real versus nominal interest rates\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The nominal interest rate is the rate quoted in money terms. The real interest rate adjusts it for inflation: real rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation rate (guide PDF p. 48). It measures the change in purchasing power that a lender gains or a borrower gives up, so a negative real rate means savers lose and borrowers gain. A rate calculated from last year’s inflation is backward-looking (ex post); spending decisions depend on the real rate people expect over the life of a loan, so the same nominal rate can be loose when inflation is high and tight when it is low.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Nominal rate\",\"Inflation rate\",\"Real rate (≈)\",\"Who gains in real terms? (Illustrative data)\"],\"rows\":[[\"6%\",\"4%\",\"+2%\",\"Savers and lenders\"],[\"3%\",\"7%\",\"−4%\",\"Borrowers: the real value of debts falls\"],[\"1%\",\"−1% (deflation)\",\"+2%\",\"Lenders: the real burden of debt rises\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Closing the gaps\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Expansionary (loose) monetary policy lowers interest rates or expands the money supply to shift AD right. It targets a deflationary gap, where real GDP is below potential output. Contractionary (tight) policy raises rates to shift AD left, and targets an inflationary gap, where demand pushes output beyond potential and drives inflation up (guide PDF p. 48). On the diagram, show AD₁ shifting to AD₂ and state the change in both real GDP and the price level. How the effect divides between output and prices depends on where the economy sits on the AS curve.\"},{\"h\":\"Strengths and constraints\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide names two constraints (guide PDF p. 48). First, there is little room to cut when rates are already close to zero, because people and banks can hold cash rather than accept a large negative return. Second, confidence: if households fear job losses and firms expect weak sales, cheaper credit may not tempt them to borrow — “pushing on a string”. Against these, the guide lists strengths: changes can be small, frequent and easily reversed, and time lags are short. That last point applies mainly to decisions; the effect on inflation usually builds over many months.\"},{\"h\":\"Tools and money creation (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide lists four tools, all HL only (guide PDF p. 47). The minimum lending rate — also called the base, discount or refinancing rate — is what the central bank charges commercial banks. Open market operations buy or sell government bonds to add or drain bank reserves. Reserve requirements fix the share of deposits banks must hold. Quantitative easing (QE) is large-scale asset buying when rates are near zero. Banks create money when they lend: with a 10% reserve requirement, a new $1,000 deposit supports a $900 loan, which is spent and redeposited, supporting $810 more, until deposits reach up to $10,000 (Illustrative data). Cash holdings, spare reserves and weak loan demand make the real expansion smaller.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A policy-rate cut to close a deflationary gap\",\"cause\":\"Real GDP is below potential and inflation is below target, so the central bank cuts its policy rate.\",\"mechanism\":\"Banks’ funding costs fall and they lower loan and deposit rates, often by less than the full cut. Asset prices tend to rise and the currency to weaken.\",\"immediate\":\"Bond yields and the exchange rate react within hours, and borrowers on variable rates have more disposable income.\",\"secondary\":\"Spending on durables, investment and net exports rise with a lag. The extra spending becomes other people’s income, so AD shifts right.\",\"longrun\":\"Output moves back towards potential. Rates held low for too long risk inflation or asset-price booms, and potential output is not directly raised.\",\"depends\":\"Confidence and the demand for loans, how fully banks pass on the cut, the share of fixed-rate debt, the size of the output gap, openness to trade, and how close rates already are to zero.\"},{\"t\":\"A rate rise to curb demand-pull inflation\",\"cause\":\"Demand is pushing output beyond potential and inflation well above target, so the central bank raises its policy rate.\",\"mechanism\":\"Loan and mortgage rates rise, saving becomes more attractive, asset prices tend to fall, and capital inflows can appreciate the currency.\",\"immediate\":\"Variable-rate borrowers face higher repayments, firms postpone marginal projects, and imports become cheaper.\",\"secondary\":\"C, I and net exports fall, so AD shifts left. If the central bank is credible, wage and price setters also lower their inflation expectations.\",\"longrun\":\"Inflation tends to return towards target, usually with a temporary loss of output and jobs. The size of that cost depends on how well anchored expectations were.\",\"depends\":\"Whether inflation is demand-pull or cost-push (with cost-push, a rate rise lowers inflation mainly by lowering output), household debt, central-bank credibility and the length of the impact lag.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Monetary policy in the AD/AS model\",\"dg\":\"defgap\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how an interest-rate change shifts AD and so changes real GDP and the price level.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Interest rates move C, I and net exports in the expected direction\",\"Confidence and fiscal policy do not offset the shift\",\"The shape of the AS curve reflects how much spare capacity exists\"],\"mechanism\":\"A lower rate raises C, I and X − M, shifting AD from AD₁ to AD₂; a higher rate shifts it left.\",\"prediction\":\"With a large deflationary gap, a cut raises output with little inflation; near full employment, more of the effect is inflation. A rise closes an inflationary gap at some short-run cost in output.\",\"application\":\"Explaining a central bank’s decision in a Paper 2 text, or analysing the trade-off between output and inflation in a Paper 1 essay.\",\"limitations\":[\"It shows end points, not the long and variable lags in between\",\"It assumes a predictable AD response, when confidence can offset it\",\"It hides who gains and who loses\",\"It says little about the lower bound, where the rate tool runs out\"],\"real\":\"Many central banks, including the US Federal Reserve, raised rates rapidly in 2022 as inflation rose far above target. Inflation later eased in many economies, but economists still debate how much of the fall came from tighter policy and how much from supply pressures fading.\",\"misconception\":\"Shifting AS instead of AD, or treating the policy rate as the rate households pay and skipping the transmission steps.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): draw the AD shift and write the chain in steps. Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g): use it as the analytical core, then evaluate with lags, confidence and the lower bound.\",\"deeper\":\"The diagram treats AD as responding to “the” interest rate. Central banks think instead about the gap between the real policy rate and a neutral rate that neither stimulates nor restrains the economy. The neutral rate cannot be observed and estimates of it shift over time, so a rate that looks low could still be restrictive. Expectations matter too: a trusted central bank can move inflation expectations before rates change much. Disagreement about the neutral rate is one reason policymakers read the same data differently.\"},{\"model\":\"The money market: demand for and supply of money (HL only)\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains how the equilibrium interest rate is determined and how a change in the money supply moves it (HL only — guide PDF p. 47).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The central bank fixes the money supply, so the supply curve is vertical\",\"Money demand slopes down because the interest rate is the opportunity cost of holding money rather than bonds\",\"Income and the price level are held constant\"],\"mechanism\":\"Above the equilibrium rate, people hold surplus money and buy bonds, pushing bond prices up and rates down; below it, the reverse. A larger money supply shifts the vertical line right and lowers the equilibrium rate.\",\"prediction\":\"Open market purchases or QE lower interest rates. A rise in nominal income raises money demand, and so the rate, unless the central bank adds money.\",\"application\":\"HL answers explaining how open market operations or QE change interest rates.\",\"limitations\":[\"Most central banks set a rate and supply the reserves demanded at it, so supply acts more like a horizontal line at the policy rate\",\"Near zero rates, money demand may become very flat (a liquidity trap), so extra money barely lowers rates\",\"It treats many interest rates as one\"],\"real\":\"After 2008 several central banks expanded reserves enormously through QE while short-term rates stayed near zero — consistent with a liquidity trap, though central banks were also holding short rates at their floor deliberately.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing the money supply curve as upward-sloping, or confusing the money multiplier (bank lending) with the Keynesian multiplier (rounds of spending).\",\"exam\":\"HL: Paper 1, Paper 2 or Paper 3 explanations of how a named tool changes the money supply and the interest rate.\",\"deeper\":\"Some central banks once targeted the quantity of money; today most set an interest rate and let reserves adjust, which is why they announce rate decisions. Several, including the Bank of England, have published explanations of money creation that reverse the textbook order: a bank lends, creating a deposit at that moment, then obtains any reserves it needs at the policy rate. The US Federal Reserve set reserve requirements to zero in 2020. The diagram and the money multiplier remain useful teaching models, but they describe limits more than levers.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"In the AD/AS model, a lower policy rate raises C, I and net exports and shifts AD right; a higher rate shifts it left. The HL money market model adds that a larger money supply lowers the equilibrium interest rate.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies generally find that policy-rate changes do affect output and inflation, but with lags that can exceed a year and that vary between episodes. Evidence suggests QE lowered long-term bond yields; its effects on output and inflation are harder to pin down.\",\"interp\":\"Most economists accept that monetary policy affects output in the short run and mainly prices in the long run, but they disagree about its power near zero rates. Some read the slow recoveries after 2008 as a sign that it needed fiscal support; others argue that QE did much of the work and that recoveries were slow because households and banks were repairing their balance sheets.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why “short time lags” needs unpacking\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"The guide lists short time lags as a strength (guide PDF p. 48). Compared with fiscal policy, the decision lag is short: a committee can change the rate within days of new data, with no legislation. The impact lag is another matter. Milton Friedman described monetary policy as working with “long and variable lags”, and central banks commonly say that much of the effect on inflation takes more than a year to appear. So central banks act on forecasts rather than on today’s inflation, and a cut made because output is weak now may land in an economy that has already recovered.\"},{\"h\":\"Who wins and who loses from a rate change\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"Monetary policy is often called a blunt tool because it acts on the whole economy at once, but its effects are uneven. A rate rise hurts households with large variable-rate mortgages and firms with heavy debts, while helping savers. Low rates and QE tend to raise share and property prices, which benefits those who already own them. The exchange-rate channel shifts gains between exporters and importers. Central banks usually argue that stable inflation and employment help lower-income households most over time; critics reply that the side effects on wealth deserve more weight.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of the central bank as setting the price of borrowing. When it cuts its main interest rate, banks usually lower the rates they charge on loans and pay on savings. A car loan or a new factory becomes cheaper to finance and saving becomes less rewarding, so people and firms tend to spend more. When the central bank raises the rate, spending cools. The aim is to keep total spending in step with what the economy can produce, so that inflation stays low and jobs stay available. It is not precise: worried people may not borrow however cheap credit is, and the full effect takes many months.\",\"exam\":\"Define monetary policy precisely: central-bank action on interest rates or the money supply to influence aggregate demand. Identify the gap in the context and the policy that fits it. Draw AD/AS with the AD shift, labelling the price level and real GDP axes, AD₁ and AD₂, the AS curve, both equilibria and potential output. Write the chain in steps: policy rate → bank lending and saving rates → cost of borrowing and reward for saving → C and I, and exchange rate → X − M → AD → real GDP and price level. Evaluate with conditions from the context: the size of the gap, confidence, how close rates are to zero, the impact lag, and who gains or loses. HL students can add a named tool and the money market diagram.\",\"deep\":\"Behind the simplified model sits a question about expectations. Spending decisions depend on the real interest rate people expect over the life of a loan or project, not on today’s policy rate alone. That is why central banks invest in communication and credibility: by signalling the likely path of rates, they try to move longer-term rates now, and a trusted inflation target can restrain wage and price setting before rates change much. At the lower bound, a central bank can still try to lower expected real rates by promising to keep rates low or by buying long-term bonds. How powerful these tools are is disputed, partly because central banks act exactly when the economy is changing for other reasons, which makes the effect of policy hard to isolate.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Monetary policy intervenes through a price — the price of borrowing — rather than through direct spending or regulation.\",\"tension\":\"Raising rates to protect price stability can raise unemployment in the short run.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a rate rise could be framed as an intervention whose benefits arrive later than its costs.\"},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Central banks respond to changing inflation, output and expectations, and each decision takes months to work through.\",\"tension\":\"Acting early on a forecast risks being wrong; waiting for clear data risks acting too late.\",\"ia\":\"An article about a central bank reversing course could be analysed through the lag between decision and effect.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"Domestic rate changes move capital flows and the exchange rate, and rate changes abroad affect domestic conditions.\",\"tension\":\"A small open economy may feel pressure to follow larger economies’ rate moves even when domestic conditions differ.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a currency falling after foreign rate rises could explore how interdependence limits a central bank’s room for manoeuvre.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why did very low interest rates after 2008 not produce rapid recoveries in many advanced economies?\",\"expect\":\"Cheap credit should encourage borrowing and spending, so recovery should be quick.\",\"model\":\"AD/AS predicts that lower rates shift AD right; the HL money market model adds that near zero rates extra money may be held rather than spent.\",\"evidence\":\"Available data indicate that recoveries in several advanced economies were slower than after many earlier recessions, despite near-zero policy rates and large QE programmes, while many households and banks were reducing debts.\",\"match\":\"Partly: the direction matched, but the strength did not, because weak confidence and debt repair weakened transmission.\",\"assume\":\"That households and firms want to borrow when rates fall, and that banks want to lend.\",\"change\":\"Convincing evidence that output would have fallen much further without the cuts and QE would change the verdict from “weak” to “working but offset”.\",\"policy\":\"Near the lower bound, monetary policy may need fiscal support, and the health of the banking system matters as much as the level of the rate.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how a cut in the central bank’s policy rate could close a deflationary gap.\",\"check\":[\"Defines a deflationary gap as real GDP below potential output\",\"States that banks pass on the lower rate to loans and savings, at least in part\",\"Links cheaper borrowing and a lower reward for saving to higher C and I\",\"Includes the exchange-rate channel: a weaker currency raises net exports\",\"Shows AD shifting right on a labelled diagram\",\"Adds a condition, such as weak confidence or rates near zero, under which the effect is small\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a nominal interest rate of 4% could be expansionary in one year and contractionary in another (use illustrative inflation rates).\",\"check\":[\"Defines the real interest rate as the nominal rate adjusted for inflation\",\"Uses the approximation: real rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation rate\",\"Shows that with 6% inflation the real rate is about −2%, which favours borrowers\",\"Shows that with 1% inflation the real rate is about +3%, which discourages borrowing\",\"Notes that decisions depend on expected inflation\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"What is monetary policy?\",\"a\":\"The central bank’s use of interest rates and the money supply to influence aggregate demand, in pursuit of objectives such as low and stable inflation.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the nominal interest rate is 2.5% and inflation is 5%. What is the real interest rate?\",\"a\":\"About −2.5% (2.5 − 5): savers lose purchasing power and borrowers gain.\"},{\"q\":\"On an AD/AS diagram, what does contractionary monetary policy shift, and which way?\",\"a\":\"AD shifts left, lowering real GDP and the price level (or inflation), at least in the short run.\"},{\"q\":\"Name the two constraints on monetary policy listed in the guide.\",\"a\":\"Limited room to cut when rates are already close to zero, and low consumer and business confidence.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: why does the demand for money slope downwards against the interest rate?\",\"a\":\"The interest rate is the opportunity cost of holding money rather than interest-bearing assets, so a higher rate reduces the money people want to hold.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is monetary policy called incremental, flexible and easily reversible?\",\"a\":\"The rate can be changed in small steps at regular meetings and moved back if conditions change, without new legislation.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A central bank cuts its policy rate by 0.5 percentage points. Which statement is most accurate?\",\"opts\":[\"Every interest rate in the economy falls by exactly 0.5 percentage points\",\"Banks’ lending rates tend to fall, by an amount the banks decide\",\"Only the government’s borrowing costs change\",\"Mortgage rates rise because banks lose income\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The policy rate is the rate at which the central bank deals with commercial banks; how much reaches customers depends on competition, funding costs and how many loans are on fixed rates. The first option is tempting because news reports speak of “interest rates” as if there were only one.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-13\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: inflation is 8% and the nominal interest rate is 5%. A student says policy must be tight because 5% is high by recent standards. What is the best response?\",\"opts\":[\"Agree: a 5% nominal rate is restrictive\",\"Disagree: the real rate is about −3%, which tends to encourage borrowing\",\"Disagree: the real rate is about 13%\",\"Agree: real rates matter only for savers\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Borrowing decisions depend on the real rate, roughly the nominal rate minus inflation; at about −3%, debts shrink in real terms, so the stance is loose. Adding inflation, as in the third option, reverses the adjustment.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-02\"},{\"q\":\"Rates are close to zero and firms expect sales to fall. The central bank cuts its rate a little further. What is the most likely outcome?\",\"opts\":[\"Investment rises strongly because borrowing is cheaper\",\"The price level falls because AS shifts right\",\"AD rises only slightly, because weak confidence limits borrowing\",\"Real GDP falls because the cut is contractionary\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Investment depends on expected returns as well as the cost of borrowing, so when firms expect weak demand a small cut may not make projects worthwhile. The first option treats transmission as mechanical and ignores expectations.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-13\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): explain, using an AD/AS diagram, how a stated interest-rate change would affect real GDP or the price level\",\"Paper 2: calculate a real interest rate from data and comment on its sign\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate the effectiveness of monetary policy in closing a deflationary gap or reducing inflation\",\"HL only: explain money creation, a named tool or the money market diagram; in Paper 3, recommend a policy-rate decision (guide PDF pp. 47, 68)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"calculate\",\"draw\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"to what extent\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"AD/AS with AD shifting right (expansionary) or left (contractionary): label the price level and real GDP axes, AD₁ and AD₂, the AS curve, both equilibria and potential output (guide PDF p. 48). HL only: the money market with a vertical money supply curve, downward-sloping money demand and the equilibrium interest rate (guide PDF p. 47).\",\"calc\":\"Real interest rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation rate, for SL and HL (guide PDF p. 48). Show the working and keep the sign when the result is negative. The guide lists no money-multiplier calculation.\",\"apply\":\"Name the central bank and its target, quote the rate change from the text, and identify the gap the policy targets and the channel that matters most in that economy.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh the size of the output gap, confidence, the lower bound, the impact lag, pass-through, the exchange-rate effect, and the gains and losses of borrowers, savers, exporters and asset holders over time.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating the policy rate as the rate households pay and skipping transmission steps\",\"Shifting AS rather than AD for an interest-rate change\",\"Adding inflation to the nominal rate, or dropping a negative sign\",\"Asserting that a rate cut raises investment regardless of confidence or the output gap\"],\"strong\":[\"Write the transmission chain in explicit steps, including the exchange-rate channel\",\"Condition the outcome on where the economy sits on the AS curve\",\"Use the real interest rate to judge the stance of policy\",\"Draw evaluation from the context rather than a generic list\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain how an increase in the central bank’s policy interest rate may reduce demand-pull inflation.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the effectiveness of monetary policy in closing a deflationary gap when interest rates are already low.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the policy rate is 0.5%, inflation is 0.2% and falling, unemployment has risen from 5% to 8% in a year, and business confidence is at a ten-year low. (i) Calculate the real interest rate. (ii) Recommend a monetary policy measure to raise aggregate demand.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"Every monetary-policy effect is shown on AD/AS, because interest rates are a determinant of C and I.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Inflation targeting and the conflict between low unemployment and low inflation frame what the central bank balances.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Fiscal policy is the other demand-side tool, and near the lower bound the two are often used together.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Interest-rate changes move capital flows and the exchange rate, one of the transmission channels.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Because fiscal policy is the other demand-management tool, and its strengths are clearest where monetary policy is weakest, such as near the lower bound.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on a central bank changing, or holding, its policy rate, ideally one quoting inflation and growth figures. Intervention or Change could frame it; note which stakeholders the article says will feel the effect first.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did changes in a chosen central bank’s policy rate pass through to banks’ lending rates over a defined period, using published central-bank data?\",\"tok\":\"If economists cannot observe what would have happened without a rate cut, how can they know whether monetary policy worked?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can trace a rate change through three transmission channels to AD and evaluate one condition that weakens it.\",\"starter\":\"Show two invented reactions to the same rate rise — a homeowner’s and a pensioner’s — and ask who is right.\",\"activity\":\"Central-bank simulation: groups receive illustrative data on inflation, the output gap and confidence, choose a rate decision and defend it with a transmission chain and diagram; the outcome is revealed two rounds later.\",\"discussion\":\"Should independent central banks, rather than elected governments, set interest rates, given their distributional effects?\",\"check\":\"Calculate the real rate for three illustrative pairs of nominal rate and inflation and say which is most expansionary.\",\"extension\":\"HL: draw the money market for an open market purchase, then explain why money demand may flatten near zero rates.\",\"homework\":\"Write a five-step transmission chain for a rate rise, then one evaluation point on lags and one on confidence.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define monetary policy and state its goals, including inflation targeting.\",\"I can explain why the policy rate influences but does not set the rates households pay.\",\"I can calculate a real interest rate and interpret a negative result.\",\"I can draw AD/AS diagrams for expansionary and contractionary monetary policy.\",\"HL: I can explain money creation, the four tools and the money market diagram.\",\"I can evaluate monetary policy using the lower bound, confidence, time lags and distribution.\",\"I can connect monetary policy to exchange rates and fiscal policy.\",\"I can explain the transmission mechanism without notes.\"]},\"3.6\":{\"code\":\"3.6\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Fiscal policy is the government’s use of taxation and its own spending to influence aggregate demand and the wider economy. Spending more or taxing less adds to demand; the reverse removes it. Because one person’s spending is another’s income, a change in government spending can ripple through the economy. How far it ripples, and what the borrowing costs later, is where economists disagree.\",\"why\":\"The budget decides how much of national income passes through the state, who pays and who receives. It is the main demand-side tool when interest rates cannot fall much further, and the main tool for redistribution.\",\"q\":\"Should a government borrow to spend its way out of a recession, or will the debt cost more than the recession?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The circular flow of income: injections and withdrawals\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"The components of AD, and why G is one of them\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"The Keynesian and monetarist/new classical views of aggregate supply\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"Progressive, regressive and proportional taxes; direct and indirect taxes\",\"sub\":\"3.4\"},{\"t\":\"How monetary policy works, and its limits near zero interest rates\",\"sub\":\"3.5\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"List the sources of government revenue and the three types of government expenditure\",\"State the goals of fiscal policy, including an equitable distribution of income\"],\"understand\":[\"Distinguish a budget deficit (a flow) from government debt (a stock)\",\"Distinguish discretionary fiscal policy from automatic stabilisers (HL only — guide PDF p. 49)\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a measure in a news report as expansionary or contractionary, and as current, capital or transfer spending or a tax change\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse fiscal policy on AD/AS diagrams for both the Keynesian and the monetarist/new classical schools\",\"Analyse how government borrowing can crowd out private investment (HL only — guide PDF p. 49)\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate fiscal policy using political pressure, time lags, sustainable debt and, at HL, crowding out\",\"Weigh the strengths of fiscal policy — sector targeting and effectiveness in a deep recession — against its constraints\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate the Keynesian multiplier and the change in real GDP from a change in an injection (HL only — guide PDF pp. 48–49)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw expansionary and contractionary fiscal policy on AD/AS for both schools of thought (guide PDF p. 48)\",\"Draw a diagram showing crowding out (HL only — guide PDF p. 49)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret budget data to judge the fiscal stance, allowing at HL for the effect of the business cycle on revenue and spending (automatic stabilisers)\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a fiscal measure to close an output gap, sizing it with the multiplier and weighing debt sustainability (HL Paper 3 — guide PDF p. 68)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Where the money comes from and where it goes\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Governments raise revenue from direct taxes on income, profits and wealth; indirect taxes on spending, such as VAT or GST and excise duties; sales of goods and services by state-owned enterprises; and sales of government assets (guide PDF p. 48). Spending falls into three groups. Current expenditure covers day-to-day costs. Capital expenditure buys long-lived assets such as roads and schools. Transfer payments, such as pensions and unemployment benefits, move income to recipients without buying any output in return, which is why they are not part of G in the AD equation: they affect AD only when recipients spend them.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Category\",\"Examples\",\"Effect on AD\"],\"rows\":[[\"Current expenditure\",\"Public-sector wages, medicines, school supplies\",\"Direct: part of G\"],[\"Capital expenditure\",\"Roads, rail, hospitals, broadband\",\"Direct: part of G; may also raise LRAS\"],[\"Transfer payments\",\"Pensions, unemployment and child benefits\",\"Indirect: raise disposable income, then C\"],[\"Direct taxes\",\"Income tax, corporate tax, wealth taxes\",\"Higher rates reduce disposable income or profits\"],[\"Indirect taxes\",\"VAT/GST, excise duties\",\"Higher rates raise prices and cut real spending power\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Goals and budget outcomes\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The guide lists six goals for fiscal policy: low and stable inflation, low unemployment, a stable environment for long-term growth, smaller business-cycle fluctuations, an equitable distribution of income and external balance (guide PDF p. 48). When spending exceeds revenue in a year the budget is in deficit; when revenue exceeds spending it is in surplus. A deficit is a flow financed by borrowing. Government debt is the stock of past borrowing still owed, measured as a percentage of GDP in the HL content on sustainable debt (guide PDF p. 44). A smaller deficit still adds to the stock of debt.\"},{\"h\":\"Expansionary and contractionary fiscal policy\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Expansionary fiscal policy raises government spending or cuts taxes to shift AD right, targeting a deflationary gap. Contractionary fiscal policy cuts spending or raises taxes to shift AD left, targeting an inflationary gap (guide PDF p. 48). The route differs by instrument. A rise in G adds to AD directly, while a tax cut works only if households and firms spend what they keep. In the simple Keynesian model, a rise in G therefore tends to have a larger first-round effect than a tax cut of the same size.\",\"because\":[\"The government raises spending on road repairs\",\"construction firms receive orders and hire workers\",\"G rises, so AD shifts right\",\"with spare capacity, real GDP rises with little pressure on the price level\",\"the deficit widens unless tax revenue rises enough to cover it\"]},{\"h\":\"Two schools, two diagrams\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for fiscal policy on both frameworks (guide PDF p. 48). On the Keynesian AS curve, an economy deep in recession sits on the flat section, so a rightward AD shift raises real GDP with little change in the price level. Near full employment, the same shift mostly raises prices. In the monetarist/new classical model, higher AD raises output along SRAS in the short run, but rising wages and costs shift SRAS left until output returns to LRAS at a higher price level. The disagreement is about how quickly the economy corrects itself.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Keynesian view\",\"Monetarist/new classical view\"],\"rows\":[[\"Where the economy can settle\",\"Below full employment for long periods\",\"At potential output in the long run\"],[\"Higher G in a deep recession\",\"Large rise in real GDP, little inflation\",\"Short-run rise in output; wage adjustment would restore full employment anyway\"],[\"Long-run effect\",\"Can prevent a lasting gap\",\"Mainly a higher price level\"],[\"Policy each tends to favour\",\"Active demand management\",\"Rules, low deficits and supply-side reform\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The Keynesian multiplier (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"New spending becomes someone’s income, part of which is spent again. The Keynesian multiplier (k) is the final change in real GDP per unit of the initial change in an injection (HL only — guide PDF pp. 48–49): k = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) or k = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM). The two agree when MPC is the share of extra income spent on domestic output, so MPC + MPS + MPT + MPM = 1. Illustrative data: MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.2 and MPM = 0.2 give k = 1 ÷ 0.5 = 2, so a $5bn rise in G can raise real GDP by up to $10bn. Apply k to the change in the injection, not to GDP.\"},{\"h\":\"Automatic stabilisers versus discretionary policy (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Discretionary fiscal policy is a deliberate decision to change taxes or spending. Automatic stabilisers are features of the tax and benefit system that change the budget without a new decision (HL only — guide PDF p. 49). In a downturn, incomes fall, so progressive income tax takes a smaller share and more people claim unemployment benefits; disposable income falls by less than GDP, cushioning AD. In a boom the process reverses. Stabilisers avoid the recognition and decision lags, but they only soften fluctuations and cannot close a large gap alone.\"},{\"h\":\"Strengths and constraints\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide lists two strengths (guide PDF p. 49). Spending can target particular sectors or regions, such as construction in a depressed area. And government spending can work in a deep recession, when weak confidence and near-zero rates blunt monetary policy, because G is itself demand. The constraints are political pressure (tax rises and spending cuts are unpopular, so contraction is often delayed), time lags (recognising the problem, passing the measure, starting the spending), sustainable debt (higher debt raises interest costs and may unsettle lenders) and, for HL, crowding out.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Extra infrastructure spending in a recession\",\"cause\":\"Real GDP is well below potential and interest rates are already low, so the government borrows to fund road and rail repairs.\",\"mechanism\":\"G rises directly, and the income paid to contractors and workers is partly spent again; at HL, the simple model predicts a total rise in real GDP of up to k × ΔG.\",\"immediate\":\"Once projects start, construction firms hire and buy materials, and the budget deficit widens.\",\"secondary\":\"Households with new income spend some of it, so consumption rises. As output recovers, tax revenue rises and benefit claims fall, offsetting part of the deficit.\",\"longrun\":\"Useful infrastructure can raise productivity and shift LRAS right, a supply-side effect of fiscal policy. The extra debt must be serviced, and its burden depends on interest rates relative to growth.\",\"depends\":\"The size of the output gap, the size of the leakages, how quickly projects can start, how well they are chosen, and the interest rate on government debt.\"},{\"t\":\"Tax rises to close an inflationary gap\",\"cause\":\"Demand is running ahead of capacity and inflation is above target, so the government raises income tax and freezes some spending.\",\"mechanism\":\"Higher taxes cut disposable income and lower G removes demand directly, so AD shifts left; the multiplier works in reverse.\",\"immediate\":\"Households cut spending, especially on non-essentials, and the budget balance improves.\",\"secondary\":\"Firms face weaker demand and less room to raise prices, and employment growth slows.\",\"longrun\":\"Inflation eases and debt grows more slowly. If the freeze falls on productive spending such as education or maintenance, potential output may suffer.\",\"depends\":\"Political will (tax rises may be delayed), whether inflation is demand-pull or cost-push, how progressive the tax change is, and whether monetary policy is tightening at the same time.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Fiscal policy on AD/AS: Keynesian and monetarist/new classical\",\"dg\":\"lras\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how tax and spending changes shift AD, and why the two schools predict different effects on real GDP.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Changes in G and taxes shift AD by broadly predictable amounts\",\"Keynesian: wages and prices are sticky downward, so gaps can persist\",\"Monetarist/new classical: wages and prices adjust over time, returning output to LRAS\"],\"mechanism\":\"Expansionary policy shifts AD right and contractionary policy shifts it left; the outcome depends on the section of the Keynesian AS curve, or on the short- and long-run response in the SRAS/LRAS model.\",\"prediction\":\"Keynesian: in a deep recession, expansion raises real GDP substantially with little inflation. Monetarist/new classical: output rises only temporarily, and the lasting effect is a higher price level.\",\"application\":\"Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g) answers on closing a deflationary gap, where the contrast between the schools is itself evaluation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Neither diagram shows the lags in getting there\",\"Neither shows how the policy is financed or what happens to debt\",\"The size of the output gap is uncertain in real time\"],\"real\":\"Many governments used large fiscal packages in 2008–09 and in 2020, including China’s 2008 stimulus and the UK’s furlough scheme. Economists broadly agree these cushioned the downturns, but they still debate their size, their later effects on inflation and their long-run cost.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating the monetarist/new classical view as saying fiscal policy has no effect at all, when it predicts a short-run effect on output.\",\"exam\":\"Draw both diagrams when the question asks for them, and use the contrast to evaluate: the answer depends on the size of the gap and how quickly wages and prices adjust.\",\"deeper\":\"The two diagrams encode different beliefs about how fast markets clear. Much modern mainstream work combines them: prices are sticky in the short run, so demand policy affects output, while expectations and long-run supply decide where the economy settles. The live question is less “does fiscal policy work?” than “when, and by how much?”. Research since 2008 suggests effects on output may be larger in deep slumps and when interest rates are near zero, and smaller near capacity or when the central bank offsets the policy.\"},{\"model\":\"The Keynesian multiplier (HL only)\",\"dg\":\"multiplier\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why an injection can raise real GDP by more than its own size, and how to calculate the effect (HL only — guide PDF pp. 48–49).\",\"assumptions\":[\"There is spare capacity, so extra demand raises real output rather than prices\",\"The marginal propensities to save, tax and import are stable\",\"Interest rates and the price level do not change enough to offset the process\"],\"mechanism\":\"Each round of new income is partly withdrawn through saving, taxes and imports; the rest is spent on domestic output and becomes the next round’s income. The shrinking rounds sum to k × the initial change.\",\"prediction\":\"k = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM). With withdrawals of 0.25, k = 4; with 0.5, k = 2 (Illustrative data). Larger leakages mean a smaller multiplier.\",\"application\":\"Calculating the change in real GDP from a change in G, I or X in HL Paper 2 or Paper 3, and judging the size of package needed to close a gap.\",\"limitations\":[\"Near full employment, extra demand raises prices more than output\",\"Crowding out reduces the net effect\",\"Economies with high import shares and tax rates have small multipliers\"],\"real\":\"Estimates of real-world fiscal multipliers vary widely by country, period and type of spending. Research at the IMF after 2010 suggested that forecasters had underestimated multipliers while economies were depressed, so early fiscal tightening in some countries cut output more than expected.\",\"misconception\":\"Using 1 ÷ MPS when MPT and MPM are given, or multiplying the level of GDP instead of the change in the injection.\",\"exam\":\"HL: show the formula, the substitution and the answer with units, then interpret what the number means for the context.\",\"deeper\":\"The formula is the sum of a geometric series: ΔY = ΔG × (1 + c + c² + …) = ΔG ÷ (1 − c), where c is the share of each round spent on domestic output. It assumes nothing else moves. In practice the central bank may raise rates, prices may rise, and some households may save more if they expect future taxes to repay the debt — an argument called Ricardian equivalence that many economists regard as only partly true. The simple multiplier is best read as a mechanism under spare capacity, not a constant.\"},{\"model\":\"Crowding out (HL only)\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains why borrowing-financed government spending may raise AD by less than the multiplier suggests (HL only — guide PDF p. 49).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The government borrows in a domestic market for loanable funds\",\"The supply of loanable funds does not rise to match the extra borrowing\",\"The central bank does not hold interest rates down\"],\"mechanism\":\"Government borrowing shifts the demand for loanable funds right, raising the interest rate from r₁ to r₂; private borrowers facing higher rates cut investment and some consumption.\",\"prediction\":\"The net rise in AD is smaller than the rise in G. Near full employment the offset may be large; in a deep recession with idle saving it may be small.\",\"application\":\"Evaluating a fiscal expansion by stating the conditions under which crowding out binds.\",\"limitations\":[\"With spare capacity and low rates, the effect can be weak\",\"Foreign lending can fill the gap, though an appreciating currency may reduce net exports instead\",\"Public projects that raise private returns can crowd in private investment\"],\"real\":\"After 2008 several advanced economies ran large deficits while long-term interest rates stayed low, which is hard to square with strong crowding out at that time. Some economists argue it became more relevant as rates rose from 2022.\",\"misconception\":\"Asserting crowding out as automatic and complete, without saying what state of the economy would make it bind.\",\"exam\":\"HL: draw the diagram, commonly the market for loanable funds with the interest rate on the vertical axis, show demand shifting right, then link the higher rate to lower private investment.\",\"deeper\":\"Crowding out has several versions. The IB version works through interest rates. A stronger version, associated with new classical economists, says that at full employment each unit of public spending must displace a unit of private use of resources, whatever happens to rates. Keynesians reply that in a slump resources are idle, so there is little to crowd out. A further channel runs through the exchange rate: higher rates attract capital, the currency appreciates and net exports fall. Which dominates depends on the state of the economy.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"In the Keynesian model, higher government spending raises real GDP by a multiple of the injection when there is spare capacity. The monetarist/new classical model predicts that any output gain is temporary, with higher prices and, at HL, crowding out limiting the effect.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies generally find that fiscal multipliers vary: they appear larger in deep recessions and when interest rates are near zero, and smaller in open economies and near full capacity. Estimates are wide because policy is usually changed in response to the conditions that also drive output.\",\"interp\":\"Many economists read the evidence as support for a conditional view: fiscal policy is most useful when monetary policy is constrained. Others stress the long-run costs of debt and the risk that stimulus arrives late or is hard to reverse, and prefer rules and automatic stabilisers.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Why “time lags” is really three problems\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Economists split the lags in discretionary fiscal policy. The recognition lag is the time before policymakers see that a downturn has started, partly because GDP data arrive late and are revised. The decision lag is the time taken to design a measure and pass it through the legislature. The implementation or impact lag is the time before money flows and output responds: a new railway may take years to start. Tax and transfer changes usually act faster than capital projects, and automatic stabilisers avoid the first two lags altogether.\"},{\"h\":\"When does government debt become a problem?\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"The guide names sustainable debt as a constraint (guide PDF p. 49). There is no agreed threshold at which debt becomes dangerous. Relevant factors include the interest rate on the debt compared with the growth rate, whether it is owed in the country’s own currency, who holds it, and whether lenders trust the government’s plans. During the euro-area crisis Greece could not issue its own currency and faced sharply rising borrowing costs. The UK’s 2022 “mini-budget”, which announced large unfunded tax cuts, was followed by a sharp rise in government bond yields, showing that credibility can matter as much as the numbers.\"},{\"h\":\"Fiscal policy and equity\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"Of the two demand-side policies, only fiscal policy has an equitable distribution of income among its listed goals (guide PDF pp. 47–48). The same deficit can come from tax cuts for high earners or higher benefits for low-income households, with different effects on inequality and on AD, since lower-income households tend to spend more of any extra income. Contractionary policy has a distribution too: cuts to public services and benefits tend to weigh most on those who rely on them. Evaluation should ask who pays and who benefits, not only how far AD moves.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"The government spends money on schools, hospitals and roads, and collects money through taxes. If the economy is slowing, it can spend more or tax less, so people and firms have more to spend. If the economy is overheating, it can do the opposite. The money a government spends becomes people’s wages, and they spend some of it in turn, so the total effect can be larger than the first payment. But extra spending usually means borrowing, and debt has to be paid for later. Deciding how much to do, and when, involves both economics and politics.\",\"exam\":\"Define fiscal policy as the use of government spending and taxation to influence AD and other objectives. Identify the gap and the measure in the context. Draw AD/AS: for a deflationary gap, show AD shifting right on the Keynesian AS curve and, where both schools are needed, on SRAS/LRAS too, with labelled axes and equilibria. Explain the chain from the measure to C or G, then to AD, real GDP and the price level. HL: calculate the multiplier from the given propensities, apply it to the change in the injection and interpret the result. Evaluate with the size of the gap, time lags, debt, political pressure, the leakages that shrink the multiplier and, at HL, crowding out and automatic stabilisers.\",\"deep\":\"The case for fiscal policy is strongest when private spending has collapsed and interest rates are already near zero. Then the usual offsets are weak: the central bank has no reason to raise rates, idle saving limits crowding out, and unemployed workers spend much of any extra income. The case weakens near full employment, where extra demand mostly raises prices and interest rates, and where debt must be serviced at higher rates. Composition matters: capital spending that raises productivity can pay a supply-side return, while poorly chosen projects do not. Expectations matter too: households expecting higher future taxes may save more now, though evidence suggests this offset is usually partial. The same measure can work well in one setting and be largely wasted in another.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Fiscal policy is the most direct form of macroeconomic intervention: the government itself becomes a buyer of output.\",\"tension\":\"Stabilising the economy today can mean higher debt and interest costs for future taxpayers.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a stimulus package could be framed around whether the intervention is timely, targeted and temporary.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"Each person’s spending is another’s income, which is why the multiplier exists; imports link a country’s fiscal policy to its trading partners.\",\"tension\":\"In a very open economy, much of a stimulus leaks abroad through imports.\",\"ia\":\"An article about a coordinated stimulus could explore how interdependence changes the size of the effect at home.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"An equitable distribution of income is a listed goal, and progressive taxes and transfers are its main instruments.\",\"tension\":\"The quickest ways to cut a deficit, such as benefit cuts, may fall hardest on low-income households.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a budget could be analysed by asking which income groups bear the tax rises or spending cuts.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why did some countries that cut deficits quickly after 2010 grow more slowly than forecasters had expected?\",\"expect\":\"Cutting a deficit should reassure lenders and investors, so growth should hold up.\",\"model\":\"The Keynesian model with a multiplier predicts that cutting G or raising taxes in a depressed economy reduces AD by a multiple of the cut.\",\"evidence\":\"Research at the IMF and elsewhere suggested that forecasters had underestimated fiscal multipliers in this period, so output losses from consolidation were larger than expected.\",\"match\":\"Largely: the model predicted a contraction, and the evidence suggests it was bigger than assumed.\",\"assume\":\"Spare capacity, little room for interest rates to fall further, and no dominant confidence effect.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that confidence gains from deficit cuts outweighed the direct demand effect — the idea of “expansionary austerity” — would change the conclusion; studies of that idea have produced mixed results.\",\"policy\":\"Timing matters: many economists suggest consolidating when the economy is stronger and monetary policy can offset the tightening.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"HL only: explain, using the multiplier, how a rise in government spending can raise real GDP by more than the initial spending.\",\"check\":[\"States that government spending is an injection that becomes income for firms and workers\",\"Explains that part of each round is withdrawn through saving, taxation and imports\",\"Explains that the rest is spent on domestic output, creating further rounds of income\",\"Gives k = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM) and applies it to the change in G, not to GDP\",\"Adds a limit, such as crowding out, rising prices near capacity or a high import share\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why the Keynesian and monetarist/new classical schools disagree about the effect of expansionary fiscal policy on real GDP.\",\"check\":[\"Shows AD shifting right in both models\",\"Keynesian: with a large deflationary gap, output rises with little change in the price level\",\"Monetarist/new classical: output rises in the short run along SRAS\",\"Monetarist/new classical: wages and costs adjust, SRAS shifts left and output returns to LRAS at a higher price level\",\"Names the key difference: how quickly wages and prices adjust, and whether gaps persist\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Name the three types of government expenditure.\",\"a\":\"Current expenditure, capital expenditure and transfer payments.\"},{\"q\":\"What is the difference between a budget deficit and government debt?\",\"a\":\"A deficit is one year’s shortfall of revenue below spending (a flow); debt is the accumulated stock of past borrowing still owed.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: MPS = 0.15, MPT = 0.25, MPM = 0.1. Calculate the multiplier and the effect of a $4bn rise in G.\",\"a\":\"Withdrawals total 0.5, so k = 2 and real GDP rises by up to $8bn (Illustrative data).\"},{\"q\":\"On the Keynesian AS curve, where does expansionary fiscal policy raise output most and prices least?\",\"a\":\"On the flat section, where there is a large deflationary gap and spare capacity.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: give two examples of automatic stabilisers.\",\"a\":\"Progressive income taxes and unemployment benefits.\"},{\"q\":\"Why might fiscal policy work better than monetary policy in a deep recession?\",\"a\":\"Government spending adds to AD directly, whereas rate cuts need households and firms to borrow, which they may not do when confidence is weak and rates are near zero.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: MPC (on domestic output) = 0.6, MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.2, MPM = 0.1. Government spending rises by $10m. What is the maximum rise in real GDP?\",\"opts\":[\"$100m, using 1 ÷ MPS\",\"$25m, using 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM)\",\"about $16.7m, using 1 ÷ MPC\",\"$10m, because spending equals income\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"All three withdrawals leave the circular flow, so they sum to 0.4 and k = 2.5, giving $25m. Using 1 ÷ MPS ignores taxes and imports and overstates the effect fourfold.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-07\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a government’s budget deficit falls from 6% of GDP to 4% of GDP. What must be true?\",\"opts\":[\"Government debt is falling\",\"The government is still borrowing, so the stock of debt in money terms is still rising\",\"The budget is now in surplus\",\"Taxes have been raised\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A deficit of 4% of GDP is still new borrowing that adds to the stock of debt. Debt as a share of GDP could fall if GDP grows fast enough, but the first option confuses the flow with the stock.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-12\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: an economy is in a deep recession, with interest rates near zero and many idle resources. The government borrows to fund new spending. What is the most likely effect on private investment?\",\"opts\":[\"It falls one-for-one with the new government spending\",\"It is unlikely to fall much, and may rise as demand recovers\",\"It falls because the money supply falls\",\"It is unaffected because investment does not depend on interest rates\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Crowding out depends on conditions: with idle saving and a central bank holding rates low, extra borrowing puts little upward pressure on rates, and rising sales can encourage investment. Full, one-for-one crowding out is a limiting case more relevant near full employment.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-15\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain how expansionary or contractionary fiscal policy affects AD, real GDP and the price level, with a diagram\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate the effectiveness of fiscal policy in closing a deflationary gap in the context given\",\"HL only: calculate the multiplier and the change in real GDP from a change in an injection (guide PDF p. 49)\",\"Paper 3 (HL only): recommend a fiscal measure using data on the output gap, debt and the multiplier (guide PDF p. 68)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"calculate\",\"draw\",\"distinguish\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"to what extent\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"AD/AS showing expansionary and contractionary fiscal policy for both the Keynesian and the monetarist/new classical schools (guide PDF p. 48): label the price level, real GDP, AD₁ and AD₂, the AS curve(s), equilibria and potential output. HL only: a crowding-out diagram (guide PDF p. 49), commonly the loanable funds market with demand shifting right and the interest rate rising.\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF pp. 48–49): the Keynesian multiplier, 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) or 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM), and the change in real GDP from a change in investment, government spending or exports. Show the formula, substitution and units. No fiscal calculation is listed for SL.\",\"apply\":\"Quote the measure from the text (its size and type), identify the gap, and use the country’s debt level, interest rates and openness as evidence.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh the size of the gap, the school of thought, the multiplier, time lags, political pressure, debt sustainability, crowding out (HL) and effects on different income groups over time.\",\"errors\":[\"Treating transfer payments as a direct part of G\",\"HL: using 1 ÷ MPS when MPT and MPM are given, or applying the multiplier to the level of GDP\",\"Confusing the budget deficit with government debt\",\"HL: asserting crowding out without stating the conditions that make it bind\"],\"strong\":[\"Draw both schools when invited and use the contrast as evaluation\",\"Distinguish recognition, decision and implementation lags\",\"Consider how the measure is financed and who bears the cost\",\"Separate the demand-side effect from any supply-side effect of capital spending\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain how a cut in personal income tax could reduce a deflationary gap.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, discuss whether fiscal policy is more effective than monetary policy during a deep recession.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: real GDP is $400bn and potential output is $420bn; MPS = 0.2, MPT = 0.15, MPM = 0.15; government debt is 95% of GDP. (i) Calculate the rise in government spending needed to close the gap. (ii) Recommend whether the government should use a spending increase of this size to close the gap.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"G is a component of AD, and the shape of AS decides how fiscal policy splits between output and prices.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"HL students study how government debt is measured and what it costs, which sets the limits of fiscal expansion.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Progressive taxes and transfers are both fiscal instruments and tools for reducing inequality.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"why\":\"The import leakage in the multiplier links a fiscal expansion to the current account.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Because many supply-side policies — training, infrastructure, tax incentives — are delivered through the budget, and the guide asks for the supply-side effects of fiscal policy.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on a national budget, a stimulus package or a debate about government debt. Intervention or Equity could frame it by asking who gains and who pays; at HL, the size of a package invites a hedged multiplier comment.\",\"ee\":\"How far did a specific public infrastructure programme affect employment in the region where it was built? Local data may allow a before-and-after comparison, with care about other causes.\",\"tok\":\"Economists looking at the same fiscal data reach opposite conclusions about austerity. Is this a failure of evidence, or a sign that values shape which evidence counts?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can show expansionary and contractionary fiscal policy on AD/AS; HL students can also distinguish discretionary policy from automatic stabilisers and calculate and interpret the multiplier.\",\"starter\":\"Students sort ten budget items into current, capital and transfer spending and revenue sources.\",\"activity\":\"Multiplier chain: each student receives an illustrative sum, withdraws the stated shares for saving, tax and imports, and passes the rest on; the class totals the rounds and compares them with k × ΔG.\",\"discussion\":\"Should governments follow fixed fiscal rules, or keep discretion to respond to recessions?\",\"check\":\"HL: three quick multiplier calculations with different MPM values, then one sentence on why import-heavy economies have smaller multipliers.\",\"extension\":\"Compare Keynesian and new classical responses to a stimulus announced when the economy is close to capacity.\",\"homework\":\"Draw both AD/AS frameworks for the same fiscal expansion and write two evaluation points that depend on the output gap.\"},\"after\":[\"I can list sources of government revenue and types of expenditure.\",\"I can distinguish a budget deficit from government debt.\",\"I can draw fiscal policy on both the Keynesian and the monetarist/new classical diagrams.\",\"HL: I can calculate the multiplier and the resulting change in real GDP.\",\"HL: I can explain crowding out and when it is likely to bind.\",\"HL: I can distinguish automatic stabilisers from discretionary policy.\",\"I can evaluate fiscal policy using time lags, political pressure, debt and equity.\",\"HL: I can explain the multiplier process without notes.\"]},\"3.7\":{\"code\":\"3.7\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Supply-side policies try to raise how much an economy can produce, not how much people want to buy today. They work on the quantity and quality of labour and capital, on technology, and on how efficiently markets use them, shifting LRAS (or the Keynesian AS curve) to the right. Some rely on markets: more competition, fewer rules, sharper incentives. Others rely on government: education, health, research and infrastructure. Both take time, and both create losers as well as winners.\",\"why\":\"Long-run living standards depend on productivity, which demand-side policy cannot raise directly. Supply-side choices also decide who bears the cost of change, from workers in newly exposed industries to the taxpayers who fund infrastructure.\",\"q\":\"Can an economy grow faster without higher inflation, and who pays for making that possible?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"LRAS and the Keynesian AS curve, and what shifts them in the long run\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"Actual versus potential growth; structural unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"},{\"t\":\"Price floors, including a minimum wage\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Fiscal policy instruments and their effect on AD\",\"sub\":\"3.6\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the goals of supply-side policy listed in the guide\",\"Classify policies as market-based (competition, labour market, incentive-related) or interventionist (education, health care, R&D, infrastructure, industrial policy)\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why supply-side policies aim to shift LRAS or the Keynesian AS curve rather than SRAS\",\"Explain how one policy can have both a demand-side and a supply-side effect, and which usually comes first\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a policy in a news report by type and by the curve it is meant to shift\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse, using AD/AS with LRAS, how a named supply-side policy raises potential output and eases inflationary pressure\",\"Analyse the market-based case for abolishing a minimum wage using a labour-market diagram\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate market-based policies against equity, time lags, vested interests and environmental impact\",\"Evaluate interventionist policies against their budget cost, time lags and the risk of government failure\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw AD/AS with LRAS (or the Keynesian AS curve) shifting right because of a supply-side policy (guide PDF p. 49)\",\"Draw a minimum wage diagram showing excess supply of labour (guide PDF p. 49)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret productivity or labour-market data to judge whether a supply-side policy is having its intended effect\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a supply-side policy for a stated growth or unemployment problem, including its time lag and cost (HL Paper 3 — guide PDF p. 68)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"What supply-side policy is for\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Supply-side policies are government measures intended to raise the economy’s productive capacity by improving the quantity or quality of factors of production, or the efficiency with which markets use them. The guide lists five goals: long-term growth through higher productive capacity; more competition and efficiency; lower labour costs and unemployment through a more flexible labour market; lower inflation to improve international competitiveness; and stronger incentives for firms to invest in innovation by reducing their costs (guide PDF p. 49). Demand-side policy moves the economy within its existing capacity; supply-side policy tries to enlarge that capacity.\"},{\"h\":\"Market-based versus interventionist\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide sorts supply-side policies into two families (guide PDF pp. 49–50). Market-based policies try to make markets work better with less government involvement, by encouraging competition, making labour markets more flexible and sharpening incentives. Interventionist policies use government to provide or fund what markets underprovide, often because of positive externalities, as with education and research. The classification is by method, and most governments use both at once.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Market-based\",\"Interventionist\"],\"rows\":[[\"Examples in the guide\",\"Deregulation, privatisation, trade liberalisation, anti-monopoly regulation; reducing union power, cutting unemployment benefits, abolishing minimum wages; cutting income, business and capital gains taxes\",\"Education and training; health care; research and development; infrastructure; industrial policy\"],[\"Main mechanism\",\"Competition and incentives raise efficiency and effort\",\"Government raises the quantity and quality of labour, capital and technology\"],[\"Strengths listed\",\"Better resource allocation; no burden on the government budget\",\"Direct support for sectors important for growth\"],[\"Constraints listed\",\"Equity issues, time lags, vested interests, environmental impact\",\"Cost, time lags\"]]}},{\"h\":\"How the diagram shows it\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A successful supply-side policy shifts LRAS right in the monetarist/new classical model, or shifts the Keynesian AS curve right in the Keynesian model: potential output rises (guide PDF p. 49). If AD also grows, real GDP can rise with less inflationary pressure than if AD grew alone. The curve that defines a supply-side policy is LRAS, because its aim is to change the quantity or quality of resources, not only this year’s costs. A measure that also lowers firms’ costs, such as a labour-market reform aimed at lower labour costs, may shift SRAS right as well. A temporary cut in an indirect tax, by contrast, shifts SRAS and is not a supply-side policy in this sense.\",\"because\":[\"A training programme raises workers’ skills\",\"each worker can produce more per hour\",\"the economy’s productive capacity rises\",\"LRAS shifts right from LRAS₁ to LRAS₂\",\"with steady AD growth, real GDP rises with less pressure on the price level\"]},{\"h\":\"Market-based policies in practice\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Deregulation removes rules that raise costs or block entry. Privatisation transfers state firms to private owners who face a profit motive. Trade liberalisation exposes firms to foreign competition and cheaper inputs. Anti-monopoly regulation stops firms from abusing market power. Labour-market measures aim at flexibility: weaker union bargaining power, lower unemployment benefits to strengthen the incentive to take jobs, and abolishing minimum wages to cut labour costs. Incentive-related tax cuts aim to raise the reward for work, enterprise and investment. Each works through efficiency or incentives, and each creates losers.\"},{\"h\":\"Interventionist policies in practice\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Education and training raise human capital and can reduce structural unemployment by matching skills to vacancies. Better quality of, and access to, health care increases the number of people able to work and their productivity. Public support for research and development encourages innovation that firms underprovide because they cannot capture all its benefits. Infrastructure such as transport, energy and digital networks lowers costs for many firms at once. Industrial policy backs selected sectors with subsidies, tax breaks or public investment. These measures usually cost public money, and their pay-off may take years.\"},{\"h\":\"Two effects, two timescales\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for the demand-side effects of supply-side policies and the supply-side effects of fiscal policy (guide PDF p. 50). Many supply-side policies shift AD first: building a port raises G at once, while its effect on capacity arrives when it opens. Income-tax cuts raise consumption before any effect on work incentives, and cutting unemployment benefits can reduce consumption. In the other direction, fiscal spending on education, health or infrastructure can raise potential output. In an answer, separate the two effects and say which arrives first.\",\"because\":[\"The government builds a new port\",\"construction raises G, so AD shifts right in the short run\",\"once the port opens, transport costs fall for exporters\",\"productive capacity rises, so LRAS shifts right\",\"the size of the long-run gain depends on whether the port was well chosen\"]},{\"h\":\"Evaluating supply-side policy honestly\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"Supply-side policies can support growth without adding to inflation, but three cautions apply. Time: education and research may take years or decades to pay off, so they do little to close a current deflationary gap. Distribution: labour-market deregulation and tax cuts for higher earners tend to widen inequality, while education and health can widen opportunity. Cost and failure: interventionist measures are spending, and industrial policy can back the wrong firms or be captured by lobbies. The guide’s point that market-based policies place no burden on the budget fits deregulation well, but tax cuts reduce revenue, at least at first.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A national vocational training programme (interventionist)\",\"cause\":\"Firms report skill shortages while structural unemployment stays high among workers from declining industries, so the government funds new training places.\",\"mechanism\":\"Spending on colleges and trainers rises, and trainees gain skills that match vacancies.\",\"immediate\":\"G rises and AD shifts right slightly; the budget deficit widens unless other spending is cut.\",\"secondary\":\"As trainees finish, the mismatch between skills and jobs narrows, so structural unemployment falls and labour productivity rises.\",\"longrun\":\"Potential output rises and LRAS shifts right, allowing growth with less inflationary pressure. Higher incomes may return part of the cost through taxes.\",\"depends\":\"The quality and relevance of the training, employer involvement, course length, whether trainees stay in the country, and the opportunity cost of the public funds.\"},{\"t\":\"A cut in corporate tax to encourage investment (market-based)\",\"cause\":\"The government wants more business investment and cuts the corporate tax rate.\",\"mechanism\":\"The after-tax return on new projects rises, and firms keep more profit to reinvest.\",\"immediate\":\"Tax revenue falls. Some firms raise dividends or buy back shares rather than invest.\",\"secondary\":\"If investment rises, AD increases first through I, and the capital stock then grows. The deficit widens unless spending is cut or growth recovers the revenue.\",\"longrun\":\"A larger, more modern capital stock can shift LRAS right. If investment barely responds, the main lasting effects are lower revenue and higher post-tax profits, with equity implications.\",\"depends\":\"Whether weak investment was caused by tax or by weak demand, uncertainty or poor access to finance; other countries’ tax rates; how the revenue loss is covered; and who owns the firms.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Supply-side policy in the AD/AS model\",\"dg\":\"growth\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how raising productive capacity increases potential output and can reduce inflationary pressure (guide PDF p. 49).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The policy raises the quantity or quality of factors of production, or efficiency\",\"Potential output is set by supply-side factors, not by the price level\",\"Other influences on AD are unchanged, or AD grows steadily\"],\"mechanism\":\"LRAS shifts right from LRAS₁ to LRAS₂, or the vertical section of the Keynesian AS curve moves right. Full-employment output rises, and at a given AD the price level is lower than it would otherwise be.\",\"prediction\":\"Higher potential output and real GDP, lower inflationary pressure, possibly a lower natural rate of unemployment and better international competitiveness.\",\"application\":\"Explaining how a policy in a Paper 2 text could raise long-term growth, or contrasting demand-side and supply-side routes to growth in Paper 1 (b).\",\"limitations\":[\"It shows no time, yet the shift may take years\",\"It hides who gains and who loses\",\"It shows the intended effect; whether a policy achieves it is an empirical question\",\"It usually leaves out the demand-side effect that tends to arrive first\"],\"real\":\"South Korea’s industrial policy from the 1970s is often cited as a case where state support helped build heavy industries, while attempts elsewhere to pick winners proved costly. Economists still debate how much of Korea’s growth came from industrial policy rather than high saving, education and export competition.\",\"misconception\":\"Shifting SRAS for a capacity-raising policy, or showing a supply-side policy only as an AD shift.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 and Paper 2: draw LRAS (and AD where relevant) shifting and name the mechanism that moved the curve; in Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g), evaluate with time lags and cost.\",\"deeper\":\"Growth accounting splits output growth into contributions from more labour, more capital and a residual, total factor productivity, which captures technology and efficiency. Supply-side policies aim at all three, but the residual is the hardest to target and the least understood. Endogenous growth theory suggests that education and research can raise the growth rate, not just the level, of output, because knowledge spills over between firms. If so, interventionist policies may matter more than the diagram suggests; if not, their effect is a one-off shift.\"},{\"model\":\"The minimum wage in a competitive labour market\",\"dg\":\"labour\",\"purpose\":\"Shows why market-based policies include abolishing minimum wages, and what that argument assumes (guide PDF p. 49).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The labour market is competitive, with many employers and workers\",\"Labour demand slopes down because of diminishing marginal productivity\",\"The minimum wage is set above the equilibrium wage, so it binds\"],\"mechanism\":\"A binding minimum wage raises the quantity of labour supplied and reduces the quantity demanded; the gap is excess supply of labour, or unemployment. Abolishing it lets the wage fall towards equilibrium, cutting labour costs and the excess supply.\",\"prediction\":\"Lower labour costs, higher employment and lower unemployment in that market, but lower pay for workers who were on the minimum.\",\"application\":\"Explaining the market-based case for labour-market flexibility, then evaluating it with equity and evidence.\",\"limitations\":[\"Where employers have wage-setting power (monopsony), a minimum wage can raise employment\",\"Higher pay can raise effort and reduce staff turnover, offsetting the cost\",\"Lower wages can reduce consumption, a demand-side effect the diagram omits\"],\"real\":\"Card and Krueger’s study of fast-food employment in New Jersey and Pennsylvania in the early 1990s began a long debate. Many later studies of moderate minimum wage increases in high-income economies have found small employment effects, although methods and the effects of higher minimums remain disputed.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing the minimum wage below equilibrium, where it does not bind, or treating it as a maximum wage.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) or Paper 2 (c)–(f): draw the labour market with the wage and quantity of labour, the minimum wage above equilibrium and the excess supply labelled, and explain it. Evaluation (monopsony, equity) belongs in Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g), the only parts that assess AO3 (guide PDF pp. 66–67).\",\"deeper\":\"The competitive model predicts a clean trade-off: a higher minimum wage means fewer jobs. The monopsony model predicts that a modest minimum can raise both pay and employment, because a dominant employer had been holding wages and hiring below the competitive level. Which model fits depends on the labour market: a town with one large employer differs from a city with many. That is why economists hesitate to generalise and look at evidence for specific places, industries and levels of the minimum.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Supply-side policies shift LRAS or the Keynesian AS curve to the right, raising potential output and allowing growth without rising inflation. Market-based policies work through competition and incentives; interventionist policies through public investment in people, knowledge and infrastructure.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence is strongest that education, health and well-chosen infrastructure raise productivity over long periods. Evidence on tax cuts, deregulation and industrial policy is more mixed and depends on design and context; most estimates suggest broad tax cuts recover only part of their cost through faster growth.\",\"interp\":\"Economists who favour markets stress incentive effects and the risk of government failure; those who favour intervention stress market failures such as underinvestment in skills and research. Many would agree that design and institutions matter more than the label.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"The equity cost of labour-market flexibility\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"The guide lists equity issues as a constraint on market-based policies (guide PDF p. 50). Weakening unions, cutting benefits and removing minimum wages aim to lower labour costs, which by design means lower pay or weaker bargaining power for some workers. Income-tax cuts are usually worth most in cash terms to high earners. Such policies may raise employment, but they can also widen inequality and job insecurity, while education and health care tend to widen opportunity. A balanced evaluation asks who gains, who loses, and whether the losers are compensated.\"},{\"h\":\"Vested interests and the politics of reform\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"Supply-side reforms often concentrate costs on organised groups while spreading benefits thinly. Trade liberalisation threatens firms and workers in import-competing industries, and deregulation threatens incumbents protected by the old rules. These groups lobby, which is one reason reforms stall. The same logic creates a risk for interventionist policy: firms may lobby for subsidies presented as industrial policy, a form of rent-seeking. Making support conditional, time-limited and measured against results is one proposed safeguard.\"},{\"h\":\"Environmental impact\",\"tag\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"p\":\"The guide lists environmental impact as a constraint on market-based policies (guide PDF p. 50). Deregulation can weaken pollution controls, and faster growth can raise resource use. Yet some supply-side policies target the environment directly: public research into clean energy, or industrial policies such as the US Inflation Reduction Act of 2022, which used tax credits to encourage investment in low-carbon technologies. Whether such policies add to productive capacity or mainly redirect investment is still being evaluated.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine a bakery already baking as much bread as its ovens allow. If customers want more, prices will simply rise. To sell more without raising prices, the bakery needs another oven, better-trained bakers or a faster method. Supply-side policies are the economy’s version of that: they try to raise what the whole economy can produce. Some do it by letting markets work more freely, with more competition and stronger incentives. Others use government spending on schools, hospitals, research and roads. Most take years to work, and some people can be made worse off along the way.\",\"exam\":\"Define supply-side policy as a measure intended to raise productive capacity by improving the quantity or quality of factors of production or the efficiency of markets. Identify the policy in the context and classify it as market-based or interventionist. Draw AD/AS with LRAS (or the Keynesian AS) shifting right, labelling LRAS₁ and LRAS₂, the axes and the change in potential output. Explain the specific channel that moved the curve — skills, capital, competition or incentives — rather than writing “efficiency rises”. Separate any demand-side effect and say which arrives first. Evaluate with time lags, budget cost, equity, vested interests, environmental impact and whether the policy targets the real cause of weak growth.\",\"deep\":\"The supply side is where long-run growth happens, but it is also where economists’ tools are weakest. Productivity differs hugely between countries, yet explaining why involves institutions, management quality, the spread of technology and history as much as policy. That explains the range of views. Market-oriented economists stress that competition pushes firms to adopt better methods and that high taxes and heavy regulation blunt incentives. Interventionists point to positive externalities in education and research, to coordination problems that markets solve slowly, and to the role of the state in several successful catch-up economies. Because policies interact with institutions, evidence from one country is hard to generalise. The most defensible judgement is conditional: identify what is holding supply back, match the policy to it, and weigh the time horizon and who bears the cost.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Market-based policies aim to improve allocative and productive efficiency through competition and incentives.\",\"tension\":\"Efficiency gains can come with job losses and insecurity for workers in restructured industries.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a privatisation or deregulation could be framed around whether efficiency gains are likely to reach consumers.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Interventionist policies use government to provide what markets underprovide, such as training and research.\",\"tension\":\"Intervention can correct market failure but risks government failure, such as backing the wrong industry.\",\"ia\":\"An article on an industrial subsidy could weigh its market-failure rationale against the risk of rent-seeking.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"The guide names equity issues as a constraint on market-based supply-side policies.\",\"tension\":\"Measures that raise flexibility and incentives may widen inequality, while those that widen opportunity cost public money.\",\"ia\":\"An article on labour-market reform could be analysed by asking which workers gain and which lose.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do governments keep choosing supply-side policies when their effects take years to appear?\",\"expect\":\"Governments want quick results, so they should prefer demand-side policy.\",\"model\":\"AD/AS predicts that only an LRAS shift raises potential output, so sustained growth without rising inflation needs supply-side improvement.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests education, health and infrastructure can raise productivity over long horizons, while results for tax cuts and deregulation vary with design and context.\",\"match\":\"Partly: the logic is widely accepted, but the size and timing of real effects are uncertain and differ between policies.\",\"assume\":\"That the policy targets the actual constraint on growth and is well designed and implemented.\",\"change\":\"Clear evidence that a policy raised productivity in a comparable economy would strengthen the case; evidence that it mainly redistributed income would weaken it.\",\"policy\":\"Supply-side policy works best as part of a mix: demand management for the cycle, supply-side measures for the trend, with explicit attention to who bears the costs.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how an interventionist supply-side policy can raise potential output, and why its demand-side effect usually comes first.\",\"check\":[\"Names a specific interventionist policy, such as infrastructure or training\",\"Explains how it raises the quantity or quality of factors of production\",\"Shows LRAS (or the Keynesian AS) shifting right on a labelled diagram\",\"Explains that the spending raises G and shifts AD right before the capacity effect arrives\",\"States a time lag and the cost to the budget\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain the market-based argument for abolishing a minimum wage, and one reason it might not hold.\",\"check\":[\"Draws a competitive labour market with the minimum wage above equilibrium\",\"Identifies the excess supply of labour as unemployment\",\"Explains that abolition lowers labour costs and raises the quantity of labour demanded\",\"Notes the loss of income for low-paid workers who keep their jobs\",\"Gives a limit, such as monopsony or evidence of small employment effects\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"What is a supply-side policy?\",\"a\":\"A government measure intended to raise productive capacity by improving the quantity or quality of factors of production or the efficiency of markets.\"},{\"q\":\"Classify: privatisation, infrastructure spending, cutting unemployment benefits, public funding for R&D.\",\"a\":\"Market-based: privatisation and cutting unemployment benefits. Interventionist: infrastructure spending and R&D funding.\"},{\"q\":\"Which curve does a successful supply-side policy shift, and which way?\",\"a\":\"LRAS (or the vertical section of the Keynesian AS curve) shifts right, raising potential output.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one demand-side effect of an income-tax cut.\",\"a\":\"Higher disposable income raises consumption, shifting AD right, usually before any effect on work incentives.\"},{\"q\":\"Name two constraints on market-based supply-side policies listed in the guide.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: equity issues, time lags, vested interests, environmental impact.\"},{\"q\":\"Why might supply-side policy be a weak response to a sudden recession?\",\"a\":\"A recession is a shortfall in AD, and most supply-side policies raise capacity only after long lags, though their demand-side effects may help in the short run.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A government funds a large programme to train workers in digital skills. Which shift best shows the intended long-run effect?\",\"opts\":[\"AD shifts right only\",\"SRAS shifts left\",\"LRAS shifts right\",\"AD shifts left and LRAS shifts right\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Training raises the quality of labour and so productive capacity, a rightward shift of LRAS. The spending also shifts AD right in the short run, which makes the first option tempting, but that misses the intended long-run effect.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-17\"},{\"q\":\"Which of these is best classified as a supply-side policy?\",\"opts\":[\"A temporary VAT cut to boost spending during a recession\",\"A cut in the policy interest rate\",\"Public funding for research into battery technology\",\"A one-off cash payment to every household\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Supply-side policy is classified by its target, productive capacity, and research funding aims to raise technology and productivity. A temporary VAT cut is a tax cut, but its aim is to raise AD now, so it is demand-side.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-14\"},{\"q\":\"A government abolishes a minimum wage that was above the equilibrium wage in a competitive labour market. What does the competitive model predict?\",\"opts\":[\"Unemployment rises because wages fall\",\"The excess supply of labour shrinks, but low-paid workers who keep their jobs earn less\",\"A labour shortage appears\",\"Nothing changes, because the minimum wage did not bind\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A binding minimum wage creates excess supply of labour; removing it lets the wage fall, raising the quantity demanded and shrinking the excess supply, at an equity cost to low-paid workers. The third option reverses the logic of a price floor.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-12\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain how a named market-based or interventionist policy can shift LRAS, with a diagram\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): discuss whether supply-side policies are more effective than demand-side policies in achieving long-term growth\",\"Paper 2: explain the demand-side and supply-side effects of a policy described in the text\",\"Paper 3 (HL only): recommend a supply-side measure for a stated problem, with its time lag and cost (guide PDF p. 68)\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"compare and contrast\",\"to what extent\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"AD/AS with LRAS (or the Keynesian AS curve) shifting right, labelling LRAS₁ and LRAS₂, AD, the price level, real GDP and the change in potential output (guide PDF p. 49). A minimum wage diagram: the labour market with the minimum wage above equilibrium and the excess supply of labour labelled (guide PDF p. 49).\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Name the specific policy in the text, classify it, identify the constraint on growth it targets, and use the country’s circumstances — skills, infrastructure, market structure — as evidence.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh time lags, budget cost, equity effects on named groups, vested interests, environmental impact and the risk of government failure, separating short-run demand effects from long-run supply effects.\",\"errors\":[\"Shifting SRAS instead of LRAS for a capacity-raising policy\",\"Classifying every tax cut as supply-side regardless of its purpose\",\"Presenting interventionist policies as free to the budget\",\"Ignoring the equity consequences of labour-market reforms\"],\"strong\":[\"Explain the specific channel — skills, capital, competition or incentives — that moves LRAS\",\"Separate the demand-side effect from the supply-side effect and say which comes first\",\"Match the policy to the actual cause of weak growth in the context\",\"Reach a conditional judgement weighing time horizon and distribution\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain two market-based supply-side policies that could increase an economy’s potential output.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that interventionist supply-side policies are more effective than market-based policies in raising long-term growth.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: youth unemployment is 18%, firms report that 40% of vacancies are hard to fill because of skill gaps, and government debt is 60% of GDP. Recommend a supply-side policy to reduce unemployment in this economy.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"why\":\"The determinants of LRAS and the Keynesian AS curve are exactly what supply-side policies target.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Structural unemployment and long-term growth are the objectives supply-side policy addresses most directly.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"A minimum wage is a price floor in the labour market, analysed with the same tools.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"why\":\"Trade liberalisation rests on the gains from trade and specialisation.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Many growth and development strategies, from education to industrial policy, are supply-side policies applied to economies at different stages.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"why\":\"Because trade liberalisation, one of the market-based supply-side policies, depends on the gains from international trade, where Unit 4 begins.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on a training scheme, a privatisation, a change to labour law or an industrial subsidy. Efficiency or Equity could frame it: does the measure raise capacity, and who bears the cost?\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has a named industrial policy, such as India’s Production-Linked Incentive scheme in one sector, raised that sector’s output or exports? Published production and trade data could be compared before and after, with care about other causes.\",\"tok\":\"Supply-side policies may take a decade to work. How can economists know that a policy caused later growth when so much else changed in the meantime?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can classify supply-side policies, show them on LRAS, and separate their demand-side and supply-side effects.\",\"starter\":\"Card sort: twelve policies into demand-side, market-based supply-side and interventionist supply-side, with a column for “could be both”.\",\"activity\":\"Structured debate on market-based versus interventionist policies for a fictional economy with illustrative data on skills, infrastructure and regulation; each side must name a time lag and an equity effect.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a government cut unemployment benefits to strengthen work incentives if doing so widens inequality?\",\"check\":\"Students draw LRAS shifting for one policy and annotate the mechanism in one sentence and the time lag in another.\",\"extension\":\"Compare the competitive and monopsony models of the minimum wage and predict the effect of abolition in each.\",\"homework\":\"Choose a policy from the news, classify it, draw the diagram and write a paragraph separating its demand-side and supply-side effects.\"},\"after\":[\"I can state the goals of supply-side policy.\",\"I can classify policies as market-based or interventionist, with examples of each.\",\"I can draw LRAS or the Keynesian AS shifting right and explain the mechanism.\",\"I can draw and explain the minimum wage diagram and its limits.\",\"I can separate the demand-side and supply-side effects of a policy and say which comes first.\",\"I can evaluate supply-side policies using time lags, cost, equity, vested interests and environmental impact.\",\"I can compare supply-side and demand-side routes to growth.\",\"I can explain without notes why supply-side policy shifts LRAS.\"]},\"4.1\":{\"code\":\"4.1\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Countries trade because buying some goods abroad costs less than making them at home, and selling others abroad earns more. Trade widens choice, sharpens competition and opens bigger markets. At HL, comparative advantage shows that two countries can both gain by specialising, even if one is more productive at everything.\",\"why\":\"Trade shapes prices and choice for households, costs and markets for firms, and jobs in whole regions. Who gains, and who pays, sits at the centre of every trade policy argument.\",\"q\":\"Why would a country that could make almost everything itself still choose to import?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Opportunity cost as the next best alternative forgone\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Linear and bowed PPCs: constant versus increasing opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Consumer and producer surplus on a demand and supply diagram\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Areas of rectangles on a diagram (price × quantity)\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"List the nine benefits of trade in the syllabus\",\"Define absolute and comparative advantage (HL only)\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a world price below the domestic price leads to imports, and one above it to exports\",\"Explain why comparative advantage rests on opportunity cost, not output (HL only)\"],\"apply\":[\"Select and justify the benefits of trade most relevant to a named economy\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how opening a market to imports changes output, consumption and surplus\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate comparative advantage theory against its assumptions (HL only)\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate opportunity cost ratios to identify comparative advantage (HL only)\",\"Calculate imports, exports, import expenditure and export revenue from a diagram (HL only)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw free trade diagrams showing imports and exports at a world price\",\"Draw two linear PPCs on one diagram showing the gains from specialisation (HL only)\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret the slope of a linear PPC as an opportunity cost (HL only)\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Why countries trade\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"Trade is the exchange of goods and services across borders. Voluntary trade is positive-sum: each side expects to gain. Imports bring goods that are cheaper, better or unavailable at home; exports pay for them. The guide lists nine benefits (guide PDF p. 51). Learn them by mechanism and by who gains, and treat each as conditional on real competition and on resources moving into new uses.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Benefit\",\"Mechanism\",\"Main gainers\"],\"rows\":[[\"Increased competition\",\"Foreign rivals press firms to cut costs\",\"Consumers\"],[\"Lower prices\",\"Lower-cost producers supply the market\",\"Consumers; input buyers\"],[\"Greater choice\",\"Imports add new varieties\",\"Consumers\"],[\"Acquisition of resources\",\"Access to inputs a country lacks\",\"Producers\"],[\"More foreign exchange earnings\",\"Exports earn currency to pay for imports\",\"The economy\"],[\"Access to larger markets\",\"Exporters reach more buyers\",\"Exporters; their workers\"],[\"Economies of scale\",\"Higher output lowers average cost\",\"Exporters; consumers\"],[\"More efficient resource allocation\",\"Resources move to low opportunity cost goods\",\"The economy overall\"],[\"More efficient production\",\"Competition and technology raise productivity\",\"Firms; consumers\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Free trade at a world price\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A small economy is a price taker: it can trade any quantity at the world price (Pw), so the world price is drawn as a horizontal line at Pw (world supply when the country imports, world demand when it exports). If Pw is below the no-trade price, domestic firms supply less, consumers buy more, and imports fill the gap. If Pw is above it, the excess of domestic output over domestic demand is exported. Calculating these quantities, import expenditure and export revenue from a diagram is HL only (guide PDF p. 51).\",\"because\":[\"Pw is below the domestic equilibrium price\",\"so domestic output falls to Qs and quantity demanded rises to Qd\",\"so imports equal Qd − Qs, not Qd\",\"and import expenditure equals Pw × (Qd − Qs)\"]},{\"h\":\"Absolute and comparative advantage (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Absolute advantage means producing more of a good than another country with the same resources. Comparative advantage means producing it at a lower opportunity cost. The opportunity cost of one unit of a good is the other good’s output forgone divided by that good’s output. In the illustrative data, Alpha has an absolute advantage in both goods, yet Beta has the comparative advantage in textiles. With hours-per-unit data, flip the ratio. The concepts and this calculation are HL only (guide PDF p. 51).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Illustrative data\",\"Food (tonnes)\",\"Textiles (bales)\",\"Cost of 1 food\",\"Cost of 1 textile\"],\"rows\":[[\"Alpha\",\"60\",\"30\",\"30 ÷ 60 = 0.5 textiles\",\"60 ÷ 30 = 2 food\"],[\"Beta\",\"20\",\"20\",\"20 ÷ 20 = 1 textile\",\"20 ÷ 20 = 1 food\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why specialisation creates gains (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Before trade, suppose Alpha makes 48 food and 6 textiles, and Beta 12 food and 8 textiles: 60 food and 14 textiles in all. Fully specialised, Alpha makes 60 food and Beta 20 textiles, so six extra bales come from the same resources. Trading at a ratio between the two opportunity costs, say 0.75 textiles per unit of food, Alpha swaps 12 food for 9 textiles. Alpha then consumes 48 food and 9 textiles, Beta 12 food and 11 textiles: each gains 3 textiles.\",\"because\":[\"Each country moves resources to its lower opportunity cost good\",\"so combined output rises\",\"trade takes place at a ratio between the two costs\",\"so each country consumes outside its own PPC\"]},{\"h\":\"Sources and limits of comparative advantage (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"Comparative advantage comes from differences in natural resources, climate, skills, capital, technology and institutions. Some are built through investment, so it can shift. The theory’s limitations are HL only (guide PDF p. 52). It assumes constant costs, freely mobile resources, no transport costs or barriers, and full employment. In practice, displaced workers may stay unemployed, and heavy specialisation exposes an economy to swings in a few export prices. The model shows a potential national gain, not a gain for everyone.\"},{\"h\":\"Where the WTO fits\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The World Trade Organization (WTO), which succeeded the GATT in 1995, administers agreed trade rules, hosts negotiations to reduce barriers and settles disputes between members, aiming to secure the kind of gains described here. The guide teaches its objectives and functions in 4.4, together with limits on its influence, such as the difficulty of agreeing on services and primary products and members’ unequal bargaining power (guide PDF pp. 53–54).\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A small economy opens its clothing market to imports\",\"cause\":\"A tariff is removed, so buyers can pay the lower world price.\",\"mechanism\":\"Price falls to Pw; quantity demanded rises, domestic output falls and imports fill the gap.\",\"immediate\":\"Consumers gain; domestic clothing firms lose sales and cut jobs.\",\"secondary\":\"Retailers and firms that buy clothing, such as work uniforms, face lower costs; displaced workers and capital look for new uses.\",\"longrun\":\"If resources are re-employed where the economy’s costs are relatively low, output and welfare rise; if they stay idle, part of the gain is lost.\",\"depends\":\"Factor mobility, the elasticity of domestic supply, the size of the price gap, and whether other sectors are hiring.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Free trade at a world price\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Shows how opening a market to trade changes price, output, consumption and trade flows.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Small country: the world price is given\",\"Domestic and foreign goods are identical\",\"No transport costs or barriers\"],\"mechanism\":\"The domestic price moves to Pw. Below the no-trade price, imports fill the gap Qd − Qs; above it, the excess Qs − Qd is exported.\",\"prediction\":\"Importing: consumers gain more surplus than domestic producers lose. Exporting: producers gain more than consumers lose.\",\"application\":\"Explaining why cheap imports hurt some firms but help buyers, and why an export boom can raise a good’s domestic price.\",\"limitations\":[\"Large economies can move the world price\",\"One market only: it ignores jobs elsewhere and the exchange rate\",\"The net gain hides redistribution between groups\"],\"real\":\"China’s WTO entry in 2001 was followed by rapid growth in its manufactured exports. Consumers abroad generally gained, while research on the “China shock” suggests some US manufacturing regions suffered lasting job losses.\",\"misconception\":\"Reading imports as all of Qd at the world price, rather than Qd − Qs.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 and Paper 2 answers on the benefits of trade; at HL, Paper 2 or Paper 3 may ask for imports, import expenditure or export revenue from it.\",\"deeper\":\"The diagram is partial equilibrium: it studies one market with everything else held constant. Much of the gain depends on resources released by the shrinking industry being re-employed elsewhere. If they stay idle, the consumer gain may be offset by lost income, which is why economists who agree on the diagram can still disagree about a trade shock’s effect on a particular region.\"},{\"model\":\"Comparative advantage with linear PPCs (HL only)\",\"dg\":\"compadv\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why two countries can both gain from trade even if one is more productive in every good.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Two countries, two goods, constant opportunity costs\",\"Resources move between industries but not between countries\",\"No transport costs, barriers or unemployment\"],\"mechanism\":\"Each linear PPC’s slope is an opportunity cost. Each country specialises where its cost is lower and trades at a ratio between the two slopes.\",\"prediction\":\"World output rises and both countries consume beyond their own PPCs.\",\"application\":\"Explaining patterns of specialisation, and answering the claim that a less productive country cannot gain from trade.\",\"limitations\":[\"Rising costs make full specialisation unlikely\",\"Workers may be unable to switch industries or regions\",\"Transport costs can outweigh small cost differences\",\"Silent on how gains are shared and on dependence risks\"],\"real\":\"Bangladesh’s large garment export sector is often cited as consistent with comparative advantage in labour-intensive goods, though policy and preferential access to some markets also mattered.\",\"misconception\":\"Assigning specialisation to whichever country produces more, rather than by opportunity cost.\",\"exam\":\"HL only: draw both PPCs on one diagram with labelled intercepts, calculate opportunity costs from data, and use the limitations when evaluating.\",\"deeper\":\"Ricardo traced comparative advantage to technology; the Heckscher–Ohlin approach traces it to factor endowments, with countries exporting goods that use their abundant factor intensively. That approach suggests trade can lower the real income of a country’s scarce factor, creating organised losers. Much trade, though, is between similar rich economies exchanging similar goods, which newer theories explain through scale economies and demand for variety.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The free trade diagram predicts lower prices and more consumption where imports enter; at HL, comparative advantage predicts consumption beyond each country’s PPC.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of trade liberalisation generally find lower prices and productivity gains, but also concentrated, lasting adjustment costs where import competition arrives suddenly.\",\"interp\":\"Most economists accept the aggregate gains. They disagree about the size of adjustment costs, and whether to respond with slower opening or better support for those who lose.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Gains for the country, sharing by policy\",\"tag\":\"Distribution\",\"p\":\"When a market opens to imports, consumers gain more than domestic producers lose, so the winners could in principle compensate the losers. The trade model does not decide whether they do. That depends on taxes and transfers, retraining, and whether new jobs appear where old ones disappeared. Where compensation is weak, a change that raises national income can lower the living standards of identifiable groups for years, which helps explain why support for trade varies between countries.\"},{\"h\":\"Comparative advantage can change (HL only)\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"The model is static, but opportunity costs change over decades. Several East Asian economies moved from exporting textiles to steel, ships and electronics as capital and skills accumulated. Economists disagree about why: some stress education, high saving and export competition; others credit selective state support. With increasing opportunity costs, specialisation is also partial, which is closer to what we observe. Today’s pattern of specialisation is not a permanent verdict on what a country can do.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Two friends are cooking for a party. One is faster at both chopping and baking, but far faster at baking. If her friend takes over most of the chopping and she concentrates on baking, together they can prepare more in the same time than if each does half of every job. Countries are similar. Trade lets them buy what would cost a lot to make, measured by what they would give up, and sell what they make at a small sacrifice. Buyers get lower prices and more choice, but firms and workers competing with imports may lose out, at least for a while. (At HL, the friends’ logic is called comparative advantage.)\",\"exam\":\"Define terms precisely; at HL, comparative advantage means producing a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country. Draw the free trade diagram with a horizontal world supply line at Pw below the domestic equilibrium, and label Qs, Qd and the imports gap. Explain the chain: lower price → more demanded and less produced at home → imports → consumer surplus rises and producer surplus falls. Apply only the benefits that fit the context, such as access to resources for an economy without energy reserves. At HL, evaluate by weighing the gains against the model’s assumptions (factor immobility, transport costs, constant costs) and the unequal sharing of gains.\",\"deep\":\"The gain from trade is the gap between what a country sacrifices to make a good and what it sacrifices to buy it. Exporting is an indirect way of producing imports: make goods where the sacrifice is small and swap them for goods where it would be large. That is why the HL theory runs on opportunity cost, not absolute productivity. The free trade diagram shows the same idea in one market: the country stops making units whose domestic cost exceeds Pw and buys them instead, and consumers buy extra units they value above Pw; together these form the net gain. Two cautions follow. The gain is potential, because it needs displaced resources to find new uses. And it is net: some groups lose, and the model does not say whether they are compensated.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"Trade ties one economy’s prices and jobs to decisions made in others.\",\"tension\":\"Integration raises incomes but exposes a country to shocks such as a supplier’s export ban.\",\"ia\":\"An article on reliance on imported energy or food.\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Trade moves resources to more productive uses and presses firms to cut waste.\",\"tension\":\"Economy-wide gains can mean real losses for particular regions.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a factory closure blamed on imports.\"},{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"Trade widens what consumers can buy and where firms can sell.\",\"tension\":\"Deep specialisation can raise income but narrow options if export demand falls.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a trade deal widening consumer choice.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do high-income and low-income countries keep trading if one side is more productive at almost everything?\",\"expect\":\"That the productive country gains and the other is undercut.\",\"model\":\"Comparative advantage (HL only): both can gain if opportunity costs differ.\",\"evidence\":\"Such trade is large and persistent, and many lower-income economies have grown by exporting labour-intensive goods; some high-income workers competing with those goods lost out.\",\"match\":\"Largely for countries; less well for groups within them.\",\"assume\":\"Costless movement of resources between industries; full employment.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that displaced workers stay unemployed for years, or that trade costs swamp cost differences.\",\"policy\":\"Keep markets open, paired with retraining and regional support.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why a country imports a good when the world price is below its domestic equilibrium price.\",\"check\":[\"World supply horizontal at Pw, below domestic equilibrium\",\"Domestic output falls to Qs\",\"Quantity demanded rises to Qd\",\"Imports equal Qd − Qs\",\"Consumers gain surplus; domestic producers lose some\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a country with an absolute advantage in both goods can still gain from trade (HL only).\",\"check\":[\"Absolute advantage compares output; comparative advantage compares opportunity costs\",\"Opportunity costs calculated for each good in each country\",\"Each country has the lower cost in one good\",\"Specialising raises combined output\",\"Trade at a ratio between the costs lets both consume beyond their PPCs\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Name four benefits of trade listed in the syllabus.\",\"a\":\"Any four of: competition, lower prices, choice, resources, foreign exchange earnings, larger markets, economies of scale, efficient allocation, efficient production.\"},{\"q\":\"How are imports measured on a free trade diagram?\",\"a\":\"As domestic quantity demanded minus domestic quantity supplied at the world price.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: at Pw = $4, Qd = 600 units and Qs = 300 units. Calculate imports and import expenditure (HL only).\",\"a\":\"Imports = 600 − 300 = 300 units; import expenditure = $4 × 300 = $1,200.\"},{\"q\":\"Define comparative advantage (HL only).\",\"a\":\"The ability to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: A makes 40 cars or 80 computers; B makes 10 cars or 40 computers. Which has the comparative advantage in cars? (HL only)\",\"a\":\"A: a car costs it 80 ÷ 40 = 2 computers, against 40 ÷ 10 = 4 in B.\"},{\"q\":\"Give two limitations of comparative advantage theory (HL only).\",\"a\":\"Any two: rising costs, immobile factors, ignored transport costs and barriers, silence on sharing gains.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: with the same resources, Northland makes 90 tonnes of grain or 30 machines; Southland makes 20 tonnes of grain or 10 machines. Which is correct? (HL only)\",\"opts\":[\"Northland has a comparative advantage in both goods\",\"Northland has a comparative advantage in grain; Southland in machines\",\"Southland has a comparative advantage in grain; Northland in machines\",\"Neither gains from trade, as Northland is better at both\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Grain costs Northland 1/3 of a machine but Southland 1/2; a machine costs Southland 2 tonnes of grain but Northland 3. Producing more of both goods is absolute advantage, which does not decide specialisation.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-01\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: at the world price, domestic consumers buy 500 units and domestic firms supply 200. How many units are imported? (HL only)\",\"opts\":[\"200\",\"500\",\"300\",\"700\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Imports fill the gap between domestic quantity demanded and supplied: 500 − 200 = 300. Choosing 500 treats all consumption as imported, ignoring the 200 units domestic firms still sell.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"“We run a trade deficit with our neighbour, so trading with it makes us poorer.” What is the main flaw?\",\"opts\":[\"None: imports reduce welfare by definition\",\"Imports are goods buyers chose as cheaper or better; a deficit with one partner does not show trade lowers welfare\",\"Tariffs should be raised until trade with the neighbour balances\",\"Deficits matter only with larger partners\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Voluntary trade is positive-sum, and exports to all partners, not one, pay for imports. Treating imports as losses is the mercantilist error.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-02\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain how a benefit of trade arises, using a free trade diagram\",\"Paper 2: draw or label a free trade diagram showing imports or exports\",\"HL, Paper 2 or 3: opportunity cost ratios, or imports, import expenditure and export revenue from a diagram\",\"HL, Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate comparative advantage or specialisation\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"draw\",\"label\",\"identify\",\"calculate\",\"analyse\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"Free trade: domestic demand and supply, world supply at Pw, Qs, Qd and the imports or exports gap. HL only: two linear PPCs on one diagram with labelled intercepts, and a post-trade consumption point beyond each.\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 51): opportunity costs from a set of data; from a diagram, imports or exports, import expenditure and export revenue.\",\"apply\":\"Name the economy and the good, place Pw relative to the domestic price, and use only the benefits that fit that context.\",\"eval\":\"At HL, weigh national gains against the losers, the short run against the long run, and the assumptions against the context.\",\"errors\":[\"Comparative advantage read from output levels (HL)\",\"Imports measured as Qd rather than Qd − Qs\",\"Claiming trade benefits everyone\"],\"strong\":[\"Opportunity cost working shown for every good and country (HL)\",\"Every diagram quantity labelled and used in the text\",\"Each benefit tied to a mechanism and a stakeholder\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a diagram, explain how opening a market to international trade can lower prices and widen choice for consumers in a small economy.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: with the same resources, Country P makes 50 rice or 25 steel, and Country Q 30 rice or 10 steel. Calculate each country’s opportunity cost of one unit of steel and identify which has a comparative advantage in steel.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"HL only. Evaluate the view that a developing economy should specialise in the goods in which it currently has a comparative advantage.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"why\":\"At HL, comparative advantage applies opportunity cost and the linear PPC to two countries.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"why\":\"Consumer and producer surplus show who gains and loses when price moves to Pw.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"Every protection diagram starts from the free trade position drawn here.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"why\":\"The WTO and trading blocs try to secure the gains from trade described here.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"Because the tariff, quota and subsidy diagrams are built on the free trade diagram and show what is lost when trade is restricted.\"},\"ia\":\"Suitable articles report cheaper imports entering a market, a new export opportunity, or dependence on imported resources. Interdependence suits articles showing gains alongside vulnerability; efficiency suits articles about resources shifting between industries.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has a named country’s export pattern in one industry reflected comparative advantage (the HL theory) based on factor endowments, using trade data over a defined period?\",\"tok\":\"If the theory of comparative advantage (HL only) rests on assumptions known to be unrealistic, such as workers moving between industries at no cost, on what grounds can economists claim to know that trade is mutually beneficial?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students identify imports and exports on a free trade diagram and, at HL, assign specialisation using opportunity cost.\",\"starter\":\"Students check where five items in the room were made and sort the reasons for importing them: cost, availability, variety.\",\"activity\":\"Two teams with different ‘technologies’ make two paper products, first separately, then after specialising and trading; compare output and, at HL, draw both PPCs.\",\"discussion\":\"Both teams gained in total; did every individual gain?\",\"check\":\"A numbered free trade diagram: identify imports and, at HL, import expenditure.\",\"extension\":\"HL: redo the example with increasing opportunity costs and explain partial specialisation.\",\"homework\":\"SL: draw both free trade diagrams. HL: also three opportunity cost tables, one using hours per unit.\"},\"after\":[\"I can explain the benefits of trade, linking each to a stakeholder\",\"I can draw free trade diagrams showing imports and exports\",\"I can calculate imports, exports, import expenditure and export revenue from a diagram (HL only)\",\"I can distinguish absolute from comparative advantage and calculate opportunity costs (HL only)\",\"I can show the gains from specialisation on two linear PPCs (HL only)\",\"I can apply the benefits of trade to a named economy\",\"I can evaluate comparative advantage theory using its assumptions (HL only)\",\"I can explain without notes why trade can raise national welfare while some groups lose\"]},\"4.2\":{\"code\":\"4.2\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Trade protection means government measures that restrict imports or favour domestic producers over foreign ones. The main tools are tariffs, quotas, subsidies and administrative barriers. Each tends to raise domestic output and cut imports (an export subsidy instead raises exports), but each moves gains and costs between consumers, producers, taxpayers and foreign firms in a different way.\",\"why\":\"The choice of tool decides who pays, who gains and how much output is wasted. Firms that import inputs and households on tight budgets feel the difference directly.\",\"q\":\"When a government puts a tariff on imported steel, who ends up paying for it?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The free trade diagram, with world supply horizontal at the world price\",\"sub\":\"4.1\"},{\"t\":\"Consumer and producer surplus, and how a price change redistributes them\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"Indirect tax and subsidy diagrams, including welfare loss\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Areas of rectangles and triangles on a diagram\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define tariff, quota, production subsidy, export subsidy and administrative barrier\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a tariff’s producer gain and revenue are transfers but its two triangles are a net loss\",\"Explain why quota rent goes to licence holders unless licences are auctioned\"],\"apply\":[\"Identify the winners and losers from a named tariff, quota or subsidy\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse why a production subsidy cuts imports without raising the consumer price\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate which form of protection meets a stated aim at least cost, and to whom\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate stakeholder effects of a tariff, quota or subsidy from a diagram (HL only)\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw tariff, quota and production subsidy diagrams from a free trade starting point\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret each area on a protection diagram as a gain, a transfer or a net loss\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend a form of support that meets a stated aim at least welfare cost (HL Paper 3)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Four families of protection\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A tariff is a tax on imports, charged per unit or as a percentage of value. A quota limits the quantity or value of a good imported in a period. A production subsidy pays domestic firms that compete with imports for each unit they make; an export subsidy pays firms for each unit sold abroad. Administrative barriers, such as product standards, licensing and customs checks, raise the cost of selling into a market. The guide covers all four (guide PDF p. 52).\"},{\"h\":\"A tariff: price, quantities and welfare\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Start from free trade at Pw, with domestic output Qs₁, consumption Qd₁ and imports Qd₁ − Qs₁. A tariff t lifts the domestic price to Pw + t, because a small country cannot push down the world price. Consumers lose surplus a + b + c + d. Area a goes to domestic producers and c to the government: both are transfers. Areas b and d go to nobody and form the net welfare loss: b from producing at home above Pw, d from buyers priced out.\",\"because\":[\"The tariff raises the domestic price to Pw + t\",\"so output rises to Qs₂ and consumption falls to Qd₂\",\"so imports shrink to Qd₂ − Qs₂\",\"and revenue equals t × (Qd₂ − Qs₂)\"],\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Area\",\"Meaning\",\"Status\"],\"rows\":[[\"a\",\"Extra domestic producer surplus\",\"Transfer from consumers\"],[\"b\",\"Extra cost of output Qs₁ to Qs₂ made above Pw\",\"Net welfare loss\"],[\"c\",\"Tariff revenue: t × new imports\",\"Transfer to government\"],[\"d\",\"Surplus lost on purchases Qd₂ to Qd₁\",\"Net welfare loss\"]]}},{\"h\":\"A quota: same price effect, different winner\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A quota caps imports, so the domestic price rises until domestic quantity demanded minus domestic quantity supplied equals the quota; supply becomes domestic supply plus the quota. Price, output and the two triangles can match a tariff’s. But the rectangle (new price − Pw) × quota is not tax revenue. It is quota rent for whoever holds import licences, domestic importers or foreign exporters, unless the government auctions them. If demand grows, a quota lets the price keep rising, whereas a tariff lets imports rise at Pw + t.\"},{\"h\":\"A production subsidy: fewer imports, same consumer price\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A subsidy s per unit shifts domestic supply down by s. Consumers still buy at Pw, so consumption stays at Qd₁. Firms receive Pw + s and expand to Qs₂, so imports fall to Qd₁ − Qs₂. The government pays s × Qs₂: part becomes extra producer surplus, the rest is one triangle of welfare loss from home production costing more than Pw. With no consumption loss, a subsidy usually wastes less than a tariff that raises domestic output by the same amount, but taxpayers fund it.\"},{\"h\":\"Export subsidies and administrative barriers\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"An export subsidy raises exporters’ receipts per unit, so exports rise at a cost to the government. Firms that can earn Pw + s abroad may refuse to sell at home for less, so domestic consumers can pay more. Foreign rivals lose sales, and WTO rules restrict export subsidies. Administrative barriers raise the cost or risk of importing without raising revenue. Some protect consumers from real hazards; others are disguised protection, and the two can be hard to tell apart.\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: a tariff calculated from a diagram\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Illustrative data: Pw = $10. Under free trade, Qs = 20,000 and Qd = 80,000, so imports are 60,000. A $2 tariff raises the price to $12, Qs to 30,000 and cuts Qd to 70,000, so imports fall to 40,000. Check: the consumer surplus loss of $150,000 equals the producer surplus gain ($50,000) plus revenue ($80,000) plus welfare loss ($20,000). This stakeholder calculation is HL only (guide PDF p. 52).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Measure\",\"Before\",\"After\",\"Working\"],\"rows\":[[\"Consumer expenditure\",\"$800,000\",\"$840,000\",\"$10 × 80,000; $12 × 70,000\"],[\"Domestic producer revenue\",\"$200,000\",\"$360,000\",\"$10 × 20,000; $12 × 30,000\"],[\"Foreign producer revenue\",\"$600,000\",\"$400,000\",\"$10 × 60,000; $10 × 40,000\"],[\"Tariff revenue\",\"$0\",\"$80,000\",\"$2 × 40,000\"],[\"Welfare loss\",\"–\",\"$20,000\",\"½ × 10,000 × $2, twice\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A tariff on imported steel in a small economy\",\"cause\":\"The government imposes a per-unit tariff on steel imports.\",\"mechanism\":\"The domestic price rises by the tariff; domestic output rises, purchases fall and imports shrink.\",\"immediate\":\"Steelmakers and their workers gain, the government collects revenue and steel buyers pay more.\",\"secondary\":\"Car and machinery makers face dearer inputs and may cut jobs; partners may retaliate against exports.\",\"longrun\":\"Protected mills face less pressure to cut costs, and the welfare loss lasts as long as the tariff.\",\"depends\":\"Elasticities, whether the country can move the world price, steel’s share of downstream costs, and retaliation.\"},{\"t\":\"An import quota when domestic demand is growing\",\"cause\":\"Imports are capped and domestic incomes then rise.\",\"mechanism\":\"Extra demand cannot be met from abroad, so the domestic price rises until domestic supply fills the gap.\",\"immediate\":\"Producers gain, consumers pay more and licence holders earn more rent per unit.\",\"secondary\":\"Licence-holding exporters may switch to higher-value versions; the government gains nothing unless it sells licences.\",\"longrun\":\"The domestic–world price gap can widen, and licence holders gain a stake in keeping the quota.\",\"depends\":\"The pace of demand growth, supply elasticity, who holds the licences and whether they are auctioned.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Tariff\",\"dg\":\"tariff\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how an import tax changes price, output, consumption, imports, revenue and welfare.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The importer is a price taker\",\"Domestic and imported goods are identical\",\"No retaliation\"],\"mechanism\":\"Price rises by the full tariff; output rises, consumption falls and imports shrink; consumer surplus is partly transferred and partly lost.\",\"prediction\":\"Higher price, fewer imports, government revenue, and a net loss equal to two triangles.\",\"application\":\"Any tariff decision and its stakeholders.\",\"limitations\":[\"Large importers can lower the world price\",\"It ignores retaliation and downstream users\",\"It weighs every group’s dollar equally\"],\"real\":\"The US tariffs of 2018, including those on steel and aluminium justified on security grounds, are widely studied; several studies found their cost fell largely on US buyers, as the diagram predicts.\",\"misconception\":\"Thinking the exporting country pays. Domestic buyers pay the higher price; foreign firms lose sales.\",\"exam\":\"Core to Paper 1 and 2 protection answers; HL calculations in Paper 2 or 3. Label Pw, Pw + t, both Qs and Qd, revenue and both triangles.\",\"deeper\":\"A large importer that cuts its purchases can lower the world price, shifting part of the tariff onto foreign exporters. In theory a modest ‘optimal tariff’ could then raise national welfare at partners’ expense. In practice it invites retaliation, and if both sides impose tariffs, both can end up worse off. Even when it works, world welfare falls: one country gains by shifting costs onto another.\"},{\"model\":\"Import quota\",\"dg\":\"quota\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how a quantity limit on imports raises price, and who receives the rent.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The importer is a price taker\",\"The quota binds\",\"Licences go to particular traders\"],\"mechanism\":\"Supply becomes domestic supply plus the quota; price rises until Qd − Qs equals the quota.\",\"prediction\":\"Tariff-like price and welfare effects, with quota rent going to licence holders.\",\"application\":\"Quotas on farm goods, textiles or cars, and export limits agreed with exporting countries.\",\"limitations\":[\"The rent recipient depends on licence allocation\",\"Allocation invites lobbying\",\"Effects diverge from a tariff’s when demand grows\"],\"real\":\"In the 1980s Japan agreed to limit car exports to the US; studies suggest Japanese firms moved to pricier models and captured much of the rent.\",\"misconception\":\"Giving the rectangle to the government; it goes to licence holders unless licences are auctioned.\",\"exam\":\"Draw domestic supply plus the quota, the new price and the rent rectangle with its recipient; at HL, calculate the rent.\",\"deeper\":\"A quota can be compared with its ‘equivalent tariff’, the tariff that raises the domestic price by the same amount. In stable conditions their price and welfare effects match. They diverge when conditions change: rising demand raises price and rent under a quota, and a fall in the world price does not reach domestic buyers. Quotas also hide their cost, because nothing appears in the government budget.\"},{\"model\":\"Production subsidy to import-competing firms\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Shows how payments to domestic producers cut imports without raising the consumer price.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The importer is a price taker\",\"A fixed subsidy per unit of domestic output\",\"Funding is treated as costless (a simplification)\"],\"mechanism\":\"Domestic supply shifts down by s; producers receive Pw + s and expand, while consumers still pay Pw, so imports fall.\",\"prediction\":\"More domestic output, fewer imports, unchanged consumption, a cost of s × new output, and one welfare loss triangle.\",\"application\":\"Support for strategic or emerging industries, compared with a tariff.\",\"limitations\":[\"The taxes that fund it distort other markets\",\"It competes with other public spending\",\"It may trigger countervailing duties\"],\"real\":\"In the Airbus–Boeing dispute, the WTO found both the EU and the US had given their aircraft makers support that broke its rules, and let each retaliate with tariffs.\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing the consumer price falling, as in a closed market; with a fixed world price, consumers still pay Pw.\",\"exam\":\"Draw a free trade diagram plus a supply curve shifted down by s; show Qs₁, Qs₂, unchanged Qd, the cost rectangle and one triangle.\",\"deeper\":\"The subsidy result illustrates the targeting principle: if the aim is more domestic production, a tool aimed at production costs less in welfare than a tariff, which also taxes consumption. The tariff creates two distortions to achieve one goal. That is why economists who accept some case for support often prefer subsidies. The counterweight is fiscal: public money has an opportunity cost, and subsidies are visible in the budget.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"In a small importing country, a tariff raises the domestic price by the full tariff and creates a net welfare loss; a quota does the same but hands the rent to licence holders.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies of the US tariffs of 2018–19 generally found import prices rose by close to the full tariff, with losses for downstream industries and for exporters facing retaliation.\",\"interp\":\"Most economists see this as support for the incidence prediction, but disagree about total cost, which depends on effects the diagram omits, and about whether strategic aims justify it.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Who really pays? Evidence on incidence\",\"tag\":\"Evidence\",\"p\":\"Theory allows a large importer to push part of a tariff onto foreign exporters through a lower world price. Studies of the US tariffs of 2018–19 generally found close to full pass-through into the prices US importers paid, with foreign export prices changing little. Some work suggests retailers absorbed part of the cost in their margins rather than passing it all to shoppers. Incidence is an empirical question; the diagram gives a sensible first estimate, not a verdict.\"},{\"h\":\"What the triangles leave out\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"The welfare loss triangles are often small relative to national income, which can make protection look cheap. They omit harder-to-measure costs: dearer inputs for downstream firms, retaliation against exporters, resources spent lobbying and weaker pressure to innovate. They also omit benefits some governments value, such as secure supply of a strategic good. Estimates of the ‘cost per job saved’ try to capture this trade-off, but depend on assumptions about what would have happened otherwise.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Protection gives home producers an edge over foreign ones. A tariff adds a tax to imports, so imports cost more and home firms can charge more too. A quota limits how much can come in, which also pushes the price up. A subsidy pays home firms for each unit they make, so they can compete without the price rising. Either way, home producers sell more and foreign producers less. What differs is who pays: shoppers under a tariff or quota, taxpayers under a subsidy. Part of what shoppers lose goes to producers or the government; part is simply wasted.\",\"exam\":\"Define the instrument, then draw the diagram from free trade: domestic demand and supply, Pw and the world supply line. For a tariff, show Pw + t and label Qs₁, Qs₂, Qd₁ and Qd₂. Explain the chain: higher price → more domestic output, less consumption → fewer imports → revenue of t × new imports. Decompose welfare: consumer loss, producer gain and revenue as transfers, two triangles as net loss. For a quota, say who gets the rent; for a subsidy, note that consumption does not change. Evaluate by weighing stakeholders, elasticities, retaliation and downstream firms. At HL, show every step of a calculation, with units.\",\"deep\":\"Every protection diagram is a story of transfers and waste. A tariff moves income from consumers to domestic producers and the government, and destroys two slices of surplus: one because goods are made at home at a cost above the world price, one because some buyers are priced out. A quota causes the same waste, but the transfer goes to licence holders. A production subsidy moves income from taxpayers to producers and destroys only the production slice, because consumers still pay the world price. That is why economists who accept a case for supporting domestic output often prefer subsidies: tackle a problem at its source with the fewest side effects. The limits matter as much: large countries can shift world prices, partners retaliate, and protected inputs raise costs for other industries.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Each instrument is a deliberate choice with its own pattern of gainers and payers.\",\"tension\":\"Helping one industry can raise costs for industries using its output.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new tariff or quota, tracing each stakeholder.\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Protection keeps resources in production costing more than the world price and prices some buyers out.\",\"tension\":\"The cost may be accepted for other goals, such as secure supply.\",\"ia\":\"An article defending a subsidy as cheaper than a tariff.\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"One country’s protection cuts another’s exports and can provoke a response.\",\"tension\":\"Helping domestic producers can hurt domestic exporters if partners retaliate.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a tit-for-tat tariff dispute.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do governments often use tariffs or quotas when a production subsidy could support domestic output at lower welfare cost?\",\"expect\":\"That governments pick the cheapest tool for their aim.\",\"model\":\"A subsidy avoids the consumption loss that a tariff or quota creates.\",\"evidence\":\"Tariffs and quotas remain widely used; tariffs raise revenue that is easy to collect at borders, which matters where other taxes are hard to collect.\",\"match\":\"Partly: the welfare comparison holds, but ignores budgets and politics.\",\"assume\":\"Costless public funds, and governments minimising welfare loss rather than responding to lobbying.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that raising tax revenue is very costly, or that subsidies are harder to withdraw.\",\"policy\":\"Targeted, time-limited subsidies where support is justified; where tax capacity is weak, tariffs may be the practical choice.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a tariff diagram, why a tariff creates a net welfare loss even though producers and the government gain.\",\"check\":[\"Price rises from Pw to Pw + t in a small importing country\",\"Output rises to Qs₂; consumption falls to Qd₂\",\"Producer gain and revenue are transfers from consumers\",\"The two triangles are the net loss: inefficient production and lost consumption\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain how a quota differs from a tariff that raises the domestic price by the same amount.\",\"check\":[\"Both raise price and output and cut consumption and imports\",\"Under a tariff the rectangle is government revenue\",\"Under a quota it is rent to licence holders, unless licences are auctioned\",\"With growing demand, a quota lets price rise further; a tariff lets imports rise\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Distinguish a tariff from a quota.\",\"a\":\"A tariff is a tax on imports; a quota is a limit on the quantity imported.\"},{\"q\":\"Which tariff diagram areas are transfers, and which are net loss?\",\"a\":\"Producer surplus gain and revenue are transfers from consumers; the two triangles are the net welfare loss.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a $3 tariff; afterwards Qd = 50,000 and Qs = 35,000. Calculate tariff revenue (HL only).\",\"a\":\"Imports = 15,000, so revenue = $3 × 15,000 = $45,000.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a quota of 40,000 units raises the price from $10 to $12. Calculate the quota rent and name its recipient (HL only).\",\"a\":\"($12 − $10) × 40,000 = $80,000, going to licence holders unless licences are auctioned.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: Pw = $10; a $2 per-unit subsidy raises domestic output from 20,000 to 30,000 while consumers still buy 80,000. Calculate the cost to government and the new imports (HL only).\",\"a\":\"Cost = $2 × 30,000 = $60,000; imports = 80,000 − 30,000 = 50,000.\"},{\"q\":\"Why can administrative barriers be hard to challenge?\",\"a\":\"They may serve a genuine health or safety aim, making discrimination hard to show.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A small country imposes a tariff on imported bicycles. Under the standard model, who pays the higher price of bicycles sold there?\",\"opts\":[\"Foreign producers, because the tariff is charged on their goods\",\"Domestic consumers, because the domestic price rises by the tariff\",\"The government, which collects the tariff\",\"Nobody, because domestic supply rises\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The world price is unchanged, so the domestic price rises by the full tariff and domestic buyers pay it. Foreign producers lose by selling fewer units, not by paying the tax, which is why the first option tempts but is wrong.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-03\"},{\"q\":\"On a tariff diagram, which areas measure the net welfare loss to the importing country?\",\"opts\":[\"The tariff revenue rectangle\",\"The whole fall in consumer surplus\",\"The two triangles either side of the revenue rectangle\",\"The producer surplus gain plus revenue\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Producer gain and revenue are transfers to other domestic groups, so they are not lost. Only the production and consumption triangles go to nobody; counting the whole consumer loss double-counts the transfers.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-04\"},{\"q\":\"A tariff is replaced by a quota keeping the same domestic price, with licences given free to domestic importers. What happens to the former revenue rectangle?\",\"opts\":[\"It remains government revenue\",\"It becomes quota rent for licence holders\",\"It becomes extra welfare loss\",\"It becomes extra producer surplus\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The price gap on each imported unit remains but no tax is collected, so the gain goes to those allowed to import. The government gets it only by auctioning licences; it is not lost, because domestic importers gain it.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-05\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with a diagram, the effect of a tariff, quota or subsidy\",\"Paper 2: draw or label a protection diagram\",\"HL, Paper 2 or 3: stakeholder calculations from a diagram\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate a named measure’s effects on stakeholders\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"explain\",\"draw\",\"label\",\"calculate\",\"analyse\",\"compare\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"Tariff, quota and subsidy diagrams drawn from free trade: domestic D and S, Pw, the new price or shifted supply, Qs and Qd before and after, the revenue, rent or cost rectangle, and the triangle(s) (guide PDF p. 52).\",\"calc\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 52): stakeholder effects of tariffs, quotas and subsidies from a diagram, such as expenditure, domestic and foreign revenue, tariff revenue or subsidy cost, quota rent and welfare loss.\",\"apply\":\"Use the product, instrument and figures given; name industries that use the protected good and partners likely to respond.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh protected producers and workers against consumers, downstream firms and exporters, considering elasticities, time horizon, the instrument and retaliation.\",\"errors\":[\"Saying foreign producers pay the tariff\",\"Giving quota rent to the government by default\",\"Omitting a triangle or counting transfers as gains\",\"Revenue calculated on consumption, not imports\"],\"strong\":[\"Start every diagram from free trade\",\"Separate transfers from net loss\",\"Name the stakeholder behind each area\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Using a diagram, explain why an import quota may raise the domestic price of a good without increasing government revenue.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative scenario: a country with a large car industry imposes a 20% tariff on imported steel to protect steelworks jobs. Using a diagram, evaluate the tariff’s likely effects on different stakeholders.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: world price of rice $400 per tonne; under free trade, domestic output 2 million tonnes and consumption 5 million tonnes. A $50 tariff raises output to 2.5 million and cuts consumption to 4.6 million tonnes. Calculate the change in imports, the tariff revenue and the welfare loss, and recommend whether a subsidy giving the same output would be preferable.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"why\":\"The tariff and subsidy diagrams apply indirect tax and subsidy logic to a market open to imports.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"why\":\"Each protection diagram starts from free trade and reverses part of its gain.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"why\":\"These welfare areas are the evidence for weighing arguments about protection.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Fewer imports reduce the supply of domestic currency on foreign exchange markets, which can raise its value and hurt exporters.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"why\":\"Because arguments for and against protection are weighed using the gains, transfers and losses identified here.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles announcing a tariff, quota, anti-subsidy duty or new product standard suit this subtopic, especially with product names and figures. Intervention is a natural lens; efficiency suits articles on higher prices or wasted resources.\",\"ee\":\"What were the effects of a specific tariff or quota on the price, import volume and domestic output of one product in one country, using data from before and after its introduction?\",\"tok\":\"A protection diagram adds consumers’ losses and producers’ gains in the same money units. Does calling the result a ‘net’ welfare loss involve a value judgement about whose welfare counts?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students draw tariff, quota and subsidy diagrams from free trade and classify each area as transfer or net loss.\",\"starter\":\"Show an imported product’s price: if a 20% tariff is added, who pays the extra and who receives it?\",\"activity\":\"Build the tariff diagram in layers, shading each area a different colour; repeat for a quota and a subsidy and compare.\",\"discussion\":\"Stakeholder role-play (suggested in the teacher-created course roadmap): consumer, steelmaker, car maker, foreign exporter and finance minister each argue their case.\",\"check\":\"An unlabelled numbered tariff diagram: SL identify the areas; HL calculate revenue and welfare loss.\",\"extension\":\"Show on the quota diagram what happens when demand rises, and contrast it with a tariff.\",\"homework\":\"Sketch the diagram for a tariff or quota in the news and list each stakeholder’s gain or loss.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define tariffs, quotas, production and export subsidies, and administrative barriers\",\"I can draw tariff, quota and subsidy diagrams from free trade\",\"I can separate transfers from net welfare loss\",\"I can explain who receives quota rent and why\",\"I can calculate revenue, rent, subsidy cost and welfare loss from a diagram (HL only)\",\"I can apply the diagrams to a named product and evaluate effects on stakeholders\",\"I can connect protection diagrams to indirect taxes and subsidies in 2.7\",\"I can explain without notes why domestic buyers pay a tariff\"]},\"4.3\":{\"code\":\"4.3\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Governments restrict trade to nurture new industries, protect jobs, secure strategic supplies, answer dumping or foreign subsidies, raise revenue or diversify a narrow economy. Each argument has a version economists take seriously and a version that mainly shields particular producers. The skill is to state when an argument holds and weigh it against protection’s costs.\",\"why\":\"Trade policy affects household prices, firms’ input costs, and jobs in protected and exporting industries alike. Economics offers a way to test confident claims from both sides.\",\"q\":\"Can protecting a young industry help a developing economy grow, or does it mainly shield inefficient firms?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"The effects of tariffs, quotas and subsidies on each stakeholder\",\"sub\":\"4.2\"},{\"t\":\"The benefits of trade and the gains from specialisation\",\"sub\":\"4.1\"},{\"t\":\"Reasons for government intervention in markets, and its consequences\",\"sub\":\"2.7\"},{\"t\":\"Structural unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"},{\"t\":\"Positive versus normative statements\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"List the arguments for and against protection named in the syllabus\",\"Define infant industry and dumping\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain when the infant industry argument holds and when it fails\",\"Explain why protecting jobs in one industry can cost jobs elsewhere\"],\"apply\":[\"Attach each argument to the stakeholder it serves in a given context\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how retaliation and dearer inputs reduce export competitiveness\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate free trade against protection for a specific economy, reaching a conditional judgement\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Use the tariff diagram from 4.2 to show the cost behind a protection argument\"],\"interpret\":[\"Separate evidence from assertion in claims about jobs saved or dumping\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend whether and how to support an industry facing import competition (HL Paper 3)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"The debate is about conditions\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for arguments on both sides and a judgement on free trade versus protection (guide PDF pp. 52–53). Protection has costs, shown in 4.2: higher prices, less choice and welfare loss. Some arguments for it identify real problems that markets may not solve, such as missing finance for new industries or dependence on a hostile supplier; others collapse on inspection. Start from neither slogan: ask what problem a measure addresses, whether it works, and who pays.\"},{\"h\":\"Arguments for protection: when stronger, when weaker\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Each argument the guide lists has a condition that strengthens it and a common way it fails. Failure is often political: protection granted for a good reason is hard to remove once firms and workers depend on it. Use the table to plan. In an answer, develop two or three arguments in depth, linked to the context, rather than naming all ten.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Argument\",\"Stronger when…\",\"Weaker when…\"],\"rows\":[[\"Infant (sunrise) industries\",\"Costs can reach world levels; protection has an end date\",\"No end date or no prospect of competing\"],[\"National security\",\"Supply of an essential good could be cut off\",\"Stretched to ordinary industries\"],[\"Health and safety\",\"Real risk; same rules for domestic goods\",\"Designed to exclude foreign goods\"],[\"Environmental standards\",\"Imports made by methods banned at home\",\"Shields firms without cutting harm\"],[\"Anti-dumping\",\"Below-cost sales threaten efficient firms\",\"Low prices reflect real cost advantages\"],[\"Unfair competition\",\"Foreign state subsidies distort prices\",\"‘Unfair’ just means lower wages\"],[\"Balance of payments correction\",\"Short-term, while other policies work\",\"Used as a lasting fix\"],[\"Government revenue\",\"Other taxes are hard to collect\",\"Better taxes are available\"],[\"Protection of jobs\",\"Sudden, concentrated losses\",\"Cost per job exceeds its value\"],[\"Economically least developed country (ELDC) diversification\",\"Reliance on a few volatile primary exports\",\"Creates high-cost industries that do not export\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The infant industry argument\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"An infant (sunrise) industry is a new domestic industry whose costs are, for now, above those of established foreign rivals, because workers are still learning and output is too small for economies of scale. Temporary protection buys time to grow. The argument is defensible when learning and scale economies are real, private lenders will not fund the early losses, and protection ends on schedule. It fails when governments back industries with no prospect of competing, firms lobby to keep protection, or shielded firms stop trying to cut costs.\",\"because\":[\"Protection raises the price domestic firms receive\",\"so the industry expands and gains experience\",\"so average costs fall through learning and scale\",\"so protection can end once costs reach world levels; if not, consumers keep paying\"]},{\"h\":\"Protecting jobs: seen and unseen effects\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A tariff can preserve jobs in the protected industry, and those jobs are visible and concentrated. The costs are spread thinly: consumers pay more, firms using the protected good face higher costs, and exporters may face retaliation. Fewer imports can also strengthen the currency, making exports dearer. So the net effect on total employment may be small or negative even when protected jobs rise. Direct support for displaced workers, such as retraining, may achieve more at lower cost.\",\"because\":[\"A tariff protects jobs in the import-competing industry\",\"but raises costs for industries using its output\",\"and may provoke retaliation against exporters\",\"so the net employment effect depends on which effects are larger\"]},{\"h\":\"Arguments against protection\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide lists seven (guide PDF p. 53). Explain them as linked consequences rather than a list. Protection keeps resources in activities whose costs exceed the world price (misallocation). Consumers face higher prices and less choice, and firms using protected inputs face increased costs. Shielded firms have weaker incentives to become efficient. Partners may retaliate, and dearer inputs plus retaliation reduce export competitiveness. Because poorer households spend more of their income on goods, higher prices can weigh most heavily on them.\"},{\"h\":\"Reaching a judgement: a real case\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"In 2018 the US imposed tariffs on steel and aluminium, citing national security, and later on a wide range of Chinese goods; China retaliated, including against US farm products. The case shows security, protected jobs, retaliation and dearer inputs at once. Evidence suggests the price effects fell mainly on US buyers, and farm exports were hit hard enough that the government made support payments to farmers. A strong judgement asks: is the aim a genuine market failure? Is there a more direct tool? Will protection end? How will partners respond?\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Infant industry protection in a developing economy\",\"cause\":\"A tariff is placed on imported electronic components to help a new domestic industry.\",\"mechanism\":\"The higher domestic price lets new firms sell profitably despite higher costs than foreign rivals.\",\"immediate\":\"Domestic firms expand and hire; buyers of components pay more.\",\"secondary\":\"If firms learn and grow, costs fall; without performance conditions, firms may lobby to keep protection.\",\"longrun\":\"Success means an industry able to export once protection ends; failure means a lasting high-cost industry.\",\"depends\":\"Realistic prospects of competing, a credible end date, export targets, finance and skills.\"},{\"t\":\"A large economy raises tariffs and its partner retaliates\",\"cause\":\"A large economy raises tariffs on many imports from a partner, citing unfair competition.\",\"mechanism\":\"Import prices rise at home; the partner answers with tariffs on the first country’s exports.\",\"immediate\":\"Protected industries gain; importers and consumers pay more; exporters in both countries lose sales.\",\"secondary\":\"Supply chains shift, sometimes to third countries; uncertainty can delay investment.\",\"longrun\":\"A long dispute costs both sides gains from trade; if it wins policy changes, supporters may judge the cost worthwhile.\",\"depends\":\"Both economies’ size, how easily buyers switch suppliers, and how long the tariffs last.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The infant industry argument\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Identifies when temporary protection could raise long-run welfare by helping an industry reach competitive costs.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Costs fall with experience and scale\",\"Private finance will not cover early losses\",\"Protection can be removed on schedule\"],\"mechanism\":\"Protection raises the domestic price, letting the industry expand while its costs fall; once costs reach world levels, protection ends.\",\"prediction\":\"Short-run costs to consumers, then long-run gains if the industry becomes competitive.\",\"application\":\"Evaluating import substitution, and support for new industries such as electric vehicles or semiconductors.\",\"limitations\":[\"Governments may back industries with no real potential\",\"Protection creates lobbies to keep it\",\"Shielded firms may not cut costs\",\"A subsidy or credit may target the problem more directly\"],\"real\":\"Evidence is mixed. South Korea combined temporary support for new industries with export targets, and several became competitive; many mid-twentieth-century import substitution programmes elsewhere left high-cost industries that struggled when protection fell.\",\"misconception\":\"Assuming any new industry deserves protection; the case holds only if it can become competitive and protection is withdrawn.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g) evaluation: state the conditions for success and failure, and show the cost with a tariff diagram.\",\"deeper\":\"The strongest version rests on market failure, not youth. If one firm’s learning spills over to others, no firm captures the full benefit and too little investment occurs; if lenders cannot finance a long learning period, viable firms may not start at all. Both problems point first to targeted tools, such as training subsidies or credit, rather than tariffs, which make consumers fund the learning in a way that is hard to monitor or end.\"},{\"model\":\"Protection as a transfer with an efficiency cost\",\"dg\":\"tariff\",\"purpose\":\"Uses the tariff diagram to show what any argument for protection must outweigh.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The importer is a price taker\",\"Costs and benefits can be compared in money terms\",\"No retaliation (relaxed when evaluating)\"],\"mechanism\":\"The tariff moves surplus from consumers to producers and the government and creates two loss triangles; gains are concentrated, losses spread thinly.\",\"prediction\":\"Protection lowers welfare unless it corrects a market failure or delivers a benefit, such as security, that the diagram does not value.\",\"application\":\"Testing any argument: does the claimed benefit exceed the welfare loss and the costs the diagram omits?\",\"limitations\":[\"It does not value security, resilience or learning\",\"It omits downstream and retaliation costs\",\"It assumes displaced resources are re-employed\"],\"real\":\"Studies of tariffs aimed at saving jobs have often found that the consumer cost per job saved exceeded the wages of those jobs, though estimates depend on assumptions about the alternative.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating protected jobs as a free gain rather than a transfer with an efficiency cost.\",\"exam\":\"Draw it in protection essays to make the costs concrete, then weigh them against the specific argument.\",\"deeper\":\"The diagram also helps explain why protection persists. The gain per producer is large, so producers organise and lobby; the loss per consumer is small, so consumers rarely do. This collective action problem means trade policy can reflect organised interests rather than national welfare. Rules that bind governments, such as WTO commitments, can help resist lobbying, though critics argue they also limit legitimate policy choices, especially for developing economies.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Standard theory predicts that protection lowers national welfare unless it corrects a market failure or a large country can shift world prices in its favour.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence on infant industries is mixed; studies of recent tariffs suggest domestic buyers bore most of the cost and retaliation hurt exporters.\",\"interp\":\"Many economists see protection as costly on average but sometimes justified, with targeted, temporary support easier to defend than broad tariffs. Others argue security and resilience justify more active policy; the disagreement is partly about values and risk.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Did protection cause East Asia’s growth?\",\"tag\":\"Causality\",\"p\":\"Several fast-growing East Asian economies used protection alongside export targets, directed credit and heavy investment in education. It is tempting to credit protection. But much happened at once, and many countries that protected heavily without export discipline grew slowly. Economists disagree about how much of the success came from protection rather than from saving, skills and export competition. This is an identification problem: we cannot observe the same economy growing under free trade.\"},{\"h\":\"Why tariffs rarely fix a trade deficit\",\"tag\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"p\":\"A tariff cuts imports, but fewer imports reduce the supply of domestic currency on foreign exchange markets, which can raise its value and make exports dearer. Dearer inputs and retaliation also hurt exporters. More fundamentally, the current account reflects the gap between a country’s saving and its investment, and a tariff that leaves that gap unchanged tends to be offset elsewhere. Many economists therefore see tariffs as a weak long-run tool for external balance, though short-run effects are possible.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine a new bakery opening next to a big supermarket. Its bread costs more at first, because it is small and still learning. The council could tax supermarket bread for a few years to give it a chance. That might work if the bakery improves and the tax ends. If the bakery stays inefficient, or the tax stays in place, the town pays more for bread indefinitely. Many arguments for protection look like this: sound in some cases, a costly habit in others. Which applies depends on the details, not on slogans.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (b) and Paper 2 (g) questions here reward weighing, not listing. Define protection and any key term, such as infant industry or dumping. Develop two or three arguments that fit the context as chains of reasoning, each tied to a stakeholder. Use the tariff diagram from 4.2 to show the cost: higher price, lost consumer surplus and welfare loss. Then evaluate. Does the argument meet its conditions in this case? Is a less costly tool available? What changes between the short and the long run? Is retaliation likely? End with a judgement that states what it depends on.\",\"deep\":\"The case for free trade is about efficiency: specialisation and competition raise the total available. It does not claim that everyone gains or that markets cannot fail. The strongest arguments for protection identify a specific market failure, such as learning spillovers, missing finance, an externality or dependence on a hostile supplier, and argue that trade policy can correct it. Economists usually reply with the targeting principle: tackle a problem at its source. A tariff is rarely the most direct tool, because it also taxes consumers and invites retaliation. Arguments that simply redistribute income to one industry are weaker, since other policies can support displaced workers more cheaply. Political economy adds a warning: even well-designed protection creates groups with an interest in keeping it.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Protection is intervention justified by market failure or national interest, and it can fail in turn.\",\"tension\":\"Support that fixes a real problem can become permanent.\",\"ia\":\"An article on protection for a new industry: is there an end date?\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"One country’s protection changes prices, jobs and policy in others.\",\"tension\":\"Less dependence on one supplier can raise security but cut the gains from trade.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a tariff dispute, tracing effects on both sides’ exporters.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Protection’s costs often fall on consumers, including poorer households, while benefits go to particular industries and regions.\",\"tension\":\"Saving jobs in a struggling region can raise prices for poorer households elsewhere.\",\"ia\":\"An article on tariffs on basic goods: who pays?\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do governments keep protecting industries when most economists argue that free trade raises national welfare?\",\"expect\":\"That governments follow the efficiency case.\",\"model\":\"The tariff diagram shows a net loss but concentrated gains and dispersed costs; the infant industry and security arguments name conditions where protection may help.\",\"evidence\":\"Protection persists and often rises after shocks or in strategic sectors; evidence on its success is mixed.\",\"match\":\"Partly: the model explains the costs, while political economy and security concerns help explain the persistence.\",\"assume\":\"Governments maximise national welfare; displaced resources are quickly re-employed.\",\"change\":\"Evidence on how fast displaced workers recover, how often protected industries mature, and how large strategic risks are.\",\"policy\":\"Time limits, performance targets and worker support reduce protection’s costs; direct tools may suit jobs or security aims better.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain when infant industry protection is defensible, and why it often fails.\",\"check\":[\"Young industries have higher costs from limited experience and scale\",\"Defensible if costs are likely to fall to world levels\",\"Needs a credible end date, ideally with performance targets\",\"Fails if the industry has no realistic prospect of competing\",\"Firms may lobby to keep protection and lose the incentive to cut costs\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a tariff that protects jobs in one industry may not raise total employment.\",\"check\":[\"Output and jobs rise in the protected industry\",\"Firms using the protected good face higher costs\",\"Consumers have less to spend on other goods\",\"Partners may retaliate against exports\",\"The net effect depends on these offsetting effects\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Name four arguments for protection in the syllabus.\",\"a\":\"Any four of: infant industries, national security, health and safety, environmental standards, anti-dumping, unfair competition, balance of payments correction, government revenue, protection of jobs, ELDC diversification.\"},{\"q\":\"Define dumping.\",\"a\":\"Selling a good abroad below its cost of production, or below its price in the home market.\"},{\"q\":\"Why can tariffs matter more for government revenue in low-income economies?\",\"a\":\"Border taxes are relatively easy to collect when income and sales taxes are hard to administer.\"},{\"q\":\"Give two arguments against protection that concern exporters.\",\"a\":\"Retaliation cuts demand for exports; dearer protected inputs raise exporters’ costs.\"},{\"q\":\"What must a strong evaluation of the infant industry argument include?\",\"a\":\"Its conditions for success, such as falling costs and a credible end date, and its risks, such as permanent protection and weak incentives.\"},{\"q\":\"On a tariff diagram, what measures the efficiency cost of protection?\",\"a\":\"The two welfare loss triangles: inefficient domestic production and lost consumption.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Which condition most strengthens the infant industry case for a tariff?\",\"opts\":[\"The industry is new and its costs exceed foreign rivals’\",\"The industry employs many voters in marginal seats\",\"The industry can realistically reach world-level costs, and protection has a fixed end date\",\"The industry faces imports from a lower-wage country\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The case rests on costs falling through learning and scale, then protection ending. Being new and high-cost describes every infant industry, including those that fail to mature, so it cannot justify protection alone; votes and foreign wages are not part of the economic case.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-13\"},{\"q\":\"A government says a steel tariff “saved 5,000 jobs”. Which point most weakens the claim that the tariff raised national welfare?\",\"opts\":[\"The jobs were in high-unemployment regions\",\"Steel users faced higher costs, consumers paid more, and partners retaliated against exports\",\"The tariff raised government revenue\",\"Domestic steel output rose\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Protected jobs are visible, but the claim ignores costs spread across steel users, consumers and exporters, and jobs lost there. Revenue and higher output are part of the transfer the tariff creates.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-13\"},{\"q\":\"“We must protect our industries from countries with much lower wages, because we cannot compete.” What is the main economic weakness?\",\"opts\":[\"Low wages usually reflect lower productivity, and a high-wage country can still gain by trading goods in which its relative costs are lower\",\"Low-wage countries cannot make goods of acceptable quality\",\"Tariffs do not affect imports from low-wage countries\",\"None: a high-wage country cannot gain from trade with a low-wage one\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Wages broadly reflect productivity, and gains from trade depend on relative costs, not wage levels, so both sides can gain (the formal theory, comparative advantage, is HL only). Labour standards raise a separate equity question.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-01\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain two arguments for or against protection in a given context\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate or discuss protection for a particular industry, such as an infant industry\",\"Paper 2 (g): evaluate a government’s protection decision described in an extract\",\"HL Paper 3: recommend whether and how to support an industry facing import competition\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"outline\",\"analyse\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"examine\",\"justify\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"No new diagram: use the tariff, quota or subsidy diagram from 4.2 to show the cost of protection, fully labelled and referred to in the text.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Identify the country, industry and measure; choose arguments that fit, such as diversification for a commodity exporter or security for a strategic good.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh the aim against costs to consumers, downstream firms and exporters; test its conditions, retaliation risk, less costly alternatives, and short- versus long-run effects.\",\"errors\":[\"Listing arguments without weighing them\",\"Treating protected jobs as costless\",\"Ignoring retaliation by large trading partners\",\"Accepting the infant industry case without conditions\"],\"strong\":[\"Develop two or three arguments in depth\",\"Tie each argument to a stakeholder and the context\",\"Use the tariff diagram to show the cost\",\"Reach a judgement that states its conditions\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain two reasons why the government of an economically less developed country might protect its domestic industries.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that tariffs are an effective way to protect employment.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative scenario: a government imposes anti-dumping duties on imported solar panels after domestic makers complain of below-cost pricing, while also aiming to expand renewable energy. Using the information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the decision.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"The welfare costs calculated in 4.2 are what each argument for protection must outweigh.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"The jobs argument turns on structural unemployment: how quickly displaced workers find new work.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"why\":\"Trading blocs and WTO rules limit how members may use protection, including anti-dumping duties.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Import substitution and export promotion apply the infant industry and diversification arguments.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"why\":\"Because economic integration and the WTO are institutional responses to the costs of protection weighed here.\"},\"ia\":\"Articles on a new tariff, anti-dumping duty or support for a strategic industry suit this subtopic, especially those quoting supporters and critics. Intervention or equity can frame it, depending on whether the focus is the policy’s design or who bears its cost.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent did a specific protection measure achieve its stated aims in one country, judged by changes in domestic output, employment and prices?\",\"tok\":\"Economists largely agree on the efficiency costs of protection yet disagree on whether it is justified. Can evidence settle a debate that depends on how we value security, jobs in particular places and lower prices?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students state the conditions under which an argument for protection holds and weigh it against the costs on a tariff diagram.\",\"starter\":\"Show three headlines backing protection (jobs, security, dumping); students name the stakeholder each one speaks for.\",\"activity\":\"Structured debate on a real measure (suggested in the teacher-created course roadmap): each side uses the tariff diagram and a conditional argument; judges write a conditional verdict.\",\"discussion\":\"Which arguments identify a genuine market failure, and which mainly transfer income to one group?\",\"check\":\"For a named case, students tabulate the argument, the condition it needs and whether it is met.\",\"extension\":\"Compare two economies’ experience of infant industry protection and explain why the outcomes differed.\",\"homework\":\"A 200-word conditional judgement on a recent protection measure, naming stakeholders and a less costly alternative.\"},\"after\":[\"I can list the arguments for and against protection in the syllabus\",\"I can explain when the infant industry argument holds and when it fails\",\"I can explain why protecting jobs in one industry may cost jobs elsewhere\",\"I can link each argument to the stakeholder it serves\",\"I can use the tariff diagram to show the cost of protection\",\"I can evaluate free trade versus protection with a conditional judgement\",\"I can connect these arguments to development strategies in 4.10\",\"I can explain without notes why a trade deficit alone does not justify protection\"]},\"4.4\":{\"code\":\"4.4\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Countries can agree to trade with some partners on better terms than with everyone else. The deeper the agreement, the more each member hands over: tariffs on partners, then its own tariffs on outsiders, then control of its borders to workers and capital, and finally its own currency. Each step can widen markets and raise efficiency, but each removes a policy tool.\",\"why\":\"Firms gain or lose market access, workers gain or lose job options, and governments trade some sovereignty for growth and influence.\",\"q\":\"Why would a country give up control of its tariffs, borders or currency to join a group of neighbours?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Why countries gain from trade: specialisation, larger markets and economies of scale\",\"sub\":\"4.1\"},{\"t\":\"How a tariff changes price, domestic output, imports and government revenue\",\"sub\":\"4.2\"},{\"t\":\"Arguments for and against protection, including retaliation\",\"sub\":\"4.3\"},{\"t\":\"How a central bank uses interest rates to influence aggregate demand\",\"sub\":\"3.5\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Distinguish bilateral, regional and multilateral (WTO) trade agreements.\",\"Define the four levels of integration and state what each adds.\",\"Outline the objectives and functions of the WTO.\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a free trade area needs rules of origin but a customs union does not.\",\"Explain why a monetary union removes a member’s own interest rate and exchange rate.\"],\"apply\":[\"Classify a real agreement by its features rather than its name.\",\"Use illustrative prices and tariffs to show trade creation or diversion (HL only).\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how a larger market can lower average costs through economies of scale.\",\"Explain why a bloc’s net effect on welfare is ambiguous (HL only).\",\"Explain the factors that limit the WTO’s influence.\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate trading blocs for different stakeholders, weighing market access against sovereignty.\",\"Evaluate monetary union for a named member (HL only).\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Optional (HL): adapt the tariff diagram to show trade creation; the guide requires no diagram for 4.4.\"],\"interpret\":[\"Judge from given costs and tariffs whether a bloc creates or diverts trade (HL only).\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend whether a small open economy should turn its free trade area into a customs union, given data on partners and tariffs.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Preferential agreements, from two countries to many\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A preferential trade agreement gives selected partners better access to a market than others receive, usually through lower tariffs. The guide groups agreements by the number of countries involved (guide PDF p. 53): bilateral (two countries), regional (a group, often neighbours) and multilateral (members of the World Trade Organization). Under the WTO’s most-favoured-nation principle, a tariff cut agreed multilaterally applies to all members. Bilateral and regional deals discriminate by design: partners get terms that outsiders do not.\"},{\"h\":\"The ladder of integration\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Trading blocs remove barriers among their members (guide PDF p. 53). Classify a bloc by what it adds to the level below, not by its name: an agreement called a ‘partnership’ may be a free trade area. USMCA and the African Continental Free Trade Area are free trade areas; the EU is a customs union and a single market; the euro area is a monetary union. Deeper levels offer larger potential gains but leave each government fewer policy tools.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Level\",\"Adds\",\"Each member gives up\"],\"rows\":[[\"Free trade area\",\"No tariffs or quotas on most internal trade\",\"Little: own external tariffs kept\"],[\"Customs union\",\"A common external tariff\",\"Own tariff policy\"],[\"Common market\",\"Free movement of labour and capital\",\"Control of migration from partners\"],[\"Monetary union\",\"One currency and central bank\",\"Own interest rate and exchange rate\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why a customs union needs a common external tariff\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"In a free trade area each member sets its own tariffs on outsiders. An outside exporter could therefore ship goods into the member with the lowest tariff and move them on, tariff-free, to a member with a higher one. Free trade areas block this with rules of origin, which require firms to show that enough of a product was made inside the area, adding paperwork and cost. A customs union blocks it by making every member charge the same tariff on outsiders.\",\"because\":[\"Members keep different external tariffs\",\"Outside goods could enter via the lowest-tariff member\",\"Rules of origin stop this, at a cost\",\"A common external tariff removes the incentive\",\"But members lose their own tariff policy\"]},{\"h\":\"What blocs offer and what they cost\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Each advantage in the guide works through a chain (guide PDF p. 53). A larger market lets firms spread fixed costs over more output, reaching economies of scale and lowering prices. Labour mobility widens job opportunities. A bloc negotiating as one carries more weight, and closer ties can support political cooperation. Against this, members lose sovereignty, and firms with preferential access may lobby against wider liberalisation, slowing multilateral talks. Gains and losses fall on different people.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Advantages\",\"Disadvantages\"],\"rows\":[[\"Trade creation (HL only)\",\"Trade diversion (HL only)\"],[\"Economies of scale; jobs through labour mobility\",\"Loss of sovereignty\"],[\"Bargaining power; political stability\",\"A challenge to multilateral negotiations\"]]}},{\"h\":\"HL only: trade creation and trade diversion\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Trade creation occurs when a bloc replaces higher-cost domestic production with cheaper imports from a partner, so resources are used more efficiently. Trade diversion occurs when imports switch from a lower-cost outsider to a higher-cost partner only because the outsider still pays the tariff. Illustrative data: a country buys from outsider Z at $10 plus a 20% tariff, so $12. It forms a customs union with partner B, which produces at $11, and buyers switch to B. Consumers save $1 a unit, but the government loses $2 of tariff revenue, and each unit now uses $11 of resources instead of $10.\"},{\"h\":\"Monetary union\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"A monetary union is a group of countries sharing one currency and one central bank that sets a single interest rate (guide PDF p. 53); the euro area is the main example. HL only: the gains are no currency-conversion costs, no exchange rate risk between members, and easier price comparison, which can sharpen competition. The central cost is losing two policy tools: a member cannot set its own interest rate or let its currency fall to regain competitiveness. If one member slumps while others boom, one interest rate cannot suit both.\"},{\"h\":\"The World Trade Organization\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The WTO, set up in 1995 to succeed the GATT, aims to make trade freer, more predictable and non-discriminatory. It administers trade agreements, hosts negotiations, settles disputes, reviews members’ trade policies and supports developing countries. Its influence has limits (guide PDF pp. 53–54). Agreement on services and primary products has been hard to reach, partly because farm support is politically sensitive in richer countries. Bargaining power is unequal, as large economies bring more expertise and market leverage, and decisions usually need consensus, so progress is slow.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Two neighbours form a free trade area\",\"cause\":\"Two countries remove tariffs and quotas on each other’s goods.\",\"mechanism\":\"Partner goods become cheaper; firms face competition at home but gain duty-free access abroad.\",\"immediate\":\"Consumers switch to cheaper partner goods; efficient exporters expand and higher-cost rivals contract.\",\"secondary\":\"Growing firms reach economies of scale; workers move between industries, with some temporary unemployment.\",\"longrun\":\"Real incomes rise if trade creation dominates; part of the gain is lost if higher-cost partners displace cheaper outsiders (HL only).\",\"depends\":\"Existing trade between members, tariff levels, cost gaps between partners and outsiders, and labour mobility.\"},{\"t\":\"HL only: a shock that hits one member of a monetary union\",\"cause\":\"World demand for one member’s main exports falls while the rest of the union keeps growing.\",\"mechanism\":\"One interest rate is set for the whole union, and the member has no currency of its own to depreciate.\",\"immediate\":\"Output and employment fall in that member, while interest rates stay suited to the majority.\",\"secondary\":\"Competitiveness returns only if its wages and prices rise more slowly than partners’ (an ‘internal devaluation’) or workers leave.\",\"longrun\":\"Adjustment tends to be slow and painful where wages are sticky, and union fiscal rules may limit support.\",\"depends\":\"Labour mobility, wage flexibility, any shared budget, and how similar members’ economies are.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Trade creation and trade diversion (HL only)\",\"dg\":\"tariff\",\"purpose\":\"Judges whether joining a bloc raises or lowers efficiency and welfare for a member.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Domestic, partner and outside producers have different costs\",\"Tariffs are passed on in full to prices\",\"Buyers purchase from the cheapest source after tariffs\"],\"mechanism\":\"Removing internal tariffs cuts the price of partner goods. If they undercut higher-cost domestic output, trade is created; if they undercut a cheaper outsider only because it still pays the tariff, trade is diverted.\",\"prediction\":\"Welfare rises when creation outweighs diversion. Diversion is less likely when the common external tariff is low.\",\"application\":\"Assessing a proposed customs union, or explaining why a high common external tariff can harm members as well as outsiders.\",\"limitations\":[\"Static: it omits later gains from scale, competition and investment\",\"Hard to measure, because trade without the bloc cannot be observed\",\"It hides distribution between consumers, producers and taxpayers\"],\"real\":\"When the UK left the EU customs union and single market in 2021, its trade with the EU stayed tariff-free but faced new customs checks and rules of origin; economists still debate how far trade shifted between partners.\",\"misconception\":\"Calling trade diversion a benefit because trade with partners rises.\",\"exam\":\"HL Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): explain creation and diversion with a short illustrative calculation, or the tariff diagram read in reverse, then judge which dominates in the context.\",\"deeper\":\"Jacob Viner’s 1950 analysis showed that a customs union moves insiders towards free trade but can move the world away from it. Because it removes some distortions while keeping others, the theory of the second best applies: removing one distortion need not raise welfare. Later work stressed dynamic gains, such as larger markets, stronger competition and inward investment, that the static model leaves out.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"At HL, a bloc is judged to raise welfare when trade creation outweighs diversion, and dynamic gains add to this. A monetary union should raise trade further by removing currency risk.\",\"evidence\":\"Gravity-model studies generally find that trade agreements raise trade between members, though by widely varying amounts. Early estimates suggested currency unions could multiply members’ trade; later studies of the euro generally found much smaller effects.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that early estimates reflected small currency unions unlike large economies. Economists also disagree about how much trade within blocs is creation rather than diversion, because the counterfactual cannot be observed.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Optimum currency areas\",\"tag\":\"Assumptions\",\"p\":\"The theory of optimum currency areas, begun by Robert Mundell in 1961 and extended by later economists, asks when sharing a currency makes sense. Members should face similar shocks, labour should move easily between them, wages and prices should be flexible, and there should be a way to transfer fiscal resources to a region in trouble. Where these hold, losing the exchange rate costs little. Where they do not, one interest rate can be too high for a slumping member and too low for a booming one. Debate about the euro area after 2010 turned largely on these conditions.\"},{\"h\":\"Building blocks or stumbling blocks?\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"Economists disagree about whether regional deals help or hinder global free trade. Optimists see blocs as building blocks: willing countries can go further and faster, and blocs can later be linked. Sceptics, notably Jagdish Bhagwati, warned of a ‘spaghetti bowl’ of overlapping agreements, each with its own rules of origin, raising costs for firms and drawing effort away from the WTO. Evidence is mixed; the answer may depend on whether blocs keep external barriers low and stay open to new members.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine neighbouring shops that stop charging each other a fee to sell in each other’s streets. Customers get more choice and lower prices, and the best shops grow. That is a free trade area. If the shops also charge outsiders the same entry fee, it is a customs union. If staff and money can move freely between them too, it is a common market. If they share one till and one manager sets its terms, it is a monetary union. Each step can make the group richer, but each shop has less say over its own rules.\",\"exam\":\"Define the level of integration and name the feature that separates it from the level below: a common external tariff, free factor movement, or one currency and central bank. Explain each advantage as a chain, for example larger market → longer production runs → economies of scale → lower average costs and prices, and apply it to the bloc in the question. At HL, use trade creation and diversion, ideally with a short illustrative calculation, and explain why the net welfare effect is ambiguous. Evaluate by asking who gains (exporters, consumers, mobile workers) and who loses (protected firms, their workers, outsiders), over what time horizon, and how much sovereignty is given up.\",\"deep\":\"The case for a bloc rests on comparative advantage and scale: removing barriers lets production move to lower-cost producers and lets firms serve larger markets. But a bloc is a discriminatory move towards free trade. It lowers barriers for some partners while keeping them for others, so its net effect on efficiency is ambiguous. Trade creation (HL only) is more likely to dominate when members already trade heavily with each other, when their tariffs before the bloc were high, and when the common external tariff is low. Deeper integration adds regulatory alignment and factor mobility, which can raise productivity but constrain national choices on migration, standards and subsidies. Monetary union goes furthest: lower transaction costs and greater certainty must be set against losing the interest rate and exchange rate as shock absorbers.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"Integration deliberately links members’ markets, workforces and, in a monetary union, interest rates.\",\"tension\":\"Closer links spread gains but also transmit shocks between members.\",\"ia\":\"A country joining or leaving a bloc: who becomes more dependent on whom?\"},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"here\":\"Economies of scale and trade creation are efficiency gains; diversion is a loss (HL only).\",\"tension\":\"A bloc can raise efficiency among members while lowering it for the world.\",\"ia\":\"Firms relocating within a bloc: are resources moving to lower-cost producers?\"},{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"here\":\"Governments choose how far to integrate, trading market access against control of tariffs, borders and currency.\",\"tension\":\"Deeper integration narrows future national choices.\",\"ia\":\"A debate over membership, framed by the opportunity cost of sovereignty.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do most trading blocs stop at a free trade area?\",\"expect\":\"If integration raises efficiency, you might expect countries to integrate fully.\",\"model\":\"The ladder predicts larger gains from deeper integration but larger losses of policy independence.\",\"evidence\":\"Most regional agreements notified to the WTO are free trade areas; few are customs unions, and fewer still share a currency.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes: countries seem to stop where the extra gain no longer outweighs the extra loss of sovereignty.\",\"assume\":\"That governments weigh economic gains against political costs, and that members’ economies differ.\",\"change\":\"More similar economies, mobile labour or trusted shared institutions would favour deeper integration.\",\"policy\":\"Compare trade patterns, tariffs and the need for independent policy before choosing how deep to go.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain the difference between a free trade area and a customs union, and why it matters for trade policy.\",\"check\":[\"Both remove tariffs and quotas between members\",\"A customs union adds a common external tariff\",\"A free trade area needs rules of origin\",\"Customs union members cannot sign separate tariff deals\",\"A correct example of each\"]},{\"prompt\":\"HL only: Explain why joining a customs union might reduce a country’s welfare.\",\"check\":[\"Defines trade diversion\",\"The cause: the outsider still pays the tariff, the partner does not\",\"More resources used per unit; tariff revenue lost\",\"Net effect depends on creation versus diversion\",\"A condition, such as the height of the external tariff\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"What does a customs union add to a free trade area?\",\"a\":\"A common external tariff on imports from non-members.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a common market add to a customs union?\",\"a\":\"Free movement of labour and capital.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a free trade area need rules of origin?\",\"a\":\"Members keep different external tariffs, so outside goods could otherwise enter through the lowest-tariff member.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only. Illustrative data: an outsider sells at $20, a partner at $22; both face a 25% tariff until a customs union removes it for the partner. Where does the country buy before and after?\",\"a\":\"Before: outsider ($25 against $27.50). After: partner ($22 against $25), which is trade diversion.\"},{\"q\":\"State two functions of the WTO.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: administering agreements, hosting negotiations, settling disputes, reviewing trade policies.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: What is the main cost of a monetary union?\",\"a\":\"Losing your own interest rate and exchange rate as tools against a shock that hits you alone.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Countries A, B and C remove all tariffs on trade with each other but keep their own tariffs on imports from elsewhere. What have they formed?\",\"opts\":[\"A customs union\",\"A free trade area\",\"A common market\",\"A monetary union\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Free internal trade with separate external tariffs defines a free trade area. A customs union is tempting, but it requires a common external tariff, which is missing here.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-11\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: After joining a customs union, Country X stops importing from a low-cost non-member and buys from a higher-cost member. This is:\",\"opts\":[\"Trade creation, because trade with the bloc has increased\",\"Trade diversion, which lowers efficiency\",\"Trade creation, because consumers may pay less\",\"Neither, because import volume is unchanged\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Supply has moved to a higher-cost source only because the outsider still pays the tariff. More trade with partners and a lower price are tempting, but creation means lower-cost partner output replacing higher-cost domestic output.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-11\"},{\"q\":\"Which statement about a monetary union is most accurate?\",\"opts\":[\"Members keep their own central banks but fix their exchange rates\",\"Members share one currency and one interest rate, so none can use monetary policy against a shock that hits it alone\",\"It is the same as a customs union\",\"Members no longer need fiscal policy\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"One central bank means one interest rate for all, so no member can cut rates or depreciate alone. The first option describes a fixed exchange rate, which keeps separate currencies and central banks.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-11\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain the difference between two levels of integration, or two advantages of blocs\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate the effects on a named country of joining or leaving a bloc\",\"HL: discuss monetary union, or whether creation outweighs diversion in a context\",\"Paper 2: explain a WTO function or a limit on its influence\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"distinguish\",\"explain\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"to what extent\"],\"dg\":\"No diagram is required for 4.4 (guide PDF pp. 53–54). HL students may adapt the tariff diagram to show trade creation, labelling the price with and without the tariff and imports before and after.\",\"calc\":null,\"apply\":\"Name the bloc, classify it by its features, and use the text’s details on trade, tariffs or labour flows.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh gains to exporters, consumers and mobile workers against losses to protected firms, outsiders and policy freedom, short run and long run.\",\"errors\":[\"Using ‘customs union’ and ‘free trade area’ interchangeably\",\"Listing advantages without a causal chain\",\"Assuming that joining a bloc must raise welfare (HL: ignoring diversion)\",\"Describing monetary union without the loss of the national interest rate\"],\"strong\":[\"Name the precise feature separating each level\",\"Build full chains from market access to lower costs\",\"Use a short calculation to separate creation from diversion (HL)\",\"Condition the judgement on tariffs, trade patterns and depth\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain two reasons why a country might prefer membership of a customs union to membership of a free trade area.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"HL: Using real-world examples, discuss whether joining a monetary union is likely to benefit a small economy whose exports differ greatly from those of the other members.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative scenario: a small economy that sells most of its exports to its customs union partners leaves the union and signs a free trade agreement with them, keeping zero tariffs but facing rules of origin and new customs checks. Using this information and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the likely impact of the change on the economy.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"why\":\"Blocs rest on the gains from trade: larger markets, scale and specialisation.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"why\":\"The tariff diagram shows what a bloc removes for partners and keeps for outsiders.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"why\":\"In a monetary union one central bank sets one interest rate for all members.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"A monetary union removes exchange rates between members, so what a flexible rate does is what members give up.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Because judging a monetary union requires knowing how exchange rates are set and what a change in them does.\"},\"ia\":\"An article about a country negotiating a trade deal, joining a bloc, or facing new border checks after leaving one works well. Classify the agreement by its features, then use interdependence or efficiency to frame who gains and who loses.\",\"ee\":\"How far did a named country’s imports of a chosen product shift between bloc partners and non-members after it joined a specific bloc, and does the pattern suggest creation or diversion?\",\"tok\":\"If the trade that would have happened without a bloc cannot be observed, how can economists know whether the bloc created or diverted trade?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students classify blocs by their features and evaluate integration using sovereignty, efficiency and, at HL, creation and diversion.\",\"starter\":\"Four descriptions of real agreements, names hidden: students place each on the ladder and justify it from one feature.\",\"activity\":\"HL price game: groups hold cost cards for a domestic firm, a partner and an outsider, apply a tariff before and after a customs union, and record who supplies the market.\",\"discussion\":\"Should a small economy whose exports differ from its neighbours’ adopt their currency? What would it give up?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: what does a customs union add to a free trade area, and why does that remove the need for rules of origin?\",\"extension\":\"Research one product’s rules of origin in a real agreement and explain how they shape a firm’s sourcing.\",\"homework\":\"A Paper 1 (a)-style answer on two advantages of blocs as full chains, each with one limitation.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define the four levels of integration and state what each adds.\",\"I can explain why a free trade area needs rules of origin.\",\"I can explain the advantages and disadvantages of blocs as causal chains.\",\"I can distinguish trade creation from trade diversion with numbers (HL only).\",\"I can explain monetary union and evaluate it (evaluation HL only).\",\"I can outline the WTO’s functions and what limits its influence.\",\"I can classify a real agreement described in a text.\",\"I can evaluate integration for different stakeholders and time horizons.\",\"I can explain the difference between a customs union and a common market without notes.\"]},\"4.5\":{\"code\":\"4.5\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"An exchange rate is the price of one currency in terms of another. Under floating it is set by demand and supply: people buy a currency to purchase that country’s exports and assets, and sell it to buy foreign ones. Because the rate feeds into export and import prices, a change spreads to inflation, growth, jobs and the current account. Governments can fix or manage it, but only by buying and selling.\",\"why\":\"A currency move changes what exporters earn, what households pay for imports, and how hard the central bank’s job becomes.\",\"q\":\"Why does a fall in a country’s currency please its exporters but worry its central bank?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Equilibrium price, and the difference between a shift of a curve and a movement along it\",\"sub\":\"2.3\"},{\"t\":\"How interest rates affect aggregate demand\",\"sub\":\"3.5\"},{\"t\":\"Net exports (X − M) in aggregate demand, and the AD/AS diagram\",\"sub\":\"3.2\"},{\"t\":\"Demand-pull and cost-push inflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"},{\"t\":\"Calculating a percentage change\",\"sub\":null}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define appreciation, depreciation, devaluation and revaluation, and match each to its system.\",\"List the factors in the guide that change the demand for and supply of a currency.\",\"Describe floating, fixed and managed exchange rate systems.\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain where the demand for and supply of a currency come from.\",\"Explain why higher relative inflation tends to cause depreciation, and why depreciation can raise inflation.\"],\"apply\":[\"Identify which curve shifts, and which way, for a described event.\",\"Apply the consequences of a currency change to a named economy using its trade data.\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse a depreciation’s effects on inflation, growth, unemployment, the current account and living standards.\",\"Analyse how a central bank maintains a fixed or managed rate.\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate a currency change for exporters, importers, consumers and foreign-currency borrowers.\",\"Evaluate fixed against floating exchange rate systems (HL only).\"],\"calculate\":[\"Convert the price of a good from one currency to another.\",\"Calculate the percentage change in a currency’s value from data, and explain why the two currencies’ changes differ.\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw the foreign exchange market with both currencies named, showing a floating change and how a fixed or managed rate is held.\",\"Draw AD/AS showing the effects of a depreciation.\"],\"interpret\":[\"Read a table of exchange rates to identify appreciations and depreciations.\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend whether a small open economy with volatile export earnings should peg, manage or float its currency.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"A price with two names\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"An exchange rate is the value of one currency in another, and it reads both ways. Illustrative data: if £1 = $1.25, then $1 = £0.80. A £40 jacket costs 40 × 1.25 = $50 in the US; a $30 book costs 30 ÷ 1.25 = £24 in the UK. If the rate moves to £1 = $1.00, the pound’s value changes by (1.00 − 1.25) ÷ 1.25 × 100 = −20%, a 20% depreciation, while the dollar rises from £0.80 to £1.00, a 25% appreciation. The percentages differ because each uses a different base. Both calculations are listed for SL and HL (guide PDF p. 54).\"},{\"h\":\"Who demands and who supplies a currency\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Each currency trades in its own market. For the pound, the horizontal axis shows the quantity of pounds and the vertical axis the price of the pound in dollars. Foreigners demand pounds to buy UK goods, services and assets; UK residents supply pounds to buy foreign ones. If the price of the pound rises, it has appreciated; if it falls, it has depreciated. Some events move both curves: higher UK interest rates raise demand for pounds and reduce their supply.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Source\",\"Demand for £ comes from\",\"Supply of £ comes from\"],\"rows\":[[\"Trade\",\"Foreign buyers of UK exports\",\"UK buyers of imports\"],[\"FDI and portfolio investment\",\"Foreign investment into the UK\",\"UK investment abroad\"],[\"Remittances\",\"Money sent home to the UK\",\"Money sent out by migrant workers in the UK\"],[\"Speculation\",\"Traders expecting the £ to rise\",\"Traders expecting the £ to fall\"],[\"Central bank\",\"Buying £ with reserves\",\"Selling £ for foreign currency\"]]}},{\"h\":\"What shifts the curves\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The guide lists ten factors (guide PDF p. 54). Most work through the table above. Relative inflation matters because it changes price competitiveness. Relative interest rates matter because they change the return on holding the currency: higher UK rates attract inflows and keep savings at home, so the pound appreciates. Relative growth is ambiguous: faster UK growth raises spending on imports, increasing the supply of pounds, but may also attract investment, increasing demand. The net effect depends on which is larger.\",\"because\":[\"UK inflation rises above its trading partners’\",\"UK goods become relatively dearer\",\"Foreigners buy fewer UK exports: demand for £ shifts left\",\"UK residents buy more imports: supply of £ shifts right\",\"The pound depreciates\"]},{\"h\":\"Floating, fixed and managed systems\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Under a floating system the market sets the rate. Under a fixed system the authorities commit to a stated rate, or peg, against another currency, a basket or a commodity, and intervene to hold it. A managed system lets the market set the rate most of the time, but the central bank steps in when movements look excessive. A currency held above the rate the market would set is overvalued, making exports dear and imports cheap; one held below it is undervalued, helping exporters and building up reserves (guide PDF pp. 54–55).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Floating\",\"Fixed\",\"Managed\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rate set by\",\"Demand and supply\",\"The authorities\",\"The market, with intervention\"],[\"A rise is called\",\"Appreciation\",\"Revaluation\",\"Appreciation\"],[\"A fall is called\",\"Depreciation\",\"Devaluation\",\"Depreciation\"]]}},{\"h\":\"How a peg is held\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Suppose imports surge, so the supply of the currency shifts right. At the peg there is now excess supply, and left alone the currency would fall. The central bank buys its own currency with foreign reserves, shifting demand right until it meets supply at the peg. It may also raise interest rates to attract inflows, borrow abroad or restrict purchases of foreign currency. Under upward pressure it sells its own currency and adds to reserves. The limit is lopsided: it can create its own currency to sell, but can run out of reserves to buy with.\",\"because\":[\"Supply of the currency rises\",\"Excess supply appears at the peg\",\"The central bank buys its currency with reserves\",\"Demand shifts right and the peg holds\",\"Reserves fall; if they run low, devaluation pressure builds\"]},{\"h\":\"What a depreciation does to the economy\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A depreciation makes exports cheaper for foreign buyers and imports dearer at home (guide PDF p. 54). If volumes respond, net exports rise and AD shifts right, raising real GDP and employment in export and import-competing industries. Meanwhile imported fuel and materials cost more, shifting SRAS left. Both shifts raise the price level, so inflation tends to rise. The current account may improve, but only under conditions covered in 4.6. Living standards change unevenly: exporters and their workers gain, while households pay more for imported goods and foreign travel. An appreciation works broadly in reverse.\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: fixed versus floating\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"A floating rate frees monetary policy for domestic goals and lets the currency absorb shocks without reserves, but volatility adds risk for traders and investors and can import inflation. A fixed rate offers certainty and, if pegged to a low-inflation currency, discipline on prices, but it needs reserves, ties interest rates to the peg, and invites speculative attack if markets doubt it. Managed systems try to gain some of each (guide PDF p. 55).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Floating\",\"Fixed\"],\"rows\":[[\"Certainty for trade and investment\",\"Lower\",\"Higher while the peg is credible\"],[\"Monetary policy\",\"Free for domestic goals\",\"Tied to the peg\"],[\"Adjustment to shocks\",\"The exchange rate moves\",\"Output, wages and prices must move\"],[\"Main risk\",\"Volatility and imported inflation\",\"Speculative attack; forced devaluation\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"The central bank raises its interest rate (floating rate)\",\"cause\":\"The policy rate rises while rates abroad stay the same.\",\"mechanism\":\"Assets in the currency pay more, so foreign investors buy it (demand shifts right) and residents keep funds at home (supply shifts left).\",\"immediate\":\"The currency appreciates.\",\"secondary\":\"Exports become dearer abroad and imports cheaper, so net exports tend to fall, adding to the direct effect of higher rates on AD; cheaper imports ease inflation.\",\"longrun\":\"The appreciation lasts while investors expect the rate gap to persist; if expectations change, short-term ‘hot money’ can leave quickly.\",\"depends\":\"The rise relative to what markets expected, other central banks’ actions, perceived risk, capital mobility and trade elasticities.\"},{\"t\":\"A sharp depreciation in an economy that imports most of its energy\",\"cause\":\"Investors lose confidence and sell the currency; supply shifts right and it depreciates.\",\"mechanism\":\"Import prices rise in domestic currency, while exports become cheaper for foreign buyers.\",\"immediate\":\"Fuel and input costs rise quickly, shifting SRAS left: imported cost-push inflation.\",\"secondary\":\"If export and import volumes respond, net exports rise and AD shifts right, supporting output and jobs, while pushing prices up further.\",\"longrun\":\"If wages chase prices, the competitive gain erodes; debts owed in foreign currency become dearer to service.\",\"depends\":\"Import dependence, how far firms pass on costs, spare capacity, price elasticities of export and import demand, and central bank credibility.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The foreign exchange market: floating, fixed and managed\",\"dg\":\"fx\",\"purpose\":\"Predicts the direction of a currency change after an event, and shows what holding a target requires.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Demand comes from purchases of exports and domestic assets; supply from purchases of imports and foreign assets\",\"One factor changes at a time\",\"Under a peg, the central bank has reserves to intervene\"],\"mechanism\":\"An event shifts demand or supply, creating excess demand or supply at the old rate. Under floating, the rate moves to clear the market; under a peg or managed range, the central bank buys or sells the currency to hold the target.\",\"prediction\":\"More demand or less supply causes appreciation; less demand or more supply causes depreciation. A peg holds only while reserves and resolve last.\",\"application\":\"Explaining a reported currency move, or a central bank’s changing reserves.\",\"limitations\":[\"Several factors usually change at once, so the net effect can be ambiguous\",\"Expectations can move the rate before any flow happens\",\"Reserves are finite, so a peg under persistent pressure may fail\"],\"real\":\"The Swiss National Bank capped the franc’s value against the euro from 2011 and removed the cap in January 2015, after which the franc rose sharply within a day.\",\"misconception\":\"Labelling the vertical axis ‘price’ without naming the currencies, or calling a cut in a peg a depreciation.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (a) and Paper 2: label ‘price of £ in $’ and ‘quantity of £’, show the shift and both rates, and explain who is buying or selling; for a peg, draw the target as a horizontal line and the intervention shift.\",\"deeper\":\"The model treats the exchange rate like any market price, but most currency trading involves financial assets, not goods. Assets are priced on expected future returns, so news about future interest rates or risk can move the rate before any trade or investment flow occurs. This helps explain why exchange rates are volatile and why short-run movements are hard to forecast.\"},{\"model\":\"Exchange rate changes in AD/AS\",\"dg\":\"adas\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how a currency move reaches output, jobs and prices.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Trade volumes respond to relative prices\",\"Firms use some imported inputs\",\"There is some spare capacity\"],\"mechanism\":\"A depreciation raises net exports (AD shifts right) and raises imported costs (SRAS shifts left). An appreciation does the reverse.\",\"prediction\":\"After a depreciation the price level rises; real GDP rises if the AD effect outweighs the SRAS effect.\",\"application\":\"Judging whether a central bank should welcome or resist a depreciation.\",\"limitations\":[\"Volumes may respond slowly, so the price effect can come first\",\"Import dependence and pass-through vary by economy\",\"It ignores effects through foreign-currency debt\"],\"real\":\"Energy-importing economies have often seen inflation rise after sharp depreciations, as local fuel prices climb quickly.\",\"misconception\":\"Showing only the AD shift and concluding that depreciation simply boosts growth.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): draw AD/AS with the AD and, where relevant, SRAS shifts; judge which dominates in context.\",\"deeper\":\"How far a depreciation raises domestic prices, called exchange rate pass-through, varies. It tends to be higher in small, open economies and where inflation expectations are poorly anchored, and lower where the central bank is credible. Pass-through to consumer prices is often incomplete, because retailers absorb some of the cost and much of a final price pays for local distribution.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Higher relative inflation tends to cause depreciation, and higher relative interest rates tend to cause appreciation. Over the long run, purchasing power parity suggests exchange rates move to offset inflation differences.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies generally find that purchasing power parity holds only loosely and over long periods, with deviations fading slowly over years. Short-run movements are hard to forecast: a 1983 study by Meese and Rogoff found standard models did no better than assuming no change.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that expectations, risk and capital flows dominate in the short run, while inflation matters more over years. Economists disagree about how much short-run noise reflects rational responses to news rather than speculative herding.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"The impossible trinity\",\"tag\":\"Institutions\",\"p\":\"The ‘impossible trinity’ holds that a country cannot at once fix its exchange rate, allow capital to move freely and run an independent monetary policy; it must give up one. Hong Kong’s long-standing peg to the US dollar comes with free capital flows, so its interest rates largely track US rates. China has combined a managed rate with controls on capital movements. Floating economies keep monetary independence but accept volatility. This is why the HL judgement between fixed and floating depends on which goal a country values most.\"},{\"h\":\"When depreciation hurts: foreign-currency debt\",\"tag\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"p\":\"Textbook analysis treats depreciation as a boost to competitiveness. But when firms, banks or governments have borrowed in foreign currency, a depreciation raises the local-currency value of their debts while their incomes stay in local currency. Balance sheets weaken, spending falls and defaults can follow, which may outweigh the export gain. This helps explain why many emerging economies intervene to limit depreciation even when they officially float, a pattern Guillermo Calvo and Carmen Reinhart called ‘fear of floating’.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of a currency as a product that can be bought and sold. People want pounds when they want British things: a holiday in London, a British car, shares in a British company. People sell pounds when they want foreign things. If more people want pounds than want to sell them, the price of the pound rises and it appreciates. British goods become dearer for foreigners and foreign goods cheaper for Britons. A government that dislikes these swings can fix the price, but only by buying and selling pounds itself, and it can run out of the foreign currency it needs.\",\"exam\":\"Define the exchange rate and name both currencies. Draw the foreign exchange market with ‘quantity of £’ on the horizontal axis and ‘price of £ in $’ on the vertical axis. Identify the factor, say which curve shifts and which way, and state the new rate: higher UK interest rates increase demand for pounds and reduce supply, so the pound appreciates. For consequences, trace the change to export and import prices, then to net exports, AD, inflation and employment, using AD/AS where helpful. Evaluate by conditioning on elasticities, import dependence, time horizon and stakeholders. Use ‘devaluation’ and ‘revaluation’ only for fixed rates. At HL, weigh fixed against floating systems.\",\"deep\":\"The demand-and-supply model describes flows, but currencies are also assets. Their value depends on expected returns, so news about future interest rates or political risk can move a rate before any goods or capital cross a border. Over long periods relative inflation matters most, since a currency that loses purchasing power at home tends to lose it abroad; purchasing power parity describes this tendency but holds only loosely. In the short run, interest differentials, risk appetite and speculation dominate. Policy choices sit inside a trilemma: a country cannot fix its rate, allow free capital movement and set its own interest rates all at once. Fixed, managed and floating regimes are different answers to that constraint, so the HL judgement between them depends on context.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Floating rates change continuously, altering export and import prices across the economy.\",\"tension\":\"Flexibility helps an economy adjust to shocks but adds uncertainty for firms.\",\"ia\":\"A sharp currency move: how does it change prices and incentives for different groups?\"},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"A currency’s value depends on decisions abroad: foreign interest rates, investor confidence, export demand.\",\"tension\":\"Openness to capital brings investment but exposes the currency to sudden reversals.\",\"ia\":\"Capital outflows after a foreign central bank raises rates.\"},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Central banks fix or manage exchange rates using reserves and interest rates.\",\"tension\":\"Defending a rate can conflict with domestic goals such as low unemployment.\",\"ia\":\"A central bank selling reserves to support its currency: what does intervention cost?\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why can a currency rise while its country runs a large trade deficit?\",\"expect\":\"With more currency sold for imports than bought for exports, you might expect it to fall.\",\"model\":\"Demand and supply come from all sources, so large enough financial inflows can outweigh trade flows.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests capital flows often dominate short-run movements; the US dollar has at times strengthened while the US ran large trade deficits.\",\"match\":\"Yes, once all sources are included; the puzzle arises only if the model is reduced to trade.\",\"assume\":\"That investors see the country’s assets as safe and attractive, and capital moves freely.\",\"change\":\"Lost confidence or lower relative interest rates could reverse inflows sharply.\",\"policy\":\"Watch the mix of inflows: FDI is steadier than short-term portfolio money.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why a rise in a country’s relative interest rates is likely to cause its currency to appreciate.\",\"check\":[\"Axes name both currencies\",\"Higher returns attract inflows: demand shifts right\",\"Residents invest less abroad: supply shifts left\",\"Higher equilibrium rate, with conditions such as expectations and risk\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain the likely consequences of a depreciation for inflation and economic growth.\",\"check\":[\"Imports dearer at home, exports cheaper abroad\",\"Imported costs rise: SRAS shifts left\",\"If volumes respond, net exports rise: AD shifts right\",\"Outcome depends on import dependence, elasticities, spare capacity and time\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Distinguish a depreciation from a devaluation.\",\"a\":\"A depreciation is a market-driven fall in a floating currency; a devaluation is a deliberate cut in a fixed rate by the authorities.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: €1 = ¥160. What does a ¥4,000 book cost in euros?\",\"a\":\"4,000 ÷ 160 = €25.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the rate moves from $1 = ₹80 to $1 = ₹88. By how much has the dollar appreciated?\",\"a\":\"(88 − 80) ÷ 80 × 100 = 10%. The rupee has depreciated, by about 9.1%.\"},{\"q\":\"Which curve shifts, and which way, when residents buy more imports?\",\"a\":\"Supply of the domestic currency shifts right, so it depreciates.\"},{\"q\":\"How does a central bank stop its pegged currency from falling?\",\"a\":\"It buys its own currency with reserves, and may raise interest rates to attract inflows.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: State one advantage of floating and one of fixed rates.\",\"a\":\"Floating: monetary policy is free for domestic goals. Fixed: more certainty for traders and investors.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"The exchange rate moves from £1 = $1.30 to £1 = $1.20. Which statement is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"The pound has appreciated, so UK exports become cheaper in the US\",\"The pound has depreciated, so UK exports become cheaper for US buyers\",\"The pound has depreciated, so US goods become cheaper for UK buyers\",\"The dollar has depreciated, so US exports become dearer in the UK\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Each pound buys fewer dollars, so the pound has depreciated and UK goods cost fewer dollars. The third option has the right direction but the wrong effect: US goods now cost more pounds.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-06\"},{\"q\":\"A central bank that pegs its currency to the US dollar announces a new, lower pegged rate. What has happened?\",\"opts\":[\"A depreciation, because the currency is worth less\",\"A devaluation, because the authorities have deliberately lowered a fixed rate\",\"A revaluation, because the peg has been reset\",\"An appreciation against the dollar\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A deliberate cut in a fixed rate is a devaluation. ‘Depreciation’ is tempting because the currency is worth less, but it describes a market-driven fall under floating.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-08\"},{\"q\":\"Country Z runs a trade deficit. Its central bank then raises interest rates well above those abroad. Other things equal, the most likely short-run effect on Z’s currency is:\",\"opts\":[\"Depreciation, because the trade deficit means more of the currency is supplied than demanded\",\"Appreciation, because higher returns raise demand for the currency and reduce its supply\",\"No change, because interest rates affect only domestic borrowing\",\"Depreciation, because higher rates reduce demand for Z’s exports\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Higher relative rates attract inflows and discourage outflows, so the currency appreciates. The trade-deficit answer is tempting, but currencies respond to all sources of demand and supply, and capital flows often dominate in the short run.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-12\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2: a currency conversion or percentage change, with a comment\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain with a diagram how a factor changes a floating rate, or how a peg is held\",\"Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g): evaluate a depreciation or appreciation for a named economy\",\"HL: discuss fixed against floating systems\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"calculate\",\"draw\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\"],\"dg\":\"Foreign exchange market: ‘price of [currency] in [second currency]’, ‘quantity of [currency]’, the shift and both rates. Fixed rate: the peg as a horizontal line and the intervention shift. Consequences: AD/AS with AD and, where relevant, SRAS shifts.\",\"calc\":\"SL and HL (guide PDF p. 54): converting a price between currencies, and the percentage change in a currency’s value from data. Show the working and the base used. Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 (guide PDF p. 66).\",\"apply\":\"Use the text’s data: which currency moved, by how much, and how dependent the country is on imports or foreign-currency debt.\",\"eval\":\"Condition the outcome on elasticities, import dependence, spare capacity and time; separate winners from losers.\",\"errors\":[\"Labelling the vertical axis ‘price’ without naming the currencies\",\"Reversing appreciation and depreciation, or calling a market fall a devaluation\",\"Explaining moves only through trade, ignoring capital flows\",\"Claiming a depreciation improves the current account without conditions\"],\"strong\":[\"Name the currency pair and which currency is priced\",\"Explain each shift through who is buying or selling\",\"Trace consequences through AD and SRAS\",\"Judge ‘good’ or ‘bad’ only by stakeholder and context\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data: the exchange rate changes from 1 Brazilian real = 0.20 US dollars to 1 real = 0.18 US dollars. (i) Calculate the percentage change in the value of the real. (ii) Calculate the price in reals of a US machine costing $9,000 after the change.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, how a central bank could prevent its fixed exchange rate from falling when demand for its exports declines.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the consequences of a large depreciation for an economy that imports most of its energy.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"why\":\"A rate change moves the currency, giving monetary policy a second route to AD and inflation.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Relative inflation shifts currency demand and supply, and depreciation feeds back into inflation.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"why\":\"A monetary union gives up the exchange rate between members.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"Whether a depreciation raises export earnings and cuts import spending depends on price elasticity of demand.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"why\":\"Because the balance of payments records the flows that make up currency demand and supply, and its HL analysis relies on how exchange rates respond.\"},\"ia\":\"An article on a sharp currency move, a central bank intervening in the currency market, or a change to a peg works well. Identify the factor behind the move, then use change or intervention to frame its effects on named stakeholders.\",\"ee\":\"How far have changes in a chosen currency’s exchange rate against the US dollar been passed through to the local price of a specific import, such as fuel, over a defined period?\",\"tok\":\"Commentators call a falling currency ‘weak’; to what extent does the language used to describe exchange rate changes shape judgements about them?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students shift the foreign exchange market correctly, calculate conversions and percentage changes, and evaluate a change for stakeholders.\",\"starter\":\"£100 for a New York trip; the pound falls from $1.30 to $1.20. How many fewer dollars, and who at home is pleased?\",\"activity\":\"Currency market simulation: half the class buy exports and assets, half are importers; announce events and have students shift demand and supply cards on a large diagram.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a strong currency good? Answer for exporters, importers, tourists and dollar borrowers.\",\"check\":\"Mini whiteboards: for five events, write D or S, left or right, and appreciate or depreciate.\",\"extension\":\"HL: research a country that abandoned a peg and explain what made the peg hard to hold.\",\"homework\":\"Four currency calculations and one paragraph on a depreciation using AD/AS.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define appreciation, depreciation, devaluation and revaluation and match each to its system.\",\"I can explain who demands and supplies a currency and what shifts each curve.\",\"I can draw the foreign exchange market with correct axes, and do both currency calculations.\",\"I can explain how a peg is held and what over- and undervaluation mean.\",\"I can analyse and evaluate a currency change’s effects on the economy and its stakeholders.\",\"I can evaluate fixed against floating systems (HL only).\",\"I can explain without notes why higher relative interest rates tend to cause appreciation.\"]},\"4.6\":{\"code\":\"4.6\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"The balance of payments records every transaction between a country’s residents and the rest of the world over a period. Money in is a credit; money out is a debit. Because every purchase must be paid for, the accounts balance overall: a current account deficit is matched by a capital and financial account surplus. The real questions are why a deficit arises, how it is financed, and whether it can last.\",\"why\":\"It shows how a country pays its way in the world, and a persistent imbalance can put pressure on the exchange rate, interest rates and debt.\",\"q\":\"If a country’s international accounts must balance, what does it mean to say it has a ‘deficit’?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Appreciation, depreciation and what shifts the demand for and supply of a currency\",\"sub\":\"4.5\"},{\"t\":\"GDP and GNI: income earned abroad and paid to foreigners\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"Price elasticity of demand and total spending\",\"sub\":\"2.5\"},{\"t\":\"Contractionary fiscal and monetary policy\",\"sub\":\"3.6\"},{\"t\":\"Supply-side policies and their time lags\",\"sub\":\"3.7\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Define the balance of payments, credits, debits, and a surplus or deficit on an account.\",\"List the components of the current, capital and financial accounts named in the guide.\",\"State the Marshall–Lerner condition (HL only).\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why a current account deficit is matched by a capital and financial account surplus.\",\"Explain how the current and financial accounts relate to the exchange rate (HL only).\"],\"apply\":[\"Place a described transaction in the correct account with the correct sign.\",\"Apply the J-curve to a country that has recently depreciated (HL only).\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse the implications of a persistent current account deficit (HL only).\",\"Distinguish expenditure-switching from expenditure-reducing policies (HL only).\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate policies to correct a persistent current account deficit (HL only).\",\"Evaluate the implications of a persistent current account surplus (HL only).\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate the current account balance, or a missing account balance, from data.\"],\"draw\":[\"Draw a foreign exchange diagram linking a current account deficit to the exchange rate (HL only).\",\"Draw the J-curve, marking where the Marshall–Lerner condition begins to hold (HL only).\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret balance of payments data to find what drives a deficit and how it is financed.\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend a policy to reduce a persistent current account deficit, given data on its causes, inflation and unemployment.\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"A record of flows in and out\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The balance of payments records all transactions between a country’s residents and the rest of the world over a period, such as a quarter or a year. A credit is a transaction that brings money in, such as an export sale or a foreign firm buying a local factory. A debit sends money out, such as buying imports or investing abroad. An account is in surplus when its credits exceed its debits and in deficit when debits exceed credits (guide PDF p. 55). These are flows over a period, not stocks.\"},{\"h\":\"Three accounts\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The guide divides the balance of payments into three accounts (guide PDF p. 55). The current account records trade and income flows. The capital account is small for most countries. The financial account records the buying and selling of assets, so it shows how the current account is financed. Official statistics call ‘income’ primary income and ‘current transfers’ secondary income.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Account\",\"Components\",\"Credit example\",\"Debit example\"],\"rows\":[[\"Current\",\"Trade in goods\",\"Car exports\",\"Oil imports\"],[\"Current\",\"Trade in services\",\"Foreign tourists’ spending\",\"Residents’ holidays abroad\"],[\"Current\",\"Income\",\"Profits earned on assets abroad\",\"Dividends paid to foreign owners\"],[\"Current\",\"Current transfers\",\"Remittances received\",\"Remittances sent abroad\"],[\"Capital\",\"Capital transfers; non-produced, non-financial assets\",\"Debt forgiveness received\",\"A grant for another country’s infrastructure\"],[\"Financial\",\"FDI; portfolio investment; reserve assets; official borrowing\",\"Foreigners buy local bonds; the central bank runs down reserves\",\"Local firms invest abroad; reserves are added\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why the accounts balance\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Every international transaction has two sides. If a country’s current payments abroad exceed its current receipts, the extra spending still has to be paid for: by selling assets to foreigners, by borrowing abroad, or by running down reserves. Each of these is a financial account credit. So the current, capital and financial accounts sum to zero, with a balancing item for errors and omissions (guide PDF p. 55). Credits are matched by debits, and a deficit on one account by surpluses elsewhere.\",\"because\":[\"Imports exceed exports: a current account deficit\",\"The extra imports must still be paid for\",\"The country sells assets, borrows or uses reserves\",\"These inflows are financial account credits\",\"So the accounts together sum to zero\"]},{\"h\":\"A worked calculation\",\"kind\":\"example\",\"p\":\"Illustrative data, $bn: goods exports 420, goods imports 510, service exports 180, service imports 120, net income −15, net current transfers +25. Balance of trade in goods = 420 − 510 = −90. Balance of trade in services = 180 − 120 = +60. Current account = −90 + 60 − 15 + 25 = −20. If the capital account is +5 and errors and omissions are zero, the financial account must be +15. Interpretation: a goods deficit, partly offset by services, financed by net inflows. This calculation is listed for SL and HL (guide PDF p. 55).\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: when a deficit persists\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A current account deficit is financed each period; the concern is the stock of liabilities that builds up if it persists (guide PDF pp. 55–56). Whether it matters depends on its size relative to GDP, its cause and its financing. A deficit that pays for imported machinery may raise future output; one that pays for a consumption boom with short-term borrowing is riskier.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Implication\",\"How a persistent deficit can cause it\"],\"rows\":[[\"Exchange rate\",\"Downward pressure, or falling reserves under a peg\"],[\"Interest rates\",\"Higher rates may be needed to attract financing\"],[\"Foreign ownership\",\"Asset sales raise foreign ownership; profits then flow abroad\"],[\"Debt\",\"Borrowing raises external debt and interest payments\"],[\"Credit ratings\",\"Rising debt risks downgrades and dearer borrowing\"],[\"Demand management\",\"AD may have to be restrained to cut imports\"],[\"Economic growth\",\"Correction can slow growth\"]]}},{\"h\":\"HL only: when a surplus persists\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"A persistent surplus is not in itself a sign of strength (guide PDF p. 56). It means the country spends less than its income, so domestic consumption and investment are lower than its income would allow, and savings flow abroad. Under floating, it pushes the currency up, eroding export competitiveness over time; under a peg, the central bank piles up reserves. Strong export demand adds to AD and can cause inflation near full capacity. Export industries support employment, but depend on foreign demand, and one country’s surplus is another’s deficit, which can provoke trade tensions.\"},{\"h\":\"HL only: correcting a deficit\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Expenditure-switching policies move spending from imports to domestic output, through a depreciation or devaluation, or through protection. Expenditure-reducing policies cut total spending, including on imports, through contractionary fiscal or monetary policy. Supply-side policies raise productivity and quality so exports compete better over time. How effective each is depends on the deficit’s cause, the time available and the costs the government will accept (guide PDF p. 56).\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Method\",\"Main limitation\"],\"rows\":[[\"Depreciation or devaluation\",\"Needs the Marshall–Lerner condition, after a J-curve delay; raises inflation\"],[\"Tariffs, quotas, subsidies\",\"Retaliation, WTO commitments, shelters inefficient firms\"],[\"Contractionary fiscal or monetary policy\",\"Lower growth and jobs; higher rates may cause an offsetting appreciation\"],[\"Supply-side policies\",\"Long lags and uncertain effects\"]]}}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"HL only: a depreciation when trade is price-inelastic in the short run (the J-curve)\",\"cause\":\"The currency depreciates.\",\"mechanism\":\"Import prices rise at home and export prices fall abroad, but contracts, habits and limited capacity mean volumes barely change at first.\",\"immediate\":\"More domestic currency is spent on roughly the same imports, so the current account worsens.\",\"secondary\":\"Over months, foreign buyers switch to the cheaper exports and domestic buyers find substitutes for dearer imports.\",\"longrun\":\"Once |PEDx| + |PEDm| > 1 (the Marshall–Lerner condition), the current account improves, possibly beyond its starting point.\",\"depends\":\"How fast elasticities rise, spare export capacity, the invoicing currency, and whether import inflation erodes the gain.\"},{\"t\":\"HL only: a persistent deficit financed by short-term borrowing\",\"cause\":\"A consumption boom pulls in imports year after year.\",\"mechanism\":\"The deficit is financed by foreign purchases of short-term bonds and bank lending, attracted by high interest rates.\",\"immediate\":\"Inflows hold the currency up while external debt rises.\",\"secondary\":\"Interest payments abroad grow, worsening the income account; rating agencies may downgrade the country.\",\"longrun\":\"If investors lose confidence, inflows stop; the currency falls, interest rates may have to rise and contractionary policy may be forced, cutting growth.\",\"depends\":\"The deficit’s size relative to GDP, the currency of the debt, the share of stable FDI, and policy credibility.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The balance of payments identity\",\"dg\":\"bop\",\"purpose\":\"Organises international transactions and shows how the accounts are linked.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Every transaction has a matching credit and debit\",\"Errors and omissions capture measurement gaps\"],\"mechanism\":\"Credits bring money in and debits send it out; a current account deficit is financed by net inflows on the capital and financial accounts, so the total is zero.\",\"prediction\":\"A country with a current account deficit shows net asset sales, net borrowing or falling reserves.\",\"application\":\"Reading data to see what drives a deficit and how it is financed.\",\"limitations\":[\"It is an identity: it shows that the accounts balance, not why a deficit arises\",\"Errors and omissions can be large\"],\"real\":\"The United States has run current account deficits in most years since the early 1980s, financed by large foreign purchases of US assets and helped by the dollar’s reserve-currency role.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating a current account deficit as unfinanced, or as a stock of debt rather than a flow.\",\"exam\":\"Paper 2: calculate a balance and explain it. Paper 1 (a): explain why a current account deficit is matched by a financial account surplus.\",\"deeper\":\"The identity has a macroeconomic twin: the current account balance equals national saving minus domestic investment. A deficit means a country invests more than it saves and borrows the difference from abroad. This is why a large government budget deficit, which lowers national saving, can be linked to a current account deficit, the ‘twin deficits’ idea, though evidence on how tight the link is remains mixed.\"},{\"model\":\"HL only: the current account, the financial account and the exchange rate\",\"dg\":\"fx\",\"purpose\":\"Shows how imbalances put pressure on a floating exchange rate (guide PDF p. 55).\",\"assumptions\":[\"The exchange rate floats\",\"Import spending supplies the currency; export earnings demand it\",\"Financial flows respond to returns and confidence\"],\"mechanism\":\"More import spending shifts the currency’s supply right, causing depreciation; more financial inflows shift demand right, causing appreciation. The two can offset each other.\",\"prediction\":\"A current account deficit tends to depreciate the currency unless financial inflows offset it; strong inflows can appreciate it and widen the deficit.\",\"application\":\"Explaining why a deficit country’s currency can be stable for years, then fall sharply when inflows reverse.\",\"limitations\":[\"Many flows move at once, so the net effect is an empirical question\",\"Under a peg, pressure shows up in reserves rather than the rate\"],\"real\":\"In 2013, signals that the US Federal Reserve would slow its bond purchases triggered capital outflows from several emerging economies with current account deficits, including India, and their currencies fell.\",\"misconception\":\"Explaining the exchange rate only through the current account, ignoring financial flows.\",\"exam\":\"HL Paper 1 or Paper 2: draw the foreign exchange market with the relevant shift and link it to the account named in the context.\",\"deeper\":\"Economists often judge a deficit partly by its financing. FDI tends to be long term and hard to withdraw; portfolio and bank flows can reverse quickly. Research on ‘sudden stops’, associated with Guillermo Calvo, shows that abrupt reversals of capital inflows can force a rapid and painful current account adjustment, as imports and output fall together. Deficits financed mainly by FDI are therefore usually seen as less risky.\"},{\"model\":\"HL only: the Marshall–Lerner condition and the J-curve\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains when, and how quickly, a depreciation or devaluation improves the current account (guide PDF p. 56).\",\"assumptions\":[\"Trade starts roughly balanced\",\"Exporters can supply more without raising prices\",\"Other influences on trade are unchanged\"],\"mechanism\":\"A depreciation cuts export prices abroad and raises import prices at home. Import spending falls only if volumes fall proportionally more than the price rises. Taking exports and imports together, the balance improves if |PEDx| + |PEDm| > 1.\",\"prediction\":\"Because elasticities are low at first and higher later, the balance first worsens, then improves: the J-curve, drawn with the current account balance against time.\",\"application\":\"Judging whether a depreciation will help, and why early data may look worse.\",\"limitations\":[\"Elasticities are hard to estimate and change over time\",\"Import-led inflation can erode the competitive gain\",\"Rising output raises imports, which the model ignores\"],\"real\":\"After a sharp depreciation, early trade data can show a larger import bill before volumes adjust, which is one reason policymakers tend to wait before judging its effect.\",\"misconception\":\"Stating the condition without ‘sum’ and ‘absolute values’, or assuming an immediate improvement.\",\"exam\":\"HL Paper 1 (b), Paper 2 (g) or Paper 3: draw the J-curve with zero marked, the depreciation, the dip and the point where the condition is met; use it to evaluate depreciation as a policy.\",\"deeper\":\"The textbook condition assumes trade starts in balance and supply is perfectly elastic. With an initial deficit, the required elasticities are somewhat higher. Many exporters also ‘price to market’, holding foreign-currency prices steady and taking higher margins, which slows the volume response. Research on invoicing suggests that when trade is priced in US dollars, export volumes may respond weakly to a country’s own depreciation.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The Marshall–Lerner condition and J-curve (HL only) predict that a depreciation first worsens, then improves, the current account as trade becomes more price-elastic.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies find J-curve patterns in some countries and periods but not others, and estimated trade elasticities vary widely. Improvements after large depreciations often coincide with falling domestic demand, making the price effect hard to isolate.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that the mechanism is real but often swamped by inflation, dollar invoicing, income changes and capital flows. Some economists therefore see saving and investment decisions, not the exchange rate, as the main driver of the current account.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Two sign conventions\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Classroom tables usually record the financial account as inflows minus outflows, so a current account deficit is matched by a positive financial account. Official statistics following IMF standards record the financial account as the net acquisition of foreign assets minus the net incurrence of liabilities. On that convention, a deficit country shows a negative financial account, meaning net borrowing from abroad. The economics is identical; only the presentation differs. Check the convention before interpreting a real figure.\"},{\"h\":\"Flows, stocks and how long a deficit can last\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"A current account deficit is a flow. The stock that builds up is the net international investment position: foreign assets owned by residents minus domestic assets owned by foreigners. A country can run deficits for decades if foreigners keep wanting its assets, as with the United States. The risk rises when the stock grows faster than income, is owed in foreign currency, or is held by investors who can sell quickly. This is why economists look at financing and debt, not the deficit alone.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of the balance of payments as a student’s bank statement for a year. Money in: wages, a gift, a loan. Money out: rent, food, a laptop. If spending on goods and services exceeds earnings, the gap has to be covered by borrowing, selling something or using savings. The current account is the everyday earning and spending; the financial account shows how any gap is covered. So the statement adds up by construction, but whether the gap is a problem depends on what it paid for and how it was covered.\",\"exam\":\"Define the balance of payments and place each item in the right account and component with the right sign. In calculations, show each sub-balance and state whether the result is a surplus or deficit. Explain interdependence: a current account deficit is financed by a capital and financial account surplus. At HL, analyse a persistent deficit through a chain, for example borrowing → rising external debt → higher interest payments → a worse income balance. For correction, distinguish switching, reducing and supply-side policies, and evaluate using the Marshall–Lerner condition, the J-curve, time lags, retaliation and the costs to growth and jobs. Condition the verdict on the deficit’s size, cause and financing.\",\"deep\":\"The balance of payments is an accounting identity, but behind it lies a macroeconomic one: the current account equals national saving minus domestic investment. A deficit means a country spends more than its income and borrows, or sells assets, to cover the gap. Whether that is a problem is a question about time. Borrowing to finance investment that raises future output can be sensible, like a firm borrowing to build a factory; borrowing for consumption is riskier. The exchange rate links the accounts: capital inflows push the currency up, which can widen the deficit, and a reversal can force sudden adjustment. Correction faces the usual macroeconomic trade-off: fast routes cut demand, output and jobs, while supply-side routes take years to work.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"here\":\"A deficit is financed by other countries’ willingness to buy the country’s assets.\",\"tension\":\"Foreign financing supports spending now but exposes the economy to changes in investor confidence.\",\"ia\":\"A widening current account deficit: how, and by whom, is it financed?\"},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"here\":\"Imbalances build over time, and the J-curve shows effects changing with the time horizon (HL only).\",\"tension\":\"Fast corrections hurt growth; slow ones leave the imbalance in place longer.\",\"ia\":\"A depreciation followed by weak trade data: short run versus long run.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"A deficit can raise living standards now, while debt and interest payments may lower them later.\",\"tension\":\"Cutting a deficit through lower spending can reduce incomes and jobs now.\",\"ia\":\"Austerity or IMF support after a balance of payments crisis, judged by its effects on households.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why can some countries run current account deficits for decades while others face a crisis within a few years?\",\"expect\":\"You might expect any long-running deficit to end in crisis.\",\"model\":\"A deficit must be financed; trouble is predicted when financing is short term, debt is in foreign currency or confidence falls (HL).\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests deficits financed by FDI, or by countries whose assets are in strong demand, last longer; crises often follow rapid build-ups of short-term foreign-currency debt.\",\"match\":\"Broadly yes: financing and confidence, not the deficit itself, seem to decide the outcome.\",\"assume\":\"That investors’ willingness to hold a country’s assets depends on its prospects, institutions and debt currency.\",\"change\":\"A shift in confidence or a rise in global interest rates.\",\"policy\":\"Monitor the deficit relative to GDP, its financing mix and the currency of external debt.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why a current account deficit must be matched by a surplus on the capital and financial accounts.\",\"check\":[\"Defines a current account deficit\",\"The extra imports and payments must still be paid for\",\"Means of payment: selling assets, borrowing abroad or using reserves\",\"These are financial account credits, so the accounts sum to zero apart from errors and omissions\"]},{\"prompt\":\"HL only: Explain, using a J-curve, why a depreciation may worsen the current account before improving it.\",\"check\":[\"Axes: current account balance (zero marked) against time\",\"At first volumes respond little, so more is spent on imports and the balance worsens\",\"Over time demand becomes more elastic\",\"Once |PEDx| + |PEDm| > 1, the balance improves\",\"Inflation or dollar invoicing can weaken the effect\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Where is a remittance received from a family member working abroad recorded?\",\"a\":\"The current account, as a current transfer (a credit).\"},{\"q\":\"Where is a foreign firm’s purchase of a controlling stake in a local company recorded?\",\"a\":\"The financial account, as FDI (a credit).\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: goods −40, services +15, net income −10, net current transfers +5. What is the current account balance?\",\"a\":\"−40 + 15 − 10 + 5 = −30, a deficit of 30.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does the balance of payments sum to zero?\",\"a\":\"Every transaction is paid for, so a current account deficit is financed by net capital and financial inflows.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: State the Marshall–Lerner condition.\",\"a\":\"A depreciation improves the current account if the sum of the absolute values of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceeds one.\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: Give one expenditure-switching and one expenditure-reducing policy.\",\"a\":\"Switching: a devaluation or a tariff. Reducing: higher taxes, lower government spending or higher interest rates.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A US firm pays a Singapore bank for financial advice. How is this recorded in Singapore’s balance of payments?\",\"opts\":[\"A financial account credit, because a bank is involved\",\"A current account credit, as an export of services\",\"A capital account credit, as a capital transfer\",\"A current account debit, because money crosses borders\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Selling a service to a non-resident is a service export, a current account credit. The financial account records trade in assets, not payment for a service, even when a bank provides it.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-09\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: Country P has a current account deficit of $30 bn and a capital account surplus of $2 bn; errors and omissions are zero. Recording the financial account as inflows minus outflows, what must be true?\",\"opts\":[\"A financial account deficit of $28 bn\",\"A financial account surplus of $28 bn\",\"A financial account surplus of $32 bn\",\"Nothing, because the deficit may be unpaid\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The accounts sum to zero: −30 + 2 + FA = 0, so FA = +28 on the inflows-minus-outflows convention. The last option reflects the belief that a deficit can go unfinanced; every import has to be paid for somehow.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-09\"},{\"q\":\"HL only: In the first year after a depreciation, |PED| is 0.3 for exports and 0.5 for imports. What is the most likely effect on the current account that year?\",\"opts\":[\"It improves, because exports are cheaper for foreigners\",\"It worsens, because the elasticities sum to less than one\",\"It improves, because each elasticity is below one\",\"No effect, because elasticities matter only in the long run\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"0.3 + 0.5 = 0.8 < 1, so the Marshall–Lerner condition fails and the balance worsens, as in the J-curve’s dip. Cheaper exports are tempting, but with weak volume responses the price effect dominates.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-07\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2: calculate the current account or a missing balance from a table, and explain it\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain why a current account deficit is matched by a financial account surplus\",\"HL: evaluate the implications of a persistent deficit or surplus for a named economy\",\"HL: evaluate policies to correct a deficit; in Paper 3, part (b) asks students to recommend (guide PDF p. 71)\"],\"ct\":[\"define\",\"calculate\",\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"recommend\"],\"dg\":\"None for the accounts themselves. HL only: a foreign exchange diagram (rising import spending shifts the currency’s supply right; financial inflows shift demand right), and a J-curve with the current account against time, zero marked, the depreciation and the point where Marshall–Lerner holds.\",\"calc\":\"SL and HL: elements of the balance of payments from data, such as the current account or a missing financial account figure (guide PDF p. 55). Testing Marshall–Lerner with given elasticities is an HL application, not a listed calculation.\",\"apply\":\"Use the text’s figures to find which component drives the deficit and how it is financed, and note the currency regime and debt position.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh the deficit’s size, cause, financing and time horizon; for policies, weigh effectiveness against costs to growth, jobs and inflation.\",\"errors\":[\"Recording services or income in the capital or financial account\",\"Treating a deficit as unfinanced or as a stock of debt\",\"Stating Marshall–Lerner without ‘sum’ and ‘absolute values’\",\"Recommending a depreciation or tariff without elasticities, retaliation or inflation\"],\"strong\":[\"Show every sub-balance and interpret the sign\",\"Link the accounts: deficit → financing → consequences\",\"Use the J-curve to condition claims about depreciation (HL)\",\"Match the correction policy to the deficit’s cause\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (b)\",\"q\":\"Illustrative data ($ bn): goods exports 250, goods imports 310, service exports 95, service imports 60, net income −12, net current transfers +7, capital account +3. (i) Calculate the current account balance. (ii) Calculate the financial account balance, assuming errors and omissions are zero.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain why a country with a current account deficit must have a surplus on its combined capital and financial accounts.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"HL: Using the information provided and your knowledge of economics, recommend a policy that a government facing a persistent current account deficit, rising inflation and large external debt should use to reduce the deficit.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Current and financial account flows are the demand for and supply of the currency.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"why\":\"Income flows in the current account explain the gap between GDP and GNI.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"why\":\"Contractionary fiscal policy is an expenditure-reducing way to cut imports (HL only).\"},{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"The Marshall–Lerner condition is a statement about price elasticities of demand (HL only).\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"why\":\"Indebtedness, capital flight and dependence on primary production, all visible in the balance of payments, are barriers to growth and development.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"why\":\"Because the unit turns from how economies trade and finance themselves to whether growth can be sustained, and indebtedness and dependence on primary exports return as development barriers in 4.9.\"},\"ia\":\"An article about a widening current account deficit, a credit rating downgrade, a surge in remittances or a request for IMF support works well. Identify the account and component involved, then frame the analysis through interdependence or economic well-being.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has a chosen country’s current account deficit been financed by FDI rather than portfolio or debt flows over a defined period, and what does this suggest about its sustainability?\",\"tok\":\"The balance of payments sums to zero by construction; what kind of knowledge about an economy can an identity that cannot be wrong provide?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students classify transactions, calculate balances and, at HL, evaluate the implications and correction of persistent imbalances.\",\"starter\":\"Sort ten transaction cards (a hotel bill, a remittance, a factory purchase, a bond sale, a dividend) into accounts, marking each credit or debit.\",\"activity\":\"Build a balance of payments step by step from illustrative data; HL groups then plot a J-curve timeline for a depreciation, labelling each phase.\",\"discussion\":\"Is a current account deficit a sign of weakness or strength? Argue both using how it is financed.\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: SL, where is a remittance recorded and why? HL, state Marshall–Lerner precisely and explain the J-curve dip.\",\"extension\":\"Find a country’s latest balance of payments release and identify what drives its current account.\",\"homework\":\"Two balance of payments calculations, plus (HL) a paragraph evaluating one way to correct a deficit.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define the key terms and place a transaction in the correct account with the correct sign.\",\"I can calculate the current account and a missing account balance.\",\"I can explain why a current account deficit is matched by a financial account surplus.\",\"I can link the current and financial accounts to the exchange rate with a diagram (HL only).\",\"I can analyse the implications of persistent deficits and surpluses (HL only).\",\"I can use Marshall–Lerner and the J-curve to evaluate policies to correct a deficit (HL only).\",\"I can explain without notes why a deficit is a financed flow, not an unpaid debt.\"]},\"4.7\":{\"code\":\"4.7\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Sustainable development asks whether today’s progress can last. An economy can raise output by running down forests, soils and a stable climate, but that borrows from people not yet born. Development is sustainable when it meets present needs without shrinking the options of future generations. The 17 Sustainable Development Goals turn this into shared targets, some of which compete.\",\"why\":\"Governments must decide how much present consumption to give up to protect future capacity, and the poorest households usually feel environmental damage first.\",\"q\":\"Can a low-income country cut poverty quickly without damaging the environment its people depend on?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Scarcity, opportunity cost and the PPC\",\"sub\":\"1.1\"},{\"t\":\"Negative externalities and welfare loss\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Common pool resources and the tragedy of the commons\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"The trade-off between high growth and environmental sustainability\",\"sub\":\"3.3\"},{\"t\":\"Absolute and relative poverty\",\"sub\":\"3.4\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"State the Brundtland definition of sustainable development and identify its intergenerational clause\",\"Outline what the SDGs are, who adopted them and the period they cover\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why sustainable development has economic, social and environmental dimensions\",\"Explain why some SDGs reinforce each other while others involve trade-offs\"],\"apply\":[\"Link a named SDG to a specific economic mechanism, such as a negative externality\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse why unregulated markets can produce unsustainable outcomes\",\"Analyse the two-way relationship between poverty and environmental degradation (HL only; guide PDF p. 57)\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate how far high growth and environmental sustainability conflict in a given context (the AO3 trade-off in 3.3; guide PDF p. 45)\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw a negative production externality diagram and show why the welfare loss is also a future cost\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret paired growth and emissions data to identify relative or absolute decoupling\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"What makes development sustainable\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The standard definition comes from the Brundtland Commission’s 1987 report, Our Common Future: “development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs”. The guide’s key concept of sustainability rests on the same intergenerational idea (guide PDF p. 19). “Compromising” is about capacity: a society may use resources, but it should pass on enough natural, physical, human and social capital for later generations to do as well. The idea has economic, social and environmental dimensions, so “sustainable” means neither “financially viable” nor simply “green”.\"},{\"h\":\"The Sustainable Development Goals\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"In 2015 all UN member states adopted 17 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) with a target date of 2030. They replaced the eight Millennium Development Goals (2000–2015), which focused on poverty, health and education in developing countries. The SDGs apply to every country and add goals on climate, oceans, land, cities and institutions. The guide sets the SDGs at AO2 in 4.7, so the task is to explain goals and their economic links; evaluating progress towards selected goals in two or more countries belongs to 4.10 (guide PDF pp. 57, 59).\"},{\"h\":\"Goals that pull together, goals that pull apart\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"The SDGs are objectives with opportunity costs, not a list to recite. Resources spent on one goal are unavailable for another, and some goals conflict directly; others reinforce each other. A strong answer names two goals, the mechanism linking them and the conditions that make the link positive or negative, such as the energy source used or crop yields on existing land.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Pair of goals\",\"Link\",\"Mechanism\"],\"rows\":[[\"SDG 7 Clean energy + SDG 3 Good health\",\"Synergy\",\"Clean cooking fuel cuts indoor smoke\"],[\"SDG 2 Zero hunger + SDG 15 Life on land\",\"Possible trade-off\",\"Clearing forest for farmland raises food but destroys habitat\"],[\"SDG 8 Growth + SDG 13 Climate action\",\"Possible trade-off\",\"Fossil-fuel growth raises emissions unless decoupled\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why markets can produce unsustainable outcomes\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Unsustainable resource use is usually a market failure (2.8). When producers do not pay for the damage they cause, marginal private cost is below marginal social cost and output is too high. When a resource such as a fishery is rivalrous but no one can be excluded, each user gains from taking more today. GDP then records the output as income without subtracting the natural capital that has gone.\",\"because\":[\"The resource has no owner and no price\",\"each user bears little of the depletion cost\",\"so each takes more now\",\"so the stock falls faster than it regenerates\",\"so future output and incomes fall\"]},{\"h\":\"Poverty and sustainability: a two-way relationship (HL only)\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"HL only (guide PDF p. 57). Poverty can cause environmental damage: households with no savings, insecure land rights and no access to credit or clean energy may cut forests for fuel or overgraze shared land, because survival now outweighs yields later. Damage can deepen poverty: poor households depend directly on soil, water and forests and have little insurance against floods or drought. The link also works in reverse as incomes rise: richer households use more energy and goods, so poverty reduction can raise environmental pressure unless technology and policy change.\",\"because\":[\"Low income, no credit\",\"present needs dominate\",\"local resources are overused\",\"yields fall and shocks hit harder\",\"income falls: the trap closes\"]},{\"h\":\"Growth and sustainability: a conflict with conditions\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"Growth conflicts with sustainability when extra output uses resources and creates pollution faster than the environment can absorb them. The conflict is not fixed. Relative decoupling means emissions grow more slowly than real GDP; absolute decoupling means emissions fall while real GDP rises. Evidence suggests some high-income economies have achieved absolute decoupling of emissions produced within their borders, while consumption-based measures, which include emissions embodied in imports, show smaller improvements. Whether decoupling can be fast enough is disputed.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Growth based on resource extraction\",\"cause\":\"A government expands logging and mining concessions to raise exports and growth.\",\"mechanism\":\"Firms do not pay the full cost of deforestation and water pollution, so extraction exceeds the social optimum.\",\"immediate\":\"Real GDP, export earnings and tax revenue rise.\",\"secondary\":\"Downstream farmers and fishing communities face higher costs; biodiversity and carbon storage are lost.\",\"longrun\":\"If revenue is consumed rather than reinvested, future capacity falls: the PPC grows more slowly or shifts inwards.\",\"depends\":\"Whether rents are taxed and reinvested, whether extraction is regulated, how fast the resource regenerates, and institutional quality.\"},{\"t\":\"Subsidised clean cooking fuel for poor rural households (HL only)\",\"cause\":\"A government subsidises bottled gas for low-income households that cook with collected wood.\",\"mechanism\":\"The subsidy lowers the private cost of clean fuel relative to wood.\",\"immediate\":\"Where refills are affordable, indoor smoke and wood collection fall.\",\"secondary\":\"Health may improve, especially for women and children; pressure on nearby forests eases.\",\"longrun\":\"Sustained use can weaken a poverty–environment trap; if refills stay unaffordable, households may return to wood.\",\"depends\":\"Refill prices relative to income, supply networks, habits, and whether wood was cut faster than it regrows.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Negative externalities and common pool resources\",\"dg\":\"negprod\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why unregulated markets use the environment beyond the socially optimal level.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Producers ignore costs they do not pay\",\"Property rights over the environment are absent or unenforced\"],\"mechanism\":\"With MSC above MPC, market output exceeds the optimum and a welfare loss results. For common pool resources, rivalry plus non-excludability leads to depletion.\",\"prediction\":\"Without intervention, pollution and depletion exceed the social optimum, and part of the cost falls on future generations.\",\"application\":\"Emissions, overfishing and deforestation; linking SDGs 13–15 to market failure.\",\"limitations\":[\"Valuing future damage needs a discount rate, which is a value judgement\",\"Global externalities need international cooperation the model does not supply\"],\"real\":\"Many open-access fisheries have been depleted; where catch limits were enforced, some stocks appear to have recovered.\",\"misconception\":\"Treating a common pool resource as an under-provided public good. It is rivalrous, so the problem is overuse (MIC-16).\",\"exam\":\"In Paper 1 (a) or Paper 2 (g), label MSC, MPC, both quantities and the welfare loss, then show that part of the loss falls on future generations.\",\"deeper\":\"Elinor Ostrom, awarded the Nobel memorial prize in 2009, documented communities that managed forests, irrigation and fisheries sustainably for long periods through their own rules, monitoring and sanctions. Her work shows the choice is not only between state control and private property, and warns that outside intervention can destroy local institutions that work. It is harder for global problems like climate change, where users are too many and distant to monitor each other.\"},{\"model\":\"The poverty–environment trap (HL only)\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"HL only. Explains why poverty and environmental degradation can reinforce each other.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Poor households have short time horizons and lack credit or secure land rights\",\"They depend directly on local natural resources\"],\"mechanism\":\"Low income leads to overuse of local resources; degradation lowers yields and raises exposure to shocks; income falls further.\",\"prediction\":\"Without an outside change — secure rights, credit, clean energy — some communities stay poor on degraded land.\",\"application\":\"Fuelwood collection, overgrazing, soil erosion, climate exposure in low-lying areas.\",\"limitations\":[\"Global emissions come mainly from higher-income consumption, not from the poorest\",\"Poor communities with secure rights often conserve resources well\"],\"real\":\"Bangladesh is highly exposed to floods and cyclones that can destroy poor households’ assets; its cyclone preparedness programmes are widely credited with reducing deaths (Real World case DEV-041).\",\"misconception\":\"Assuming poverty reduction is good for the environment by default: rising incomes can ease local pressure while raising energy use and emissions.\",\"exam\":\"HL only. Explain the loop in a named context, then ask which link a policy breaks.\",\"deeper\":\"Causality is hard to establish. Poor households may live on degraded land because it is cheap, rather than degrade it because they are poor. Studies generally find that secure land tenure is associated with more investment in trees and soil conservation in some settings, which fits the trap but also other explanations. Policy therefore works best when it is aimed at a specific, observed link.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"The environmental Kuznets curve hypothesis predicts that environmental damage rises with income during industrialisation and falls once societies can afford, and demand, cleaner production.\",\"evidence\":\"Studies generally find this pattern for some local pollutants, such as sulphur dioxide, in several high-income countries; evidence for carbon dioxide is much weaker, especially counting emissions embodied in imports.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that regulation, technology and prices clean the environment, and they respond to income only where institutions turn public demand into policy. “Green growth” supporters see decoupling as evidence that the two can coexist; critics argue it is too slow.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Discounting: how much is the future worth?\",\"tag\":\"Time horizon\",\"p\":\"Sustainability decisions compare costs today with benefits decades away using a discount rate, and the rate can decide the answer. A high rate makes damage in 2100 look trivial; a low rate makes strong action now worthwhile. The UK’s Stern Review (2006) used a very low rate of pure time preference on ethical grounds and argued for early, strong action. William Nordhaus argued that rates should reflect observed market returns and favoured a more gradual path. The disagreement is partly about ethics, not only about data.\"},{\"h\":\"Weak and strong sustainability\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"Weak sustainability holds that natural capital can be replaced by other capital if the total is maintained: an economy that depletes oil but invests the revenue in schools and roads may pass on as much capacity as it received. The World Bank’s adjusted net saving measure follows this view, subtracting depletion and pollution damage from saving. Strong sustainability holds that some natural capital, such as a stable climate or key ecosystems, has no adequate substitute and must be kept intact. Which view is right depends on substitutability, which is contested.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine a family that pays for one good year by selling the field it farms. This year looks excellent; next year there is nothing to farm. Sustainable development is the opposite: improve lives now in a way that leaves the next generation at least as able to improve theirs. That means looking after what produces future income — soils, forests, a stable climate, healthy and educated people, and working institutions. The SDGs are 17 goals countries agreed in 2015 to reach by 2030. Some help each other; others compete for scarce resources.\",\"exam\":\"Define sustainable development with its intergenerational clause. Then show the mechanism behind an unsustainable outcome: a negative externality (MSC above MPC, overproduction, welfare loss) or a common pool resource (rivalry plus non-excludability, depletion). Label the diagram fully and extend the analysis in time: part of the loss falls on future generations. When linking growth and sustainability, do not treat the conflict as fixed. Evaluate by asking whether growth is resource-intensive, whether externalities are priced, whether resource revenue is reinvested and who bears the costs. Name a real SDG and the mechanism it addresses; HL students can add the poverty–environment link.\",\"deep\":\"Behind the definition sits a capital approach: an economy’s capacity to generate well-being depends on its stocks of produced, human, social and natural capital, and sustainability requires that the capacity passed on does not fall. Whether that means keeping the total value of capital constant or keeping particular natural assets intact depends on substitutability. For a timber plantation, substitution is plausible; for a stable climate, it is doubtful. Two further problems complicate policy. Damage may cross thresholds that are hard to predict and impossible to reverse, which supports precaution. And comparing present with future costs requires a discount rate, which is partly an ethical choice. Economics can clarify the trade-offs and their likely size; the final judgement also involves values about fairness between generations.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Sustainability\",\"here\":\"The test is whether present activity reduces future generations’ capacity to meet their needs.\",\"tension\":\"Faster growth now can raise living standards while running down natural capital.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a new mine or coal-fired power plant, weighing present gains against future costs.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Fairness applies between generations and within them: the poorest often bear environmental damage first.\",\"tension\":\"Carbon pricing can hit poor households hardest in the short run while protecting future ones.\",\"ia\":\"A report on fuel-subsidy reform, analysed by who gains and loses now and later.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do some fast-growing economies see worsening environmental damage while others see improvements?\",\"expect\":\"Students often expect richer countries to be either greener or dirtier; each is partly right.\",\"model\":\"Externality models predict overuse unless costs are priced; the environmental Kuznets curve predicts damage first rises, then falls, with income.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests some local pollutants fell as incomes rose, often after regulation, while carbon emissions fell far less.\",\"match\":\"Partly. The pattern fits some pollutants, but income alone does not seem to be the driver.\",\"assume\":\"That demand for a clean environment rises with income, that governments respond, and that damage is reversible.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that emissions fell mainly because production moved abroad would weaken the optimistic reading.\",\"policy\":\"Pricing externalities and enforcing resource rights look more promising than waiting for incomes to rise.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why an economy can record economic growth while becoming less able to produce in the future.\",\"check\":[\"Real GDP counts output from depletion as income\",\"GDP does not subtract lost natural capital\",\"Negative externalities mean the resource is overused\",\"Future capacity depends on all forms of capital\",\"Depletion not offset by other investment reduces future capacity\"]},{\"prompt\":\"(HL only) Explain how poverty and environmental degradation can reinforce each other.\",\"check\":[\"Present needs dominate for poor households\",\"Lack of credit and secure rights limits conservation\",\"Dependence on local resources leads to overuse\",\"Degradation lowers yields and raises exposure to shocks\",\"Rising incomes may ease local pressure but raise emissions\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"State the Brundtland definition of sustainable development.\",\"a\":\"Development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs.\"},{\"q\":\"How many SDGs are there, and what are their adoption and target dates?\",\"a\":\"17, adopted by all UN member states in 2015, with a target date of 2030.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one pair of SDGs that can conflict, with the mechanism.\",\"a\":\"SDG 2 and SDG 15: clearing forest for farmland raises food output but destroys habitat.\"},{\"q\":\"Why is the market quantity in a negative production externality unsustainable as well as inefficient?\",\"a\":\"It exceeds the optimum, and part of the external cost falls on future generations.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: real GDP rises 5% while CO₂ emissions rise 2%. Relative or absolute decoupling?\",\"a\":\"Relative: emissions per unit of GDP fall, but total emissions still rise.\"},{\"q\":\"(HL only) Why might rising incomes ease pressure on local forests but raise emissions?\",\"a\":\"Households switch from fuelwood to purchased energy, but they also consume more energy and goods.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A student writes: “The project is sustainable because it will pay for itself within five years.” What is wrong?\",\"opts\":[\"Nothing: financial viability is what sustainability means in economics\",\"It ignores whether the project reduces future generations’ ability to meet their needs\",\"The payback period should be ten years\",\"Sustainability applies only to renewable energy\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Sustainability concerns intergenerational capacity. A project can repay its costs quickly while depleting a resource or imposing external costs. The last option is too narrow: sustainability also has social and economic dimensions.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"A coastal fishery is open to anyone with a boat. Which market failure best explains falling fish stocks?\",\"opts\":[\"A public good, under-provided by the market\",\"A common pool resource, rivalrous and non-excludable, and so overused\",\"A positive externality of consumption\",\"Asymmetric information between buyers and sellers\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Fish caught by one boat are unavailable to another, and no one can be excluded, so each user takes more before others do. A public good is non-rivalrous; its problem is under-provision, not overuse.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-16\"},{\"q\":\"(HL only) Income in a poor rural region doubles over 15 years. Which environmental prediction is most defensible?\",\"opts\":[\"It must improve, because poverty causes environmental damage\",\"It must worsen, because growth and sustainability are in fixed conflict\",\"Pressure on local resources may fall while energy use rises; the net effect depends on technology and policy\",\"Income and the environment are unrelated\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Higher incomes can loosen a poverty–environment trap but also raise energy use. Neither “must” option is justified; the outcome depends on energy sources, pricing of externalities and resource rights.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with an externality diagram, why a market outcome is unsustainable\",\"Paper 1 (b): discuss the growth–sustainability conflict in a named context (the AO3 trade-off in 3.3; guide PDF p. 45)\",\"Paper 2 (g): evaluate a policy in a developing economy with sustainability as one criterion\",\"HL only (guide PDF p. 57): explain the relationship between poverty and sustainability\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"distinguish\",\"analyse\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"No diagram is prescribed for 4.7, and its content is set at AO2 (guide PDF p. 57). Transfer the negative externality diagram, fully labelled, or a PPC showing reduced future capacity.\",\"calc\":\"None listed for 4.7 (guide PDF p. 57).\",\"apply\":\"Name the resource, the SDG and the stakeholders: for example, mangroves cleared for shrimp farms, SDGs 14–15, fishing households and exporters.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh short-run income against long-run capacity, who bears costs now and later, and whether institutions can price externalities or reinvest revenue.\",\"errors\":[\"Using “sustainable” to mean financially viable\",\"Listing SDGs without linking any goal to a mechanism\",\"Treating growth and sustainability as in fixed conflict, or as compatible with no conditions\",\"Calling a common pool resource a public good\"],\"strong\":[\"Define sustainable development with its intergenerational clause\",\"Use a Unit 2 diagram to carry a Unit 4 argument over time\",\"Link two named SDGs through a mechanism\",\"Reach a conditional judgement on when growth and sustainability conflict\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, why unregulated logging of a tropical forest may be inconsistent with sustainable development.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, discuss whether a low-income economy should give priority to rapid economic growth over environmental sustainability.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Using information from a text about an economy converting mangrove forests into shrimp farms, and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the expansion is consistent with sustainable development, weighing economic growth against environmental sustainability.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Externalities and common pool resources supply the mechanism behind unsustainable outcomes.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"why\":\"GDP’s failure to subtract depletion explains why growth figures can hide unsustainable development.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"why\":\"Growth versus environmental sustainability is one of the objective trade-offs evaluated there.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Progress towards selected SDGs in two or more countries is evaluated with development strategies.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"why\":\"Because judging whether development is happening, and whether it lasts, needs indicators, and 4.8 examines what each one captures and hides.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article about a project with present gains and future costs: a new mine, a dam, a fuel-subsidy change or a fishing quota. Sustainability is the natural lens; equity could frame a similar article in a different commentary.\",\"ee\":\"An investigation into whether a named resource-exporting economy has reinvested its resource revenue, using World Bank adjusted net saving data.\",\"tok\":\"If the value of future generations’ well-being depends on the discount rate an economist chooses, can economics answer sustainability questions, or only inform them?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can define sustainable development, link two SDGs through a mechanism and explain why growth and sustainability may conflict.\",\"starter\":\"Show forest-loss imagery beside a rising GDP chart: is this economy better off?\",\"activity\":\"Pairs draw an externality diagram for a named resource, then a PPC ten years on with revenue consumed or reinvested.\",\"discussion\":\"Which SDGs pull against each other in our country, and who should decide which comes first?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: the definition, plus one SDG trade-off and its mechanism.\",\"extension\":\"HL: map a poverty–environment trap for a named region and the link a policy could break.\",\"homework\":\"Retrieval quiz, plus 250 words evaluating one growth–sustainability trade-off in a named country.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define sustainable development, including its intergenerational clause\",\"I can explain what the SDGs are and why some conflict\",\"I can draw a negative externality diagram to show an unsustainable outcome\",\"I can explain why GDP growth can hide depletion of natural capital\",\"I can apply sustainability to a named project and its stakeholders\",\"I can analyse the two-way link between poverty and environmental degradation (HL only)\",\"I can evaluate whether growth and sustainability conflict, stating conditions\",\"I can explain sustainable development without notes\"]},\"4.8\":{\"code\":\"4.8\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Development means people living longer, healthier, better-educated and more secure lives, so no single number captures it. Single indicators measure one dimension clearly; composite indicators such as the HDI combine several into one index. Every indicator involves choices, so reading one well means knowing what it leaves out.\",\"why\":\"Governments and aid agencies use these indicators to set priorities and judge policy; a misleading indicator can send resources to the wrong problem.\",\"q\":\"Why can two countries with similar income per head have very different life expectancy and schooling?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"GDP, GNI and real values\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"GDP/GNI per capita at purchasing power parity\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"Limits of GDP as a measure of well-being\",\"sub\":\"3.1\"},{\"t\":\"The Gini coefficient and the Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"sub\":\"3.4\"},{\"t\":\"Percentage change\",\"sub\":\"1.2\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Identify single indicators in each category the guide lists: income, health and education, inequality, energy, environment\",\"State the components of the HDI, IHDI, GII and Happy Planet Index\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain why economic development is multidimensional\",\"Explain why PPP conversion is needed to compare incomes across countries\"],\"apply\":[\"Use data to compare the development of two economies across several indicators\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse the possible two-way relationship between economic growth and economic development\",\"Analyse what a gap between HDI and IHDI reveals about a country\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate composite indicators by their construction, not by the claim that they are imperfect\",\"Evaluate whether an indicator suits a particular purpose\"],\"calculate\":[\"Calculate a percentage change in an indicator, or the percentage loss from HDI to IHDI (skills from 1.2; no calculation is listed for 4.8)\"],\"draw\":[],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret a table of indicators for several countries, identifying where income and HDI rankings diverge\"],\"recommend\":[]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Growth and development: different, but linked\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Economic growth is an increase in real output. Economic development is a multidimensional improvement in living standards: health, knowledge, income, less poverty and inequality, and more freedom to choose. The guide sets the indicators at AO2, and their strengths and limitations and the growth–development link at AO3 (guide PDF p. 57). Growth can fund development through household incomes and tax revenue for clinics and schools; development can fund growth through healthier, more skilled workers. Neither link is assured: growth may bypass the poor or be absorbed by debt service, and educated workers may find no jobs.\",\"because\":[\"Real GDP rises\",\"incomes and tax revenue rise\",\"if spent on health, education and infrastructure\",\"human capital and productivity rise\",\"potential output grows\"]},{\"h\":\"Why income comparisons need PPP and GNI\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Income per head is the most common single indicator, but converting it at market exchange rates distorts comparisons. Non-traded services such as haircuts and local transport are usually cheaper in low-income countries, so a dollar converted at the market rate buys more there. Purchasing power parity (PPP) conversion values output at common prices, so each international dollar buys a similar bundle everywhere. GNI is often preferred to GDP because it counts income received by residents: where foreign-owned firms send large profits abroad, GDP can overstate national income.\"},{\"h\":\"Single indicators: five categories\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The guide groups single indicators into income; health and education; economic and social inequality; energy; and environment (guide PDF p. 57). Each measures one dimension clearly, is easy to explain and is widely available for comparison across countries and years. Each also has a blind spot: an average hides distribution, and years spent in school say nothing about how much was learned.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Category\",\"Examples\",\"Blind spot\"],\"rows\":[[\"Income\",\"GNI or GDP per capita at PPP\",\"Distribution; non-market and informal activity\"],[\"Health\",\"Life expectancy; infant and maternal mortality\",\"Quality of life while alive\"],[\"Education\",\"Mean and expected years of schooling; literacy\",\"Learning quality\"],[\"Inequality\",\"Gini coefficient; income share of the top 10%\",\"Which groups are behind\"],[\"Energy\",\"Access to electricity; energy use per head\",\"Reliability and cleanliness\"],[\"Environment\",\"CO₂ emissions per head; air pollution\",\"Emissions embodied in imports\"]]}},{\"h\":\"The Human Development Index\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The UN Development Programme’s HDI, first published in 1990, combines three dimensions: a long and healthy life (life expectancy at birth), knowledge (mean years of schooling of adults and expected years of schooling of children) and a decent standard of living (GNI per capita at PPP). Each is scaled between 0 and 1 using fixed goalposts. Income enters in logarithms, so an extra dollar counts for more in a poor country. Since 2010 the dimensions are combined with a geometric mean, which limits how far strength in one can offset weakness in another. The HDI measures capabilities, not happiness or inequality.\"},{\"h\":\"Adjusting for inequality, gender and the planet\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Each other composite indicator in the guide can be read as addressing a blind spot of the HDI (guide PDF p. 57). The HDI is an average, so the IHDI discounts each dimension for the inequality within it. The HDI ignores gender gaps, so the GII measures women’s disadvantage. The HDI ignores environmental cost, so the Happy Planet Index relates well-being to ecological footprint. Each fixes one blind spot and creates others.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Index\",\"What it combines\",\"How to read it\"],\"rows\":[[\"IHDI\",\"HDI dimensions, each discounted for inequality\",\"Equals the HDI under perfect equality; the % gap is the loss\"],[\"GII\",\"Reproductive health (maternal mortality, adolescent births); empowerment (parliamentary seats and secondary education, each by sex); labour-force participation by sex\",\"0 = equality; higher = more inequality\"],[\"Happy Planet Index\",\"Life satisfaction and life expectancy relative to ecological footprint per head (versions differ)\",\"Higher = more well-being per unit of footprint\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Every composite involves value judgements\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"A composite indicator reduces several dimensions to one number, which requires three choices: which dimensions to include, how to scale them and how to weight them. The HDI gives health, knowledge and income equal weight; nothing in economics makes that correct. So “the index is not perfect” is a weak criticism. A strong one names what is omitted (freedom, distribution, environment), explains how construction affects rankings and says what an alternative would measure. The strengths are real too: breadth, simplicity and easy comparison.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A mineral price boom in an economy with weak public services\",\"cause\":\"World prices for a country’s main mineral export rise sharply.\",\"mechanism\":\"Export revenue and real GDP rise, but gains go largely to the state and foreign-owned mining firms; profits sent abroad mean GNI rises less than GDP.\",\"immediate\":\"GDP per capita climbs and the country rises in income rankings.\",\"secondary\":\"If revenue is not spent on health and education, or is captured by elites, life expectancy and schooling change little and the HDI–IHDI gap may widen.\",\"longrun\":\"Development depends on whether rents become human and physical capital; if not, the economy is exposed when prices fall.\",\"depends\":\"Ownership of the resource, the tax regime, the quality of public spending and existing inequality.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The Human Development Index as a composite indicator\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Summarises development in one number that goes beyond income, for comparison across countries and over time.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Development can be represented by health, knowledge and income\",\"The three dimensions deserve equal weight\",\"Income has diminishing importance, so it enters in logarithms\"],\"mechanism\":\"Each dimension is scaled between fixed goalposts to give an index from 0 to 1; the three indices are combined with a geometric mean.\",\"prediction\":\"Countries that turn income into health and education effectively rank higher on the HDI than on GNI per capita.\",\"application\":\"Comparing countries, spotting where income and human outcomes diverge, tracking progress.\",\"limitations\":[\"Equal weights are a value judgement\",\"As an average, it hides inequality (the IHDI addresses this)\",\"It omits freedom, gender gaps and sustainability\"],\"real\":\"Oil-rich Equatorial Guinea has long ranked far lower on the HDI than on GNI per capita, while Sri Lanka has tended to rank higher on the HDI than its income suggests.\",\"misconception\":\"Describing the HDI as a measure of happiness or inequality. It measures average capabilities in three dimensions.\",\"exam\":\"In Paper 2 data questions, compare countries across dimensions using the figures; in Paper 1 (b), explain the construction before evaluating it.\",\"deeper\":\"The HDI grew from Amartya Sen’s capability approach, in which development is the expansion of what people are able to be and do, and income matters only as a means; Mahbub ul Haq led the first report. The 2010 switch to a geometric mean answered criticism that an arithmetic mean let high income fully offset poor health. Martin Ravallion showed that any aggregation implies trade-offs, such as how much income a year of life is ‘worth’, which can look strange once made explicit.\"},{\"model\":\"The Preston curve: income and life expectancy\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Describes the cross-country relationship between income per head and life expectancy.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Income buys nutrition, sanitation and health care\",\"Returns to extra income fall once basic needs are met\",\"Health knowledge spreads across countries over time\"],\"mechanism\":\"At low incomes, small rises in income are associated with large gains in life expectancy; at high incomes the curve flattens. Over decades the whole curve has shifted upwards.\",\"prediction\":\"Income growth matters most for health in poor countries, but much improvement over time comes from knowledge and public health rather than income.\",\"application\":\"Explaining why growth and development are related but distinct.\",\"limitations\":[\"It is a correlation: better health also raises income\",\"Countries far from the curve show that policy matters\"],\"real\":\"Costa Rica has reached life expectancy close to high-income levels at much lower income per head, often attributed to broad access to primary health care (compare Real World case DEV-047 on Kerala).\",\"misconception\":\"Reading the curve as evidence that growth alone produces health. The upward shift shows much health progress did not come from income.\",\"exam\":\"Useful in Paper 1 (b) or Paper 2 (g) evaluation of the growth–development link; describe its shape in words, since no diagram is required.\",\"deeper\":\"Samuel Preston’s 1975 study found that upward shifts of the curve, rather than movements along it, accounted for a large share of the rise in life expectancy in the mid-twentieth century. That points to vaccines, clean water and medical knowledge more than income. Later work, including Angus Deaton’s, stresses that institutions matter: knowledge helps most where health systems can deliver it. The debate bears on whether health spending or growth policy should come first.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"If development follows from growth, ranking countries by income per head should give much the same ranking as health, education and the HDI.\",\"evidence\":\"Income and HDI are strongly correlated, partly because income is an HDI component. But some countries rank far higher on the HDI than on income, and some resource exporters far lower.\",\"interp\":\"One interpretation is that income is necessary but not sufficient: public provision, institutions and distribution decide how far income becomes capabilities. Economists who stress growth see it as the most reliable route; others, following Sen, argue that direct investment in health and schooling can raise development before incomes are high.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"What the data cannot see\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Indicators are only as good as their data. Many low-income countries have large informal sectors and subsistence farming that national accounts estimate imperfectly. PPP conversions rest on international price surveys that are revised periodically, and revisions have substantially changed estimated incomes of some large economies. Life expectancy where deaths are not fully registered is partly modelled. When two indicators disagree, the first question is whether the data are comparable, not which country is ‘really’ more developed.\"},{\"h\":\"Whose values define development?\",\"tag\":\"Schools of thought\",\"p\":\"The guide’s TOK prompts cite Denis Goulet’s core values of development — life sustenance, self-esteem and freedom (guide PDF p. 59). Goulet’s point was that development is normative: deciding what counts is a question of values, not only data. Sen’s capability approach treats freedom as both the end and the means of development. The Happy Planet Index asks people how satisfied they are. These approaches can rank the same countries differently, so an evaluation should state which conception of development it uses.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Think of a school report. One grade tells you something, but you would not judge a student on maths alone. Development is similar. Income per head shows what people can buy on average. Life expectancy and schooling show health and knowledge. Inequality, energy and pollution measures show who benefits and at what cost. The HDI combines health, knowledge and income into one score, which is handy but hides detail. Growth means more output; development means better lives. They usually move together, but not everywhere or at the same pace.\",\"exam\":\"Define economic development as a multidimensional improvement in living standards and distinguish it from growth. In a data question, compare several indicators, quote the figures and say what each shows: a higher GNI per capita at PPP with lower life expectancy suggests income is not becoming health. To evaluate composite indicators, first name each component and its indicator, then criticise construction: weights are value judgements, averages hide distribution, dimensions are omitted. Balance this with strengths: breadth, simplicity, comparability. Judge by purpose: the HDI suits broad comparison between countries, but a policy on gender gaps needs the GII or sex-disaggregated data.\",\"deep\":\"Composite indicators raise problems from index-number theory. Scaling each dimension between goalposts makes units comparable but builds in judgements about what counts as the minimum and maximum. Aggregation decides substitutability: an arithmetic mean lets a gain in income offset any loss in health, while a geometric mean penalises imbalance. Logging income assumes that each doubling adds the same amount to well-being. Rankings can be sensitive to small data revisions, so small differences in HDI between countries say little about real differences. For these reasons the Stiglitz–Sen–Fitoussi Commission (2009) favoured dashboards of separate indicators alongside headline indices. The trade-off is clarity against information: a single number is easy to communicate and compare, while a dashboard shows more but is harder to summarise.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"Development indicators try to measure well-being beyond output: health, knowledge, security and life satisfaction.\",\"tension\":\"Measures that capture more of well-being tend to rely on weights and subjective data.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a country’s change in HDI ranking, asking what the change reveals about living standards.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Averages can hide who is left behind; the IHDI, GII and Gini make distribution visible.\",\"tension\":\"An index that rewards equality may rank a poorer, more equal country above a richer, unequal one.\",\"ia\":\"A report on a gender gap in schooling or pay, read through the GII.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do some countries have better health and education than their income per head predicts?\",\"expect\":\"One might expect health and schooling to rise in step with income, since richer countries can afford more of both.\",\"model\":\"The Preston curve predicts a strong but flattening link between income and life expectancy.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests large deviations: Costa Rica, Sri Lanka and the Indian state of Kerala have achieved strong outcomes at modest income, while some resource-rich economies lag.\",\"match\":\"Partly. Income is associated with better outcomes, but deviations show that policy, institutions and distribution matter.\",\"assume\":\"That data are comparable and that the chosen indicators capture what matters.\",\"change\":\"Evidence that high performers had unusual starting conditions would weaken the lesson for others.\",\"policy\":\"Public investment in primary health care and schooling can raise development early, given fiscal capacity and persistence.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain why two countries with similar GNI per capita at PPP could differ in economic development.\",\"check\":[\"GNI per capita is an average that hides distribution\",\"Spending on health and education may differ\",\"Inequality differs: a larger HDI–IHDI gap means gains are less evenly shared\",\"Gender gaps (GII) or access to energy and water may differ\",\"Income may come from an enclave sector with few links to the rest of the economy\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain two limitations of the HDI and what an alternative would measure instead.\",\"check\":[\"Names the three dimensions and their indicators\",\"Equal weighting is a value judgement\",\"It is an average: the IHDI adjusts for inequality\",\"It omits gender gaps (GII) or sustainability (Happy Planet Index)\",\"Years of schooling measure access, not learning\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Name the three dimensions of the HDI and one indicator for each.\",\"a\":\"Long and healthy life (life expectancy at birth); knowledge (mean and expected years of schooling); decent standard of living (GNI per capita at PPP).\"},{\"q\":\"Why compare incomes at PPP rather than at market exchange rates?\",\"a\":\"PPP adjusts for price differences, especially in non-traded services, so incomes buy comparable bundles.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: HDI 0.70, IHDI 0.56. Calculate the percentage loss due to inequality.\",\"a\":\"(0.70 − 0.56) ÷ 0.70 × 100 = 20%.\"},{\"q\":\"What does a higher Gender Inequality Index value show?\",\"a\":\"Greater inequality between women and men in reproductive health, empowerment and the labour market; 0 means equality.\"},{\"q\":\"Give one reason growth may not lead to development.\",\"a\":\"Gains may go to a small group or foreign owners, or revenue may not be spent on health and education.\"},{\"q\":\"State one strength and one limitation of composite indicators.\",\"a\":\"Strength: several dimensions in one comparable number. Limitation: weights and components are value judgements, and one number hides which dimension is weak.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"Which statement about the Human Development Index is correct?\",\"opts\":[\"It measures how happy people are\",\"It combines indicators of health, education and income per head\",\"It measures income inequality\",\"It uses GDP converted at market exchange rates\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The HDI combines life expectancy, years of schooling and GNI per capita at PPP. Happiness is closer to the Happy Planet Index, which uses life satisfaction; inequality is captured by the IHDI or Gini. Income enters at PPP, not market rates.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Country A’s real GDP grew 7% a year for a decade, but life expectancy and school enrolment barely changed. What is the best conclusion?\",\"opts\":[\"Country A developed rapidly because output grew quickly\",\"Country A had economic growth with little evidence of broad development\",\"The data must be wrong, because growth causes development\",\"Country A had development without growth\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Growth is a rise in real output; development is a multidimensional improvement in living standards. With health and schooling stagnant, growth has not become broad development. Treating the two as synonyms is the tempting error.\",\"mis\":\"MAC-03\"},{\"q\":\"A student writes: “The HDI is a poor measure because it is not perfect.” Which criticism is stronger?\",\"opts\":[\"It is too complicated for governments to use\",\"Its equal weights are a value judgement, and it omits inequality, which the IHDI captures\",\"It uses GNI, which leaves out income from abroad\",\"It is published by the UN\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A strong criticism names a construction choice and what an alternative would measure. “Not perfect” applies to every measure. The third option is wrong: GNI includes net income from abroad, which is why it differs from GDP.\",\"mis\":null}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (c)–(f): using data, explain what differences across several indicators suggest about two economies’ development\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain why economic growth may not lead to economic development\",\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate the use of composite indicators to measure development\",\"Paper 2 (g): use development indicators as evidence when evaluating a strategy\"],\"ct\":[\"compare\",\"compare and contrast\",\"distinguish\",\"explain\",\"evaluate\"],\"dg\":\"No diagram is required for 4.8 (guide PDF p. 57). A PPC or LRAS shift can illustrate growth, but development is shown through data.\",\"calc\":\"None listed for 4.8 (guide PDF p. 57). Data questions may still need percentage changes or per-capita figures, using skills from 1.2 and 3.1.\",\"apply\":\"Quote specific values for named countries and explain what each reveals, such as a large HDI–IHDI gap or a high GII.\",\"eval\":\"Judge an indicator against its purpose: what it includes, omits and weights, and whether its data are reliable.\",\"errors\":[\"Using growth and development interchangeably\",\"Describing the HDI as a measure of happiness or inequality\",\"Criticising an indicator as “not perfect” without naming what it omits\",\"Comparing incomes at market exchange rates or in nominal terms\"],\"strong\":[\"Use indicators from several categories, not income alone\",\"Explain a composite’s construction before criticising it\",\"Show how rankings change when inequality or gender is included\",\"Treat the growth–development link as two-way and conditional\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain why an increase in GDP per capita may not lead to an improvement in economic development.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the use of composite indicators to measure economic development.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Using a table of GNI per capita (PPP), HDI, IHDI and GII for two economies (illustrative data) and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that the economy with the higher GNI per capita is more developed.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"why\":\"GDP, GNI, PPP and alternative well-being measures such as the Happy Planet Index are introduced there (guide PDF p. 42).\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"The Gini coefficient, poverty lines and the MPI feed directly into development measurement.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"why\":\"Environmental indicators and the Happy Planet Index bring sustainability into measurement.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Progress towards selected SDGs is judged with these same indicators.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"why\":\"Because once indicators show where a country lags, the next question is what holds it back.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on a new Human Development Report, a change in a country’s HDI rank, or a gap between growth and social indicators. Economic well-being is a natural lens; equity suits articles on inequality or gender gaps.\",\"ee\":\"To what extent has income growth in one Indian state been matched by gains in health and schooling, compared with a state with a different public-spending record?\",\"tok\":\"If different weights would reorder country rankings, in what sense does a composite indicator give us knowledge about development?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can distinguish growth from development and evaluate indicators by their construction.\",\"starter\":\"Rank four unnamed countries using income alone, then re-rank after revealing life expectancy, schooling and inequality.\",\"activity\":\"Groups build a composite index from a small data set, choose weights and compare the rankings with the HDI’s.\",\"discussion\":\"Which dimension of development should count most, and who should decide?\",\"check\":\"Mini-whiteboards: the HDI’s three dimensions, and which index fixes each of its three blind spots.\",\"extension\":\"Plot life expectancy against GNI per capita for 20 countries and identify outliers.\",\"homework\":\"Compare two countries with similar GNI per capita but different HDI ranks in 300 words.\"},\"after\":[\"I can distinguish economic growth from economic development\",\"I can explain why income comparisons use PPP and GNI\",\"I can name single indicators in each of the guide’s five categories\",\"I can state what the HDI, IHDI, GII and Happy Planet Index measure\",\"I can compare the development of two countries from data\",\"I can evaluate a composite indicator by its construction\",\"I can explain the two-way link between growth and development\",\"I can explain how development is measured without notes\"]},\"4.9\":{\"code\":\"4.9\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"Some countries stay poor because obstacles reinforce each other. Low income means low saving, low investment and weak health and schooling, which keep income low: a poverty cycle. Barriers can be economic, such as reliance on one commodity, or political and social, such as weak institutions. The skill is judging which barrier binds hardest in a particular country.\",\"why\":\"A strategy works only if it targets the barrier that actually constrains growth; misdiagnosis wastes aid, borrowing and political effort.\",\"q\":\"Why have some resource-rich economies grown slowly while some resource-poor economies have grown quickly?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Growth versus development, and development indicators\",\"sub\":\"4.8\"},{\"t\":\"PED, and why inelastic demand makes prices volatile\",\"sub\":\"2.5\"},{\"t\":\"PES for primary products\",\"sub\":\"2.6\"},{\"t\":\"Causes of inequality and poverty\",\"sub\":\"3.4\"},{\"t\":\"Appreciation and export competitiveness\",\"sub\":\"4.5\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Identify the economic, political and social barriers listed in the guide\",\"Define a poverty cycle, capital flight and the informal economy\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain how a poverty cycle perpetuates low income through linked factors\",\"Explain why primary-product dependence makes export earnings volatile, using PED and PES\"],\"apply\":[\"Apply the barriers to a named country and identify the most significant\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse how indebtedness and capital flight can reinforce each other\",\"Analyse how institutions can offset geographical disadvantage\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate the relative significance of different barriers for a given country\",\"Discuss whether institutions or geography better explain persistent poverty\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw a poverty cycle showing a linked combination of factors that perpetuate poverty\",\"Draw a supply shift against inelastic demand to show commodity price volatility\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret data on export concentration or debt service to identify a barrier\"],\"recommend\":[\"HL: recommend which barrier a government should tackle first, with evidence (Paper 3)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"The poverty cycle\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"A poverty cycle, or poverty trap, is a chain of linked factors in which poverty creates conditions that keep people poor. The guide asks for a diagram showing any linked combination of such factors (guide PDF p. 57). The classic loop runs through saving: low incomes leave little to save, so investment in equipment, land and skills stays low, productivity stays low and so does income. Other loops run through health (poor nutrition lowers productivity) or the state (low incomes mean low tax revenue and weak services). Each arrow must be a causal step suited to the context.\",\"because\":[\"Low income\",\"low saving, little access to credit\",\"low investment in physical and human capital\",\"low productivity\",\"low income\"]},{\"h\":\"Economic barriers\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The guide lists ten economic barriers (guide PDF pp. 57–58). Each works through a mechanism that lowers investment, productivity or the return to effort, and several also make incomes more volatile. Knowing the mechanism is what turns naming a barrier into explaining it, and it is what lets you judge which barrier matters most in a given country and period.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Barrier\",\"How it holds back growth or development\"],\"rows\":[[\"Rising economic inequality\",\"Poorer households cannot finance schooling; tension deters investors\"],[\"Lack of infrastructure and appropriate technology\",\"Unreliable power and roads raise costs; imported methods may not suit local skills\"],[\"Low human capital\",\"Poor health and schooling lower productivity\"],[\"Dependence on primary production\",\"Low PED and PES make prices and export earnings volatile\"],[\"Lack of access to international markets\",\"Tariffs, subsidies and standards abroad limit exports\"],[\"Informal economy\",\"Firms stay small and cannot borrow; the tax base is narrow\"],[\"Capital flight\",\"Savings leave, cutting investment and weakening the currency\"],[\"Indebtedness\",\"Debt service diverts revenue from health and education\"],[\"Geography, including being landlocked\",\"Higher transport costs; dependence on neighbours’ ports\"],[\"Tropical climates and endemic diseases\",\"Malaria and similar diseases lower productivity\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Political and social barriers\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"The guide groups the remaining barriers as political and social (guide PDF p. 58). Many economists see them as deeper causes, because they decide whether the economic barriers can be overcome at all. A country with secure property rights and honest courts can borrow, trade and invest its way around a poor location; one without them may waste even rich natural resources.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Barrier\",\"Mechanism\"],\"rows\":[[\"Weak institutions: legal system, taxation, banking, property rights\",\"Contracts are costly to enforce, revenue is low, credit goes to insiders, untitled assets cannot back loans\"],[\"Gender inequality\",\"Less access for women to schooling, credit, land and paid work wastes talent\"],[\"Poor governance and corruption\",\"Bribes act like a tax on investment; public funds are diverted\"],[\"Unequal political power and status\",\"Elites can block reforms that threaten them; excluded groups lack a voice\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Why commodity dependence creates volatility\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"Primary products tend to have price-inelastic demand, because they are necessities or small shares of final costs, and price-inelastic supply in the short run, because crops take time to grow and mines years to open. With both curves steep, a shift in either — a good harvest, a recession abroad — causes a large price change. For an economy whose exports are concentrated in one or two commodities, export earnings, tax revenue and the exchange rate swing with world prices, which makes planning and investment harder.\",\"because\":[\"A good harvest shifts world supply right\",\"demand is price-inelastic\",\"so price falls proportionally more than quantity rises\",\"so total revenue falls\",\"so export earnings and tax revenue fall\"]},{\"h\":\"Debt, capital flight and informality reinforce each other\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"High external debt means export earnings and tax revenue go to creditors; if the debt is in foreign currency, a depreciation raises its domestic cost. Fear of default or inflation encourages capital flight, which weakens the currency further and shrinks funds for investment. A large informal economy — unregistered, not illegal — narrows the tax base, so governments borrow more. Weak institutions can sit behind all three, which helps explain why they tend to appear together in debt crises.\"},{\"h\":\"Ranking barriers: which one binds?\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide asks for the relative significance of barriers at AO3 (guide PDF p. 58). Listing ten earns little; judging which matters most in a context earns more. The growth diagnostics approach of Hausmann, Rodrik and Velasco asks which constraint binds: if interest rates are high and firms queue for loans, finance may be the problem; if credit is cheap but few invest, low returns from poor infrastructure, insecure property or corruption may be. The most significant barrier is the one whose removal would raise growth most; it differs between countries and over time.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"A collapse in the world price of the main export crop\",\"cause\":\"World cocoa prices fall sharply after good harvests elsewhere.\",\"mechanism\":\"World demand is price-inelastic, so extra supply causes a large price fall; supply is inelastic in the short run, so farmers cannot switch crops quickly.\",\"immediate\":\"Farm incomes, export earnings and tax revenue fall; the currency may depreciate.\",\"secondary\":\"Households cut spending on schooling and health; the government cuts investment or borrows.\",\"longrun\":\"Repeated shocks discourage investment and can deepen a poverty cycle, strengthening the case for diversification.\",\"depends\":\"The crop’s share of exports, stabilisation funds or savings, access to credit and insurance, and whether other sectors can absorb labour.\"},{\"t\":\"A depreciation when public debt is in foreign currency\",\"cause\":\"A country with large dollar-denominated debt sees its currency depreciate sharply.\",\"mechanism\":\"Each dollar of debt service now costs more in domestic currency.\",\"immediate\":\"The government cuts spending or borrows more; imported fuel and medicine cost more.\",\"secondary\":\"Investors fear default and move funds abroad, pushing the currency down further; borrowing costs rise.\",\"longrun\":\"Default and restructuring may follow, often with an IMF programme; public investment is squeezed for years.\",\"depends\":\"The foreign-currency share of debt, reserves, export earnings, access to concessional finance and policy credibility.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"The poverty cycle (poverty trap)\",\"dg\":null,\"purpose\":\"Explains why low income can persist across generations and why small improvements may not break it.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Saving and investment depend on current income\",\"Credit and insurance are missing or costly for the poor\",\"Returns to investment may be low below some threshold\"],\"mechanism\":\"Low income → low saving → low investment in physical and human capital → low productivity → low income. Each step feeds the next.\",\"prediction\":\"Without an outside change, some households or economies stay poor; a large enough push may move them to a higher path.\",\"application\":\"Framing the case for aid, microfinance and public investment.\",\"limitations\":[\"Many countries escaped poverty without a large outside push, so traps are not universal\",\"It overstates saving: technology, trade and institutions also drive growth\",\"It does not say which link is weakest\"],\"real\":\"Evaluations of graduation programmes, which give very poor households an asset, training and temporary support, have found lasting income gains in several countries including Bangladesh; some researchers read this as evidence of asset thresholds (Real World case DEV-044).\",\"misconception\":\"Drawing the cycle as a list of barriers in boxes with no causal links.\",\"exam\":\"Draw it in Paper 1 (a) or Paper 2 (f), tailored to the context; in Paper 1 (b), ask whether a strategy can break a link.\",\"deeper\":\"Economists disagree about how common traps are. Jeffrey Sachs argued that many of the poorest countries need a large, coordinated big push of aid. William Easterly argued that past big pushes largely failed and that progress comes from local searchers, not outside planners. Abhijit Banerjee and Esther Duflo take a middle view: traps exist in some areas, such as nutrition or asset thresholds, but not others, so each claim should be tested case by case.\"},{\"model\":\"Commodity dependence and price volatility\",\"dg\":\"sshift\",\"purpose\":\"Explains why economies relying on one or two primary exports face unstable earnings.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Demand for primary products is price-inelastic\",\"Supply is price-inelastic in the short run\",\"The country is a price-taker in world markets\"],\"mechanism\":\"Supply shocks (weather, new mines) and demand shocks (recessions abroad) shift the curves; with steep curves, prices and revenue swing widely.\",\"prediction\":\"Export earnings, government revenue and the exchange rate are volatile, so planning and investment become harder.\",\"application\":\"Cocoa in Ghana, copper in Zambia and Chile, oil in Nigeria; linking 2.5 and 2.6 to development.\",\"limitations\":[\"Some exporters manage volatility with stabilisation funds and fiscal rules\",\"It ignores the scope to add value by processing\",\"It omits exchange-rate and political channels of the resource curse\"],\"real\":\"Chile, a large copper exporter, uses a structural fiscal rule and stabilisation funds to save in booms and spend in slumps, which is often credited with steadying public spending (Real World case DEV-023).\",\"misconception\":\"Assuming a bumper harvest raises farm incomes: with inelastic demand, revenue falls (MIC-05).\",\"exam\":\"In Paper 1 (a) or Paper 2 (f), show a supply shift against steep demand, the large price change and the link to export earnings and revenue.\",\"deeper\":\"Resource dependence can also harm growth through the exchange rate and politics, the so-called resource curse. A boom raises demand for the currency, which appreciates and makes other exports less competitive (Dutch disease, named after the Netherlands’ gas discoveries). Large rents can reduce governments’ need to tax, and so their accountability. Some cross-country studies find the curse mainly where institutions were weak before extraction began; Botswana’s diamond-led growth is often cited as the contrast (Real World case DEV-015).\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Geography-based explanations predict that tropical, landlocked or disease-prone countries stay poorer, because trade costs more and disease lowers productivity.\",\"evidence\":\"Tropical and landlocked countries are poorer on average, and malaria is associated with lower income. Some influential studies argue geography adds little once institutions are accounted for; others dispute the methods and find it still matters, especially through disease.\",\"interp\":\"A reasonable reading is that geography raises costs but does not fix outcomes. Landlocked Switzerland and tropical Singapore suggest institutions and policy can offset disadvantage, while the disease burden in parts of sub-Saharan Africa suggests some costs are hard to escape without outside help.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"Geography or institutions?\",\"tag\":\"Causality\",\"p\":\"One school, associated with Jeffrey Sachs, stresses geography: distance from coasts, tropical disease and poor soils. Another, associated with Daron Acemoglu, Simon Johnson and James Robinson, whose work on institutions received the 2024 Nobel memorial prize, argues that institutions such as secure property and limits on elites are the deeper cause, and that geography mattered mainly through the institutions colonisers set up. Testing this is hard because the two are correlated. For answers, the lesson is that geography raises costs and institutions decide whether they are overcome.\"},{\"h\":\"The informal economy is not the illegal economy\",\"tag\":\"Measurement\",\"p\":\"Informal activity is legal work or trade that is not registered, taxed or regulated: a street vendor, a small workshop, a day labourer. In many low-income countries it employs a majority of workers. It provides livelihoods and flexibility, but informal firms tend to stay small, cannot easily borrow or enforce contracts, and their workers lack protection. Economists disagree on the cause: some blame heavy regulation and taxes, others low productivity, in which case registering firms without raising productivity changes little.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"Imagine starting a small business with no savings, no bank willing to lend, a road that floods every rainy season and officials who expect a bribe for a licence. Each problem makes the others worse. That is what barriers to development look like. A poverty cycle is when being poor causes the very things — low savings, poor health, weak schooling — that keep you poor. Some barriers are economic, like relying on one crop or owing large debts; others are political or social, like corruption or women being kept out of work.\",\"exam\":\"Define the barrier, then explain its mechanism step by step: for commodity dependence, low PED and PES → large price swings → volatile export earnings and revenue → lower investment. Draw a poverty cycle with causal arrows tailored to the context, or a supply-shift diagram for volatility. Develop a real example in detail rather than merely naming it. For questions on significance, rank barriers for the context: explain why one binds more tightly, show how barriers interact — weak institutions can worsen debt and capital flight — and consider how the ranking would change in another country or time period.\",\"deep\":\"Barriers are rarely independent, which is why a list misleads. Many are complements: a road is worth little without secure property, and credit is worth little without profitable projects. When constraints are complementary, removing one may change little until others are removed too — the logic behind big-push arguments. Alternatively, one constraint may bind so tightly that easing it yields large gains, the logic of growth diagnostics. Distinguishing the two is an empirical task. Economists look for signs of scarcity: high returns to what is missing, and firms that pay heavily to get round an obstacle. Causality runs in several directions: weak institutions cause poverty, but poverty also weakens institutions by limiting tax revenue. Good answers treat each barrier as a mechanism in a system, not a label.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Many barriers, such as gender inequality and unequal political power, are forms of inequity that also hold back growth.\",\"tension\":\"Reforms that remove barriers for the excluded often threaten the gains of powerful groups.\",\"ia\":\"An article on women’s access to land or credit, analysed for its effects on fairness and output.\"},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"here\":\"Barriers explain why well-being stays low even where people work hard.\",\"tension\":\"Resource extraction can raise income today while leaving well-being exposed to price shocks.\",\"ia\":\"A report on a commodity price collapse and its effects on household incomes.\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do some resource-rich countries grow slowly while some resource-poor countries grow quickly?\",\"expect\":\"One might expect natural resources to make development easier, since they generate exports and revenue.\",\"model\":\"Resource-curse and Dutch disease models predict that booms raise volatility, appreciate the currency and can weaken institutions.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence suggests resource wealth is linked to weaker growth mainly where institutions were weak; Botswana and Norway combine resource wealth with strong growth.\",\"match\":\"Partly. Resources are not a barrier in themselves; institutions appear to decide the outcome.\",\"assume\":\"That appreciation harms other exporters and that rents weaken accountability.\",\"change\":\"If resource-rich countries with weak institutions grew as fast as others, the institutional explanation would weaken.\",\"policy\":\"Fiscal rules, stabilisation funds and transparent resource contracts look more promising than avoiding extraction.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain, using a poverty cycle, why low income can persist in a low-income economy.\",\"check\":[\"Defines a poverty cycle as linked, self-reinforcing factors\",\"Low income → low saving → low investment\",\"Includes human capital as well as physical capital\",\"Low productivity leads back to low income\",\"Identifies an outside change that could break a link\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain why dependence on primary commodities can be a barrier to economic growth.\",\"check\":[\"Demand and short-run supply are price-inelastic\",\"Shifts cause large price swings\",\"Export earnings and government revenue become volatile\",\"Volatility deters investment and complicates borrowing\",\"Booms can appreciate the currency and harm other exports\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define capital flight.\",\"a\":\"The movement of money and assets out of a country, usually in response to risk, instability or expected depreciation.\"},{\"q\":\"Is the informal economy the same as the illegal economy?\",\"a\":\"No: informal activity is legal but unregistered and untaxed; illegal activity involves prohibited goods or services.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does being landlocked raise trade costs?\",\"a\":\"Goods must cross neighbours’ territory, so transport costs are higher and trade depends on others’ ports, roads and border procedures.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: cocoa’s world price falls 20% and export volume rises 5%. Roughly how does export revenue change?\",\"a\":\"0.80 × 1.05 = 0.84, a fall of about 16%; the small volume response suggests inelastic demand.\"},{\"q\":\"Name two political or social barriers in the guide.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: weak institutions, gender inequality, poor governance and corruption, unequal political power and status.\"},{\"q\":\"How can high external debt reduce development spending?\",\"a\":\"Debt service absorbs revenue and foreign exchange that could fund health, education and infrastructure.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A student lists eight barriers to development for Country X, one sentence each. What would most improve the answer?\",\"opts\":[\"Adding three more barriers\",\"Explaining the mechanism of the most relevant barriers and judging which binds most in Country X\",\"Removing real-world examples to focus on theory\",\"Stating that all barriers matter equally\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The guide asks for the significance of barriers (guide PDF p. 58), which means ranking them for a context. Breadth without mechanism or judgement is description, and saying all barriers matter equally avoids the judgement required.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Which is an example of the informal economy?\",\"opts\":[\"A registered firm paying corporate tax\",\"A street vendor selling legal goods without a licence or tax registration\",\"Drug trafficking\",\"A state-owned bank\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The informal economy consists of legal activities that are unregistered, untaxed or unregulated. Drug trafficking is illegal activity, a different category, and confusing the two is the common error.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"A good harvest raises world coffee output by 10%. Demand is price-inelastic. What happens to exporters’ total revenue?\",\"opts\":[\"It rises, because they sell more coffee\",\"It falls, because price falls proportionally more than quantity rises\",\"It is unchanged, because the effects cancel\",\"It rises, because inelastic demand keeps the price stable\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"With inelastic demand, the percentage price fall needed to sell the extra output exceeds the 10% rise in quantity, so revenue falls. “More output means more revenue” ignores the price effect.\",\"mis\":\"MIC-05\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 2 (f): using the text, explain one barrier to development in the named economy\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain, with a poverty cycle diagram, how poverty can perpetuate itself\",\"Paper 1 (b): discuss which barrier is most significant for a named economy\",\"Paper 2 (g): evaluate a strategy by whether it addresses the barrier the data reveal\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"analyse\",\"discuss\",\"evaluate\",\"to what extent\"],\"dg\":\"The guide specifies a poverty cycle showing any linked combination of factors that perpetuate poverty (guide PDF p. 57); each arrow should be a causal step. A supply shift against inelastic demand supports commodity arguments.\",\"calc\":\"None listed for 4.9 (guide PDF pp. 57–58). Revenue reasoning from 2.5 can support commodity arguments.\",\"apply\":\"Name the country, the barrier and the evidence, such as export concentration or debt service relative to revenue.\",\"eval\":\"Rank barriers for the context, show how they interact, separate symptoms from causes and consider how the ranking changes over time.\",\"errors\":[\"Listing barriers with no ranking or context\",\"Treating geography as destiny rather than a cost institutions can offset\",\"Confusing the informal economy with the illegal economy\",\"Drawing a poverty cycle of unconnected boxes\"],\"strong\":[\"Explain each barrier’s mechanism, not just its name\",\"Tailor the poverty cycle to the context\",\"Judge which barrier binds most, with evidence\",\"Show interactions between barriers\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain, using a diagram, how a poverty cycle can prevent economic development in a low-income economy.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, discuss whether weak institutions are a more significant barrier to economic development than geography.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 2 (g)\",\"q\":\"Using information from a text about an economy whose exports are dominated by one mineral, and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the view that commodity dependence is the main barrier to its development.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"why\":\"Low PED for primary products explains volatile commodity prices and export earnings.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"why\":\"Low short-run PES in farming and mining magnifies price swings.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"why\":\"Unequal power, discrimination and inequality of opportunity appear in both lists.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"why\":\"Resource booms appreciate the currency and hurt other exports.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"why\":\"Because a strategy can only be evaluated once the barrier it is meant to remove has been diagnosed.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on a commodity price fall, a debt crisis, a corruption case or an informal-sector policy. Economic well-being or equity can frame it.\",\"ee\":\"How far has price volatility in a named primary export affected government revenue and development spending in a named economy over a chosen period?\",\"tok\":\"Economists disagree about whether geography or institutions explain persistent poverty, partly because history cannot be rerun as an experiment. How can historical causes be known?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can explain barriers through their mechanisms and rank them for a context.\",\"starter\":\"Show two export profiles, one diversified and one dominated by a single commodity: which is more exposed?\",\"activity\":\"Groups build a poverty cycle for a named country from cards, then remove one and argue whether the loop breaks.\",\"discussion\":\"Is geography destiny, or can institutions overcome it?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: explain one barrier through its mechanism in three causal steps.\",\"extension\":\"Use growth-diagnostics logic to identify the binding constraint in a short country case.\",\"homework\":\"A 300-word ranking of the three most significant barriers for a chosen country.\"},\"after\":[\"I can define a poverty cycle, capital flight and the informal economy\",\"I can draw a poverty cycle with causal links suited to a context\",\"I can explain commodity volatility using PED and PES\",\"I can explain how debt, capital flight and weak institutions interact\",\"I can apply the barriers to a named country\",\"I can evaluate which barrier is most significant in a context\",\"I can connect barriers to strategies in 4.10\",\"I can explain the main barriers without notes\"]},\"4.10\":{\"code\":\"4.10\",\"big\":{\"idea\":\"No single strategy reliably produces development. Countries have protected new industries, pushed exports, opened markets, redistributed income, courted foreign firms, received aid and reformed institutions. Each has worked in some conditions and disappointed in others. The economist’s task is to match a strategy to the barrier it targets and to state the conditions for success.\",\"why\":\"Strategies commit large public and donor resources; choosing badly can leave debt and disappointment for a generation.\",\"q\":\"Should a low-income country rely mainly on markets and trade, or on active government intervention, to develop?\"},\"before\":[{\"t\":\"Barriers to growth and development, and their significance\",\"sub\":\"4.9\"},{\"t\":\"Arguments for and against protection, including infant industries\",\"sub\":\"4.3\"},{\"t\":\"Market-based and interventionist supply-side policies\",\"sub\":\"3.7\"},{\"t\":\"Merit goods and positive externalities\",\"sub\":\"2.8\"},{\"t\":\"Redistribution through taxes, transfers and minimum wages\",\"sub\":\"3.4\"}],\"obj\":{\"know\":[\"Identify the strategies the guide lists\",\"Distinguish the main forms of aid and multilateral lending\"],\"understand\":[\"Explain how each strategy is meant to work and which barrier it targets\",\"Explain the difference between import substitution and export promotion\"],\"apply\":[\"Match a strategy to a barrier diagnosed in a named country and justify the match\"],\"analyse\":[\"Analyse the benefits and costs of inward FDI for a host economy\",\"Analyse how microfinance and mobile banking widen access to finance\"],\"evaluate\":[\"Evaluate a strategy by its conditions for success\",\"Evaluate government intervention against market-oriented approaches\",\"Evaluate progress towards selected SDGs in two or more countries\"],\"calculate\":[],\"draw\":[\"Draw a diagram from another subtopic to analyse a strategy, such as a tariff or a positive externality\"],\"interpret\":[\"Interpret SDG, aid or debt data for two countries\"],\"recommend\":[\"Recommend one strategy for a given economy, justified against its binding barrier (HL Paper 3)\"]},\"blocks\":[{\"h\":\"Start from the barrier\",\"kind\":\"idea\",\"p\":\"The guide lists many strategies (guide PDF pp. 58–59). They are better seen as responses to specific barriers from 4.9 than as a menu of good things. A strategy that works where one barrier binds may be irrelevant where another does. So an evaluation starts with a diagnosis, and a recommendation says which barrier it addresses, why that barrier matters most there and what conditions must hold.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Barrier (4.9)\",\"Strategy (4.10)\",\"A condition for success\"],\"rows\":[[\"Primary-product dependence\",\"Diversification; export promotion\",\"Skills, infrastructure and market access for new sectors\"],[\"Low human capital\",\"Education and health programmes\",\"Service quality, not just enrolment\"],[\"No access to credit\",\"Microfinance; mobile banking\",\"Profitable uses for loans; consumer protection\"],[\"Indebtedness\",\"Debt relief\",\"Freed funds spent well; no rapid re-borrowing\"],[\"Weak institutions, corruption\",\"Institutional reform; property and land rights\",\"Political support and enforcement\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Trade strategies: inward- and outward-looking\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Import substitution protects domestic industries with tariffs, quotas and subsidies so that goods previously imported are made at home, using the infant-industry argument (4.3). Export promotion orients production towards world markets through export incentives and competitive exchange rates. Economic integration enlarges the market by joining trading blocs (4.4). Many Latin American economies used import substitution from the 1950s to the 1980s; several East Asian economies turned early to export promotion. Evidence links the export path with faster growth, but East Asian states also intervened heavily, so the lesson is contested.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Import substitution\",\"Export promotion\"],\"rows\":[[\"Main risk\",\"Firms stay uncompetitive and lobby for permanent protection\",\"Exposure to world demand; pressure on wages and standards\"],[\"Works better when\",\"Protection is temporary and tied to performance\",\"Infrastructure and skills exist and partners’ markets stay open\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Diversification and social enterprise\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Diversification widens the range of goods and services an economy produces and exports, reducing dependence on a few primary products. It can mean new crops, processing (chocolate rather than cocoa beans), or manufacturing and services such as tourism. It lowers exposure to price shocks but needs investment, skills and market access. A social enterprise sells in markets but uses its profits mainly for social goals, such as affordable solar lighting or clean water. It can reach people that markets and states miss, but is usually small relative to national needs.\"},{\"h\":\"Markets or the state?\",\"kind\":\"compare\",\"p\":\"Market-based strategies — trade liberalisation, privatisation and deregulation — aim to raise efficiency through competition and price signals. Interventionist strategies — redistribution through taxes, transfer payments and minimum wages, and provision of merit goods such as education, health programmes and infrastructure (energy, transport, telecommunications, clean water and sanitation) — aim to correct market failure and reduce inequality. The guide asks you to weigh the two (guide PDF p. 59). China’s reforms from 1978 and Vietnam’s Doi Moi from 1986 introduced markets while the state kept a large role: the real question is balance, sequence and implementation.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Market-oriented\",\"Interventionist\"],\"rows\":[[\"Strengths\",\"Efficiency, incentives to invest, less scope for government failure\",\"Corrects under-provision of merit goods and infrastructure; reduces inequality\"],[\"Limitations\",\"Inequality may rise; private monopolies after weak privatisation\",\"Corruption, capture, weak capacity, fiscal cost\"],[\"Works better when\",\"Institutions enforce contracts and competition\",\"The state is capable and accountable\"]]}},{\"h\":\"External finance: FDI, aid, debt relief and multilateral lending\",\"kind\":\"definition\",\"p\":\"Inward FDI is long-term investment by a foreign firm with a lasting management interest. It brings capital, technology, jobs and export access; gains are larger when firms buy locally and train workers, smaller when they operate as enclaves, repatriate profits and receive large tax breaks. Foreign aid transfers resources on concessional terms, in several forms. Official Development Assistance (ODA) is aid from governments and official agencies to promote the development and welfare of developing countries.\",\"tbl\":{\"head\":[\"Form\",\"Purpose\",\"Main concern\"],\"rows\":[[\"Humanitarian aid\",\"Short-term relief after disasters or conflict\",\"If prolonged, can undercut local producers\"],[\"Development aid\",\"Long-term projects in health, schooling, infrastructure\",\"Dependence; donor priorities\"],[\"Tied aid\",\"Must be spent on the donor’s goods or services\",\"Higher costs; may not fit local needs\"],[\"NGO aid\",\"Targeted local programmes\",\"Small scale\"],[\"Debt relief\",\"Cancels debt so revenue can fund development\",\"New debts may build up; moral hazard\"],[\"World Bank\",\"Development lending, concessional for the poorest\",\"Conditions; local ownership\"],[\"IMF\",\"Lending for balance-of-payments problems\",\"Conditions that may require austerity\"]]}},{\"h\":\"Institutional change\",\"kind\":\"because\",\"p\":\"The guide lists improved access to banking (including microfinance and mobile banking), women’s empowerment, reducing corruption, property rights and land rights (guide PDF p. 59). These change the rules that shape incentives. Microfinance lends small sums without collateral, often through group liability. Mobile money lets people save and transfer money by phone, as M-Pesa did in Kenya from 2007. Institutional change is not cost-free: it needs administrative capacity and political will, and it creates losers, such as those who profited from corruption, who may resist.\",\"because\":[\"Mobile money cuts the cost of sending and storing money\",\"households receive remittances quickly and save safely\",\"they cope with shocks without selling assets\",\"some shift into business\",\"consumption and incomes become more stable\"]},{\"h\":\"Judging progress towards the SDGs\",\"kind\":\"note\",\"p\":\"The guide asks you to evaluate progress towards selected SDGs in two or more countries (guide PDF p. 59). Choose goals with clear indicators, such as extreme poverty or under-five mortality, and compare trends rather than single years. Then ask why countries differ: starting points, growth, public spending, conflict and institutions. UN progress reports have found many targets off track, with setbacks during the COVID-19 pandemic. Attribute change to a strategy cautiously: many factors move at once.\"}],\"mech\":[{\"t\":\"Debt relief for a heavily indebted low-income country\",\"cause\":\"Creditors cancel a large share of a country’s external debt, conditional on a poverty-reduction plan.\",\"mechanism\":\"Debt-service payments fall, freeing revenue and foreign exchange.\",\"immediate\":\"Spending on health, education or infrastructure can rise without new borrowing; default risk falls.\",\"secondary\":\"Lower debt may attract investment, but expected future relief can weaken borrowing discipline.\",\"longrun\":\"Gains last if freed funds are spent well and new borrowing is managed. Zambia received relief under the HIPC initiative in the 2000s, yet defaulted on its external debt in 2020.\",\"depends\":\"Governance, commodity prices, the terms of new loans and whether all creditors take part.\"},{\"t\":\"Microfinance in a rural area\",\"cause\":\"A microfinance institution offers small group loans to poor households without collateral.\",\"mechanism\":\"Group liability and frequent repayment replace collateral.\",\"immediate\":\"Access to credit rises; some borrowers start or expand micro-enterprises; others smooth consumption.\",\"secondary\":\"Some households gain income and resilience; others become over-indebted if interest rates are high and businesses earn little.\",\"longrun\":\"Several randomised evaluations suggest modest average effects on business activity and little change in average household income, with larger gains for some existing entrepreneurs.\",\"depends\":\"Interest rates, returns to small businesses, consumer protection, and whether savings products are offered too.\"}],\"models\":[{\"model\":\"Import substitution versus export promotion\",\"dg\":\"tariff\",\"purpose\":\"Analyses protection of domestic industry against an outward-looking alternative.\",\"assumptions\":[\"The country is small and a price-taker\",\"Domestic firms start with higher costs than world producers\",\"Firms can learn and cut costs if given time (the infant-industry assumption)\"],\"mechanism\":\"A tariff raises the domestic price above the world price; domestic output rises, consumption and imports fall, and efficiency losses result. Export promotion keeps prices close to world levels and pushes firms to compete abroad.\",\"prediction\":\"Protected output rises at a cost to consumers; unless firms learn and protection ends, losses persist.\",\"application\":\"Comparing Latin American and East Asian industrial policy.\",\"limitations\":[\"The static diagram omits learning and scale economies, the dynamic gains that justify protection\",\"Export success depends on partners keeping markets open\"],\"real\":\"South Korea combined export targets and state-directed credit with selective protection, and later became an aid donor; some read this as a success of intervention, others of export orientation (Real World case DEV-020).\",\"misconception\":\"Treating import substitution as a sure failure or export promotion as a sure success (GLO-13).\",\"exam\":\"Draw the tariff diagram from 4.2 with prices, output, imports and welfare losses, then weigh dynamic gains against static losses.\",\"deeper\":\"The World Bank’s 1993 report The East Asian Miracle emphasised macroeconomic stability, education and openness, while acknowledging some role for intervention. Robert Wade argued that the state ‘governed the market’ through selective protection and credit, and Alice Amsden stressed that support was conditional on performance, such as meeting export targets. Critics reply that such policies need unusually capable, insulated bureaucracies, and that failed industrial policies elsewhere attract less attention.\"},{\"model\":\"Merit goods and conditional cash transfers\",\"dg\":\"poscons\",\"purpose\":\"Justifies provision of education and health, and analyses conditional cash transfers.\",\"assumptions\":[\"Education and health generate external benefits (MSB above MPB)\",\"Poor households under-consume them because of low income, credit constraints or information failure\"],\"mechanism\":\"Consumption is below the social optimum. Free provision, subsidies or transfers conditional on school attendance and clinic visits raise it towards the optimum.\",\"prediction\":\"Enrolment and use of health services rise, with possible long-run gains in productivity and income.\",\"application\":\"Cash-transfer programmes, free primary schooling, school meals.\",\"limitations\":[\"Enrolment is not learning: poor schools limit gains\",\"Conditions need services that remote areas may lack\"],\"real\":\"Evaluations of Mexico’s Progresa, rolled out with random assignment, found higher school enrolment and use of health services; evidence on long-run learning and earnings from such programmes is more mixed (Real World cases DEV-021, DEV-022).\",\"misconception\":\"Using “merit good” and “positive externality” as synonyms: a merit good is under-consumed partly because people undervalue its private benefit (MIC-14).\",\"exam\":\"Draw the positive consumption externality diagram, show the welfare gain from moving towards the optimum, then evaluate cost, quality and targeting.\",\"deeper\":\"Randomised studies have compared conditional and unconditional transfers. Some find conditions raise school attendance more; unconditional transfers are cheaper to run and do not exclude households unable to meet conditions. Studies of unconditional transfers, such as GiveDirectly’s in Kenya, suggest households often spend cash on food and productive assets rather than on temptation goods. The design choice depends on whether the binding constraint is income, information or access to services.\"}],\"tei\":{\"theory\":\"Aid is meant to fill a financing gap: by adding to domestic saving and foreign exchange, it should raise investment and growth.\",\"evidence\":\"Cross-country studies of aid and growth give mixed and fragile results that change with the sample and method. Evaluations of specific interventions, such as immunisation and some health programmes, more often find clear benefits.\",\"interp\":\"This gap is sometimes called the micro–macro paradox. One interpretation is that aid helps where well targeted and governed, but effects are too small or varied to show in national growth. William Easterly argues aid can weaken accountability; Jeffrey Sachs argues larger, coordinated aid could be decisive. The evidence does not settle it.\"},\"deeper\":[{\"h\":\"What randomised trials can and cannot tell us\",\"tag\":\"Evidence\",\"p\":\"Randomised controlled trials compare people randomly assigned to a programme with those who are not, so differences can be attributed to the programme. Abhijit Banerjee, Esther Duflo and Michael Kremer shared the 2019 Nobel memorial prize for this approach to reducing poverty. Critics, including Angus Deaton, note that results may not transfer to other places, that small pilots may not scale, and that trials suit narrow questions better than big ones such as trade strategy or institutional reform.\"},{\"h\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"tag\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"p\":\"Strategies create incentives their designers did not intend. Privatisation without regulation can turn a state monopoly into a private one. Tariff protection creates lobbies that keep it in place. Rapid growth of microfinance lending contributed to over-indebtedness in Andhra Pradesh, where the state government restricted lending in 2010 after reports of coercive collection. Formal land titling can override women’s or communal customary rights. None of this makes a strategy wrong; it shows why design, regulation and monitoring matter as much as the choice.\"}],\"explain\":{\"simple\":\"There is no single recipe for development. A doctor chooses medicine after a diagnosis, and development strategy works the same way. A country held back by lack of credit may gain from microfinance or mobile banking; one weighed down by debt may need relief; one reliant on a single crop may need to diversify. Some strategies use markets: opening trade, selling state firms, cutting red tape. Others use government: building schools and clinics, redistributing income. Most countries that developed used a mix, and every strategy works only under certain conditions.\",\"exam\":\"Define the strategy precisely, explain its mechanism and link it to a named barrier. Use diagrams from other subtopics: a tariff diagram for import substitution, a positive externality diagram for education or health, AD/AS with LRAS shifting for long-run effects. Develop a real example with specific detail. Evaluate by stating conditions for success — governance, absorptive capacity, time frame, who gains and who loses — and compare with an alternative strategy. For an HL Paper 3 recommendation, choose one strategy, justify it with the data and weigh feasibility and trade-offs. Do not declare a strategy a success or failure without conditions.\",\"deep\":\"Strategies interact with each other. Aid for schools raises little human capital if teachers are absent; FDI brings little technology if local firms cannot absorb it; trade liberalisation helps exporters only if roads and ports work. Economists therefore stress complementarities and sequencing. The Washington Consensus, John Williamson’s 1989 summary of market-oriented reforms, was followed in the 1990s by results widely judged disappointing in parts of Latin America and Africa, though defenders argue the reforms were incomplete. Critics such as Joseph Stiglitz emphasised institutions and sequencing; Dani Rodrik argued for ‘one economics, many recipes’. Evidence is uneven: trials give strong answers to narrow questions, while judgements about national strategies must rely on case comparisons in which cause and effect are hard to isolate.\"},\"kc\":[{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"here\":\"Every strategy involves choosing how far to rely on the state rather than markets.\",\"tension\":\"Intervention can correct market failure but risks government failure through corruption or weak capacity.\",\"ia\":\"An article on a state-run infrastructure or cash-transfer programme.\"},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"here\":\"Strategies differ in who gains: export zones may favour urban workers, cash transfers target the poorest.\",\"tension\":\"Strategies that maximise growth may widen inequality in the short run.\",\"ia\":\"A report on microfinance or mobile money: who benefits, who is left out?\"}],\"inquiry\":{\"q\":\"Why do some development strategies succeed in one country and fail in another?\",\"expect\":\"One might expect a strategy that worked in one country to work anywhere.\",\"model\":\"Each model predicts benefits if its assumptions hold: firms learn behind protection, borrowers have profitable projects.\",\"evidence\":\"Evidence varies widely: export-led growth was rapid in parts of East Asia but not everywhere, and microfinance trials show modest average effects.\",\"match\":\"Only conditionally: the assumptions hold in some contexts and not others.\",\"assume\":\"That policies are implemented as designed and that gains reach the poor.\",\"change\":\"Success across very different institutional settings would support more general conclusions.\",\"policy\":\"Diagnose the binding barrier, pilot and evaluate before scaling, and combine complementary strategies.\"},\"selfexp\":[{\"prompt\":\"Explain how inward FDI might promote growth and development in a host country, and why it might not.\",\"check\":[\"Defines FDI, including the lasting management interest\",\"Adds capital, jobs, tax revenue and export capacity\",\"Technology spreads through links with local suppliers and workers\",\"Profits owed to foreign owners, whether sent home or reinvested, make GNI rise less than GDP\",\"Enclave production, tax breaks and weak regulation limit gains\"]},{\"prompt\":\"Explain the difference between import substitution and export promotion.\",\"check\":[\"Import substitution replaces imports with domestic production behind protection\",\"Export promotion orients production towards world markets\",\"Different risks: inefficiency versus exposure to world demand\",\"Outcomes depend on conditions such as time-limited protection\"]}],\"retrieve\":[{\"q\":\"Define official development assistance.\",\"a\":\"Aid from governments or official agencies, on concessional terms, to promote the development and welfare of developing countries.\"},{\"q\":\"Distinguish humanitarian aid from development aid.\",\"a\":\"Humanitarian aid relieves short-term suffering after disasters; development aid funds long-term projects such as schools.\"},{\"q\":\"Why might tied aid be less effective than untied aid?\",\"a\":\"It must be spent on the donor’s goods or services, which may cost more or fit local needs less well.\"},{\"q\":\"Which diagram analyses import substitution?\",\"a\":\"A tariff diagram: the domestic price rises above the world price, domestic output rises, imports fall and efficiency losses appear.\"},{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a foreign-owned subsidiary earns $50m profit and sends $40m abroad. How does GNI compare with GDP?\",\"a\":\"All $50m counts in GDP. If the subsidiary is wholly foreign-owned, all $50m is income of its foreign owners, including the $10m reinvested locally, so GNI is $50m lower on this account; the $40m sent abroad is only the visible part.\"},{\"q\":\"State one strength and one limitation of microfinance.\",\"a\":\"Strength: credit without collateral. Limitation: modest average income effects and a risk of over-indebtedness.\"}],\"mcq\":[{\"q\":\"A student recommends foreign aid for Country Y without saying what problem it solves. What is the main weakness?\",\"opts\":[\"Aid does not work, so it should not be recommended\",\"The recommendation does not identify the barrier aid would address or the conditions for success\",\"The student should have recommended debt relief instead\",\"Aid helps only high-income countries\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Strategies are judged by whether they address a diagnosed barrier and whether the conditions for success hold. Aid can help or fail depending on its type, targeting and governance, so a blanket rejection is as weak as a blanket endorsement.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"Which is an example of tied aid?\",\"opts\":[\"Emergency food supplies after a cyclone\",\"A grant that must be spent on equipment from the donor country\",\"A World Bank loan for a road project\",\"A remittance sent home by a migrant worker\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Tied aid must be spent on goods or services from the donor. Emergency food is humanitarian aid, a World Bank loan is multilateral lending and a remittance is a private transfer, not aid.\",\"mis\":null},{\"q\":\"“Import substitution failed in Latin America, so protection should not be used for development.” What is the strongest response?\",\"opts\":[\"Agree: protection harms growth wherever it is used\",\"Disagree: protection has no costs because it creates jobs\",\"It depends on conditions, such as whether protection is temporary and tied to export performance\",\"Protection is only a problem for developed countries\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Prolonged, unconditional protection often produced inefficient industries, but some East Asian economies used selective protection with export discipline. Claiming protection has no costs ignores higher prices, efficiency losses and retaliation.\",\"mis\":\"GLO-13\"}],\"exam\":{\"types\":[\"Paper 1 (b): evaluate a named strategy, such as aid, FDI or microfinance, or market versus intervention\",\"Paper 2 (g): evaluate a strategy described in a text on a developing economy\",\"Paper 1 (a): explain how a strategy addresses a barrier\",\"Paper 3 (HL only; guide PDF p. 64): recommend a strategy for a given economy, using the data provided\"],\"ct\":[\"explain\",\"evaluate\",\"discuss\",\"to what extent\",\"compare and contrast\",\"recommend\",\"justify\"],\"dg\":\"No new diagrams are prescribed; students draw on diagrams from other sections (guide PDF p. 58), such as a tariff, a positive consumption externality or AD/AS with an LRAS shift.\",\"calc\":\"None listed for 4.10 (guide PDF pp. 58–59). HL Paper 3 may still involve quantitative work from other subtopics.\",\"apply\":\"Name the country, barrier and strategy, and use specific features: Kenya’s mobile money spread partly because domestic remittances were common.\",\"eval\":\"Weigh strengths and limitations under stated conditions — governance, absorptive capacity, time frame, stakeholders — and compare with an alternative.\",\"errors\":[\"Recommending a strategy without naming the barrier it addresses\",\"Treating aid as one thing rather than distinguishing its forms\",\"Assuming institutional change is cost-free because it is not a spending programme\",\"Declaring a strategy a success or failure without conditions\"],\"strong\":[\"Match the strategy to a diagnosed barrier\",\"Apply a diagram from another subtopic to the context\",\"State conditions for success and compare with an alternative\",\"Use hedged evidence and note where studies disagree\"],\"practice\":[{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (a)\",\"q\":\"Explain how microfinance may help to overcome a barrier to economic development.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 1 (b)\",\"q\":\"Using real-world examples, evaluate the view that foreign direct investment is more effective than foreign aid in promoting economic development.\"},{\"p\":\"Paper 3\",\"q\":\"(HL only) Illustrative context: a low-income economy earns most export revenue from one crop, and few adults have a bank account. Recommend one strategy to promote economic development, justifying it by the barrier it addresses and its trade-offs.\"}]},\"connect\":[{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"why\":\"Every strategy is evaluated against the barrier it is meant to remove.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"why\":\"Import substitution rests on the infant-industry argument and bears the costs of protection.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"why\":\"Market-based and interventionist supply-side policies reappear as strategies to shift LRAS.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"why\":\"Positive externalities and merit goods justify education and health programmes.\"}],\"next\":{\"sub\":null,\"why\":\"Because this final subtopic draws on externalities, supply-side policy, trade protection, exchange rates and inequality, the next step is course-wide synthesis: link micro, macro and global arguments in extended answers and across your IA portfolio.\"},\"ia\":\"Look for an article on an aid programme, a debt restructuring, a new foreign-owned plant, a mobile-money scheme or a cash-transfer programme. Intervention is a natural lens; equity or sustainability suits a story about distribution or lasting effects.\",\"ee\":\"How far has a named microfinance or mobile-money programme affected small-business activity in a chosen region, using published evaluations and local survey data?\",\"tok\":\"Randomised trials give strong evidence on small interventions but little on big questions such as trade strategy. Does the method economists can use shape which questions they treat as answerable?\",\"teach\":{\"objective\":\"Students can match a strategy to a barrier and evaluate it by its conditions for success.\",\"starter\":\"Three headlines — a debt deal, a foreign-owned factory, a microfinance crisis: which barrier does each target?\",\"activity\":\"Groups diagnose the binding barrier from a country profile, choose a strategy and draw a supporting diagram.\",\"discussion\":\"Did East Asian economies develop because of markets or the state?\",\"check\":\"Exit ticket: one strategy, the barrier it addresses, one condition for success and one risk.\",\"extension\":\"HL: write a Paper 3-style recommendation for a country profile.\",\"homework\":\"Compare progress on two SDGs in two countries using UN data, attributing change cautiously.\"},\"after\":[\"I can list and explain the strategies the guide names\",\"I can distinguish the main forms of aid and multilateral lending\",\"I can draw a supporting diagram from another subtopic\",\"I can match a strategy to a diagnosed barrier\",\"I can evaluate a strategy by its conditions for success\",\"I can weigh market-oriented against interventionist approaches\",\"I can compare progress towards SDGs in two countries\",\"I can explain development strategies without notes\"]}},\"misc\":[{\"id\":\"MIC-01\",\"title\":\"Demand vs quantity demanded\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.1\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The price went up, so demand fell – the demand curve shifts left.”\",\"right\":\"A change in the good’s own price moves you along the existing demand curve: that is a change in quantity demanded. Demand itself – the whole curve – shifts only when a non-price determinant changes, such as income, tastes, the price of a substitute or complement, expectations of future prices or the number of consumers.\",\"why\":\"In everyday speech “demand” just means how much people buy, so a price rise that cuts purchases sounds like “lower demand”.\",\"analogy\":\"Demand is the whole menu of what you would buy at every price; quantity demanded is the dish you pick at today’s price. Choosing a cheaper dish from the same menu does not give you a new menu.\",\"exam\":\"Writing “the price rise reduced demand” and shifting the curve left instead of moving up along it. The diagram then contradicts the economics, and the analysis built on it is undermined.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming that a price change in the article “reduced demand” when only quantity demanded changed along the curve.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"The price of coffee rises while everything else stays the same. What happens in the coffee market?\",\"opts\":[\"Demand for coffee falls and the demand curve shifts left\",\"Quantity demanded falls: a movement up along the demand curve\",\"Demand falls and quantity demanded rises\",\"Both demand and quantity demanded fall\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Only the good’s own price changed, so buyers move along the existing curve to a lower quantity demanded. A leftward shift is tempting because people buy less, but the curve shifts only when a non-price determinant, such as income or tastes, changes.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name three changes that would shift the demand curve for a good to the right.\",\"a\":\"Any three of: higher incomes (for a normal good), a favourable change in tastes, a rise in the price of a substitute, a fall in the price of a complement, expectations of higher future prices, more consumers.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Everyday language uses “demand” loosely, and early teaching often says “when price rises, demand falls” without enforcing the distinction.\",\"diag\":[\"Raise the price on a drawn demand curve: has demand changed, or quantity demanded?\",\"What, specifically, would have to change for the whole curve to move?\"],\"intervention\":\"Adopt a strict vocabulary rule – “demand” means the curve, “quantity demanded” means a point on it – and colour-code movements and shifts on every diagram.\",\"activity\":\"Students sort six scenarios into “movement along” or “shift”, draw and label each, then peer-check the verb used in each caption.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers reserve “shift” for non-price determinants; a price-induced shift signals that the model is not secure and undermines the analysis that follows.\",\"warning\":[\"Says “demand goes down when price goes up” in discussion\",\"Draws a leftward shift in response to a price rise\",\"Labels axes “price” and “quantity” with no good named\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-02\",\"title\":\"Supply: shifts vs movements\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Higher wages mean firms supply less, so I move down along the supply curve.”\",\"right\":\"Only a change in the good’s own price causes a movement along the supply curve. A change in costs of production, technology, indirect taxes or subsidies, the prices of related goods, expectations or the number of firms shifts the whole curve: higher costs shift supply to the left.\",\"why\":\"It mirrors the demand habit: “firms supply less” sounds like a smaller quantity supplied, so a cost change gets drawn as a movement.\",\"analogy\":\"A supply curve is a firm’s price list for output. When ingredients cost more, the firm writes a new price list – it does not just read further down the old one.\",\"exam\":\"Drawing a movement along supply when costs change, or shifting supply for a change in the good’s own price. Either way the new equilibrium ends up in the wrong place.\",\"ia\":\"Describing a cost-driven price rise in the article as a “fall in quantity supplied” rather than a leftward shift of supply.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Wages paid by bakeries rise sharply. What happens in the market for bread?\",\"opts\":[\"There is a movement down along the supply curve\",\"The demand curve shifts left because bread becomes dearer\",\"Nothing shifts; bakeries simply charge more\",\"The supply curve shifts left, so price rises and quantity falls\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Wages are a cost of production, a non-price determinant, so the whole supply curve shifts left. A movement along supply would need a change in bread’s own price first; here the higher price is the result of the shift, not its cause.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What is the only thing that causes a movement along a supply curve?\",\"a\":\"A change in the good’s own price.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"By analogy with demand, students map cost changes onto price-induced movements rather than recognising a change in a determinant.\",\"diag\":[\"Wages rise for producers: does the supply curve shift, or do we move along it?\",\"What single change causes a movement along supply?\"],\"intervention\":\"Use the same colour code as for demand, and require students to consult a checklist of supply determinants before drawing any shift.\",\"activity\":\"Card sort of twelve events into “shift S left”, “shift S right” or “movement along S”; students then draw two of each.\",\"examiner\":\"In the teacher’s experience, supply-side shift/movement errors are about as frequent as demand-side ones, particularly treating a subsidy (a shift) as if it were a change in price.\",\"warning\":[\"Cannot name the single cause of a movement along supply\",\"Shifts supply “because the price went up”\",\"Treats a subsidy as a price change rather than a shift\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-03\",\"title\":\"Who sets the price?\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The price is set by the firm’s costs – demand has nothing to do with it.”\",\"right\":\"In a competitive market the equilibrium price comes from the interaction of demand and supply: a surplus pushes price down and a shortage pushes it up until the market clears. Costs matter through supply, but buyers’ willingness to pay matters just as much, and even a firm with power over price faces a demand curve that limits what it can charge.\",\"why\":\"Everyday talk says firms “set” prices, and adding a margin to cost feels like how pricing works.\",\"analogy\":\"Price is like the balance point of a seesaw: it settles where both sides push equally, not where one rider decides.\",\"exam\":\"Explaining a price change through one curve only, or forgetting to locate the new intersection. When both curves shift, missing that the change in price or quantity may be indeterminate weakens the analysis.\",\"ia\":\"Explaining the article’s price change from costs alone, without showing how demand and supply interact.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Demand for a good rises and, at the same time, supply rises. What can you conclude with confidence?\",\"opts\":[\"Quantity rises, but the change in price is uncertain\",\"Price and quantity both rise\",\"Price rises, but the change in quantity is uncertain\",\"Price and quantity both fall\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Both shifts push quantity up, so quantity rises. The price effect depends on which shift is larger: more demand pushes price up, more supply pushes it down. Saying both rise assumes the demand shift is bigger, which you cannot know without more information.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What pressure removes a shortage in a competitive market?\",\"a\":\"Excess demand leads buyers to bid the price up; as price rises, quantity demanded falls and quantity supplied rises until the market clears.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Real-world pricing talk (“they set the price”) and cost-plus intuition crowd out the idea of two interacting curves.\",\"diag\":[\"If costs fall but buyers value the good far more, what determines the new price?\",\"Can a firm charge any price it likes?\"],\"intervention\":\"Require both curves on the diagram before any discussion of price, and challenge single-cause sentences such as “the price is set by…”.\",\"activity\":\"Two-shock task: shift one curve and find the new equilibrium, then shift the other and predict the combined effect on P and Q, identifying which is indeterminate.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify when the effect on price or quantity is indeterminate after two simultaneous shifts.\",\"warning\":[\"Draws only one curve\",\"Explains price changes purely from costs\",\"States a definite price change after two opposing pressures\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-04\",\"title\":\"Steepness is not elasticity\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The demand curve is steep, so demand is inelastic all the way along it.”\",\"right\":\"PED is the ratio of percentage changes, not the slope, so a straight-line demand curve has a constant slope but a changing PED: elastic towards the top, unit elastic at the midpoint and inelastic towards the bottom (HL only). Steepness also depends on the scale of the axes; only when two curves pass through the same point on the same axes is the flatter one more elastic at that point. Judge elasticity from its determinants: substitutes, necessity, share of income and time.\",\"why\":\"Slope is visible at a glance, while percentage changes have to be worked out.\",\"analogy\":\"A $1 price cut is large on a good costing $2 and tiny on one costing $50. The same absolute change is a very different percentage change at different points on the line.\",\"exam\":\"Calling a whole straight-line curve “inelastic” because it looks steep, or using the slope in place of the PED formula. Any revenue or tax analysis built on that reading inherits the error.\",\"ia\":\"Stating that the good in the article is inelastic “because the curve is steep” instead of citing substitutes, necessity or share of income.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"HL only. On a straight-line, downward-sloping demand curve, how does PED change as price falls?\",\"opts\":[\"It stays the same, because the slope is constant\",\"It rises, from inelastic towards elastic\",\"It falls, from elastic towards inelastic\",\"It depends only on how steeply the line is drawn\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Moving down the line, each $1 change in price is a larger percentage of a lower price, while each unit change in quantity is a smaller percentage of a larger quantity, so PED falls. “It stays the same” confuses slope, which is constant, with elasticity, which is a ratio of percentage changes.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"HL only: why can a single straight-line demand curve be both elastic and inelastic?\",\"a\":\"Because PED uses percentage changes: the same absolute changes are different percentages at different price–quantity points, so PED falls as you move down the line.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Slope is visually obvious and is taught before the percentage definition is secure; a straight line looks uniformly “steep”.\",\"diag\":[\"On one straight-line demand curve: is PED the same at the top and the bottom?\",\"If I redraw the same demand schedule with a stretched price axis, has elasticity changed?\"],\"intervention\":\"Compute PED at three points on one linear curve to show that it changes, and keep the words “steep” (slope) and “inelastic” (responsiveness) separate.\",\"activity\":\"From a linear demand schedule, students calculate PED at high, middle and low prices and plot how it changes.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers justify elasticity with its determinants rather than inferring it from the slope of a sketched curve.\",\"warning\":[\"“The curve is steep so it is inelastic”\",\"Reports a single PED value for a whole straight-line curve\",\"Reasons from the sign of PED rather than its magnitude\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-05\",\"title\":\"Higher price, higher revenue?\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Raising the price always brings in more revenue.”\",\"right\":\"Total revenue is price × quantity, and a price rise cuts quantity demanded. If demand is price elastic, quantity falls proportionally more than price rises, so total revenue falls; if demand is price inelastic, total revenue rises. HL only: along a straight-line demand curve, total revenue is at its maximum where PED = 1.\",\"why\":\"Charging more per unit feels as if it must earn more; the fall in quantity is easy to overlook.\",\"analogy\":\"Think of revenue as a rectangle: price is its height and quantity its width. Raising the height only enlarges the rectangle if the width does not shrink by a larger proportion.\",\"exam\":\"Recommending a price rise to raise revenue without checking PED. The recommendation then ignores the quantity effect and is unsupported.\",\"ia\":\"Advising the firm or government in the article to raise prices for more revenue without analysing whether demand is elastic or inelastic.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A theatre faces price-elastic demand for its tickets and raises its prices. What happens to its total revenue?\",\"opts\":[\"It rises, because each ticket earns more\",\"It falls, because quantity demanded falls proportionally more than price rises\",\"It is unchanged, because the two effects cancel out\",\"It rises, because demand shifts to the right\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"With elastic demand the percentage fall in tickets sold is larger than the percentage rise in price, so price × quantity falls. “Each ticket earns more” counts only the price effect; revenue would rise only if demand were inelastic.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"HL only: at what value of PED is total revenue maximised on a straight-line demand curve?\",\"a\":\"Where PED = 1 (unit elasticity), at the midpoint of the line.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"It seems intuitive that charging more brings in more; the link between elasticity and revenue is counter-intuitive.\",\"diag\":[\"You sell concert tickets and demand is elastic. You raise the price: what happens to revenue?\",\"Where on a straight-line demand curve is revenue highest, and why?\"],\"intervention\":\"Build a total revenue table from a demand schedule; students locate where revenue peaks and link it to PED = 1.\",\"activity\":\"Students role-play pricing decisions for an elastic and an inelastic good and predict revenue before the result is revealed.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use the PED–revenue link to evaluate pricing or tax decisions rather than asserting “higher price, higher revenue”.\",\"warning\":[\"Recommends price rises “to earn more” with no mention of elasticity\",\"Ignores the fall in quantity when discussing a price change\",\"Treats revenue and profit as the same thing\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-06\",\"title\":\"Reading the sign of YED (and XED)\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.5\"],\"beyond\":\"Cross-price elasticity of demand (XED) is not in the 2022 guide; YED is (2.5). Treat the XED part of this entry as enrichment.\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A negative YED can still be a normal good, and substitutes are the ones with a negative cross elasticity.”\",\"right\":\"YED measures how quantity demanded responds to a change in income. A positive YED means a normal good (above 1: income elastic, often called a luxury), and a negative YED means an inferior good, bought less as incomes rise. For XED (not in the 2022 guide), a positive value means substitutes and a negative value means complements.\",\"why\":\"Signs are easy to memorise and just as easy to mix up under pressure if you have not reasoned them out from how buyers behave.\",\"analogy\":\"Complements travel together: when phones get dearer, fewer phones and fewer chargers are bought. Substitutes are rivals: when one gets dearer, the other gains customers.\",\"exam\":\"Mislabelling the type of good from the sign, or giving the label with no reasoning. A label tied to what buyers actually do is far more convincing than a label alone.\",\"ia\":\"Misclassifying the goods in the article, for example calling a good normal when the extract shows purchases falling as incomes rise.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"When average incomes rise by 10%, quantity demanded of a good falls by 4% (Illustrative data). What is its YED, and what type of good is it?\",\"opts\":[\"+0.4: a normal good\",\"−2.5: an inferior good\",\"+2.5: a luxury\",\"−0.4: an inferior good\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"YED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY = −4 ÷ 10 = −0.4. The negative sign means buyers purchase less as they become richer, which defines an inferior good. −2.5 comes from dividing the wrong way round.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"A good has YED = +1.6. What type of good is it?\",\"a\":\"A normal good that is income elastic, often called a luxury: quantity demanded rises proportionally more than income.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Signs are memorised rather than reasoned from the underlying relationship, so they blur under pressure.\",\"diag\":[\"YED = −0.4: normal or inferior?\",\"XED = −1.5: substitutes or complements? Explain from buyer behaviour.\"],\"intervention\":\"Sign-reasoning drill: students derive each sign from a sentence about behaviour instead of recalling a rule.\",\"activity\":\"Students sort ten goods by predicted YED sign at different income levels (and, as enrichment, ten pairs of goods by XED sign).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers tie the sign to a reason about buyer behaviour, not just a label.\",\"warning\":[\"Gets the sign right but cannot explain why\",\"Divides the percentage changes the wrong way round\",\"Gives a label without interpreting the magnitude\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-07\",\"title\":\"PES depends on time\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A good has one PES, whatever the time period.”\",\"right\":\"PES tends to rise as the time period lengthens. In the very short run output may be fixed, so supply is close to perfectly inelastic; over longer periods firms can hire, invest and enter the market, so supply becomes more elastic. Spare capacity, mobility of factors, the ability to store output and how fast costs rise as output increases also matter.\",\"why\":\"Supply is usually drawn as a single curve, which hides the fact that firms need time to respond.\",\"analogy\":\"A fishing fleet already at sea can land only what it has caught today; given a year, it can buy boats and hire crew.\",\"exam\":\"Quoting a PES value, or describing supply as elastic, without stating the time period, or ignoring spare capacity and stocks. Explanations of volatile commodity prices are then weaker.\",\"ia\":\"Explaining a price spike in the article without noting that low short-run PES magnifies the price effect of a rise in demand.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Demand for fresh fish jumps this afternoon. Why is supply more price inelastic today than it would be over a year?\",\"opts\":[\"Output cannot be raised quickly because boats, crews and catches are fixed in the short run\",\"Fish is a necessity, so buyers keep buying whatever the price\",\"Demand for fresh fish is price inelastic in the short run\",\"Fishers do not respond to higher prices, however large the rise\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"PES depends on how easily producers can change output, and that takes time. “Fish is a necessity” and “demand is inelastic” describe demand, not supply: how necessary a good is affects PED, not PES.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name two factors, other than time, that make supply more price elastic.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: spare capacity, mobile factors of production, the ability to store output, costs that rise only slowly as output increases.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Supply is taught as a single curve, so the distinction between the very short run and the long run is overlooked.\",\"diag\":[\"Why is the supply of fresh fish more inelastic this afternoon than over a year?\",\"Why might the supply of housing stay inelastic for years?\"],\"intervention\":\"Map the same good’s PES across the very short run, short run and long run, tying each stage to factor mobility.\",\"activity\":\"Students predict short- and long-run supply responses for oil, software, milk, gold and housing, and justify each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers link low short-run PES to volatile primary-commodity prices (why PES for primary commodities is generally low is HL only).\",\"warning\":[\"Quotes a PES value with no time horizon\",\"Confuses the determinants of PED with those of PES\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-08\",\"title\":\"Consumer and producer surplus\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.3\",\"2.7\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Any surplus lost after a tax is deadweight loss – it all disappears.”\",\"right\":\"Consumer surplus is the area below the demand curve and above the price: the value buyers receive beyond what they pay. Producer surplus is the area above the supply curve and below the price. After an indirect tax, part of the original surplus becomes government revenue (a transfer), and only the remainder – the triangle between the old and new quantities – is deadweight loss.\",\"why\":\"The triangles are easy to memorise by position and easy to swap once the diagram changes.\",\"analogy\":\"Consumer surplus is the bargain you did not have to pay for; producer surplus is what a seller receives above the lowest price they would accept. A tax skims a slice off for the government and spills a little on the floor: the spill is the deadweight loss.\",\"exam\":\"Shading the wrong area, forgetting deadweight loss, or counting tax revenue as a loss to society. The welfare conclusion then rests on the wrong areas.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming that a tax in the article “destroys” all the surplus consumers and producers lose, when part of it becomes government revenue.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"An indirect tax is imposed on a competitive market. Which area is lost to society as a whole rather than transferred?\",\"opts\":[\"The tax revenue collected by the government\",\"The whole fall in consumer surplus\",\"The deadweight-loss triangle between the old and new quantities\",\"The whole fall in producer surplus\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Tax revenue is a transfer from consumers and producers to the government, so society as a whole does not lose it. Only the deadweight loss – the surplus on units no longer traded – disappears. The whole falls in consumer or producer surplus both include part of that transferred revenue.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"After an indirect tax, what does the original community surplus split into?\",\"a\":\"The remaining consumer surplus, the remaining producer surplus, government tax revenue and deadweight loss.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The triangles are memorised by position rather than understood as value minus price (or price minus cost).\",\"diag\":[\"Shade consumer surplus on this diagram.\",\"Now add a tax: which areas become government revenue, and which become deadweight loss?\"],\"intervention\":\"Re-derive each area from its definition every time rather than recalling positions, and label value, price and cost on every diagram.\",\"activity\":\"Before-and-after-tax shading race: students colour consumer surplus, producer surplus, revenue and deadweight loss and check that the areas add up to the original total.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers separate the transfer to government from deadweight loss; conflating the two undermines the welfare analysis.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls all lost surplus “deadweight loss”\",\"Shades producer surplus above the price line\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-09\",\"title\":\"Who really pays an indirect tax?\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.7\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The tax is placed on producers, so producers pay all of it.”\",\"right\":\"The burden (incidence) of an indirect tax is shared between consumers and producers according to relative price elasticities, whoever legally hands the money to the government. The more inelastic side of the market bears the larger share: if demand is more price inelastic than supply, consumers pay most of the tax through a higher price.\",\"why\":\"The legal wording – “a tax on producers” – sounds like a description of who bears the cost.\",\"analogy\":\"A tax is a hot potato: whoever can pass it on most easily ends up holding it least. The side that cannot adjust – the inelastic side – gets burned.\",\"exam\":\"Assuming the whole tax is passed on, or none of it, without reference to elasticity. The stakeholder analysis then puts the burden in the wrong place.\",\"ia\":\"Stating that “the firm pays the tax” in the article without analysing how inelastic demand shifts the burden to consumers.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A specific tax is placed on cigarette producers. Demand is price inelastic and supply is relatively elastic. Who bears most of the tax?\",\"opts\":[\"Producers, because the tax is levied on them\",\"Consumers, because they cut back little when the price rises\",\"The government, because it collects the revenue\",\"Consumers and producers equally\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Incidence depends on elasticities, not on who is legally liable. With inelastic demand, producers can pass most of the tax on as a higher price because buyers reduce their purchases only slightly. “Producers, because the tax is levied on them” confuses the legal side with the economic burden.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Does the side of the market on which a tax is legally levied decide who bears it?\",\"a\":\"No. The burden is shared according to relative elasticities; the more inelastic side bears more.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Statutory wording (“a tax on producers”) is read as economic burden, and the role of elasticity is not yet intuitive.\",\"diag\":[\"A specific tax is placed on cigarette producers: who pays most of it, and why?\",\"What would change if demand were perfectly elastic?\"],\"intervention\":\"Vary PED and PES for a fixed tax to show the burden split changing, and separate “who is taxed” from “who pays”.\",\"activity\":\"Three markets with different elasticities: students predict, then calculate, the consumer and producer shares of the tax (the calculation is HL only).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers let elasticity drive the burden analysis rather than asserting that one side bears the whole tax.\",\"warning\":[\"“The producer pays the tax”\",\"Splits the burden equally whatever the elasticities\",\"Calculates tax revenue using the pre-tax quantity\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-10\",\"title\":\"How much of a subsidy reaches consumers?\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.7\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A $2 subsidy cuts the price consumers pay by exactly $2.”\",\"right\":\"A per-unit subsidy shifts supply down by the amount of the subsidy, but the price consumers pay usually falls by less than the subsidy. The benefit is shared between consumers (a lower price paid) and producers (a higher price received once the subsidy is included), according to relative elasticities. The government pays the subsidy on every unit sold, so its cost is the subsidy per unit × the new, larger quantity.\",\"why\":\"It seems obvious that a payment designed to lower prices should lower them by its full amount.\",\"analogy\":\"A subsidy is like a discount voucher shared at the till: part of its value lands in the shopper’s pocket, part in the seller’s, and the issuer pays the full face value.\",\"exam\":\"Drawing the consumer price falling by the full subsidy, or leaving out the cost to the government and its opportunity cost. The stakeholder analysis is then incomplete.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating the benefit to consumers of the subsidy in the article, and omitting the producers’ share and the cost to the government.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A $3 per-unit subsidy is paid to producers of a good with price-elastic demand and upward-sloping supply (Illustrative data). What happens to the price consumers pay?\",\"opts\":[\"It falls by exactly $3\",\"It does not change\",\"It falls by more than $3\",\"It falls by less than $3\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"The subsidy shifts supply down by $3, but as quantity rises along the demand curve, producers keep part of the subsidy as a higher price received. The consumer price falls by the full $3 only in extreme cases, such as perfectly inelastic demand or perfectly elastic supply.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"How do you calculate the total cost to the government of a per-unit subsidy?\",\"a\":\"Subsidy per unit × the new quantity produced and sold after the subsidy.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"It seems that a $2 subsidy must cut the price by $2; the sharing of the benefit through elasticity is not seen.\",\"diag\":[\"A $3 subsidy is given: will the consumer price fall by exactly $3? What decides how much it falls?\",\"Which quantity do you use to calculate the government’s outlay?\"],\"intervention\":\"Teach subsidy incidence as the mirror image of tax incidence, and compute the consumer share, producer share and government cost.\",\"activity\":\"Given a subsidy and a diagram, students calculate the new consumer price, producer revenue and total government outlay (the calculation is HL only).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers note the shared benefit and the opportunity cost of the subsidy, not just “prices fall”.\",\"warning\":[\"Says the price falls by the full subsidy\",\"Calculates the subsidy cost using the original quantity\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-11\",\"title\":\"Where a price ceiling bites\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.7\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A maximum price is a high price, so I draw it above equilibrium.”\",\"right\":\"A price ceiling (maximum price) only has an effect if it is set below the equilibrium price. It then creates a shortage: quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at the controlled price. Likely consequences include queues, non-price rationing, parallel (black) markets and falling quality; buyers who obtain the good gain, but others go without.\",\"why\":\"“Maximum” sounds like “high”, and the condition for the ceiling to bind is easy to forget.\",\"analogy\":\"A ceiling is a low doorway: it only makes people stoop if it is below their heads. Set higher, nobody notices it.\",\"exam\":\"Placing the ceiling above equilibrium, leaving out the shortage, or claiming that it helps everyone. The evaluation then misses who gains and who loses.\",\"ia\":\"Analysing a rent-control article without showing the shortage or the non-price rationing it creates.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Equilibrium rent in a city is $1,000 a month (Illustrative data). Which maximum rent would create a shortage?\",\"opts\":[\"$1,200\",\"$1,000\",\"$800\",\"Any maximum rent creates a shortage\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A ceiling binds only below equilibrium: at $800 quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied. At $1,200 the ceiling is above the market rent and has no effect, and at $1,000 the market already clears.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name two consequences of a binding price ceiling other than a lower price.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: a shortage, queues or waiting lists, non-price rationing, parallel (black) markets, falling quality.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"“Maximum price” is confused with “high price”, and the binding condition (below equilibrium) is forgotten.\",\"diag\":[\"For a ceiling to do anything, must it be above or below equilibrium?\",\"Draw it and mark the shortage.\"],\"intervention\":\"Drill the binding condition first, and require the shortage to be labelled before any evaluation.\",\"activity\":\"Give three ceiling levels (above, at and below equilibrium); students identify which bind and quantify the shortage.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify the shortage, declining quality and parallel markets; weak answers stop at “prices are lower”.\",\"warning\":[\"A ceiling drawn above equilibrium\",\"A shortage described for a non-binding ceiling\",\"An evaluation claiming that everyone benefits\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-12\",\"title\":\"Price floors and the minimum wage\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.7\",\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":\"Monopsony is not named in the 2022 guide; the monopsony exception mentioned here is enrichment.\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A minimum price creates a shortage, just like a maximum price.”\",\"right\":\"A price floor (minimum price) only binds if it is set above the equilibrium price, and it then creates a surplus: quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded. A binding minimum wage is a floor in the labour market, so the basic competitive model predicts an excess supply of labour – unemployment in that market. Real-world effects depend on elasticities and market structure, and evidence on minimum wages is mixed.\",\"why\":\"The words are spatially confusing – a “floor” sits above equilibrium – and in the labour market firms are the buyers and workers the sellers, which reverses the usual roles.\",\"analogy\":\"A floor is a raised stage: set the fee for performing above the market level and more performers turn up than there are slots – a surplus of acts.\",\"exam\":\"Predicting a shortage from a price floor, drawing the minimum wage below equilibrium where it does not bind, or swapping labour demand and supply. The conclusion about employment is then reversed.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming the minimum wage in the article causes “labour shortages”, or leaving out the excess supply of labour.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A binding minimum wage is set above the equilibrium wage in a competitive labour market. What does the basic model predict?\",\"opts\":[\"A shortage of labour\",\"No change in employment\",\"Higher employment and no surplus\",\"Excess supply of labour (unemployment)\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"At a wage above equilibrium, more people want to work while firms want to hire fewer, so the quantity of labour supplied exceeds the quantity demanded. A shortage reverses the floor and ceiling logic; higher employment is possible only under different assumptions, such as monopsony, which is not in the 2022 guide.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Does a binding price floor create a surplus or a shortage?\",\"a\":\"A surplus (excess supply): quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at the floor.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Floor and ceiling vocabulary is spatially counter-intuitive (a floor sits above equilibrium), and the labour-market context swaps buyers and sellers in students’ minds.\",\"diag\":[\"A minimum wage above the equilibrium wage: labour shortage or surplus?\",\"In the labour market, who demands labour and who supplies it?\"],\"intervention\":\"Contrast floor and ceiling side by side on the same axes, and flag the monopsony exception as enrichment beyond the 2022 guide.\",\"activity\":\"Compare a minimum wage in a competitive labour market with one in a monopsony (enrichment); students predict the employment effect in each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers label the surplus clearly and weigh the minimum wage with nuance; reversing floor and ceiling effects is the error to watch for.\",\"warning\":[\"Expects a shortage from a floor\",\"Draws the minimum wage below equilibrium\",\"Swaps the labour demand and supply curves\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-13\",\"title\":\"Externalities are not just pollution\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.8\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“An externality means pollution, and MSC sits below MPC.”\",\"right\":\"An externality is any cost or benefit that falls on a third party, not only pollution; it can be negative or positive, in production or in consumption. In a negative production externality, marginal social cost exceeds marginal private cost (MSC > MPC), so the free market produces more than the social optimum. The welfare loss is the triangle between MSC and MSB, running from the social optimum (where MSC = MSB) to the market quantity.\",\"why\":\"Pollution is the headline example, and the four curves are easy to memorise without asking who bears each cost or benefit.\",\"analogy\":\"Private cost is the bill the producer sees; social cost adds the unpaid bill left for the neighbours. Reading only its own bill, the market orders too much.\",\"exam\":\"Placing MSC below MPC, drawing the welfare-loss triangle between the wrong curves, or ignoring positive externalities. The welfare analysis then points the wrong way.\",\"ia\":\"Treating every market failure in the article as “pollution” and mislabelling the cost and benefit curves.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"In a negative production externality, where is the socially optimal level of output?\",\"opts\":[\"Where MSC = MSB\",\"Where MPC = MPB\",\"Where MSC = MPC\",\"Where price is highest\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The social optimum counts all costs and benefits, so it is where MSC = MSB. The market settles where MPC = MPB, a larger output, because producers ignore the external cost.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"In a negative production externality, is MSC above or below MPC, and why?\",\"a\":\"Above: MSC = MPC + the external cost imposed on third parties.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Pollution is the headline example, and the four curves are memorised rather than reasoned from “who bears the cost or benefit?”.\",\"diag\":[\"In a negative production externality, which is higher, MSC or MPC, and why?\",\"Where exactly is the welfare loss?\"],\"intervention\":\"Build the four curves from definitions (“who pays?”, “who benefits?”) and derive the social optimum before the market outcome.\",\"activity\":\"Curve-placement card sort across the four types of externality; students mark the market quantity, the optimum quantity and the welfare loss.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers place the curves correctly and anchor the welfare-loss triangle at the social optimum.\",\"warning\":[\"Equates “externality” with “pollution”\",\"Shifts the wrong curve for a consumption externality\",\"Draws the triangle with its apex away from the social optimum\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-14\",\"title\":\"Positive externalities vs merit goods\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.8\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A merit good and a positive externality are the same thing.”\",\"right\":\"A positive consumption externality means third parties gain when someone consumes the good, so marginal social benefit exceeds marginal private benefit (MSB > MPB) and the market under-consumes relative to the social optimum. (A positive production externality separates the cost curves instead: MSC lies below MPC.) A merit good is under-consumed partly because people underestimate its benefit to themselves – an information failure – and governments may also judge that everyone should have it on equity grounds. Many goods, such as education and vaccination, are both, but the two ideas are not identical.\",\"why\":\"The standard examples overlap, so the two mechanisms blur together.\",\"analogy\":\"A positive externality is a gift to bystanders: your vaccination protects others. A merit good is a present you underrate for yourself until someone explains its worth.\",\"exam\":\"Using the two terms interchangeably or mislabelling MSB and MPB. The argument about why intervention is needed then becomes vague.\",\"ia\":\"Justifying intervention by calling the article’s good a “merit good” when the argument is really about benefits to third parties, or the reverse.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Why may a merit good be under-consumed even when there are no spillover effects?\",\"opts\":[\"MSB is below MPB\",\"Producers refuse to supply it\",\"Consumers underestimate its private benefit because of imperfect information\",\"Its price is too high for everyone\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Merit goods are under-consumed partly because people undervalue what the good does for them, an information failure. “MSB is below MPB” reverses the positive-externality condition, and in any case a spillover is not needed for a good to be a merit good.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"In a positive consumption externality, which is higher: MSB or MPB?\",\"a\":\"MSB, because third parties also benefit from the consumption.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The two ideas overlap in the standard examples (education, vaccines), so the distinct mechanisms blur.\",\"diag\":[\"Is a merit good under-consumed because of an external benefit, because consumers undervalue it, or both?\",\"Are the two ideas the same thing? Give a case that is one but not the other.\"],\"intervention\":\"Separate the two definitions explicitly and show a case that is one but not clearly the other.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify eight goods or services as positive externality, merit good, both or neither, with a justification for each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use the terms precisely; loose interchange weakens the market-failure argument.\",\"warning\":[\"Uses “merit good” and “positive externality” as synonyms\",\"Labels MSB and MPB the wrong way round\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-15\",\"title\":\"What makes a good “public”\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.9\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A public good is anything the government provides.”\",\"right\":\"In economics a public good is defined by two properties: it is non-excludable (people cannot be prevented from using it) and non-rivalrous (one person’s use does not reduce what is available to others). These cause the free-rider problem, so the market may not provide the good at all, whoever ends up supplying it. Many government-provided services, such as state schooling or hospital care, are rival and excludable, so they are not public goods; they are usually classed as merit goods because of their external benefits or because people may undervalue them.\",\"why\":\"The word “public” is read literally, so who provides the good gets confused with what kind of good it is.\",\"analogy\":\"A lighthouse beam cannot be fenced off, and one ship’s use does not dim it for another. A hospital bed is neither: access can be restricted, and a bed in use is not available to the next patient.\",\"exam\":\"Calling state-provided private or merit goods “public goods”, or naming only one of the two properties. Classifying by provider rather than by the two properties undermines the market-failure analysis.\",\"ia\":\"Labelling the article’s good a public good to justify government provision when it is excludable or rival.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Which of these is the best example of a public good?\",\"opts\":[\"State schooling\",\"Street lighting\",\"A public hospital\",\"A toll motorway\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Street lighting is non-excludable and non-rivalrous: no one can easily be prevented from benefiting, and one person’s use does not reduce anyone else’s. Schools and hospitals are often state-provided, but places in them are rival and access can be restricted, so they are not public goods; they are usually classed as merit goods.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Why does non-excludability create the free-rider problem?\",\"a\":\"If people cannot be excluded, they can benefit without paying, so few will pay and private firms cannot cover their costs.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The label “public” is read literally, and provision is confused with the defining characteristics.\",\"diag\":[\"Is public healthcare a public good in the economic sense? Is a firework display?\",\"What two properties define a public good?\"],\"intervention\":\"Test every candidate good against the two properties before any discussion of provision.\",\"activity\":\"Two-by-two grid (rival/non-rival × excludable/non-excludable): students place ten goods and identify the pure public goods.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers classify by the two properties; “the government provides it” carries little weight.\",\"warning\":[\"Applies “public good” to any state service\",\"Names non-excludability alone\",\"Assumes public goods have no opportunity cost because they are free at the point of use\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-16\",\"title\":\"Common pool resources are rival\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.8\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A fishery is a public good because nobody can be excluded from it.”\",\"right\":\"Common pool (common access) resources are non-excludable but rivalrous: a fish caught by one boat cannot be caught by another. Because no one owns the stock and each user ignores the effect of their use on others, the resource tends to be over-used and depleted – the tragedy of the commons. Responses include property rights, quotas and tradable permits, taxes, regulation, international agreements and collective self-governance.\",\"why\":\"Public goods and common pool resources are both non-excludable, so it is easy to forget that common pool resources are rival.\",\"analogy\":\"A common pasture is an open buffet with no host: because no one owns the next plate, everyone overeats and the table empties.\",\"exam\":\"Treating a fishery or the atmosphere as a public good and predicting under-provision instead of over-use. The policy discussion then targets the wrong problem.\",\"ia\":\"Framing an overfishing or deforestation article as a public-good problem and missing the tragedy of the commons.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Why are common pool resources such as ocean fisheries over-used?\",\"opts\":[\"They are non-rivalrous, so one boat’s catch leaves as much for every other boat\",\"They are non-rivalrous and non-excludable, so firms will not provide them\",\"Governments hold the price of fish below its equilibrium level\",\"They are non-excludable and rivalrous, so each user ignores the cost of depletion to others\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Rivalry means each catch reduces what others can take, while non-excludability means no one can stop users; together they lead to over-use. “Firms will not provide them” describes the under-provision of public goods, a different problem.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"How do common pool resources differ from public goods?\",\"a\":\"Both are non-excludable, but common pool resources are rivalrous, so they are over-used; public goods are non-rivalrous and tend to be under-provided.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both are non-excludable, so students lump them together and forget that common pool resources are rival.\",\"diag\":[\"A fishery is non-excludable, but is it non-rival?\",\"What problem would you predict for it: under-provision or over-use?\"],\"intervention\":\"Contrast public goods (under-provided) with common pool resources (over-used) on the rival/excludable grid.\",\"activity\":\"Students design a quota or property-rights scheme for a shared fishery and predict its effect on the catch and on the stock.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify rivalry and over-use; conflation with public goods is the error to probe.\",\"warning\":[\"Predicts under-provision for a fishery\",\"Calls the atmosphere or a fishery a public good\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-17\",\"title\":\"Adverse selection vs moral hazard (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.10\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Adverse selection and moral hazard are two names for the same thing.”\",\"right\":\"Adverse selection arises before a transaction, when one side has hidden information about its own characteristics or the quality of what it sells – for example, people who know they are high-risk are keenest to buy insurance. Moral hazard arises after a transaction, when one side changes its behaviour because someone else bears the cost – for example, taking less care once insured. The responses differ: screening, signalling and disclosure rules for adverse selection; excesses, co-payments and monitoring for moral hazard.\",\"why\":\"Both come under “asymmetric information”, and the difference depends on timing, which is easy to overlook.\",\"analogy\":\"Adverse selection is finding that most used cars still on sale are poor ones, because owners of good cars will not sell at a price based on average quality; moral hazard is driving carelessly because the insurer pays for any damage.\",\"exam\":\"Swapping the two terms or matching the wrong remedy to each. The market-failure analysis then misidentifies the problem.\",\"ia\":\"Misnaming the information problem in the article, so the proposed remedy does not fit it.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"After buying comprehensive phone insurance, a student stops using a protective case. Which problem is this?\",\"opts\":[\"Moral hazard\",\"Adverse selection\",\"A negative externality\",\"The free-rider problem\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The change in behaviour happens after the contract, and the insurer cannot observe it: that is moral hazard. Adverse selection concerns hidden characteristics before the deal, such as careless owners being the keenest to buy cover.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Which problem occurs before the transaction, and which after?\",\"a\":\"Adverse selection occurs before (hidden information); moral hazard occurs after (hidden action).\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both fall under asymmetric information, and the timing distinction is subtle.\",\"diag\":[\"High-risk people buying insurance most eagerly: which problem?\",\"Drivers taking more risk once insured: which problem?\"],\"intervention\":\"Sort scenarios by “before the deal” or “after the deal” and match each to its remedy.\",\"activity\":\"Students match six real cases to adverse selection or moral hazard and name a suitable government or private response for each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers label the problem by its timing and pair it with a matching remedy.\",\"warning\":[\"Confident but reversed definitions\",\"Attributes signalling to the uninformed party\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-18\",\"title\":\"Monopoly maximises profit, not price (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.11\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A monopoly charges the highest price it can, and perfect competition describes most real markets.”\",\"right\":\"A monopolist maximises profit where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR) and reads the price from the demand (AR) curve; charging the highest conceivable price would lose most of its customers. In the standard model monopoly is allocatively inefficient (price above MC), but economies of scale may give it lower costs than many small firms, and abnormal profit can fund research and development. Perfect competition is a benchmark for comparison, rarely observed in full.\",\"why\":\"Monopoly is often cast as the villain and perfect competition as the ideal, so the nuance is lost.\",\"analogy\":\"A monopolist is less like a child grabbing the biggest number and more like a chess player choosing the price–quantity move that wins most overall, which is rarely the extreme.\",\"exam\":\"Claiming that a monopolist sets the highest price, ignoring economies of scale, or treating perfect competition as common. One-sided condemnation weakens the evaluation.\",\"ia\":\"Applying a perfect-competition model to a clearly imperfect market in the article, or asserting harm from monopoly without evaluation.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Where does a profit-maximising monopolist choose its output?\",\"opts\":[\"Where price is highest\",\"Where AR = AC\",\"Where MC = MR\",\"Where MR = 0\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Profit is maximised where the extra revenue from one more unit equals its extra cost, MC = MR; the price is then read from the demand (AR) curve. MR = 0 maximises revenue, not profit, and the highest possible price would mean selling almost nothing.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Give one potential benefit of monopoly.\",\"a\":\"Economies of scale that lower average costs, or abnormal profit that funds research and development.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Monopoly is taught as the villain and perfect competition as the ideal, so nuance is lost.\",\"diag\":[\"Does a monopolist maximise price or profit?\",\"Can a monopoly ever be more efficient than many small firms?\",\"Name a truly perfectly competitive market.\"],\"intervention\":\"Contrast profit maximisation with price maximisation graphically, and list real markets by structure to show how rare perfect competition is.\",\"activity\":\"Students evaluate one monopoly case for both harms and possible benefits, then classify ten markets by structure.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers evaluate monopoly in a balanced way – economies of scale and innovation against allocative inefficiency; one-sided condemnation is weaker.\",\"warning\":[\"“Monopolies charge the highest price”\",\"Marks the price on the MR curve instead of reading up to AR\",\"Treats perfect competition as a description of most markets\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-19\",\"title\":\"Costs: short run, long run and MC–AC (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.11\",\"2.2\"],\"beyond\":\"The 2022 guide does not list short- and long-run cost theory as separate content; MC and AC appear in the 2.11 market-power diagrams (HL only), and diminishing marginal returns in 2.2 (HL only).\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Fixed costs stay fixed in the long run, and MC can cross AC anywhere.”\",\"right\":\"In the long run all factors of production can be varied, so all costs are variable; fixed costs exist only in the short run. Marginal cost cuts average cost at the minimum of the AC curve: when MC is below AC it pulls the average down, and when MC is above AC it pulls the average up.\",\"why\":\"The short run–long run distinction is abstract, and the MC–AC geometry is easy to memorise without understanding it.\",\"analogy\":\"MC is your latest test score and AC your running average: a score below the average drags it down, one above pulls it up, so the two meet where the average is lowest.\",\"exam\":\"Labelling costs as fixed on a long-run diagram, or drawing MC cutting AC away from its minimum. The diagram is then internally inconsistent.\",\"ia\":\"Misapplying cost concepts when the article discusses a firm expanding or cutting its capacity.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Average cost is falling as output rises. What must be true of marginal cost?\",\"opts\":[\"MC is above AC\",\"MC is below AC\",\"MC equals AC\",\"MC is falling\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"An average falls only when the extra unit costs less than the current average, so MC < AC. MC can be rising while still below AC, so “MC is falling” is not required.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Where does MC intersect AC, and why?\",\"a\":\"At the minimum of AC, because AC falls while MC is below it and rises once MC is above it.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The short run–long run distinction is abstract, and the MC–AC geometry is memorised rather than understood.\",\"diag\":[\"Are there any fixed costs in the long run?\",\"When AC is falling, is MC above or below it?\"],\"intervention\":\"Derive the MC–AC crossing from the logic of averages rather than by recall, and stress that all costs are variable in the long run.\",\"activity\":\"Students build a cost table, locate the minimum of AC and check that MC equals AC there.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers draw MC through the minimum of AC and can explain why.\",\"warning\":[\"Fixed costs shown on a long-run diagram\",\"MC drawn cutting AC away from its minimum\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MIC-20\",\"title\":\"Behavioural economics is real economics (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Microeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"2.4\",\"2.7\"],\"beyond\":\"Loss aversion and the sunk-cost fallacy are not among the biases the 2022 guide names (rule of thumb, anchoring and framing, availability); treat them as enrichment.\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Economics assumes people are perfectly rational, so biases and nudges are not real economics.”\",\"right\":\"Behavioural economics (HL only) tests the assumptions of rational consumer choice against how people actually decide. People show bounded rationality, bounded self-control and bounded selfishness, and systematic biases such as rules of thumb, anchoring and framing, and availability. Governments can use choice architecture – default, restricted and mandated choices – and nudges, not only prices, to influence behaviour.\",\"why\":\"Early units rely on rational-choice models, so behavioural ideas can feel like an afterthought.\",\"analogy\":\"People are less like flawless calculators and more like busy commuters who take the default route; a well-placed signpost (a nudge) changes their path without forcing them.\",\"exam\":\"Evaluating an intervention only with price-based models and ignoring defaults, framing or bounded rationality. The evaluation then misses a relevant perspective.\",\"ia\":\"Missing the behavioural angle when the article is about defaults, nudges or choice architecture.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A government switches organ-donor registration from opt-in to opt-out, and registrations rise although nobody’s options have changed. Which idea best explains this?\",\"opts\":[\"Perfect information\",\"A price incentive\",\"Diminishing marginal utility\",\"The default effect in choice architecture\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Many people stay with whatever option is pre-set, so changing the default changes behaviour without changing prices or banning anything. A fully informed, fully rational chooser would pick the same option under either default, so perfect information cannot explain the change.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Define bounded rationality.\",\"a\":\"The idea that people decide with limited information, time and mental capacity, so they often settle for choices that are good enough rather than optimal.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Rational-choice models dominate early units, so behavioural ideas feel like an afterthought.\",\"diag\":[\"Why does opt-out organ donation raise registrations if people are fully rational?\",\"What is a sunk cost, and why should it not affect a forward-looking decision?\"],\"intervention\":\"Run a quick default or framing demonstration in class, then connect it to real nudge policies.\",\"activity\":\"Students design a nudge for a school behaviour (recycling, attendance) and predict its effect through a named bias.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers bring behavioural evaluation of interventions alongside traditional price-based analysis.\",\"warning\":[\"Dismisses nudges as “not economics”\",\"Treats “irrational” as “random” rather than systematic\",\"Presents nudges as costless\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-01\",\"title\":\"GDP is not well-being\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.1\",\"4.8\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Higher GDP means people are better off.”\",\"right\":\"GDP measures the value of output produced, not well-being. It leaves out how income is distributed, much non-market and informal activity, leisure, environmental damage and what the output actually consists of. Real GDP per capita is a useful but partial indicator, best read alongside measures such as the HDI or the OECD Better Life Index.\",\"why\":\"GDP is the headline number in the news, and “the economy is growing” sounds like “people are better off”.\",\"analogy\":\"GDP is a shop’s turnover; well-being is whether the family running it is healthy and happy. A busy shop does not necessarily mean a thriving family.\",\"exam\":\"Concluding that higher GDP means higher living standards with no qualification. Without limitations such as distribution, informal activity and externalities, the answer stays descriptive.\",\"ia\":\"Treating a growth figure in the article as evidence of rising welfare without discussing distribution or sustainability.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Two countries have the same real GDP per capita. Which difference would most directly mean their living standards differ?\",\"opts\":[\"Income is far more unequally distributed in one of them\",\"They use different currencies\",\"One publishes GDP figures more often\",\"One has a larger population\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"GDP per capita is an average, so it hides how income is shared: a very unequal distribution can leave many people far worse off at the same average. Population size is already allowed for by measuring per capita.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name three things GDP leaves out that affect well-being.\",\"a\":\"Any three of: the distribution of income, non-market and informal activity, leisure, environmental damage, the composition of output, health and education outcomes.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"GDP is the headline number, and “the economy is growing” is equated with “people are better off”.\",\"diag\":[\"Two countries have identical GDP per head: give three reasons their living standards could differ.\",\"Can GDP rise while well-being falls? How?\"],\"intervention\":\"Pair every GDP claim with at least one limitation, and introduce composite indicators such as the HDI as complements.\",\"activity\":\"Given two economies with equal GDP, students list differences in well-being and rank them by importance.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers evaluate the limits of growth figures; uncritical “growth is good” answers stay at the level of description.\",\"warning\":[\"Makes GDP claims with no qualification\",\"Uses “GDP” and “living standards” as synonyms\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-02\",\"title\":\"Real vs nominal\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.1\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Nominal GDP went up, so the economy grew.”\",\"right\":\"Nominal values are measured at current prices; real values remove the effect of price changes. If the price level rises faster than nominal GDP, real GDP falls even though the nominal figure has risen. To judge changes over time, compare real values – and real values per capita when living standards are the question.\",\"why\":\"Money values are concrete and visible; adjusting them with a price index or deflator is an extra step that is easy to skip.\",\"analogy\":\"Nominal is the number on your pay slip; real is what that pay actually buys. A bigger number means little if prices rose faster.\",\"exam\":\"Claiming growth from nominal figures, or forgetting the price-index adjustment. The conclusion can then point in the wrong direction.\",\"ia\":\"Using nominal data from the article to claim a real change in output or wages.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Nominal GDP rises by 5% while the GDP deflator rises by 6% (Illustrative data). What happened to real GDP?\",\"opts\":[\"It rose by about 5%\",\"It rose by about 11%\",\"It fell by about 1%\",\"It was unchanged\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Real growth is roughly nominal growth minus the rise in the price level: 5% − 6% ≈ −1%. Answering “about 5%” reports the nominal figure as if it were real.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"How do you convert nominal GDP to real GDP using a deflator expressed as an index?\",\"a\":\"Real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ price deflator × 100.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Money values are concrete and visible; the deflator step is an extra abstraction.\",\"diag\":[\"Nominal GDP rose 5% while the deflator rose 6%: did the economy grow?\",\"Why do we divide by the deflator and multiply by 100?\"],\"intervention\":\"Require an explicit real-or-nominal check on any time-series data before conclusions are drawn.\",\"activity\":\"Students convert three nominal series to real values using a given deflator and re-interpret each trend.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers deflate before interpreting a trend; uncritical comparison of nominal values is the weakness to target.\",\"warning\":[\"Growth claims from money values with no mention of inflation\",\"Divides by the deflator but forgets to multiply by 100\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-03\",\"title\":\"Growth is not development\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"4.8\",\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Economic growth and economic development mean the same thing.”\",\"right\":\"Economic growth is an increase in real output (real GDP). Economic development is a broader, multidimensional improvement in people’s lives: health, education, freedom, equity and sustainability as well as income. Growth can support development, but a country can grow without developing if the gains are narrowly shared or come at the cost of health and the environment.\",\"why\":\"Both sound positive and are often discussed together, so the line between them blurs.\",\"analogy\":\"Growth is a more powerful engine; development is whether the trip actually improves the passengers’ lives. A faster engine on a road to nowhere is not progress.\",\"exam\":\"Answering a development question purely with growth analysis and ignoring health, education and equity. The answer then misses the focus of the question.\",\"ia\":\"Treating an article about development as a pure growth story and missing its multidimensional side.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Which observation best shows growth without broad development?\",\"opts\":[\"Real GDP and literacy rates both rise steadily over a decade\",\"The HDI rises while real GDP stays unchanged\",\"Real GDP rises while life expectancy and school enrolment stagnate for most people\",\"Real GDP per capita falls sharply during a recession\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Output grew, but core development indicators did not improve for most people, so the gains were not broadly shared. A rising HDI with flat GDP is the opposite case: some development without growth.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Give one way growth can fail to bring development.\",\"a\":\"For example: the gains go to a small group; growth depletes resources or harms health; output rises in sectors that do little for most people’s lives.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both terms sound positive and are often discussed together, so the conceptual line blurs.\",\"diag\":[\"Can a country grow without developing?\",\"Give an example of growth that did not improve well-being broadly.\"],\"intervention\":\"Distinguish the two concepts on every relevant question; map growth on one axis and development indicators on another.\",\"activity\":\"Students find a real case of growth without broad development and justify the distinction with indicators.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers keep the concepts distinct and use development indicators when the question is about development.\",\"warning\":[\"Uses the two words interchangeably\",\"Cites GDP data alone for a development question\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-04\",\"title\":\"AD is not market demand\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“AD slopes down for the same reason a demand curve does: people switch to cheaper goods.”\",\"right\":\"Aggregate demand is total planned spending on an economy’s output: AD = C + I + G + (X − M). It slopes downward for macroeconomic reasons – a lower price level raises the real value of wealth, tends to lower interest rates and makes exports more competitive – not because buyers switch between goods. A change in any component at a given price level shifts AD.\",\"why\":\"The AD curve looks exactly like a market demand curve, so micro reasoning gets carried across.\",\"analogy\":\"Market demand is one shop’s customers choosing between products; AD is the whole nation’s spending flowing through four taps: households, firms, government and net exports.\",\"exam\":\"Explaining AD’s slope with substitution between goods, or leaving out the components. Either signals that the macro model has been confused with the micro one.\",\"ia\":\"Attributing a change in AD in the article to a single-market mechanism rather than to a change in one of its components.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Which is a valid reason why the AD curve slopes downward?\",\"opts\":[\"Consumers switch to relatively cheaper goods\",\"A lower price level raises the real value of wealth, so consumption rises\",\"Firms supply less at lower prices\",\"A lower price level shifts AD to the right\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The wealth effect is a macro reason: when the whole price level falls, the same money holdings buy more, so spending rises. Switching to cheaper goods is the micro substitution effect, which does not apply when all prices fall together.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"State the four components of AD.\",\"a\":\"Consumption (C), investment (I), government spending (G) and net exports (X − M).\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The downward slope looks identical to a micro demand curve, so micro reasoning is transplanted.\",\"diag\":[\"Why does AD slope down – is it the same reason as for a single market’s demand?\",\"List the components of AD.\"],\"intervention\":\"Teach the four components first, and keep the macro reasons for the slope separate from micro logic.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify ten shocks by the AD component they affect and the direction of the shift.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers name the components and give macro reasons for the slope; substitution-based explanations signal confusion.\",\"warning\":[\"Explains AD’s slope by “switching to cheaper goods”\",\"Omits net exports or government spending\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-05\",\"title\":\"The price level is not a price\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“When the price level falls, AD shifts to the right.”\",\"right\":\"The vertical axis of the AD–AS diagram measures the average price level of all goods and services, not the price of one good. A change in the price level causes a movement along AD or AS. A change in a determinant – a component of AD, or costs, indirect taxes and productivity for AS – shifts the curve.\",\"why\":\"The diagram mirrors micro demand and supply, so the habit of reading the axis as one good’s price carries over.\",\"analogy\":\"The price level is like the average temperature across a whole country, not the temperature in your street. Reading the axis as one good’s price is like reading the national average as your local forecast.\",\"exam\":\"Shifting AD for a change in the price level, or labelling the vertical axis as a single price. Both suggest the macro model is not secure.\",\"ia\":\"Describing a change in the article as a shift of AD when it is a movement along the curve, or the reverse.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Which event shifts the AD curve to the right?\",\"opts\":[\"A fall in the price level\",\"A rise in the price level\",\"A fall in the price of one good\",\"A cut in income tax that raises consumption\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"A tax cut raises a component of AD (consumption) at every price level, so the whole curve shifts. A change in the price level causes a movement along AD, not a shift.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What does the vertical axis of an AD–AS diagram measure?\",\"a\":\"The average price level (for example, a price index), not the price of any single good.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The diagram mirrors micro demand and supply, so micro movement/shift habits and “price means one good” carry over.\",\"diag\":[\"A fall in the price level: movement along AD, or a shift?\",\"A tax cut: movement or shift?\"],\"intervention\":\"Reuse the micro movement/shift colour code, but stress that the axis is the price level.\",\"activity\":\"Students sort macro shocks into “movement along AD”, “shift of AD”, “movement along AS” and “shift of AS”.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers treat price-level changes as movements; shifting AD for a price-level change suggests the macro model is not secure.\",\"warning\":[\"Treats the vertical axis as a single good’s price\",\"Shifts AD in response to inflation\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-06\",\"title\":\"SRAS, LRAS and the Keynesian AS\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“LRAS slopes upward just like SRAS, and the Keynesian and new classical AS curves are the same model.”\",\"right\":\"SRAS slopes upward because some costs, especially wages, are slow to adjust. In the monetarist/new classical view, LRAS is vertical at potential output, set by the quantity and quality of factors of production, technology and efficiency rather than by the price level. The Keynesian AS curve is horizontal when there is a lot of spare capacity, slopes upward as the economy approaches capacity and becomes vertical at full-employment output.\",\"why\":\"Two competing AS models are taught, and the spare-capacity logic behind the Keynesian shape is subtle.\",\"analogy\":\"SRAS is a sprint in the kit you already have; LRAS is your fitness ceiling. No burst of adrenaline (a higher price level) lifts the ceiling – only training (more or better resources) does.\",\"exam\":\"Drawing LRAS sloping upward, ignoring spare capacity, or mixing the two models in one diagram. The predicted effect of a change in AD is then wrong.\",\"ia\":\"Using the wrong section of the AS curve for the article’s economy, for example treating an economy in recession as if it were at full capacity.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"In the Keynesian model, AD rises while the economy has a large deflationary (recessionary) gap. What is the most likely result?\",\"opts\":[\"Real GDP rises with little change in the price level\",\"The price level rises with no change in real GDP\",\"Real GDP and the price level both fall\",\"LRAS shifts to the right\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"On the horizontal section of the Keynesian AS there are idle resources, so output can expand without much pressure on prices. A rise in the price level with no extra output describes the vertical section at full employment.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What determines the position of LRAS?\",\"a\":\"The quantity and quality of factors of production, technology, efficiency and institutions – not the price level.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Two competing AS models are taught, and the spare-capacity logic of the Keynesian curve is subtle.\",\"diag\":[\"Why is LRAS vertical?\",\"On the Keynesian AS, why is the horizontal range flat?\"],\"intervention\":\"Build LRAS from the determinants of potential output, and show where the Keynesian and new classical models agree.\",\"activity\":\"Students place an economy on the Keynesian AS in three states and predict the effect of an increase in AD in each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use the AS range that fits the context; an upward-sloping LRAS is a clear error.\",\"warning\":[\"LRAS drawn sloping upward\",\"Keynesian AS drawn as a smooth curve with no horizontal section\",\"Every increase in AD described as inflationary\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-07\",\"title\":\"The multiplier (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The multiplier means any injection simply doubles.”\",\"right\":\"The Keynesian multiplier shows how an injection raises real GDP by more than its initial size, because each round of spending becomes someone else’s income. Its size depends on leakages: multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM), where MPC is the share of extra income spent on domestic output, so larger leakages into saving, tax and imports mean a smaller multiplier. It is applied to the change in the injection, not to the level of GDP.\",\"why\":\"The word “multiplier” suggests a fixed factor, and the formula can feel mechanical until you build it up round by round.\",\"analogy\":\"A splash in a pool sends out ripples, each smaller than the last as water drains away through saving, taxes and imports. Fewer drains, bigger ripples.\",\"exam\":\"Using an arbitrary multiplier, ignoring tax and import leakages when the data include them, or applying the multiplier to total GDP. Both the calculation and the analysis then go astray.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating the effect of a policy in the article by assuming a large or fixed multiplier.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"MPS = 0.1, MPT = 0.15 and MPM = 0.15 (Illustrative data). Government spending rises by $100 million. In the simple Keynesian model, what is the eventual increase in real GDP?\",\"opts\":[\"$1,000 million\",\"$200 million\",\"$250 million\",\"$100 million\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"The multiplier is 1 ÷ (0.1 + 0.15 + 0.15) = 1 ÷ 0.4 = 2.5, so real GDP rises by $250 million. $1,000 million uses 1 ÷ MPS and ignores tax and import leakages; $200 million assumes the injection simply doubles.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Do larger leakages raise or lower the multiplier?\",\"a\":\"They lower it: more of each round of income leaks out of the circular flow, so less is re-spent.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The word suggests a fixed factor, and the leakage-based formula feels mechanical.\",\"diag\":[\"MPC is 0.8: what is the multiplier? If government spends 100, what is the final effect on output?\",\"What happens to the multiplier if the marginal propensity to import rises?\"],\"intervention\":\"Derive the multiplier from a round-by-round income table, then generalise to the formula.\",\"activity\":\"Given three sets of marginal propensities, students compute the multiplier and the total effect of a fixed injection.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers link the size of the multiplier to leakages; an assumed multiplier with no link to the marginal propensities carries little weight.\",\"warning\":[\"Assumes the injection doubles\",\"Uses a multiplier with no link to the MPC or leakages\",\"Applies the multiplier to the level of GDP\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-08\",\"title\":\"Cyclical vs structural unemployment\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.3\",\"3.7\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Unemployment is unemployment – more spending will fix any kind.”\",\"right\":\"Cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment comes from too little aggregate demand and is addressed mainly by demand-side policy. Structural unemployment comes from a mismatch between workers’ skills or locations and the jobs available, and is addressed mainly by supply-side measures such as retraining and support for mobility. Frictional unemployment is short-term job search and seasonal unemployment follows regular patterns in demand; together with structural unemployment, these make up the natural rate.\",\"why\":\"The unemployed look alike in the headline figures, and separating the causes needs a model.\",\"analogy\":\"Cyclical unemployment is a whole team benched because the match was called off; structural is players whose position no longer exists; frictional is players between clubs.\",\"exam\":\"Recommending demand-side stimulus for structural unemployment, or the reverse. A policy that does not match the cause weakens the evaluation.\",\"ia\":\"Misclassifying the unemployment in the article and so recommending an unsuitable policy.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Automation makes one type of factory skill obsolete, and those workers cannot find jobs even in a boom. Which policy targets the cause most directly?\",\"opts\":[\"A cut in interest rates\",\"Higher government spending on defence\",\"Retraining and education programmes\",\"A rise in unemployment benefits\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"This is structural unemployment: the workers’ skills no longer match the jobs available, so retraining addresses the mismatch. Raising AD, through lower interest rates or more government spending, mainly reduces cyclical unemployment and leaves the skills gap in place.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Which type of unemployment is mainly addressed by demand-side policy?\",\"a\":\"Cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The unemployed look alike in the data, and the causes (deficient demand versus mismatch) need a model to separate.\",\"diag\":[\"Automation makes a skill obsolete: which type of unemployment?\",\"A recession cuts demand for all labour: which type?\"],\"intervention\":\"Match each type explicitly to its cause and to an appropriate policy.\",\"activity\":\"Students diagnose six labour-market scenarios by type and prescribe the matching policy.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers match the policy to the type of unemployment; mismatches signal weak diagnosis.\",\"warning\":[\"Prescribes stimulus for a skills mismatch\",\"Calls all unemployment “cyclical”\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-09\",\"title\":\"Inflation: a rate, not a level\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.3\",\"3.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Inflation means prices are high.”\",\"right\":\"Inflation is a sustained increase in the general price level – prices are rising, not simply high. Demand-pull inflation comes from AD growing faster than the economy can expand output (AD shifting right near capacity); cost-push inflation comes from rising costs, shown by SRAS shifting left. The cause matters for policy: contractionary demand policy can reduce cost-push inflation, but only by deepening the fall in output and employment.\",\"why\":\"In everyday speech “inflation” means “expensive”, and both causes raise the price level on the diagram.\",\"analogy\":\"Inflation is an escalator moving upward, not standing on a high floor: prices that are high but steady are not inflation.\",\"exam\":\"Defining inflation as “high prices”, misidentifying the cause, or recommending demand-side policy for cost-push inflation without noting the output cost. The policy evaluation then rests on the wrong diagnosis.\",\"ia\":\"Misdiagnosing the type of inflation in the article, which leads to the wrong evaluation of policy.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A sharp rise in world oil prices raises firms’ costs across the economy. How is the resulting inflation shown?\",\"opts\":[\"AD shifts to the right\",\"SRAS shifts to the left\",\"LRAS shifts to the right\",\"AD shifts to the left\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Higher input costs make production dearer at every level of output, so SRAS shifts left: cost-push inflation, with lower real GDP. A rightward shift of AD is demand-pull inflation, which comes from rising spending rather than rising costs.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Is inflation a high price level or a rising price level?\",\"a\":\"A sustained rise in the general price level.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"“Inflation” in everyday talk means “expensive”, and both causes raise the price level.\",\"diag\":[\"Prices are high but steady: is that inflation?\",\"A surge in wage costs versus a spending boom: which inflation is which?\"],\"intervention\":\"Separate level from rate first, then split the causes with AD–AS diagrams and match each to a policy.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify four inflation episodes as demand-pull or cost-push and justify each with a diagram.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify the cause and note that demand-side policy against cost-push inflation carries a heavy output cost.\",\"warning\":[\"Defines inflation as “high prices”\",\"Treats falling inflation as falling prices\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-10\",\"title\":\"Deflation is not simply good news\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Falling prices are always good for the economy.”\",\"right\":\"Deflation is a sustained fall in the general price level; it differs from disinflation, which is a fall in the rate of inflation. Deflation caused by falling AD can be damaging: households delay purchases expecting lower prices, the real value of debts rises, and falling spending can feed further cuts in output and jobs – a deflationary spiral. Deflation caused by SRAS rising, for example through productivity gains, is usually far less harmful, so the cause decides which story applies.\",\"why\":\"As a shopper, lower prices feel like a gain, which hides the macroeconomic risks.\",\"analogy\":\"Demand-side deflation is like a shop that keeps promising a better sale tomorrow: customers wait, the tills fall silent and staff are laid off.\",\"exam\":\"Treating deflation as simply beneficial and ignoring delayed spending, the rising real burden of debt and the risk of a spiral. The evaluation then misses the main costs.\",\"ia\":\"Welcoming falling prices in the article without analysing their cause or their effects on spending and debt.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Prices across an economy are expected to keep falling because demand is weak. What is the most likely effect on current consumption?\",\"opts\":[\"It rises, because goods are cheaper today\",\"It rises, because debts become easier to repay\",\"It is unaffected by expectations\",\"It falls, because households delay purchases to buy more cheaply later\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"If prices are expected to fall, waiting pays, so spending is postponed and AD weakens further. The claim that debts become easier to repay is backwards: deflation raises the real value of debt.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"How does deflation affect the real burden of debt?\",\"a\":\"It increases it: debts are fixed in money terms, so each unit of debt represents more purchasing power as prices fall.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Lower prices feel good as a consumer, masking the macroeconomic dangers.\",\"diag\":[\"If you expect prices to fall next month, what do you do about a big purchase? What does that do to AD?\",\"What is the difference between deflation and disinflation?\"],\"intervention\":\"Contrast benign (supply-side) and malign (demand-side) deflation, and trace the spiral through expectations and debt.\",\"activity\":\"Students model a deflationary spiral step by step and identify a policy that could break it.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify the spiral and the debt effect; “cheaper is better” answers miss the danger.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls all deflation “good news”\",\"Confuses deflation with disinflation\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-11\",\"title\":\"The Phillips curve trade-off (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Governments can always buy lower unemployment by accepting higher inflation.”\",\"right\":\"The short-run Phillips curve shows a trade-off: lower unemployment tends to come with higher inflation. In the long run the Phillips curve is vertical at the natural rate of unemployment: holding unemployment below that rate raises inflation expectations, which shifts the short-run curve up, so unemployment drifts back to the natural rate at a higher rate of inflation. High inflation together with high unemployment (stagflation) is one sign that the simple trade-off has broken down.\",\"why\":\"The short-run trade-off is easy to remember; the long-run version, which depends on expectations, is harder.\",\"analogy\":\"Chasing lower unemployment with inflation is like running up a down escalator that speeds up as you run: you gain ground briefly, then end up back where you started, only more tired.\",\"exam\":\"Asserting a permanent trade-off and ignoring expectations and the vertical long-run curve. The policy evaluation then overstates what demand management can achieve.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming a lasting trade-off when evaluating the policy in the article.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"In the expectations-augmented view, what happens if a government keeps unemployment below the natural rate for a long time?\",\"opts\":[\"Inflation keeps rising as expectations adjust upward\",\"Unemployment stays low at a stable rate of inflation\",\"Inflation falls to zero\",\"The natural rate of unemployment falls to match\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Workers and firms revise their inflation expectations upward, shifting the short-run curve up again and again, so holding unemployment below the natural rate requires ever-rising inflation. A stable low-unemployment outcome treats the short-run curve as if it stayed put while expectations changed.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What shape is the long-run Phillips curve, and where is it?\",\"a\":\"Vertical at the natural rate of unemployment, which corresponds to full-employment output on LRAS.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The short-run trade-off is memorable, and the expectations-augmented long-run version is more demanding.\",\"diag\":[\"Can policymakers permanently buy lower unemployment with higher inflation?\",\"What does stagflation suggest about the simple trade-off?\"],\"intervention\":\"Link the long-run Phillips curve explicitly to LRAS and to inflation expectations.\",\"activity\":\"Students trace a sequence of attempts to exploit the trade-off and show the short-run curve shifting up each time.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use the expectations-augmented view; a naive permanent trade-off is a clear weakness.\",\"warning\":[\"Believes in a permanent trade-off\",\"Discusses inflation and unemployment with no mention of expectations\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-12\",\"title\":\"Deficit vs debt\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.6\",\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“If the government cuts its deficit, the national debt falls – and any deficit is reckless.”\",\"right\":\"The budget deficit is the gap between government spending and revenue in one year – a flow. Government (national) debt is the accumulated stock of past borrowing, measured as a percentage of GDP (HL only). A deficit adds to debt, so the level of debt keeps rising while there is any deficit, even a shrinking one; deficits can be appropriate in a downturn, and sustainability depends on debt relative to GDP and the cost of servicing it.\",\"why\":\"Both involve the government owing money, so the difference between a flow and a stock collapses.\",\"analogy\":\"The deficit is how fast water flows into a bath each year; the debt is how much water is in it. Turn the tap down and the level still rises until the flow stops.\",\"exam\":\"Using “deficit” and “debt” interchangeably, or claiming that any deficit harms the economy. The fiscal evaluation then lacks context.\",\"ia\":\"Misusing fiscal terms when the article discusses government borrowing, and misjudging whether the debt is sustainable.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A government’s budget deficit falls from 6% to 3% of GDP (Illustrative data). What happens to the level of national debt that year?\",\"opts\":[\"It falls\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It rises, but more slowly\",\"It is halved\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"A deficit is still new borrowing added to the stock of debt, so debt keeps rising, just more slowly. The level of debt falls only when the government runs a budget surplus and repays some of it.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Is the budget deficit a stock or a flow?\",\"a\":\"A flow, measured over a period such as a year. Debt is the stock.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both involve “the government owing money”, so the flow/stock distinction collapses.\",\"diag\":[\"A government runs a smaller deficit than last year: is the national debt rising or falling?\",\"Can the debt-to-GDP ratio fall while the government runs a deficit?\"],\"intervention\":\"Drill the flow/stock distinction with a multi-year table, and separate cyclical from structural deficits.\",\"activity\":\"Given five years of deficits, students compute the path of debt and identify when it would fall.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use flow and stock correctly and evaluate deficits in context.\",\"warning\":[\"Uses “deficit” and “debt” interchangeably\",\"Assumes a smaller deficit means falling debt\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-13\",\"title\":\"What the central bank controls\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“The central bank sets every interest rate, and printing money always causes inflation straight away.”\",\"right\":\"The central bank sets its policy rate (the base or minimum lending rate), which influences the rates banks charge households and firms through the transmission mechanism, with time lags. Faster money growth tends to feed inflation mainly when the economy is close to capacity; with large spare capacity and weak confidence, measures such as quantitative easing (HL only) need not cause much inflation in the short run.\",\"why\":\"Headlines simplify monetary policy, and the link between money and prices is easy to apply without its conditions.\",\"analogy\":\"The policy rate is the thermostat setting, not the temperature in every room: how warm each room gets depends on the insulation (transmission) and the weather outside (the output gap).\",\"exam\":\"Claiming the central bank directly sets all interest rates, or that money growth equals inflation whatever the output gap. The evaluation then ignores lags, confidence and spare capacity.\",\"ia\":\"Oversimplifying the monetary policy in the article and ignoring transmission lags and spare capacity.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A central bank cuts its policy rate during a deep recession, but household borrowing hardly rises. Which explanation fits best?\",\"opts\":[\"The central bank does not influence any market interest rates\",\"Weak confidence and slow transmission limit the effect of the cut on spending\",\"Lower interest rates reduce borrowing\",\"Monetary policy works only through government spending\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"The policy rate influences other rates, but households and firms may not borrow more if they are pessimistic about future incomes and profits, which limits monetary policy in deep downturns. Saying the central bank influences no market rates over-corrects: the influence is real, just not complete or instant.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"When is faster money growth most likely to be inflationary?\",\"a\":\"When the economy is close to full capacity, so extra spending raises prices rather than output.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Headlines simplify monetary policy, and the quantity theory is applied without its conditions.\",\"diag\":[\"Does the central bank set your mortgage rate directly?\",\"Does quantitative easing cause inflation if there is large spare capacity?\"],\"intervention\":\"Teach the transmission mechanism and the role of the output gap in the link from money to inflation.\",\"activity\":\"Students trace a policy-rate cut through the transmission mechanism to AD, then vary the output gap and predict inflation.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers reason through the transmission mechanism and add the spare-capacity caveat.\",\"warning\":[\"Claims quantitative easing “must” cause inflation\",\"Treats the policy rate as the rate households pay\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-14\",\"title\":\"Supply-side or demand-side?\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.7\",\"3.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Every tax cut is a supply-side policy.”\",\"right\":\"Demand-side policy aims to shift AD, affecting output, employment and inflation in the short run. Supply-side policy aims to raise productive capacity – shifting LRAS (or the Keynesian AS) to the right – through productivity, incentives, competition and the quality of factors of production. The same tool can serve either aim, so classify a policy by its intended target, and note that many supply-side measures also shift AD first.\",\"why\":\"Some instruments, such as tax cuts and government spending, appear in both toolkits, which blurs the line between aim and mechanism.\",\"analogy\":\"Demand-side policy is turning up the heating now; supply-side policy is insulating the house so it stays warmer for longer. The toolbox overlaps, but the goals differ.\",\"exam\":\"Labelling demand-side measures as supply-side, or shifting SRAS when the policy is meant to raise potential output. The evaluation then judges the policy against the wrong objective.\",\"ia\":\"Misclassifying the policy in the article and evaluating it against the wrong objective.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A government funds a large programme of apprenticeships and technical training. Which curve is it mainly trying to shift?\",\"opts\":[\"AD, to raise spending this year\",\"SRAS, by lowering wages\",\"None, because training takes years to work\",\"LRAS, by improving the quality of labour\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Training raises the quality of labour and so productive capacity, a supply-side aim shown by LRAS shifting right. The spending also adds to AD in the short run, but that is a side effect rather than the policy’s target.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Give a tool that can be demand-side or supply-side depending on its aim.\",\"a\":\"A tax cut (to boost spending now, or to strengthen incentives to work and invest) or government spending (on current consumption, or on infrastructure and education).\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Some instruments (such as tax) appear in both contexts, blurring intent and mechanism.\",\"diag\":[\"A tax cut to boost spending now versus a tax cut to raise long-run incentives: same policy, different aim?\",\"Which curve does each target?\"],\"intervention\":\"Classify policies by their target curve (AD versus LRAS), noting tools that can do both.\",\"activity\":\"Students sort ten policies into demand-side, supply-side or both, and state the intended effect of each.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers classify by intended effect and evaluate against the right objective.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls all tax cuts “supply-side”\",\"Shifts SRAS for a policy aimed at potential output\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-15\",\"title\":\"Crowding out depends on conditions (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Government borrowing always crowds out private investment.”\",\"right\":\"Crowding out happens when government borrowing pushes up interest rates and so reduces private investment and consumption. It is most likely when the economy is near full capacity and interest rates respond strongly to extra borrowing. In a deep recession with idle resources and low interest rates, extra government spending may crowd out little and can even encourage private spending by raising demand (“crowding in”).\",\"why\":\"The mechanism is easy to learn as a rule, separated from the state of the economy.\",\"analogy\":\"Crowding out is two diners competing for the last table; in an empty restaurant (spare capacity) there is no competition and everyone gets a seat.\",\"exam\":\"Asserting full crowding out regardless of spare capacity or the interest-rate environment. The fiscal evaluation then lacks the conditions that make it convincing.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating crowding out in an article about an economy with plenty of spare capacity.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"When is crowding out likely to be strongest?\",\"opts\":[\"When the economy is close to full capacity and extra borrowing pushes up interest rates\",\"In a deep recession, when interest rates are very low and resources are idle\",\"When the government runs a budget surplus and repays some of its debt\",\"When private investment does not respond to changes in interest rates\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"Crowding out works through higher interest rates reducing private spending, so it bites when resources are scarce and borrowing costs rise. In a deep recession with low rates, idle savings and resources leave little to crowd out.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What is “crowding in”?\",\"a\":\"When government spending raises demand and business confidence enough to increase private investment rather than reduce it.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The mechanism is taught as a rule, divorced from the state of the economy.\",\"diag\":[\"In a deep recession with idle resources and low rates, will extra government borrowing crowd out private investment?\",\"What conditions would make crowding out bind?\"],\"intervention\":\"Condition every crowding-out claim on the output gap and the interest-rate environment.\",\"activity\":\"Students predict the crowding-out outcome in a boom and in a slump and justify each through the interest-rate (loanable funds) channel.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers condition crowding out on spare capacity; an unconditional claim is a weakness.\",\"warning\":[\"States that borrowing crowds out investment with no context\",\"Makes no reference to interest rates or spare capacity\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-16\",\"title\":\"Automatic stabilisers vs discretionary policy (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Every change in government spending and taxes is a decision the government has to make.”\",\"right\":\"Automatic stabilisers – progressive taxes and unemployment benefits – change with the business cycle without any new decision: in a recession tax revenue falls and benefit payments rise, cushioning AD. Discretionary fiscal policy needs deliberate decisions, so it faces recognition, decision and implementation lags. Stabilisers act faster, but they soften fluctuations rather than remove them.\",\"why\":\"Both are fiscal policy, and the automatic mechanism is invisible compared with an announced budget.\",\"analogy\":\"Automatic stabilisers are like a car’s suspension absorbing bumps without the driver doing anything; discretionary policy is the driver deciding to brake, which takes longer.\",\"exam\":\"Treating every fiscal change as discretionary and missing the speed advantage of stabilisers. The evaluation of time lags is then incomplete.\",\"ia\":\"Ignoring automatic stabilisers when evaluating the government’s fiscal response in the article.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"During a recession, spending on unemployment benefits rises without any new law. What is this?\",\"opts\":[\"Discretionary expansionary fiscal policy\",\"Contractionary monetary policy\",\"An automatic stabiliser\",\"Crowding out\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Benefit payments rise because more people qualify, not because the government decided to spend more, so this is an automatic stabiliser. Discretionary policy would require a new decision, such as a stimulus package.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name two automatic stabilisers.\",\"a\":\"Progressive income taxes and unemployment benefits.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both are fiscal policy, and the automatic mechanism is invisible compared with announced measures.\",\"diag\":[\"Unemployment benefits rising in a recession without a new law: automatic or discretionary?\",\"Which lags do automatic stabilisers avoid, and which effects still take time?\"],\"intervention\":\"Separate the two on timing and decision-making, highlighting that stabilisers avoid recognition and decision lags.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify a list of fiscal responses as automatic or discretionary and rank them by speed of impact.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers cite the speed advantage of automatic stabilisers without claiming they remove the business cycle.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls every fiscal change “discretionary”\",\"Claims stabilisers act with no lag of any kind\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-17\",\"title\":\"Actual vs potential growth\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.3\",\"3.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Any rise in output, including a spending boom, means the economy can now produce more.”\",\"right\":\"Actual growth is a rise in real GDP; in the short run it can come from AD rising and using spare capacity. Potential growth is an increase in productive capacity, shown by LRAS (or the vertical section of the Keynesian AS) shifting right, from more or better factors of production, technology or efficiency. Actual growth can be sustained over the long run only if potential output grows too.\",\"why\":\"Both raise real GDP on the diagram, so the difference between actual and potential output is easy to lose.\",\"analogy\":\"Actual growth is filling more seats in the same stadium; potential growth is building a bigger stadium. Only the second raises the limit.\",\"exam\":\"Treating an AD-driven boom as long-run growth, or ignoring the determinants of LRAS. The analysis then confuses a cyclical upswing with rising capacity.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming the article shows long-run growth when it describes a cyclical rise in demand.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Which change represents potential economic growth?\",\"opts\":[\"A rise in consumer spending that uses idle factories\",\"An improvement in technology that raises labour productivity\",\"A cut in income tax that raises household spending\",\"A rise in exports during a boom\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Better technology raises the economy’s productive capacity, shifting LRAS right. The other changes raise AD, which gives actual growth by using existing capacity but does not, on its own, raise the long-run limit.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name two determinants of LRAS.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: the quantity of factors of production, their quality (for example education and health), technology, efficiency, institutions.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both raise real GDP on the diagram, so the actual-versus-potential distinction is lost.\",\"diag\":[\"A spending boom raises output this year: is that the same as the economy being able to produce more permanently?\",\"Which curve must shift for growth to be sustained?\"],\"intervention\":\"Distinguish actual from potential growth on AD–AS and tie potential growth to the determinants of LRAS.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify five growth episodes as actual or potential and identify the relevant determinants.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers separate actual from potential growth and cite the determinants of LRAS.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls every rise in output “growth” without the actual/potential split\",\"Shifts LRAS for a demand boom\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"MAC-18\",\"title\":\"Expectations and credibility\",\"area\":\"Macroeconomics\",\"subs\":[\"3.5\",\"3.3\"],\"beyond\":\"Central-bank credibility and the anchoring of expectations are not named in the 2022 guide; inflation targeting (3.5) and the long-run Phillips curve (3.3, HL only) are. Treat this entry as enrichment.\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Policy only works once interest rates actually change; announcements make no difference.”\",\"right\":\"Households and firms act on what they expect. If people expect higher inflation, they push for higher wages and set higher prices, which helps to bring that inflation about. A credible central bank – for example one with a clear inflation target and a record of meeting it – can anchor expectations, so inflation may come down with a smaller loss of output than if people doubted its commitment.\",\"why\":\"Expectations cannot be seen or drawn as easily as a curve shift, so they tend to drop out of the analysis.\",\"analogy\":\"When people hear that a shop may run out of bread, they rush to buy extra and the shelves empty – the expectation fulfils itself. A trusted announcement that supplies are ample stops the rush.\",\"exam\":\"Leaving expectations out of inflation and policy analysis. The evaluation then misses why credibility can make policy more or less effective.\",\"ia\":\"Missing the expectations channel when the article discusses central-bank announcements or inflation targets.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Why might a credible inflation target reduce inflation even before interest rates change much?\",\"opts\":[\"It increases the money supply, so interest rates fall\",\"It commits the government to higher spending, which raises AD\",\"It shifts LRAS to the left, so output falls\",\"It lowers expected inflation, so wage and price setting becomes more moderate\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"If people believe the central bank will act, they build lower inflation into wage claims and pricing decisions, which reduces inflation directly. A larger money supply or higher government spending would tend to raise AD, not reduce inflation.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name one source of central-bank credibility.\",\"a\":\"For example: independence from government, a clear public inflation target, or a record of meeting the target.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Expectations are intangible and harder to diagram than curve shifts.\",\"diag\":[\"Why might a credible inflation target reduce inflation even before rates change much?\",\"What happens to the output cost of bringing inflation down if the public doubts the central bank?\"],\"intervention\":\"Add an “expectations” step to every inflation and policy analysis, and discuss credibility explicitly.\",\"activity\":\"Students compare a credible and a non-credible disinflation and predict the difference in the output cost.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers bring expectations and credibility into policy evaluation.\",\"warning\":[\"Policy analysis with no mention of expectations or credibility\",\"Treats announcements as irrelevant\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-01\",\"title\":\"Comparative, not absolute, advantage (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.1\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A country should produce whatever it is best at – and if it is best at everything, it gains nothing from trade.”\",\"right\":\"Gains from specialisation and trade depend on comparative advantage: producing the good with the lower opportunity cost. Even if one country has an absolute advantage in both goods, both countries can gain when each specialises according to relative costs and they trade at terms between their opportunity-cost ratios.\",\"why\":\"Absolute advantage matches everyday intuition (“we are best at this”); opportunity-cost reasoning is more abstract.\",\"analogy\":\"A lawyer who types faster than her assistant should still leave the typing to him: every hour she spends typing costs her an hour of far more valuable legal work.\",\"exam\":\"Assigning specialisation by absolute output rather than by opportunity-cost ratios. The pattern of trade and the gains from it then come out wrong.\",\"ia\":\"Justifying trade in the article with “they are better at it” rather than relative opportunity costs.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Country A can produce either 10 cars or 20 tonnes of wheat; Country B either 2 cars or 10 tonnes of wheat (Illustrative data). Which pattern of specialisation creates gains from trade?\",\"opts\":[\"A specialises in cars and B in wheat\",\"A specialises in wheat and B in cars\",\"A produces both goods because it is better at both\",\"No pattern: A’s absolute advantage in both goods rules out gains\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"In A one car costs 2 tonnes of wheat; in B it costs 5 tonnes, so A has the lower opportunity cost of cars, and B the lower opportunity cost of wheat (0.2 cars per tonne against 0.5). An absolute advantage in both goods does not remove the gains from specialising by comparative advantage.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Can a country with an absolute advantage in both goods still benefit from trade? Why?\",\"a\":\"Yes. What matters is comparative advantage: it gains by specialising in the good in which its opportunity cost is lower.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Absolute advantage is intuitive (“we are best at this”); the opportunity-cost logic of comparative advantage is abstract.\",\"diag\":[\"Country A is better at producing both goods: can it still gain by specialising and trading? Why?\",\"What ratio do you calculate to identify comparative advantage?\"],\"intervention\":\"Require opportunity-cost ratios before any specialisation is assigned, and do not accept absolute-advantage reasoning for the gains from trade.\",\"activity\":\"Given a two-country, two-good output table, students derive opportunity costs and the pattern of trade.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers reason from opportunity cost; answers resting on absolute advantage miss the model.\",\"warning\":[\"Assigns production by “who is best”\",\"Compares absolute outputs instead of opportunity-cost ratios\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-02\",\"title\":\"Trade is not a zero-sum game\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.1\",\"4.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Exports are good and imports are bad, so if one country gains from trade the other must lose.”\",\"right\":\"Voluntary trade is positive-sum: each side trades because it expects to gain. Imports provide consumer goods, cheaper inputs and variety, and exports earn the foreign currency that pays for them, so a trade deficit is not in itself a loss. The gains are not evenly shared, however, and some groups – such as workers in import-competing industries – can lose.\",\"why\":\"Political rhetoric and national framing present trade as a contest won by running surpluses.\",\"analogy\":\"Trade is not a boxing match with one winner; it is two people swapping a sandwich for a drink because each prefers what the other has.\",\"exam\":\"Treating a trade deficit as failure, or valuing exports while ignoring the imports they pay for. This misses the mutual gains that justify trade.\",\"ia\":\"Framing the trade relationship in the article as winners and losers between countries and missing the mutual gains.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Why does voluntary international trade usually benefit both trading partners?\",\"opts\":[\"The surplus country gains exactly what the deficit country loses\",\"Only the exporter gains, because imports reduce national welfare\",\"Each specialises where its opportunity cost is lower and exchanges for goods it values more\",\"Only the richer partner gains, because it has more bargaining power\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Specialisation by comparative advantage (HL only) raises total output, and exchange lets each side consume combinations it could not produce alone. The zero-sum view counts a surplus as a win, but a surplus country is exchanging goods for imports or financial claims, not gaining at its partner’s expense.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What do exports ultimately pay for?\",\"a\":\"Imports: exports earn the foreign currency used to buy goods and services from abroad.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"National framing and political rhetoric present trade as a contest to be “won” through surpluses.\",\"diag\":[\"If imports are bad, why do we import at all?\",\"Can both sides of a trade be better off?\"],\"intervention\":\"Reframe exports as the cost of imports, and challenge win/lose language whenever it appears.\",\"activity\":\"Debate the motion “A trade deficit means a country is losing”; students argue both sides using mutual-gains logic.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers recognise mutual gains and the purpose of imports while noting who loses within a country; mercantilist framing is a weakness.\",\"warning\":[\"Treats all imports as harmful\",\"Calls every surplus a victory\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-03\",\"title\":\"Who pays a tariff?\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A tariff on imported steel is paid by the country that exports the steel.”\",\"right\":\"A tariff is a tax on imports, collected by the importing country’s government from domestic importers. In the standard IB diagram, where the world price is unaffected, the domestic price rises by the full tariff: domestic consumers pay more and buy less, domestic producers gain and the government collects revenue. Foreign exporters lose sales, and if the importing country is large enough to push down the world price, they also bear part of the tariff through a lower price received.\",\"why\":\"“A tariff on Chinese goods” sounds like a charge on China rather than on buyers in the importing country.\",\"analogy\":\"A tariff is a toll on your own bridge: your government collects it, but it is mostly your own commuters who pay to cross.\",\"exam\":\"Stating that the exporting country pays, and missing the rise in the domestic price and the loss to domestic consumers. The stakeholder analysis is then reversed.\",\"ia\":\"Claiming the tariff in the article “punishes” the exporter while overlooking the higher prices paid by domestic consumers.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A small country imposes a tariff on imported steel. In the standard model, who pays the higher price?\",\"opts\":[\"Foreign steel producers, because the tariff is on their goods\",\"Domestic buyers of steel, because the domestic price rises by the tariff\",\"Domestic steel producers, because they face more competition\",\"Nobody, because the government collects the revenue\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"With the world price unchanged, the tariff raises the domestic price, so domestic consumers and steel-using firms pay more. Foreign producers lose sales, but in the small-country model the price they receive does not change.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Who receives the tariff revenue?\",\"a\":\"The government of the importing country.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"“A tariff on Chinese goods” sounds like a charge on China, not on the importing country’s buyers.\",\"diag\":[\"A country imposes a tariff on imported steel: whose consumers and firms feel the price rise?\",\"Who hands the tariff money to the government?\"],\"intervention\":\"Walk through the tariff diagram, emphasising the rise in the domestic price and the loss of consumer surplus; introduce the large-country case as a refinement.\",\"activity\":\"Students label a tariff diagram: the change in domestic price, the changes in consumer and producer surplus, government revenue and deadweight loss.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers get the incidence right and label the tariff diagram fully; “the exporter pays” is the error to watch for.\",\"warning\":[\"Says the foreign country pays the tariff\",\"Omits the rise in the domestic price\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-04\",\"title\":\"Transfers vs losses on a tariff diagram\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“All the consumer surplus lost because of a tariff is deadweight loss.”\",\"right\":\"Under a tariff, the loss of consumer surplus splits four ways: a gain in domestic producer surplus (a transfer), government tariff revenue (a transfer) and two deadweight-loss triangles. One triangle is the production inefficiency of higher-cost domestic output replacing cheaper imports; the other is the consumption loss from units no longer bought. The net welfare loss to the country is only the two triangles.\",\"why\":\"There are several areas to track, and they are easy to memorise by position rather than by meaning.\",\"analogy\":\"The consumer’s lost slice of cake is shared out: some goes to domestic producers, some to the government, and two crumbs fall on the floor that nobody gets.\",\"exam\":\"Counting revenue as a loss, omitting one of the two triangles, or treating the producer gain as a net gain to society. The welfare conclusion is then overstated or understated.\",\"ia\":\"Drawing a tariff diagram without separating the transfers (revenue, producer gain) from the net welfare loss.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"On a standard tariff diagram, which areas together make up the net welfare loss to the importing country?\",\"opts\":[\"The whole fall in consumer surplus\",\"Government tariff revenue plus the two triangles\",\"The gain in domestic producer surplus\",\"The two deadweight-loss triangles only\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"The producer gain and tariff revenue are transfers from consumers to other domestic groups, so the country does not lose them. Only the two triangles – inefficient domestic production and lost consumption – are net losses.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What do the two deadweight-loss triangles on a tariff diagram represent?\",\"a\":\"The production inefficiency of higher-cost domestic output replacing imports, and the consumption loss from units no longer bought.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"There are several areas to track (consumer loss, producer gain, revenue, two efficiency losses), and they are memorised by position.\",\"diag\":[\"Shade the loss of consumer surplus, the producer gain, government revenue and the two deadweight-loss areas.\",\"Which of these areas are transfers?\"],\"intervention\":\"Re-derive each area from its definition and insist on naming transfers versus losses every time.\",\"activity\":\"Shade-and-total exercise: students colour the four component areas and check that together they make up the loss of consumer surplus (calculation is HL only).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers separate transfers from the net welfare loss; lumping them together is the error to watch for.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls the whole consumer loss “deadweight loss”\",\"Omits one of the two triangles\",\"Treats the producer gain as a gain to society\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-05\",\"title\":\"Quotas are not tariffs\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.2\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A quota works just like a tariff, so the government collects revenue from it.”\",\"right\":\"A quota limits the quantity of imports, which raises the domestic price. The extra value on the permitted imports – the quota rent – goes to whoever holds the right to import (domestic importers or foreign exporters), not to the government, unless the licences are sold or auctioned. The price and quantity effects can resemble a tariff’s, but who gains differs.\",\"why\":\"Both measures restrict imports and raise domestic prices, so their differences are easy to overlook.\",\"analogy\":\"A tariff is a paid entry fee collected at the gate; a quota caps the number of tickets, and whoever holds the tickets pockets the premium.\",\"exam\":\"Assuming a quota raises tariff-style revenue, or ignoring the quota rent. The stakeholder analysis then credits the wrong group.\",\"ia\":\"Describing the effects of an import quota in the article as though they produced tariff revenue.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"An import quota raises the domestic price of sugar. Who receives the extra value on the sugar that is still imported?\",\"opts\":[\"Whoever holds the import licences, unless the government sells them\",\"The government, as tariff revenue\",\"Domestic consumers\",\"Nobody: it is all deadweight loss\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The quota creates scarcity, so permitted imports sell at the higher domestic price and the gap is captured by licence holders as quota rent. The government gains only if it sells or auctions the licences, so treating the rent as tariff revenue misreads the policy.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Does an import quota raise government revenue by default?\",\"a\":\"No. The quota rent goes to licence holders unless the licences are sold or auctioned.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Both restrict imports and raise domestic prices, so the differences are overlooked.\",\"diag\":[\"A tariff raises government revenue: where does the equivalent gain go under an import quota?\",\"How could a government capture the quota rent?\"],\"intervention\":\"Compare tariff and quota diagrams side by side and identify where the rent lands under each.\",\"activity\":\"Given identical price effects, students locate the revenue (tariff) and the rent (quota) and say who gains in each case.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify the quota rent and its recipient; treating a quota as a tariff is a clear weakness.\",\"warning\":[\"Says a quota “raises government revenue” by default\",\"Awards the quota rent to the government\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-06\",\"title\":\"Appreciation, depreciation and “strong” currencies\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A strong currency is always good for the economy.”\",\"right\":\"Appreciation means a currency buys more foreign currency than before; depreciation means it buys less. An appreciation makes exports dearer for foreign buyers and imports cheaper at home, which helps consumers and importing firms and can reduce inflation, but hurts exporters and import-competing firms. Whether a stronger currency is good depends on who you are and on the state of the economy.\",\"why\":\"The terms are easy to reverse, and “strong” sounds like “good”.\",\"analogy\":\"A strong currency is like a strong passport for shopping abroad: great for tourists and importers, tough for the souvenir sellers back home who rely on foreign buyers.\",\"exam\":\"Calling a rise in a currency’s value a “depreciation”, or assuming appreciation benefits everyone. The analysis of winners and losers is then wrong.\",\"ia\":\"Misstating the direction of the currency movement in the article, and so its effect on trade.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Last year €1 bought $1.10; now €1 buys $1.25 (Illustrative data). What has happened, and who is most likely to be hurt?\",\"opts\":[\"The euro has depreciated; eurozone exporters are hurt\",\"The euro has appreciated; eurozone consumers are hurt\",\"The euro has appreciated; eurozone exporters are hurt\",\"The euro has depreciated; eurozone consumers benefit\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"Each euro now buys more dollars, so the euro has appreciated. Eurozone goods become dearer in dollar terms, which hurts exporters, while eurozone consumers gain from cheaper imports.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Does an appreciation make a country’s exports cheaper or dearer for foreign buyers?\",\"a\":\"Dearer.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The terminology is slippery, and “strong = good” is a patriotic intuition.\",\"diag\":[\"The currency buys more foreign currency than before: appreciation or depreciation?\",\"Is that good for exporters?\"],\"intervention\":\"Drill a mnemonic such as SPICED (Strong Pound, Imports Cheaper, Exports Dearer) and identify winners and losers for each move.\",\"activity\":\"Given five currency moves, students state appreciation or depreciation and name who gains and who loses.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers identify winners and losers rather than labelling a currency move simply “good” or “bad”.\",\"warning\":[\"Reverses appreciation and depreciation\",\"Treats a strong currency as good for everyone\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-07\",\"title\":\"Depreciation and the J-curve (HL only)\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.6\",\"4.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A depreciation improves the trade balance straight away, every time.”\",\"right\":\"A depreciation makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home, but it improves the trade balance only if demand for exports and imports is elastic enough: the Marshall–Lerner condition requires the sum of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports (in absolute terms) to exceed 1. Because contracts and habits take time to change, volumes adjust slowly, so the balance often worsens at first before improving – the J-curve.\",\"why\":\"The first-step result – cheaper exports, dearer imports – is easy to learn without the elasticity and timing conditions.\",\"analogy\":\"Turning a large ship: you push the rudder, but the ship carries on along its old course for a while before the new heading takes hold.\",\"exam\":\"Claiming an immediate, unconditional improvement in the trade balance, with no mention of Marshall–Lerner or the J-curve lag. The evaluation of exchange-rate policy is then one-sided.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating the trade benefit of the depreciation in the article without timing or elasticity caveats.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Just after a depreciation, a country’s trade deficit widens. Which explanation fits best?\",\"opts\":[\"The Marshall–Lerner condition means the deficit widens permanently\",\"Volumes respond slowly, so dearer imports raise import spending before quantities adjust\",\"The depreciation has made imports cheaper, so more are bought\",\"Exports have become dearer for foreign buyers, so fewer are sold\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"Right after a depreciation, import and export volumes are largely fixed by existing contracts and habits, so paying more for the same imports worsens the balance. As demand becomes more elastic over time, the balance can improve – the J-curve. A depreciation makes imports dearer and exports cheaper, not the reverse.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"State the Marshall–Lerner condition.\",\"a\":\"A depreciation improves the trade balance if the sum of the absolute values of PED for exports and PED for imports is greater than 1.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The textbook result (cheaper exports, dearer imports) is taught without the elasticity and timing conditions.\",\"diag\":[\"A currency depreciates: why might the trade balance worsen before it improves?\",\"What must be true of elasticities for a lasting improvement?\"],\"intervention\":\"Introduce Marshall–Lerner and the J-curve, and condition every trade-balance claim on elasticities and time.\",\"activity\":\"Students sketch the J-curve and explain each phase, then test whether Marshall–Lerner is met for given elasticities.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers include the Marshall–Lerner and J-curve nuance; an unconditional improvement claim is a weakness.\",\"warning\":[\"Claims instant improvement with no mention of elasticity or lags\",\"Quotes Marshall–Lerner without saying the elasticities are summed in absolute terms\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-08\",\"title\":\"Devaluation vs depreciation\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Devaluation and depreciation mean the same thing – the currency falls.”\",\"right\":\"Devaluation and revaluation are deliberate changes by the authorities to a fixed (pegged) exchange rate. Depreciation and appreciation are market-driven changes in a floating rate. To maintain a peg, the central bank buys or sells its currency using foreign reserves and may adjust interest rates; managed systems sit between the two.\",\"why\":\"In both cases the currency ends up worth less, so the difference in how it happened gets lost.\",\"analogy\":\"Devaluation is a shop deciding to re-price its menu; depreciation is the price being haggled down in the market.\",\"exam\":\"Calling a market-driven fall a “devaluation”, or mixing up which regime needs reserves and intervention. The terminology error suggests the regimes are not understood.\",\"ia\":\"Using regime terminology loosely when the article discusses a currency policy.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A central bank that pegs its currency announces a lower official rate. What is this?\",\"opts\":[\"A depreciation under a floating exchange rate\",\"An appreciation under a fixed exchange rate\",\"A revaluation under a fixed exchange rate\",\"A devaluation under a fixed exchange rate\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"A deliberate change to a pegged rate by the authorities is a devaluation (if lower) or a revaluation (if higher). Depreciation describes a fall driven by market forces under a floating rate.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What does a central bank need to defend a peg?\",\"a\":\"Foreign currency reserves to buy or sell its own currency, and a willingness to adjust interest rates.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The mechanisms look similar (the currency falls), so the regime distinction is lost.\",\"diag\":[\"A central bank deliberately lowers a pegged rate: devaluation or depreciation? Which regime?\",\"How is a peg maintained?\"],\"intervention\":\"Pair each term with its regime and stress reserves and intervention under fixed rates.\",\"activity\":\"Students classify six currency events as devaluation/revaluation or depreciation/appreciation and name the regime.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers use regime terminology correctly; loose interchange signals a gap.\",\"warning\":[\"Calls a free-market fall a “devaluation”\",\"Is unsure which regime needs reserves\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-09\",\"title\":\"A current account deficit is financed\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.6\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A current account deficit is always bad – the country is losing money.”\",\"right\":\"The balance of payments sums to zero overall: a current account deficit is matched by a surplus on the capital and financial accounts combined – inflows of investment, borrowing from abroad or use of reserves – plus errors and omissions. A deficit may reflect strong investment or a fast-growing economy importing capital goods. Whether it is a problem depends on its size, persistence and financing: long-term FDI is usually less risky than short-term borrowing.\",\"why\":\"“Deficit” sounds like a loss, and the offsetting financial flows are less visible.\",\"analogy\":\"A household that spends more than its income to build a business, financed by a loan or by selling shares, is in a different position from one borrowing to fund a spending spree: the financing matters.\",\"exam\":\"Condemning any current account deficit without discussing how it is financed. The evaluation then ignores the accounting identity and sustainability.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating the harm from the current account deficit in the article without considering how it is financed.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A country runs a large current account deficit. Ignoring errors and omissions, and recording the financial account as inflows minus outflows, what must be true?\",\"opts\":[\"It has a matching surplus on the capital and financial accounts combined\",\"It has a matching deficit on the financial account\",\"Its exchange rate must be fixed by the central bank\",\"Its government must be running a budget deficit\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The accounts balance, so net inflows on the capital and financial accounts – investment, borrowing or use of reserves – must offset the current account deficit. That financing, not the deficit alone, determines how sustainable it is.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"What determines whether a current account deficit is a problem?\",\"a\":\"Its size and persistence, and how it is financed (for example long-term FDI versus short-term borrowing).\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"“Deficit” carries negative connotations, and the offsetting accounts are not intuitive.\",\"diag\":[\"A country runs a current account deficit: what must be true of its financial account?\",\"Is the deficit necessarily harmful?\"],\"intervention\":\"Teach the balance of payments identity, and evaluate deficits by their financing and sustainability, not their sign.\",\"activity\":\"Given current account data, students infer the financial account position and judge sustainability (the implications of a persistent deficit are HL only).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers evaluate financing and sustainability rather than asserting that any deficit is bad.\",\"warning\":[\"Condemns any deficit with no discussion of financing\",\"Treats a current account deficit as a debt rather than a flow\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-10\",\"title\":\"Terms-of-trade improvements\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.6\",\"4.9\"],\"beyond\":\"Terms of trade is not in the 2022 guide, so this entry is enrichment. Its logic draws on PED and total revenue (2.5) and dependence on primary commodities (4.9).\",\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“An improvement in the terms of trade is always good for an economy.”\",\"right\":\"The terms of trade compare the average price of a country’s exports with the average price of its imports; an improvement means each unit of exports buys more imports. Whether that raises welfare depends on the cause and on elasticities: if rising world demand pushes export prices up, volumes and revenue tend to rise too, but if export prices rise because of domestic inflation or an appreciation and export demand is price elastic, export volumes and revenue may fall.\",\"why\":\"The word “improvement” sounds positive, and the volume effects are harder to see than the price effect.\",\"analogy\":\"Charging more per item helps only if you do not lose too many customers: a higher price with far fewer sales can mean a smaller till.\",\"exam\":\"Equating a terms-of-trade improvement with higher welfare and ignoring its cause and the effect on export volumes. The evaluation is then one-sided.\",\"ia\":\"Overstating the benefit of a terms-of-trade change in the article without considering its cause or the elasticity of export demand.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"A country’s export prices rise relative to its import prices because of high domestic inflation, and demand for its exports is price elastic. What is the likely effect on export revenue?\",\"opts\":[\"It rises, because the terms of trade have improved\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It falls, because export volumes fall proportionally more than prices rise\",\"It rises, because import prices have fallen\"],\"a\":2,\"why\":\"With elastic demand, a higher export price reduces the quantity demanded by a larger percentage, so export revenue falls. An improvement in the terms of trade does not on its own mean higher revenue or welfare; the cause and the elasticities matter.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Why might a terms-of-trade improvement reduce export revenue?\",\"a\":\"If it comes from higher export prices and export demand is price elastic, volumes fall proportionally more than prices rise.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"“Improvement” sounds positive, and the volume and elasticity effects are subtle.\",\"diag\":[\"A country’s export prices rise relative to import prices: could it still be worse off? When?\",\"Does the cause of the change matter?\"],\"intervention\":\"Separate the price effect from the volume effect, condition the verdict on the cause and on elasticity, and flag the topic as beyond the 2022 guide.\",\"activity\":\"Given a terms-of-trade change and an export elasticity, students predict the effect on export revenue.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers make the verdict depend on the cause of the change and on elasticities; treating any improvement as unambiguously good is weak.\",\"warning\":[\"Treats any terms-of-trade improvement as unambiguously good\",\"Makes no mention of export volumes or elasticity\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-11\",\"title\":\"Levels of integration and trade diversion\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.4\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A free trade area, a customs union and a common market are the same thing, and joining a bloc always raises welfare.”\",\"right\":\"Integration deepens in steps: a free trade area removes tariffs between members; a customs union adds a common external tariff; a common market adds free movement of factors such as labour and capital; a monetary union adds a common currency and a single monetary policy. Blocs can bring trade creation (lower-cost partner producers replace higher-cost domestic output) but also trade diversion (higher-cost partner producers replace cheaper outside suppliers), so the net welfare effect is uncertain (trade creation and diversion are HL only).\",\"why\":\"The categories are similar in spirit, and the trade-off between creation and diversion is an advanced idea.\",\"analogy\":\"Integration is a series of doors opening wider inside a building, but a high outer wall – the common external tariff – can steer you away from a cheaper shop outside.\",\"exam\":\"Misnaming the level of integration, or treating integration as purely beneficial. The evaluation then misses trade diversion and the loss of sovereignty.\",\"ia\":\"Mislabelling the bloc in the article, or ignoring trade diversion when evaluating it.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"What does a customs union add to a free trade area?\",\"opts\":[\"Free movement of labour and capital\",\"A common external tariff on non-members\",\"A single currency\",\"The removal of tariffs between members\"],\"a\":1,\"why\":\"A free trade area already removes internal tariffs; a customs union adds a common external tariff so that all members treat outsiders the same way. Free movement of factors defines a common market, and a single currency a monetary union.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Define trade diversion.\",\"a\":\"When joining a bloc causes imports to switch from a lower-cost non-member to a higher-cost member, because the non-member still faces a tariff that members do not (in a customs union, the common external tariff).\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"The categories are similar in spirit, and the trade-creation/diversion trade-off is advanced.\",\"diag\":[\"What does a customs union add that a free trade area lacks?\",\"Can joining a bloc ever reduce welfare?\"],\"intervention\":\"Build a ladder of integration levels and teach trade creation versus diversion as the key evaluation point (HL only).\",\"activity\":\"Students place five real blocs on the integration ladder and identify a plausible case of trade diversion.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers name the level of integration correctly and weigh creation against diversion.\",\"warning\":[\"Cannot order the levels of integration\",\"Treats a customs union and a free trade area as interchangeable\",\"Describes monetary union without the loss of independent monetary policy\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-12\",\"title\":\"Exchange rates are not set by trade alone\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.5\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“A country with a trade deficit must have a falling currency.”\",\"right\":\"A floating exchange rate is set by the demand for and supply of the currency from all sources: trade in goods and services, foreign direct and portfolio investment, remittances, speculation, relative interest rates and inflation, and central bank intervention. Capital flows can be large and fast-moving, so they often dominate short-run movements; a country with a trade deficit can still see its currency appreciate if higher interest rates attract inflows.\",\"why\":\"Trade is usually the first mechanism taught, while capital flows are larger but less visible.\",\"analogy\":\"A currency’s price is set in a busy marketplace, not just at the trade stall: investors, speculators and the central bank are buying and selling too.\",\"exam\":\"Explaining currency movements through the trade balance alone. The analysis is then incomplete, especially for short-run changes.\",\"ia\":\"Attributing the currency movement in the article only to trade and missing interest-rate or capital-flow drivers.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"Country Z has a trade deficit, but its central bank raises interest rates well above those abroad. What is the most likely short-run effect on its currency?\",\"opts\":[\"It depreciates, because of the trade deficit\",\"It is unchanged\",\"It depreciates, because higher rates reduce demand for the currency\",\"It appreciates, as financial inflows raise demand for the currency\"],\"a\":3,\"why\":\"Higher relative interest rates attract portfolio inflows, raising demand for the currency, and this can outweigh the downward pressure from the trade deficit in the short run. Looking only at the trade balance misses the capital flows.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Name two drivers of exchange rates other than trade in goods and services.\",\"a\":\"Any two of: relative interest rates, portfolio and direct investment flows, speculation, remittances, central bank intervention.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Trade is the first mechanism taught, and capital flows are larger but less visible.\",\"diag\":[\"A country has a trade deficit but high interest rates that attract capital: what happens to its currency?\",\"Which drivers dominate day-to-day movements?\"],\"intervention\":\"List all sources of demand for and supply of a currency, stressing capital flows in short-run moves.\",\"activity\":\"Students diagnose four currency movements by their likely main driver (trade, interest rates, capital flows or intervention).\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers give multi-cause exchange-rate analysis; trade-only explanations are incomplete.\",\"warning\":[\"Explains every currency move through the trade balance\",\"Omits interest rates and capital flows\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"},{\"id\":\"GLO-13\",\"title\":\"Weighing protection\",\"area\":\"Global economy\",\"subs\":[\"4.3\"],\"beyond\":null,\"student\":{\"watch\":\"“Protectionism is always bad – or, for infant industries, always justified.”\",\"right\":\"Protection raises prices for consumers, reduces efficiency and risks retaliation, but there are arguments for it in some cases, such as infant industries, national security, anti-dumping and giving an economy time to adjust. The infant-industry argument holds only if the industry does become competitive and the protection is removed; evidence is mixed, and protection can persist, leaving inefficiency and firms lobbying to keep it. A strong evaluation weighs both sides in context and reaches a justified judgement.\",\"why\":\"Economic models emphasise the costs of protection while political debate emphasises its benefits, so it is tempting to pick a side.\",\"analogy\":\"Protection is like training wheels: useful for a while for a learner, but if they stay on for good, the rider does not learn to balance.\",\"exam\":\"Writing a one-sided answer with no counter-argument, or asserting that infant-industry protection works without conditions. A list of arguments that is not weighed stays at the level of description.\",\"ia\":\"Presenting a one-sided view of the protection in the article instead of weighing costs and benefits for different stakeholders.\",\"check\":{\"q\":\"When is the infant-industry argument for protection weakest?\",\"opts\":[\"When the industry is unlikely to become competitive and protection is hard to remove\",\"When the industry is new and its costs are still high\",\"When the protection is temporary and has a clear end date\",\"When the country has a potential comparative advantage in the industry\"],\"a\":0,\"why\":\"The argument relies on the industry maturing so that the protection can end; if that is unlikely, consumers bear the costs indefinitely. Temporary, time-limited protection for an industry with a potential comparative advantage is where the argument is strongest.\"},\"retrieve\":{\"q\":\"Why might infant-industry protection fail?\",\"a\":\"The industry may not become competitive, the protection may be hard to remove because of lobbying, and consumers pay higher prices in the meantime.\"}},\"teacher\":{\"root\":\"Textbooks emphasise the efficiency costs while real-world politics emphasises protection, so students pick a side.\",\"diag\":[\"Name a defensible reason for protection and a strong reason against it.\",\"When might the infant-industry argument fail?\"],\"intervention\":\"Require a two-sided evaluation with at least one argument each way and a justified judgement.\",\"activity\":\"Structured debate on a real tariff, with each side using welfare analysis, ending in a reasoned judgement.\",\"examiner\":\"Strong answers give a balanced, two-sided evaluation and a justified judgement; one-sided answers are weaker.\",\"warning\":[\"Holds a fixed pro- or anti-protection stance with no counter-argument\",\"Lists arguments without weighing them\",\"Forgets retaliation when the country is a large trader\"]},\"src\":\"Teacher-created: IB Economics Misconception Database (Arjun Agrawal)\"}],\"gloss\":[{\"term\":\"Abnormal profit\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"Profit above normal profit, earned when average revenue exceeds average cost (AR > AC), where cost includes the opportunity cost of the owner’s resources.\",\"plain\":\"Profit beyond the minimum needed to keep the firm in business.\",\"why\":\"Abnormal profit attracts new firms where entry is free, so its persistence points to barriers to entry.\",\"ex\":\"If a new kind of drink earns its first producer abnormal profit, rivals are likely to launch similar drinks unless something prevents them.\",\"related\":[\"Normal profit\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Total revenue\",\"Monopoly\",\"Perfect competition\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with any positive accounting profit, when economists count only profit above the opportunity cost of the owner’s resources.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 38–39\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide notes that abnormal profits may finance research and development, one argument for tolerating some market power (guide PDF p. 39).\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Absolute advantage\",\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"def\":\"The ability of a country to produce more of a good than another country using the same resources.\",\"plain\":\"Being able to produce more of something than another country can with the same resources.\",\"why\":\"It is the starting point for showing that comparative advantage, not absolute advantage, is what creates gains from trade.\",\"ex\":\"If one country’s workers can produce both more wheat and more cloth per hour than another country’s, it has an absolute advantage in both goods.\",\"related\":[\"Comparative advantage\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Protectionism\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with comparative advantage, which is about opportunity cost, not output.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 51\"},{\"term\":\"Absolute poverty\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A condition in which income is insufficient to meet the basic needs required for survival, measured against a fixed line.\",\"plain\":\"Not having enough income to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water.\",\"why\":\"It is the measure behind global targets to end extreme poverty.\",\"ex\":\"A family living below the international poverty line cannot reliably afford enough food, whatever the incomes of those around it.\",\"related\":[\"Relative poverty\",\"International poverty line\",\"Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"Poverty trap\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated as 'being poor' without reference to a threshold.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"Actual growth\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"An increase in the output an economy actually produces (real GDP); on a PPC diagram it is shown by the production point moving outward, for example from inside the curve towards it.\",\"plain\":\"The economy produces more, for example by using resources that were previously idle.\",\"why\":\"It separates a rise in output from a rise in the economy’s capacity, a distinction that returns in Unit 3.\",\"ex\":\"When unemployed workers find jobs after a downturn, output rises towards the PPC without any change in the curve itself.\",\"related\":[\"Growth in production possibilities\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Economic growth\",\"Opportunity cost\"],\"confusion\":\"Any outward movement on the diagram is called ‘economic growth’ without saying whether the curve has shifted or the economy has moved towards it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Administrative barrier\",\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"def\":\"A trade barrier created by rules, standards, procedures or paperwork that raise the cost of importing, rather than by a tax or a quantity limit.\",\"plain\":\"Red tape or strict standards that make it harder to import goods.\",\"why\":\"Such barriers can protect consumers but can also serve as hidden protection that is hard to challenge.\",\"ex\":\"A country requiring lengthy safety tests that only foreign-made toys must pass is using an administrative barrier.\",\"related\":[\"Protectionism\",\"Tariff\",\"Quota\",\"World Trade Organization\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be disguised protectionism, although some standards genuinely protect health and safety.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Adverse selection\",\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"def\":\"A market outcome in which asymmetric information before a transaction leads the worse risks or lower qualities to be disproportionately represented.\",\"plain\":\"When hidden information before a deal means the lowest-quality products or riskiest customers are the most likely to trade.\",\"why\":\"It can shrink or even close markets, which justifies private and government responses such as inspections and compulsory insurance.\",\"ex\":\"If insurers cannot tell healthy from unhealthy applicants, the people keenest to buy health insurance tend to be those who expect to claim.\",\"related\":[\"Asymmetric information\",\"Moral hazard\",\"Screening\",\"Signalling\"],\"confusion\":\"Used interchangeably with moral hazard, which arises after the transaction.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"In a used-car market, buyers who fear poor quality offer a lower price, which drives the owners of good cars out of the market, so the average quality on sale falls further.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Aggregate demand\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The total planned spending on domestic goods and services at each price level, equal to C + I + G + (X − M).\",\"plain\":\"Total demand.\",\"why\":\"Changes in AD drive short-run swings in output, employment and the price level, and AD is the target of monetary and fiscal policy.\",\"ex\":\"A fall in consumer confidence that leads households to cut spending shifts the AD curve to the left.\",\"related\":[\"Aggregate supply\",\"Investment\",\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Fiscal policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Its downward slope is explained by buyers switching between goods, a microeconomic reason, instead of by the wealth, interest rate and trade effects of a lower price level.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Transfer payments are left out of G in AD because no output is bought with them; they affect AD only when recipients spend them, through consumption.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"Aggregate supply\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The total quantity of output firms are willing and able to produce at each price level.\",\"plain\":\"The total amount that firms across the economy plan to produce at each possible price level.\",\"why\":\"The shape of AS decides whether extra demand mostly raises output or mostly raises prices.\",\"ex\":\"If energy costs rise for firms across the economy, they are willing to produce less at every price level, so SRAS shifts left.\",\"related\":[\"SRAS\",\"LRAS\",\"Keynesian AS curve\",\"Aggregate demand\",\"Full employment output\"],\"confusion\":\"The short-run and long-run versions are treated as one curve.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Allocative efficiency\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The allocation where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost, so community surplus is maximised.\",\"plain\":\"Producing the goods society values most, in the amounts at which the benefit of one more unit just equals its cost.\",\"why\":\"It is the efficiency benchmark behind every market failure argument in Unit 2.\",\"ex\":\"If the benefit to society of one more bus service is greater than its cost, more services should run until the two are equal.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Social surplus\",\"Market failure\",\"Socially optimum output\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated as ‘producing what people want’ with no marginal condition.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 32–33, 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"At HL, a perfectly competitive firm is allocatively efficient where P = MC; a monopolist sets P above MC, which is why market power counts as a market failure (guide PDF p. 38).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Producing the right things.’, is the vague wording the glossary lists as the common confusion: it has no quantity and no marginal condition.\"},{\"term\":\"Anchoring\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"A bias in which a decision is disproportionately influenced by an initial reference value.\",\"plain\":\"Being swayed by the first number you see, even when it should not matter.\",\"why\":\"It helps explain why sellers show a high ‘original’ price next to a sale price.\",\"ex\":\"A menu that lists one very expensive dish can make the other dishes seem reasonably priced by comparison.\",\"related\":[\"Framing\",\"Behavioural economics\",\"Bounded rationality\",\"Choice architecture\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as general irrationality, rather than as a specific and predictable bias in which an initial reference number pulls later judgements towards it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Appreciation\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A rise in the value of a currency against another under a floating exchange rate, caused by market forces.\",\"plain\":\"A rise in a currency’s value in a floating system, so that it buys more of other currencies.\",\"why\":\"It makes imports cheaper and exports dearer, which affects inflation, growth and the current account.\",\"ex\":\"If the pound appreciates against the dollar, holidays in the USA become cheaper for UK tourists, but UK exports become more expensive for US buyers.\",\"related\":[\"Depreciation\",\"Revaluation\",\"Floating exchange rate\",\"Exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Used for a fixed-rate change, which is a revaluation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Asymmetric information\",\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"def\":\"A situation in which one party in a transaction has more or better information than the other, leading to inefficient outcomes.\",\"plain\":\"Unequal information.\",\"why\":\"It is a source of market failure at HL and helps explain insurance problems, warranties and the regulation of professions.\",\"ex\":\"The seller of a used car usually knows more about its faults than the buyer does.\",\"related\":[\"Adverse selection\",\"Moral hazard\",\"Signalling\",\"Screening\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Used to mean any lack of information, when the defining feature is that one side of the transaction knows more than the other.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Automatic stabiliser\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"A feature of the tax and benefit system that dampens fluctuations in aggregate demand without discretionary government action.\",\"plain\":\"Parts of the tax and benefit system that cushion booms and slumps on their own, without any new government decision.\",\"why\":\"They respond as soon as incomes change, avoiding the decision and implementation lags of discretionary policy.\",\"ex\":\"In a recession, income tax receipts fall and unemployment benefit payments rise without any new law, which supports household spending.\",\"related\":[\"Fiscal policy\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Transfer payment\",\"Budget deficit\",\"Business cycle\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as a policy choice rather than a built-in mechanism.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Things that stabilise the economy.’) is circular and would also describe discretionary policy, which is the confusion this term exists to prevent.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 49\"},{\"term\":\"Availability bias\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"A bias in which people judge how likely an event is by how easily examples come to mind, rather than by how often it actually occurs.\",\"plain\":\"Thinking something is more common because you can easily remember an example of it.\",\"why\":\"It can distort decisions such as buying insurance after vivid events that receive heavy media coverage.\",\"ex\":\"After extensive news coverage of a flood, households in areas at low risk may buy flood insurance they are unlikely to need.\",\"related\":[\"Framing\",\"Anchoring\",\"Bounded rationality\",\"Behavioural economics\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a lack of information, when the information exists but recent or dramatic examples are given too much weight.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Average tax rate\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"Total tax paid divided by total income.\",\"plain\":\"The share of your total income that you pay in tax.\",\"why\":\"Comparing average rates at different incomes is how you tell whether a tax is progressive, regressive or proportional.\",\"ex\":\"Someone who pays 10,000 in tax on an income of 50,000 has an average tax rate of 20% (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Marginal tax rate\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Regressive tax\",\"Proportional tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the marginal rate, which applies to the next unit of income.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide lists average and marginal tax rates within 3.4 and makes the calculation of total tax and average tax rates from data HL only (guide PDF p. 46).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Balance of payments\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"A record of all transactions between residents of a country and the rest of the world over a period.\",\"plain\":\"A country’s record of all its economic transactions with the rest of the world over a period.\",\"why\":\"It shows how a country pays for its imports and whether it is borrowing from, or lending to, the rest of the world.\",\"ex\":\"When a Brazilian firm sells coffee to a German buyer, the payment is recorded as a credit in Brazil’s balance of payments.\",\"related\":[\"Current account\",\"Capital account\",\"Financial account\",\"Exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a measure of trade alone, omitting income and transfers.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Because every transaction has two sides, the accounts sum to zero overall, apart from errors and omissions: a deficit on one account is matched by surpluses elsewhere.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Balance of trade\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The value of a country’s exports minus the value of its imports over a period; the guide separates the balance of trade in goods from the balance of trade in services.\",\"plain\":\"The value of exports minus the value of imports.\",\"why\":\"It is the part of the current account most directly affected by exchange rates and competitiveness.\",\"ex\":\"A country whose car exports are worth less than its oil imports, with no other trade, has a deficit on its balance of trade in goods.\",\"related\":[\"Current account\",\"Terms of trade\",\"Marshall-Lerner condition\",\"J-curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as the whole current account, or as the same thing as the terms of trade, which compares prices rather than values.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Barriers to entry\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"Obstacles that make it costly or impossible for new firms to enter a market.\",\"plain\":\"Things that make it hard for new firms to start competing in a market.\",\"why\":\"They decide whether abnormal profits can last, and so separate monopoly and oligopoly from more competitive markets.\",\"ex\":\"The high cost of building a national mobile network is a barrier to entry for new phone companies.\",\"related\":[\"Monopoly\",\"Oligopoly\",\"Economies of scale\",\"Abnormal profit\",\"Market power\"],\"confusion\":\"Listed without explaining their effect, which is to let existing firms keep abnormal profit in the long run.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 37–38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Basic economic questions\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The three questions every economy must answer because of scarcity: what and how much to produce, how to produce it, and for whom to produce it.\",\"plain\":\"Every society has to decide what gets made, how it gets made and who gets it.\",\"why\":\"Economic systems differ mainly in who answers these questions: markets, the government or a mix of both.\",\"ex\":\"Whether a country allocates health care by ability to pay or by medical need is its answer to the ‘for whom’ question.\",\"related\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Free market economy\",\"Planned economy\",\"Mixed economy\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a list to memorise rather than as the problem that markets and governments are each trying to solve.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Behavioural economics\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The study of how psychological and social factors cause decisions to depart systematically from the rational choice model.\",\"plain\":\"The study of how real people make decisions, including the predictable ways they depart from purely rational choice.\",\"why\":\"It tests the rational consumer assumption and supports policy tools such as nudges.\",\"ex\":\"Studies of workplace pensions generally find that more people save when they are enrolled by default and can opt out than when they must choose to opt in.\",\"related\":[\"Bounded rationality\",\"Choice architecture\",\"Nudge\",\"Anchoring\",\"Utility\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as evidence that economics is wrong, rather than as a correction to one assumption of the rational choice model.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 30, 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"SL students meet behavioural economics in 1.2 as a 21st-century development in economic thought; the detailed critique of rational consumer choice in 2.4 is HL only (guide PDF pp. 30, 33).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Bounded rationality\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The idea that people try to make rational decisions but are limited by the information available, the time they have and their ability to process information, so they often rely on shortcuts.\",\"plain\":\"People try to choose sensibly but cannot know or work out everything.\",\"why\":\"It replaces the assumption of perfect rationality and explains why rules of thumb and nudges can change decisions.\",\"ex\":\"Faced with dozens of mobile phone contracts, many consumers choose a familiar brand rather than comparing every tariff.\",\"related\":[\"Behavioural economics\",\"Bounded self-control\",\"Bounded selfishness\",\"Satisficing\",\"Anchoring\"],\"confusion\":\"Taken to mean people are foolish or random, when the claim is that decisions are reasonable given limited information and time and depart from the model in systematic ways.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Bounded self-control\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The tendency for people to lack the self-discipline to carry out their own long-term plans, so they make choices they later regret.\",\"plain\":\"Knowing what is good for you in the long run but struggling to stick to it.\",\"why\":\"It helps explain under-saving and over-consumption of demerit goods, and it supports default options and commitment devices.\",\"ex\":\"Someone who plans to start saving next month but keeps putting it off is showing bounded self-control.\",\"related\":[\"Bounded rationality\",\"Demerit good\",\"Choice architecture\",\"Nudge\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a lack of information, when the person knows the better choice but does not follow through.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Bounded selfishness\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The observation that people are not purely self-interested and often care about fairness and the welfare of others, even at some cost to themselves.\",\"plain\":\"People sometimes put others first, even when it costs them.\",\"why\":\"It challenges the rational self-interest assumption and helps explain charitable giving and cooperation over shared resources.\",\"ex\":\"Many people donate blood or leave a tip in a restaurant they do not expect to visit again, although neither choice brings them a material benefit.\",\"related\":[\"Bounded rationality\",\"Collective self-governance\",\"Behavioural economics\",\"Corporate social responsibility\"],\"confusion\":\"Taken to mean people are generally unselfish, when the claim is only that self-interest has limits the rational model ignores.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Budget deficit\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"The excess of government spending over government revenue in a period.\",\"plain\":\"The amount by which a government’s spending exceeds its revenue in a year, which it must borrow.\",\"why\":\"Deficits let governments support demand in downturns, but each deficit adds to the national debt.\",\"ex\":\"A government that spends more on benefits during a recession, while its tax revenue falls, sees its budget deficit widen.\",\"related\":[\"National debt\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Automatic stabiliser\",\"Crowding out\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with national debt, which is the accumulated stock.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide names the budget deficit explicitly in the HL-only content on government debt (guide PDF p. 44); at SL the idea appears through fiscal policy and the constraint of sustainable debt (guide PDF p. 49).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 44, 49\"},{\"term\":\"Business cycle\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"Short-term fluctuations of real output around its long-term growth trend.\",\"plain\":\"The economy’s output rises and falls in waves around its long-run path.\",\"why\":\"Where the economy sits in the cycle shapes which problem, unemployment or inflation, policymakers should worry about most.\",\"ex\":\"A country’s real GDP grows quickly for several years, then shrinks for two quarters before recovering, completing one cycle around its trend.\",\"related\":[\"Recession\",\"Economic growth\",\"Full employment output\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Inflationary gap\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn without the trend line, which is the part that carries the meaning.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"Capital account\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The part of the balance of payments recording capital transfers and transactions in non-produced, non-financial assets.\",\"plain\":\"A small part of the balance of payments recording one-off capital transfers, such as debt forgiveness, and trade in assets such as land rights or brand names.\",\"why\":\"It completes the balance of payments, and keeping it separate from the financial account keeps the analysis of investment flows clear.\",\"ex\":\"When one government cancels debt owed to it by another, the debt forgiveness is recorded as a capital transfer.\",\"related\":[\"Financial account\",\"Current account\",\"Balance of payments\",\"Debt relief\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the financial account, which records investment flows.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Capital expenditure\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"Government spending on assets that last for many years, such as roads, schools, hospitals and equipment.\",\"plain\":\"Government spending on things that will be used for years, such as new roads and schools.\",\"why\":\"It adds to AD now and can raise productive capacity later, unlike day-to-day spending.\",\"ex\":\"Building a new railway line is capital expenditure, while paying the train drivers’ wages is current expenditure.\",\"related\":[\"Fiscal policy\",\"Investment\",\"Transfer payment\",\"Interventionist supply-side policies\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with current expenditure, which covers recurring costs such as public sector wages and supplies.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 48\"},{\"term\":\"Capital flight\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"The movement of financial capital out of a country, typically in response to risk or instability.\",\"plain\":\"Money moving rapidly out of a country as investors lose confidence in it.\",\"why\":\"It drains funds needed for domestic investment and can trigger a currency crisis.\",\"ex\":\"Fearing political instability, wealthy residents move their savings into bank accounts abroad.\",\"related\":[\"Financial account\",\"Portfolio investment\",\"Depreciation\",\"Poverty trap\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as trade rather than as an asset flow.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 58\"},{\"term\":\"Carbon tax\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"An indirect tax levied on the carbon content of fuels, intended to internalise the external cost of emissions.\",\"plain\":\"A tax on fuels based on how much carbon dioxide they release when burned.\",\"why\":\"It puts a price on emissions so that firms and households take climate costs into account.\",\"ex\":\"A carbon tax raises the price of coal-fired electricity relative to wind power, which encourages a shift towards cleaner generation.\",\"related\":[\"Pigouvian tax\",\"Tradable permit\",\"Negative externality\",\"Marginal social cost\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as a punishment, rather than as a price that makes emitters pay for the external cost while leaving them to choose how far to cut emissions.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Central bank\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The institution responsible for a country’s monetary policy, which usually also acts as banker to the government and lender of last resort to commercial banks.\",\"plain\":\"The bank in charge of a country’s money and interest rates.\",\"why\":\"Its decisions on interest rates and the money supply affect borrowing, spending and inflation across the economy.\",\"ex\":\"The Bank of England, the US Federal Reserve and the Reserve Bank of India are central banks.\",\"related\":[\"Monetary policy\",\"Inflation targeting\",\"Minimum lending rate\",\"Quantitative easing\",\"Money creation\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the government’s finance ministry, although in many countries the central bank sets monetary policy independently of the government.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Ceteris paribus\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"The assumption that all other relevant variables are held constant while one variable is varied.\",\"plain\":\"When nothing else changes.\",\"why\":\"It lets economists isolate one cause and effect, such as the effect of a good’s price on the quantity demanded.\",\"ex\":\"The law of demand holds ceteris paribus, so it is not refuted when ice-cream sales rise in a heatwave despite a price rise: the weather has shifted demand.\",\"related\":[\"Economic model\",\"Law of demand\",\"Positive economics\",\"Shift of the demand curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a flaw that makes models useless, rather than as a deliberate simplification that lets one relationship be examined at a time.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Choice architecture\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The deliberate design of how a choice is presented (for example a default, restricted or mandated choice) in order to influence the decision made. A nudge is the subset that leaves every option available.\",\"plain\":\"The way choices are set out, which can steer what people pick.\",\"why\":\"It is the practical side of behavioural economics named in the guide, and it links to nudges as a form of intervention.\",\"ex\":\"A school canteen that places fruit at eye level and desserts at the back of the counter is using choice architecture.\",\"related\":[\"Nudge\",\"Behavioural economics\",\"Bounded rationality\",\"Framing\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as the same thing as a nudge, when choice architecture also covers restricted choices, which remove some options, whereas a nudge leaves every option open.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide names three forms: default choices (an option applies unless people opt out), restricted choices (the options on offer are limited) and mandated choices (people must make an active decision).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Circular flow of income\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"A model of the flows of income, expenditure and output between households, firms, government, the financial sector and the foreign sector.\",\"plain\":\"A diagram of money moving round the economy: households spend at firms, and firms pay households for their work and resources.\",\"why\":\"It shows that one agent’s spending is another agent’s income, and it underpins national income accounting in Unit 3.\",\"ex\":\"When households save more and spend less, firms receive less revenue and may pay out less income, unless an injection such as investment fills the gap.\",\"related\":[\"Leakages\",\"Injections\",\"Interdependence\",\"Factors of production\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn as a closed loop of households and firms only, leaving out the leakages and injections that explain why national income rises or falls.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The flow is in equilibrium when total leakages (saving, taxes, imports) equal total injections (investment, government spending, exports); each pair need not match on its own.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Collective self-governance\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The management of a common pool resource by its own users, who agree rules, monitor use and apply sanctions without relying mainly on government control or private ownership.\",\"plain\":\"People who share a resource agree their own rules for using it and enforce them.\",\"why\":\"The guide names it as an approach to managing common pool resources, and it challenges the idea that only governments or markets can prevent overuse.\",\"ex\":\"Fishing communities that agree seasons and catch limits among themselves, and penalise members who break them, are practising collective self-governance.\",\"related\":[\"Common pool resource\",\"Tragedy of the commons\",\"Bounded selfishness\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to work anywhere, when it tends to succeed where users are few, know each other and can monitor use, and struggles with global resources such as the atmosphere.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 37, 40\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"The approach is associated with the political economist Elinor Ostrom, whose studies of how communities manage shared resources earned her a share of the 2009 Nobel memorial prize in economics.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Collusion\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"An agreement between firms in an oligopoly to restrict output or fix price, acting jointly as a monopolist.\",\"plain\":\"Rival firms agreeing, openly or secretly, not to compete, for example by fixing prices.\",\"why\":\"Collusion lets an oligopoly behave like a monopoly, raising prices and reducing output, which is why it is illegal in many countries.\",\"ex\":\"Construction firms that secretly agree which of them will win each government contract, and at what price, are colluding.\",\"related\":[\"Oligopoly\",\"Game theory\",\"Monopoly\",\"Concentration ratio\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed stable; the incentive to cheat is what makes it fragile.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Collusion can be formal, as in a cartel, or tacit, as when firms follow a price leader without any explicit agreement.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Command and control regulation\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"Government rules, backed by legal penalties, that directly require or forbid particular behaviour, such as emission limits, product standards or bans.\",\"plain\":\"Laws that tell people or firms what they must or must not do.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the main forms of intervention in the guide and is often compared with market-based tools such as taxes and tradable permits.\",\"ex\":\"A law banning shops from handing out single-use plastic bags is command and control regulation.\",\"related\":[\"Tradable permit\",\"Pigouvian tax\",\"Nudge\",\"Negative externality\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be more effective than market-based policies in all cases, when its success depends on monitoring and enforcement and it gives firms no reason to cut pollution below the legal limit.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Common market\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"A customs union in which labour and capital can also move freely between member countries.\",\"plain\":\"A customs union in which workers and money can also move freely between members.\",\"why\":\"Free movement of factors can raise efficiency and widen job opportunities, but raises questions about sovereignty and migration.\",\"ex\":\"The EU single market allows goods, services, capital and people to move between member states.\",\"related\":[\"Customs union\",\"Free trade area\",\"Monetary union\",\"Trading bloc\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a monetary union, although a common market does not require a shared currency.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Common pool resource\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A resource that is rivalrous in consumption but non-excludable, leading to over-use.\",\"plain\":\"A shared natural resource that is hard to keep people away from and that gets used up as people use it.\",\"why\":\"It explains why resources such as fisheries, forests and aquifers tend to be over-used when there are no rules or ownership.\",\"ex\":\"Groundwater drawn by many farms from one aquifer is a common pool resource: each farm’s pumping lowers the water left for the others.\",\"related\":[\"Tragedy of the commons\",\"Collective self-governance\",\"Tradable permit\",\"Non-excludable\"],\"confusion\":\"Called a public good because both are non-excludable, when a common pool resource is rivalrous, which is why it runs down.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 36–37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide notes that unsustainable use of common pool resources can itself create negative externalities, for example when deforestation affects rainfall and climate far beyond the forest (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Shared resources.’, leaves out rivalry, the property that the inventory’s own note uses to separate it from a public good.\",\"alias\":[\"Common access resource\"]},{\"term\":\"Comparative advantage\",\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"def\":\"The ability of a country to produce a good at a lower opportunity cost than another country.\",\"plain\":\"Being able to produce something while giving up less of other goods than another country would.\",\"why\":\"It shows that two countries can both gain from specialising and trading, even if one is better at producing everything.\",\"ex\":\"Even if Country A is absolutely better at making both wheat and cloth, it gains by specialising in the good where its advantage is greatest and importing the other.\",\"related\":[\"Absolute advantage\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"World price\"],\"confusion\":\"Identified from output levels rather than from opportunity cost ratios.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"HL students also evaluate the theory’s limitations (guide PDF p. 52): real trade involves transport costs, factors that do not move easily between industries, and opportunity costs that are not constant.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Being better at something.’) describes absolute advantage, the exact confusion the entry warns against.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 51\"},{\"term\":\"Competitive supply\",\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"def\":\"The production of alternative goods that use the same resources, so that producing more of one means producing less of the other.\",\"plain\":\"Goods that compete for the same land, labour or machines, so making more of one means making less of the other.\",\"why\":\"It explains why a price rise in one market can reduce supply in another.\",\"ex\":\"If the price of soybeans rises, some farmers switch fields from maize to soybeans, which reduces the supply of maize.\",\"related\":[\"Joint supply\",\"Supply\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Substitute\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with joint supply, or with substitutes in consumption, when competitive supply concerns what producers make rather than what consumers buy.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Complement\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"A good consumed together with another, so that a rise in the price of one reduces demand for the other.\",\"plain\":\"A good used together with another, such as printers and ink cartridges.\",\"why\":\"The price of complements is a non-price determinant of demand, so a price change in one market shifts demand in another.\",\"ex\":\"If the price of games consoles falls, more consoles are bought and demand for the games played on them tends to rise.\",\"related\":[\"Substitute\",\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"Demand\",\"Joint supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn as a movement along the other good’s demand curve, when a price change for one complement shifts the whole demand curve for the other.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Composite indicator\",\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"def\":\"A development measure combining several dimensions into a single index, such as the HDI.\",\"plain\":\"A single score that combines several measures, such as health, education and income.\",\"why\":\"It captures more of what development means than income alone.\",\"ex\":\"The Human Development Index combines life expectancy, schooling and income into a single value between 0 and 1.\",\"related\":[\"Human Development Index\",\"Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"Gender Inequality Index\",\"Happy Planet Index\",\"Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as 'more accurate' rather than as measuring a different, broader thing.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Building a composite involves judgements about which dimensions to include and how to weight them, a problem the guide raises as a TOK question (guide PDF p. 59).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"Concentration ratio\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The combined market share of the largest few firms in an industry.\",\"plain\":\"The share of a market held by its biggest few firms.\",\"why\":\"It gives a quick measure of how concentrated a market is and can indicate oligopoly.\",\"ex\":\"A four-firm concentration ratio adds together the market shares of the four largest firms in an industry.\",\"related\":[\"Oligopoly\",\"Market power\",\"Monopoly\",\"Collusion\"],\"confusion\":\"Quoted without stating how many firms are included, or read as a direct measure of market power, when it says nothing about how intensely the largest firms compete.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 39\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Constant opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The situation in which each additional unit of one good requires giving up the same amount of another, because resources are equally suited to producing both; it gives a straight-line PPC.\",\"plain\":\"Each extra unit of one good costs the same amount of the other good, however much you already make.\",\"why\":\"It is the simpler case used when comparing opportunity costs, and the contrast shows why the usual PPC is bowed.\",\"ex\":\"If a workshop’s staff can make either three chairs or one table in a day, whatever the current mix, each table costs three chairs.\",\"related\":[\"Increasing opportunity cost\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Comparative advantage\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be the normal case, when it requires resources that are equally productive in both uses, which is unusual.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Straight-line PPCs are also the usual starting point for comparative advantage in Unit 4 (HL only, guide PDF p. 51), where the slope of each country’s line measures its opportunity cost.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Consumer price index\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A weighted index of the prices of a representative basket of goods and services bought by households, used to measure inflation.\",\"plain\":\"A number that tracks how the cost of a typical household’s shopping basket changes over time.\",\"why\":\"Inflation targets, and many adjustments to wages and benefits, are based on the CPI.\",\"ex\":\"If the CPI rises from 100 to 104 over a year, the inflation rate is 4% (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Inflation\",\"Price deflator\",\"Deflation\",\"Inflation targeting\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as measuring every household’s inflation, although households whose spending differs from the average basket face different price changes.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Each price is weighted by its share of household spending, so a rise in rents counts for more than an equal rise in the price of matches. Calculating a weighted price index from data is HL only (guide PDF p. 44).\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Consumer surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The difference between the price consumers are willing to pay and the price they actually pay.\",\"plain\":\"The extra benefit buyers get when they pay less than the most they would have been willing to pay.\",\"why\":\"It measures buyers’ gain from a market, so it shows who gains and who loses from taxes, subsidies and price controls.\",\"ex\":\"A fan who would have paid well above the ticket price enjoys consumer surplus equal to the difference between what she would have paid and what she did pay.\",\"related\":[\"Producer surplus\",\"Social surplus\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Demand\"],\"confusion\":\"Shaded below the price line rather than between demand and price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 32–33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Calculating consumer and producer surplus from a diagram is an HL-only calculation; SL students still need to identify and explain the areas (guide PDF pp. 32–33).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Money consumers save.’, contradicts the inventory’s own note that consumer surplus is a benefit measure, not saving.\"},{\"term\":\"Contractionary policy\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"Policy intended to reduce aggregate demand, through higher interest rates or a tighter budget.\",\"plain\":\"Action by the government or central bank to slow the growth of total spending in the economy.\",\"why\":\"It is the standard demand-side response to an inflationary gap.\",\"ex\":\"A central bank raising its policy interest rate to cool a boom is using contractionary monetary policy.\",\"related\":[\"Expansionary policy\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Inflationary gap\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as 'reducing inflation' rather than by its mechanism.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 48\"},{\"term\":\"Corporate social responsibility\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"The pursuit by a firm of social and environmental objectives beyond what the law requires, such as ethical sourcing or cutting emissions, alongside or instead of maximising profit.\",\"plain\":\"A business choosing to act ethically or protect the environment beyond what the law demands.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the alternative business objectives in the guide and raises the question of whether firms can reduce market failures voluntarily.\",\"ex\":\"A coffee retailer that pays growers above the market price in return for meeting labour and environmental standards is pursuing a CSR objective.\",\"related\":[\"Profit maximisation\",\"Satisficing\",\"Negative externality\",\"Bounded selfishness\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be purely charitable, when CSR may also serve long-run profit by building reputation and customer loyalty.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Cost-push inflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A sustained rise in the price level caused by an increase in the costs of production, shifting SRAS left.\",\"plain\":\"Prices across the economy rise because it has become more expensive for firms to produce.\",\"why\":\"It can raise inflation and unemployment at the same time, which makes the policy response harder.\",\"ex\":\"A large rise in imported energy prices raises costs for firms across the economy and pushes the price level up.\",\"related\":[\"Demand-pull inflation\",\"SRAS\",\"Inflation\",\"Phillips curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn by shifting AD, which is demand-pull.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Crowding out\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"The reduction in private investment that can result when government borrowing raises interest rates.\",\"plain\":\"Government borrowing that can push up interest rates and so reduce private investment.\",\"why\":\"If crowding out is strong, fiscal expansion raises output by less than the multiplier suggests.\",\"ex\":\"A government borrows heavily to fund new spending near full employment; interest rates rise and some firms postpone investment projects.\",\"related\":[\"Fiscal policy\",\"Budget deficit\",\"Investment\",\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Real interest rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Asserted as automatic and complete, although it is most likely near full employment and least likely in a deep recession with low interest rates.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Keynesians argue that with large spare capacity, government spending may raise business confidence and ‘crowd in’ private investment instead.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 49\"},{\"term\":\"Current account\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The part of the balance of payments recording trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income.\",\"plain\":\"The record of a country’s trade in goods and services with the rest of the world, plus income and transfers.\",\"why\":\"A persistent deficit or surplus has implications for the exchange rate, debt and foreign ownership of domestic assets.\",\"ex\":\"A country that imports more goods than it exports but earns large interest and dividends from abroad may have only a small current account deficit.\",\"related\":[\"Balance of trade\",\"Financial account\",\"Capital account\",\"Current transfers\",\"Marshall-Lerner condition\"],\"confusion\":\"Equated with the balance of trade in goods, leaving out services, income and current transfers.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Trade balance.’) contradicts its own note (‘Four components, not one’); the trade balance is only part of the current account.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Current transfers\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"Payments made with nothing received in return, such as remittances and foreign aid, recorded in the current account.\",\"plain\":\"Money sent across borders with nothing given in return, such as remittances or aid.\",\"why\":\"For some countries they are a large source of foreign currency that helps pay for imports.\",\"ex\":\"Emergency cash aid sent to a country after a flood is recorded as a current transfer in its current account.\",\"related\":[\"Remittance\",\"Current account\",\"Foreign aid\",\"Capital account\"],\"confusion\":\"Placed in the financial account.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Customs union\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"An agreement between countries to abolish trade barriers among members and adopt a common external tariff.\",\"plain\":\"A group of countries that trade freely with each other and charge the same tariffs on imports from outside.\",\"why\":\"Its common external tariff means members must agree a joint trade policy towards non-members, which reduces national control over trade.\",\"ex\":\"The European Union is a customs union: goods move between members without tariffs, and the same tariff applies to imports from outside.\",\"related\":[\"Free trade area\",\"Common market\",\"Trading bloc\",\"Trade creation\",\"Trade diversion\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a free trade area, which has no common external tariff.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"Unemployment caused by a fall in aggregate demand during a downturn in the business cycle.\",\"plain\":\"Recession unemployment.\",\"why\":\"It is the type of unemployment that demand-side policy can reduce.\",\"ex\":\"Construction workers lose their jobs when a recession cuts spending on new buildings, and are rehired when demand recovers.\",\"related\":[\"Structural unemployment\",\"Frictional unemployment\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Expansionary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Called structural, which has a different cause and a different remedy.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Debt relief\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"The partial or full cancellation, or rescheduling, of debts that a country owes to foreign governments or institutions.\",\"plain\":\"Cancelling or easing the debts a poor country owes abroad.\",\"why\":\"It frees government revenue from debt repayments for spending on health, education and infrastructure.\",\"ex\":\"Under the Heavily Indebted Poor Countries initiative, many low-income countries had part of their external debt cancelled.\",\"related\":[\"Foreign aid\",\"Official development assistance\",\"Capital account\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to help a country’s people directly, although the benefit depends on how the government uses the revenue it frees.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 59\"},{\"term\":\"Deflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A sustained decrease in the general price level of an economy over time.\",\"plain\":\"The general level of prices keeps falling over time.\",\"why\":\"Expected price falls can lead households to delay spending and raise the real burden of debt, which can deepen a downturn.\",\"ex\":\"Japan experienced long periods of mild deflation from the late 1990s, alongside weak growth in spending.\",\"related\":[\"Inflation\",\"Disinflation\",\"Consumer price index\",\"Real interest rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with disinflation, which is a fall in the rate of inflation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Economists separate deflation caused by falling AD, which tends to come with rising unemployment, from deflation caused by rising SRAS, for example rapid productivity gains, which can accompany growing output.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Prices falling.’) could describe a single good; deflation is a sustained fall in the general price level, as the inventory note itself says.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Deflationary gap\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The situation in which real output is below the full employment level of output, so there is a negative output gap.\",\"plain\":\"When the economy is weak.\",\"why\":\"A deflationary gap means idle resources and cyclical unemployment, which is the case for expansionary policy.\",\"ex\":\"During a recession in which firms cut output and lay off workers, real GDP falls below potential and a deflationary gap opens.\",\"related\":[\"Inflationary gap\",\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"Full employment output\",\"Expansionary policy\",\"Keynesian AS curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Marked on the price axis rather than the output axis.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide also calls it a recessionary gap. Monetarist/new classical economists expect it to close as wages and costs adjust; Keynesians argue it can persist, which supports active demand management.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Demand\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"The quantity of a good consumers are willing and able to purchase at each price over a given period.\",\"plain\":\"How much of a good people would buy at each possible price, counting only purchases they can afford.\",\"why\":\"Demand is one half of the price mechanism, so almost every microeconomic argument starts from it.\",\"ex\":\"Many students may want a new laptop, but only those willing and able to pay a given price count in the demand for laptops at that price.\",\"related\":[\"Law of demand\",\"Supply\",\"Market equilibrium\",\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"Utility\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined as what people want, leaving out ‘willing and able’ and the time period, or used interchangeably with quantity demanded at a single price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘How much people want a good.’, describes desire; the inventory’s own note says ‘willing and able’ is what separates demand from desire.\"},{\"term\":\"Demand-pull inflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A sustained rise in the price level caused by increases in aggregate demand.\",\"plain\":\"Prices across the economy rise because total spending is growing faster than the economy can produce.\",\"why\":\"Diagnosing inflation as demand-pull points to contractionary demand-side policy as a suitable remedy.\",\"ex\":\"A credit-fuelled consumer boom near full employment shifts AD to the right and pulls the price level up.\",\"related\":[\"Cost-push inflation\",\"Inflationary gap\",\"Aggregate demand\",\"Contractionary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Diagnosed from the fact of rising prices rather than from which curve moved.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Demerit good\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A good considered to be over-consumed relative to the social optimum because consumption generates negative externalities or consumers misjudge the private benefit.\",\"plain\":\"A good that people buy more of than is best for society, often because they underrate its harm.\",\"why\":\"It justifies taxes, bans, age limits and information campaigns on goods such as tobacco and alcohol.\",\"ex\":\"Heavy drinking can harm both the drinker and others, and drinkers may underestimate the long-term damage, so alcohol is often treated as a demerit good.\",\"related\":[\"Merit good\",\"Negative externality\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Bounded self-control\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined as ‘bad for you’, which is a value judgement rather than a market failure.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Depreciation\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A fall in the value of a floating currency in terms of another currency, caused by market forces.\",\"plain\":\"Currency falling.\",\"why\":\"It makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home, which can boost AD but also raise inflation.\",\"ex\":\"If the yen depreciates against the dollar, Japanese cars become cheaper for US buyers.\",\"related\":[\"Appreciation\",\"Devaluation\",\"Marshall-Lerner condition\",\"J-curve\",\"Floating exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Used for a fixed-rate change, which is a devaluation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Deregulation\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"The removal or reduction of government rules that restrict competition or entry into a market.\",\"plain\":\"Cutting government rules so that more firms can compete.\",\"why\":\"More competition can lower costs and prices, although removing rules may also remove protections for consumers, workers or the environment.\",\"ex\":\"US airline deregulation in 1978 removed government control of fares and routes, allowing new airlines to enter the market.\",\"related\":[\"Privatisation\",\"Market-based supply-side policies\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Supply-side policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Taken to mean removing all regulation, including safety or environmental rules that correct market failure.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 49\"},{\"term\":\"Devaluation\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A deliberate reduction in the value of a currency by the authorities under a fixed exchange rate.\",\"plain\":\"The government or central bank deliberately lowers the value of a fixed currency.\",\"why\":\"It is a policy choice to regain export competitiveness, with risks for inflation and the credibility of the currency.\",\"ex\":\"A country whose currency is pegged to the US dollar announces a new, lower peg to make its exports cheaper.\",\"related\":[\"Revaluation\",\"Depreciation\",\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Expenditure switching\"],\"confusion\":\"Used interchangeably with depreciation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Direct tax\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A tax levied on income or wealth, paid directly by the person or firm on whom it is levied.\",\"plain\":\"A tax on income or wealth, paid to the government by the person or firm it is charged on.\",\"why\":\"Direct taxes are the main tool for making a tax system progressive.\",\"ex\":\"Personal income tax, corporate income tax and wealth taxes are all direct taxes.\",\"related\":[\"Indirect tax\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Average tax rate\",\"Fiscal policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Classified by who hands the money to the tax authority, rather than by what is taxed: income, profits or wealth for a direct tax, spending for an indirect tax.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Disinflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A fall in the rate of inflation, where the general price level continues to rise but more slowly.\",\"plain\":\"Slower inflation.\",\"why\":\"Most anti-inflation policy aims at disinflation rather than deflation: prices keep rising, only more slowly.\",\"ex\":\"If inflation falls from 6% to 3% over a year, the price level is still rising, so this is disinflation (illustrative figures).\",\"related\":[\"Inflation\",\"Deflation\",\"Contractionary policy\",\"Inflation targeting\"],\"confusion\":\"Reported as prices falling, which is deflation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Dumping\",\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"def\":\"The sale of a good in an export market at a price below its cost of production, or below the price charged in the home market.\",\"plain\":\"Selling exports abroad below cost, or below the price charged at home.\",\"why\":\"Claims of dumping are a common justification for anti-dumping duties, so judging them carefully matters for trade policy.\",\"ex\":\"A government imposes an extra import duty after finding that a foreign producer sells steel abroad more cheaply than it does at home.\",\"related\":[\"Protectionism\",\"Tariff\",\"World Trade Organization\",\"Export subsidy\"],\"confusion\":\"Any cheap import is called dumping, even when its low price reflects genuine comparative advantage.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Economic development\",\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"def\":\"A multidimensional improvement in living standards, encompassing income, health, education, equity and freedom.\",\"plain\":\"A broad improvement in people’s lives, including health, education, income, freedom and fairness.\",\"why\":\"It reminds economists that output growth is a means to better lives, not the goal itself.\",\"ex\":\"In some resource-rich economies, national income has risen quickly while many people have seen little improvement in health or schooling, which is growth without broad development.\",\"related\":[\"Economic growth\",\"Human Development Index\",\"Sustainable development\",\"Composite indicator\",\"Poverty trap\"],\"confusion\":\"Used as a synonym for growth.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"Economic growth\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"An increase in real output (real GDP) over time. An increase in potential output is shown by an outward shift of the production possibilities curve or a rightward shift of LRAS.\",\"plain\":\"An increase in the amount the economy actually produces, measured by real GDP.\",\"why\":\"Growth raises the average income available to a country, although it does not by itself ensure better lives for everyone.\",\"ex\":\"New factories and a better-trained workforce let a country produce more each year, shifting its PPC outward.\",\"related\":[\"Real GDP\",\"Economic development\",\"LRAS\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Business cycle\"],\"confusion\":\"Any rise in output is called growth without separating short-term actual growth, which uses spare capacity (an AD effect), from long-term growth in potential output (an LRAS or PPC shift).\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Growth rate = (real GDP this year − real GDP last year) ÷ real GDP last year × 100, a calculation the guide requires (guide PDF p. 43).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘GDP going up.’) would count a purely nominal rise caused by inflation; growth is a rise in real GDP.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Economic model\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"A simplified representation of economic behaviour, often a diagram or equation, built on stated assumptions in order to explain relationships and make testable predictions.\",\"plain\":\"A simplified picture of how part of the economy works.\",\"why\":\"Almost all IB analysis uses models, and evaluating them means asking whether their assumptions hold in the case being studied.\",\"ex\":\"The demand and supply diagram is a model: it leaves out many real-world details in order to predict how price responds to a change in costs.\",\"related\":[\"Ceteris paribus\",\"Positive economics\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Circular flow of income\"],\"confusion\":\"Dismissed as useless because its assumptions are unrealistic, when a model is judged by whether it explains and predicts well enough for its purpose.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Economies of scale\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"Reductions in long-run average cost as the scale of output increases.\",\"plain\":\"Costs per unit fall as a firm grows bigger.\",\"why\":\"They can justify large firms and natural monopoly, and they are also a benefit of international trade in Unit 4.\",\"ex\":\"A large supermarket chain can buy in bulk and spread its distribution costs over more sales, lowering its cost per item.\",\"related\":[\"Natural monopoly\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Monopoly\",\"Law of diminishing marginal returns\"],\"confusion\":\"Used to justify monopoly without weighing allocative inefficiency.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 39, 51\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"In 2.11, which is HL only, economies of scale appear as an advantage of large firms; all students also meet them as a benefit of international trade in 4.1 (guide PDF pp. 39, 51).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Elasticity\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A measure of the responsiveness of one variable to a change in another, expressed as a ratio of percentage changes.\",\"plain\":\"How strongly one thing reacts when another thing changes.\",\"why\":\"Elasticity tells firms and governments how much a change in price or income will actually change behaviour.\",\"ex\":\"If a small price rise makes many customers switch to a rival brand, demand for that brand is price elastic.\",\"related\":[\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"Percentage change\"],\"confusion\":\"Read off the steepness of a curve, which depends on the scale chosen.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Engel curve\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A curve showing the relationship between consumer income and the quantity demanded of a good, with price held constant, used to illustrate income elasticity of demand.\",\"plain\":\"A graph of how much of a good people buy as their income changes.\",\"why\":\"It shows at a glance whether a good is a necessity, a luxury or an inferior good.\",\"ex\":\"An Engel curve for a staple food shows the quantity bought rising less than proportionately with income, while one for foreign holidays shows it rising more than proportionately.\",\"related\":[\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"Normal good\",\"Inferior good\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn with price on an axis, when an Engel curve plots income against quantity demanded with price held constant.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Equality\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"A situation in which economic outcomes, such as income or wealth, are the same or similar across people or groups; it describes a distribution rather than judging whether it is fair.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone ends up with the same or similar amounts.\",\"why\":\"The guide separates equality from equity, and evaluating redistribution depends on keeping the two apart.\",\"ex\":\"Paying every worker the same wage regardless of hours worked would create more equality of income, but many people would not consider it fair.\",\"related\":[\"Equity\",\"Normative economics\",\"Value judgement\",\"Wealth\"],\"confusion\":\"Used as a synonym for equity, when equity is a normative judgement about fairness and a fair outcome need not be an equal one.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 19, 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Economists distinguish equality of outcome from equality of opportunity; a society can move towards one while moving away from the other.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Equity\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"Fairness in the distribution of income, wealth or opportunity: a normative judgement.\",\"plain\":\"Fairness in how income, wealth or opportunities are shared out, which is a matter of judgement.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the nine key concepts and the main justification for policies that redistribute income.\",\"ex\":\"Some people judge equal access to schooling to be equitable even if incomes end up unequal; others also want smaller income gaps.\",\"related\":[\"Equality\",\"Gini coefficient\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Normative economics\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as identical to equality, which is a description of outcomes.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"Excess demand\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The amount by which quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a price below equilibrium.\",\"plain\":\"At the current price, more people want to buy than there are goods on offer.\",\"why\":\"Excess demand is the pressure that pushes price up towards equilibrium, and it is the shortage created by a binding price ceiling.\",\"ex\":\"When a popular new trainer is priced below what many fans would pay, queues form and shops sell out.\",\"related\":[\"Excess supply\",\"Market equilibrium\",\"Price ceiling\",\"Price mechanism\"],\"confusion\":\"Called ‘high demand’ rather than a shortage at a stated price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Excess supply\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The amount by which quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a price above equilibrium; also called a surplus.\",\"plain\":\"At the current price, sellers want to sell more than buyers want to buy.\",\"why\":\"It puts downward pressure on price, and it is the surplus that follows a binding price floor.\",\"ex\":\"A clothing shop left with unsold winter coats in spring cuts their price to clear the stock.\",\"related\":[\"Excess demand\",\"Market equilibrium\",\"Price floor\",\"Price mechanism\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with producer surplus, which is a welfare area on the diagram, not a quantity of unsold goods.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Exchange rate\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"The value of one currency expressed in terms of another currency.\",\"plain\":\"Currency value.\",\"why\":\"It links a country’s prices to the rest of the world, affecting trade, inflation and investment.\",\"ex\":\"If 1 euro exchanges for 1.10 US dollars, a product priced at 100 euros costs a US buyer 110 dollars (illustrative rate).\",\"related\":[\"Appreciation\",\"Depreciation\",\"Floating exchange rate\",\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Managed exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Axes drawn without naming which currency is being priced.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Expansionary policy\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"Policy intended to increase aggregate demand, through lower interest rates or a looser budget.\",\"plain\":\"Action by the government or central bank to boost total spending in the economy.\",\"why\":\"It is the standard demand-side response to a deflationary gap and rising cyclical unemployment.\",\"ex\":\"A government that cuts income tax and raises spending on infrastructure during a recession is using expansionary fiscal policy.\",\"related\":[\"Contractionary policy\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Keynesian multiplier\"],\"confusion\":\"Described by its objective rather than its mechanism.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 48\"},{\"term\":\"Expenditure reducing\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"Policies that reduce a current account deficit by lowering total spending in the economy, including spending on imports, through contractionary fiscal or monetary policy.\",\"plain\":\"Cutting overall spending in the economy so that people buy fewer imports.\",\"why\":\"It can narrow a deficit, but at the cost of slower growth and higher unemployment.\",\"ex\":\"A government raises income tax to reduce household spending, which cuts demand for imported goods as well as domestic ones.\",\"related\":[\"Expenditure switching\",\"Contractionary policy\",\"Current account\",\"Fiscal policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with expenditure switching, although expenditure reducing lowers total spending rather than redirecting it towards domestic goods.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 56\"},{\"term\":\"Expenditure switching\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"A policy to correct a current account deficit by moving spending away from imports towards domestic output.\",\"plain\":\"Policies that persuade people to buy domestic goods instead of imports.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the methods for correcting a persistent current account deficit.\",\"ex\":\"A devaluation that makes imports dearer and exports cheaper is an expenditure-switching policy.\",\"related\":[\"Expenditure reducing\",\"Devaluation\",\"Tariff\",\"Marshall-Lerner condition\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with expenditure reducing, which lowers total spending.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 56\"},{\"term\":\"Export promotion\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"A development strategy that encourages firms to produce for world markets, often through openness to trade, a competitive exchange rate and support for export industries.\",\"plain\":\"Growing the economy by selling more to other countries.\",\"why\":\"It is associated with rapid growth in several East Asian economies, although conditions there were specific.\",\"ex\":\"South Korea and Taiwan expanded exports of manufactured goods from the 1960s as part of their growth strategies.\",\"related\":[\"Import substitution\",\"Comparative advantage\",\"Export subsidy\",\"Foreign direct investment\"],\"confusion\":\"Presented as a sure route to growth, although it depends on access to foreign markets and can leave an economy exposed to swings in world demand.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 58\"},{\"term\":\"Export subsidy\",\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"def\":\"A payment by government to domestic producers for each unit they export, allowing them to sell abroad at a lower price.\",\"plain\":\"Government money given to firms so that they can sell their goods more cheaply abroad.\",\"why\":\"It helps exporters but costs taxpayers and can provoke retaliation or complaints of dumping.\",\"ex\":\"The EU once paid export refunds so that surplus farm products could be sold on world markets below EU prices.\",\"related\":[\"Subsidy\",\"Protectionism\",\"Dumping\",\"World Trade Organization\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a production subsidy, which supports domestic output whether it is sold at home or abroad.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Externality\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A cost or benefit of production or consumption falling on a third party not involved in the transaction.\",\"plain\":\"A side effect of buying or making something that falls on people who are not involved.\",\"why\":\"Externalities are the main reason markets produce too much or too little of a good relative to the social optimum.\",\"ex\":\"Noise from a nightclub that keeps nearby residents awake is an externality, because the residents took no part in the transaction.\",\"related\":[\"Negative externality\",\"Positive externality\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined without the third party, or equated with pollution only, when externalities can be positive and can arise from consumption as well as production.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Factors of production\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The resources used to produce goods and services: land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship.\",\"plain\":\"The ingredients an economy uses to make things: land, workers, equipment and the people who organise and take the risk.\",\"why\":\"Firms’ costs are largely payments for the use of factors of production, so changes in factor prices shift supply.\",\"ex\":\"A bakery uses premises (land), bakers (labour), ovens (capital) and an owner who takes the risk of opening (entrepreneurship).\",\"related\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Supply\",\"Circular flow of income\"],\"confusion\":\"Capital described as money rather than as produced means of production.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 32, 34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Financial account\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The part of the balance of payments recording foreign direct investment, portfolio investment, reserve assets and official borrowing.\",\"plain\":\"The part of the balance of payments that records cross-border investment, lending and changes in official reserves.\",\"why\":\"It shows how a current account deficit is financed, through inflows of investment or borrowing.\",\"ex\":\"When a foreign firm builds a factory in Vietnam, the investment is recorded as an inflow in Vietnam’s financial account.\",\"related\":[\"Foreign direct investment\",\"Portfolio investment\",\"Current account\",\"Capital account\",\"Capital flight\"],\"confusion\":\"Merged with the capital account.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 55\"},{\"term\":\"Fiscal policy\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"Government use of taxation and government expenditure to influence aggregate demand and achieve macroeconomic objectives.\",\"plain\":\"The government changing taxes and its own spending to influence the economy.\",\"why\":\"It can target particular sectors and groups and is especially useful in a deep recession, but it faces political pressure and time lags.\",\"ex\":\"A government raises spending on hospitals and cuts income tax to support demand in a downturn.\",\"related\":[\"Monetary policy\",\"Budget deficit\",\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Automatic stabiliser\",\"Crowding out\"],\"confusion\":\"Credited to the central bank or treated as the same as monetary policy, when it is decided by the government through its budget.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Government spending.’) leaves out taxation, although its own note says both instruments are required.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 48\"},{\"term\":\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A system in which the authorities commit to maintaining the currency at a stated value against another currency or basket.\",\"plain\":\"An exchange rate that the authorities hold at a set value by buying and selling their currency.\",\"why\":\"It gives certainty to traders and investors but uses up reserves and limits independent monetary policy.\",\"ex\":\"Hong Kong has kept its dollar within a narrow band against the US dollar for decades.\",\"related\":[\"Floating exchange rate\",\"Managed exchange rate\",\"Devaluation\",\"Revaluation\",\"Central bank\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as 'not changing' rather than as maintained by intervention.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"To defend a peg under downward pressure, the central bank buys its own currency with foreign reserves or raises interest rates to attract capital. Comparing fixed and floating systems is HL only (guide PDF p. 55).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Floating exchange rate\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"An exchange rate system in which the value of a currency is determined by demand for and supply of it in the foreign exchange market, without government intervention.\",\"plain\":\"An exchange rate that moves freely with supply and demand for the currency.\",\"why\":\"It lets the exchange rate absorb shocks and leaves monetary policy free for domestic aims, but it creates uncertainty for traders.\",\"ex\":\"The US dollar, the Japanese yen and the pound sterling float against other currencies.\",\"related\":[\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Managed exchange rate\",\"Appreciation\",\"Depreciation\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to move only in response to trade flows, although capital flows, speculation and interest rate differences often matter more in the short run.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Foreign aid\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"The transfer of money, goods or services from governments or organisations in one country to another, on concessional terms, to support development or relieve emergencies.\",\"plain\":\"Help given to poorer countries, from emergency supplies to long-term development projects.\",\"why\":\"Aid can fill gaps in savings, foreign currency and public services, but its effectiveness depends on how it is used and governed.\",\"ex\":\"Emergency shelter after an earthquake is humanitarian aid, while funding a new irrigation system is development aid.\",\"related\":[\"Official development assistance\",\"Debt relief\",\"Poverty trap\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as one thing, although humanitarian aid meets short-term emergencies while development aid aims to build long-term capacity.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Economists disagree about aid’s effect on growth: some stress its role in health and infrastructure, while others warn of dependency and the risk of corruption.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 59\"},{\"term\":\"Foreign direct investment\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"Investment by a firm in productive capacity in another country, involving a lasting management interest.\",\"plain\":\"A foreign firm setting up, buying or taking control of a business in another country.\",\"why\":\"It can bring capital, technology, skills and jobs to host countries, although profits may flow back abroad.\",\"ex\":\"A Korean electronics firm building and running a factory in Vietnam is making a foreign direct investment.\",\"related\":[\"Portfolio investment\",\"Financial account\",\"GNI\",\"Capital flight\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with portfolio investment, which buys financial assets without a lasting management interest.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 54–55, 59\"},{\"term\":\"Framing\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"A bias in which people respond differently to the same information depending on how it is presented.\",\"plain\":\"The way something is worded changes what people choose.\",\"why\":\"It shows that presentation affects decisions, which firms use in marketing and governments use in public information campaigns.\",\"ex\":\"Meat labelled ‘90% fat-free’ may seem healthier to shoppers than the same meat labelled ‘10% fat’, although the information is identical.\",\"related\":[\"Anchoring\",\"Availability bias\",\"Choice architecture\",\"Behavioural economics\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with giving misleading or false information, when framing changes decisions even though the facts presented are identical.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Free good\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"A good with no opportunity cost, because it is not scarce.\",\"plain\":\"Something so plentiful that using it takes nothing away from anyone else.\",\"why\":\"It marks the boundary of economics: only goods with an opportunity cost need to be allocated.\",\"ex\":\"Air in an open field is usually a free good, but clean air in a polluted city is not, because keeping it clean uses resources.\",\"related\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Public good\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined as any good with a zero price, although a zero-price good such as a state school place still uses scarce resources and so has an opportunity cost.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Free market economy\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"An economic system in which resources are mainly privately owned and the basic economic questions are answered through the interaction of buyers and sellers in markets, guided by prices.\",\"plain\":\"An economy where prices and private decisions, not the government, decide what is produced and who gets it.\",\"why\":\"It is the benchmark against which the case for government intervention is judged throughout Unit 2.\",\"ex\":\"In a market-based system, a rise in the price of electric bicycles signals firms to make more of them without any official instruction.\",\"related\":[\"Price mechanism\",\"Planned economy\",\"Mixed economy\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to mean no government at all, when even market economies rely on the state to define property rights and enforce contracts.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Free rider\",\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"def\":\"A consumer who benefits from a non-excludable good without paying for it.\",\"plain\":\"Someone who enjoys a benefit without paying for it because they cannot be excluded.\",\"why\":\"The free rider problem is why private firms find it hard to charge for public goods.\",\"ex\":\"A resident who refuses to contribute to a street’s shared security patrol still benefits from lower crime on the street.\",\"related\":[\"Public good\",\"Non-excludable\",\"Common pool resource\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Presented as dishonesty rather than as a rational response to non-excludability.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Free trade area\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"A trading bloc with free internal trade in which members keep their own external tariffs.\",\"plain\":\"A group of countries that trade with each other without tariffs but each set their own tariffs on outsiders.\",\"why\":\"It is the shallowest form of trading bloc, allowing gains from trade while members keep control of their trade with others.\",\"ex\":\"The agreement between the USA, Mexico and Canada creates a free trade area in which each member keeps its own tariffs on goods from outside.\",\"related\":[\"Customs union\",\"Trading bloc\",\"Common market\",\"Trade creation\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a customs union.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Frictional unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"Short-term unemployment while workers move between jobs.\",\"plain\":\"People are briefly out of work while they search for, or move to, a new job.\",\"why\":\"It is part of the natural rate, so some unemployment exists even in a healthy economy.\",\"ex\":\"A graduate who spends two months applying for jobs before starting work is frictionally unemployed.\",\"related\":[\"Structural unemployment\",\"Seasonal unemployment\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Cyclical unemployment\"],\"confusion\":\"Counted as a policy failure rather than as a feature of a functioning labour market.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Full employment output\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The level of real output at which the only unemployment remaining is the natural rate.\",\"plain\":\"The output an economy can keep producing when its labour market works normally, with no cyclical unemployment.\",\"why\":\"It is the benchmark against which inflationary and deflationary gaps are measured.\",\"ex\":\"An economy producing at the level shown by its LRAS curve has only frictional, seasonal and structural unemployment left.\",\"related\":[\"LRAS\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Inflationary gap\",\"Business cycle\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as zero unemployment.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide also calls this potential output. Because some unemployment remains, it is not the most the economy could ever produce: output can rise above it for a while, which is what an inflationary gap shows.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Game theory\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The analysis of strategic interaction in which each agent's best action depends on what others do.\",\"plain\":\"A way of analysing decisions where each player’s best move depends on what the others do.\",\"why\":\"It explains why oligopolists may end up in price wars or in tacit agreements.\",\"ex\":\"Two rival airlines each deciding whether to cut fares, knowing the other may respond, can be analysed with a simple payoff matrix.\",\"related\":[\"Oligopoly\",\"Collusion\",\"Interdependence\"],\"confusion\":\"A payoff matrix is read by comparing combined totals, rather than by asking what each firm does best for each possible choice by its rival.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 39\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"In the classic case, each firm’s dominant strategy is to cut price, so both end up worse off than if they had cooperated; this is why the incentive to cheat undermines collusion.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"GDP\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"The total market value of final goods and services produced within a country in a period.\",\"plain\":\"The market value of all the final goods and services produced inside a country’s borders over a period.\",\"why\":\"It is the standard headline measure of the size of an economy and the starting point for measuring growth.\",\"ex\":\"A car assembled in Mexico by a Japanese-owned firm counts in Mexico’s GDP, because the production takes place in Mexico.\",\"related\":[\"GNI\",\"Real GDP\",\"Circular flow of income\",\"Price deflator\",\"Real GDP per capita\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with GNI, which adjusts for net income from abroad.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"GDP measured at current prices is nominal GDP. The guide asks for nominal GDP to be calculated with the expenditure approach, C + I + G + (X − M) (guide PDF p. 41).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 41\"},{\"term\":\"Gender Inequality Index\",\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"def\":\"A composite index of gender-based disadvantage in reproductive health, empowerment and labour market participation, in which higher values indicate greater inequality.\",\"plain\":\"A score showing how far women are disadvantaged compared with men in health, education, politics and work.\",\"why\":\"Gender inequality is a development problem in itself and a barrier to growth.\",\"ex\":\"A country with high maternal mortality and few women in parliament tends to have a high GII value.\",\"related\":[\"Human Development Index\",\"Composite indicator\",\"Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Read like the HDI, where higher is better, when for the GII a higher value means more inequality.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"Gini coefficient\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A summary measure of income inequality derived from the Lorenz curve, ranging from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality).\",\"plain\":\"Inequality measure.\",\"why\":\"It lets you compare inequality across countries or over time with a single number.\",\"ex\":\"South Africa has one of the highest measured Gini coefficients in the world, while the Nordic countries have some of the lowest.\",\"related\":[\"Lorenz curve\",\"Equality\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index\"],\"confusion\":\"Read the wrong way round: a value closer to 1 (or to 100 when shown as an index) means more inequality, not less.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The Gini coefficient is the area between the line of equality and the Lorenz curve, divided by the whole area under the line of equality. Quite different distributions can share the same value, so it summarises inequality rather than describing it.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"GNI\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"The total income earned by a country's residents, wherever earned, equal to GDP plus net property income from abroad.\",\"plain\":\"National income.\",\"why\":\"It shows the income available to a country’s residents, which can differ sharply from what is produced inside its borders.\",\"ex\":\"Ireland’s GDP is well above its GNI because foreign-owned multinationals record large profits there that flow back to their owners abroad.\",\"related\":[\"GDP\",\"Foreign direct investment\",\"Current account\",\"Real GDP per capita\"],\"confusion\":\"Used interchangeably with GDP where the two diverge materially.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Strictly, GNI adds net primary income from abroad: wages, profits, interest and dividends. Workers’ remittances are recorded as current transfers, so they raise households’ disposable income but are not part of GNI.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 41\"},{\"term\":\"Growth in production possibilities\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"An increase in the maximum output an economy can produce, shown as an outward shift of the PPC, resulting from more or better factors of production or improved technology.\",\"plain\":\"The economy becomes able to produce more than it could before.\",\"why\":\"It is the long-run source of higher living standards and corresponds to growth in potential output and shifts of LRAS in Unit 3.\",\"ex\":\"Wider access to training that raises workers’ skills can shift a country’s PPC outward over time.\",\"related\":[\"Actual growth\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Factors of production\",\"Economic growth\",\"LRAS\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to mean the economy is producing more, when capacity can grow while actual output stays inside the curve.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Happy Planet Index\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"A composite measure that relates a country’s average reported well-being and life expectancy to its ecological footprint, so that it rewards long, satisfied lives achieved with low resource use.\",\"plain\":\"A score for how well a country turns its use of the planet’s resources into long and happy lives.\",\"why\":\"It challenges the assumption that higher output means higher well-being.\",\"ex\":\"A country with modest income, high life satisfaction and a small ecological footprint can rank above a much richer country with a very large footprint.\",\"related\":[\"Human Development Index\",\"Composite indicator\",\"Sustainable development\",\"GDP\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a measure of happiness alone, ignoring that environmental cost is built into the score.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 42, 57\"},{\"term\":\"Human capital\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"The knowledge, skills, health and experience of workers, which determine their productivity.\",\"plain\":\"The skills, education and health that make people more productive.\",\"why\":\"Low human capital is both a cause of poverty and a barrier to growth, so investing in it is central to many development strategies.\",\"ex\":\"A programme that expands access to primary schooling and vaccination builds a country’s human capital.\",\"related\":[\"Structural unemployment\",\"Interventionist supply-side policies\",\"Economic development\",\"Poverty trap\",\"LRAS\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as physical capital that people use, such as tools, rather than as the productive qualities of people themselves.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Because the benefits of education and health spread beyond the individual, markets may under-provide them, which links human capital to merit goods and positive externalities.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 45, 58\"},{\"term\":\"Human Development Index\",\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"def\":\"A composite index of development based on health, education and income indicators.\",\"plain\":\"Development measure.\",\"why\":\"It is the best-known alternative to income per person for comparing countries’ development.\",\"ex\":\"Two countries with similar GNI per capita can have very different HDI values if one has much better health and education outcomes.\",\"related\":[\"Composite indicator\",\"Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index\",\"Gender Inequality Index\",\"Real GDP per capita\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as measuring happiness or equality.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The HDI is published by the United Nations Development Programme. Its income dimension uses GNI per capita at PPP, and its education dimension combines expected and mean years of schooling.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\",\"alias\":[\"HDI\"]},{\"term\":\"Import substitution\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"A development strategy of producing domestically what was previously imported, usually behind protection.\",\"plain\":\"A strategy of protecting domestic industries so that they can make goods the country used to import.\",\"why\":\"It was a leading development strategy in the mid-twentieth century, and its mixed record still informs debates about trade and development.\",\"ex\":\"Several Latin American countries used high tariffs in the mid-twentieth century to build domestic car and consumer goods industries.\",\"related\":[\"Export promotion\",\"Infant industry\",\"Protectionism\",\"Tariff\"],\"confusion\":\"Presented as always failing, or always succeeding, without conditions.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 58\"},{\"term\":\"Income effect\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"The part of the change in quantity demanded after a price change that results from the change in consumers’ real income (purchasing power).\",\"plain\":\"When a price falls, your money goes further, so you can afford to buy more.\",\"why\":\"Together with the substitution effect, it explains the downward slope of the demand curve at HL.\",\"ex\":\"If bus fares fall, a regular commuter has more money left each month and may use some of it for extra trips.\",\"related\":[\"Substitution effect\",\"Law of demand\",\"Normal good\",\"Inferior good\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a change in money income, which shifts the demand curve, whereas the income effect is part of a movement along it caused by the price change.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"For a normal good the income effect reinforces the substitution effect; for an inferior good it works in the opposite direction.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A measure of the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in consumer income: %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY.\",\"plain\":\"How income affects demand.\",\"why\":\"It shows which industries are likely to grow as incomes rise, which matters for firms’ planning and, at HL, for changes in the structure of the economy.\",\"ex\":\"As incomes rise in a growing economy, spending on restaurant meals and travel tends to rise faster than spending on staple foods.\",\"related\":[\"Normal good\",\"Inferior good\",\"Engel curve\",\"Percentage change\"],\"confusion\":\"The sign is reported but not used to classify the good.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"At HL, YED helps explain why the share of agriculture in output and employment tends to fall as incomes rise, while the share of services grows (guide PDF p. 34).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Increasing opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The situation in which each additional unit of one good requires giving up a larger amount of another, because resources are not equally suited to producing both; it gives a PPC that is bowed outward (concave to the origin).\",\"plain\":\"The more you make of one thing, the more of the other you have to give up for each extra unit.\",\"why\":\"It explains the usual bowed shape of the PPC and why specialising completely in one good becomes expensive.\",\"ex\":\"Turning farmland into housing uses the least fertile fields first, so early homes cost little food, but later homes use the best land and cost much more food.\",\"related\":[\"Constant opportunity cost\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Opportunity cost\",\"Factors of production\"],\"confusion\":\"Explained by saying resources ‘run out’, rather than by resources being specialised and less suited to the new use.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Indirect tax\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"A tax on expenditure on goods and services, levied on producers who may pass it on to consumers through higher prices.\",\"plain\":\"A tax on things you buy.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the main tools governments use to raise revenue and discourage consumption, and elasticities decide who bears it.\",\"ex\":\"An excise tax on cigarettes raises their price, and because demand is price inelastic, much of the burden tends to fall on smokers.\",\"related\":[\"Pigouvian tax\",\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"Subsidy\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"The burden is assumed to fall on whoever hands the tax to the government, or to be split evenly, when the side of the market with the more inelastic curve bears more of it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"A specific tax adds a fixed amount per unit and shifts supply up in parallel, while an ad valorem tax is a percentage of price and pivots the supply curve. Calculating the effects of indirect taxes on markets and stakeholders is HL only (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Inequality-adjusted Human Development Index\",\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"def\":\"The Human Development Index adjusted for inequality within each of its dimensions, so that it falls further below the HDI the more unequal the distribution.\",\"plain\":\"The HDI with a deduction for how unevenly health, education and income are shared.\",\"why\":\"It shows how much human development is lost to inequality, which the average HDI hides.\",\"ex\":\"Two countries with the same HDI can have very different IHDI values if income and schooling are far more unequal in one of them.\",\"related\":[\"Human Development Index\",\"Gini coefficient\",\"Composite indicator\",\"Gender Inequality Index\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a separate measure unrelated to the HDI, when it is the HDI adjusted downwards for inequality.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"Infant industry\",\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"def\":\"A new domestic industry argued to need temporary protection until it can achieve competitive scale.\",\"plain\":\"A young domestic industry that, it is argued, needs temporary protection until it grows large enough to compete.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the stronger economic arguments for protection, especially for developing economies.\",\"ex\":\"A government protects a new solar panel industry for a limited period while firms gain experience and scale.\",\"related\":[\"Protectionism\",\"Economies of scale\",\"Import substitution\",\"Tariff\"],\"confusion\":\"The argument is accepted without a sunset condition.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide calls these sunrise industries. The argument depends on the industry maturing and the protection being removed; if firms lobby successfully to keep it, the industry may stay inefficient.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Inferior good\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A good for which demand falls as income rises, so income elasticity of demand is negative.\",\"plain\":\"Something people buy less of when their income rises, because they switch to something they prefer.\",\"why\":\"It explains why a rise in income can reduce demand for some products, which matters for firms selling budget goods.\",\"ex\":\"As incomes rise, some commuters stop taking the bus and buy a car, so bus travel can behave as an inferior good.\",\"related\":[\"Normal good\",\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"Income effect\",\"Shift of the demand curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined by low quality rather than by the sign of YED.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Inflation\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A sustained increase in the general price level of an economy over time.\",\"plain\":\"The general level of prices keeps rising over time.\",\"why\":\"High or unstable inflation erodes purchasing power, creates uncertainty and redistributes income, for example between savers and borrowers.\",\"ex\":\"When a country’s CPI rises steadily year after year, a fixed sum of money buys less each year.\",\"related\":[\"Deflation\",\"Disinflation\",\"Consumer price index\",\"Demand-pull inflation\",\"Cost-push inflation\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated as 'prices going up' without 'general' or 'sustained'.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Prices going up.’) drops ‘general’ and ‘sustained’, which the inventory’s own note and confusion say are essential.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Inflation targeting\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"A monetary policy framework in which the central bank announces a numerical target for inflation and adjusts policy to keep inflation close to it.\",\"plain\":\"The central bank publicly commits to keeping inflation near a stated number.\",\"why\":\"A credible target can anchor expectations, so that wages and prices rise more steadily.\",\"ex\":\"The Bank of England sets interest rates to meet an inflation target set by the UK government.\",\"related\":[\"Central bank\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Consumer price index\",\"Inflation\",\"Disinflation\"],\"confusion\":\"Read as a commitment to zero inflation, although most targets aim for low, positive inflation.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Inflationary gap\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The excess of equilibrium real output above the full employment level of output.\",\"plain\":\"The economy is producing more than it can sustain, so pressure builds on wages and prices.\",\"why\":\"It signals an overheating economy and is the case for contractionary policy.\",\"ex\":\"A spending boom pushes real GDP above potential, and firms compete for scarce workers by bidding up wages.\",\"related\":[\"Deflationary gap\",\"Demand-pull inflation\",\"Contractionary policy\",\"Full employment output\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a high price level, which is a level rather than a gap.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Informal economy\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"Economic activity that is not registered, taxed or captured in official statistics.\",\"plain\":\"Work and trade that are not registered with, or taxed by, the government.\",\"why\":\"A large informal economy limits tax revenue, workers’ protections and access to credit, and makes GDP data less reliable.\",\"ex\":\"A street vendor who sells food for cash without a licence works in the informal economy.\",\"related\":[\"GDP\",\"Property rights\",\"Microfinance\",\"Unemployment rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Equated with illegal activity.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 58\"},{\"term\":\"Injections\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"Additions to the circular flow that do not come from domestic households’ spending out of income: investment, government spending and export revenue.\",\"plain\":\"New spending that enters the loop from outside households’ own purchases.\",\"why\":\"A rise in injections relative to leakages tends to increase national income and output.\",\"ex\":\"When foreign tourists spend money in a country’s hotels, that export revenue is an injection into the country’s circular flow.\",\"related\":[\"Leakages\",\"Circular flow of income\",\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Aggregate demand\"],\"confusion\":\"Transfer payments are counted as government spending on output, although they redistribute income rather than buy goods and services.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"alias\":[\"Injection\"]},{\"term\":\"Interdependence\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The condition in which the decisions of one economic agent generate consequences for others.\",\"plain\":\"What one person, firm or country decides affects others.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the nine key concepts and explains why policies often have unintended consequences for groups they were not aimed at.\",\"ex\":\"When a government raises the tax on fuel, delivery firms, their customers and oil exporters abroad are all affected.\",\"related\":[\"Circular flow of income\",\"Externality\",\"Oligopoly\",\"Game theory\"],\"confusion\":\"Used as a synonym for international trade, when it covers any link between decision-makers, including rival firms in an oligopoly and households and firms in the circular flow.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 19, 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"International poverty line\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"An income threshold per person per day, set by the World Bank and measured at purchasing power parity, used to count people in extreme poverty across countries.\",\"plain\":\"A daily income level, adjusted for price differences between countries, below which a person is counted as extremely poor.\",\"why\":\"It allows absolute poverty to be compared across countries and over time.\",\"ex\":\"The World Bank uses the line to estimate how many people worldwide live in extreme poverty and to track progress towards ending it.\",\"related\":[\"Absolute poverty\",\"PPP\",\"Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"Relative poverty\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a fixed sum of money, although the World Bank revises the line as new price data become available.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"Interventionist supply-side policies\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"Policies in which the government directly invests in, or supports, the factors that raise productive capacity, such as education, health care, research and infrastructure.\",\"plain\":\"Government spending that aims to make the economy more productive over time.\",\"why\":\"They can support sectors important for growth, but they cost money and take time to pay off.\",\"ex\":\"A government that funds apprenticeships and builds broadband networks in rural areas is using interventionist supply-side policy.\",\"related\":[\"Market-based supply-side policies\",\"Human capital\",\"Capital expenditure\",\"Supply-side policy\",\"LRAS\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with expansionary fiscal policy: the spending does raise AD, but the aim is to shift LRAS.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 50\"},{\"term\":\"Investment\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"Spending by firms on capital goods, such as machinery, buildings and technology, that will be used to produce other goods and services.\",\"plain\":\"Firms buying things that help them produce more in the future.\",\"why\":\"It is the most volatile component of AD and, over time, it adds to productive capacity.\",\"ex\":\"A bakery buying a new oven is investment; a person buying shares in the bakery is not.\",\"related\":[\"Aggregate demand\",\"Injections\",\"Crowding out\",\"Real interest rate\",\"Capital expenditure\"],\"confusion\":\"Buying shares or depositing money in a bank is called investment, when in national income terms these are saving or financial transactions.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"J-curve\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The path by which a current account balance worsens before improving after a depreciation, as volumes adjust more slowly than prices.\",\"plain\":\"After a currency falls, the trade balance often gets worse before it gets better.\",\"why\":\"It explains why a depreciation may not improve the current account for some time.\",\"ex\":\"Firms that signed import contracts before a depreciation must pay more in domestic currency for the same goods, so the deficit widens at first.\",\"related\":[\"Marshall-Lerner condition\",\"Depreciation\",\"Current account\",\"Expenditure switching\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn without reference to the Marshall-Lerner condition.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 56\"},{\"term\":\"Joint supply\",\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"def\":\"The production of two or more goods from the same process or resource, so that producing more of one also produces more of the other.\",\"plain\":\"Two products that come out of the same process, such as beef and leather.\",\"why\":\"A price change for one good shifts the supply of the other, linking markets that might seem separate.\",\"ex\":\"If beef prices rise and farmers raise more cattle, the supply of leather hides also tends to increase.\",\"related\":[\"Competitive supply\",\"Supply\",\"Complement\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with competitive supply: in joint supply a rise in one good’s price increases the supply of the other rather than reducing it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Keynesian AS curve\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"An aggregate supply curve with three ranges: horizontal when there is a large amount of spare capacity, upward-sloping as the economy approaches full capacity, and vertical at full employment output.\",\"plain\":\"A supply curve for the whole economy that is flat when many resources are idle and becomes vertical once they are all in use.\",\"why\":\"It explains why extra demand in a deep recession can raise output without raising prices.\",\"ex\":\"In a deep recession with many idle factories, a rise in government spending raises real GDP while the price level barely changes.\",\"related\":[\"LRAS\",\"SRAS\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Full employment output\"],\"confusion\":\"The Keynesian curve and the monetarist/new classical LRAS are mixed in one answer without saying which model is being used.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Keynesians argue that wages are slow to fall, so a deflationary gap can persist; this is why the horizontal range matters for the case for fiscal policy.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"The ratio of the change in national income to the initial change in an injection.\",\"plain\":\"An initial rise in spending raises national income by more than itself, because one person’s spending becomes another person’s income.\",\"why\":\"It shows why a change in government spending, investment or exports can have a larger final effect on GDP.\",\"ex\":\"If the multiplier is 2, a rise in government spending of 10 billion raises real GDP by 20 billion, other things equal (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Marginal propensity to consume\",\"Leakages\",\"Injections\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Crowding out\"],\"confusion\":\"Applied to the level of GDP instead of to the change in the injection.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC), or 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM). The larger the leakages, the smaller the multiplier (guide PDF pp. 48–49).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 48–49\"},{\"term\":\"Labour force\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"Those in employment plus those unemployed and actively seeking work.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone who is either in work or actively looking for work.\",\"why\":\"It is the denominator of the unemployment rate, so who is counted in it changes the rate.\",\"ex\":\"A full-time student who is not looking for work is outside the labour force, while a laid-off engineer applying for jobs is inside it.\",\"related\":[\"Unemployment rate\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Cyclical unemployment\"],\"confusion\":\"Taken to include discouraged workers, who are outside it.\",\"ib\":\"Quantitative skill\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The guide does not name the labour force, but its unemployment-rate calculation needs it (guide PDF p. 44). Discouraged workers who stop searching leave the labour force, so the measured rate can fall even when job prospects have not improved.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Law of demand\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"As the price of a good rises, ceteris paribus, the quantity demanded of that good falls.\",\"plain\":\"When a good’s price rises and nothing else changes, people buy less of it.\",\"why\":\"It explains the downward slope of the demand curve, which is the starting point for predicting responses to price changes.\",\"ex\":\"If a café raises the price of its coffee while other prices and customers’ incomes stay the same, it will usually sell fewer cups.\",\"related\":[\"Demand\",\"Ceteris paribus\",\"Movement along the demand curve\",\"Income effect\",\"Substitution effect\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated without ceteris paribus, so any case where price and quantity rose together because demand shifted appears to refute it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"At HL, the guide lists the income and substitution effects and the law of diminishing marginal utility as the assumptions underlying the law of demand (guide PDF p. 31).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Higher price, less demand.’, uses ‘demand’ where the inventory’s own note requires ‘quantity demanded’.\"},{\"term\":\"Law of diminishing marginal returns\",\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"def\":\"As successive units of a variable factor are added to a fixed factor, the marginal product eventually falls.\",\"plain\":\"Adding more workers to a fixed amount of equipment eventually adds less and less extra output.\",\"why\":\"It explains why marginal cost rises in the short run, which gives the supply curve its upward slope at HL.\",\"ex\":\"A small kitchen with two ovens gains a lot from a second cook but much less from a sixth, who mostly waits for oven space.\",\"related\":[\"Law of supply\",\"Marginal cost\",\"Factors of production\",\"Supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Applied to the long run, where all factors can vary and the relevant idea is economies or diseconomies of scale.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Law of diminishing marginal utility\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"As consumption of a good increases, the additional satisfaction from each further unit eventually falls.\",\"plain\":\"Each extra unit of something tends to add less satisfaction than the one before.\",\"why\":\"At HL it helps explain why consumers buy more only at a lower price, giving the demand curve its downward slope.\",\"ex\":\"A second slice of pizza usually adds less satisfaction than the first, so a hungry customer would pay less for it.\",\"related\":[\"Utility\",\"Law of demand\",\"Income effect\",\"Substitution effect\"],\"confusion\":\"Used to explain a shift of the demand curve rather than its downward slope.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Law of supply\",\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"def\":\"As the price of a good rises, ceteris paribus, the quantity supplied of that good rises.\",\"plain\":\"When a good’s price goes up and nothing else changes, firms offer more of it for sale.\",\"why\":\"It explains the upward slope of the supply curve used in every market diagram.\",\"ex\":\"A rise in the market price of wheat, with costs unchanged, makes it worthwhile for farmers to sell more wheat.\",\"related\":[\"Supply\",\"Ceteris paribus\",\"Law of diminishing marginal returns\",\"Price elasticity of supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated as ‘higher price, more supply’, which confuses a movement along the supply curve with a shift of the whole curve.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"At HL, the guide explains the upward slope through the law of diminishing marginal returns and increasing marginal costs: extra output costs more to produce, so firms need a higher price to supply it (guide PDF p. 32).\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Leakages\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"Withdrawals of income from the circular flow that are not passed on as spending on domestic output: saving, taxation and spending on imports.\",\"plain\":\"Money that drops out of the spending loop instead of being spent on things produced at home.\",\"why\":\"Changes in leakages relative to injections help explain why national income rises or falls.\",\"ex\":\"If households decide to save a larger share of their income, less is spent at domestic firms in the next round of the flow.\",\"related\":[\"Injections\",\"Circular flow of income\",\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Interdependence\"],\"confusion\":\"Saving is treated as lost from the economy for good, when banks may lend it on to firms as investment, which is an injection.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"In Unit 3, the share of each extra unit of income that leaks out determines the size of the Keynesian multiplier.\",\"new\":true,\"alias\":[\"Leakage\"]},{\"term\":\"Lorenz curve\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A plot of the cumulative share of income against the cumulative share of population, ranked from poorest.\",\"plain\":\"A graph showing what share of total income goes to the poorest 10%, 20% and so on of the population.\",\"why\":\"It turns a whole income distribution into one picture: the further it bows away from the diagonal, the greater the inequality.\",\"ex\":\"If the poorest fifth of households receives only a small fraction of total income, the curve starts well below the 45° line.\",\"related\":[\"Gini coefficient\",\"Equality\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Transfer payment\"],\"confusion\":\"Axes reversed, or the 45° line omitted.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Constructing a Lorenz curve from income quintile data is HL only (guide PDF p. 45).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"LRAS\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The long-run aggregate supply curve in the monetarist/new classical model, vertical at potential output.\",\"plain\":\"The economy’s productive capacity, which in the long run does not depend on the price level.\",\"why\":\"Only an increase in LRAS raises the output an economy can sustain, which is why supply-side policy targets it.\",\"ex\":\"A rise in the size and skills of the workforce shifts LRAS to the right.\",\"related\":[\"SRAS\",\"Keynesian AS curve\",\"Full employment output\",\"Supply-side policy\",\"Economic growth\"],\"confusion\":\"Shifted for a change in demand.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"In the monetarist/new classical view, wages and other input prices eventually adjust fully to the price level, so long-run output depends only on the quantity and quality of resources. That is why the curve is vertical.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 43\"},{\"term\":\"Managed exchange rate\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"An exchange rate that is mainly determined by market forces but in which the central bank intervenes to influence its value or limit its movements.\",\"plain\":\"A mostly floating exchange rate that the central bank steers from time to time.\",\"why\":\"Many countries manage their exchange rates to some degree, so it describes much real-world policy better than the pure fixed or floating models.\",\"ex\":\"Singapore’s central bank manages the value of its currency against a basket of trading partners’ currencies as its main monetary policy tool.\",\"related\":[\"Floating exchange rate\",\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Central bank\",\"Exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a fixed rate, although a managed rate can change every day within limits the authorities tolerate.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide links managed rates to overvalued and undervalued currencies (guide PDF p. 54): a central bank that holds its currency below its market level makes exports cheaper, a practice trading partners may criticise.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 54–55\"},{\"term\":\"Marginal benefit\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The additional benefit gained from consuming one more unit of a good; where there are no externalities it is shown by the demand curve.\",\"plain\":\"How much extra benefit you get from one more unit.\",\"why\":\"The guide states allocative efficiency at the competitive equilibrium as marginal benefit equals marginal cost.\",\"ex\":\"The most a commuter would pay for one more gym session each week reflects the marginal benefit of that session.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal cost\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Law of diminishing marginal utility\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with total benefit, although marginal benefit can be falling while total benefit is still rising.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Marginal cost\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The addition to total cost from producing one more unit.\",\"plain\":\"The extra cost of making one more unit.\",\"why\":\"Comparing marginal cost with marginal benefit or marginal revenue is how economists find the efficient or profit-maximising output.\",\"ex\":\"If a bakery must pay overtime to bake one more batch of bread, the marginal cost of that batch is higher than for earlier batches.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal revenue\",\"Marginal benefit\",\"Law of diminishing marginal returns\",\"Profit maximisation\",\"Allocative efficiency\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with average cost, although marginal cost is the cost of the last unit only and pulls average cost towards it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 33, 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"SL students meet marginal cost in the condition MB = MC at the competitive equilibrium; HL students also calculate it from data and use MC = MR to find profit-maximising output (guide PDF pp. 33, 38).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Marginal propensity to consume\",\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"def\":\"The fraction of an increase in income that is spent on consumption of domestic goods and services: MPC = ΔC ÷ ΔY.\",\"plain\":\"Out of each extra unit of income, the share that people spend on the country’s own output.\",\"why\":\"The higher the MPC, the larger the multiplier effect of a change in spending.\",\"ex\":\"If households spend 80 of every extra 100 of income on domestic output, the MPC is 0.8 and the multiplier is 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8) = 5 (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Keynesian multiplier\",\"Leakages\",\"Injections\",\"Aggregate demand\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the average propensity to consume, which is total consumption divided by total income rather than the share of extra income.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"The guide does not state it, but its two formulae assume MPC + MPS + MPT + MPM = 1, so 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) and 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM) give the same multiplier (guide PDF pp. 48–49).\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 48–49\"},{\"term\":\"Marginal revenue\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The addition to total revenue from selling one more unit.\",\"plain\":\"The extra money a firm receives from selling one more unit.\",\"why\":\"Profit is maximised at the output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost.\",\"ex\":\"A firm that must lower its price to sell one extra unit gains the new price on that unit but loses revenue on the units it could have sold at the higher price, so its MR is below price.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal cost\",\"Total revenue\",\"Profit maximisation\",\"Price maker\",\"Price taker\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed equal to price for a price maker, where it is not.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Marginal social benefit\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The total benefit to society of one more unit, comprising private benefit plus any external benefit.\",\"plain\":\"The total benefit to everyone, the buyer and other people, from one more unit.\",\"why\":\"Comparing MSB with MSC identifies the socially optimum output.\",\"ex\":\"One more student vaccinated benefits that student and lowers the risk of infection for classmates, so MSB is greater than the private benefit.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal social cost\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Positive externality\",\"Marginal benefit\",\"Socially optimum output\"],\"confusion\":\"Set equal to marginal private benefit even when a consumption externality is present, so the gap between the two curves disappears from the diagram.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Marginal social cost\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The total cost to society of one more unit, comprising private cost plus any external cost.\",\"plain\":\"The total cost to everyone, the producer and other people, of one more unit.\",\"why\":\"Where MSC differs from private cost, the market price sends the wrong signal about the resources used.\",\"ex\":\"A lorry trip costs the haulier fuel and wages, but congestion and exhaust fumes add costs for others, so MSC exceeds private cost.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Negative externality\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Pigouvian tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Shifted for a consumption externality, which moves benefit instead.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Marginal tax rate\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"The rate of tax paid on an additional unit of income.\",\"plain\":\"The share of your next unit of income that goes in tax.\",\"why\":\"It shapes incentives to work and earn more, which matters for supply-side arguments about tax cuts.\",\"ex\":\"Someone whose income rises into a higher tax band pays the higher rate only on the income above the threshold, not on all of it.\",\"related\":[\"Average tax rate\",\"Progressive tax\",\"Proportional tax\",\"Market-based supply-side policies\"],\"confusion\":\"Believed to apply to the whole of a person’s income once a threshold is crossed, when it applies only to income above that threshold.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Market equilibrium\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied, so there is no tendency for price to change.\",\"plain\":\"Where the lines cross.\",\"why\":\"It is the reference point for analysing every shock and policy: economists compare the new equilibrium with the old one.\",\"ex\":\"If buyers want exactly as many concert tickets at the going price as the promoter wants to sell, the market for those tickets is in equilibrium.\",\"related\":[\"Excess demand\",\"Excess supply\",\"Price mechanism\",\"Demand\",\"Supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be fair or desirable, when equilibrium means only that there is no pressure for price to change; it can be inefficient when there are externalities and inequitable in its distribution.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Market failure\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A situation in which the free market fails to allocate resources efficiently, so marginal social benefit does not equal marginal social cost at the market outcome.\",\"plain\":\"When a free market produces too much or too little of something compared with what is best for society as a whole.\",\"why\":\"It is the main economic case for government intervention in Unit 2.\",\"ex\":\"Open-access fishing that leads to the collapse of a fish stock is an example of market failure.\",\"related\":[\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Externality\",\"Public good\",\"Asymmetric information\",\"Market power\"],\"confusion\":\"Used for any outcome the writer dislikes, including inequitable ones, which is a separate failure.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 35–39\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The guide treats externalities, common pool resources, public goods and, at HL, asymmetric information and market power as market failures; inequity is handled separately in 2.12.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘When markets go wrong.’, invites the misuse the glossary itself warns against (any disliked outcome); the replacement ties it to the efficiency condition in the inventory’s note.\"},{\"term\":\"Market power\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The ability of a firm to influence the price of its product, so that it can raise price without losing all its sales, rather than having to accept the market price.\",\"plain\":\"How much control a firm has over the price it charges.\",\"why\":\"Market power lets firms restrict output and set price above marginal cost, which the guide treats as a market failure at HL.\",\"ex\":\"A firm that owns the only cinema in a remote town can raise ticket prices without losing all its customers.\",\"related\":[\"Price maker\",\"Price taker\",\"Monopoly\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Concentration ratio\"],\"confusion\":\"Equated with a firm’s size, when a large firm in a highly competitive market may have little power over its price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Market-based supply-side policies\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"Policies that aim to raise productive capacity by increasing competition and letting markets, especially labour markets, work more freely, with a smaller role for government.\",\"plain\":\"Policies that try to grow the economy by letting markets work more freely.\",\"why\":\"Some of them, such as deregulation, place little direct burden on the government budget, although tax cuts reduce revenue; they may also widen inequality.\",\"ex\":\"Deregulating an industry, privatising state-owned firms and cutting income tax rates to strengthen work incentives are market-based supply-side policies.\",\"related\":[\"Interventionist supply-side policies\",\"Deregulation\",\"Privatisation\",\"Supply-side policy\",\"Minimum wage\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to shift only LRAS, although measures such as tax cuts also raise AD in the short run.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 49–50\"},{\"term\":\"Marshall-Lerner condition\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"The condition that the sum of the absolute values of the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports must exceed one for a depreciation to improve the trade balance.\",\"plain\":\"When depreciation works.\",\"why\":\"It sets out when a depreciation can improve a country’s trade balance.\",\"ex\":\"If demand for a country’s exports and imports is very price inelastic, a depreciation may worsen its trade balance, because volumes barely change while import prices rise.\",\"related\":[\"J-curve\",\"Depreciation\",\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Current account\",\"Expenditure switching\"],\"confusion\":\"Quoted without 'absolute values' or without 'sum'.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 56\"},{\"term\":\"Merit good\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A good that is under-consumed relative to the social optimum, typically because of positive externalities or imperfect information.\",\"plain\":\"A good that people buy less of than is best for society, often because they underrate its benefits.\",\"why\":\"It justifies subsidies, direct provision and information campaigns for goods such as education and preventive health care.\",\"ex\":\"Many people under-use preventive health check-ups because the benefits come later and are hard to judge, so check-ups are often treated as a merit good.\",\"related\":[\"Demerit good\",\"Positive externality\",\"Subsidy\",\"Asymmetric information\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined as ‘good for you’, or confused with a public good because the state often provides it, although most merit goods are rivalrous and excludable.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The label involves a value judgement about what people ought to consume, so some economists question it where no externality or information problem is present.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Something good for you.’, is the misconception recorded in the glossary’s own confusion field; the inventory’s note says the definition is about under-consumption relative to the optimum.\"},{\"term\":\"Microfinance\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"The provision of small loans, savings accounts and other financial services to people on low incomes who lack access to conventional banks.\",\"plain\":\"Small loans and banking services for poor people who cannot use ordinary banks.\",\"why\":\"It can help people start businesses and cope with shocks, although evidence on its effect on poverty is mixed.\",\"ex\":\"The Grameen Bank in Bangladesh became widely known for lending small sums to poor women without requiring collateral.\",\"related\":[\"Poverty trap\",\"Informal economy\",\"Property rights\",\"Human capital\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to lift borrowers out of poverty reliably, although careful studies generally find modest average effects on income.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 59\"},{\"term\":\"Minimum lending rate\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The interest rate at which the central bank lends to commercial banks, which influences the rates banks charge their own customers; also called the base rate, discount rate or refinancing rate.\",\"plain\":\"The central bank’s own interest rate, which pulls other interest rates in the economy up or down.\",\"why\":\"Changes in this rate are the most visible monetary policy decisions and the first step in the transmission mechanism.\",\"ex\":\"When a central bank raises its policy rate, banks tend to raise their mortgage and loan rates soon afterwards.\",\"related\":[\"Central bank\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Real interest rate\",\"Open market operations\",\"Contractionary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be the rate every borrower pays, when commercial banks set their own rates above it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide lists changes in this rate among the HL tools of monetary policy (guide PDF p. 47); SL students still analyse the effects of the central bank raising or lowering interest rates.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Minimum reserve requirement\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The minimum fraction of customer deposits that commercial banks must hold as reserves, set by the central bank.\",\"plain\":\"A rule that banks must keep a certain share of their deposits aside rather than lend it.\",\"why\":\"Raising it limits how much banks can lend, and lowering it frees them to lend more.\",\"ex\":\"If a central bank raises the required reserve ratio, banks have less to lend, which tends to slow the growth of credit.\",\"related\":[\"Money creation\",\"Central bank\",\"Open market operations\",\"Monetary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be every central bank’s main tool, although many rely mainly on interest rates and some have set the requirement to zero.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"The People’s Bank of China adjusts its reserve requirement ratio as an active policy tool, whereas the US Federal Reserve reduced its requirement to zero in 2020.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Minimum wage\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"A legally enforced price floor in the labour market.\",\"plain\":\"The lowest hourly wage that employers are legally allowed to pay.\",\"why\":\"It aims to reduce poverty among low-paid workers but may cause unemployment if it is set well above the market wage.\",\"ex\":\"Many countries, including the UK, set a legal minimum hourly wage that employers must pay.\",\"related\":[\"Price floor\",\"Relative poverty\",\"Structural unemployment\",\"Market-based supply-side policies\"],\"confusion\":\"Analysed without stating whether it binds above the market wage.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide places the minimum wage in several settings: unemployment (3.3), policies to reduce inequality (3.4), a rule that market-based supply-side policies might abolish (3.7), and redistribution strategies for development (4.10, guide PDF p. 58). Evidence on its employment effects is mixed and depends on how high it is set relative to local wages.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 44, 46, 49\"},{\"term\":\"Mixed economy\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"An economic system in which both markets and the government allocate resources, with private and public ownership existing side by side.\",\"plain\":\"An economy where most things are left to markets but the government also provides some goods, taxes and regulates.\",\"why\":\"Almost all real economies are mixed, so much IB evaluation concerns the right balance between market and government.\",\"ex\":\"An economy in which food is sold by private shops while roads, defence and most schooling are provided by the state is a mixed economy.\",\"related\":[\"Free market economy\",\"Planned economy\",\"Market failure\",\"Public good\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as a fixed midpoint, when economies differ widely in how much they rely on the state and the balance shifts over time.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Monetary policy\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"Central bank actions affecting interest rates and the money supply in order to influence aggregate demand and achieve macroeconomic objectives.\",\"plain\":\"Interest rate policy.\",\"why\":\"It is the main tool most economies use to keep inflation low and stable.\",\"ex\":\"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate to encourage borrowing and spending during a downturn.\",\"related\":[\"Fiscal policy\",\"Central bank\",\"Inflation targeting\",\"Real interest rate\",\"Quantitative easing\"],\"confusion\":\"The central bank is said to set all interest rates directly, when it sets a policy rate that influences other rates through the banking system.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Monetary policy can be changed quickly and in small steps, but its effects on spending can take many months to appear. It loses power when rates are already near zero or when confidence is very weak.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Monetary union\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"An arrangement in which members share a single currency and a single monetary policy.\",\"plain\":\"A group of countries that share one currency and one central bank.\",\"why\":\"It removes exchange-rate risk and conversion costs between members but gives up the ability to set interest rates or devalue independently.\",\"ex\":\"Countries in the euro area share the euro, and their policy interest rate is set by the European Central Bank.\",\"related\":[\"Common market\",\"Customs union\",\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Trading bloc\"],\"confusion\":\"Described without the loss of independent monetary policy.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Evaluating the advantages and disadvantages of monetary union is HL only (guide PDF p. 53). A central concern is that one interest rate may not suit members whose economies are at different points in the business cycle.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Money creation\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The process by which commercial banks create new money, in the form of deposits, when they make loans.\",\"plain\":\"When a bank lends, it credits the borrower’s account, creating money that did not exist before.\",\"why\":\"It explains why the money supply depends on the lending decisions of commercial banks, not only on the central bank.\",\"ex\":\"A bank approves a loan to a small business and adds the amount to the firm’s deposit account, increasing the money supply.\",\"related\":[\"Minimum reserve requirement\",\"Central bank\",\"Open market operations\",\"Monetary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Believed to be done only by the central bank printing notes, when most money consists of bank deposits created through lending.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"In the textbook model, the amount banks can create is limited by a reserve ratio; in practice lending also depends on the demand for loans, banks’ capital and the central bank’s interest rate.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Monopolistic competition\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A market with many firms, free entry and differentiated products, giving each firm limited market power.\",\"plain\":\"Many firms selling similar but not identical products, each with a little control over its price.\",\"why\":\"It describes many everyday markets and shows how product variety can come at the cost of some inefficiency.\",\"ex\":\"Hairdressers in a city compete on style, location and service, so each has some loyal customers but many rivals.\",\"related\":[\"Perfect competition\",\"Monopoly\",\"Abnormal profit\",\"Normal profit\",\"Price maker\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to keep abnormal profit in the long run, when free entry pushes each firm back towards normal profit.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 38–39\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"It is allocatively inefficient because price exceeds marginal cost, but the guide notes the offsetting benefit of greater product variety (guide PDF p. 39).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Monopoly\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A market structure with a single or dominant supplier, no close substitutes and high barriers to entry, in which the firm is a price maker.\",\"plain\":\"A market with one firm, or one dominant firm, and no close substitutes.\",\"why\":\"Monopoly is the clearest case of market power as a market failure: output tends to be lower and price higher than under perfect competition.\",\"ex\":\"A water company that is the only supplier to homes in a region is a monopoly in that market.\",\"related\":[\"Natural monopoly\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Market power\",\"Perfect competition\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to produce abnormal profit regardless of the demand it faces.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 37–38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The market-structure analysis of monopoly is HL only, but the guide also names anti-monopoly regulation as a supply-side policy for all students in 3.7 (guide PDF pp. 37–38, 49).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Moral hazard\",\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"def\":\"A change in behaviour that occurs after a transaction because the party no longer bears the full consequences of their actions.\",\"plain\":\"Taking more risks because someone else will bear the cost.\",\"why\":\"It explains why insurers make policyholders pay part of any claim and why the expectation of a government rescue can encourage riskier behaviour.\",\"ex\":\"A driver with full insurance cover may park in a riskier place than a driver who would pay for any damage personally.\",\"related\":[\"Adverse selection\",\"Asymmetric information\",\"Screening\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with adverse selection: moral hazard concerns hidden actions after a deal, while adverse selection concerns hidden characteristics before it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Movement along the demand curve\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"A change in quantity demanded caused only by a change in the good’s own price, with all non-price determinants of demand held constant.\",\"plain\":\"People buy more or less because the price itself has changed.\",\"why\":\"Distinguishing movements from shifts is the basis of every demand and supply analysis in the course.\",\"ex\":\"When a cinema cuts its ticket price and sells more tickets, with nothing else changing, it has moved down its demand curve.\",\"related\":[\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"Law of demand\",\"Demand\",\"Ceteris paribus\"],\"confusion\":\"Called an ‘increase in demand’, when a price change alters quantity demanded and the curve itself stays where it is.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 31–32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A composite poverty measure combining deprivations in health, education and living standards.\",\"plain\":\"A measure that counts people as poor if they lack several basics at once, such as schooling, health and decent living conditions.\",\"why\":\"It captures deprivations that income-based poverty lines miss.\",\"ex\":\"A household with some cash income but no children in school, no clean water and no electricity is deprived on several MPI indicators.\",\"related\":[\"Absolute poverty\",\"Composite indicator\",\"Human Development Index\",\"International poverty line\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as an income measure.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"National debt\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"The accumulated stock of government borrowing outstanding, usually expressed as a percentage of GDP.\",\"plain\":\"The total amount the government owes, built up from its past borrowing.\",\"why\":\"A high debt raises interest costs, can lower a country’s credit rating, and may limit future spending or require higher taxes.\",\"ex\":\"Japan’s government has built up debt that is large relative to its GDP after many years of budget deficits.\",\"related\":[\"Budget deficit\",\"Fiscal policy\",\"Crowding out\",\"Real interest rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the annual budget deficit.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The deficit is a flow and the debt is a stock, so debt keeps rising while any deficit continues, even a shrinking one. Sustainability is usually judged by the debt-to-GDP ratio, which can fall if nominal GDP grows faster than the debt. The topic is HL only, but SL students meet ‘sustainable debt’ as a constraint on fiscal policy (guide PDF p. 49).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Natural monopoly\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A market in which a single firm can supply the whole market at lower average cost than two or more firms, because of very large economies of scale.\",\"plain\":\"A market where one firm can supply everyone more cheaply than several firms could.\",\"why\":\"It is a case where competition would raise costs, so regulation or public ownership is often preferred to breaking the firm up.\",\"ex\":\"Building two separate water pipe networks to every home would duplicate costs, so water supply is often a natural monopoly.\",\"related\":[\"Monopoly\",\"Economies of scale\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Market power\"],\"confusion\":\"Used for any large or dominant firm, when the defining feature is that average cost keeps falling over the whole range of output the market demands.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"The rate of unemployment that exists when the labour market is in equilibrium, comprising structural, frictional and seasonal unemployment.\",\"plain\":\"Normal unemployment.\",\"why\":\"In the monetarist/new classical model it is the rate the economy returns to in the long run, so demand-side policy cannot hold unemployment below it for long.\",\"ex\":\"Even in a strong economy some workers are between jobs, some lack the skills firms want and some work only in certain seasons; together they make up the natural rate.\",\"related\":[\"Full employment output\",\"Structural unemployment\",\"Frictional unemployment\",\"Seasonal unemployment\",\"Phillips curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as zero or as fixed, when it is positive and can be lowered by supply-side policies that reduce structural and frictional unemployment.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Negative externality\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A cost imposed on third parties not involved in the transaction, not reflected in market prices.\",\"plain\":\"Harm to others.\",\"why\":\"It explains why unregulated markets tend to over-produce goods whose production or use harms others, such as electricity from coal.\",\"ex\":\"Dust from a cement plant that damages crops on neighbouring farms is a negative externality of production.\",\"related\":[\"Externality\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Welfare loss\",\"Pigouvian tax\",\"Demerit good\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn by shifting the wrong curve: a negative production externality puts MSC above marginal private cost, while a negative consumption externality puts MSB below marginal private benefit.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Non-excludable\",\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"def\":\"A property of a good whereby it is impossible, or too costly, to prevent people who have not paid from using it.\",\"plain\":\"You cannot stop people from using it, even if they do not pay.\",\"why\":\"It causes the free rider problem and is shared by public goods and common pool resources.\",\"ex\":\"Once a lighthouse is lit, every passing ship can use its light, whether or not the ship’s owner has paid towards it.\",\"related\":[\"Non-rivalrous\",\"Free rider\",\"Public good\",\"Common pool resource\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as enough on its own to make something a public good, when common pool resources are also non-excludable but are rivalrous.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 36–37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Non-rivalrous\",\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"def\":\"A property of a good whereby one person’s consumption does not reduce the amount available for others to consume.\",\"plain\":\"My using it does not leave less for you.\",\"why\":\"Together with non-excludability it defines a public good, and it separates public goods from common pool resources.\",\"ex\":\"A radio broadcast is non-rivalrous: one extra listener does not reduce what anyone else can hear.\",\"related\":[\"Non-excludable\",\"Public good\",\"Common pool resource\",\"Free rider\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with non-excludable, when a good can be non-excludable but rivalrous, like fish in the open sea.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Normal good\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A good for which demand rises as income rises, so income elasticity of demand is positive.\",\"plain\":\"Something people buy more of when their income rises.\",\"why\":\"Most goods are normal, so rising incomes usually shift demand curves to the right.\",\"ex\":\"When incomes rise, households tend to buy more fresh fruit and more clothing.\",\"related\":[\"Inferior good\",\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"Income effect\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined by quality rather than by the sign of YED.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Normal goods divide into necessities, with YED between 0 and 1, and luxuries and many services, with YED greater than 1.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Normal profit\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The level of profit at which average revenue equals average cost, just sufficient to keep the firm in the industry.\",\"plain\":\"Just enough profit to make it worth the owner staying in the business.\",\"why\":\"It is the long-run outcome where entry is free and the benchmark for judging whether profit is abnormal.\",\"ex\":\"A café owner who could earn a manager’s salary elsewhere earns normal profit if the café covers that forgone salary as well as its other costs.\",\"related\":[\"Abnormal profit\",\"Perfect competition\",\"Monopolistic competition\",\"Opportunity cost\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as zero profit without explaining that opportunity cost is covered.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Normative economics\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"Statements involving value judgements about what ought to be, which cannot be settled by evidence alone.\",\"plain\":\"Statements about what should happen, based on views about what is good or fair.\",\"why\":\"Policy recommendations contain value judgements, so good evaluation states whose values and which objectives are being used.\",\"ex\":\"‘The government ought to reduce inequality even if growth slows’ is a normative statement.\",\"related\":[\"Positive economics\",\"Value judgement\",\"Equity\",\"Equality\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as wrong or unscientific, when normative judgements are a necessary part of choosing between policies.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Nudge\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"A change to choice architecture that predictably alters behaviour without forbidding options or significantly changing incentives.\",\"plain\":\"A gentle push towards a better choice that leaves people free to choose otherwise.\",\"why\":\"It is a low-cost form of intervention named in the guide that uses behavioural insights instead of taxes or bans.\",\"ex\":\"Sending text message reminders before medical appointments is a nudge intended to reduce the number of missed appointments.\",\"related\":[\"Choice architecture\",\"Behavioural economics\",\"Bounded self-control\",\"Command and control regulation\"],\"confusion\":\"Presented as costless and uncontroversial, when nudges have design and monitoring costs and raise questions about who decides which choice is ‘better’.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 33, 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Official development assistance\",\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"def\":\"Government aid designed to promote the economic development and welfare of developing countries.\",\"plain\":\"Aid given by governments, on generous terms, to promote development and welfare in developing countries.\",\"why\":\"It is a major source of external finance for some low-income countries.\",\"ex\":\"A government grant to build rural health clinics in a low-income country counts as ODA.\",\"related\":[\"Foreign aid\",\"Debt relief\",\"Economic development\",\"Poverty trap\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as identical to all foreign aid, including humanitarian and private flows.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 59\"},{\"term\":\"Oligopoly\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A market dominated by a few large firms whose decisions are interdependent.\",\"plain\":\"A market dominated by a few large firms that watch and react to each other.\",\"why\":\"Interdependence makes oligopoly hard to predict and helps explain price wars, collusion and stable prices.\",\"ex\":\"When one of a country’s few mobile networks cuts its prices, the others often respond quickly.\",\"related\":[\"Collusion\",\"Game theory\",\"Concentration ratio\",\"Barriers to entry\",\"Interdependence\"],\"confusion\":\"Analysed without the interdependence, which is its defining feature.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 38–39\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Open market operations\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The buying and selling of government bonds or other securities by the central bank to influence the money supply and short-term interest rates.\",\"plain\":\"The central bank buys or sells bonds to add money to, or take money out of, the banking system.\",\"why\":\"They are the routine way many central banks keep market interest rates close to their chosen policy rate.\",\"ex\":\"To push short-term interest rates down, a central bank buys bonds from banks, increasing the reserves they hold.\",\"related\":[\"Quantitative easing\",\"Central bank\",\"Minimum lending rate\",\"Yield\",\"Money creation\"],\"confusion\":\"The direction is reversed: central bank purchases of bonds add money to the banking system, while sales withdraw it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Opportunity cost\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.\",\"plain\":\"What you give up when you choose something.\",\"why\":\"It turns every decision, including government spending, into a comparison of alternatives rather than a question of money alone.\",\"ex\":\"If a student spends Saturday working a paid shift instead of revising, and revising was the next best use of the day, the opportunity cost is the revision forgone.\",\"related\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Production possibilities curve\",\"Increasing opportunity cost\",\"Free good\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as the money price paid, or as the sum of every alternative given up, rather than the single next best alternative.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Percentage change\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"The change in a value expressed as a proportion of its original value and multiplied by 100: (new value − original value) ÷ original value × 100.\",\"plain\":\"How big a change is compared with where you started, written as a percentage.\",\"why\":\"Every elasticity in the course is a ratio of two percentage changes, so this calculation comes before any elasticity result.\",\"ex\":\"Illustrative data: if a ticket price rises from 2.00 to 2.50, the percentage change is (2.50 − 2.00) ÷ 2.00 × 100 = 25%.\",\"related\":[\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Income elasticity of demand\",\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"Elasticity\"],\"confusion\":\"Calculated using the new value as the base, or confused with a change in percentage points.\",\"ib\":\"Quantitative skill\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 33–34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Perfect competition\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A market with many firms, free entry and homogeneous products, in which each firm is a price taker.\",\"plain\":\"A model market with many small firms selling identical products, where no single firm can affect the price.\",\"why\":\"It is the benchmark for judging efficiency and the effects of market power.\",\"ex\":\"Markets for some agricultural products come close to the model, because many farmers sell a very similar product.\",\"related\":[\"Price taker\",\"Monopoly\",\"Normal profit\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Monopolistic competition\"],\"confusion\":\"Used as a description of a real market rather than as a benchmark.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 37–38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"In the long run, free entry and exit push profit to normal and price to the minimum of average cost, while P = MC gives allocative efficiency.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Phillips curve\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"A curve showing the short-run inverse relationship between the inflation rate and the unemployment rate.\",\"plain\":\"A curve showing that, in the short run, lower unemployment tends to come with higher inflation.\",\"why\":\"It frames the policy trade-off between low inflation and low unemployment, and the debate about whether that trade-off lasts.\",\"ex\":\"In the 1970s several advanced economies had high inflation and high unemployment together, which the simple short-run curve could not explain.\",\"related\":[\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Inflation\",\"Unemployment rate\",\"LRAS\",\"Cost-push inflation\"],\"confusion\":\"The short-run trade-off is presented as permanent, and the vertical long-run Phillips curve at the natural rate is left out.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Once people come to expect higher inflation, the short-run curve shifts up and unemployment drifts back to the natural rate. The long-run Phillips curve is therefore vertical, mirroring the vertical LRAS curve.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 44–45\"},{\"term\":\"Pigouvian tax\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"An indirect tax set equal to the external cost at the socially optimal output, intended to internalise the externality.\",\"plain\":\"A tax designed to make polluters pay for the damage they cause to others.\",\"why\":\"It aims to bring output down to the socially optimum level by closing the gap between private and social cost.\",\"ex\":\"A tax on each tonne of waste sent to landfill makes firms pay part of the external cost of dumping and encourages recycling.\",\"related\":[\"Carbon tax\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Negative externality\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Socially optimum output\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as a tax whose purpose is to raise revenue, when its aim is to change behaviour; if it works well, the revenue from it may shrink over time.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Setting the tax correctly requires a money value for the external cost, which is hard to measure; the guide lists measurement as a limitation of these policies (guide PDF p. 37).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Planned economy\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"An economic system in which the government owns most productive resources and answers the basic economic questions through central planning rather than through prices.\",\"plain\":\"An economy where the government decides what is made, how it is made and who gets it.\",\"why\":\"Comparing it with the free market shows the information and incentive roles that prices play.\",\"ex\":\"A planning agency that sets output targets for steel and fixes the price of bread is answering questions that a market would leave to prices.\",\"related\":[\"Free market economy\",\"Mixed economy\",\"Basic economic questions\",\"Price mechanism\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to describe any economy with a large public sector, when the defining feature is that planning rather than prices allocates most resources.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Portfolio investment\",\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"def\":\"Cross-border purchase of financial assets without a controlling interest, recorded in the financial account.\",\"plain\":\"Buying foreign shares or bonds as an investment, without gaining control of the company.\",\"why\":\"Portfolio flows can move quickly, so they can cause sharp swings in exchange rates.\",\"ex\":\"A Canadian pension fund buying a small shareholding in an Indian bank is making a portfolio investment.\",\"related\":[\"Foreign direct investment\",\"Financial account\",\"Capital flight\",\"Exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with foreign direct investment.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 54–55\"},{\"term\":\"Positive economics\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"Statements about what is, which can in principle be tested against evidence.\",\"plain\":\"Statements about how the economy works that could, in principle, be checked against facts.\",\"why\":\"Separating testable claims from opinions is the first step in judging any economic argument.\",\"ex\":\"‘A higher tax on sugary drinks will reduce the quantity bought’ is a positive statement, because evidence can support or refute it.\",\"related\":[\"Normative economics\",\"Economic model\",\"Value judgement\",\"Ceteris paribus\"],\"confusion\":\"Equated with ‘true’, when a positive statement can be false; what makes it positive is that evidence could test it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Positive externality\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A benefit of production or consumption accruing to a third party.\",\"plain\":\"A benefit that spills over to people who did not pay for it.\",\"why\":\"It explains why markets tend to under-provide goods such as vaccination and education.\",\"ex\":\"A beekeeper’s bees pollinate nearby orchards, benefiting fruit growers who pay nothing for the service.\",\"related\":[\"Externality\",\"Merit good\",\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Subsidy\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the private benefit to the buyer, or treated as identical to a merit good, which is a wider category.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Poverty trap\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"A self-reinforcing mechanism in which poverty causes conditions that perpetuate poverty.\",\"plain\":\"Being stuck poor.\",\"why\":\"It explains why poverty can persist across generations unless something breaks the cycle.\",\"ex\":\"Low incomes mean a family cannot afford schooling, so the children grow up with few skills and low incomes of their own.\",\"related\":[\"Human capital\",\"Absolute poverty\",\"Microfinance\",\"Foreign aid\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as a lack of effort by poor people, rather than as a self-reinforcing loop in which low income limits saving, investment and human capital.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"PPP\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"Purchasing power parity: a conversion that adjusts for differences in the cost of living between countries.\",\"plain\":\"A way of converting incomes between countries that allows for how much money actually buys in each place.\",\"why\":\"Without it, incomes in low-price countries look smaller than the living standards they actually support.\",\"ex\":\"A haircut costs far less in India than in the UK once prices are converted at the market exchange rate, so converting Indian incomes at that rate understates what they can buy.\",\"related\":[\"Real GDP per capita\",\"GNI\",\"Exchange rate\",\"Human Development Index\"],\"confusion\":\"Applied and then described as removing all comparison problems.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"Price ceiling\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"A maximum price set by government below the market equilibrium price, making it illegal to trade above that price.\",\"plain\":\"A maximum price.\",\"why\":\"It is used to make essentials more affordable, but a binding ceiling creates a shortage and can lead to queues or informal markets.\",\"ex\":\"A cap on rents set below the market rent can leave more people seeking flats than there are flats available to rent.\",\"related\":[\"Price floor\",\"Excess demand\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Analysed as binding when drawn above the equilibrium price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 35–36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Price deflator\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"A price index used to convert a nominal value into a real value by removing the effect of changes in the price level.\",\"plain\":\"A number showing how much prices have changed, so that price rises can be stripped out of money figures.\",\"why\":\"Without deflating, a rise in money values cannot be separated from a rise in the quantity of output.\",\"ex\":\"If nominal GDP is 1,100 and the price deflator is 110 (base year = 100), real GDP is 1,100 ÷ 110 × 100 = 1,000 in base-year prices (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Real GDP\",\"GDP\",\"Inflation\",\"Consumer price index\"],\"confusion\":\"The deflator is subtracted from nominal GDP, instead of nominal GDP being divided by the deflator and multiplied by 100.\",\"ib\":\"Quantitative skill\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 41\"},{\"term\":\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A measure of the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price: %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP.\",\"plain\":\"How strongly the amount people buy reacts when the price changes.\",\"why\":\"It predicts what happens to a firm’s total revenue after a price change and who bears the burden of an indirect tax.\",\"ex\":\"A firm selling a product with inelastic demand, such as a daily medicine with no close substitute, can raise its price without losing many sales.\",\"related\":[\"Total revenue\",\"Elasticity\",\"Substitute\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Percentage change\"],\"confusion\":\"Reported with its sign and then reasoned from as though the sign were the magnitude.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 33–34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Along a straight-line demand curve, PED falls from very elastic at high prices to very inelastic at low prices even though the slope is constant; this is HL only in the guide (guide PDF p. 33).\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘How much demand changes with price.’, uses ‘demand’ where PED measures the response of quantity demanded.\"},{\"term\":\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"def\":\"A measure of the responsiveness of quantity supplied to a change in price: %ΔQs ÷ %ΔP.\",\"plain\":\"How strongly the amount firms offer for sale reacts when the price changes.\",\"why\":\"It determines how far price rather than quantity adjusts when demand changes, and how the burden of a tax is shared.\",\"ex\":\"Hotels in a small resort cannot add rooms quickly, so a surge in demand during a festival raises room prices more than the number of rooms available.\",\"related\":[\"Unused capacity\",\"Primary commodity\",\"Supply\",\"Percentage change\",\"Indirect tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as fixed rather than as rising with the time horizon.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"A straight-line supply curve through the origin has PES = 1 at every point; one that meets the price axis is price elastic throughout, and one that meets the quantity axis is price inelastic throughout.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘How much supply changes with price.’, uses ‘supply’ where PES measures the response of quantity supplied.\"},{\"term\":\"Price floor\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"A minimum price set by government above the market equilibrium price, making it illegal to trade below that price.\",\"plain\":\"A minimum price.\",\"why\":\"It is used to support producers’ incomes or workers’ wages, but a binding floor creates a surplus that someone has to deal with.\",\"ex\":\"A government-set minimum price for milk above the market price can leave farmers producing more milk than consumers buy at that price.\",\"related\":[\"Price ceiling\",\"Excess supply\",\"Minimum wage\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Expected to cause a shortage, as a ceiling does, or analysed without checking that it is set above equilibrium, where it binds.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 35–36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Price maker\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A firm that can set its own price because it faces a downward-sloping demand curve, although it cannot choose price and quantity independently of each other.\",\"plain\":\"A firm with enough control to choose its price, but it sells less if it charges more.\",\"why\":\"All forms of imperfect competition involve price makers, which is why their marginal revenue lies below price.\",\"ex\":\"A popular smartphone brand can raise its price, but it will sell fewer phones as some buyers switch to rivals.\",\"related\":[\"Price taker\",\"Market power\",\"Monopoly\",\"Marginal revenue\",\"Monopolistic competition\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be able to charge any price and still sell the same amount, when the demand curve limits how much it can sell at each price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Price mechanism\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The process by which prices signal, incentivise and ration in allocating resources.\",\"plain\":\"The way changing prices tell buyers and sellers what to do and share out goods without anyone being in charge.\",\"why\":\"It is how a market economy answers the basic economic questions, and its failures are the case for intervention.\",\"ex\":\"A frost that damages orange crops raises orange prices, which rations the smaller supply and encourages growers elsewhere to sell more.\",\"related\":[\"Market equilibrium\",\"Free market economy\",\"Basic economic questions\",\"Market failure\"],\"confusion\":\"Reduced to rationing alone, leaving out how prices signal information and create incentives that move resources between uses.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Price taker\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"A firm that cannot influence the market price and must accept the price determined by market demand and supply, so it faces a perfectly elastic demand curve (P = D = AR = MR).\",\"plain\":\"A seller too small to affect the price, so it sells at whatever the market price is.\",\"why\":\"It is the defining behaviour of firms in perfect competition and the benchmark for allocative efficiency.\",\"ex\":\"A single wheat farmer selling into a large national market cannot charge more than the going price without losing her buyers.\",\"related\":[\"Perfect competition\",\"Price maker\",\"Market power\",\"Marginal revenue\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to mean the market price is fixed, when the market price can change but the individual firm cannot influence it.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Primary commodity\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"A raw material or unprocessed product of agriculture, fishing, forestry or mining, such as wheat, coffee, copper or crude oil.\",\"plain\":\"A basic product taken from the land or sea before it is processed.\",\"why\":\"At HL, the low PED and PES of primary commodities help explain why their prices are volatile and why commodity-dependent economies face risk.\",\"ex\":\"Because new coffee plants take years to produce a crop, growers cannot quickly expand output when coffee prices rise.\",\"related\":[\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"Unused capacity\",\"Substitute\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to have inelastic demand without a reason, rather than because many commodities are necessities with few substitutes and their supply takes time to adjust.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Demand for one producer’s or one country’s output of a commodity can be elastic, because buyers can switch to identical supplies from elsewhere; the low PED applies to the commodity as a whole.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Privatisation\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"The transfer of ownership of a firm or asset from the public sector to the private sector.\",\"plain\":\"Selling a government-owned business to private owners.\",\"why\":\"Supporters argue that private ownership improves efficiency, while critics worry about natural monopolies and the loss of public control.\",\"ex\":\"The UK privatised British Telecom in the 1980s by selling its shares to private investors.\",\"related\":[\"Deregulation\",\"Market-based supply-side policies\",\"Natural monopoly\",\"Supply-side policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to create competition on its own, although a privatised monopoly remains a monopoly unless rivals are allowed to enter.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 49\"},{\"term\":\"Producer surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The difference between the price producers receive and the minimum price they would accept.\",\"plain\":\"The extra gain sellers get when they receive more than the lowest price they would have accepted.\",\"why\":\"It measures sellers’ gain from trade and shows how policies redistribute welfare between groups.\",\"ex\":\"A grower who would sell apples at any price covering the cost of picking them gains producer surplus whenever the market price is higher than that.\",\"related\":[\"Consumer surplus\",\"Social surplus\",\"Supply\",\"Allocative efficiency\"],\"confusion\":\"Shaded above the price line instead of between the price line and the supply curve, or equated with profit.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 32–33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Producers’ profit.’, contradicts the inventory’s own note that producer surplus is not profit because it does not deduct fixed costs.\"},{\"term\":\"Production possibilities curve\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"A model showing the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce with its resources fully and efficiently employed.\",\"plain\":\"A graph showing the most an economy can make of two goods when it uses all its resources fully and well.\",\"why\":\"It illustrates scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, unemployment of resources and growth in a single diagram.\",\"ex\":\"A country that moves along its curve towards more capital goods and fewer consumer goods gives up some consumption today in return for more productive capacity later.\",\"related\":[\"Opportunity cost\",\"Scarcity\",\"Increasing opportunity cost\",\"Actual growth\",\"Growth in production possibilities\"],\"confusion\":\"A point inside the curve is called unattainable, when it is attainable but reflects unemployed or inefficiently used resources; only points outside the curve are unattainable.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The curve is usually bowed outward because resources are specialised, so each extra unit of one good costs more of the other. It is a straight line only if resources are equally suited to both goods.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Profit maximisation\",\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"def\":\"The objective of producing the output at which total revenue exceeds total cost by the largest amount, found where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR).\",\"plain\":\"Choosing the level of output that gives the biggest possible profit.\",\"why\":\"It is the standard assumption about what firms aim for and the basis of the market-structure diagrams at HL.\",\"ex\":\"If making one more unit adds more to revenue than to cost, a profit-maximising firm produces it, and it stops expanding when the two are equal.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal revenue\",\"Marginal cost\",\"Satisficing\",\"Corporate social responsibility\",\"Abnormal profit\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with maximising revenue or price, when a profit-maximising firm may charge less than the highest price some buyers would pay.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 33, 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Progressive tax\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A tax for which the average rate of tax rises as income rises.\",\"plain\":\"The richer you are, the larger the share of your income you pay in tax.\",\"why\":\"Progressive taxes reduce income inequality and also act as an automatic stabiliser.\",\"ex\":\"An income tax that exempts the first slice of income and charges higher rates on higher slices is progressive.\",\"related\":[\"Regressive tax\",\"Proportional tax\",\"Average tax rate\",\"Marginal tax rate\",\"Automatic stabiliser\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined by the amount paid rather than by the average rate, although richer people also pay more money under a proportional tax.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Rich pay more.’) is also true of a proportional tax, which the inventory’s own note points out; the test is the average rate.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Property rights\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"Legally enforceable rights to own, use, sell and benefit from assets such as land, buildings and businesses.\",\"plain\":\"Clear, protected rights to own and use things such as land or a business.\",\"why\":\"Secure property rights encourage investment and borrowing, while weak rights are a barrier to development.\",\"ex\":\"A farmer with a registered land title can use the land as collateral for a loan to buy better equipment.\",\"related\":[\"Informal economy\",\"Microfinance\",\"Tragedy of the commons\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as relevant only to wealthy owners, when insecure land rights often matter most for poor farmers and informal businesses.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 58–59\"},{\"term\":\"Proportional tax\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A tax for which the average rate of tax stays the same at all levels of income.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone pays the same share of their income in tax.\",\"why\":\"It is the benchmark that separates progressive taxes from regressive ones.\",\"ex\":\"A flat 15% income tax with no tax-free allowance takes the same share of income from a low earner as from a high earner (illustrative rate).\",\"related\":[\"Progressive tax\",\"Regressive tax\",\"Average tax rate\",\"Marginal tax rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Thought to take the same amount of money from everyone, when richer people pay more money but the same percentage of their income.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Protectionism\",\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"def\":\"Government measures restricting international trade to shield domestic producers.\",\"plain\":\"Government action that restricts imports to shield domestic producers from foreign competition.\",\"why\":\"Protection creates winners and losers at home and abroad, so weighing them is central to evaluating trade policy.\",\"ex\":\"Tariffs on imported steel, quotas on imported cars and subsidies for domestic farmers are all forms of protectionism.\",\"related\":[\"Tariff\",\"Quota\",\"Export subsidy\",\"Administrative barrier\",\"Infant industry\"],\"confusion\":\"Evaluated only on the producer effect, ignoring consumers and downstream firms.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 52–53\"},{\"term\":\"Public good\",\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"def\":\"A good that is non-rivalrous and non-excludable, so the free-rider problem weakens the incentive to pay and the market tends to under-provide it.\",\"plain\":\"Something that one person’s use does not reduce and that no one can be stopped from using, so few people choose to pay for it.\",\"why\":\"It explains why markets tend to under-provide goods such as defence and flood defences, and why governments provide or fund them.\",\"ex\":\"A flood barrier protects every house behind it, and residents who do not pay cannot be excluded from that protection.\",\"related\":[\"Free rider\",\"Non-rivalrous\",\"Non-excludable\",\"Common pool resource\",\"Merit good\"],\"confusion\":\"Any good the government provides is called a public good, although state schooling and health care are rivalrous and excludable and are better classed as merit goods.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Some goods are quasi-public: a road is hard to exclude people from but becomes rivalrous when it is congested.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘Something the government provides.’, is the misconception the inventory’s own note corrects: provision is a consequence, not the definition.\"},{\"term\":\"Quantitative easing\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"Central bank purchase of financial assets to increase the money supply when interest rates are already low.\",\"plain\":\"The central bank creates new money to buy bonds and other assets, aiming to push down longer-term interest rates.\",\"why\":\"It gives a central bank a tool when its policy interest rate is close to zero and cannot usefully be cut further.\",\"ex\":\"After the 2008 financial crisis, central banks including the Bank of England and the US Federal Reserve bought large quantities of government bonds.\",\"related\":[\"Open market operations\",\"Central bank\",\"Yield\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Money creation\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as printing money to fund government spending.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Buying bonds raises their prices and lowers their yields, which feeds through to borrowing costs for households and firms. Whether QE adds to inflation depends on how much spare capacity the economy has.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 47\"},{\"term\":\"Quota\",\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"def\":\"A physical limit on the quantity of a good that may be imported over a given period.\",\"plain\":\"Import limit.\",\"why\":\"It raises the domestic price and helps domestic producers but, unlike a tariff, may raise no revenue for the government.\",\"ex\":\"A government allows only a fixed number of foreign cars to be imported each year and issues licences to importers.\",\"related\":[\"Tariff\",\"Protectionism\",\"World price\",\"Export subsidy\"],\"confusion\":\"The revenue rectangle is awarded to the government rather than to the licence holder.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Real GDP\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"The total value of all final goods and services produced within a country in a given period, adjusted for changes in the price level.\",\"plain\":\"Total output.\",\"why\":\"Only real GDP tells you whether an economy is producing more, rather than just charging more.\",\"ex\":\"If nominal GDP rises by 5% in a year in which the price level also rises by 5%, real GDP is roughly unchanged (illustrative figures).\",\"related\":[\"GDP\",\"Price deflator\",\"Economic growth\",\"Real GDP per capita\",\"Inflation\"],\"confusion\":\"A rise in nominal GDP is reported as economic growth, even when most of it reflects higher prices rather than more output.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 41\"},{\"term\":\"Real GDP per capita\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"Real GDP divided by the population, giving average real output per person.\",\"plain\":\"The value of output per person after allowing for changes in prices.\",\"why\":\"It is the usual first measure for comparing living standards over time or between countries.\",\"ex\":\"If real GDP grows more slowly than the population, real GDP per capita falls even though the economy has grown.\",\"related\":[\"Real GDP\",\"PPP\",\"GNI\",\"Human Development Index\",\"Economic development\"],\"confusion\":\"Read as the income a typical person receives, when it is an average that says nothing about how output is shared.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The guide also asks for real GNI per capita, and for comparisons between countries at purchasing power parity (guide PDF p. 42).\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"Real interest rate\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The nominal interest rate adjusted for inflation.\",\"plain\":\"The interest rate after allowing for inflation, showing the real reward to saving and the real cost of borrowing.\",\"why\":\"Spending and saving decisions respond to real, not nominal, interest rates.\",\"ex\":\"A saver earning 3% interest when inflation is 5% loses roughly 2% of purchasing power a year (illustrative figures).\",\"related\":[\"Inflation\",\"Monetary policy\",\"Investment\",\"Deflation\"],\"confusion\":\"Calculated by adding inflation to the nominal rate instead of subtracting it, or confused with the policy rate the central bank sets.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Real interest rate ≈ nominal interest rate − inflation rate. Deflation therefore raises the real interest rate even when nominal rates are close to zero.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 48\"},{\"term\":\"Recession\",\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"def\":\"A period in which real GDP falls, commonly identified as at least two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth.\",\"plain\":\"A period when the economy’s output shrinks, usually with rising unemployment.\",\"why\":\"Recessions bring cyclical unemployment, falling incomes and business failures, and are the main target of expansionary policy.\",\"ex\":\"Many economies went into recession in 2020 when pandemic restrictions sharply reduced spending and production.\",\"related\":[\"Business cycle\",\"Deflationary gap\",\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"Economic growth\",\"Automatic stabiliser\"],\"confusion\":\"A slowdown in growth is called a recession, although in a recession output actually falls.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The two-quarter rule is a convention, not an official definition; some statistical bodies judge recessions from a wider set of indicators, such as employment and income.\",\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 43, 49\"},{\"term\":\"Regressive tax\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A tax whose average rate falls as income rises.\",\"plain\":\"The poorer you are, the larger the share of your income you pay in tax.\",\"why\":\"Regressive taxes widen inequality, so their use has to be weighed against other aims such as raising revenue or discouraging harmful goods.\",\"ex\":\"A tax on basic food takes a larger share of a low-income household’s income than of a high-income household’s, even though both pay the same tax per item.\",\"related\":[\"Progressive tax\",\"Proportional tax\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Average tax rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Asserted of any indirect tax without reference to the share of income spent.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Relative poverty\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A condition of having income below a threshold set relative to the distribution in a particular society.\",\"plain\":\"Having much less income than is typical in your own society.\",\"why\":\"It links poverty to exclusion from normal life in a society, so it can persist even as a country grows richer.\",\"ex\":\"A household with income far above the international poverty line can still be in relative poverty in a high-income country if its income is well below the national median.\",\"related\":[\"Absolute poverty\",\"Gini coefficient\",\"Transfer payment\",\"Equity\"],\"confusion\":\"Compared across countries as though the threshold were common.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Thresholds are often set at a fraction of median household income, such as 50% or 60%, but the exact line is a policy choice.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 45\"},{\"term\":\"Remittance\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A transfer of money by a worker to their home country, recorded in the current account.\",\"plain\":\"Money that people working abroad send home, usually to their families.\",\"why\":\"For some developing economies, remittances are a large source of foreign currency and household income.\",\"ex\":\"A nurse from the Philippines working in the Gulf sends part of her wages home to her family each month.\",\"related\":[\"Current transfers\",\"Current account\",\"Exchange rate\",\"Foreign aid\"],\"confusion\":\"Placed in the financial account.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Remittances raise demand for the receiving country’s currency, which is why the guide lists them among the factors that affect exchange rates (guide PDF p. 54).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Revaluation\",\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"def\":\"A deliberate increase in the value of a currency by the authorities under a fixed or managed exchange rate system.\",\"plain\":\"The government or central bank deliberately raises the value of a fixed currency.\",\"why\":\"It lowers import prices and can reduce inflation, but it makes exports less competitive.\",\"ex\":\"China revalued the renminbi against the US dollar in 2005 as it moved away from a strict peg.\",\"related\":[\"Devaluation\",\"Appreciation\",\"Fixed exchange rate\",\"Managed exchange rate\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with appreciation, which is a market-driven rise under a floating rate.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 54\"},{\"term\":\"Satisficing\",\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"def\":\"Pursuing an outcome that is good enough against several objectives rather than maximising one.\",\"plain\":\"Aiming for a result that is good enough rather than the best possible.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the alternative business objectives that challenge the assumption that firms maximise profit.\",\"ex\":\"A family-owned firm may accept a satisfactory profit so that its owners keep reasonable working hours and good relations with staff.\",\"related\":[\"Profit maximisation\",\"Corporate social responsibility\",\"Bounded rationality\",\"Behavioural economics\"],\"confusion\":\"Read as irrationality or poor management rather than as a reasonable response to limited information and competing objectives.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 33\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Scarcity\",\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"def\":\"The condition in which resources are limited relative to unlimited wants, which forces choice.\",\"plain\":\"There is not enough to go round, so we cannot have everything we want.\",\"why\":\"Scarcity is the reason economics exists: every economic question is about using limited resources as well as possible.\",\"ex\":\"A town with one plot of spare land must choose between a hospital and housing, because the same land cannot be used for both.\",\"related\":[\"Opportunity cost\",\"Free good\",\"Factors of production\",\"Production possibilities curve\"],\"confusion\":\"Stated as a shortage, which is a market condition at a price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 18, 29\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Screening\",\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"def\":\"Action by the less informed party to extract information before a transaction.\",\"plain\":\"The less informed side of a deal finding ways to learn what the other side knows.\",\"why\":\"It is one of the private responses to asymmetric information named in the guide.\",\"ex\":\"An insurer that asks applicants to take a medical examination before offering cover is screening.\",\"related\":[\"Signalling\",\"Asymmetric information\",\"Adverse selection\"],\"confusion\":\"Attributed to the informed party, which is signalling.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Seasonal unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"Unemployment that arises because demand for some types of labour varies predictably with the seasons.\",\"plain\":\"People out of work at certain times of year because their jobs are seasonal.\",\"why\":\"It is part of the natural rate and raises measured unemployment at predictable times of year.\",\"ex\":\"Ski instructors and staff at beach resorts may be out of work during their off-season.\",\"related\":[\"Frictional unemployment\",\"Structural unemployment\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Cyclical unemployment\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with cyclical unemployment, although seasonal patterns repeat every year whatever the state of the economy.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"A change in the quantity demanded at every price, caused by a change in a non-price determinant such as income, tastes and preferences, future price expectations, the prices of related goods or the number of consumers.\",\"plain\":\"People want to buy more or less at every price because something other than the price has changed.\",\"why\":\"Shifts of demand and supply are what move the market to a new equilibrium price and quantity.\",\"ex\":\"A widely reported health warning about a snack can shift its demand curve to the left, so less is bought at every price.\",\"related\":[\"Movement along the demand curve\",\"Complement\",\"Substitute\",\"Normal good\",\"Market equilibrium\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn after a change in the good’s own price, which causes a movement along the curve rather than a shift.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 31–32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Signalling\",\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"def\":\"Action by the better informed party to credibly convey information to the less informed party.\",\"plain\":\"The better informed side of a deal doing something that credibly shows the quality of what it offers.\",\"why\":\"It can reduce adverse selection without government action, for example through warranties and qualifications.\",\"ex\":\"A car dealer offering a long warranty signals confidence that its cars are reliable.\",\"related\":[\"Screening\",\"Asymmetric information\",\"Adverse selection\",\"Price mechanism\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with screening, or with the signalling function of prices in 2.3, which is a different idea.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 37\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Social surplus\",\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"def\":\"The sum of consumer and producer surplus, maximised at allocative efficiency.\",\"plain\":\"The total gain to buyers and sellers together from a market.\",\"why\":\"It is the welfare yardstick used to judge efficiency and to measure welfare loss.\",\"ex\":\"In a competitive market with no externalities, the combined areas of consumer and producer surplus are as large as they can be at the equilibrium.\",\"related\":[\"Consumer surplus\",\"Producer surplus\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be maximised at any market equilibrium, when externalities or market power mean the market outcome leaves some surplus unrealised.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 32, 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide uses the term ‘social/community surplus’. When externalities are present it must be judged using marginal social benefit and marginal social cost rather than private demand and supply.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Socially optimum output\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The level of output at which marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost (MSB = MSC), so allocative efficiency is achieved and social/community surplus is maximised.\",\"plain\":\"The amount that is best for society once everyone’s costs and benefits are counted.\",\"why\":\"It is the target against which market output is compared in every externality diagram.\",\"ex\":\"With traffic congestion, the socially optimum number of car trips is lower than the number drivers choose, because each driver ignores the delay imposed on others.\",\"related\":[\"Marginal social benefit\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be zero for a harmful good, when the optimum is usually a lower but positive output at which the extra benefit still covers the full social cost.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"SRAS\",\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"def\":\"The short-run aggregate supply curve, along which at least one input cost is fixed.\",\"plain\":\"How much firms produce at each price level while wages and other input prices have not yet adjusted.\",\"why\":\"Shifts in SRAS explain cost-push inflation and supply shocks.\",\"ex\":\"A sharp rise in world oil prices raises firms’ costs and shifts SRAS to the left.\",\"related\":[\"LRAS\",\"Cost-push inflation\",\"Aggregate supply\",\"Indirect tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Shifted for changes in productive capacity, which move LRAS.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 42\"},{\"term\":\"Structural unemployment\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"Unemployment arising from a mismatch between the skills or location of the unemployed and the requirements of available jobs.\",\"plain\":\"People are out of work because their skills or location do not match the jobs that are available.\",\"why\":\"Boosting demand alone does little to cure it, so it calls for supply-side measures such as retraining.\",\"ex\":\"Coal miners who lose their jobs when mines close may stay unemployed even while software jobs go unfilled in distant cities.\",\"related\":[\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"Frictional unemployment\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Supply-side policy\",\"Human capital\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as curable by demand management.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Long-term unemployment.’) defines it by duration, which the inventory’s own note rejects: the mismatch, not the duration, is the definition.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Subsidy\",\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"def\":\"A payment made by government to firms per unit of output, reducing costs of production and increasing supply.\",\"plain\":\"Government money for firms.\",\"why\":\"Subsidies are used to support producers, lower prices and correct positive externalities, but they carry an opportunity cost for the government budget.\",\"ex\":\"A per-unit subsidy to solar panel installers lowers their costs, which increases supply and reduces the price households pay.\",\"related\":[\"Indirect tax\",\"Positive externality\",\"Merit good\",\"Supply\"],\"confusion\":\"The cost to government is read as the payment per unit rather than per unit times the new quantity.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Consumers usually gain less than the full subsidy per unit: how far the price falls depends on the price elasticities of demand and supply.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Substitute\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"A good that can be consumed in place of another, so a rise in the price of one raises demand for the other.\",\"plain\":\"A good that can be used instead of another, such as tea instead of coffee.\",\"why\":\"Substitutes are a non-price determinant of demand, and their number and closeness are a main determinant of price elasticity of demand.\",\"ex\":\"When the price of bus travel rises, demand for train travel on the same route tends to increase.\",\"related\":[\"Complement\",\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Shift of the demand curve\",\"Competitive supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Identified by physical similarity, when what matters is whether consumers actually switch to it when the other good’s price rises.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 31, 34\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Substitution effect\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"The part of the change in quantity demanded after a price change that results from the good becoming relatively cheaper or more expensive than alternatives.\",\"plain\":\"When something becomes cheaper compared with alternatives, people switch towards it.\",\"why\":\"It is the main reason quantity demanded rises when price falls, even before any change in purchasing power.\",\"ex\":\"When the price of chicken falls while beef prices stay the same, some shoppers replace beef meals with chicken.\",\"related\":[\"Income effect\",\"Law of demand\",\"Substitute\",\"Price elasticity of demand\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with a shift in demand for a substitute good, whereas the substitution effect concerns the quantity demanded of the good whose own price changed.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 31\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Supply\",\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"def\":\"The quantity of a good producers are willing and able to sell at each price over a given period.\",\"plain\":\"How much of a good firms are willing and able to sell at each possible price.\",\"why\":\"Supply is the other half of the price mechanism, and its shifts explain how costs, taxes and technology affect prices.\",\"ex\":\"At a higher price for strawberries, growers are willing to pick more of their crop and send it to market.\",\"related\":[\"Law of supply\",\"Demand\",\"Market equilibrium\",\"Factors of production\",\"Price elasticity of supply\"],\"confusion\":\"Defined as the amount firms produce, leaving out ‘willing and able’, each price and the time period, or confused with quantity supplied at a single price.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 32\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version, ‘How much firms make.’, describes output produced and drops both the price schedule and the ‘willing and able’ condition that the inventory’s own note stresses.\"},{\"term\":\"Supply-side policy\",\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"def\":\"Policies designed to increase the productive capacity of the economy by improving the quantity or quality of factors of production or the efficiency of markets.\",\"plain\":\"Making the economy more efficient.\",\"why\":\"It is the main route to long-term growth without adding to inflationary pressure.\",\"ex\":\"A government funds vocational training so that workers can fill skilled jobs, raising the economy’s productive capacity.\",\"related\":[\"LRAS\",\"Market-based supply-side policies\",\"Interventionist supply-side policies\",\"Structural unemployment\",\"Economic growth\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as any policy that helps firms, including demand-side measures.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 49\"},{\"term\":\"Sustainable development\",\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"def\":\"Development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs, in the widely used definition from the Brundtland Commission’s 1987 report.\",\"plain\":\"Improving lives today in ways that do not leave future generations less able to meet their own needs.\",\"why\":\"It frames the tension between raising living standards now and protecting resources for the future.\",\"ex\":\"Replacing coal-fired power with renewable energy while extending electricity to more households aims to meet current needs without harming future ones.\",\"related\":[\"Sustainable Development Goals\",\"Economic development\",\"Happy Planet Index\",\"Tragedy of the commons\",\"Poverty trap\"],\"confusion\":\"Used to mean financially viable.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The relationship between sustainability and poverty is HL only (guide PDF p. 57): poor households may have little choice but to overuse local resources, which can deepen their poverty over time.\",\"new\":false,\"plainFix\":\"The inventory plain version (‘Green growth.’) narrows the idea to the environment and to output growth, and drops the intergenerational clause that the inventory’s own note calls the definition.\",\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 57\"},{\"term\":\"Sustainable Development Goals\",\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"def\":\"A set of 17 goals adopted by United Nations member states in 2015, covering poverty, health, education, inequality and the environment, with targets for 2030.\",\"plain\":\"The UN’s list of 17 global goals for improving lives and protecting the planet by 2030.\",\"why\":\"They give a shared framework for judging development progress, which the guide asks students to assess for selected countries.\",\"ex\":\"Goal 1 aims to end poverty in all its forms, and Goal 13 calls for urgent action on climate change.\",\"related\":[\"Sustainable development\",\"Economic development\",\"Multidimensional Poverty Index\",\"Composite indicator\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as environmental goals only, although most address poverty, health, education, inequality and institutions.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 57, 59\"},{\"term\":\"Tariff\",\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"def\":\"A tax placed on imported goods and services, raising their domestic price.\",\"plain\":\"Import tax.\",\"why\":\"It is the most widely analysed trade barrier, with clear effects on consumers, producers, government revenue and welfare.\",\"ex\":\"A tax on each imported tonne of steel raises its domestic price, protecting domestic steelmakers but raising costs for car makers that use steel.\",\"related\":[\"Quota\",\"Protectionism\",\"World price\",\"Indirect tax\",\"Welfare loss\"],\"confusion\":\"Analysed without the two welfare loss triangles, or with the revenue rectangle measured on total consumption rather than on remaining imports.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"Calculating the effects of a tariff on stakeholders from a diagram is HL only (guide PDF p. 52).\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 52\"},{\"term\":\"Terms of trade\",\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"def\":\"The ratio of export prices to import prices, usually expressed as an index. Enrichment: beyond the currently named IB Economics requirements, since the guide for first assessment 2022 names neither the topic nor the calculation.\",\"plain\":\"How many imports a country can buy with a given quantity of its exports.\",\"why\":\"Changes in the terms of trade affect a country’s real income, especially if it exports only a few commodities.\",\"ex\":\"When world prices of a country’s main mineral export fall while the prices of its imports stay steady, its terms of trade worsen.\",\"related\":[\"Balance of trade\",\"Exchange rate\",\"Current account\",\"Export promotion\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with the balance of trade, which is a value. Also treated as an examinable calculation, which on this course it is not.\",\"ib\":\"Platform term\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Terms of trade are outside the 2022 guide: neither the topic nor the calculation is named there. They are included as enrichment because they help explain why dependence on primary commodities can be a barrier to development.\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Total revenue\",\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"def\":\"The total amount a firm receives from selling its output, equal to price multiplied by quantity sold (TR = P × Q).\",\"plain\":\"All the money a firm takes in from its sales.\",\"why\":\"Linking PED to total revenue shows whether raising or cutting price will increase a firm’s sales income.\",\"ex\":\"When a rail company cuts fares on a route where demand is price elastic, the extra passengers can raise its total revenue.\",\"related\":[\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"Marginal revenue\",\"Abnormal profit\",\"Normal profit\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with profit, which is total revenue minus total cost.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 34, 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"When demand is price elastic, a price cut raises total revenue; when demand is price inelastic, a price rise raises it; at unit elasticity total revenue is unchanged.\",\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Tradable permit\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"A transferable right to emit a fixed quantity of a pollutant, fixing quantity and letting price vary.\",\"plain\":\"A right to pollute a set amount, which firms can buy and sell.\",\"why\":\"It caps total pollution while letting the firms that can cut emissions most cheaply do most of the cutting.\",\"ex\":\"A firm that installs cleaner technology can sell its unused permits to a firm that finds cutting emissions more expensive.\",\"related\":[\"Carbon tax\",\"Command and control regulation\",\"Negative externality\",\"Pigouvian tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as a tax, which fixes price and lets quantity vary.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Trade creation\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"The replacement of higher-cost domestic production by lower-cost production from a bloc partner.\",\"plain\":\"Joining a trading bloc lets a country buy from a cheaper partner instead of from more expensive producers at home.\",\"why\":\"It is the efficiency gain from economic integration.\",\"ex\":\"After joining a bloc, a country stops producing high-cost shoes at home and imports cheaper shoes tariff-free from a partner.\",\"related\":[\"Trade diversion\",\"Customs union\",\"Free trade area\",\"Comparative advantage\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with trade diversion.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Trade diversion\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"The replacement of lower-cost production from outside a bloc by higher-cost production from inside it.\",\"plain\":\"Joining a trading bloc makes a country switch from a cheaper supplier outside the bloc to a more expensive one inside it.\",\"why\":\"It is the efficiency loss from economic integration, and it can outweigh trade creation.\",\"ex\":\"After joining a customs union, the tariff on outside suppliers makes a higher-cost partner’s goods cheaper to buy than those of the world’s lowest-cost producer.\",\"related\":[\"Trade creation\",\"Customs union\",\"Trading bloc\",\"Comparative advantage\"],\"confusion\":\"Presented as a benefit of integration.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term, HL only\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Trading bloc\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"A group of countries that agree to reduce or remove barriers to trade among themselves.\",\"plain\":\"A group of countries that trade with each other on better terms than with outsiders.\",\"why\":\"Blocs can widen markets and strengthen bargaining power, but they may divert trade away from lower-cost outsiders.\",\"ex\":\"The EU and the agreement between the USA, Mexico and Canada are trading blocs of different depths.\",\"related\":[\"Free trade area\",\"Customs union\",\"Common market\",\"Monetary union\",\"Trade diversion\"],\"confusion\":\"Treated as the same as multilateral free trade, although a bloc lowers barriers only among its own members.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 53\"},{\"term\":\"Tragedy of the commons\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The depletion of a rivalrous, non-excludable resource because individual users bear only part of the cost of their use.\",\"plain\":\"When everyone uses a shared resource for their own benefit, it can be used up, even though all would gain from holding back.\",\"why\":\"It explains the link between non-excludability and unsustainable resource use.\",\"ex\":\"Herders sharing open grazing land each add animals, because each gains the full benefit of an extra animal but shares its cost, until the land is overgrazed.\",\"related\":[\"Common pool resource\",\"Collective self-governance\",\"Negative externality\"],\"confusion\":\"Attributed to selfishness rather than to the absence of enforceable rights.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 36\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Transfer payment\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"A government payment for which no good or service is received in return.\",\"plain\":\"Money the government gives to people, such as pensions or benefits, without receiving any good or service in return.\",\"why\":\"Transfers are a direct tool for reducing poverty and inequality.\",\"ex\":\"Unemployment benefits and state pensions are transfer payments.\",\"related\":[\"Universal basic income\",\"Automatic stabiliser\",\"Aggregate demand\",\"Fiscal policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Counted in government spending within aggregate demand, where it does not belong.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Unemployment rate\",\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"def\":\"The number unemployed as a percentage of the labour force.\",\"plain\":\"The share of the labour force that wants work but cannot find it.\",\"why\":\"It is the headline measure of how well the labour market is using the people who want to work.\",\"ex\":\"If 3 million people are unemployed out of a labour force of 30 million, the unemployment rate is 10% (illustrative data).\",\"related\":[\"Labour force\",\"Natural rate of unemployment\",\"Cyclical unemployment\"],\"confusion\":\"Calculated as a percentage of the population.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"foundation\",\"deeper\":\"The guide asks about the difficulties of measuring unemployment (guide PDF p. 44): the official rate can miss discouraged workers, people working fewer hours than they want, and those in the informal economy.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 44\"},{\"term\":\"Universal basic income\",\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"def\":\"An unconditional periodic payment made to all individuals regardless of income or employment.\",\"plain\":\"A regular cash payment made to everyone, whatever their income or job status.\",\"why\":\"It is debated as a way to reduce poverty with less bureaucracy and weaker disincentives to work than means-tested benefits.\",\"ex\":\"Finland ran a two-year basic income trial that paid a monthly unconditional sum to a group of unemployed people.\",\"related\":[\"Transfer payment\",\"Relative poverty\",\"Absolute poverty\",\"Equity\"],\"confusion\":\"Evaluated only on cost, without the incentive and administration arguments.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 46\"},{\"term\":\"Unused capacity\",\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"def\":\"Productive resources, such as machinery, buildings or staff time, that a firm already has but is not fully using; also called spare capacity.\",\"plain\":\"Room for a firm to produce more with what it already has.\",\"why\":\"Firms with unused capacity can raise output quickly, so their supply tends to be more price elastic.\",\"ex\":\"A factory running only one shift a day can add a second shift if prices rise, without building a new plant.\",\"related\":[\"Price elasticity of supply\",\"Supply\",\"Factors of production\",\"Actual growth\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to be a sign of failure, when firms may deliberately keep spare capacity so that they can respond to changes in demand.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 34\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Utility\",\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"def\":\"The satisfaction derived from consuming a good or service.\",\"plain\":\"The satisfaction or benefit a person gets from using something.\",\"why\":\"Utility underlies diminishing marginal utility and the rational consumer model that behavioural economics critiques.\",\"ex\":\"A thirsty runner gets high utility from a first bottle of water and much less from a third.\",\"related\":[\"Law of diminishing marginal utility\",\"Behavioural economics\",\"Consumer surplus\",\"Demand\"],\"confusion\":\"Confused with usefulness, or compared between people as though one person’s satisfaction could be set against another’s.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 30, 31, 33\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The guide names utility in 1.2 as part of 19th-century classical microeconomics; using it to explain the demand curve and rational consumer choice is HL only (guide PDF pp. 30, 31, 33).\",\"new\":false},{\"term\":\"Value judgement\",\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"def\":\"A view about what is desirable, fair or better that rests on personal or social values and cannot be settled by evidence alone.\",\"plain\":\"An opinion about what is right or better, rather than a statement of fact.\",\"why\":\"Economists can predict the effects of a policy, but choosing between its winners and losers requires value judgements.\",\"ex\":\"Deciding that cheaper rents for sitting tenants matter more than longer waits for people looking for a flat is a value judgement about rent control.\",\"related\":[\"Normative economics\",\"Positive economics\",\"Equity\",\"Equality\"],\"confusion\":\"Assumed to have no place in good economics, when the problem is leaving value judgements unstated rather than making them.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF p. 29\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Wealth\",\"sub\":\"2.12\",\"def\":\"The value of the assets a person or household owns at a point in time, such as property, savings and shares, minus any debts; wealth is a stock, whereas income is a flow over a period.\",\"plain\":\"What you own, minus what you owe, as opposed to what you earn each month.\",\"why\":\"The guide explains that free markets can produce unequal distributions of both income and wealth, and wealth gaps can persist across generations.\",\"ex\":\"Two workers with the same salary can have very different wealth if one of them has inherited a house.\",\"related\":[\"Equity\",\"Equality\",\"Circular flow of income\",\"Direct tax\"],\"confusion\":\"Used interchangeably with income, when income is a flow received over a period and wealth is a stock held at a point in time.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 39, 45\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true},{\"term\":\"Welfare loss\",\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"def\":\"The loss of community surplus arising when output differs from the allocatively efficient level.\",\"plain\":\"The benefit society misses out on when the amount produced is not the best amount.\",\"why\":\"It measures the size of a market failure and the cost of leaving it uncorrected, which helps judge whether intervention is worthwhile.\",\"ex\":\"If a factory over-produces because it ignores the pollution it causes, the extra units cost society more than they are worth, creating a welfare loss.\",\"related\":[\"Social surplus\",\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Marginal social cost\",\"Marginal social benefit\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn between the wrong pair of curves: the triangle lies between MSB and MSC, stretching from the market output to the social optimum, where its point sits.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"guideRef\":\"guide PDF pp. 35–36, 38\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"Calculating welfare loss from a diagram is HL only (guide PDF p. 36).\",\"new\":false,\"alias\":[\"Deadweight loss\"]},{\"term\":\"World price\",\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"def\":\"The price at which a good is traded on international markets, which a small open economy takes as given.\",\"plain\":\"The price a good sells for on global markets.\",\"why\":\"Comparing the world price with the domestic price shows whether a country will import or export a good under free trade.\",\"ex\":\"If the world price of rice is below the price at which domestic farmers would supply the whole home market, the country imports rice.\",\"related\":[\"Tariff\",\"Quota\",\"Comparative advantage\",\"Export subsidy\"],\"confusion\":\"Drawn as a curve that shifts when domestic demand changes, although for a small country it is a horizontal line set by world markets.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":null,\"new\":true,\"ref\":\"guide PDF p. 51\"},{\"term\":\"World Trade Organization\",\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"def\":\"The multilateral institution that administers trade rules and settles disputes between members.\",\"plain\":\"The international body that sets rules for trade between its members and helps settle their trade disputes.\",\"why\":\"It is the main forum for agreeing multilateral trade rules and resolving trade disputes.\",\"ex\":\"A country that believes another has broken trade rules can bring a complaint to the WTO’s dispute settlement process.\",\"related\":[\"Protectionism\",\"Dumping\",\"Trading bloc\",\"Tariff\"],\"confusion\":\"Described as setting tariffs, which members do.\",\"ib\":\"Guide syllabus term\",\"diff\":\"core\",\"deeper\":\"The WTO was set up in 1995, succeeding the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade. Its influence depends on members reaching agreement, which has proved difficult in areas such as agriculture and services.\",\"new\":false,\"ref\":\"guide PDF pp. 53–54\"},{\"term\":\"Yield\",\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"def\":\"The return on a financial asset expressed as a percentage of its price.\",\"plain\":\"The yearly return you earn on an asset, shown as a percentage of what it costs.\",\"why\":\"Bond yields link central bank actions to the interest rates that households and firms actually pay.\",\"ex\":\"When a central bank buys government bonds, their price tends to rise and their yield tends to fall.\",\"related\":[\"Quantitative easing\",\"Open market operations\",\"Real interest rate\",\"Monetary policy\"],\"confusion\":\"Used interchangeably with the interest rate set by the central bank.\",\"ib\":\"Platform term\",\"diff\":\"deeper\",\"deeper\":\"Yield is not named in the 2022 guide; it is a platform term that helps explain the HL tools of monetary policy. For a bond that pays a fixed sum each year, the yield moves in the opposite direction to its price.\",\"new\":false}],\"compare\":[{\"id\":\"positive-normative\",\"terms\":[\"Positive economics\",\"Normative economics\"],\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"key\":\"Positive statements describe what is, or predict what will happen, and can be tested against evidence; normative statements say what ought to be and rest on value judgements.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Type of claim\",\"vals\":[\"Factual or predictive\",\"A value judgement\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Can evidence settle it?\",\"vals\":[\"In principle, yes\",\"Not on its own\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Typical wording\",\"vals\":[\"“will”, “causes”, “is associated with”\",\"“should”, “ought to”, “fair”, “too high”\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Example\",\"vals\":[\"“A higher minimum wage tends to raise the pay of low-paid workers who keep their jobs.”\",\"“The minimum wage should be raised to reduce poverty.”\"]}],\"trap\":\"Assuming a positive statement must be true: it only has to be testable, and the test may show it to be false.\",\"example\":\"“A tax on sugary drinks will reduce their consumption” is positive; “the government should tax sugary drinks” is normative.\"},{\"id\":\"demand-quantity-demanded\",\"terms\":[\"Demand\",\"Quantity demanded\"],\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"key\":\"Demand is the whole relationship between price and the amount buyers want; quantity demanded is one point on that relationship, at one price.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"What it is\",\"vals\":[\"The whole demand curve: the amounts consumers are willing and able to buy at each price\",\"The amount consumers are willing and able to buy at one particular price\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Changed by\",\"vals\":[\"Non-price determinants such as income, tastes or the prices of related goods\",\"A change in the good’s own price\"]},{\"aspect\":\"On the diagram\",\"vals\":[\"A shift of the whole curve\",\"A movement along the curve\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Correct wording\",\"vals\":[\"“Demand increased”\",\"“Quantity demanded increased”\"]}],\"trap\":\"Saying “demand fell” after the good’s own price rose: a price change causes a movement along the curve, not a shift.\",\"example\":\"If coffee becomes dearer, the quantity of coffee demanded falls; if a health report praises coffee, demand for coffee rises at every price.\"},{\"id\":\"normal-inferior\",\"terms\":[\"Normal good\",\"Inferior good\"],\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"key\":\"Demand for a normal good rises as income rises; demand for an inferior good falls as income rises, whatever the good’s quality.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Sign of YED\",\"vals\":[\"Positive\",\"Negative\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Demand when income rises\",\"vals\":[\"Increases (curve shifts right)\",\"Decreases (curve shifts left)\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Possible examples\",\"vals\":[\"Restaurant meals, foreign holidays\",\"Bus travel or cheap staple foods, for some consumers\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Depends on\",\"vals\":[\"The consumer and their income level\",\"The consumer and their income level\"]}],\"trap\":\"Defining an inferior good as a low-quality good: it is defined by the negative sign of YED.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: incomes rise by 10% and demand for instant noodles falls by 5%, so YED = −0.5 and noodles are an inferior good for these consumers.\"},{\"id\":\"allocative-productive\",\"terms\":[\"Allocative efficiency\",\"Productive efficiency\"],\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"key\":\"Allocative efficiency means producing the combination of goods that society values most; productive efficiency means producing whatever is produced at the lowest possible cost.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Condition\",\"vals\":[\"MSB = MSC (P = MC for a firm)\",\"Output at the minimum point of average total cost\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Question it answers\",\"vals\":[\"Are we producing the right goods in the right quantities?\",\"Are we producing them at the least cost?\"]},{\"aspect\":\"On a PPC\",\"vals\":[\"The point on the curve that society prefers\",\"Any point on the curve rather than inside it\"]},{\"aspect\":\"In the 2022 guide\",\"vals\":[\"Named in 2.3 and in HL 2.11 (guide PDF pp. 32, 38)\",\"Not named\"]}],\"trap\":\"Assuming that a productively efficient market must also be allocatively efficient: a firm can minimise cost while producing the wrong quantity.\",\"example\":\"A monopolist might produce at low average cost yet restrict output below the level where price equals marginal cost, so it is not allocatively efficient.\",\"note\":\"Productive efficiency is not named in the 2022 guide; it is included as background for market power (2.11, HL only).\"},{\"id\":\"adverse-selection-moral-hazard\",\"terms\":[\"Adverse selection\",\"Moral hazard\"],\"sub\":\"2.10\",\"key\":\"Adverse selection comes from hidden information before a transaction; moral hazard comes from hidden actions after it.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Timing\",\"vals\":[\"Before the transaction\",\"After the transaction\"]},{\"aspect\":\"What is hidden\",\"vals\":[\"Information about quality or risk\",\"Behaviour or effort\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Classic example\",\"vals\":[\"Mainly high-risk people buying insurance at an average premium\",\"Insured drivers taking less care\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Possible responses\",\"vals\":[\"Screening, signalling, compulsory insurance\",\"Excesses, monitoring, performance-related pay\"]}],\"trap\":\"Swapping the two: if the problem arises because someone behaves differently once they are protected, it is moral hazard, not adverse selection.\",\"example\":\"A health insurer charging one premium attracts mainly people who expect high medical bills (adverse selection); once insured, some take fewer precautions (moral hazard).\",\"note\":\"Subtopic 2.10 is HL only (guide PDF p. 26).\"},{\"id\":\"public-good-cpr\",\"terms\":[\"Public good\",\"Common pool resource\"],\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"key\":\"Both are non-excludable, but a public good is non-rivalrous and so is under-provided, whereas a common pool resource is rivalrous and so is over-used.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Rivalrous?\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"Yes\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Excludable?\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"No\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Market failure\",\"vals\":[\"Under-provision or no provision, because of free riders\",\"Over-use and depletion (the tragedy of the commons)\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Examples\",\"vals\":[\"Street lighting, national defence\",\"Ocean fish stocks, groundwater\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Typical responses\",\"vals\":[\"Government provision funded by taxation\",\"Quotas, tradable permits, community management\"]}],\"trap\":\"Calling any shared or government-run good a public good: if one person’s use leaves less for others, it is not a public good.\",\"example\":\"A lighthouse’s beam serves every passing ship at once (public good), while each tonne of fish caught leaves less for other boats (common pool resource).\"},{\"id\":\"tax-incidence\",\"terms\":[\"Consumer share of the tax\",\"Producer share of the tax\"],\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"key\":\"The incidence of an indirect tax is how its burden is split: consumers pay part through a higher price, producers bear part through a lower price received, and the more inelastic side of the market bears more.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Per unit, measured as\",\"vals\":[\"New price paid − original price\",\"Original price − price producers keep after tax\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Larger when\",\"vals\":[\"Demand is relatively inelastic or supply relatively elastic\",\"Supply is relatively inelastic or demand relatively elastic\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Area on the diagram\",\"vals\":[\"Consumers’ per-unit share × new quantity\",\"Producers’ per-unit share × new quantity\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Effect on surplus\",\"vals\":[\"Loss of consumer surplus\",\"Loss of producer surplus\"]}],\"trap\":\"Assuming the burden is split equally, or that whoever legally pays the tax to the government bears all of it.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: a tax of 2 per packet raises the price from 10 to 11.50 and producers keep 9.50, so consumers bear 1.50 and producers 0.50 of each 2.\",\"note\":\"The guide does not use the word ‘incidence’; it asks for the effects of indirect taxes on markets and stakeholders, with the related calculations HL only (guide PDF p. 36).\"},{\"id\":\"nominal-real-gdp\",\"terms\":[\"Nominal GDP\",\"Real GDP\"],\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"key\":\"Nominal GDP values output at current prices; real GDP removes the effect of price changes, so only real GDP shows changes in the quantity of output.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Prices used\",\"vals\":[\"Current prices of each year\",\"Constant prices of a base year\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Affected by inflation?\",\"vals\":[\"Yes\",\"No, by construction\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Main use\",\"vals\":[\"The size of the economy in money terms\",\"Measuring economic growth\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Conversion\",\"vals\":[\"Real GDP × price deflator ÷ 100\",\"Nominal GDP ÷ price deflator × 100\"]}],\"trap\":\"Calling a rise in nominal GDP “economic growth” when prices rose as fast as, or faster than, output.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: nominal GDP rises from 500 to 540 while the price deflator rises from 100 to 108, so real GDP stays at 500 and there has been no growth.\"},{\"id\":\"as-models\",\"terms\":[\"SRAS\",\"LRAS\",\"Keynesian AS curve\"],\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"key\":\"SRAS shows output responding to the price level while input prices are fixed; the monetarist/new classical LRAS is vertical at potential output; the Keynesian AS is flat when there is spare capacity and vertical at full employment.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Shape\",\"vals\":[\"Upward-sloping\",\"Vertical at potential output\",\"Horizontal, then upward-sloping, then vertical\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Shifted by\",\"vals\":[\"Costs of factors of production, indirect taxes\",\"Quantity and quality of factors, technology, efficiency, institutions\",\"The same factors as LRAS, over the long term\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Effect of a rise in AD\",\"vals\":[\"Output and the price level both rise in the short run\",\"Only the price level rises in the long run\",\"Output rises with little inflation if there is plenty of spare capacity\"]},{\"aspect\":\"School of thought\",\"vals\":[\"Introduced before the two schools; the monetarist/new classical model pairs it with LRAS, while the Keynesian model uses its single AS curve\",\"Monetarist/new classical: a deflationary gap closes as wages and prices adjust\",\"Keynesian: a deflationary gap can persist\"]}],\"trap\":\"Drawing LRAS as upward-sloping, or shifting LRAS for a change in the price level or in demand.\",\"example\":\"A rise in wages across the economy shifts SRAS left; a better-educated workforce shifts LRAS, or the vertical section of the Keynesian AS, to the right.\"},{\"id\":\"inflation-deflation-disinflation\",\"terms\":[\"Inflation\",\"Deflation\",\"Disinflation\"],\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"key\":\"Inflation and deflation describe the direction of the general price level, while disinflation describes a slowing of inflation, with prices still rising.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"General price level\",\"vals\":[\"Rises\",\"Falls\",\"Still rises, but more slowly\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Inflation rate\",\"vals\":[\"Positive\",\"Negative\",\"Positive but falling\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Possible causes\",\"vals\":[\"AD growing faster than capacity (demand-pull) or rising costs (cost-push)\",\"Falling AD, or rising SRAS from productivity gains\",\"Contractionary policy or easing cost pressures\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Main concern\",\"vals\":[\"Eroded purchasing power and uncertainty\",\"Delayed spending and a heavier real burden of debt\",\"Usually welcome if inflation was too high, though it may cost output in the short run\"]}],\"trap\":\"Reading a fall in the inflation rate as a fall in prices: if inflation drops from 5% to 2%, prices are still rising.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: a price index moves 100 → 105 → 107 → 106. Both of the first two years show inflation (the second also shows disinflation), and the third shows deflation.\"},{\"id\":\"demand-pull-cost-push\",\"terms\":[\"Demand-pull inflation\",\"Cost-push inflation\"],\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"key\":\"Demand-pull inflation starts with rising AD; cost-push inflation starts with rising costs that shift SRAS to the left.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Curve that shifts\",\"vals\":[\"AD shifts right\",\"SRAS shifts left\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Real output\",\"vals\":[\"Rises, towards or beyond potential\",\"Falls\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Unemployment\",\"vals\":[\"Tends to fall\",\"Tends to rise\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Possible triggers\",\"vals\":[\"A consumer or credit boom, a large rise in government spending\",\"Higher energy or wage costs, a depreciation that raises import costs\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Contractionary demand-side policy\",\"vals\":[\"Can reduce inflation and close the gap\",\"Lowers inflation but deepens the fall in output\"]}],\"trap\":\"Diagnosing the type of inflation from rising prices alone: the test is which curve moved first.\",\"example\":\"A surge in world energy prices raises costs across the economy (cost-push); a borrowing-fuelled spending boom near full capacity raises AD (demand-pull).\"},{\"id\":\"unemployment-types\",\"terms\":[\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"Structural unemployment\",\"Frictional unemployment\"],\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"key\":\"Cyclical unemployment comes from too little aggregate demand, structural unemployment from a mismatch between workers and jobs, and frictional unemployment from the time taken to move between jobs.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Cause\",\"vals\":[\"Deficient aggregate demand\",\"Mismatch of skills or location\",\"Search time between jobs\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Usual duration\",\"vals\":[\"As long as the downturn lasts\",\"Can be long-term\",\"Usually short\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Part of the natural rate?\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"Yes\",\"Yes\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Suitable policy\",\"vals\":[\"Expansionary demand-side policy\",\"Supply-side measures: retraining, help with moving\",\"Better information about vacancies\"]}],\"trap\":\"Recommending more demand to cure structural unemployment: extra spending does not give workers the skills that vacant jobs need.\",\"example\":\"Factory workers laid off in a recession (cyclical), printers replaced by digital publishing (structural), and a nurse who moves city and takes a month to start a new post (frictional).\"},{\"id\":\"equity-equality\",\"terms\":[\"Equity\",\"Equality\"],\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"key\":\"Equality describes whether things are shared identically; equity is a judgement about whether the sharing is fair.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Type of statement\",\"vals\":[\"Normative: a value judgement\",\"Positive: a description that can be measured\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Question it answers\",\"vals\":[\"Is the distribution fair?\",\"Is the distribution the same for everyone?\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Measured by\",\"vals\":[\"No single measure; it depends on the view of fairness\",\"Gini coefficient, Lorenz curve, income shares\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Policy link\",\"vals\":[\"Justifies redistribution such as progressive taxes\",\"Describes the outcome that redistribution changes\"]}],\"trap\":\"Treating the two as synonyms, as if a fair outcome must be an equal one.\",\"example\":\"Giving extra tutoring only to students who fall behind is unequal treatment that many people would judge equitable.\"},{\"id\":\"absolute-relative-poverty\",\"terms\":[\"Absolute poverty\",\"Relative poverty\"],\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"key\":\"Absolute poverty means lacking the income for basic needs, measured against a fixed line; relative poverty means having much less than is typical in your own society.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Benchmark\",\"vals\":[\"A fixed threshold, such as the international poverty line\",\"A share of median income in that country\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Changes as average income rises?\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"Yes\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Best for\",\"vals\":[\"Comparing extreme poverty across countries and over time\",\"Measuring exclusion within one society\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Effect of economic growth\",\"vals\":[\"Growth can reduce it substantially\",\"Growth alone may not reduce it; distribution matters\"]}],\"trap\":\"Comparing relative poverty rates between countries as if the threshold were the same everywhere.\",\"example\":\"A household in a high-income country may be in relative poverty even though its income is far above the international poverty line.\"},{\"id\":\"direct-indirect-tax\",\"terms\":[\"Direct tax\",\"Indirect tax\"],\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"key\":\"A direct tax is levied on income or wealth and paid by the person or firm taxed; an indirect tax is levied on spending and can be partly passed on to buyers.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Levied on\",\"vals\":[\"Income, profits or wealth\",\"Spending on goods and services\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Who bears the burden\",\"vals\":[\"Mainly the taxpayer on whom it is levied\",\"Shared between producers and consumers, depending on elasticities\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Examples\",\"vals\":[\"Personal income tax, corporate income tax, wealth tax\",\"VAT or GST, excise duties on fuel or tobacco\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Usual effect on income inequality\",\"vals\":[\"Often progressive\",\"Often regressive relative to income\"]}],\"trap\":\"Classifying a tax by who hands the money to the government, rather than by what is taxed: income, profits or wealth (direct) or spending (indirect).\",\"example\":\"A worker’s income tax is a direct tax; the VAT on the phone she buys is an indirect tax.\"},{\"id\":\"progressive-regressive-proportional\",\"terms\":[\"Progressive tax\",\"Regressive tax\",\"Proportional tax\"],\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"key\":\"The three are defined by what happens to the average tax rate as income rises: it rises, falls or stays the same.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Average tax rate as income rises\",\"vals\":[\"Rises\",\"Falls\",\"Stays constant\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Effect on income inequality\",\"vals\":[\"Reduces it\",\"Increases it\",\"Leaves relative shares unchanged\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Marginal rate compared with average rate\",\"vals\":[\"Marginal rate above average rate\",\"Marginal rate below average rate\",\"Marginal rate equals average rate\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Possible example\",\"vals\":[\"An income tax with rising bands\",\"A flat per-unit tax on basic goods, relative to income\",\"A flat-rate income tax with no allowance\"]}],\"trap\":\"Defining a progressive tax as one where richer people pay more money: under a proportional tax they also pay more, but the same percentage.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: on incomes of 20,000 and 100,000, taxes of 2,000 and 20,000 are progressive (10% v 20%); 2,000 and 10,000 are proportional (10% v 10%); 2,000 and 5,000 are regressive (10% v 5%).\"},{\"id\":\"monetary-fiscal\",\"terms\":[\"Monetary policy\",\"Fiscal policy\"],\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"key\":\"Both are demand-side policies, but monetary policy works through interest rates and the money supply, set by the central bank, while fiscal policy works through government spending and taxation.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Decided by\",\"vals\":[\"The central bank, often independent of government\",\"The government, through its budget\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Main instruments\",\"vals\":[\"Policy interest rate and money supply; HL only: open market operations, reserve requirements, quantitative easing\",\"Government spending, taxation, transfer payments\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Speed and flexibility\",\"vals\":[\"Can change quickly and in small steps, though effects take time\",\"Slower to decide and implement, but can target particular sectors\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Main constraints\",\"vals\":[\"Little room when rates are near zero; weak confidence\",\"Political pressure, time lags, sustainable debt; crowding out (HL only)\"]}],\"trap\":\"Attributing interest rate changes to the government, or tax changes to the central bank.\",\"example\":\"In a recession, the central bank may cut its policy rate (monetary policy) while the government raises spending on infrastructure (fiscal policy).\"},{\"id\":\"deficit-debt\",\"terms\":[\"Budget deficit\",\"National debt\"],\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"key\":\"The budget deficit is a flow over one year; the national debt is the stock of past borrowing, which grows whenever there is a deficit.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Stock or flow\",\"vals\":[\"Flow (per year)\",\"Stock (at a point in time)\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Measured as\",\"vals\":[\"Spending minus revenue in a year, often as a % of GDP\",\"Total outstanding government borrowing, often as a % of GDP\"]},{\"aspect\":\"How they are linked\",\"vals\":[\"Each year’s deficit adds to the debt\",\"Generally falls in money terms only when the budget is in surplus\"]},{\"aspect\":\"In the guide\",\"vals\":[\"Named in the HL content on debt; the idea is used in SL fiscal policy\",\"HL only as a topic (guide PDF p. 44); SL meets ‘sustainable debt’ as a constraint on fiscal policy (guide PDF p. 49)\"]}],\"trap\":\"Thinking a smaller deficit means the debt is falling: the debt still rises, only more slowly.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: a government with debt of 600 runs deficits of 50 and then 30, so its debt rises to 650 and then to 680.\"},{\"id\":\"absolute-comparative-advantage\",\"terms\":[\"Absolute advantage\",\"Comparative advantage\"],\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"key\":\"Absolute advantage compares how much each country can produce; comparative advantage compares opportunity costs, and it is comparative advantage that creates gains from trade.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Based on\",\"vals\":[\"Output per unit of resources\",\"Opportunity cost\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Can one country have it in every good?\",\"vals\":[\"Yes\",\"No: with different opportunity costs, each country has a comparative advantage in something\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Implication for trade\",\"vals\":[\"Not enough on its own to explain trade\",\"Specialising and trading on it can benefit both countries\"]},{\"aspect\":\"In the guide\",\"vals\":[\"HL only (guide PDF p. 51)\",\"HL only (guide PDF p. 51)\"]}],\"trap\":\"Identifying comparative advantage from who produces more, rather than from who gives up less.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: a worker in Country A makes 10 wheat or 20 cloth; a worker in Country B makes 2 wheat or 8 cloth. A has the absolute advantage in both, but 1 wheat costs A 2 cloth and B 4 cloth, so A has the comparative advantage in wheat and B in cloth.\"},{\"id\":\"tariff-quota\",\"terms\":[\"Tariff\",\"Quota\"],\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"key\":\"A tariff raises the price of imports through a tax, while a quota limits their quantity directly; both raise the domestic price, but the extra revenue goes to different people.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Instrument\",\"vals\":[\"A tax on each imported unit\",\"A physical limit on the quantity imported\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Where the revenue goes\",\"vals\":[\"The government\",\"Holders of import licences or foreign exporters, unless the government sells the licences\"]},{\"aspect\":\"If domestic demand rises\",\"vals\":[\"More imports enter at the tariff-inclusive price\",\"Imports cannot rise, so the domestic price rises further\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Welfare loss\",\"vals\":[\"Two deadweight-loss triangles\",\"Two deadweight-loss triangles, plus any quota rent that goes abroad\"]}],\"trap\":\"Showing government revenue on a quota diagram: that rectangle goes to licence holders unless the licences are auctioned.\",\"example\":\"A government can protect its car makers either by taxing each imported car or by allowing only a set number of foreign cars into the country each year.\"},{\"id\":\"integration-levels\",\"terms\":[\"Free trade area\",\"Customs union\",\"Common market\",\"Monetary union\"],\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"key\":\"Each stage adds a layer: free trade between members, then a common external tariff, then free movement of labour and capital, then a single currency and monetary policy.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Tariffs between members\",\"vals\":[\"Removed\",\"Removed\",\"Removed\",\"Removed\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Common external tariff\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"Yes\",\"Yes\",\"Usually, since it is normally built on a common market\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Free movement of labour and capital\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"No\",\"Yes\",\"Usually\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Shared currency and monetary policy\",\"vals\":[\"No\",\"No\",\"No\",\"Yes\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Example\",\"vals\":[\"USA, Mexico and Canada agreement\",\"Southern African Customs Union\",\"EU single market\",\"Euro area\"]}],\"trap\":\"Calling a free trade area a customs union: only a customs union has a common external tariff.\",\"example\":\"Hypothetical: in a free trade area, goods from outside could enter through the member with the lowest tariff and then move on duty-free, which is why such areas need rules of origin; a customs union’s common external tariff removes that incentive.\"},{\"id\":\"depreciation-devaluation\",\"terms\":[\"Depreciation\",\"Devaluation\"],\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"key\":\"Both are falls in a currency’s value, but depreciation is caused by market forces under a floating rate, while devaluation is a deliberate decision under a fixed rate.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Exchange rate system\",\"vals\":[\"Floating (or managed)\",\"Fixed\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Cause\",\"vals\":[\"Changes in the demand for and supply of the currency\",\"A decision by the government or central bank to set a lower rate\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Opposite\",\"vals\":[\"Appreciation\",\"Revaluation\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Effect on export prices abroad\",\"vals\":[\"Lower, ceteris paribus\",\"Lower, ceteris paribus\"]}],\"trap\":\"Calling a market-driven fall a devaluation, or a policy decision a depreciation.\",\"example\":\"Illustrative: the authorities reset a pegged currency from 10 to 12 units per US dollar (devaluation); a floating currency drifts from 15 to 17 units per dollar as investors sell it (depreciation).\"},{\"id\":\"bop-accounts\",\"terms\":[\"Current account\",\"Capital account\",\"Financial account\"],\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"key\":\"The current account records trade, income and transfer flows; the capital account records capital transfers and non-produced, non-financial assets; the financial account records investment, borrowing and reserve flows.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"Components\",\"vals\":[\"Trade in goods and services, income, current transfers\",\"Capital transfers, transactions in non-produced, non-financial assets\",\"FDI, portfolio investment, reserve assets, official borrowing\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Example entry\",\"vals\":[\"A country’s exports of wine\",\"Debt forgiveness by a foreign government\",\"A foreign firm building a factory\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Usual size\",\"vals\":[\"Large\",\"Usually small\",\"Large\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Link to the other accounts\",\"vals\":[\"A deficit must be matched by net inflows on the other accounts\",\"Often small enough to be discussed together with the financial account\",\"A surplus here usually mirrors a current account deficit\"]}],\"trap\":\"Placing FDI or portfolio investment in the capital account, when both belong in the financial account.\",\"example\":\"Hypothetical: a country imports more goods than it exports (current account deficit) and pays for this partly by selling government bonds to foreign investors (financial account inflow).\"},{\"id\":\"growth-development\",\"terms\":[\"Economic growth\",\"Economic development\"],\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"key\":\"Economic growth is an increase in real output; economic development is a broader improvement in living standards, to which growth may or may not contribute.\",\"rows\":[{\"aspect\":\"What changes\",\"vals\":[\"Real GDP (output)\",\"Health, education, income, freedom and equity\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Typical measure\",\"vals\":[\"Percentage change in real GDP\",\"Composite indicators such as the HDI, GII or MPI\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Number of dimensions\",\"vals\":[\"One\",\"Many\"]},{\"aspect\":\"Can it happen without the other?\",\"vals\":[\"Yes: output can rise while most people’s lives barely improve\",\"Some gains in health and education are possible at low income, but sustained development usually needs some growth\"]}],\"trap\":\"Using the two terms interchangeably, or answering a development question with growth analysis alone.\",\"example\":\"Hypothetical: a country’s oil exports double its real GDP in a decade, but life expectancy and school enrolment barely change, so growth has far outpaced development.\"}],\"ct\":[{\"term\":\"Analyse\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Break down in order to bring out the essential elements or structure.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Take an economic situation apart to show its key elements and how they connect — usually a cause-and-effect mechanism supported by a model and, often, a diagram. You show how and why; a final verdict is not what is being asked for.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify the change or shock at the heart of the question and the market or economy it affects.\",\"Choose the model that captures the mechanism (for example demand and supply, or AD/AS) and set out the starting position.\",\"Trace each link in order, naming which curve shifts or which variable changes, and why.\",\"Use a diagram to show the new outcome and refer to it in the text (P₁ to P₂, Q₁ to Q₂).\",\"Draw out the consequences for the variables or stakeholders the question names.\"],\"weak\":\"Lists effects (“prices rise, jobs are lost”) without showing the links between them, or includes a diagram that the writing does not use.\",\"strong\":\"Builds a complete causal chain from the initial change to each outcome, anchored in a named model and a diagram that the prose works through step by step.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Analyse the likely effects of a rise in the national minimum wage on a competitive labour market.\",\"outline\":[\"Define a minimum wage as a legal price floor for labour, binding when set above the market-clearing wage.\",\"Draw a labour market diagram with the minimum wage above equilibrium: quantity of labour supplied rises, quantity demanded falls.\",\"Explain that the gap represents unemployment in this model, while workers who keep their jobs earn more.\",\"Separate the stakeholders: employed low-wage workers, those who lose or cannot find work, firms facing higher costs, consumers who may face higher prices.\",\"Show how the size of these effects depends on the wage elasticity of labour demand.\"]},\"trap\":\"Drifting into evaluation and judgement that the question does not ask for, while leaving gaps in the mechanism itself.\",\"related\":[\"Explain\",\"Examine\",\"Distinguish\",\"Apply\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Apply\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Use an idea, equation, principle, theory or law in relation to a given problem or issue.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Take a concept, principle or model you know and use it to make sense of a specific situation — a named market, policy or set of data. The situation sets the agenda, not the theory.\",\"structure\":[\"Pick out the idea or model the question names or clearly implies.\",\"State it briefly and precisely, including any assumption that matters here.\",\"Map each element of the model onto the features of the situation: who, which market, which variable.\",\"Show what the model predicts or explains in this particular case, and where the fit is loose if that changes the result.\"],\"weak\":\"Explains the theory in general terms and adds one sentence mentioning the context, so the answer could fit any case.\",\"strong\":\"Uses the details of the situation — the product, the stakeholders, the figures — at each step, so the theory is visibly doing work on this problem.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Apply the concept of opportunity cost to a government’s decision to increase spending on public healthcare.\",\"outline\":[\"Define opportunity cost as the value of the next best alternative forgone.\",\"Identify the scarce resource: government revenue, and the labour and capital it commands, with competing uses.\",\"Name plausible alternatives forgone: education, infrastructure, lower taxes or lower borrowing.\",\"Use a PPC with healthcare on one axis to show that, at full employment, more healthcare means less of something else.\",\"Note that where resources are unemployed, the opportunity cost in the short run may be lower.\"]},\"trap\":\"Writing a textbook account of the theory and reaching the given situation only in the final line.\",\"related\":[\"Analyse\",\"Explain\",\"Suggest\",\"Comment\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Calculate\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Obtain a numerical answer showing the relevant stages in the working.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Work out a numerical answer and show the working. The steps matter as well as the final figure.\",\"structure\":[\"Write the formula or relationship you are using, in words or symbols.\",\"Substitute the values from the data, with their units.\",\"Show each intermediate step on its own line.\",\"State the final answer with units, sign and the precision requested.\",\"Check that the answer is plausible — the sign you expect, and a size that makes sense for the data.\"],\"weak\":\"Writes only a final number, drops units or percentage signs, or confuses a percentage change with a percentage-point change.\",\"strong\":\"Sets out formula, substitution and result so that a reader can follow and check each step, and labels the answer clearly.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a consumer price index is 120 in Year 1 and 126 in Year 2. Calculate the rate of inflation between the two years.\",\"outline\":[\"Formula: inflation rate = (CPI₂ − CPI₁) ÷ CPI₁ × 100.\",\"Substitute: (126 − 120) ÷ 120 × 100.\",\"Work: 6 ÷ 120 = 0.05; 0.05 × 100 = 5.\",\"Answer: prices rose by 5% between Year 1 and Year 2.\"]},\"trap\":\"Skipping the working, so that if the final figure is wrong there is nothing to show the method was sound.\",\"related\":[\"Show\",\"Show that\",\"Determine\",\"Solve\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Comment\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Give a judgment based on a given statement or result of a calculation.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Make a reasoned judgement about a given statement or a result you have just calculated: what does it mean, and does it matter here? It can be short, but it needs an economic reason.\",\"structure\":[\"Restate the statement or result precisely (for example, “PED = −0.5”).\",\"Interpret it with the relevant concept (an absolute value below 1 means demand is price-inelastic).\",\"Link the interpretation to the context: what it implies for the firm, consumer or government in the question.\",\"Add a brief qualification if the data or assumptions limit the conclusion.\"],\"weak\":\"Repeats the number or statement without saying what it means, or offers an opinion with no economic reason.\",\"strong\":\"Turns the figure or statement into a clear economic meaning and a consequence for the case, in two or three precise sentences.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a café raises the price of a coffee from $3.00 to $3.30 and weekly sales fall from 400 to 380 cups, giving a PED of −0.5. Comment on what this value suggests for the café’s pricing decision.\",\"outline\":[\"Interpret: quantity demanded responds less than proportionately to price, so demand is price-inelastic over this range.\",\"Link to revenue: with inelastic demand a price rise increases total revenue — here from $1,200 to $1,254 a week.\",\"Judge: on revenue grounds alone, the price rise looks worthwhile for the café.\",\"Qualify: the estimate covers one small change over a short period; demand may be more elastic if nearby rivals hold their prices, and profit also depends on costs.\"]},\"trap\":\"Treating “comment” as “restate”: repeating the figure without saying what it implies.\",\"related\":[\"Explain\",\"Suggest\",\"Evaluate\",\"Calculate\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Compare\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Give an account of the similarities between two (or more) items or situations, referring to both (all) of them throughout.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Set out the ways in which two or more items are alike, discussing them together throughout rather than one after the other. The official definition is about similarities; differences belong to “contrast”, or to “compare and contrast” when both are asked for.\",\"structure\":[\"Choose the criteria that matter for the question (for example effect on price, on domestic producers, on consumers).\",\"For each criterion, treat both items in the same paragraph and show how they produce a similar result.\",\"Explain why the similarity arises — the shared mechanism, supported by theory or a diagram.\",\"Say where a similarity holds only under certain conditions.\",\"Finish with an overall judgement on how alike the items are on the criteria that matter most.\"],\"weak\":\"Writes one paragraph on each item and leaves the reader to spot what they have in common.\",\"strong\":\"Organises the answer by criterion rather than by item, so each paragraph sets the items side by side and explains why they behave alike.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Compare the effects of a tariff and an import quota on domestic producers.\",\"outline\":[\"Domestic price: both raise the price in the domestic market above the world price.\",\"Domestic output: both let domestic producers supply more at the higher price — show on a tariff diagram and a quota diagram.\",\"Producer surplus: both increase the surplus of domestic producers.\",\"Incentives: both can weaken the pressure on protected firms to lower costs over time.\",\"Judgement: for domestic producers the two tools are closely alike; the main differences (revenue, and who captures the price gap) fall on other stakeholders.\"]},\"trap\":\"Writing two separate descriptions instead of a single, point-by-point comparison.\",\"related\":[\"Contrast\",\"Compare and contrast\",\"Distinguish\",\"Examine\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Compare and contrast\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Give an account of similarities and differences between two (or more) items or situations, referring to both (all) of them throughout.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Show both how the items are alike and how they differ, handling them together throughout. The value lies in explaining why the similarities and differences arise, and which of them matter most.\",\"structure\":[\"Set a small number of criteria relevant to the question.\",\"Under each criterion, state where the items are similar and where they differ, with the reason for each.\",\"Support the most important points with theory, a diagram or an example.\",\"Weigh which similarities or differences matter most in the given context.\",\"Conclude with a judgement that follows from the comparison.\"],\"weak\":\"Gives a block of similarities followed by a block of differences, or describes each item in turn, with no explanation of why they are alike or different.\",\"strong\":\"Works criterion by criterion, explains the source of each similarity and difference, and ends with a judgement about which are significant here.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Compare and contrast fixed and floating exchange rate systems. (HL only)\",\"outline\":[\"Define each: a floating rate is set by the demand for and supply of the currency; a fixed rate is held at a target by the central bank or government.\",\"Similarity: under both systems the exchange rate affects export competitiveness and the domestic price of imports.\",\"Difference in adjustment: a floating rate can depreciate to absorb a shock; a fixed rate needs reserves, interest-rate changes or intervention to hold it.\",\"Difference in certainty and policy freedom: a fixed rate reduces exchange-rate uncertainty for traders but limits the use of monetary policy for domestic goals.\",\"Judgement: which system suits an economy depends on factors such as its openness to trade, its reserves and the credibility of its policy-makers.\"]},\"trap\":\"Presenting two separate lists — similarities, then differences — instead of an integrated comparison with reasons.\",\"related\":[\"Compare\",\"Contrast\",\"Distinguish\",\"Evaluate\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Construct\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Display information in a diagrammatic or logical form.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Build a diagram, table or logical sequence from information you are given, so that the information is organised and easy to read.\",\"structure\":[\"Decide which form suits the information and what goes on each axis or in each column.\",\"Process the data if the display needs it (for example, turn shares into cumulative shares).\",\"Build the display accurately, with a title, labels and units.\",\"Check that it matches the data and would make sense to a reader who has not seen the question.\"],\"weak\":\"Plots raw figures without the processing the display needs, or leaves out axis labels and units.\",\"strong\":\"Processes the data correctly, presents it in a clean, labelled form and makes the key feature easy to see — for example, the gap between a Lorenz curve and the line of equality.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: from the poorest to the richest income quintile, households receive 5%, 10%, 15%, 25% and 45% of national income. Construct a Lorenz curve from these data. (HL only)\",\"outline\":[\"Convert to cumulative shares of income: 5, 15, 30, 55 and 100.\",\"Axes: cumulative % of population (horizontal) and cumulative % of income (vertical), each from 0 to 100.\",\"Draw the diagonal line of perfect equality.\",\"Plot the origin and the five cumulative points, and join them with a smooth curve below the diagonal.\",\"Label the Lorenz curve, the line of equality and both axes.\"]},\"trap\":\"Plotting each quintile’s own share instead of the cumulative share.\",\"related\":[\"Draw\",\"Plot\",\"Label\",\"Sketch\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). The guide expects correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams where appropriate (guide PDF p. 65). The syllabus lists constructing a Lorenz curve from income quintile data as HL only (guide PDF p. 45).\"},{\"term\":\"Contrast\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Give an account of the differences between two (or more) items or situations, referring to both (all) of them throughout.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Set out the differences between two or more items, handling them together throughout, and explain why they differ.\",\"structure\":[\"Pick criteria on which the items genuinely differ.\",\"For each criterion, state how each item behaves, side by side.\",\"Explain the reason for each difference — an assumption, a mechanism or the context.\",\"Indicate which differences matter most for the question and conclude on that basis.\"],\"weak\":\"Describes each item separately, or states differences without explaining what causes them.\",\"strong\":\"Makes each paragraph a direct contrast on one criterion and explains the economic reason behind the difference.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Contrast the Keynesian and the monetarist/new classical views of how an economy returns to full employment after a recession.\",\"outline\":[\"Shape of aggregate supply: a vertical LRAS at potential output (new classical) against a Keynesian AS curve with horizontal, upward-sloping and vertical sections.\",\"Wage and price flexibility: flexible wages and prices restore full employment (new classical) against sticky wages and prices that can let a deflationary gap persist (Keynesian).\",\"Speed of adjustment: relatively quick self-correction against a possibly long period of spare capacity.\",\"Policy implication: a limited role for demand management against a case for expansionary policy.\",\"Judgement: the contrast rests largely on assumptions about how flexible wages and prices are in practice.\"]},\"trap\":\"Listing the features of each model in turn without ever setting them against each other.\",\"related\":[\"Compare\",\"Compare and contrast\",\"Distinguish\",\"Examine\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Define\",\"ao\":\"AO1\",\"def\":\"Give the precise meaning of a word, phrase, concept or physical quantity.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Give the precise meaning of an economic term — accurate, complete and short. Precision matters more than length.\",\"structure\":[\"Name the category the term belongs to: a measure, a policy, a type of good, a market outcome.\",\"State the feature or mechanism that separates it from similar terms.\",\"Add any condition the meaning depends on, such as “other things being equal” or “over a period of time”.\",\"Give the formula where a formula is part of the meaning.\"],\"weak\":\"Gives an example instead of a meaning, uses the term to define itself, or offers a description that would also fit a related term.\",\"strong\":\"Gives a one- or two-sentence definition that a reader could use to tell the term apart from its nearest neighbours.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Define price elasticity of supply.\",\"outline\":[\"Category: a measure of responsiveness.\",\"Feature: of the quantity supplied of a good to a change in its own price.\",\"Form: the percentage change in quantity supplied divided by the percentage change in price.\",\"Condition: other things being equal.\"]},\"trap\":\"Writing a circular definition, such as “inflation is when prices inflate”.\",\"related\":[\"State\",\"Describe\",\"Outline\",\"Distinguish\"],\"papers\":\"AO1 is listed for every part of Paper 1 and Paper 2 and for both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). A question may use a command term from the AO level set for the syllabus content or a less demanding one (guide PDF p. 65), so AO1 terms can be attached to content set at AO1, AO2 or AO3. The guide expects students to define, use and apply the economic terms in the syllabus (guide PDF p. 66).\"},{\"term\":\"Derive\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Manipulate a mathematical relationship to give a new equation or relationship.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Rearrange or manipulate a known relationship to produce a new equation or expression, and use it if the question asks.\",\"structure\":[\"Write down the starting relationship exactly.\",\"State what you want to isolate or express.\",\"Rearrange one step at a time, keeping both sides balanced.\",\"Present the new relationship clearly and, where data are given, use it to reach a labelled result.\"],\"weak\":\"Jumps to the final expression with no steps, or rearranges incorrectly — for example, by inverting a ratio.\",\"strong\":\"Shows each algebraic step, ends with a clean expression and, where data are provided, uses it to reach a correct, labelled result.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Starting from the formula for PED, derive an expression for the percentage change in quantity demanded. Use it to find the effect of a 5% price rise when PED is −0.4. (Illustrative data)\",\"outline\":[\"Start: PED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔP.\",\"Multiply both sides by %ΔP: %ΔQd = PED × %ΔP.\",\"Substitute: %ΔQd = −0.4 × 5 = −2.\",\"Interpret: quantity demanded falls by 2%.\"]},\"trap\":\"Inverting the ratio while rearranging, so the answer is the reciprocal of the correct one.\",\"related\":[\"Calculate\",\"Show\",\"Solve\",\"Show that\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Describe\",\"ao\":\"AO1\",\"def\":\"Give a detailed account.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Give a detailed account of what something is or what happens. The focus is on what, not why.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify what is to be described: a trend in data, a process, a feature of a model.\",\"Set out its main features or stages in a logical order.\",\"Use precise terms and, where data are given, specific figures with units.\",\"Stop short of explaining causes unless the question asks for them too.\"],\"weak\":\"Is so brief that it becomes a list, or drifts into explanation the question does not ask for.\",\"strong\":\"Covers the key features in order, uses accurate terms and, with data, quotes the relevant values and the direction of change.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Describe the phases of the business cycle.\",\"outline\":[\"Frame: short-term fluctuations in real GDP around a long-term growth trend.\",\"Expansion: real GDP grows, often with falling unemployment.\",\"Peak: the high point of the cycle, where output may be above potential.\",\"Contraction: real GDP falls (a recession if the fall is sustained, often taken as two consecutive quarters); the trough is the low point.\",\"Note that actual output can sit above or below potential output in different phases.\"]},\"trap\":\"Explaining why each phase occurs instead of describing what happens in it.\",\"related\":[\"Outline\",\"Explain\",\"State\",\"Define\"],\"papers\":\"AO1 is listed for every part of Paper 1 and Paper 2 and for both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). A question may use a command term from the AO level set for the syllabus content or a less demanding one (guide PDF p. 65), so AO1 terms can be attached to content set at AO1, AO2 or AO3.\"},{\"term\":\"Determine\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Obtain the only possible answer.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Find the single correct answer, usually by applying a rule or reading a model correctly.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify the rule or condition that pins down the answer (for example, profit is maximised where MC = MR).\",\"Apply it systematically to the data or diagram.\",\"State the answer clearly, with units.\",\"Where it helps, show the check that confirms it is the only answer.\"],\"weak\":\"Guesses from the pattern of the numbers, or applies the wrong rule — for example, choosing the output where total revenue is highest.\",\"strong\":\"Names the correct rule, applies it step by step and states one clear answer.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a table gives a firm’s marginal cost and marginal revenue at outputs from 1 to 8 units. Determine the profit-maximising level of output. (HL only)\",\"outline\":[\"State the rule: profit is maximised where marginal cost equals marginal revenue (MC = MR).\",\"Scan the table for the output where MC = MR; if no output gives exact equality, take the last unit for which MR is at least as large as MC.\",\"Check: below that output MR > MC, so producing more adds to profit; above it MC > MR.\",\"State the output as the answer, in units.\"]},\"trap\":\"Choosing the output with the highest total revenue or profit per unit instead of applying MC = MR.\",\"related\":[\"Calculate\",\"Identify\",\"Solve\",\"Measure\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Discuss\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Offer a considered and balanced review that includes a range of arguments, factors or hypotheses. Opinions or conclusions should be presented clearly and supported by appropriate evidence.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Build a balanced review that considers a range of arguments or factors, then reach a clear conclusion that your evidence supports.\",\"structure\":[\"Define the key terms and pin down the issue the question poses.\",\"Develop the main case with theory, a diagram where relevant and a real-world example.\",\"Develop the counter-case or other factors with the same care.\",\"Weigh them: which arguments are stronger, for whom, under what conditions and over what time frame?\",\"Conclude with a clear position that follows from the weighing.\"],\"weak\":\"Presents one side only, or lists points for and against with no weighing and no clear conclusion.\",\"strong\":\"Develops several arguments with theory and evidence, weighs them against each other and reaches a conclusion that depends explicitly on stated conditions.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Discuss whether a depreciation of its currency is likely to improve an economy’s living standards.\",\"outline\":[\"Define depreciation and link it to lower export prices abroad and higher import prices at home.\",\"Case for: stronger demand for exports and a switch to domestic goods can raise AD, output and employment, especially with spare capacity.\",\"Case against: dearer imports raise the cost of living and firms’ input costs, which can reduce real incomes and add to inflation.\",\"Weigh: the outcome depends on the price elasticities of demand for exports and imports, the time frame (J-curve reasoning, HL only) and the share of imported essentials in household spending.\",\"Conclude: a gain is more likely with spare capacity and price-elastic export demand; a loss is more likely where imported food and fuel take a large share of household budgets.\"]},\"trap\":\"Mistaking a list of pros and cons for a discussion: without weighing, there is no basis for the conclusion.\",\"related\":[\"Evaluate\",\"To what extent\",\"Examine\",\"Justify\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65). The Paper 1 part (b) markband also looks for developed real-world examples, and the Paper 2 part (g) markband for use of the text/data (guide PDF pp. 69–71).\"},{\"term\":\"Distinguish\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Make clear the differences between two or more concepts or items.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Make clear how two or more concepts differ, precisely enough that they cannot be confused.\",\"structure\":[\"Define each concept briefly.\",\"State the feature that separates them in a direct sentence.\",\"Illustrate the difference with a diagram or an example.\",\"Add a second point of difference if it sharpens the distinction.\"],\"weak\":\"Defines both terms separately but does not state the difference, or gives a difference that is not the essential one.\",\"strong\":\"Names the essential difference directly, supports it with a diagram or example and uses precise terms for each concept.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Distinguish between a movement along a demand curve and a shift of the demand curve.\",\"outline\":[\"Movement along: caused by a change in the good’s own price; quantity demanded changes.\",\"Shift: caused by a change in a non-price determinant such as income, tastes or the price of a related good; demand changes at every price.\",\"Diagram: a move from point A to point B as price changes from P₁ to P₂, and a separate diagram with D₁ shifting to D₂.\",\"Terms: “a change in quantity demanded” against “a change in demand”.\"]},\"trap\":\"Describing each concept in turn without ever stating the difference in a direct sentence.\",\"related\":[\"Compare\",\"Contrast\",\"Define\",\"Explain\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Draw\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Represent by means of a labelled, accurate diagram or graph, using a pencil. A ruler (straight edge) should be used for straight lines. Diagrams should be drawn to scale. Graphs should have points correctly plotted (if appropriate) and joined in a straight line or smooth curve.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Produce an accurate, fully labelled diagram or graph: straight lines ruled, and correct scale and plotting where data are given.\",\"structure\":[\"Choose the right diagram for the question and give it a title.\",\"Label both axes with the variables they measure, and units where relevant.\",\"Draw and label every curve, the original and new equilibrium, and any area you will discuss.\",\"Show the change with arrows and subscripts (P₁, P₂; Q₁, Q₂).\",\"Refer to the diagram in the writing, naming its points and areas.\"],\"weak\":\"Leaves axes or curves unlabelled, draws the wrong diagram, or includes a diagram that the writing does not mention.\",\"strong\":\"Draws a clean, correctly labelled diagram that matches the text exactly and uses it as part of the argument.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Draw a diagram to show the effect of a specific tax on a good for which demand is price-inelastic.\",\"outline\":[\"Title the diagram; label the axes price and quantity.\",\"Draw a relatively steep demand curve and a flatter (relatively more elastic) original supply curve S; mark P₁ and Q₁.\",\"Shift supply vertically upward by the tax per unit to S + tax, parallel to S; mark the new price P₂ and quantity Q₂.\",\"Mark the price producers keep after the tax and shade tax revenue, split into the consumer and producer shares.\",\"Show that most of the burden falls on consumers because demand is more price-inelastic than supply.\"]},\"trap\":\"Drawing a correct diagram and then not referring to it anywhere in the answer.\",\"related\":[\"Sketch\",\"Label\",\"Construct\",\"Plot\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). The guide expects correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams where appropriate (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Evaluate\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Make an appraisal by weighing up the strengths and limitations.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 80\",\"asks\":\"Make an appraisal: set the strengths of a policy, theory or claim against its limitations and reach a supported judgement about its overall value.\",\"structure\":[\"Define the key terms and state the aim or criterion you will judge against (for example efficiency, equity or sustainability).\",\"Explain how the policy or idea is meant to work, with theory and a diagram.\",\"Set out its strengths, with evidence or examples.\",\"Set out its limitations: size of effect, conditions such as elasticity, time frame, unintended consequences, stakeholders.\",\"Reach a judgement that weighs the two and states the conditions under which it holds.\"],\"weak\":\"Adds a short “however” paragraph at the end, or lists advantages and disadvantages without deciding which matter more.\",\"strong\":\"Weaves appraisal through the answer, prioritises the most significant strengths and limitations, and reaches a conditional, context-specific judgement.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Evaluate the use of a maximum price (a rent ceiling) in the market for rented housing in a large city.\",\"outline\":[\"Define a maximum price and draw a ceiling below equilibrium, showing the shortage (Qd > Qs).\",\"Strengths: lower rents for existing tenants, which can serve equity goals and ease housing costs for lower-income households.\",\"Limitations: shortage and non-price rationing, possible black markets, and a risk that the quantity or quality of rental housing falls over time as supply becomes more price-elastic.\",\"Stakeholders: sitting tenants, would-be renters, landlords and the government.\",\"Judgement: more defensible as a short-term measure alongside policies that raise housing supply; weaker as a long-run fix where supply responds strongly over time.\"]},\"trap\":\"Confusing balance with evaluation: two lists of points are not an appraisal until they are weighed.\",\"related\":[\"Discuss\",\"To what extent\",\"Justify\",\"Recommend\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65). The Paper 1 part (b) markband also looks for developed real-world examples, and the Paper 2 part (g) markband for use of the text/data (guide PDF pp. 69–71).\"},{\"term\":\"Examine\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Consider an argument or concept in a way that uncovers the assumptions and interrelationships of the issue.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Look closely at an argument or concept to bring out the assumptions it rests on and how its parts depend on one another — and so how far it holds.\",\"structure\":[\"State the argument or concept precisely.\",\"Identify the assumptions it depends on, stated or unstated.\",\"Trace the interrelationships: how one variable or group affects another, including feedback effects.\",\"Test what happens if a key assumption does not hold.\",\"Conclude on how robust the argument is, and in which circumstances.\"],\"weak\":\"Explains the argument as if every assumption holds, without questioning what it takes for granted.\",\"strong\":\"Brings the hidden assumptions to the surface, shows how the outcome changes when they are relaxed and links the parts of the issue together.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Examine the view that raising interest rates is an effective way to reduce inflation.\",\"outline\":[\"Mechanism: higher rates raise borrowing costs, reduce consumption and investment, may strengthen the currency, and so reduce AD and demand-pull pressure.\",\"Assumption 1 — the type of inflation: against cost-push inflation, higher rates may cut output with a smaller effect on prices.\",\"Assumption 2 — transmission: rate changes must pass through to lending rates and spending; weak confidence or already-high saving can weaken the link.\",\"Interrelationships: time lags, the exchange-rate effect on import prices, and the effects on government debt interest and on the investment that builds long-run supply.\",\"Conclusion: most effective against demand-pull inflation where transmission works; less effective, and more costly in lost output, against supply shocks.\"]},\"trap\":\"Treating “examine” as “explain”, without questioning the assumptions.\",\"related\":[\"Evaluate\",\"Analyse\",\"Discuss\",\"To what extent\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Explain\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Give a detailed account including reasons or causes.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Give a detailed account that includes the reasons or causes — not only what happens, but why and how.\",\"structure\":[\"Define the key terms the explanation depends on.\",\"Set out the causal chain one link at a time, connecting the steps with “because” and “so”.\",\"Use a diagram where it helps and refer to its shifts and points in the text.\",\"Tie the chain to the context the question gives.\"],\"weak\":\"States an outcome (“the subsidy lowers the price”) without the mechanism, or skips links in the chain.\",\"strong\":\"Builds a complete, connected chain from cause to effect, tied to a diagram and to the context in the question.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Explain, using an AD/AS diagram, how a rise in consumer confidence could affect real GDP and the average price level.\",\"outline\":[\"Define aggregate demand and consumer confidence.\",\"Chain: higher confidence → households expect higher future incomes → consumption rises → AD shifts right.\",\"Diagram: AD₁ to AD₂ against SRAS; real GDP rises from Y₁ to Y₂ and the price level from P₁ to P₂.\",\"Note that the split between higher output and higher prices depends on how close the economy is to full capacity.\"]},\"trap\":\"Describing what the diagram shows instead of explaining why the curves move.\",\"related\":[\"Describe\",\"Analyse\",\"Outline\",\"Suggest\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Identify\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Provide an answer from a number of possibilities.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Pick out the correct answer from a number of possibilities — in data, a diagram or a set of options. No explanation is needed unless the question asks for one.\",\"structure\":[\"Work out exactly what is being sought: which variable, which period, which area.\",\"Scan the data or diagram systematically, applying the relevant definition.\",\"State the answer precisely, with the value, period or label where relevant.\"],\"weak\":\"Picks an answer that fits a loose reading of the term, or writes a paragraph where a precise answer is enough.\",\"strong\":\"Applies the definition correctly and gives a short, exact answer that can be checked against the source.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a table shows the annual inflation rate for five economies over three years. Identify the economy that experienced deflation in the most recent year.\",\"outline\":[\"Apply the definition: deflation is a sustained fall in the average price level, shown by a negative inflation rate.\",\"Scan the most recent year for a negative value.\",\"Name that economy and quote its figure.\",\"Do not pick an economy whose inflation rate fell but stayed positive — that is disinflation.\"]},\"trap\":\"Answering from a loose sense of the term rather than its precise definition — for example, taking disinflation for deflation.\",\"related\":[\"State\",\"Determine\",\"Measure\",\"Label\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68).\"},{\"term\":\"Justify\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Give valid reasons or evidence to support an answer or conclusion.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Give valid economic reasons or evidence that support an answer or conclusion — show why the position is warranted.\",\"structure\":[\"State the answer or conclusion to be supported.\",\"Give the main reasons, each grounded in theory, data or evidence.\",\"Show why these reasons outweigh the most obvious objection.\",\"Tie the reasoning to the context in the question.\"],\"weak\":\"Repeats the conclusion in different words, or gives reasons that are asserted rather than explained.\",\"strong\":\"Supports the conclusion with a small number of well-developed reasons and evidence, and deals with the strongest objection.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Justify the funding of street lighting by government rather than leaving it to private firms.\",\"outline\":[\"Define a public good as non-rivalrous and non-excludable.\",\"Reason 1: street lighting is non-excludable, so a firm cannot charge users; free riding means the market is likely to under-provide it or not provide it at all.\",\"Reason 2: it is non-rivalrous, so an extra user adds almost nothing to cost; charging users, even if possible, would reduce welfare.\",\"Objection: public provision needs tax revenue and may be run inefficiently; contracting out delivery while keeping public funding can address this.\",\"Conclusion: public funding is justified; who delivers the service is a separate choice.\"]},\"trap\":\"Asserting that the conclusion is right without developing the reasons that support it.\",\"related\":[\"Recommend\",\"Evaluate\",\"Explain\",\"Discuss\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Label\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Add labels to a diagram.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Add the correct names to the parts of a diagram — axes, curves, points and areas — so that it can be read on its own.\",\"structure\":[\"Label both axes with the variables they measure.\",\"Name every curve with standard notation (for example MPC, MSC, MPB, MSB).\",\"Mark equilibrium points and quantities with subscripts or standard symbols.\",\"Shade and name any area the answer refers to.\"],\"weak\":\"Leaves curves unlabelled or uses non-standard names, so the diagram cannot be read without the text.\",\"strong\":\"Labels every element with standard, consistent notation that matches the words used in the answer.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Label a diagram showing a negative consumption externality in the market for a demerit good.\",\"outline\":[\"Axes: price, costs and benefits (vertical); quantity (horizontal).\",\"Curves: MPC = MSC for supply; MPB above MSB for demand.\",\"Quantities: Qm where MPB = MPC, and Qopt where MSB = MSC, with the matching prices.\",\"Area: the welfare loss triangle between MSC and MSB from Qopt to Qm, pointing towards the social optimum.\"]},\"trap\":\"Using improvised names (“line 1”, “new curve”) that do not match the terms used in the answer.\",\"related\":[\"Draw\",\"Sketch\",\"Construct\",\"Identify\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). The guide expects correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams where appropriate (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"List\",\"ao\":\"AO1\",\"def\":\"Give a sequence of brief answers with no explanation.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Give a sequence of brief answers with no explanation — accurate items, one after another.\",\"structure\":[\"Check how many items are asked for.\",\"Choose distinct items that do not overlap.\",\"Write each as a short phrase using the correct term.\"],\"weak\":\"Gives overlapping items or fewer than asked, or adds explanation the question does not ask for.\",\"strong\":\"Gives the required number of distinct, correctly named items and moves on.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"List three determinants of the price elasticity of demand.\",\"outline\":[\"Any three of the following, each as a short phrase:\",\"Number and closeness of substitutes.\",\"Degree of necessity.\",\"Proportion of income spent on the good.\",\"Time period considered.\"]},\"trap\":\"Giving two versions of the same idea (“many substitutes” and “close substitutes”) as separate items.\",\"related\":[\"State\",\"Identify\",\"Outline\",\"Describe\"],\"papers\":\"AO1 is listed for every part of Paper 1 and Paper 2 and for both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). A question may use a command term from the AO level set for the syllabus content or a less demanding one (guide PDF p. 65), so AO1 terms can be attached to content set at AO1, AO2 or AO3.\"},{\"term\":\"Measure\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Obtain a value for a quantity.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Obtain a value for a quantity — typically by reading it from a diagram, graph or scale — accurately and with units.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify the quantity and the unit it is measured in.\",\"Read the relevant values carefully from the scale or grid.\",\"Work out the difference or value if needed, and state it with units and direction.\"],\"weak\":\"Reads the wrong axis or point, rounds carelessly, or leaves out the unit.\",\"strong\":\"Reads the values precisely, shows any simple subtraction and states the result with units and direction.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: a line graph shows the value of a currency in US dollars over twelve months. Measure the change in the exchange rate between the first and the last month.\",\"outline\":[\"Read the rate at month 1 and at month 12 from the vertical axis.\",\"Subtract to find the absolute change, in US dollars per unit of the currency.\",\"State the direction: more dollars per unit means the currency has appreciated against the dollar.\",\"If the question asks, express the change as a percentage of the starting value.\"]},\"trap\":\"Giving a change without its direction or units, so it is unclear whether the currency rose or fell.\",\"related\":[\"Calculate\",\"Identify\",\"Determine\",\"Plot\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Outline\",\"ao\":\"AO1\",\"def\":\"Give a brief account or summary.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Give a brief account or summary of the main points — more than a list, less than an explanation.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify the points the question asks for, often a stated number.\",\"Give each point in one or two clear sentences, using the key term correctly.\",\"Keep development brief; a full causal chain is not needed.\"],\"weak\":\"Gives single words with no account, or writes a long explanation that the question does not ask for.\",\"strong\":\"Covers each point with a concise, accurate sentence or two that shows understanding.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Outline two limitations of GDP as a measure of economic well-being.\",\"outline\":[\"Limitation 1: it leaves out non-market activity such as unpaid care, household work and volunteering.\",\"Limitation 2: it says nothing about how income is distributed between households.\",\"Give each point in one or two sentences with the correct terms; a diagram or long example is not needed.\"]},\"trap\":\"Over-developing an outline into a long explanation and running short of time elsewhere.\",\"related\":[\"Describe\",\"State\",\"List\",\"Explain\"],\"papers\":\"AO1 is listed for every part of Paper 1 and Paper 2 and for both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). A question may use a command term from the AO level set for the syllabus content or a less demanding one (guide PDF p. 65), so AO1 terms can be attached to content set at AO1, AO2 or AO3.\"},{\"term\":\"Plot\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Mark the position of points on a diagram.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Mark the position of points on a diagram accurately from given data.\",\"structure\":[\"Set up the axes with the correct variables and an even, sensible scale.\",\"Mark each point precisely at its coordinates.\",\"Join the points only if the question or data call for a line or curve.\",\"Label the points or line if they represent a named schedule.\"],\"weak\":\"Uses an uneven scale, misreads coordinates, or swaps the axes.\",\"strong\":\"Uses a clear scale, plots each point accurately and labels what the points represent.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: at prices of $2, $4, $6 and $8, quantity demanded is 80, 60, 40 and 20 units, and quantity supplied is 20, 40, 60 and 80 units. Plot the demand and supply schedules and mark the equilibrium.\",\"outline\":[\"Axes: price on the vertical axis, quantity on the horizontal, with even scales.\",\"Plot the four demand points and join them to form D; do the same for S.\",\"Mark the intersection: an equilibrium price of $5 and quantity of 50 units.\",\"Label D, S, the equilibrium price and the equilibrium quantity.\"]},\"trap\":\"Putting price on the horizontal axis, which reverses the standard layout of a demand and supply diagram.\",\"related\":[\"Draw\",\"Construct\",\"Label\",\"Sketch\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). The guide expects correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams where appropriate (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Recommend\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Present an advisable course of action with appropriate supporting evidence/reason in relation to a given situation, problem or issue.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Put forward a course of action for a given situation and support it with economic reasoning and with evidence from the information provided.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify the problem and the objective the policy should achieve, using the data.\",\"Choose one policy and explain how it works, with theory and, where it helps, a diagram.\",\"Support the choice with evidence from the data and the context.\",\"Consider trade-offs, stakeholders, costs and the time frame, and say why the main alternative is less suitable.\",\"Close with a clear recommendation and the conditions under which it is most likely to succeed.\"],\"weak\":\"Names a policy, or several, with little theory, no use of the data and no consideration of costs or alternatives.\",\"strong\":\"Recommends one well-chosen policy, grounds it in theory and the data provided, weighs its costs and alternatives, and states a clear, conditional recommendation.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative context: a mid-sized city reports that average peak-hour traffic speeds have fallen steadily for five years, while use of public transport has stayed flat. Recommend a policy the city government could use to reduce road congestion.\",\"outline\":[\"Diagnose: congestion as a negative externality — each driver does not pay the delay cost imposed on other road users.\",\"Recommend a peak-time congestion charge; use a negative externality diagram to show how pricing the external cost moves road use towards the social optimum.\",\"Use the data: flat public transport use suggests commuters lack a good alternative, so pairing the charge with better public transport would make demand for car travel more price-elastic.\",\"Weigh costs and stakeholders: the burden on lower-income commuters, effects on city-centre businesses and enforcement costs; compare briefly with building more road capacity.\",\"Conclude: recommend the charge, with its revenue used to improve public transport and its level reviewed over time.\"]},\"trap\":\"Recommending several policies superficially instead of arguing one policy in depth.\",\"related\":[\"Justify\",\"Evaluate\",\"Suggest\",\"Discuss\"],\"papers\":\"HL Paper 3 part (b) is a Recommend question; the guide prints this definition with its markband (guide PDF p. 71). AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) (guide PDF pp. 66–68), and AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Show\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Give the steps in a calculation or derivation.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Set out the steps of a calculation or derivation so that a reader can follow how the result is reached.\",\"structure\":[\"State the starting formula or relationship.\",\"Substitute the values, one step per line.\",\"Carry the working through to the result, keeping units consistent.\",\"State the result clearly.\"],\"weak\":\"Writes the answer with steps missing, or in an order that cannot be followed.\",\"strong\":\"Sets out every step in a logical order, with formula, substitution and result all visible.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: nominal GDP is $500 billion and the GDP deflator is 125 (base year = 100). Show how real GDP is calculated.\",\"outline\":[\"Formula: real GDP = nominal GDP ÷ GDP deflator × 100.\",\"Substitute: 500 ÷ 125 × 100.\",\"Work: 500 ÷ 125 = 4; 4 × 100 = 400.\",\"Result: real GDP is $400 billion at base-year prices.\"]},\"trap\":\"Treating “show” as “state the answer”: the steps are the point of the task.\",\"related\":[\"Show that\",\"Calculate\",\"Derive\",\"Solve\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Show that\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Obtain the required result (possibly using information given) without the formality of proof. “Show that” questions do not generally require the use of a calculator.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Reach a result you have already been given, setting out the working that leads to it. The target is known, so the working is what matters.\",\"structure\":[\"Write the formula or relationship.\",\"Substitute the data given.\",\"Work forward, step by step, until the stated result appears.\",\"Finish by saying that the result matches the value given.\"],\"weak\":\"Restates the given answer, or works backwards from it without a clear forward chain of steps.\",\"strong\":\"Moves forward from the data to the stated result in clear, checkable steps, without using the answer along the way.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: when the price of a bus ticket rises from $2.00 to $2.20, the number of journeys falls from 5,000 to 4,500 a week. Show that the PED is −1.\",\"outline\":[\"%ΔQd = (4,500 − 5,000) ÷ 5,000 × 100 = −10%.\",\"%ΔP = (2.20 − 2.00) ÷ 2.00 × 100 = +10%.\",\"PED = −10% ÷ +10% = −1.\",\"This matches the value given: demand is unit elastic over this range.\"]},\"trap\":\"Using the given answer inside the working, which makes the argument circular.\",\"related\":[\"Show\",\"Calculate\",\"Derive\",\"Determine\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"Sketch\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Represent by means of a diagram or graph (labelled as appropriate). The sketch should give a general idea of the required shape or relationship and should include relevant features.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Produce a diagram that shows the general shape of a relationship and its key features, labelled as needed — right in form, but not drawn to scale.\",\"structure\":[\"Decide which relationship to show and which variable goes on each axis.\",\"Draw the curve or curves with the correct general shape and position.\",\"Label the axes, the curves and the features that matter (turning points, a trend, intercepts).\",\"Keep it simple; precise scale is not required.\"],\"weak\":\"Draws the wrong shape — for example, a straight line with no fluctuations for a business cycle — or leaves out the key feature.\",\"strong\":\"Captures the correct shape and the key features clearly, with enough labels to be read without the text.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Sketch a diagram of the business cycle showing actual output and the long-term growth trend.\",\"outline\":[\"Axes: real GDP (vertical) and time (horizontal).\",\"Draw an upward-sloping line for the long-term growth trend (potential output).\",\"Draw a wave-shaped line of actual output fluctuating around the trend.\",\"Label a peak, a trough, an expansion and a contraction.\"]},\"trap\":\"Spending time on scale and precision that a sketch does not need, while missing a key labelled feature.\",\"related\":[\"Draw\",\"Label\",\"Construct\",\"Plot\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). The guide expects correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams where appropriate (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"Solve\",\"ao\":\"AO4\",\"def\":\"Obtain the answer(s) using algebraic and/or numerical and/or graphical methods.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Find the answer or answers using algebra, arithmetic or a graph, setting out the method you use.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify what is unknown and the relationship that links it to the data.\",\"Choose a method: algebraic, numerical or graphical.\",\"Work through it step by step.\",\"State the answer with units and check it against the data.\"],\"weak\":\"Chooses a method that cannot give the answer, or gives a result with no method.\",\"strong\":\"Selects an efficient method, shows each step and checks that the result makes sense.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Illustrative data: in an economy with a marginal propensity to consume of 0.8, investment rises by $5 billion. Solve for the eventual change in real GDP, assuming nothing else changes. (HL only)\",\"outline\":[\"Relationship: multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC).\",\"Multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − 0.8) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5.\",\"Change in real GDP = multiplier × change in injection = 5 × $5 billion = $25 billion.\",\"Check: the final change is larger than the initial injection, as the multiplier process implies.\"]},\"trap\":\"Using the MPC itself as the multiplier, or dividing by the MPC instead of by (1 − MPC).\",\"related\":[\"Calculate\",\"Determine\",\"Derive\",\"Show\"],\"papers\":\"AO4 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1 but are allowed in Paper 2 and HL Paper 3; the guide notes that many Paper 3 question parts require one (guide PDF pp. 66, 68).\"},{\"term\":\"State\",\"ao\":\"AO1\",\"def\":\"Give a specific name, value or other brief answer without explanation or calculation.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Give a specific name, value or short answer, without explanation or calculation.\",\"structure\":[\"Identify exactly what is asked: a term, a figure, a formula or a direction of change.\",\"Give it concisely and precisely.\",\"Include units or a sign where the answer is a value.\"],\"weak\":\"Explains at length, or gives a vague answer where a specific one is needed.\",\"strong\":\"Gives the exact term, value or formula in a single line.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"State the formula for income elasticity of demand (YED).\",\"outline\":[\"Identify the two variables: quantity demanded and income.\",\"Give the formula: YED = %ΔQd ÷ %ΔY.\",\"Keep it to one line; no explanation or example is needed.\"]},\"trap\":\"Writing an explanation when a precise one-line answer is all that is asked for.\",\"related\":[\"Define\",\"List\",\"Identify\",\"Outline\"],\"papers\":\"AO1 is listed for every part of Paper 1 and Paper 2 and for both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). A question may use a command term from the AO level set for the syllabus content or a less demanding one (guide PDF p. 65), so AO1 terms can be attached to content set at AO1, AO2 or AO3.\"},{\"term\":\"Suggest\",\"ao\":\"AO2\",\"def\":\"Propose a solution, hypothesis or other possible answer.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Put forward a plausible solution, explanation or answer, using economic reasoning. More than one answer may be acceptable; what matters is that yours is sensible and linked to the context.\",\"structure\":[\"Read the context for clues about what could explain the situation or solve the problem.\",\"Propose a specific, plausible answer.\",\"Support it with a brief economic reason.\",\"If more than one is asked for, make each one distinct.\"],\"weak\":\"Gives a generic answer unrelated to the context, or a proposal with no economic reason behind it.\",\"strong\":\"Offers a plausible, specific suggestion rooted in the context and backed by a clear economic reason.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"Suggest two reasons why the price elasticity of demand for petrol may be higher in the long run than in the short run.\",\"outline\":[\"Reason 1: over time, drivers can replace their cars with more fuel-efficient or electric vehicles.\",\"Reason 2: over time, households and firms can change where they live, work or locate, or switch to public transport.\",\"Link both to time as a determinant of PED: more substitutes become available as the period lengthens.\"]},\"trap\":\"Stating a suggestion without the economic reason that makes it plausible.\",\"related\":[\"Explain\",\"Apply\",\"Recommend\",\"Comment\"],\"papers\":\"AO2 is listed for both parts of Paper 1, six of the seven parts of Paper 2 and both parts of HL Paper 3 (guide PDF pp. 66–68). AO2 terms can be used with syllabus content set at AO2 or AO3, but not with content set only at AO1 (guide PDF p. 65).\"},{\"term\":\"To what extent\",\"ao\":\"AO3\",\"def\":\"Consider the merits or otherwise of an argument or concept. Opinions and conclusions should be presented clearly and supported with appropriate evidence and sound argument.\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\",\"asks\":\"Judge how far a claim or argument holds — not simply whether it is true — by weighing the case for and against and reaching a supported conclusion about the degree to which it holds.\",\"structure\":[\"Define the key terms and identify exactly which claim is being tested.\",\"Develop the case that supports the claim, with theory and evidence.\",\"Develop its limits or the counter-arguments with the same care.\",\"Weigh them: under what conditions, for whom and over what time frame does the claim hold?\",\"Conclude with a clear position on the degree — largely, partly, or only under stated conditions — that follows from the analysis.\"],\"weak\":\"Answers yes or no, or presents both sides and ends without saying how far the claim holds.\",\"strong\":\"Reaches a qualified conclusion on the extent to which the claim holds, supported by theory, evidence and clear weighing of conditions.\",\"example\":{\"q\":\"To what extent can economic growth be achieved without harming environmental sustainability?\",\"outline\":[\"Define economic growth (a rise in real GDP) and sustainability (meeting present needs without reducing future generations’ ability to meet theirs).\",\"Case for conflict: growth based on fossil fuels and resource extraction adds to pollution and depletes common pool resources.\",\"Case for compatibility: cleaner technology, a shift towards services and policies such as carbon pricing can cut emissions per unit of output.\",\"Weigh: evidence suggests some high-income economies have grown while reducing emissions, but whether this can happen quickly and widely enough is debated; the answer depends on technology, policy and the stage of development.\",\"Conclude on the degree: for example, compatible only partly, and only where strong environmental policy is in place.\"]},\"trap\":\"Treating the question as “is it true?” and giving a yes-or-no answer instead of judging the degree.\",\"related\":[\"Discuss\",\"Evaluate\",\"Examine\",\"Justify\"],\"papers\":\"AO3 is listed only for Paper 1 part (b), Paper 2 part (g) and HL Paper 3 part (b) — the parts marked with markbands that look for synthesis or evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–72). AO3 terms are used only with syllabus content set at AO3 (guide PDF p. 65). The Paper 1 part (b) markband also looks for developed real-world examples, and the Paper 2 part (g) markband for use of the text/data (guide PDF pp. 69–71).\"}],\"writing\":{\"stages\":[{\"id\":\"define\",\"name\":\"Definition\",\"what\":\"State the precise meaning of the key terms in the question, so that you and the reader are working with the same concepts. Define the terms your argument depends on, not every word in the question.\",\"weak\":\"“PED is how much demand changes when the price changes.”\",\"better\":\"“Price elasticity of demand measures the responsiveness of the quantity demanded of a good to a change in its own price, other things being equal.”\",\"tip\":\"Test the definition against its nearest neighbour: the weak version could describe a shift in demand; the better one could not.\"},{\"id\":\"explain\",\"name\":\"Explanation\",\"what\":\"Show why or how something happens, not only that it happens. An explanation links a cause to an effect through an economic mechanism.\",\"weak\":\"“A subsidy lowers the price.”\",\"better\":\"“A subsidy is a payment to producers that lowers their costs, so they are willing to supply more at each price. Supply shifts to the right, which lowers the market price and raises the quantity traded.”\",\"tip\":\"After each claim, ask “why?” once more. If the answer is an economic reason you have not written down, write it down.\"},{\"id\":\"chain\",\"name\":\"Chain of analysis\",\"what\":\"Link several explanations into a sequence in which each step causes the next, until you reach the outcome the question is about. Each link should be one step that a reader can check.\",\"weak\":\"“Higher interest rates reduce inflation.”\",\"better\":\"“A rise in the central bank’s policy rate tends to raise the rates banks charge on loans. Borrowing becomes dearer and saving more attractive, so spending on goods bought on credit and firms’ investment tend to fall. Because consumption and investment are components of AD, AD shifts left, easing demand-pull pressure on the price level.”\",\"tip\":\"Plan the chain as arrows (rate ↑ → borrowing costs ↑ → C and I ↓ → AD ↓ → price pressure ↓), then check that no arrow hides a missing step.\"},{\"id\":\"apply\",\"name\":\"Application\",\"what\":\"Use the specific context — the named market, country, policy or data — at each step of the argument, so that the theory is seen working (or not working) in this case.\",\"weak\":\"“This happens in many countries where governments use taxes.”\",\"better\":\"“In the extract, the tax applies to sugary drinks. Because low- and no-sugar alternatives are widely available, demand for sugary drinks may be more price-elastic than demand for products with few substitutes, so the tax could shift consumption towards those alternatives.”\",\"tip\":\"If you could paste the paragraph into an answer about a different market without changing a word, it is not yet applied.\"},{\"id\":\"evaluate\",\"name\":\"Evaluation\",\"what\":\"Appraise the argument you have built: how large is the effect, how likely is it, for whom, over what time frame, and under what conditions would it fail? Evaluation weighs; it does more than add the other side.\",\"weak\":\"“However, there are also some disadvantages to the tax.”\",\"better\":\"“The effect on consumption depends on PED. If demand is price-inelastic, as it may be for habitual buyers, quantity falls only a little: the tax then raises revenue more than it changes behaviour, and it takes a larger share of income from lower-income households, who spend proportionately more on the product.”\",\"tip\":\"Useful evaluation questions: How big? How likely? Who gains and who loses? Short run or long run? What does it depend on? What is the opportunity cost?\"},{\"id\":\"judge\",\"name\":\"Judgement\",\"what\":\"Reach a clear conclusion that answers the question, follows from your evaluation and states the conditions under which it holds. A judgement takes a position; it is not a summary.\",\"weak\":\"“Overall, there are advantages and disadvantages, so it depends.”\",\"better\":\"“The tax is likely to reduce consumption most where close substitutes are available and the revenue funds healthier alternatives. For heavy consumers with inelastic demand it is a weaker tool on its own, so it works best alongside regulation and education.”\",\"tip\":\"“It depends” is only a starting point: say what it depends on, why, and which way you lean in this context.\"}],\"ladder\":[{\"level\":\"Assertion\",\"text\":\"Tax increases.\"},{\"level\":\"Mechanism\",\"text\":\"A specific tax raises firms’ costs of supplying each unit, so the price of the good rises.\"},{\"level\":\"Chain\",\"text\":\"A specific tax raises firms’ costs, shifting supply left — vertically upward by the amount of the tax per unit. The market price rises while equilibrium quantity falls. The extent to which consumers bear the tax depends on relative PED and PES: the more price-inelastic demand is relative to supply, the larger the share of the tax that consumers pay through a higher price, and the smaller the fall in quantity.\"},{\"level\":\"Applied\",\"text\":\"For a specific tax on cigarettes, demand is likely to be price-inelastic: nicotine is addictive and there are few close legal substitutes. Supply shifts up by the tax per packet, the price paid by smokers rises by most of the tax, and the quantity sold falls only a little. Smokers therefore bear most of the burden, and the government collects sizeable revenue.\"},{\"level\":\"Evaluated\",\"text\":\"Because demand is price-inelastic, the tax is better at raising revenue than at reducing smoking in the short run. Over a longer period demand may become more elastic as smokers quit and fewer young people start, so the effect on consumption may grow. A large tax gap with neighbouring countries can encourage smuggling and cross-border buying, and the tax is regressive, because lower-income smokers spend a larger share of their income on it. On balance, the tax is likely to work best as one part of a package that includes non-price measures such as advertising bans and support for quitting.\"}],\"ladders2\":[{\"topic\":\"Expansionary fiscal policy\",\"unit\":\"Unit 3: Macroeconomics\",\"rungs\":[{\"level\":\"Assertion\",\"text\":\"Government spending boosts growth.\"},{\"level\":\"Mechanism\",\"text\":\"Government spending is a component of aggregate demand, so higher spending shifts AD right and real GDP rises.\"},{\"level\":\"Chain\",\"text\":\"Higher government spending on infrastructure raises AD directly. Firms carrying out the projects hire workers, whose extra income is partly spent, raising AD further (the multiplier effect, HL only). If the economy has a deflationary gap, real GDP rises with little upward pressure on the price level; closer to full employment, more of the effect shows up as inflation.\"},{\"level\":\"Evaluated\",\"text\":\"The size of the effect depends on how much spare capacity exists, how much of each extra unit of income is saved, taxed or spent on imports, and how the spending is financed. Borrowing may push up interest rates and crowd out some private investment (HL only), although this is less likely in a deep recession. Because infrastructure can also raise LRAS over time, the long-run case may be stronger than the short-run one — provided the projects are well chosen.\"}]},{\"topic\":\"Tariffs and employment\",\"unit\":\"Unit 4: The global economy\",\"rungs\":[{\"level\":\"Assertion\",\"text\":\"Tariffs protect jobs.\"},{\"level\":\"Chain\",\"text\":\"A tariff raises the domestic price of the good above the world price. Domestic quantity supplied rises and quantity demanded falls, so imports shrink. Domestic producers gain producer surplus and may keep or add workers; consumers lose consumer surplus; the government gains tariff revenue.\"},{\"level\":\"Applied\",\"text\":\"Suppose a country puts a tariff on imported steel to protect a small domestic steel industry, while its larger car and construction industries buy steel as an input (illustrative scenario). Steel producers gain from the higher price, but car makers and builders face higher input costs, which may reduce their output and the number of people they employ.\"},{\"level\":\"Evaluated\",\"text\":\"Whether the tariff protects jobs overall depends on the size of the steel-using industries relative to steel, how far they can pass higher costs on to their customers, and whether trading partners retaliate against the country’s exports. Even if steel jobs are saved, the tariff creates a welfare loss from less efficient domestic production and lower consumption, so the cost of each job protected may be high. Temporary protection, or support for retraining workers, may meet the employment concern at lower cost.\"}]}],\"connectives\":[{\"move\":\"cause → effect\",\"use\":\"because / so / which means / as a result / this leads to\"},{\"move\":\"condition\",\"use\":\"if / provided that / as long as / only when / depending on\"},{\"move\":\"qualification or contrast\",\"use\":\"however / whereas / although / on the other hand / but only if\"},{\"move\":\"magnitude\",\"use\":\"the extent to which / the more…, the larger… / only slightly, because / by a smaller amount if\"},{\"move\":\"time frame\",\"use\":\"in the short run / over time / initially…, later… / once firms have adjusted\"},{\"move\":\"evidence and application\",\"use\":\"the data show / in the extract / for example, in… / this is consistent with\"},{\"move\":\"judgement\",\"use\":\"on balance / therefore / the stronger argument is…, because / this suggests that, in this case,\"}],\"avoid\":[\"Asserting what a diagram shows without referring to its labelled shifts, points or areas in the text.\",\"Stating an outcome without the mechanism that links the cause to the effect.\",\"Listing advantages and disadvantages with no weighing and no judgement.\",\"Name-dropping a real-world example instead of explaining how it illustrates the argument.\",\"Treating a correlation in the data as if it established a cause.\",\"Unconditional claims, such as saying a policy “will definitely” work, where the outcome depends on conditions such as elasticity or spare capacity.\",\"Reproducing a memorised paragraph that answers a different question from the one asked.\"],\"note\":\"These are reasoning moves, not a template to memorise. Different questions call for them in different proportions and orders: a short question asking you to explain may need only a definition, an explanation and a chain, while a question asking you to evaluate also calls for evaluation and a judgement. Use the stages to check the logic of your own argument, not as a fixed paragraph structure.\"},\"papers\":{\"p1\":{\"facts\":[{\"t\":\"Paper 1 is an extended-response paper taken at both SL and HL. It lasts 1 hour 15 minutes and is worth 25 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 63–64\"},{\"t\":\"You answer one question from a choice of three. Each question has two parts: (a), worth 10 marks, and (b), worth 15 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 66\"},{\"t\":\"Paper 1 carries 30% of the final assessment at SL and 20% at HL.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 63–64\"},{\"t\":\"Questions are drawn from all four units of the syllabus. At SL they exclude HL extension material; at HL they can include it. The SL and HL questions may be the same or different.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 66–68\"},{\"t\":\"Part (a) assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4. Part (b) assesses all four assessment objectives, including AO3 (synthesis and evaluation).\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 66\"},{\"t\":\"Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 66\"},{\"t\":\"Marks are awarded using a combination of an analytic markscheme and markbands. The markbands for the 10-mark and 15-mark parts are published in the guide.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 67, 69–70\"},{\"t\":\"Where appropriate, answers are expected to include correctly labelled, clearly drawn diagrams, and real-world examples that are explained in relation to the question rather than simply named.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 65\"},{\"t\":\"Every examination paper begins with five minutes of reading time.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 65\"}],\"moves\":[{\"step\":\"Identify the economic issue\",\"how\":\"Underline the command term, the named market, policy or economy, and any qualifier such as “using a diagram” or “with reference to real-world examples”. Then write one line stating the economic question you are actually being asked.\",\"trap\":\"Answering the topic instead of the question: writing everything you know about inflation when the question asks only how inflation redistributes income.\"},{\"step\":\"Choose the model\",\"how\":\"Decide which theory does the explaining: which market or economy, which curves, micro or macro. Pick the model that fits the mechanism the question names, not the one you feel most comfortable drawing.\",\"trap\":\"Using a plain demand-and-supply diagram for a market-failure question, where marginal social benefit and marginal social cost curves are needed to show the welfare loss.\"},{\"step\":\"Build the diagram\",\"how\":\"Give it a title that names the market or economy, label both axes and every curve, mark the original and new equilibria, and show the direction of any shift with arrows. Then refer to it in your writing, point by point (for example, P₁ to P₂ and Q₁ to Q₂).\",\"trap\":\"A correct diagram that the text does not mention. The markbands reward diagrams that are included and explained, not merely present.\"},{\"step\":\"Apply the context\",\"how\":\"Tie each link in the causal chain to the stakeholders, market or country in the question. In part (b), bring in a real-world example and show how it illustrates the mechanism: who was affected, how, and with what result.\",\"trap\":\"Naming an example (“as in country X”) without explaining how it connects to the argument. The guide treats a merely stated example as too limited.\"},{\"step\":\"Develop evaluation\",\"how\":\"Weigh each argument as you make it: how large is the effect, how likely, for whom, and over what time frame? Question the assumptions behind the model, such as ceteris paribus, the elasticity values involved, or the information available to the government.\",\"trap\":\"A final paragraph of advantages and disadvantages with no weighing. Balance without judgement is a list, not evaluation.\"},{\"step\":\"Construct a judgement\",\"how\":\"Answer the question directly. Say what your answer depends on, which factor matters most in this context, and why. The judgement should follow from the analysis that comes before it.\",\"trap\":\"Writing “it depends” without saying what it depends on, or reaching a conclusion that your own analysis does not support.\"}],\"ten\":{\"what\":\"Part (a) assesses AO1, AO2 and AO4 only, so evaluation is not what earns the marks here (guide PDF p. 66). Reading the markband (guide PDF p. 69), marks rise as an answer moves from describing theory to explaining it fully, from including terms to using them accurately throughout, and from including a diagram to explaining it fully. The top band also requires the answer to meet exactly what the question asks. In practice, a 10-mark answer rewards depth of explanation on a tightly defined question, not breadth or judgement.\",\"moves\":[\"Define the key terms in the question precisely, in one sentence each.\",\"Draw the one diagram that shows the mechanism, labelled for the named market or economy.\",\"Explain the causal chain step by step, referring to points on the diagram as you go (for example, the shift from S₁ to S₂ and the rise from P₁ to P₂).\",\"If the question asks for two reasons or effects, develop two distinct ones rather than one idea restated.\",\"Close by linking back to the exact wording of the question, without adding evaluation that was not asked for.\"],\"trap\":\"Spreading effort across many points that are only stated. The markband climbs from “described” to “partly explained” to “fully explained”, so a few well-developed points outscore a list of assertions.\"},\"fifteen\":{\"what\":\"Part (b) adds AO3 (guide PDF p. 66). Its markband (guide PDF pp. 69–70) keeps the part (a) strands of theory, terms and diagrams and adds two more: synthesis or evaluation, which must be effective and balanced at the top, and a real-world example that is identified and fully developed to support the argument. A 15-mark answer is therefore an argument: theory explained, applied to a real case, weighed, and brought to a reasoned judgement.\",\"moves\":[\"Frame the issue: define the key terms and state what the policy, change or claim is meant to achieve.\",\"Explain the theory with a diagram where appropriate, even if part (a) used a similar one; part (b) needs its own analysis.\",\"Develop at least one real-world example in detail: what happened, which mechanism it illustrates, and what it suggests about the question.\",\"Evaluate throughout rather than only at the end: consider magnitude, likelihood, time frame, stakeholders and the assumptions of the model.\",\"Where the question asks how effective something is, test it briefly against an alternative.\",\"Reach a justified judgement and state the conditions under which it holds.\"],\"trap\":\"Treating part (b) as a longer part (a). An accurate answer without balanced evaluation and a developed example is unlikely to reach the upper markbands, because both are written into their descriptors.\"},\"note\":\"Questions differ in command term, scope and context, so the same six moves take different shapes: a question about one named policy needs different evaluation from one that asks you to compare two outcomes. Use the moves as a check on what the markbands reward, not as a fixed paragraph order to force onto every question.\"},\"p2\":{\"facts\":[{\"t\":\"Paper 2 is a data response paper taken at both SL and HL. It lasts 1 hour 45 minutes and is worth 40 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 63–64\"},{\"t\":\"You answer one question from a choice of two. Each question has parts (a) to (g): (a)(i) and (a)(ii) are worth 2 marks each, (b) 5 marks, (c), (d), (e) and (f) 4 marks each, and (g) 15 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 67\"},{\"t\":\"Part (b) may itself be divided into two subparts; its maximum is 5 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 67\"},{\"t\":\"Paper 2 carries 40% of the final assessment at SL and 30% at HL.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 63–64\"},{\"t\":\"The paper includes some quantitative questions. At SL, questions exclude HL extension material; at HL, they can include it. SL and HL candidates may be set the same text, data and questions.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 63–64, 67–68\"},{\"t\":\"Parts (a) to (f) are marked with a markscheme; part (g) is marked with a 15-mark markband.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 70\"},{\"t\":\"Only part (g) assesses AO3 (synthesis and evaluation).\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 67\"},{\"t\":\"The part (g) markband has a strand on the use of the text and data. At the top level, information from the text/data is used to build a reasoned argument supported by analysis and evaluation.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 70–71\"},{\"t\":\"Every calculation can be completed with a four-function calculator, and graphic display calculators are allowed.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 66\"}],\"stages\":[{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"Read the source\",\"how\":\"Read the questions first, then the text and data, so that you read with a purpose. Note the country, the time period, the units of every figure and what each paragraph is about.\",\"trap\":\"Skimming the table and missing its units or base year, for example reading an index number as a percentage.\"},{\"n\":2,\"name\":\"Identify the economic issue\",\"how\":\"Name the economic problem each paragraph describes, such as cost-push inflation, a depreciating currency or the fiscal cost of a subsidy, and the syllabus area it belongs to.\",\"trap\":\"Summarising the news story instead of naming the economic concept it illustrates.\"},{\"n\":3,\"name\":\"Extract relevant data\",\"how\":\"Pick out only the figures and phrases the question needs, and note where each came from (Table 1, paragraph 3). Check units, time periods and whether a figure is nominal or real.\",\"trap\":\"Copying long passages, or quoting numbers that have no bearing on the question.\"},{\"n\":4,\"name\":\"Interpret data\",\"how\":\"Say what the numbers mean: the direction, size and pace of a change, and whether it is measured in per cent or percentage points. Consider what the data does not show.\",\"trap\":\"Reading a fall in the inflation rate as a fall in the price level, or treating two series that move together as evidence that one caused the other.\"},{\"n\":5,\"name\":\"Apply theory\",\"how\":\"Choose the model that explains what the extract describes, and apply it to this economy using the people, firms and institutions named in the text.\",\"trap\":\"Drawing a demand-pull diagram when the paragraph cited describes rising import costs. The theory has to match the evidence referred to.\"},{\"n\":6,\"name\":\"Use diagrams\",\"how\":\"Where a question asks for a diagram, label it for this context (for example, “Price of lorvan in US$”), show the change with arrows and new equilibrium points, and explain it in two or three sentences that refer to the diagram.\",\"trap\":\"Vague axis labels such as “P” and “Q”, or a diagram that the explanation does not refer to.\"},{\"n\":7,\"name\":\"Evaluate\",\"how\":\"In part (g), weigh the arguments using the extract: which stakeholders gain and lose, how large the effects are likely to be, what constraints the government faces, and how the short run differs from the long run.\",\"trap\":\"Evaluation that could have been written without reading the extract. Generic points score less well than points anchored in this economy’s data.\"},{\"n\":8,\"name\":\"Answer the exact command term\",\"how\":\"Match each response to its command term and mark allocation: “define” needs a precise meaning, “calculate” needs working and units, “explain” needs reasons or causes, and part (g) needs a reasoned judgement. Allocate time roughly in proportion to the marks.\",\"trap\":\"Writing half a page for a 2-mark definition and running short of time for the 15-mark part (g).\"}],\"exercise\":{\"title\":\"Lorvania: rising prices, a weaker currency and a subsidy under review\",\"label\":\"Illustrative data — invented for practice, not real statistics\",\"text\":[\"Consumer prices in Lorvania, a lower-middle-income economy that imports almost all of its fuel, rose by 9.8% in Year 3, the fastest rate in over a decade. A drought cut the coffee harvest, which normally earns around a third of the country’s export revenue, and several processing plants reduced their shifts.\",\"The lorvan (LVN) has depreciated sharply against the US dollar. Importers say the higher cost of fuel and machinery is being passed on to consumers, and transport firms have raised fares twice this year. Economists at the national university describe much of the recent inflation as cost-push.\",\"The central bank has raised its policy interest rate from 4% to 7%. Business groups warn that dearer credit will hit small firms already facing higher costs. The central bank governor argues that “expectations of rising prices must not become entrenched”.\",\"The finance ministry is considering replacing the universal fuel subsidy, which now costs the government about 3% of GDP a year, with targeted cash transfer payments to low-income households. Ministry estimates suggest that the richest fifth of households receive close to half of the subsidy’s benefit, because they own most private vehicles. Opponents fear that removing the subsidy would push fuel prices and inflation higher in the short term.\"],\"table\":{\"head\":[\"Table 1: Selected indicators for Lorvania\",\"Year 1\",\"Year 2\",\"Year 3\"],\"rows\":[[\"Real GDP growth (%)\",\"4.1\",\"3.2\",\"1.4\"],[\"Inflation rate (%)\",\"3.0\",\"5.5\",\"9.8\"],[\"Unemployment rate (%)\",\"6.2\",\"6.0\",\"6.9\"],[\"Central bank policy interest rate, end of year (%)\",\"4.0\",\"4.0\",\"7.0\"],[\"Exchange rate (LVN per US$1)\",\"40\",\"42\",\"50\"],[\"Current account balance (% of GDP)\",\"−1.5\",\"−2.8\",\"−4.6\"],[\"Fuel subsidy spending (% of GDP)\",\"1.8\",\"2.4\",\"3.1\"]]},\"questions\":[{\"part\":\"(a)(i)\",\"marks\":2,\"q\":\"Define the term “depreciated” used in paragraph 2.\",\"guide\":\"• A fall in the value of one currency against another\\n• in a floating exchange rate system, caused by market forces (changes in demand for or supply of the currency)\\n• Full credit needs both elements; the floating-system context is what separates depreciation from devaluation\"},{\"part\":\"(a)(ii)\",\"marks\":2,\"q\":\"Define the term “transfer payments” used in paragraph 4.\",\"guide\":\"• Payments from the government to individuals or households\\n• for which no good or service is provided in return (for example, pensions, unemployment benefits or cash transfers)\\n• They redistribute income and are not counted as government spending on goods and services in GDP\"},{\"part\":\"(b)\",\"marks\":5,\"q\":\"Using Table 1, (i) calculate the percentage change in the value of the lorvan against the US dollar between Year 2 and Year 3 [2 marks], and (ii) calculate the real policy interest rate in Year 3 and comment on its sign [3 marks].\",\"guide\":\"• Value of the lorvan in US$: Year 2 = 1 ÷ 42 ≈ US$0.0238; Year 3 = 1 ÷ 50 = US$0.0200\\n• Percentage change = (0.0200 − 0.0238) ÷ 0.0238 × 100 ≈ −16%, a depreciation of about 16% (shortcut: 42 ÷ 50 − 1 = −0.16)\\n• Common error: (50 − 42) ÷ 42 × 100 ≈ 19% measures the rise in the value of the US dollar against the lorvan, not the change in the lorvan’s value\\n• Real interest rate ≈ nominal rate − inflation rate = 7.0% − 9.8% = −2.8%\\n• Comment: the real rate is negative, so despite the rise in the nominal rate, savers lose purchasing power and borrowing is still cheap in real terms; policy may be less contractionary than the nominal rise suggests\\n• Show working and units (%) at every step\"},{\"part\":\"(c)\",\"marks\":4,\"q\":\"Using an AD/AS diagram, explain how the higher cost of imported fuel (paragraph 2) may have contributed to the changes in Lorvania’s inflation rate and real GDP growth between Year 2 and Year 3.\",\"guide\":\"• Diagram: axes labelled “Average price level” and “Real GDP”; SRAS shifts left from SRAS₁ to SRAS₂; the price level rises from P₁ to P₂ and real output falls from Y₁ to Y₂\\n• Explanation: fuel is an input for most firms, so higher import costs raise costs of production and reduce the output firms supply at each price level\\n• Link to the data: inflation rose from 5.5% to 9.8% while growth slowed from 3.2% to 1.4%, consistent with cost-push inflation\\n• Precision: growth slowed but stayed positive, so the fall from Y₁ to Y₂ shows output lower than it would otherwise have been, not an absolute fall in real GDP\"},{\"part\":\"(d)\",\"marks\":4,\"q\":\"Using a foreign exchange market diagram, explain one reason, from paragraph 1, why the lorvan may have depreciated against the US dollar.\",\"guide\":\"• Diagram: market for lorvan, axes labelled “Price of lorvan in US$” and “Quantity of lorvan”; demand shifts left from D₁ to D₂ and the exchange rate falls from e₁ to e₂\\n• Explanation: the drought reduced coffee exports, so foreign buyers needed fewer lorvan to pay for Lorvanian coffee, lowering demand for the currency\\n• A supply-side route (importers selling more lorvan to buy US dollars, so supply shifts right) needs a cause in paragraph 1, such as a rise in world fuel prices with price-inelastic fuel demand; paragraph 1 does not state one, and paragraph 2 presents dearer imported fuel partly as a result of the depreciation, so citing it as the cause would be circular\\n• The question asks for one reason, so develop one mechanism fully\"},{\"part\":\"(e)\",\"marks\":4,\"q\":\"Explain two reasons why raising the policy interest rate from 4% to 7% may reduce inflation in Lorvania less than the central bank hopes (paragraph 3).\",\"guide\":\"• Reason 1: much of the inflation is cost-push (paragraph 2); higher interest rates work mainly by reducing aggregate demand, so they may cut output and employment without addressing higher import costs\\n• Reason 2: the real interest rate is still negative (see part (b)), so the incentive to borrow less and save more may be weak\\n• Other valid reasons: time lags before borrowing and spending respond; households and firms with little debt respond less; the effect on the exchange rate depends on whether higher rates attract capital inflows\\n• Each reason needs a cause-and-effect explanation linked to Lorvania, not only a statement\"},{\"part\":\"(f)\",\"marks\":4,\"q\":\"Using a diagram, explain the effect of the universal fuel subsidy (paragraph 4) on the market for fuel in Lorvania.\",\"guide\":\"• Diagram: axes labelled “Price of fuel (LVN per litre)” and “Quantity of fuel (litres)”; supply shifts vertically down by the subsidy per litre, from S₁ to S₂; price paid by consumers falls from Pe to Pc; quantity rises from Qe to Q₁; price received by suppliers is Pp\\n• Explanation: the subsidy lowers suppliers’ costs, so supply increases; consumers pay less and buy more fuel\\n• Identify the government’s cost: subsidy per litre × Q₁ (the rectangle between Pp and Pc up to Q₁)\\n• Credit for context, within the 4-mark maximum: because Lorvania imports its fuel, higher consumption raises import spending, which links to the widening current account deficit in Table 1\"},{\"part\":\"(g)\",\"marks\":15,\"q\":\"Using information from the text/data and your knowledge of economics, evaluate the proposal to replace Lorvania’s universal fuel subsidy with targeted cash transfers to low-income households.\",\"guide\":\"• Define the key terms (subsidy, transfer payments) and state the proposal’s aims: a lower fiscal cost, better targeting and lower fuel imports\\n• Analysis with a diagram: removing the subsidy raises the price of fuel and reduces the quantity consumed; on an AD/AS diagram, higher fuel costs shift SRAS left, adding to inflation in the short run\\n• Use the data: subsidy spending of 3.1% of GDP; inflation already at 9.8%; the richest fifth receiving close to half the benefit; a current account deficit of 4.6% of GDP\\n• Equity: targeted transfers can raise the incomes of the poorest while ending spending that mainly benefits richer households, but low-income households still face higher transport and food prices\\n• Evaluation: the size of the price effect depends on PED for fuel (likely to be low in the short run); the government’s capacity to identify eligible households; the risk of excluding some who need support; political resistance; a possible environmental gain from lower fuel use; the timing, with inflation already high\\n• Stakeholders: poorer and richer households, transport firms, the government budget, the central bank\\n• A reasoned judgement, for example: the proposal is justified on fiscal and equity grounds if the transfers are in place before prices rise and the subsidy is phased out gradually\"}]}},\"p3\":{\"facts\":[{\"t\":\"Paper 3 is taken by HL students only. It is a policy paper lasting 1 hour 45 minutes and worth 60 marks.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 64\"},{\"t\":\"There are two compulsory questions, each worth 30 marks; there is no choice of question.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 64, 68\"},{\"t\":\"Paper 3 carries 30% of the final HL assessment.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 64, 68\"},{\"t\":\"Questions can draw on the whole syllabus, including HL extension material, and include both quantitative and qualitative questions.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 64, 68\"},{\"t\":\"Each question is divided into part (a), which has subparts, and part (b). Part (a) is marked with a markscheme; part (b) is marked with a markband built around the command term “recommend”.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 68, 71\"},{\"t\":\"AO3 (synthesis and evaluation) is assessed in part (b) only.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 68\"},{\"t\":\"Many question parts require a calculator. Graphic display calculators are allowed, although a four-function calculator is sufficient for every calculation.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 66, 68\"},{\"t\":\"The guide states that HL students develop their quantitative skills further, to analyse and evaluate economic relationships and give informed policy advice, and that these skills are specifically assessed in Paper 3.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 13\"},{\"t\":\"At HL, AO3 includes selecting and using economic data, together with economic theory, to make policy recommendations.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 23\"},{\"t\":\"Recommend (AO3): “Present an advisable course of action with appropriate supporting evidence/reason in relation to a given situation, problem or issue.”\",\"src\":\"IB Economics guide (first assessment 2022), glossary of command terms, guide PDF p. 81\"}],\"frame\":[{\"step\":\"Objective\",\"how\":\"State what the policy-maker is trying to achieve, in measurable terms where possible (for example, bringing unemployment back towards its pre-downturn rate). If there are several objectives, say which one the question puts first.\"},{\"step\":\"Constraints\",\"how\":\"List what limits the choice: a fiscal rule or debt ratio, an interest rate close to its floor, a commitment not to intervene in the currency market, administrative capacity, time. A constraint stated in the question binds your recommendation.\"},{\"step\":\"Options\",\"how\":\"Identify two or three realistic policies that could meet the objective within the constraints, for example one fiscal, one monetary and one supply-side option, and name the mechanism through which each would work.\"},{\"step\":\"Evidence and data\",\"how\":\"Use your part (a) calculations and the data provided to test each option. What does the multiplier imply? How large is the output gap? How close is the debt ratio to its limit? Quote figures with their units.\"},{\"step\":\"Trade-offs and stakeholders\",\"how\":\"Set out who gains, who loses and what is given up: the opportunity cost, the effect on the government budget, inflation against unemployment, equity against efficiency, domestic against foreign interests.\"},{\"step\":\"Short run and long run\",\"how\":\"Separate immediate effects from later ones. Consider time lags, whether the effect lasts, and whether the policy addresses a cyclical problem, a structural one, or both.\"},{\"step\":\"Unintended consequences\",\"how\":\"Ask what could go wrong: crowding out, leakage into imports, inflationary pressure, weaker incentives, government failure, and effects on groups the policy was not aimed at.\"},{\"step\":\"Recommendation and its condition\",\"how\":\"Choose one course of action, explain how it works, justify it with theory and the data, and state the condition on which it is the right choice, including what evidence would change your advice.\"}],\"skills\":[\"Using quantitative techniques to identify, explain and analyse economic relationships, and interpreting data sets (AO4; guide PDF p. 23)\",\"Selecting and using economic data, with economic theory, to make policy recommendations (HL AO3; guide PDF p. 23)\",\"PED, YED and PES, and the resulting changes in price, quantity or total revenue, from data (guide PDF p. 34)\",\"Consumer and producer surplus from a diagram (HL only; guide PDF p. 33)\",\"The effects on markets and stakeholders of price ceilings, price floors, indirect taxes and subsidies, and welfare loss from a diagram (HL only; guide PDF p. 36)\",\"Profit, MC, MR, AC and AR from data (HL only; guide PDF p. 38)\",\"Nominal GDP and GNI, real GDP and GNI using a price deflator, and real GDP and GNI per capita (guide PDF pp. 41–42)\",\"The rate of economic growth, the unemployment rate and the inflation rate from CPI data, and a weighted price index (the index is HL only; guide PDF pp. 43–44)\",\"Government (national) debt measured as a percentage of GDP (HL only; guide PDF p. 44)\",\"Constructing a Lorenz curve from income quintile data; indirect tax paid from a given level of spending; total tax and average tax rates from data (HL only; guide PDF pp. 45–46)\",\"Real interest rates from data (guide PDF p. 48)\",\"The Keynesian multiplier and the effect on GDP of a change in investment, government spending or exports (HL only; guide PDF pp. 48–49)\",\"From a diagram, quantities of exports and imports, import expenditure and export revenue; opportunity costs from data to identify comparative advantage (HL only; guide PDF p. 51)\",\"From a diagram, the effects on stakeholders of tariffs, quotas and subsidies (HL only; guide PDF p. 52)\",\"The price of a good in different currencies, changes in the value of a currency, and elements of the balance of payments from data (guide PDF pp. 54–55)\"],\"recommend\":\"The guide defines “recommend” as presenting an advisable course of action, supported by evidence or reasons, for a given situation (guide PDF p. 81). That makes it a decision under constraints, not a search for the best policy in the abstract: advice that suits one economy can be wrong for another with a different debt level, exchange rate system or labour market. A strong recommendation names one policy, explains its mechanism, uses the data to show why it fits this case better than the alternatives, and admits its costs. It ends by stating the condition on which the advice rests: what would have to be true for it to work, and what evidence would change it.\",\"scenario\":{\"title\":\"Corvenia: reducing unemployment under a fiscal rule\",\"label\":\"Illustrative data — invented for practice\",\"text\":[\"Corvenia is a small, open, high-income economy with its own currency, the Corvenian dollar (C$). In Year 2, demand from its largest export market weakened and two large textile manufacturers closed their factories, citing competition from lower-cost producers abroad. Unemployment rose from 5.1% to 8.4%. Households have become more cautious, and business surveys report the lowest confidence in several years.\",\"The government is bound by a fiscal rule that keeps government (national) debt below 80% of nominal GDP. The finance ministry proposes a C$12 billion increase in spending on transport infrastructure. The opposition prefers a temporary cut in income tax, and trade unions are calling for a publicly funded retraining programme for former textile workers. The central bank has cut its policy rate twice, to 0.75%, and says it has little room to cut further.\",\"Researchers at the national university estimate Corvenia’s potential real GDP at C$520 billion (at Year 1 prices). They judge that about one-third of the rise in unemployment is structural, because the textile jobs are unlikely to return, and the rest is cyclical. Imports account for a large share of household and government spending.\"],\"table\":{\"head\":[\"Table 1: Selected data for Corvenia\",\"Year 1\",\"Year 2\"],\"rows\":[[\"Nominal GDP (C$ billion)\",\"500\",\"510\"],[\"GDP deflator (Year 1 = 100)\",\"100\",\"104\"],[\"Government (national) debt (C$ billion)\",\"350\",\"380\"],[\"Unemployment rate (%)\",\"5.1\",\"8.4\"],[\"Central bank policy interest rate (%)\",\"2.00\",\"0.75\"],[\"Marginal propensity to save (MPS)\",\"–\",\"0.15\"],[\"Marginal propensity to tax (MPT)\",\"–\",\"0.20\"],[\"Marginal propensity to import (MPM)\",\"–\",\"0.25\"]]},\"tasks\":[\"Calculate real GDP in Year 2 at Year 1 prices, and the rate of real economic growth between Year 1 and Year 2. Show your working.\",\"Calculate government (national) debt as a percentage of nominal GDP in Year 1 and in Year 2. Comment on how much room the fiscal rule leaves the government.\",\"Using Table 1, calculate the Keynesian multiplier for Corvenia and the eventual change in real GDP that would follow the proposed C$12 billion increase in government spending. Assume the price level is constant.\",\"Using the university’s estimate of potential output, calculate the size of the output gap in Year 2 and the increase in government spending that would, in theory, close it. State two assumptions on which your answer depends.\",\"Draw a labelled AD/AS diagram showing Corvenia’s position in Year 2 and the intended effect of the proposed increase in government spending.\",\"Part (b) [10 marks]: Using the information provided and your knowledge of economics, recommend a policy the government of Corvenia could use to reduce unemployment while keeping within its fiscal rule.\"],\"answers\":[\"Real GDP = 510 ÷ 1.04 ≈ C$490.4 billion. Growth = (490.4 − 500) ÷ 500 × 100 ≈ −1.9%: real output fell.\",\"Year 1: 350 ÷ 500 × 100 = 70.0%. Year 2: 380 ÷ 510 × 100 ≈ 74.5%. At Year 2 GDP the rule allows debt up to 0.8 × 510 = C$408 billion, so about C$28 billion of extra borrowing is possible; less if nominal GDP falls further, more if it grows.\",\"k = 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM) = 1 ÷ (0.15 + 0.20 + 0.25) = 1 ÷ 0.60 ≈ 1.67. Change in real GDP ≈ 12 × 1.67 ≈ C$20 billion.\",\"Output gap = 520 − 490.4 ≈ C$29.6 billion (actual output below potential). Spending needed ≈ 29.6 ÷ 1.67 ≈ C$17.8 billion. Assumptions include a constant price level, stable marginal propensities, no crowding out, and an accurate estimate of potential output.\",\"AD₁ meets AS at real output Y₁, below potential output Yf; AD shifts right to AD₂, moving output towards Yf. Axes: “Average price level” and “Real GDP”; the gap between Y₁ and Yf is the deflationary (recessionary) gap.\",\"A strong answer might compare infrastructure spending (a demand boost with some long-run supply-side benefit, within the rule), a tax cut (weaker if households save it and much of it leaks into imports) and retraining (aimed at the structural third, with longer lags), and reach a recommendation that states its conditions.\"]}}},\"checklists\":[{\"id\":\"key-concepts\",\"name\":\"Key concepts\",\"for\":\"Checking that you can use the nine key concepts as lenses, not just list them.\",\"items\":[\"I can name all nine key concepts: Scarcity, Choice, Efficiency, Equity, Economic well-being, Sustainability, Change, Interdependence, Intervention.\",\"I can explain each concept in one or two sentences of my own, without notes.\",\"I can distinguish equity (fairness) from equality (sameness) and give a case where they point in different directions.\",\"I can distinguish allocative efficiency from productive efficiency.\",\"For any syllabus topic, I can name at least two key concepts it connects to and explain each link.\",\"I can use one concept as a lens on a news article: I can say how it shapes what I notice and how I judge the issue.\",\"I can show how two concepts can pull against each other, such as efficiency against equity, or growth against sustainability.\",\"I can explain how a key concept affects the people or groups in a specific example, not only define it.\",\"I know that each of my three IA commentaries must use a different key concept (guide PDF p. 75).\"]},{\"id\":\"real-world-examples\",\"name\":\"Real-world examples\",\"for\":\"Checking that your examples are accurate, specific and do real work in an answer.\",\"items\":[\"For each of Units 2, 3 and 4, I have at least two examples I can explain in detail: the country or market, the policy or event, roughly when it happened, and what followed.\",\"I can say which economic concept or mechanism each example illustrates.\",\"I can explain each example in relation to a question, not merely name it (guide PDF p. 65).\",\"I use only figures I am confident are accurate; otherwise I describe the direction and nature of the effect without numbers.\",\"I have examples from both high-income and low- or middle-income economies.\",\"For each example, I know at least one limitation or opposing view that I can use in evaluation.\",\"I keep real examples separate from hypothetical scenarios, and I label hypothetical ones as such.\",\"I know where each example came from, so I can check it again before an exam.\",\"I have prepared different examples for different questions rather than one example stretched to fit everything.\"]},{\"id\":\"policies\",\"name\":\"Policies\",\"for\":\"Checking that you can analyse and evaluate any policy the course covers.\",\"items\":[\"I can state the policy’s aim and the market failure or macroeconomic problem it responds to.\",\"I can explain the mechanism step by step, from the policy to the outcome.\",\"I can draw the diagram that shows the policy’s intended effect.\",\"I can identify the stakeholders who gain and lose, including the government budget.\",\"I can explain at least two conditions on which effectiveness depends, such as elasticity, time lags, information or enforcement.\",\"I can distinguish the policy’s short-run effects from its long-run effects.\",\"I can name an alternative policy and compare the two briefly.\",\"I can identify a possible unintended consequence or source of government failure.\",\"I can give a real-world example of the policy and explain what it suggests about effectiveness.\",\"I can reach a judgement that states the conditions under which it holds.\"]},{\"id\":\"glossary\",\"name\":\"Glossary\",\"for\":\"Checking that your definitions are precise and your terms are used correctly.\",\"items\":[\"I can define each syllabus term in one precise sentence that includes its essential features.\",\"My definitions are not circular: they do not use the term to define itself.\",\"I can distinguish commonly confused pairs: depreciation and devaluation, deflation and disinflation, equity and equality, a change in demand and a change in quantity demanded.\",\"I use the economic meaning of a word rather than its everyday meaning, for example investment as spending on capital goods.\",\"I include a time period or unit where the definition needs one, for example GDP measured over a given period.\",\"I can use each term correctly in a sentence about a real context.\",\"I know which terms belong to HL-only content.\",\"In extended answers, I define the key terms early and then use them consistently.\"]},{\"id\":\"command-terms\",\"name\":\"Command terms\",\"for\":\"Checking that you respond to what each command term asks.\",\"items\":[\"I know the assessment objective level (AO1–AO4) of each command term (guide PDF pp. 24–25).\",\"I can say in my own words what each command term requires, and I have checked this against the official glossary (guide PDF pp. 80–81).\",\"I can explain the difference between “describe” and “explain”.\",\"I can explain how “evaluate”, “discuss”, “examine” and “to what extent” differ.\",\"I know that “calculate” requires the stages of working to be shown.\",\"I know that “draw” means an accurate, labelled diagram, while “sketch” means a diagram that shows the general shape and relevant features.\",\"I know that AO3 terms such as “evaluate” and “recommend” require a judgement.\",\"I understand that a question may use a command term at the syllabus level or a less demanding one, but not a more demanding one (guide PDF p. 65).\",\"Before writing, I underline the command term, any number (“two reasons”) and any qualifier (“using a diagram”).\"]},{\"id\":\"paper-1\",\"name\":\"Paper 1\",\"for\":\"Final checks for the extended-response paper.\",\"items\":[\"I know the format: one question from a choice of three, part (a) 10 marks and part (b) 15 marks, 1 hour 15 minutes, no calculator (guide PDF p. 66).\",\"I choose my question on the strength of what I can write for part (b), not only part (a) (teacher-created advice, Arjun’s examiner guide).\",\"In part (a), I define the key terms, draw and explain a relevant diagram, and develop the causal chain fully.\",\"In part (a), I answer only what is asked; evaluation is not assessed there (guide PDF p. 66).\",\"In part (b), I give the theory and a diagram again where appropriate, rather than relying on part (a).\",\"In part (b), I develop at least one real-world example in the context of the question.\",\"In part (b), I evaluate throughout and reach a justified judgement.\",\"Every diagram has a title, labelled axes and curves, and is referred to in my writing.\",\"I plan briefly before writing and leave time to check that I answered the command term.\",\"I split my time roughly in proportion to the marks (teacher-created suggestion: about 25 minutes for part (a) and 45 minutes for part (b)).\"]},{\"id\":\"paper-2\",\"name\":\"Paper 2\",\"for\":\"Final checks for the data response paper.\",\"items\":[\"I know the format: one question from a choice of two; (a)(i) and (a)(ii) 2 marks each, (b) 5, (c) to (f) 4 each, (g) 15; 1 hour 45 minutes (guide PDF p. 67).\",\"I read the questions before the extract, so that I read with a purpose.\",\"My part (a) definitions are precise and complete, and short.\",\"In every calculation, I show the formula, the substitution, the answer and the units.\",\"I check whether each figure is in per cent, percentage points, an index number, or a nominal or real value.\",\"Every diagram is labelled for the context of the extract and explained.\",\"In parts (c) to (f), I link my explanation to the paragraph or data the question refers to.\",\"In part (g), I build my argument on specific figures and details from the text and data (guide PDF pp. 70–71).\",\"In part (g), I evaluate using stakeholders, time frame and constraints, and reach a judgement.\",\"I leave enough time for part (g) (teacher-created suggestion: at least 40 minutes).\"]},{\"id\":\"paper-3\",\"name\":\"Paper 3 (HL only)\",\"for\":\"Final checks for the HL policy paper.\",\"items\":[\"I know the format: two compulsory questions of 30 marks each, 60 marks in total, 1 hour 45 minutes (guide PDF pp. 64, 68).\",\"I can carry out every calculation listed in the syllabus, including the HL-only ones.\",\"I show working and units for every calculation.\",\"After each calculation, I write one sentence on what the figure means for the scenario.\",\"I use a calculator I know well and that the school has confirmed is permitted (guide PDF p. 66).\",\"I can draw HL diagrams accurately from data, including surplus and welfare-loss areas and the effects of trade protection.\",\"In the recommendation, I choose one policy, explain its mechanism and justify it with the data provided.\",\"I respect every constraint stated in the question.\",\"Before recommending, I consider trade-offs, stakeholders and short-run and long-run effects.\",\"I state the condition on which my recommendation rests.\",\"I manage my time so that neither question’s recommendation is rushed.\"]},{\"id\":\"ia\",\"name\":\"Internal assessment (IA)\",\"for\":\"Checking your commentary portfolio against the guide’s requirements.\",\"items\":[\"My portfolio has three commentaries, each on a different unit: Microeconomics, Macroeconomics and The global economy (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"Each commentary uses a different key concept (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"Each article comes from a different source; a newspaper and its website count as the same source (guide PDF p. 76; TSM PDF p. 61).\",\"I wrote each commentary within one year of its article’s publication date (guide PDF p. 77).\",\"I chose each article myself; none was given to the class or used in a class activity (guide PDF p. 75; TSM PDF p. 39).\",\"Each article is from the news media (a newspaper, journal or the internet), not a television or radio broadcast (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"Each commentary is no more than 800 words, counted under the guide’s rules on what is excluded (guide PDF p. 76).\",\"My diagrams are relevant, accurate, correctly labelled for the article and explained (criterion A; guide PDF p. 77).\",\"I apply theory to the article throughout and make judgements supported by reasoned argument (criteria C and E; guide PDF p. 78).\",\"I identify my key concept and fully explain its link to the article (criterion D; guide PDF p. 78); showing how it affects the people or groups in the article is teacher-created advice for developing that link.\",\"Each commentary records the article title, source, publication date, access date if online, date written, word count, unit and key concept (guide PDF p. 76).\",\"I have acknowledged every source other than the article and standard economic theory, and any assistance the school requires me to declare (TSM PDF p. 43).\"]},{\"id\":\"ee\",\"name\":\"Extended essay (EE) in economics\",\"for\":\"Checking that an economics EE meets what the Economics guide says, and flagging what to check elsewhere.\",\"items\":[\"My research question is clearly defined and based on an economics topic (guide PDF p. 14).\",\"My topic is a recent economic issue, policy or event that took place no more than five years before I began my research (guide PDF p. 14).\",\"I select and apply relevant economic theories, models and tools to develop an argument (guide PDF p. 14).\",\"I gather relevant primary and/or secondary data (guide PDF p. 14) and check how reliable it is.\",\"My EE is not an extension of my IA, and no part of it is submitted for the IA (guide PDF pp. 14, 74).\",\"I reference every source consistently in one standard style.\",\"I have read the economics subject-specific guidance in the current Extended essay guide (guide PDF p. 14).\",\"I have checked the word limit, assessment criteria and reflection requirements (Verification required: check the current EE guide).\",\"I know the school’s internal deadlines and what my supervisor can and cannot do (Verification required: check the current EE guide).\",\"If I collect data from people, I have followed the school’s guidance on consent and privacy.\"]},{\"id\":\"hl-extension\",\"name\":\"HL extension\",\"for\":\"Checking that HL-only content is covered and usable in all three papers.\",\"items\":[\"I know that 2.10, 2.11 and 2.12 are HL-only subtopics (guide PDF p. 26) and that the content of 2.4 is marked HL only (guide PDF p. 33).\",\"I can explain the HL-only assumptions behind the laws of demand and supply (guide PDF pp. 31–32).\",\"I can do every HL-only calculation: surplus, welfare loss, effects of price controls, taxes and subsidies, cost and revenue data, weighted price index, tax rates, multiplier, comparative advantage and trade protection.\",\"I can draw every HL-only diagram, including the Phillips curve, the money market, the J-curve and the linear PPC showing comparative advantage.\",\"I can evaluate behavioural economics and nudges against the rational consumer choice model.\",\"I can explain money creation by commercial banks and the tools of monetary policy (HL only; guide PDF p. 47).\",\"I can analyse government debt sustainability and crowding out (HL only; guide PDF pp. 44, 49).\",\"I can analyse the Marshall–Lerner condition, the J-curve and persistent current account deficits and surpluses (HL only; guide PDF pp. 55–56).\",\"I have practised HL content in Paper 1 and Paper 2 style questions, not only in Paper 3.\",\"I can use HL content to make a policy recommendation from data.\"]},{\"id\":\"can-i-do-this\",\"name\":\"Can I actually do this?\",\"for\":\"Seven self-ratings for any topic; test each one before you tick it.\",\"items\":[\"I know it — I can state the definitions and key facts from memory, accurately.\",\"I understand it — I can explain why it works, in my own words, to someone who has not studied it.\",\"I can apply it — I can use it to explain a case or news story I have not seen before.\",\"I can draw it — I can produce the diagram from a blank page, fully labelled, and explain every shift.\",\"I can calculate it — I can complete the related calculations from data, with working and units, and say what the answer means.\",\"I can evaluate it — I can weigh its strengths and limitations and reach a judgement that states its conditions.\",\"I can explain it without notes — I can talk or write through the whole topic, closed book, for five minutes without gaps.\"]}],\"integrity\":{\"intro\":\"Academic integrity means that the work you submit shows what you know and can do, and that you acknowledge the ideas, words and work of others. The Economics guide states that all coursework, including work submitted for assessment, must be authentic and based on your own ideas, with the work of others fully acknowledged (guide PDF p. 10). This page sets out the principles in general terms; the IB’s current policy and your school’s policy set the actual rules.\",\"topics\":[{\"id\":\"authenticity\",\"name\":\"Authenticity\",\"what\":\"Authentic work is your own: your choice of article or topic, your analysis, your diagrams and your judgements. The IA must be the student’s own work, and each student confirms that the final version is their authentic work (guide PDF p. 73).\",\"do\":[\"Choose your own IA articles and your own EE research question.\",\"Keep your notes, drafts and data so you can show how the work developed.\",\"Be ready to discuss your work with your teacher; the guide lists this as one way authenticity is checked (guide PDF pp. 73–74).\"],\"dont\":[\"Submit work that someone else has written, edited or substantially shaped.\",\"Submit the same piece of work for both the IA and the EE (guide PDF p. 74).\",\"Base a commentary on an article used for a class activity (TSM PDF p. 39).\"]},{\"id\":\"attribution\",\"name\":\"Attribution\",\"what\":\"Attribution means making clear which ideas, words, data, diagrams or images came from someone else. The guide expects every source used in assessed work to be acknowledged, including graphs, images and data from print or electronic sources (guide PDF pp. 10–11).\",\"do\":[\"Acknowledge data, charts and diagrams you did not create, even if you have adapted them.\",\"Mark direct quotations with quotation marks or indentation, followed by a citation (guide PDF p. 11).\",\"Acknowledge help from people and tools where your school requires it.\"],\"dont\":[\"Paste in a downloaded diagram without a reference (TSM PDF p. 63).\",\"Present someone else’s argument as yours because you have changed some of the words.\"]},{\"id\":\"citation\",\"name\":\"Citation\",\"what\":\"Citation is the mechanics of acknowledgement: a marker in your text linked to a full reference. The IB does not prescribe a referencing style; your school chooses one, and you use it consistently (guide PDF pp. 10–11).\",\"do\":[\"Give at least the author, date of publication, title, and page numbers or URL as applicable (guide PDF p. 11).\",\"Record the date you accessed any online source (guide PDF p. 11).\",\"In the IA, cite the publication a news-agency story appeared in, not the agency itself (TSM PDF p. 43).\"],\"dont\":[\"Mix referencing styles within one piece of work.\",\"Move definitions or quotations into IA footnotes to save words; footnotes are for references only (guide PDF p. 76).\"]},{\"id\":\"paraphrasing\",\"name\":\"Paraphrasing\",\"what\":\"Paraphrasing is restating a source’s idea in your own words and your own sentence structure. A paraphrase still needs a citation, because the idea is not yours (guide PDF p. 11).\",\"do\":[\"Read the source, close it, write from your understanding, then check your version against the source for accuracy.\",\"Cite every paraphrase and summary, not only direct quotations.\",\"Use a marked and cited quotation when the exact wording matters.\"],\"dont\":[\"Swap words for synonyms while keeping the source’s structure.\",\"Drop the citation on the grounds that the words are now yours.\"]},{\"id\":\"collaboration\",\"name\":\"Collaboration\",\"what\":\"Collaboration is working with others in ways your teacher permits, such as discussing theory in class. Collusion is giving or receiving help that lets someone submit work that is not their own. IA commentaries must not be prepared collaboratively (guide PDF p. 75), and teachers are expected to make the difference between collaboration and collusion clear (guide PDF p. 73).\",\"do\":[\"Discuss economic theory and practice questions with classmates during learning activities.\",\"Ask your teacher whenever you are unsure whether a form of help is allowed.\",\"Seek your teacher’s advice on your IA; students must not be penalised for seeking guidance (guide PDF p. 73).\"],\"dont\":[\"Share drafts of assessed work, or write any part of another student’s commentary or essay.\",\"Divide the work on an individual assessed task with a classmate.\"]},{\"id\":\"digital-tools\",\"name\":\"Use of digital tools\",\"what\":\"Digital tools include search engines, spreadsheets, graphing software, translation tools, grammar checkers and learning platforms such as this one. Some uses are routine; others change who produced the work. What is permitted in assessed work is set by the IB’s current rules and your school (Verification required).\",\"do\":[\"Use spreadsheets or graphing software to process data and draw diagrams that you design yourself.\",\"Keep a note of which tools you used and how, so you can acknowledge them if required.\",\"Check your school’s rules before using any tool on assessed work.\"],\"dont\":[\"Run someone else’s text through a translation or rewording tool and submit the result as your writing.\",\"Rely on a tool to produce analysis that you cannot explain yourself.\"]},{\"id\":\"ai-use\",\"name\":\"AI use\",\"what\":\"Generative AI tools can explain concepts, quiz you and suggest practice questions. Using them to learn is different from using them to produce assessed work. The Economics guide was published in 2020 (guide PDF p. 3) and does not address these tools, so the rules for AI in assessed work come from the IB’s and your school’s current policies (Verification required). Arjun’s examiner guide (teacher-created, not an IB document) reports that examiner feedback from the November 2025 session asks for any student use of AI to be clearly stated and acknowledged (Verification required: this IB policy detail could not be checked against an IB document).\",\"do\":[\"Where your school allows it, use AI tools to test your understanding, generate practice questions or hear a concept explained another way.\",\"Acknowledge any use of AI in assessed work in the way your school and the IB require (Verification required).\",\"Check every AI-generated explanation against your notes or the syllabus; these tools can be wrong.\"],\"dont\":[\"Submit AI-generated text, analysis, diagrams or evaluation as your own work.\",\"Use AI to choose your IA articles or to write any part of a commentary or EE.\",\"Hide or play down AI use in assessed work.\"]},{\"id\":\"source-evaluation\",\"name\":\"Source evaluation\",\"what\":\"Sources differ in reliability, relevance and neutrality. Evaluating a source means asking who produced it, why, how and when, and whether it is enough to support your claim. The TSM treats appraising sources, and judging their validity, relevance and objectivity, as part of research skills (TSM PDF p. 28).\",\"do\":[\"Prefer original data from statistical agencies, central banks and international organisations over second-hand reports of it.\",\"Check the date, definitions and units of any statistic before using it.\",\"Note possible bias: who wrote or funded the source, and what they want readers to conclude.\"],\"dont\":[\"Treat opinion pieces or blogs as evidence of what happened; for the IA, blogs are not acceptable unless linked to a recognised news organisation (TSM PDF p. 38).\",\"Use a statistic you cannot trace back to where it came from.\"]},{\"id\":\"exam-conduct\",\"name\":\"Exam conduct\",\"what\":\"In examinations, integrity means answering under the stated conditions, with only permitted materials. Calculators are not permitted in Paper 1; in Papers 2 and 3 they are allowed, including graphic display calculators, under the IB’s calculator regulations (guide PDF p. 66). The detailed rules of conduct are set by the IB and your school (Verification required).\",\"do\":[\"Bring only permitted equipment, and confirm with your school that your calculator is allowed.\",\"Use the five minutes of reading time (guide PDF p. 65) as your invigilator instructs.\",\"Follow the invigilator’s instructions throughout the examination.\"],\"dont\":[\"Bring notes, phones or other unauthorised items into the examination room.\",\"Communicate with other candidates during the examination.\",\"Discuss the content of a paper with anyone outside your session during the period your school specifies (Verification required).\"]}],\"support\":{\"learning\":[\"Lessons, explanations and worked examples on this platform\",\"Diagram walkthroughs and practice diagrams\",\"Original practice questions and exercises, with guidance on what a good answer needs\",\"Checklists and self-ratings\",\"Misconception notes and examiner-style advice (teacher-created)\",\"Feedback from your teacher during learning, including advice on one IA draft (guide PDF p. 73)\"],\"work\":[\"Your choice of IA articles and of your EE research question\",\"The economic analysis, diagrams, key-concept links and evaluation in your IA commentaries and EE\",\"The wording of every sentence you submit\",\"Your examination answers\",\"Your judgements, recommendations and conclusions\"],\"line\":\"You may learn from anything on this platform, but what you submit for assessment must be chosen, analysed, written and judged by you, with every source and any permitted assistance acknowledged.\"},\"submit\":[{\"for\":\"IA commentary\",\"items\":[\"I chose the article myself; it was not given to the class and was not used in a class activity.\",\"The article was published no earlier than one year before I wrote the commentary.\",\"The article comes from the news media, not a television or radio broadcast or a blog unconnected to a news organisation.\",\"This commentary covers a different unit, uses a different source and applies a different key concept from my other two.\",\"If the article is long, I have highlighted the sections my commentary uses; if it is in another language, I have included an accurate translation of the whole article and the original.\",\"The commentary is no more than 800 words, counted under the guide’s rules.\",\"The cover details are complete and accurate: title, source, publication date, access date if online, date written, word count, unit and key concept.\",\"Every diagram is my own or is referenced, and each is adapted to the article.\",\"Quotations from the article are in quotation marks, and every other source is cited.\",\"I have acknowledged any use of digital tools or AI as the school requires (Verification required).\",\"I can explain every part of the commentary if my teacher asks.\",\"I understand that once I submit the final version it cannot be withdrawn (guide PDF p. 73).\"]},{\"for\":\"Extended essay\",\"items\":[\"My research question is my own and focused on an economic issue.\",\"The issue, policy or event took place no more than five years before I began my research (guide PDF p. 14).\",\"No part of this essay has been or will be submitted as my IA (guide PDF p. 74).\",\"Every source, dataset, chart and image is cited consistently in one style.\",\"Every paraphrase and summary is cited, not only direct quotations.\",\"Any primary data I collected is reported honestly, with my method described.\",\"I followed the school’s guidance on consent and privacy when collecting data from people.\",\"I have acknowledged any use of digital tools or AI as required (Verification required).\",\"I have checked the word limit and other formal requirements in the current EE guide (Verification required: check the current EE guide).\",\"I can explain and defend every argument in the essay in conversation with my supervisor.\"]},{\"for\":\"Any assessed work\",\"items\":[\"The work is my own, and I can explain every part of it.\",\"Every idea, quotation, dataset and image from elsewhere is acknowledged.\",\"Quotations are marked and cited; paraphrases and summaries are cited.\",\"My references include at least the author, date, title, and page numbers or URL as applicable, plus access dates for online sources (guide PDF p. 11).\",\"I used one referencing style consistently.\",\"I have not shared the work in a way that could let someone else copy it.\",\"I have followed the IB’s and the school’s current rules on digital tools and AI (Verification required).\",\"I have kept my notes and drafts in case I am asked how the work developed.\"]}],\"eeVsIa\":{\"head\":[\"\",\"Economics IA\",\"Extended essay\"],\"rows\":[[\"What it is\",\"A portfolio of three commentaries, each applying economic theory to a published news article (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"An in-depth research essay that builds an argument around a sharply focused research question in economics (guide PDF p. 14).\"],[\"Length\",\"Each commentary no more than 800 words (guide PDF p. 76).\",\"Verification required: check the current EE guide.\"],[\"Time frame of material\",\"Each article published no earlier than one year before the commentary is written (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"A recent economic issue, policy or event that took place up to five years before the research began (guide PDF p. 14).\"],[\"Sources\",\"The article itself, from news media but not television or radio (guide PDF p. 75); the TSM advises that additional resources should not be used (TSM PDF p. 39).\",\"Primary and secondary data, for example from books, newspapers, government publications, databanks, interviews or surveys (guide PDF p. 14).\"],[\"Link to the syllabus\",\"One commentary each on Units 2, 3 and 4, each using a different key concept (guide PDF p. 75).\",\"May relate to the DP course, but may also explore wider areas of economics (guide PDF p. 14).\"],[\"Teacher’s role\",\"The teacher advises on one draft but must not edit it; the next version is the final one (guide PDF p. 73).\",\"Verification required: check the current EE guide for the supervisor’s role.\"],[\"Assessment\",\"Criteria A–F, marked by the teacher and moderated by the IB; 45 marks in total (guide PDF pp. 63–64, 77).\",\"Verification required: check the current EE guide for the criteria and marks.\"],[\"Overlap\",\"The same piece of work cannot be submitted for both the IA and the EE (guide PDF p. 74).\",\"An economics EE is not an extension of the IA (guide PDF p. 14).\"]]},\"verify\":\"The IB’s current academic integrity policy and your school’s own policy were not available to this platform when this page was written, so check both with your teacher or DP coordinator before you submit any assessed work, especially on the use of digital tools and AI.\"},\"concepts\":[{\"c\":\"Scarcity\",\"core\":\"Scarcity is the gap between the limited land, labour, capital and entrepreneurship a society has and the far larger set of things its members would like to consume. Because wants tend to grow as incomes rise, economic growth does not remove the gap, so every society has to decide how its limited resources are put to use.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 18 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"Which resource is running short in this situation, and whose competing wants are pressing against it?\",\"tension\":\"Scarcity pulls against Equity: when a scarce good is rationed by price, it goes to those willing and able to pay, who may not be those with the greatest need. It pulls against Sustainability when today’s scarcity is eased by running down stocks that later generations will need.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"Free goods and economic goods\",\"how\":\"A good is free only when using it leaves no less for anyone else, so it carries no opportunity cost. Scarcity is a relationship between a resource and the demands placed on it, which is why the same resource can move from free to scarce as population or use grows.\"},{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"The production possibilities curve\",\"how\":\"Scarcity is drawn as a boundary: combinations beyond the curve cannot be produced with current resources and technology. Unlike in a market, the constraint appears as a physical limit, with no price involved.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"context\":\"Price rationing in a competitive market\",\"how\":\"When quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, the price rises until buyers’ plans match what is available. Scarcity is resolved through willingness and ability to pay rather than by a plan or a queue.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Rent controls\",\"how\":\"A binding ceiling does not make housing less scarce; it replaces price rationing with waiting lists, queues or informal payments. The scarcity is unchanged, but the mechanism deciding who gets the home is different.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"Fish stocks as a common pool resource\",\"how\":\"The stock is physically scarce, but because access is hard to restrict, no single user pays for depleting it. Scarcity exists in nature while being missing from the price, which is why the resource tends to be overused.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"An economy close to full employment\",\"how\":\"At the macro level scarcity is the economy’s total productive capacity, so extra spending near that limit tends to raise prices more than output. The constraint applies to all resources together, not to one good.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"Allocating a government budget\",\"how\":\"Public money is scarce too: more spending on one programme requires higher taxes, more borrowing or cuts elsewhere. Here the scarcity is fiscal and political as well as physical.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"context\":\"Comparative advantage (HL only, guide PDF p. 51)\",\"how\":\"Countries face different relative scarcities of factors, and so different opportunity costs. Trade lets each economy ease its own constraint by specialising, so scarcity becomes a reason to exchange rather than only a limit.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"context\":\"Capital shortage and the poverty cycle\",\"how\":\"Where physical, human and financial capital are all scarce, output stays low, which keeps saving low and capital scarce. Scarcity here reinforces itself over time, a dynamic that the static PPC does not show.\"}],\"ia\":\"Frame the commentary around a named shortage or rationing decision: the scarce resource, the uses competing for it and the mechanism—price, queue, quota or rule—that allocates it. Evaluation can then ask whether that mechanism is efficient and whether it is fair.\",\"tok\":\"If wants tend to grow with income, is scarcity a fact about the physical world or a claim about human nature, and what evidence could tell the two apart?\",\"links\":[\"Choice\",\"Sustainability\",\"Efficiency\"]},{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"core\":\"Scarcity forces decisions, and every decision to use resources one way means giving up another use; the value of the best option forgone is the opportunity cost. Economics examines how households, firms and governments weigh their alternatives, what shapes that weighing and what follows from the options they select.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 18 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"What was given up to make this decision, and who decided on the basis of what information?\",\"tension\":\"Choice pulls against Intervention: taxes, rules and nudges reshape or narrow the options open to people, which may improve outcomes while limiting their freedom to decide. The behavioural critique, studied at HL only (guide PDF p. 33), adds a further tension, since the choices people make may not serve their own longer-term interests.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"The basic economic questions\",\"how\":\"Every society must decide what to produce, how to produce it and for whom. A market economy spreads these decisions across many buyers and sellers while a planned economy concentrates them, so choice here is a feature of the economic system rather than of one person.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"context\":\"A household deciding what to buy\",\"how\":\"A consumer chooses within an income, so a higher price raises the sacrifice attached to each unit and quantity demanded tends to fall. Choice is recorded as a movement along the demand curve.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"context\":\"Pension auto-enrolment (HL only, guide PDF p. 33)\",\"how\":\"When saving is made the default, far more workers tend to stay enrolled even though leaving is easy. The options are unchanged and only the design of the choice has moved, which the rational model struggles to explain.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"context\":\"A monopolist setting its price (HL only, guide PDF p. 37)\",\"how\":\"A firm with market power chooses its point on the demand curve and can raise price by restricting output. Unlike a price-taking firm, its choice alters the market outcome for every buyer.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Conflicting macroeconomic objectives\",\"how\":\"A policymaker may face a short-run trade-off between lower unemployment and lower inflation (the Phillips curve analysis is HL only, guide PDF p. 44). The opportunity cost is measured in an objective sacrificed, not in goods.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"Setting a government budget\",\"how\":\"Spending on health, defence or debt interest competes for the same revenue, so each programme has a visible cost in the others. The choice is political as well as economic, and the ‘best’ allocation depends on value judgements.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"context\":\"Whether to protect a domestic industry\",\"how\":\"Protection benefits the industry’s owners and workers at the expense of consumers and of firms that use its output. Because the choice picks between identifiable groups, trade policy tends to be contested.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"context\":\"Import substitution or export promotion\",\"how\":\"A developing economy’s trade strategy shapes its industrial structure for decades. The choice is made under deep uncertainty about future world markets, so its opportunity cost may only become clear long afterwards.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Choice when the article shows an identifiable decision-maker picking between named options. State the option chosen, the alternative forgone and the reason, then evaluate whether the decision was well informed and who bore its cost.\",\"tok\":\"When an economist infers a person’s preferences from what they buy, how much can behaviour alone reveal about what someone actually wants?\",\"links\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Intervention\",\"Economic well-being\"]},{\"c\":\"Efficiency\",\"core\":\"Efficiency describes how much valuable output is obtained from a given quantity of scarce inputs, so an outcome is inefficient if resources could be rearranged to produce more of what people value. Allocative efficiency is reached when resources produce the mix of goods society values most, which occurs where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 18–19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"Could the same resources give people more of what they value if they were used differently?\",\"tension\":\"Efficiency pulls against Equity: an allocation can be efficient while leaving some people with very little, and redistribution may blunt incentives or add administrative costs. It pulls against Sustainability when output that looks efficient today rests on costs pushed onto the future.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"Points inside the PPC\",\"how\":\"A point inside the curve means resources are unemployed or misused, so output can rise with no extra inputs. Efficiency here concerns using all resources, not choosing which combination to produce.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"context\":\"Competitive market equilibrium\",\"how\":\"With no externalities, the equilibrium maximises the sum of consumer and producer surplus, so allocative efficiency is reached through prices alone. This is the benchmark against which each market failure is measured.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"Pollution from a factory\",\"how\":\"Firms respond to private cost rather than social cost, so the market produces more than the social optimum. The inefficiency appears as a welfare loss triangle, and policy aims to close the gap between marginal private and marginal social cost.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"context\":\"Monopoly (HL only, guide PDF p. 37)\",\"how\":\"A monopolist restricts output to a level where price exceeds marginal cost, so allocative efficiency is lost. Economies of scale may give a large firm lower costs than many small ones, so efficiency arguments about market power can point both ways.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Cyclical unemployment\",\"how\":\"Workers who want jobs but cannot find them are an idle resource, so a recession is inefficiency across the whole economy. Unlike a failure in a single market, the usual remedy is sought in aggregate demand.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"context\":\"Market-based supply-side reform\",\"how\":\"Deregulation, privatisation and trade liberalisation aim to raise efficiency by exposing firms to competition. Whether they succeed depends on the market structure left behind: a public monopoly sold as a private monopoly may gain little.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"context\":\"Specialisation and trade\",\"how\":\"Trade lets countries shift production towards goods they make at lower opportunity cost, raising combined output from the same resources. The gain is aggregate, and the theory does not say who receives it.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"context\":\"A tariff\",\"how\":\"A tariff raises the domestic price, drawing in higher-cost domestic production and reducing consumption, which creates two areas of welfare loss. Efficiency is lost because output moves from a lower-cost foreign producer to a higher-cost domestic one.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Efficiency when the article describes a resource being wasted, overused or misallocated, so that a welfare loss can be drawn. Locate the gap between social and private costs or benefits, then evaluate whether the proposed response narrows it at an acceptable cost.\",\"tok\":\"Allocative efficiency counts preferences by willingness to pay, which depends on income; can a claim that an outcome is ‘efficient’ ever be free of value judgements?\",\"links\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Equity\",\"Intervention\"]},{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"core\":\"Equity concerns whether the distribution of income, wealth and opportunity is fair, and because fairness is a value judgement, economists and citizens disagree about what it requires. It is distinct from equality: an equal outcome can be judged unfair and an unequal one fair, depending on whether the principle applied is need, effort, contribution or equal opportunity.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"Which principle of fairness is being applied to this outcome, and would a different principle reach a different verdict?\",\"tension\":\"Equity pulls against Efficiency: taxes and transfers that narrow gaps can weaken incentives and add costs, while leaving outcomes to the market may produce distributions many judge unfair. It is bound up with Intervention, because most policies aimed at equity require government to override market outcomes.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"context\":\"Positive and normative statements\",\"how\":\"A claim about how income is distributed can be checked against data; a claim that the distribution is unfair cannot, because it rests on a value judgement. Equity is where the positive–normative distinction does most of its work.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Price ceilings on staple foods\",\"how\":\"A ceiling is usually defended as keeping a necessity affordable for low-income households. If it causes shortages, the good may go to whoever queues first or knows the seller rather than to those in most need, so the equity gain is uncertain.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Indirect taxes on fuel\",\"how\":\"Taxes on spending tend to take a larger share of low incomes than of high ones. How the burden divides between consumers and producers depends on price elasticities, so equity here is partly an empirical question.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.12\",\"context\":\"Income distribution in a free market (HL only, guide PDF p. 39)\",\"how\":\"Markets reward people according to the factors they own, so those starting with more capital, skills or land tend to earn more. The inequity comes from the market working as designed, not from a failure of it.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"context\":\"Progressive taxes and transfers\",\"how\":\"A progressive tax takes a rising share of income as income rises, and transfer payments direct resources to lower-income households. Equity is pursued directly, and the Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient measure the result.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"context\":\"A rise in interest rates\",\"how\":\"Higher rates reward savers and raise costs for borrowers, so monetary policy redistributes even though it targets inflation. Equity here is a side effect, not the aim.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.3\",\"context\":\"Gains and losses from freer trade\",\"how\":\"Freer trade may raise national income while concentrating losses on workers in import-competing industries. The equity question is whether the winners compensate the losers, which markets do not arrange by themselves.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"context\":\"Inequality-adjusted development indicators\",\"how\":\"The IHDI lowers a country’s score when health, education and income are unevenly shared, and the GII measures gaps between women and men. Equity is built into the measurement of development rather than added afterwards.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Equity when the article reports a distributional consequence—a price rise that hits low-income households, a tax change, a trade shock concentrated in one region. Name the principle of fairness you are applying, show who gains and who loses, and argue the judgement rather than assert it.\",\"tok\":\"Can an economist recommend a policy on grounds of fairness without relying on their own values, and if not, what makes one value judgement more defensible than another?\",\"links\":[\"Efficiency\",\"Intervention\",\"Economic well-being\"]},{\"c\":\"Economic well-being\",\"core\":\"Economic well-being describes how prosperous people are and the quality of life they are able to lead, which covers more than income or output. It takes in financial security now and over time, the ability to cover basic needs, and the freedom to make economic choices that people value.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"Are people actually better off here—more secure, with needs met and real options widened—or has only a measured total gone up?\",\"tension\":\"Economic well-being pulls against Sustainability when higher consumption today is financed by depleting resources that future well-being depends on. It also parts company with Efficiency, because an efficient market outcome may still leave some households unable to meet basic needs.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"The circular flow of income\",\"how\":\"The model tracks income and spending, which are means to well-being rather than well-being itself. It is silent on leisure, health and how income is shared, so it cannot settle whether people are better off.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.3\",\"context\":\"Consumer and producer surplus\",\"how\":\"Surplus measures the gain buyers and sellers make from trading in one market, a money-based proxy for well-being. It counts a unit of surplus equally whoever receives it, so it ignores distribution.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"context\":\"Present bias in saving (HL only, guide PDF p. 33)\",\"how\":\"People may save less than their future selves would wish, so a choice that feels best today can lower well-being over a lifetime. Well-being here has a time dimension inside one person’s life.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"context\":\"Public goods such as street lighting\",\"how\":\"The market may provide little or none of a good from which no one can be excluded, even when it would raise everyone’s well-being. Well-being here depends on collective provision rather than individual purchase.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"context\":\"GDP and alternative measures\",\"how\":\"GDP values output but omits unpaid work, environmental damage and distribution. Measures such as the OECD Better Life Index and happiness indices try to capture well-being more directly, at the cost of harder comparison.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Unemployment\",\"how\":\"Losing a job cuts income but can also erode skills, health and confidence, so the cost to well-being exceeds the lost output. Some of that cost may persist after the economy recovers.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"context\":\"Remittances from migrant workers\",\"how\":\"Income sent home by workers abroad appears as a credit in the current account and can pay for food, health care and schooling. Its effect on well-being depends on who receives it and how reliable the flow is.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"context\":\"The Human Development Index\",\"how\":\"Countries with similar income per person can differ sharply in life expectancy and schooling. Composite indicators treat well-being as multidimensional, which is why rankings can change with the indicator chosen.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Economic well-being when the article is about living standards—a cost-of-living squeeze, job losses, access to services—rather than output alone. Identify which dimension of well-being is affected and for whom, and evaluate whether the usual measures capture the change.\",\"tok\":\"If two people with the same income report very different levels of life satisfaction, which kind of evidence should an economist trust, and why?\",\"links\":[\"Equity\",\"Sustainability\",\"Choice\"]},{\"c\":\"Sustainability\",\"core\":\"Sustainability asks whether present economic activity leaves future generations able to meet their own needs. In economics the focus is on environmental depletion and degradation, where today’s decisions shift costs onto people who have no voice in them.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"If this activity continued at its current rate for a generation, what would be left, and who would pay for the shortfall?\",\"tension\":\"Sustainability pulls against Economic well-being in the short run, because limiting resource use can raise prices and lower measured output now. It also tests Equity, since the costs of acting and of not acting fall on different groups and different generations.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"Scarcity over time\",\"how\":\"Using a finite resource faster now can leave the PPC further in later, so today’s output is partly borrowed from tomorrow’s capacity. Sustainability adds a time dimension to scarcity.\"},{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"context\":\"The circular economy\",\"how\":\"A circular economy keeps materials in use through repair, reuse and recycling instead of discarding them. It treats sustainability as a design problem for the whole production system rather than a pricing problem in one market.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"Overfishing\",\"how\":\"Each fisher gains the full value of an extra catch while the loss to the future stock is shared by all. Sustainability fails because access is open, and remedies include quotas, permits and collective self-governance by the users themselves.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"A carbon tax\",\"how\":\"Pricing emissions makes present decisions reflect part of the damage they impose later. Its effect depends on how responsive firms and households are, and on whether the rate is close to a cost that is hard to measure.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Growth and the environment\",\"how\":\"Rapid growth can raise living standards while increasing emissions and resource depletion. This is a conflict between macroeconomic objectives, although cleaner technologies may weaken it.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"A sovereign wealth fund built from resource revenue\",\"how\":\"A government can save part of its oil or mineral revenue so that future citizens share in a non-renewable asset. Sustainability here is financial and intergenerational rather than ecological.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"context\":\"The Sustainable Development Goals\",\"how\":\"The SDGs combine environmental, social and economic targets, treating sustainability as broader than pollution. At HL only, the relationship between sustainability and poverty is added (guide PDF p. 57): poor households often depend most directly on land, water and forests.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"context\":\"Dependence on a depletable export\",\"how\":\"An economy exporting oil or minerals can record growth while its natural capital runs down. Unless the revenue is turned into other forms of capital, growth now may reduce the capacity for development later.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Sustainability when the article involves a resource stock, an emission or a cost that arrives in the future. Make the time dimension explicit—what is used now and what is lost later—and evaluate whether the policy discussed changes the rate of depletion.\",\"tok\":\"How can economists place a present value on harm to people not yet born, and does the choice of discount rate make that valuation more a matter of ethics than of evidence?\",\"links\":[\"Scarcity\",\"Intervention\",\"Equity\",\"Economic well-being\"]},{\"c\":\"Change\",\"core\":\"Economies are in continual motion, and economic analysis concentrates on how variables move and how systems adjust from one position to another rather than on their levels at a single moment. Change can be a shift in underlying conditions such as technology, tastes or policy, or the adjustment that follows it.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"What has shifted, what adjustment follows, and how long does that adjustment take?\",\"tension\":\"Change pulls against Equity and Economic well-being when adjustment costs land on particular groups, such as workers in declining industries. It also sits uneasily with Intervention: policy that resists change may protect people now while slowing the reallocation that raises living standards later.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.2\",\"context\":\"The history of economic thought\",\"how\":\"Economic ideas shift with events: the Great Depression encouraged Keynesian thinking, and the high inflation of the 1970s strengthened monetarist ideas. Change here is in the models economists use, not only in the data they study.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"context\":\"Movement along versus shift of demand\",\"how\":\"A change in the good’s own price moves consumers along the demand curve, while a change in income, tastes or the prices of related goods shifts the whole curve. Telling the two apart is the first test of whether a change has been analysed correctly.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"context\":\"Price elasticity of demand\",\"how\":\"Elasticity measures how strongly quantity responds to a change in price. It turns change into a number, which lets a firm or government predict the revenue effect of a price or tax change.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.6\",\"context\":\"Supply response over time\",\"how\":\"Supply tends to be more elastic in the long run than in the short run, because firms can adjust capacity. The same rise in demand can therefore cause a sharp price increase at first and a smaller one later.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"context\":\"A fall in aggregate demand\",\"how\":\"Lower AD reduces output and employment in the short run; in the monetarist/new classical view the economy returns to full employment in the long run, while the Keynesian view allows a gap to persist. The speed of self-correction is where the schools of thought divide.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Inflation as a rate of change\",\"how\":\"Inflation measures how fast the average price level is rising, so falling inflation means prices are still rising, only more slowly. Mixing up a change in a level with a change in a rate is a common error.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"context\":\"A floating exchange rate\",\"how\":\"The exchange rate moves continuously with trade flows, interest rates and expectations. Each movement changes import prices, export competitiveness and inflation, so a change in one price spreads through the economy.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"context\":\"The J-curve (HL only, guide PDF p. 56)\",\"how\":\"After a depreciation, the current account may worsen before it improves, because prices change at once while trade volumes respond slowly. The direction of change depends on the time period chosen.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Change when the article reports a shock, a policy shift or a trend. Trace the change on a diagram from the original to the new position, and discuss how the short-run effect may differ from the long-run one.\",\"tok\":\"If the relationships economists estimate can themselves change over time, how far can past data justify a prediction about the future?\",\"links\":[\"Interdependence\",\"Economic well-being\",\"Intervention\"]},{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"core\":\"Economic agents depend on one another as buyers, sellers, lenders, employers and taxpayers, because none can produce everything it needs. The tighter those links become, the further a single decision or shock travels, often with effects that nobody intended.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF p. 19 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"Through which link—trade, finance, a supply chain or policy—does this decision reach someone else, and how large is the knock-on effect?\",\"tension\":\"Interdependence pulls against Intervention by a single government: the more integrated an economy, the more its policies are offset or amplified by responses elsewhere. It also strains Equity, because the gains from integration and the costs of shocks are spread unevenly.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"The circular flow of income\",\"how\":\"One sector’s spending is another’s income, so a fall in household spending cuts firms’ revenue, then wages, then spending again. Interdependence here is a loop within one economy.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"context\":\"Substitutes and complements\",\"how\":\"A change in the price of one good shifts demand for the goods related to it. Interdependence here links markets rather than people.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"Externalities\",\"how\":\"An externality is interdependence that bypasses the market: one agent’s action affects another with no payment between them. The missing price is what makes it a market failure.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"context\":\"Oligopoly (HL only, guide PDF p. 38)\",\"how\":\"Each firm’s best price depends on what its rivals do, which can lead to price wars or collusion. Interdependence here is strategic: firms act on what they expect others to do.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"context\":\"Monetary policy transmission\",\"how\":\"A change in the central bank’s rate passes through banks, borrowers, asset prices and the exchange rate before it reaches spending. The length of that chain is why the full effect builds gradually, even though the decision itself can be taken quickly.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"The Keynesian multiplier (HL only, guide PDF p. 48)\",\"how\":\"An injection of spending becomes income for others, who spend part of it in turn. The final effect depends on how much leaks into saving, taxes and imports at each round.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"context\":\"Monetary union\",\"how\":\"Members sharing a currency give up their own interest rate and exchange rate, so a shock in one member affects policy for all. Interdependence here is institutional and hard to reverse (weighing its advantages and disadvantages is HL only, guide PDF p. 53).\"},{\"sub\":\"4.6\",\"context\":\"The current and financial accounts\",\"how\":\"A country with a current account deficit relies on net inflows on its capital and financial accounts to match it. Its spending on imports therefore depends on foreign investors’ willingness to lend or invest.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Interdependence when the article describes an effect passing between agents, markets or countries—a supply-chain disruption, a partner’s tariff, an interest rate change abroad. Trace the transmission step by step and evaluate how far and how strongly the effect is likely to spread.\",\"tok\":\"When an outcome has many interlocking causes, can economists ever isolate the effect of one of them, and what is lost by holding everything else equal?\",\"links\":[\"Change\",\"Intervention\",\"Equity\"]},{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"core\":\"Intervention is government action that changes what markets would otherwise produce, through taxes, subsidies, price controls, regulation, direct provision or information. It is usually defended on the grounds that an unregulated market has missed a goal such as efficiency, equity, well-being or sustainability, yet the case for any particular intervention, and its scale, is disputed.\",\"src\":\"guide PDF pp. 19–20 (Figure 4)\",\"q\":\"What specific failure is this action meant to correct, and is the remedy likely to cost less than the problem?\",\"tension\":\"Intervention pulls against Choice and Efficiency: correcting one failure can create new distortions, such as shortages or rent-seeking, when government lacks the information to set policy well. It must also reckon with Interdependence, because other agents respond and may offset the intended effect.\",\"worlds\":[{\"sub\":\"1.1\",\"context\":\"Market, planned and mixed economies\",\"how\":\"Economic systems differ in how much allocation is left to markets and how much to the state. Real economies are mixed, so the useful question is the degree and form of intervention rather than its presence.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Rent controls\",\"how\":\"A ceiling aims to protect tenants, but holding the price below equilibrium can reduce the quantity of housing supplied and create shortages. Intervention here pursues equity at a possible cost to efficiency.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Consumer nudges (HL only, guide PDF p. 36)\",\"how\":\"A nudge changes how options are presented rather than restricting them or changing their prices. It is a light form of intervention that relies on behavioural insight instead of compulsion.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"context\":\"Public goods\",\"how\":\"Where free-riding means the market provides little or nothing, government may supply the good directly or contract a private firm to do so. Intervention here replaces missing provision rather than correcting a price.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"context\":\"Responding to abuse of market power (HL only, guide PDF p. 39)\",\"how\":\"Governments may use legislation and regulation, public ownership or fines against a dominant firm. The target is the structure and conduct of a market rather than a single price.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"context\":\"Monetary policy\",\"how\":\"A central bank adjusts interest rates to influence aggregate demand and inflation. The intervention is indirect, working through many private decisions, and its full effect arrives with a lag.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.7\",\"context\":\"Interventionist supply-side policies\",\"how\":\"Spending on education, infrastructure or targeted industries aims to raise long-run productive capacity. Its results take years to appear and are hard to attribute to the policy alone.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"context\":\"Tariffs and quotas\",\"how\":\"Protection intervenes at the border, raising domestic prices for consumers and for firms that use imports. It may support jobs in one industry while raising costs across others.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"context\":\"Managing an exchange rate\",\"how\":\"A central bank can buy or sell its currency, or change interest rates, to influence the exchange rate. This intervention is limited by reserves and credibility, and can be overwhelmed by market flows.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"context\":\"State-led versus market-oriented development\",\"how\":\"Strategies range from industrial policy and public investment to liberalisation and privatisation. Evidence suggests context matters, since similar strategies have produced different results in different countries.\"}],\"ia\":\"Use Intervention when the article reports a policy that has been announced, debated or evaluated. Identify the failure it targets, show its intended effect on a diagram, and evaluate its costs, its side effects and who gains and loses.\",\"tok\":\"How can a government know that a market outcome is bad enough to justify intervention, when what would have happened without the policy cannot be directly observed?\",\"links\":[\"Efficiency\",\"Equity\",\"Sustainability\",\"Choice\"]}],\"lens\":{\"intro\":\"Economists disagree about many answers, but they tend to ask the same questions in roughly the same order. These seventeen questions turn that habit into a routine you can apply to any case, article or examination prompt.\",\"groups\":[{\"name\":\"The problem\",\"ids\":[1,2,3,4]},{\"name\":\"The explanation\",\"ids\":[5,6,7]},{\"name\":\"The consequences\",\"ids\":[8,9,10,11]},{\"name\":\"The evidence\",\"ids\":[12,13,14]},{\"name\":\"The judgement\",\"ids\":[15,16,17]}],\"qs\":[{\"n\":1,\"q\":\"What is scarce?\",\"why\":\"It fixes the constraint that makes the situation an economic problem in the first place.\",\"hint\":\"Name the specific resource and what it is scarce relative to, rather than writing that ‘resources are scarce’.\",\"ex\":\"Road space in the city centre at peak hours is scarce: at a zero price, more drivers want to use it than it can carry without heavy congestion.\"},{\"n\":2,\"q\":\"Who is choosing?\",\"why\":\"It identifies the decision-makers whose behaviour the analysis has to explain.\",\"hint\":\"List each agent separately—households, firms, government, the foreign sector—because they face different constraints and objectives.\",\"ex\":\"Commuters choose how and when to travel, businesses choose delivery times and locations, and the city authority chooses the size of the charge and the boundary of the zone.\"},{\"n\":3,\"q\":\"What is the opportunity cost?\",\"why\":\"It turns a decision into a trade-off by naming what is given up.\",\"hint\":\"State the next-best alternative in concrete terms, remembering that money is only a way of measuring that alternative.\",\"ex\":\"A driver who pays the charge gives up whatever else that money would have bought, and the city that spends the revenue on buses gives up the other uses of those funds.\"},{\"n\":4,\"q\":\"What incentive exists?\",\"why\":\"It explains why agents might change their behaviour.\",\"hint\":\"Say which price, cost or reward has changed, in which direction and for whom.\",\"ex\":\"The charge raises the private cost of driving into the centre at peak times, giving some drivers a reason to switch mode, route or time of travel.\"},{\"n\":5,\"q\":\"What mechanism is operating?\",\"why\":\"It connects cause and effect through a chain that can be explained one step at a time.\",\"hint\":\"Write the chain with ‘because’ or ‘→’ at each link, so that no step is assumed.\",\"ex\":\"Higher cost of driving → fewer peak car trips → shorter travel times for the remaining cars and buses → less congestion and lower emissions inside the zone.\"},{\"n\":6,\"q\":\"Which model explains it?\",\"why\":\"It selects the tool that disciplines the reasoning and makes a diagram possible.\",\"hint\":\"Choose the model that matches the mechanism and say why it fits, rather than drawing the first diagram that comes to mind.\",\"ex\":\"A negative externality of consumption diagram fits, because drivers weigh their own benefit but not the delay and pollution they impose on others, and the charge acts like a tax that moves quantity towards the social optimum.\"},{\"n\":7,\"q\":\"What assumptions are being made?\",\"why\":\"It exposes the conditions under which the model’s conclusion holds.\",\"hint\":\"State the ceteris paribus assumption and any assumptions about information, rationality or responsiveness on which the argument depends.\",\"ex\":\"The analysis assumes drivers respond to price, that buses and trains have room for those who switch, and that the charge can be set close to the external cost of a trip.\"},{\"n\":8,\"q\":\"Who gains?\",\"why\":\"It identifies the beneficiaries, whose support may shape whether a policy survives.\",\"hint\":\"Name the group and the channel through which it gains, rather than writing ‘society’.\",\"ex\":\"Bus passengers, cyclists, pedestrians and drivers who value shorter travel times gain, and the city gains revenue it can reinvest.\"},{\"n\":9,\"q\":\"Who loses?\",\"why\":\"It brings distribution into the analysis, which is where many policy arguments are settled.\",\"hint\":\"Look for the less visible losers, including those just outside the policy’s boundary.\",\"ex\":\"Lower-income drivers with no practical alternative may lose most, as may some shops inside the zone if fewer customers drive in.\"},{\"n\":10,\"q\":\"What happens in the short run?\",\"why\":\"It separates the immediate response from the eventual outcome.\",\"hint\":\"Say which factors are fixed in the short run and which can already adjust.\",\"ex\":\"In the first months most drivers can change only the time or route of their trips, so traffic may fall by less than it will once people have had time to adapt.\"},{\"n\":11,\"q\":\"What happens in the long run?\",\"why\":\"It shows how behaviour and capacity adjust once agents can change more of their decisions.\",\"hint\":\"Consider changes in location, technology and investment, and ask whether the first effect grows, fades or reverses.\",\"ex\":\"Over several years households and firms may relocate, bus services may expand and car ownership may fall, but traffic could creep back if the charge is not reviewed.\"},{\"n\":12,\"q\":\"What evidence exists?\",\"why\":\"It tests the reasoning against what has been observed.\",\"hint\":\"Separate data from interpretation, and correlation from causation.\",\"ex\":\"Cities that have introduced such charges have generally reported lower traffic inside the zone after launch, although separating the charge’s effect from other changes is difficult.\"},{\"n\":13,\"q\":\"What are the limitations?\",\"why\":\"It sets out how far the model and the evidence can be trusted.\",\"hint\":\"Name the specific assumption or data weakness that would change the conclusion if it failed.\",\"ex\":\"The external cost of a car trip is hard to measure, so the ‘right’ charge is an estimate, and traffic counts inside the zone miss effects on roads just outside it.\"},{\"n\":14,\"q\":\"What alternative explanation exists?\",\"why\":\"It guards against attributing an outcome to the wrong cause.\",\"hint\":\"Ask what else changed at the same time and whether it could produce the same pattern.\",\"ex\":\"A fall in traffic could also reflect higher fuel prices, a downturn or more home working, so a simple before-and-after comparison does not isolate the charge.\"},{\"n\":15,\"q\":\"What policy response is possible?\",\"why\":\"It moves the analysis from explaining an outcome to recommending an action.\",\"hint\":\"Compare at least two realistic options, including doing nothing, against the same criteria.\",\"ex\":\"Options include raising parking fees, subsidising public transport, restricting which vehicles may enter, or leaving congestion to ration road space by delay.\"},{\"n\":16,\"q\":\"What are the unintended consequences?\",\"why\":\"It checks whether solving one problem creates another.\",\"hint\":\"Follow the incentive to the agents the policy was not aimed at.\",\"ex\":\"Traffic may divert to streets just outside the boundary, and spending may shift from shops in the zone to online retailers or out-of-town centres.\"},{\"n\":17,\"q\":\"What judgement can be justified?\",\"why\":\"It draws the analysis together into a conclusion the evidence can support.\",\"hint\":\"Give a conditional verdict—effective if…, less so where…—and say which factor matters most.\",\"ex\":\"A charge is likely to work best where good alternatives exist and the revenue improves them, and to be least equitable where low-income commuters have no other way to reach work.\"}],\"example\":\"A large city introduces a daily charge for driving a car into its centre at peak times.\"},\"intel\":[{\"id\":\"rw1\",\"q\":\"How do consumers and producers make choices in trying to meet their economic objectives?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Consumers and producers both face scarcity: households have limited incomes and firms have limited resources, so each must choose. The market coordinates vast numbers of these choices through prices.\",\"subs\":[\"2.1\",\"2.2\",\"2.3\",\"2.4\",\"2.5\",\"2.6\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"The laws of demand and supply describe how planned purchases and sales respond to price, and equilibrium is where those plans match, with the price mechanism signalling, giving incentives and rationing. At HL, rational choice theory assumes consumers maximise utility and firms maximise profit, and behavioural economics tests those assumptions (guide PDF p. 33).\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"The demand and supply diagram shows how a change in a non-price determinant shifts one curve and moves the market to a new equilibrium price and quantity, with consumer and producer surplus showing the gains from exchange.\",\"dg\":\"ds\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"Surge pricing on ride-hailing apps shows price rationing scarce drivers at busy times. Pension auto-enrolment and England’s opt-out organ donation rules show choices changing when only the default changes (HL only, guide PDF p. 33).\",\"cases\":[\"MIC-039\",\"MIC-026\",\"MIC-027\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Studies generally find that quantity demanded falls when price rises, as the model predicts, but the size of the response varies widely between goods and over time. Evidence from changes to default options suggests that the way a choice is framed can shift behaviour substantially, which is hard to reconcile with a strictly rational model.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"An understanding of choice lets governments anticipate how buyers and sellers will react to taxes, subsidies and price controls, and at HL to design nudges that change behaviour without removing options (guide PDF p. 36). Firms apply the same reasoning when they use elasticity to set prices, although how far they do so in practice is an open question.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"The maximising model is a useful benchmark because it gives clear, testable predictions, but it assumes information and self-control that people may lack. A strong evaluation uses the model for the direction of change and behavioural evidence for its size and exceptions, and treats equilibrium as a tendency in markets that keep moving.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"In Paper 1, part (a) is worth 10 marks and part (b) 15 marks, and SL papers exclude HL-only content such as the behavioural economics in 2.4 (guide PDF pp. 66–67). Where a question involves a change in market conditions, a correctly labelled, fully explained diagram of the shift and the new equilibrium is central, and in part (b) a real-world example should be explained in relation to the question rather than only named (guide PDF pp. 65, 70).\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Choice\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"2.1\",\"context\":\"A household deciding what to buy\",\"how\":\"The consumer chooses within an income, so a higher price raises the sacrifice attached to each unit and quantity demanded tends to fall. Choice shows up as a movement along the demand curve.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.2\",\"context\":\"A firm deciding how much to supply\",\"how\":\"A producer compares the price it can obtain with the cost of producing more, so a higher price makes extra output worthwhile. The constraint here is cost and technology rather than income.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.4\",\"context\":\"Pension auto-enrolment (HL only, guide PDF p. 33)\",\"how\":\"When saving becomes the default, participation tends to rise sharply even though opting out is easy. The options are unchanged; only the design of the choice has moved.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.5\",\"context\":\"A firm using price elasticity to set prices\",\"how\":\"If demand is price inelastic, a price rise increases total revenue, so the firm’s best price depends on how buyers respond. The choice is strategic, because the firm must anticipate the reaction of consumers.\"}]}},{\"id\":\"rw2\",\"q\":\"When are markets unable to satisfy important economic objectives—and does government intervention help?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Markets can fail on efficiency, on sustainability or on equity, and these are different failures that call for different responses. Intervention is the government’s attempt to correct the shortfall, and it can succeed or fail on its own terms.\",\"subs\":[\"2.7\",\"2.8\",\"2.9\",\"2.10\",\"2.11\",\"2.12\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"Allocative efficiency requires marginal social benefit to equal marginal social cost; externalities, public goods, common pool resources and, at HL only (guide PDF p. 37), asymmetric information and market power each drive a wedge between private and social outcomes. Taxes, subsidies, price controls, regulation, direct provision and information are the tools that aim to close that wedge.\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"The negative externality of production diagram shows the market producing where marginal private cost meets demand, beyond the social optimum, with a welfare loss triangle that a Pigouvian tax equal to the external cost would remove.\",\"dg\":\"negprod\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"Sweden’s carbon tax and the EU Emissions Trading System price emissions in two different ways, and the UK Soft Drinks Industry Levy targets a demerit good. Overfishing shows the tragedy of the commons at work.\",\"cases\":[\"MIC-035\",\"MIC-054\",\"MIC-022\",\"MIC-051\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Evidence on carbon pricing suggests emissions can fall in the sectors covered, although the size of the effect is debated and hard to separate from other policies. Studies of sugar levies suggest producers may respond by reformulating products, which can matter as much as changes in what consumers buy.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"Market-based tools such as taxes and tradable permits work through prices, command-and-control tools set limits directly, and education and nudges work through information and the design of choices. The most suitable tool depends on how well the external cost can be measured, monitored and enforced.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"Intervention helps where the failure is well diagnosed and the tool fits it, but it can create new problems: price controls cause shortages or surpluses, subsidies can be captured by producers, and regulators may lack information. Judge each policy against a realistic alternative, not against a perfect market.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"In Paper 2, parts (c)–(f) carry 4 marks each and part (g) 15 marks, so an accurate welfare-loss diagram tied to the text supports the short parts while (g) needs a supported judgement (guide PDF p. 67). Subtopics 2.10–2.12 are HL only and can also be examined in Paper 3, which has two compulsory questions of 30 marks each (guide PDF pp. 26, 68).\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Intervention\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"2.7\",\"context\":\"Minimum support prices for crops\",\"how\":\"A price floor raises farm incomes but creates surpluses that the government must buy, store or dispose of. The intervention targets producers’ incomes and shifts part of the cost to taxpayers.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.8\",\"context\":\"A carbon tax\",\"how\":\"A tax on emissions raises the private cost of polluting towards its social cost while leaving firms free to choose how to cut. Because it works through price, its effect depends on how responsive firms are.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.9\",\"context\":\"Street lighting and flood defences\",\"how\":\"Free-riding means the market supplies little or none of these goods, so government provides them directly or pays a private firm to do so. Intervention here replaces the market rather than adjusting a price.\"},{\"sub\":\"2.11\",\"context\":\"Responding to abuse of market power (HL only, guide PDF p. 39)\",\"how\":\"Legislation and regulation, public ownership or fines can be used against a dominant firm. The intervention targets market structure and conduct rather than a single price.\"}]}},{\"id\":\"rw3\",\"q\":\"Why does economic activity vary over time and why does this matter?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Economic activity changes over time because conditions on the demand side and the supply side of the economy keep shifting. Those changes reach households as jobs, prices and incomes, which is why they matter.\",\"subs\":[\"3.1\",\"3.2\",\"3.3\",\"3.4\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"Aggregate demand, C + I + G + (X − M), and aggregate supply together determine real output and the price level: demand shocks move output and prices in the same direction, supply shocks in opposite directions. Keynesian and monetarist/new classical economists disagree about how quickly the economy returns to full employment without help.\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"The AD/AS diagram shows a fall in aggregate demand opening a deflationary gap, with real output below potential and cyclical unemployment rising.\",\"dg\":\"defgap\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"The 2008 global financial crisis is a case of a large demand shock, the oil shocks of the 1970s show a supply shock bringing stagflation, and post-pandemic supply-chain disruption combined elements of both. Youth unemployment in Spain after the euro-area crisis shows how a downturn can fall hardest on one group.\",\"cases\":[\"MAC-013\",\"MAC-042\",\"MAC-041\",\"MAC-032\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Available data indicate that recessions raise unemployment and that some losses of jobs and skills can persist after output recovers. Whether a particular burst of inflation came mainly from demand or from supply is often contested, because both can raise prices at the same time.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"Because fluctuations damage economic well-being, governments try to stabilise the cycle through demand management and to raise the trend rate of growth through supply-side measures. Those policies are the subject of the next real-world issue.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"GDP tracks activity but misses distribution, unpaid work and environmental costs, so a recovery in GDP need not mean a recovery in well-being. The same data can support different explanations depending on the model applied, so name the school of thought behind each claim.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"Paper 2 includes some quantitative questions (guide PDF p. 63), so practise calculating real GDP, growth rates and inflation from data and then explaining the result with an AD/AS diagram. The weighted price index calculation and the Phillips curve are HL only (guide PDF p. 44).\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Change\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"3.1\",\"context\":\"The business cycle\",\"how\":\"Real GDP fluctuates around a rising long-term trend, so each phase—expansion, peak, contraction, trough—describes a direction of change rather than a level. Measuring that change properly requires real rather than nominal figures.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.2\",\"context\":\"A supply shock\",\"how\":\"A sudden rise in energy costs shifts SRAS to the left, so output falls while the price level rises. Unlike a demand shock, the two variables move in opposite directions, which makes the policy response harder.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.3\",\"context\":\"Inflation and disinflation\",\"how\":\"When inflation falls, prices are still rising, only more slowly. Confusing a change in a rate with a change in a level is a common error with this variable.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.4\",\"context\":\"Inequality over the cycle\",\"how\":\"Job losses in a downturn tend to fall more heavily on lower-paid and less secure workers, so the distribution of income can shift as well as its total. A change in an aggregate can hide a change in who holds the income.\"}]}},{\"id\":\"rw4\",\"q\":\"How do governments manage their economy and how effective are their policies?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Intervention at the level of the whole economy: governments and central banks use monetary, fiscal and supply-side policies to pursue growth, low unemployment, low and stable inflation and other objectives.\",\"subs\":[\"3.5\",\"3.6\",\"3.7\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"Monetary and fiscal policy shift aggregate demand, while supply-side policies aim to shift long-run aggregate supply; each works through a chain of private responses and faces lags. At HL, the multiplier can magnify a change in spending and crowding out can offset it (guide PDF pp. 48–49).\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"The long-run growth diagram shows supply-side policy shifting LRAS to the right, raising potential output and easing pressure on the price level, in contrast to demand-side policy, which moves the economy towards its existing capacity.\",\"dg\":\"growth\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"The US Federal Reserve’s interest rate rises in 2022, the UK furlough scheme during the pandemic and India’s Production-Linked Incentive scheme show monetary, fiscal and supply-side policy in turn. The UK’s 2022 ‘mini-budget’ shows financial markets constraining fiscal policy.\",\"cases\":[\"MAC-012\",\"MAC-024\",\"MAC-005\",\"MAC-022\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Evidence suggests that interest rate decisions can be taken and reversed quickly but that their full effect on output and inflation builds over many months, and may be weaker when rates are close to zero or confidence is low. Estimates of fiscal multipliers vary widely with the state of the economy and with how the spending is financed.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"Governments often combine policies: a central bank may tighten while the government supports particular groups, or supply-side reform may accompany a demand stimulus. Central bank independence, fiscal rules and credibility shape how households, firms and markets respond.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"Effectiveness is conditional: monetary policy may struggle in a deep recession, fiscal policy faces political pressure, time lags and debt constraints, and supply-side policies take years and may widen inequality. A strong judgement names the condition under which each policy works and the one under which it does not.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"Evaluating a policy in Paper 1 part (b), worth 15 marks, needs strengths and limitations weighed for a named context and supported by an explained real-world example (guide PDF pp. 65–66). Monetary policy tools, the multiplier and crowding out are HL only and can also appear in Paper 3, which mixes quantitative and qualitative questions (guide PDF pp. 47–49, 64).\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Interdependence\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"3.5\",\"context\":\"Interest rate transmission\",\"how\":\"A change in the policy rate passes through bank lending rates, asset prices, confidence and the exchange rate before it affects spending. Each link depends on how private agents respond, which is why the full effect is gradual.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"The Keynesian multiplier (HL only, guide PDF p. 48)\",\"how\":\"Government spending becomes someone’s income, part of which is spent again, so the final change in GDP can exceed the initial injection. Its size depends on leakages into saving, taxes and imports.\"},{\"sub\":\"3.6\",\"context\":\"Crowding out (HL only, guide PDF p. 49)\",\"how\":\"Government borrowing can raise interest rates and reduce private investment, offsetting part of a fiscal expansion. Interdependence between the public and private sectors limits what fiscal policy achieves on its own.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"context\":\"Monetary policy and the exchange rate\",\"how\":\"Higher interest rates can attract capital from abroad and strengthen the currency, which makes exports dearer and imports cheaper. A domestic policy decision therefore works partly through the foreign sector.\"}]}},{\"id\":\"rw5\",\"q\":\"Who are the winners and losers of the integration of the world’s economies?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Interdependence: trade, capital flows and trading blocs tie economies together, often raising total output while redistributing gains and losses within and between countries.\",\"subs\":[\"4.1\",\"4.2\",\"4.3\",\"4.4\",\"4.5\",\"4.6\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"Free trade lowers prices for consumers and widens markets for exporters, while import-competing producers lose; at HL, comparative advantage explains why specialisation can raise combined output (guide PDF p. 51). Protection reverses some of these effects at the cost of welfare losses, and exchange rates and the balance of payments link trade to financial flows.\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"The tariff diagram shows the domestic price rising above the world price, domestic production up, consumption and imports down, revenue for the government, and two triangles of welfare loss.\",\"dg\":\"tariff\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"The US–China trade war and the ‘China shock’ to US manufacturing show integration, and its partial reversal, creating concentrated losers, while Bangladesh’s garment exports show gains from specialisation. Brexit illustrates the trade frictions that can follow from leaving a single market.\",\"cases\":[\"GLO-001\",\"GLO-049\",\"GLO-036\",\"GLO-003\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Studies generally find that trade raises aggregate income, but that losses can be concentrated in particular regions and occupations and can persist for years. Evidence on recent tariffs suggests much of their cost may fall on domestic buyers rather than foreign exporters, although this varies by case.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"Governments can choose free trade, protection, membership of a trading bloc or exchange rate management, and can pair openness with support for those who lose. Multilateral rules through the WTO limit some of these options.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"Aggregate gains do not settle the question, because markets do not require winners to compensate losers. A balanced answer weighs efficiency gains against distributional costs, and short-run disruption against long-run specialisation.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"Stakeholder analysis is central: the tariff and quota diagrams let you identify effects on consumers, domestic producers, foreign producers and the government, and at HL calculate them (guide PDF p. 52). When a Paper 2 part (g) question, worth 15 marks, asks for a judgement on a trade policy, naming the gainers and losers gives the answer its structure (guide PDF p. 67).\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Equity\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"4.1\",\"context\":\"Cheaper imports under free trade\",\"how\":\"Consumers, including low-income households, gain from lower prices, while workers in import-competing industries may lose jobs that are hard to replace locally. Equity here asks whether the aggregate gain reaches those who bear the adjustment.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.2\",\"context\":\"An import quota\",\"how\":\"A quota raises the domestic price as a tariff does, but the government collects no revenue and the higher price on permitted imports goes to foreign producers or licence holders. Equity turns on who captures that extra revenue.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.4\",\"context\":\"Trade diversion in a customs union (HL only, guide PDF p. 53)\",\"how\":\"Lower-cost producers outside the bloc lose sales to higher-cost producers inside it because of the common external tariff. The losers sit outside the agreement and have no say in it.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.5\",\"context\":\"A currency depreciation\",\"how\":\"A weaker currency helps exporters and their workers but raises the price of imported food and fuel, which may weigh most on poorer households. The same exchange rate movement creates winners and losers within one country.\"}]}},{\"id\":\"rw6\",\"q\":\"Why is economic development uneven?\",\"path\":[{\"stage\":\"Concept\",\"t\":\"Economic well-being and equity: development is multidimensional, covering health, education, living standards and freedom of choice, and countries start from very different positions.\",\"subs\":[\"4.7\",\"4.8\",\"4.9\",\"4.10\"]},{\"stage\":\"Theory\",\"t\":\"Growth raises output, but development depends on whether that output improves people’s lives, and barriers such as poverty cycles, weak institutions, commodity dependence and gender inequality can hold it back. Strategies range from market-based reform to state-led intervention, and their success depends on context.\"},{\"stage\":\"Model\",\"t\":\"A poverty cycle links low income to low saving, low investment and low human capital, which keep income low; on a PPC, breaking the cycle appears as an outward shift of the frontier.\",\"dg\":\"ppc\"},{\"stage\":\"Real world\",\"t\":\"Kerala’s record shows social outcomes running ahead of income, and Botswana’s diamond revenues contrast with Nigeria’s experience of oil dependence. Bolsa Família and M-Pesa show two different routes to widening people’s options.\",\"cases\":[\"DEV-047\",\"DEV-015\",\"DEV-016\",\"DEV-021\",\"DEV-013\"]},{\"stage\":\"Evidence\",\"t\":\"Evidence suggests that sustained growth is usually associated with falling absolute poverty, but its link to health, education and inequality is weaker and varies across countries. Randomised trials have added evidence on specific programmes such as cash transfers, although a result in one setting may not carry over to another.\"},{\"stage\":\"Policy\",\"t\":\"Options include trade strategies, diversification, investment in health, education and infrastructure, institutional reform, foreign aid, debt relief and microfinance. Many cases cited as successes combined several of these, sequenced to the country’s own constraints.\"},{\"stage\":\"Evaluation\",\"t\":\"Because development is multidimensional, choosing an indicator is itself a judgement about what matters, and the HDI, IHDI and GII can rank the same countries differently. Judge a strategy by how well it fits a country’s particular barriers rather than by its record elsewhere.\"},{\"stage\":\"Exam\",\"t\":\"Paper 1 part (b) and Paper 2 part (g) are each worth 15 marks and both call for evaluation (guide PDF pp. 66–67). Where appropriate, the guide expects real-world examples explained in relation to the question for the highest markbands, while the Paper 2 part (g) markband rewards use of the text/data (guide PDF pp. 65, 71), so prepare two or three country cases in depth rather than many in outline.\"}],\"worlds\":{\"c\":\"Sustainability\",\"items\":[{\"sub\":\"4.7\",\"context\":\"Poverty and the environment (HL only, guide PDF p. 57)\",\"how\":\"Poor households often depend directly on land, forests and water, so environmental damage can deepen poverty, and poverty can push people towards unsustainable use. Sustainability and poverty reduction can reinforce each other rather than conflict.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.8\",\"context\":\"The Happy Planet Index\",\"how\":\"The index relates well-being outcomes to ecological footprint, so a country with high income but heavy resource use can score poorly. Sustainability is written into the measure itself.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.9\",\"context\":\"Dependence on primary commodities\",\"how\":\"An economy exporting a depletable resource can record growth while its natural capital runs down. Unless the revenue is turned into other forms of capital, growth now may reduce the capacity for development later.\"},{\"sub\":\"4.10\",\"context\":\"Infrastructure and energy investment\",\"how\":\"Building power, transport and water systems can lift development, but the technologies chosen may lock in emissions for decades. Sustainability here is a question about which path of development, not whether to develop.\"}]}}]}");
